1643764	201905	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 231; Flight Crew Total 7846; Flight Crew Type 7846					1643764	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1643797	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During Takeoff in ZZZ at @ 90 kts, we started to get a strong odor of fumes in the Flight deck. I decided to continue the Takeoff and we rotated and climbed normally. We donned our O2 (Oxygen) masks and at @ 3,000 feet MSL. The odor subsided. After, the First Officer ran the after takeoff checklist, we also ran the fumes checklist in the QRH and sent Maintenance a Logbook entry. The Aft galley Flight Attendant called up front and said they smelled fumes but that it had gone away. He had also told me that the front galley flight attendants did not smell anything at all. We decided that it was safe to continue.During cruise flight, after roughly 1 hr of flying, we decided to do a bathroom break. At that time, the flight attendants in the forward galley could smell a strong odor still lingering in the Flight deck. We were just about to begin the arrival and we decided to don our O2 masks for much of the remaining flight. After parking, during our debrief we both felt like we had some throat issues and felt like it would be best that we would be checked by medical personnel. I then called the Chief Pilot and contacted Maintenance over the radio to give a more detailed account of our write up.		During our takeoff roll, during high speed out of ZZZ we experienced a strong chemical odor on the flight deck. We continued and donned our Oxygen masks shortly after takeoff, then ran the appropriate checklists. Only the aft Flight Attendant could smell anything, no passengers could smell any odors. We removed our oxygen mask when we thought it was clear and could no longer smell anything. We both decided to continue. After roughly, 1 hour into the flight, we decided to take a bathroom break, and when a Flight Attendant entered the flight deck they notice the noxious odor still lingering, we must have just gotten used to it. So immediately, we donned our oxygen masks for the remainder of the arrival. We then made the appropriate Maintenance write up and decided to get checked out by medical professionals.		B737-900 flight crew reported a strong chemical odor on departure that dissipated. Crew was checked by medical personnel at destination.	
1644373	201905	1801-2400	ZZZ1.Airport	US				3000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 371		Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1644373	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250; Flight Crew Type 15000		Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1644380	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft; Company Policy; Human Factors	Company Policy	ZZZ to ZZZ1, we were cleared direct to the Final Approach Fix going into ZZZ at 3000 feet about five miles out with the field in sight, when ATC turned us out to the west, at which point they put us into a box holding pattern. They told us that an aircraft had blown a tire and the runway would be cleaned up soon. We thought, no problem we have plenty of gas. Then about 15 minutes later we were told it would be another 20 minutes which again, no problem. We had the gas and it is a VFR day. Now we were down to about 6.0 on gas and dispatch started working on numbers to go to ZZZ2. The Captain asked ATC what the game plan was. Their response was, 'I will get back with you in a minute.' We had talked about landing on Runway 18/36 if we needed to, when Control told everyone that the airport was closed. This was the first time we heard this. The Captain requested direct to ZZZ2. We requested 23000 feet but were given 10,000 feet which put us right in the buildups and lower than we wanted, for fuel saving reasons. We were given to Center where the Captain requested direct to the field. But we were given direct to ZZZZZ for the ZZZZZ Arrival. The fuel showed landing below minimum fuel. The Captain than declared minimum fuel status and asked for priority handling and requested direct to the field and to climb higher. Center held us down. The Captain made multiple requests to climb higher. We were finally cleared to climb. Now the fuel showed us landing with even less. Now we were approaching emergency fuel. The Captain again asked for direct to the field. I pulled up the progress page and it showed us landing with less than 2.0. The Captain [notified ATC] and asked for direct. It seemed that Center was working against us instead of working with us. Finally, we received clearance to direct to the field. Now they wanted us to descend. We requested to stay high as long as we could. But ZZZ2 wanted us to descend down to 15000 feet. At this time fuel was becoming critical. On the PFD I saw ZZZ3 and told the Captain I wanted to divert there. He agreed. We told Center that we were diverting to that field. I think at this time Center realized we were in a serious situation. They began to help more expeditiously. We turned direct to the field. The Captain took control of the aircraft while I put in direct to the field and pulled the approach and worked the numbers. We landed without any problems. Dispatch had already called the FBO and they were waiting on us. We landed with 2.7 on the fuel and on engine shutdown with 2.3 on the fuel. I think we should have bugged out earlier, but, with the information we had we thought the field would be opening soon. I think we could have done a better job at querying Center but I think they could have a better job at giving us more information. When we left, I heard ZZZ1 tell another aircraft that there were 20 aircraft holding.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737-700 Flight Crew reported diverting due to emergency fuel.	
1644383	201905	1201-1800	ZLA.ARTCC	CA		100		29000		IMC			Daylight			Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class A ZLA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 379; Flight Crew Type 10000		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1644383	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 320; Flight Crew Type 2545		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1644385	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	Los Angeles Center gave us an early descent which would have placed us into a convective cell. I asked for left deviation but was told no due to military airspace. I asked for a right deviation multiple times with no response. I [requested priority handling] and using Captain's Authority, we turned the aircraft to the right just in time to avoid the cell. ATC finally responded by asking why [requested priority handling]. I told them to avoid injury from the cell and they told me I was a possible pilot deviation and gave me a phone number. I am becoming frustrated with the adversarial relationship pilots have with ATC.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737-800 flight crew reported advising ATC a need for a turn for weather, ATC did not allow. Captain claimed Captain's Authority and turned the aircraft to avoid weather.	
