1697411	201910	0601-1200					150			IMC	0.5		Daylight	200		Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS ZZZ	Landing	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								Windshield Wiper System		X	Design																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Type 10500		Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1697411												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft; Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Crew shot ILS CAT II to Runway XXL at ZZZ in heavy rain. At DA, First Officer failed to make 'minimums' call due to loss of situational awareness from heavy rain and reduced visibility. Captain knew aircraft was at DA and leveled off briefly. Runway was in sight, but landing area was obscured intermittently as windshield wipers operated. Captain picked up landing area and aircraft landed uneventfully. Combination of procedural failure and windscreen obscuration caused the aircraft to land slightly long.				B737 Captain reported that poor visibility and lack of altitude callouts on final approach were factors in a long landing.	
1697437	201911	0601-1200	ZLC.ARTCC	UT				35000		VMC						Center ZLC	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Direct	Class A ZLC																								Laptop Computer (performance, planning, etc.)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 103; Flight Crew Total 2195; Flight Crew Type 2195		Workload			1697437												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	While at cruise my iPad was connected to the aircraft internet and the iPad decided to auto update the content locker app which deleted all the manuals during flight. I had no notice of this from the iPad it just stated doing it. The Captain had his manuals on his EFB so no impact operationally however I would think there would be a safeguard for this. I wanted to report his so it does not affect other crews.				B737 First Officer reported iPad/EFB auto updated and caused all manuals and charts to be deleted without warning.	
1697442	201911	1801-2400	CLE.TRACON	OH				2500		IMC	Snow; Rain; Windshear					Tower CLE	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS ILS 24R	Final Approach		Class B CLE																								ILS/VOR		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 163; Flight Crew Total 24488; Flight Crew Type 17308		Confusion; Human-Machine Interface			1697442	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Confusion; Human-Machine Interface			1697977	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Other / Unknown; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Airport	Airport	We were cleared to fly ILS24R LLROY transition. When we called Tower they told us cleared to land with multiple departures. I believe the last departure was a heavy MD-11 departing our runway from the north side of runway. Runway 24L was closed at the time. I believe the MD-11 departing caused the full scale deflection of our glideslope. It started approaching SHERK intersection and joining the glideslope transitioning from VNAV and LNAV to localizer and glideslope. First the glideslope went up then started rapidly down followed by autopilot disconnect. The First Officer attempted to follow the down glideslope not yet recognizing the problem. I pointed out the glideslope would take us below the 2,000 ft. crossing altitude at GROZA and told her to level off and ignore glideslope. We flew level until GROZA and then the glideslope resumed working and matched up our VNAV info and we elected to continue the approach. It was gusty and raining and snowing at the time and we were between layers at time of incident. We were stabilized from FAF GROZA inbound and broke out at approximately 1,000 ft. on final. The reason I am filing this report is because of what I think is a flaw in the system that would allow a heavy jet to interfere with the safety of a CAT I approach that goes down to 200 ft. AGL. The weather was not less then 800 ft. and 2 miles that would require protection of the critical area but this is unsafe. I would appreciate somebody to look into this. I talked to Tower and they believe they did nothing wrong. It is unusual to have a plane go on the runway from the north side of Runway 24R as most aircraft come from the terminal side. I had this happen before in VFR conditions on the same runway which probably helped in my understanding of the events.		Cleared ILS 24R CLE. Passing SHERK, glideslope suddenly plunged to show us well above glideslope. Disconnected autopilot and began aggressive descent toward glideslope. Had to then level off to meet GROZA above 2,000 ft. Erroneous glideslope info identified, then reestablished by GROZA. Normal approach after that.		B737 Flight Crew reported that the glide slope indicator moved erratically and the autopilot disconnected during approach and resulted in a go-around.	
1697470	201910		LAX.Airport	CA			200			VMC						Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Final Approach; Landing	Visual Approach; STAR GOATZ1	Class B LAX																												Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B747 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC	Landing; Final Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 192; Flight Crew Type 514					1697470												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Ambiguous	On the GOATZ1 Star into LAX we were vectored off arrival prior to SLI. A 747 was also approaching SLI from south and vectored off just prior SLI. We were behind and kept 1,000 feet above the 747 by ATC. After one orbit we were sequenced behind the 747 for the visual 25L with just under 6 miles trail. With a strong tailwind directly lined up with runway, we discussed the threat of wake turbulence and agreed to fly the approach 1/2 to 1 dot high. We briefed that touching down at far end of touchdown zone (and discussed runway was 11,000 feet long) would be best method to maintain above wake with tail wind and eliminate that threat. Even though ATC did slow us down in an attempt to maintain our distance behind the 747 eventually, the 737 approach speed had us close in on the 747. At about 200 feet, we started to catch some wake turbulence and the First Officer slowed the descent rate and maintained above the turbulence, which put us higher on final. We were in the flare with the tailwind and floated with power at idle. We touched down just past the touchdown zone.				B737 NG Captain reported landing beyond the touchdown zone after an unstabilized approach caused by deliberately flying above glide slope to minimize wake turbulence from preceding B747.	
1697483	201911																Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS	Final Approach	Direct																									GPS & Other Satellite Navigation		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 206.33; Flight Crew Total 15694.87; Flight Crew Type 8626.42		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness; Confusion			1697483												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Aircraft; Airspace Structure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	Approximately 80 NM from the TLV FIR while in the Nicosia FIR, we lost our GPS. I believe it was because it was being jammed. We turned on 'Radio Updating' and advised ATC. Approaching the airport, we regained the use of the GPS system.  This is the second time my GPS has been jammed since the Ops Alert dealing with GPS jamming in the TLV FIR was rescinded.				B777 Captain reported possible GPS jamming.	
1697492	201910	1801-2400						5000		IMC	Rain					Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								Rudder Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 172; Flight Crew Type 686					1697492												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	During climb out, leveling at 5,000 ft, we received an AUTO FLT RUD TRV LIM SYS ECAM. I was hand flying and the Captain completed the ECAM. [Since] it was an auto flight ECAM, we elected to have me continue to hand fly for the time being while the Captain was completing the ECAM. We communicated the problem to ATC and after receiving an instruction to climb, we requested 10,000 ft. while troubleshooting. I selected autopilot 1 on once level and the ECAM complete. Once level with the ECAM complete screens normal we elected to continue to [destination]. Upon initiating the next climb and turn we received another of the same ECAM. We elected to return to [departure airport] due to the subsequent ECAM, even with the ECAM complete we didn't feel comfortable to continue with the forecast turbulence continual rain, bad weather, shorter runways. The Captain managed the ECAM and communications with company and the flight attendants. I flew the airplane and communicated with ATC. ATC [gave us priority handling] while I was giving them the information for the return to field. I requested the emergency vehicles to be ready and for the ILS Runway XXC as it was the longest runway. Once communications were complete and the Captain and I briefed on current position and readiness for the return for the approach, the Captain and I briefed the approach, I completed the descent checklist and the Captain completed the approach and landing in the event we had another system failure with the gusts and crosswinds. We landed without incident and taxied back to the gate.				A320 First Officer reported a malfunction with the rudder control system that resulted in a return to departure airport for a precautionary landing.	
1697583	201910	0001-0600	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						IMC	Thunderstorm; Windshear					Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1697583												Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance	Procedure	Procedure	We did a Go Around with a subsequent wind shear encounter due to the fact that you cannot deviate from the arrival and approach once you are below a certain altitude. With better ATC guidance, which we requested, we would have held or divert to the alternate airport. Having done the Go Around we got ATC clearance to fly to ZZZZ. We landed with low fuel indicated in both main tanks. We had 4K lbs on board.  We were a few hours late and did not have correct information to avoid the situation. It would be a good idea to carry more fuel than just what is required to satisfy max landing weight. The excess fuel can be burnt off in the holding pattern before landing.				B737 Captain reported that a go-around and diversion resulted in landing with low fuel.	
1697639	201910									VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach																															Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing			Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 210; Flight Crew Total 11500; Flight Crew Type 3137					1697639												Conflict NMAC			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	On approach to 16L [Company X] on 16R appeared to overshoot the LOC (Localizer) twice the second time causing the Resolution Advisory. We responded immediately and re-acquired the [Company X] jet (it appeared to very nearly end up underneath us). The auto pilot was off and we were in VMC. After we were clear of the conflict, I descended and reacquired the glide slope and flew a stabilized approach to the runway.				A320 First Officer reported an NMAC with another aircraft on approach to land.	
1697664	201911	1201-1800	ZHU.ARTCC	TX				11500								Center ZHU	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	SID PITZZ4	Class E ZHU																												Center ZHU	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger				Class E ZHU																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 152; Flight Crew Total 1503; Flight Crew Type 1229		Confusion; Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1697664												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure	Procedure	We were climbing to 16,000 feet when we received an urgent radio call from Houston departure: '[Company] Traffic Alert, turn right to heading 330 immediately, maintain 11,000 feet.' During that radio call we also got an aural 'Traffic' alert from the TCAAS, with a target showing close off our left side 900 feet below us and climbing. I reached up, selected Heading mode and set 330 while the FO (First Officer) set 11,000 feet in the altitude window. I then disconnected the autopilot and increased the rate of turn. However, we were already at 11,600 feet and climbing when told to maintain 11,000 feet; with the traffic coming up from below, there was no way I was going to descend, so I maintained the climb while in the turn. We could hear other radio calls while maneuvering, but none with our call sign. As we passed 13,000 feet. Approach Control had us turn back left to a 310 heading, then a 260 heading. The FO asked Approach what altitude they wanted us at, and they responded '16,000 feet.' We set the altitude in the FCU, turned the autopilot back on, the flight continued normally. Our jumpseater, who was listening on his own earpiece, later stated that he heard approach telling someone to 'Expedite climb to 16,000 feet,' but didn't hear our call sign used. All of us agreed that either the call sign was blocked, or the Controller omitted it in the rush to avoid a mid-air collision. We never received a TCAS RA, just the 'Traffic' call; the Controller's immediate turn must have moved us out of the way before it deteriorated into an RA event. Closest altitude the FO saw was 600 feet below us, but not sure what the closest lateral separation was.				A320 Captain reported complying with aircraft indication instead of ATC instruction to avoid traffic.	
1697944	201911	1201-1800	SFO.Airport	CA				1200		VMC			Daylight			Tower SFO	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing		Class B SFO																												Tower SFO	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Taxi																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 94; Flight Crew Total 11034; Flight Crew Type 4844		Communication Breakdown	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1697944												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Ground Incursion Runway			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure	Procedure	Flying FMS Bridge Visual Runway 28R. At approximately 1,500 to 1,200 feet AGL, SFO Tower cleared Aircraft Y to cross Runway 28R. At either Taxiway U or R. Couldn't tell which one exactly. I looked over to the First Officer and asked him did he just clear that guy to cross the runway? Immediately SFO Tower told Aircraft Y to hold position. No answer. Tower then said Aircraft Y stop. No answer. I told First Officer to prepare for a go around. After a 3rd call from Tower, Aircraft Y was now in the middle of the runway. Tower then directed the go around. The FMS bridge visual has a specific go around procedure listed. Runway heading to 3,000 ft. That's not what the go around instructions given from Tower which was an added distraction. Next approach was uneventful.				B737 Captain reported that a communication breakdown between ATC and another aircraft resulted in a runway incursion and a go-around.	
1698143	201910	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000		IMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1698143												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We departed from ZZZ as scheduled. An hour into the flight, we were cruising at FL380. We received a call from the Number 1 Flight Attendant letting us know that there was an odor in the back of the aircraft that smelled like burning wire. He said he could smell it from about 3 rows from the aft galley. No smoke was seen. The Captain asked about the 2 flight attendants in the back. The Number 1 stated they felt a little nausea and headache, but they were doing okay. At that point, the Captain elected to [advise ATC] and land at ZZZ1. We were about 40nm from ZZZ1. I continued flying while the Captain coordinated with ATC, the Company, and the flight attendants. Captain also made a PA to the passengers. We were given radar vectors to land on RWY XXC. We landed uneventful on [Runway] XXC and stopped on the runway. We talked with ARFF and had them check the aircraft for smoke and anything out of the ordinary. ARFF said they saw nothing and we continued to the gate with ARFF following us. Once parked at the gate, the passengers deplaned. The two flight attendants in the back deplaned and were met by the EMTs and checked out. Maintenance boarded and checked the aircraft for possible sources of the smell. In the end, they said it was a power port that was overheating.  Throughout the event, there was no change reported in the strength of the odor.				B737-800 First Officer reported Flight Attendant notification of 'burning wire' fumes in the passenger cabin. Captain executed an uneventful diversion after flight attendants in aft galley reported physiological symptoms.	
1698173	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Flap/Slat Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting			1698173												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On approach I was Pilot Flying landing Runway XXR in ZZZ. We had been cleared for the visual and I had the autopilot on while flying the ILS. While configuring for landing I called for flaps 25, but noticed that the flaps only indicated 15. I communicated this to the Captain and since we were approaching 1,000 ft. AGL and were not fully configured, we initiated a go around. After establishing a positive rate we got the gear up but we elected to stay below 200 kts. as the flaps were stuck at 15. We leveled off and the Captain ran the flaps disagree checklist in the QRH. We [advised ATC] and made a precautionary landing on Runway XXC with flaps 15.				B737-800 First Officer reported flap failure and disagree that resulted in a go-around and precautionary landing.	
1698322	201911	1801-2400															Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1698322	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1698323	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Figure out what is causing this.		Crew oxygen use immediately is the most important action, the QRH leads you towards systematically finding the source of the fumes/odor but the information from our lead FA was very helpful.		A320 flight crew reported fume event enroute. QRH procedure completed.	
1698522	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				33000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Powerplant Fuel Control Unit		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 136; Flight Crew Total 16918; Flight Crew Type 7016					1698522	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 164; Flight Crew Type 338					1698532	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	We departed on time. Normal taxi-out and departure from runway. Climbed to FL330 in smooth air and first 45 minutes of flight unremarkable. FO (First Officer) was PF (Pilot Flying) and Captain PM (Pilot Monitoring). Engine 1 experienced a stall accompanied by partial EPR rollback on ENG 1 and an ECAM 'ENG 1 STALL.' FO retained control of flying and took radios and Captain began ECAM and QRH. Engine recovered itself with no action required and remained stable with no further indications of a stall.Crew resumed normal PF/PM duties and we reviewed the ENG 1 STALL QRH to ensure all considerations taken. We discussed monitoring engine and reviewed the driftdown QRC items to be prepared should another event occur. FO reviewed our single engine performance capability to confirm capability at present weight. Captain shared event information with [Company] Maintenance via ACARS and notified Dispatch. All parties believed it was a momentary event and engine was operating normally.A second, deeper Engine 1 Stall occurred about 35 minutes later and again FO remained the PF and took communications so Captain could run the ECAM and QRH. Per the procedure when thrust was slowly brought back up on Engine 1 further vibrations and stalls began and thrust lever was returned to idle where engine idled with normal indications. We coordinated a driftdown with ATC while we completed our analysis of the engine status and advised ATC working a degraded engine situation. Dispatch was also advised, and closest airports were reviewed with consideration to single engine performance ability as well as overweight landing performance for a weight of 150,000 pounds.Captain decided safest plan was to land in ZZZ, [Requested Priority Handling] with ATC and advised Flight Attendants of plan. Dispatch was advised of diversion to ZZZ with degraded engine. A brief passenger announcement was also made. Flight attendants advised Captain passenger was not feeling well and oxygen was being administered. A medically trained passenger was assisting crew with the ill passenger. A pass-rider Flight Attendant also stepped up to assist cabin crew for balance of flight.We prepared and briefed a descent to ZZZ and requested Runway XXL. Performance data received from Dispatch and Captain elected to fly the descent and approach. FO provided excellent PM and callouts. An uneventful landing on ZZZ XXL completed with [airport rescue and firefighting] equipment positioned on the adjacent taxiway. Aircraft was able to safely taxi to gate with a fire truck escort.Maintenance release codes were sent for ENG 1 STALL ECAMs and a used oxygen bottle. A phone call to [Technical Support Maintenance Control] was made by Captain as well to log the overweight landing and describe events. Captain and FO debriefed in ZZZ to review CRM elements and outcome. Crew deadheaded back to ZZZ1 later in the afternoon.		We had an inflight engine stall that resulted in a reduction of power on the number 1 engine. The flight started with no unusual indications departing; it was my leg to fly. Climbout to FL330 was normal, smooth air and no icing conditions. About 45 mins into the flight, we had our first engine issue. In cruise flight the Captain and I heard a loud sound and the number 1 engine EPR starting to reduce; a few seconds later the ECAM 'engine 1 stall' appeared briefly. The number 1 engine had recovered, returning to normal operating conditions. At this point the Captain ran the associated QRH to completion with the number 1 engine continuing to operate normally. We discussed the situation and decided to continue; using the time wisely we reviewed where we were on our flight, roughly south of ZZZ2. We also reviewed the drift down procedure and discussed all of our options should the number 1 engine fail on us. We reviewed the single engine performance at our current weight and the grid mora altitudes along our route of flight; FL180 was our single engine performance and 16,800 feet was the highest grid mora in the area west of ZZZ. I maintained flying the aircraft and took over ATC communications so the Captain could message Maintenance and Dispatch to get their input in our developing situation. On advice from Maintenance we submitted a write-up for engine 1 stall in level flight no shutdown. They advised to continue and they would inspect in ZZZ1. We both felt there was a possible threat with further engine problems and did everything we could to formulate a plan and mitigate all errors associated with the elevated stress of an in-flight shutdown.About 40 minutes after the first engine 1 stall, we had a second occurrence of the same nature on engine 1. First were loud bangs, shaking of the airplane, and EPR reduction with the associated ECAM. We identified the problem, the Captain announced I had the aircraft and radios; he was going to run the ECAM and QRH. After completing ECAM and QRH the engine would not go above idle thrust without problems. We both discussed the situation and decided leaving the engine at idle thrust was our best option. The engine indications were normal for idle thrust. At this point, knowing our performance was degraded on engine 1, we asked ATC for a lower altitude and advised we were working on an engine issue involving degraded performance. We completed the drift down checklist and requested FL200 while continuing on our flight plan. Below us was ZZZ3 and 180 miles ahead of us was ZZZ. As we continued to trouble shoot the engine 1 problem, message Dispatch, and analyzing our current performance we decided to continue straight ahead in a slow decent and divert to ZZZ. At this point east of ZZZ3, about 140 miles from ZZZ we [Requested Priority Handling] with ATC with our intention to divert to ZZZ. I maintained control of the aircraft and ATC communication while the Captain messaged Dispatch, the Flight Attendant, and updated the passengers on our situation and intentions. Our performance with the degraded engine 1 allowed us to level off at FL250 to setup for a normal decent into ZZZ. We prepared for landing in ZZZ XXL, the longest runway. We checked the performance numbers, added the appropriate speeds for a flaps full landing, Dispatch ran the length calculations of 7,500 feet needed (Runway XXL 16,000 feet long), checked a single engine go-around performance, ran the overweight checklist and briefed everything. Once we had our plan ready the Captain took the controls and completed a safe uneventful landing in ZZZ. We taxied to the gate normally and deplaned normally. Being new to [Company] adds stress to an event like this but the training I received including CRM skills was invaluable for us to complete this flight safely.		A320 flight crew reported a divert due to compressor stalls on left then right engine.	
1698655	201910	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				10000			Turbulence						Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Workload			1698655												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted	Airport; Weather	Weather	I was the PF (Pilot Flying) on flight (number) from ZZZ1-ZZZ2. On the flight inbound from ZZZ we had continuous moderate turbulence from 10,000 feet to landing. Winds at 6,000 feet were still blowing over 70 kts, so we expected more turbulence on departure. With the departure off Runway XX, the path takes you nearer the mountains the strong winds were blowing over. Sure enough we quickly encountered continuous moderate turbulence again. In the right hand turn the aircraft became fairly difficult to control, even requiring full forward and left control input to maintain acceptable pitch and roll attitudes. My FO (First Officer) and I believe that between 5,000-6,000 feet we experienced a severe 'bump' or 'jolt.' As we got above 10,000 feet the ride improved and we had a little time to discuss how we wanted to report the conditions to ATC. The FAs (Flight Attendants) reported one passenger had a handbag on his lap go up and over to the row behind him and all the coat hangers in the first class closet ended up on the floor. Although we never 'lost control' of the aircraft, the difficulty controlling the aircraft combined with the intense 'bump', we felt justified reporting it as 'severe.' In fact we felt good if our report caused any other aircraft to avoid those conditions.Then we had to shift our focus to the arrival in ZZZ2. Initially the weather I believe was about 3-5 miles visibility in rain and winds gusting out of the southeast into the 30s. On final we received updated weather of sustained winds over 30 kts gusting to 43 kts at the airport. We briefed the likelihood of turbulence and low level wind shear. The ride in fact deteriorated rapidly below 2,000 feet. I also briefed my FO he could call for a go-around at any time as well. I had planned on flaps 30, but we quickly discussed then switched to flaps 15 as I saw the large fluctuations in airspeed. I kept the autopilot engaged with my hands firmly on the controls. I believe at least twice the auto throttles went to max thrust just to maintain approach speed. Our best estimate is that between 1,00-1,000 feet, we heard 'wind shear' 'go-around,' and executed the wind shear escape maneuver then flew the missed approach out toward ZZZ3. As I got the aircraft safely established on the missed, I transferred the flying duties to the FO. We didn't have the fuel to go to ZZZ4 and we weren't going back to ZZZ1. ZZZ weather wasn't great but was our best option so I made the decision (quickly with dispatch) to divert there before we entered the hold. Fortunately I had a very capable FO and I chose to have him complete the flight as the flying pilot as I continued to manage the overall progression of the flight. We broke out inside ZZZZZ for Runway XY, and we felt very good to be on the ground and at the gate. It was a long and hectic day where on one flight we experienced severe turbulence, wind shear on final approach and a diversion. I am adding the [Report] portion to this report because the FOM states that severe turbulence requires a debrief with the [Chief Pilot]. With so many things happening on that flight, along with figuring out the plan for the rest of the day, considering the weather conditions, our duty time, fatigue, etc., I never had a debrief with the [Chief Pilot]. I completely missed seeing that requirement in the FOM.This reinforced how serious a business it is flying southeast ZZZ1. I may become more conservative with my fuel requirements for diverts when all of southeast has pretty poor conditions. ZZZ weather can get really bad really quickly. Although it was blowing over 30 kts. with rain we got in fairly easy. It could have easily been very different. Whether due to visibility, ceiling, or turbulence/wind shear, a missed approach at ZZZ was a real possibility. We discussed that scenario prior to descending into ZZZ. We knew we could go missed approach once, then we would have to land the second time no matter what. That is never a situation you wantto be in.As far as the severe turbulence out of ZZZ1, I'm not sure if our situation was avoidable or if possibly a sustained wind (like over 70 kts at 5,000 feet) from a certain direction (out of southeast) should preclude operations off of Runway XX?				B737-700 Captain reported severe turbulence and wind shear conditions necessitating a diversion to an airport with more favorable weather conditions.	
1698949	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1698949												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	We received an ECAM Action Message stating we had a CAB PR SYS 1+2 FAULT. We referred to our QRC then executed the ECAM ACTION procedure. During that procedure execution, we noted our cabin altitude rate was climbing rapidly and we immediately requested priority handling for an immediate descent from FL350. As we descended, we were able to control the cabin altitude and maintained FL290 as cleared by ATC. We contacted Dispatch who provided a patch to Maintenance. It was determined our best course of action was to divert to ZZZ. We told Dispatch and Maintenance we would continue to ZZZ1 and would land in ZZZ if the cabin became uncontrollable. Dispatch and Maintenance concurred with this plan. Shortly afterwards, the cabin altitude control became marginally controllable and [priority handling was requested] and we received a descent to FL190. We immediately diverted to ZZZ where we landed uneventfully. During this entire event, the cabin altitude did not exceed 9,000 feet and the oxygen masks did not deploy in the cabin. We also did not land overweight. When we diverted to ZZZ, we did not notify Dispatch as there was not adequate time to do so and as Dispatch had already agreed to the diversion if the cabin became uncontrollable, we felt such a contact was not warranted.This event occurred due to a mechanical issue with our aircraft.				A320 flight crew reported a pressurization problem that resulted in a diversion.	
1698992	201911	0601-1200	SEA.Airport	WA						IMC	Fog		Daylight			Tower SEA	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach; Landing		Class B SEA																								Autoland		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1698992												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	A3 autoland failure. First Officer's leg. Weather was below Cat I minimums so we switched to an HGS HUD hybrid autoland, Captain flown.Engaged second auto pilot on a 10 mile final, had to re-engage both auto pilots and reset both master flight directors twice on the arrival. Prior to entering IMC, as briefed we switched to a heads up display arrival with increased minimums when the autoland autopilot system failed again. Shortly after that I received an approach warning in the heads up display, and with the approach lights [and] runway in sight, we completed the landing.The landing was on center line, but very firm. The taxi and shut down after that the normal. We wrote up the A3 automated system, and put it in the book. We debriefed the landing for the passengers and the crew.Maintenance is going to investigate the HUD, A3, and autopilot.				B737-NG Captain reported that an autopilot failure during approach resulted in a firm landing.	
1699297	201911	1801-2400	DFW.Airport	TX						VMC						Tower DFW	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B DFW																												Tower DFW	Air Carrier	EMB ERJ 170/175 ER/LR		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B DFW																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1699297												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	DFW in the past 12 months has been clearing aircraft for takeoff before the preceding aircraft has even rotated down the runway. When they do this, I'd say we hit wake turbulence about 50% of the time, and putting in huge wing corrections low to the ground has always been frustrating. Well it happened again, and I'm going to start submitting reports every time now. We were cleared for takeoff a few seconds before the preceding E-175 had even rotated, and gave it about 5 seconds before we started the takeoff roll. At about 500 feet, we hit wake turbulence, which required near full-scale deflection to maintain aircraft control in the climb. We were still configured with flaps down but gear up. Wind was under 5 knots.Cause, ATC trying to get as many aircraft out of the airport in the shortest amount of legal time.Suggestions, bring back distance spacing, not just timing for aircraft separation.				B737-800 flight crew member reported encountering wake turbulence shortly after takeoff at DFW, and stated insufficient spacing was a contributing factor.	
1699349	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1699349	Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Air Carrier	Flight Attendant (On Duty)	Flight Attendant Current			Situational Awareness			1700299	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury		Y	Person Flight Attendant; Person Passenger	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	While in cruise, the FA (Flight Attendant)-A called and said that a passenger in first class thought they smelled smoke of some kind, but it was very faint. The flight attendant and other passengers could not smell the fumes at that time. Within about twenty minutes of the first report, a second passenger in first class said they thought they smelled something like what a gas leak in a house would smell like, but again was fairly faint. FA-A had another FA come forward to verify the odor because she could still not detect anything. The other FA said she could not smell anything, either. The first passenger then reported feeling nauseous, having hot flashes, and vomited. Several minutes later, a passenger in row 8 said they smelled a 'dirty socks' smell or something that smelled like sewage. At this time FA-A had the other FA come from the back of the aircraft to verify the smell. This time the aft FA could smell a 'dirty socks' smell. When the Captain and I heard 'dirty socks', we immediately thought about a fume event and the Captain ran the QRH for Fumes in the cabin. About this time the FA-A reported that her eyes and nose felt like they were getting irritated. After the QRH, procedure was complete and we had turned PACK 1 off, the fumes subsided for the remainder of the flight. The Captain followed the guidance in the FOM and filled out the appropriate reports. The crew debriefed the event after the flight and a Report was submitted. There was some engine work done on the number one engine before we left, apparently replacing a high pressure valve for turbine cooling and additionally adding engine oil to both engines. This may be coincidental but I don't know what other parts the mechanics had to remove to perform this procedure. This was particularly insidious as the smell was quite faint for a long time. Only one passenger smelled it and it seemed to be in conjunction with the ovens at first. The description of the smell was also hard to decipher and only one passenger thought they smelled it for quite a while with no corroboration.		After service passenger in XC informed me she had been smelling an intermittent smoke/gasoline scent throughout the flight. I could not smell anything so I asked the FAs in the back to come and take a look. Neither of them could smell it. I looked for possible issues in the overhead bins and felt the walls but felt nothing strange. I let the passenger know it could've possibly been the oven since it has a strange smell but the pilots did know about the issue and to let me know if she smelled it again. About ten minutes later she rings her call light and smells it- I still did not. Passenger in YA started to get a pain in her stomach and got flashes- she started to vomit. I continued to let the pilots know but there was no guarantee that her illness was because of the smell. At this time we had to break the pilots so the C FA was guarding. After the f/d was closed the C claimed she got a little bit of the smell but she described it as the smell of a gas stove being left on. Not long after, XC claimed she was feeling a little nausea and XA agreed that he had smelled it. I still smelled nothing. C FA started to get a headache and a non rev was using the FC lav so I asked her if she smelled anything during the flight. She claimed she had actually smelled fuel for a moment but it went away during service so she thought nothing of it. XC rings her call light again and claims the smell is very strong so I asked the person in row 6 if he had smelled anything weird. He replied yes actually it smelt like a sewer or a men's locker room. This smell I actually did notice (assuming it was human) and my nose was burning so I let the pilots know. At this time the pilots agreed their nose burned too so they followed their checklist. After they turned off something no one else complained of any smells, YA was not sick anymore and eventually my nose stopped burning. C FA still had her headache. During clean up a passenger in row 7 told me that by the way during the flight she smelt burning. We needed to start precautions when the first complaint was brought to our attention. We should have asked other passengers earlier if they had smelled anything because we could've spent less time in contaminated air.		A320 First Officer reported Flight Attendant and passengers experienced physiological symptoms due to fumes in the cabin.	
1699518	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US		50		20000					Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Fuel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 140; Flight Crew Type 2850					1699518	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1699944	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing out of ZZZ, both fuel pump low pressure lights illuminated on the Number 2 tank. The FWD low pressure light would go on and off. The AFT low pressure light illuminated constantly. We stopped the climb at FL330 to troubleshoot. While troubleshooting the issue, the Number 2 engine rolled back quite a bit, and then returned to normal operation.The Captain sent an ACARS message to Dispatch to advise them. We all agreed that the QRH said we could continue. The Captain decided to talk to Dispatch over AIRINC. While on the radio with Dispatch, the number 2 engine had another minor loss of power and surge. We were pretty much right over ZZZ1, and we all decided to divert to ZZZ1 in the interest of safety. We changed our destination with ATC to ZZZ1. We did not request priority handling and landed in ZZZ1 without further incident.		On climb out from ZZZ, passing 15,000 feet, the Number 2 Aft Fuel Pump low pressure light illuminated. We performed the appropriate QRH procedure, turning off the Number 2 Aft Fuel Pump and continued to climb. Passing FL300, the Number 2 FWD Fuel Pump low pressure light illuminated. We leveled at FL330 knowing at high altitude we could lose power. Per the QRH, we were still okay to continue the flight to ZZZ1. When testing the fuel pumps power draw per Maintenance Control, the number two engine momentarily lost power when the FWD Fuel Pump was selected off. I immediately turned it back on noting when the switch was turned off the AMPS dropped from 47 to 42 AMPS.While talking to Dispatch and Maintenance Control over ARINC, the FWD low pressure light went out then cycled on and off. While the low pressure light was cycling, the Number 2 engine began to surge between 82-89% N1. At that time we elected to divert to ZZZ2 to have the aircraft inspected and repaired. We did not [request priority handling] at that time. We were met by Contract Maintenance and I initiated a call to Dispatch and Maintenance Control. The Operations Center Chief joined the call and I answered several questions for him. We were given another aircraft and we continued to ZZZ1 without incident.		B737 flight crew reported low fuel pump pressure indication for the Number 2 engine and surges in power output that resulted in a diversion.	
1699531	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				4000		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Cowling/Nacelle Fasteners, Latches		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 363; Flight Crew Type 10500					1699531	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1699986	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Bird / Animal			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We took off out of ZZZ1 and at approximately 4,000 feet and I saw what appeared to be a bird fly past the FO's (First Officer's) windscreen. It was immediately followed by a loud impact noise. The smell of an animal through an engine entered the aircraft. Additionally a change in air noise happened. Almost like a panel was open. A check of the engines indicated that they were both operating normally, and I also checked the pressurization which was also normal. The FO and I quickly agreed that we should level off, which ATC granted. We told them we had hit a bird and would probably be returning to ZZZ1 or maybe ZZZ. At that time the FA (Flight Attendants) called about the smell and that we had hit something on the number two engine. They indicated that there was a hole or dent in the thing that goes around the engine. I asked if everyone was alright and they said 'yes'. I told them I would get back to them with more information. When I returned, the FO indicated that ATC had requested priority handling for us. We then agreed to stay in the area while we got a plan in order. We believed (based on our aircraft being approximately 8000 lbs over max landing weight, and the possibility that there could be some issues with the leading edge devices), we decided ZZZ was where we wanted to go to. I called ZZZ1 Operations and had them have Dispatch call us on frequency. Once on with us, we told him our plan and he agreed and said he would get things coordinated at ZZZ. With the FO flying, I ran the Diversion Checklist, and also got landing information for ZZZ. ATC planned for us to land on Runway XXR. We briefed the approach and talked about it again to make sure we had done everything, and were not missing anything. I also read the overweight landing information from the AOM out loud. When all checklists and notifications were complete (and on the approach) we transferred controls so that I could land the aircraft. After landing, we cleared the runway, shut down the number 2 engine, and had airport rescue inspect the aircraft for any smoke or liquids coming from the aircraft. They said all was normal. We the completed the performance [calculation for] brake cooling and found that no special restrictions were needed and we could cool at the gate. We taxied to the gate and deplaned all the Passengers into the terminal.		On climbout after takeoff from Runway XXC at ZZZ1, we had a bird strike the Number 2 engine. Realizing there was aircraft damage, we ran all appropriate checklists, and diverted to ZZZ, due to the extra runway length and our overweight condition. Upon landing at ZZZ we were inspected by the Fire Department and ran brake cooling data. Both were normal. We then taxied to the gate and shut down the aircraft.		B737-700 flight crew reported a diversion and landing after a bird strike to the right engine.	
1699548	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				33000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Flap Control (Trailing & Leading Edge)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 101; Flight Crew Total 24950; Flight Crew Type 15075					1699548	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1699570	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	Our flight was dispatched with a flaperon status message displayed. Maintenance disconnected 1 of 2 sensors and sent us on our way. About 35 minutes into the flight we felt a sharp jolt then an aircraft vibration which was significant. We checked all systems and found we had an Amber X on the right flaperon along with a 20 degree left yoke displacement.We had a complete system failure at that point. After the Captain conferred with Maintenance, dispatch and the crew it was mutually agreed upon not to continue the flight and return to ZZZ. None of the personnel contacted could give us information on how our fuel burn would be over a XA:06 min flight. Flying over [international] airspace with all these unknowns and the possibility of landing short was not a good decision, we returned to ZZZ where proper maintenance could be performed.		During briefing it was noted an inbound write-up 'FLAPERON ACTUATOR STATUS MESSAGE DISPLAYED.' Maintenance performed a procedure to disconnect a sensor prior to departure and sent a valid maintenance release. According to the Maintenance Personnel, this was the second incident with the issue during the airplane's last two legs.35 minutes after departure we felt a 'bump' and then experienced a slight airframe vibration. After about 10 minutes, the yoke displaced 20 degree to the left. We contacted dispatch/[Maintenance Control] and discussed the situation. We ended up with [Maintenance Control], Dispatch, Chief Pilot, and Fleet Technical Manager on the line.While the Fleet Technical Manager did a satisfactory job explaining the situation, the question that could not be answered was what was the additional fuel burn we would experience in the next 13 hours with the additional drag produced by the drooping flaperon. The Chief Pilot and fleet technical solution was to continue and 'just see' how much fuel we burn, and if necessary land short of destination. This, along with a 'sick' airplane, flying over the most hostile environment we operate in, was not a good decision, and as a crew the decision was made to return to ZZZ.We dumped 90K of fuel per the checklist.		B777 flight crew reported the right flaperon malfunction during cruise resulted in a return to the departure airport.	
1699551	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			200			VMC			Dusk			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								System Monitor: Indicating and Warning		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 127; Flight Crew Total 7139; Flight Crew Type 5747					1699551												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Right after takeoff we had multiple system failures, to include the A/T (Autothrottles), VNAV, the Captain's F/D (Flight Director), and the autopilot. We continued the departure and assessed the situation while I hand flew the airplane. I reselected the A/T and they stayed on until I noticed they were not at climb power, but had pulled back to approximately 80 percent. I turned off the auto throttles and pushed up the power, then gave the airplane to the FO (First Officer), whose navigation systems seemed to be working. I advised ATC we needed to return to ZZZ because of the problem. ATC eventually [provided priority handling] for us. While the FO was flying on autopilot I noticed the left engine only had been retarded to 70 percent by the A/T. We turned them back on seeing that everything on his side was working. At this point I took the airplane back and hand flew the plane with F/D, A/T and VNAV off. We were 20,000 lbs over weight so I flew a flaps 40 landing to lower the approach speed. I made a smooth landing and we required no further assistance. We made the appropriate log book entries.				B737 NG Captain reported an in-flight turnback after multiple system failures.	
1699677	201911	0601-1200	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Tires		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Troubleshooting			1699677												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	At approximately 7 hours into our flight and immediately prior to my break as First Officer (Pilot Monitoring), we got an intermittent amber annunciation for 'Tire pressure,' on for 5-10 seconds and then off again. Upon immediate check of the gear synoptic, number 6 tire pressure was fluctuating between 173 and 172 while all other mains were between 215-235. Just then, the Captain returned from his break. My changeover brief included this issue. While I was showing him our indications on the synoptic, we saw the number 5 tire at 215 intermittently go amber also. At this point, I left on break. I returned from break about 50 minutes prior to landing and was thoroughly briefed by the Captain (Pilot Flying) regarding the situation and the plan. As we approached the field, the Captain told me he would like to have the ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Firefighting) equipment standing by as a precaution. To ensure ARFF would be available, I immediately [advised ATC]. Approach and landing to XXR was uneventful. We stopped on the runway. Maintenance came out, told us to shut down the engines so they could inspect our tires. They said there was no visible damage to the tires. A few minutes later, a tug came onto the runway and we were towed to the gate. The item was written up in the AML (Aircraft Maintenance Log).				B737 flight crew reported low, fluctuating tire pressure on two tires, but were able to land without incident.	
1699683	201911	1201-1800	PHX.Airport	AZ				3900		VMC			Dusk			TRACON P50	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	STAR HYDRR1	Class B PHX																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 4500		Distraction; Human-Machine Interface			1699683												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	Human Factors	Human Factors	I was the Pilot Flying on the HYDRR1 arrival to 25L approach. Assigned a heading of 290 to intercept the final approach course at an altitude of 4,000 feet and cleared for the visual approach. Speed window open with 180 kts set, flaps 5, auto throttles engaged. From a 290 heading and my situational awareness, I saw that we were going to intercept glide slope well before the localizer in fact beyond SCADE. I started a LVL CHANGE descent to 3,000 feet. I retarded the throttles to idle to begin the descent. Pilot Monitoring (Captain) asked that I go altitude hold immediately to honor the Class B airspace below us. I complied and the altitude window settled at around 3,900 feet. I started to set up to reenter VNAV path when I noticed the airspeed bleeding through 160 kts at the same time the Captain called airspeed. I immediately advanced the throttles to almost the stops and disengaged the autopilot. Airspeed I believe bottomed out around 155 kts and then quickly recovered to 175 kts with no altitude loss. From there it was a routine if tense approach and landing.				B737 NG First Officer reported getting low and slow on approach to PHX due to automation dependency.	
1699795	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC		Glare	Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1699795	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1700978	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Airport; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	On arrival ZZZ were cleared for a visual Runway XX, FO's (First Officer's) leg. Initially at 4,000 ft. on a high wide right downwind he set and descended to 3,000 ft. for traffic pattern altitude. We did not receive a response to our in-range until moments before so with things well in hand I now selected the taxi page on my Jeps (Jeppesen) to briefly review the anticipated taxi as there were several NOTAMed closures. Then thinking of the numerous towers in the area I decided I was premature and reselected the ILS XX plate which I fumbled some with. In the mean time I did not see my FO select 2,000 feet (which was only 500 feet AGL) and begin a premature descent. I noticed as we were 1,000 feet, RA (Radar Altimeter) [a few] miles from the turn to final and told him to climb back to 2,500 feet.In retrospect, I should have immediate taken control of the aircraft, but he initially was responding although not as quickly as I would have liked. I thought he had inadvertently selected the altitude and that a correction at a position on a wide base and well before the turn to final was appropriate but did not know that he had lost sight of the airport and become disoriented. I did not call go-around as we were in a position that put us well outside of stabilized approach criteria and did not feel it was required under the circumstances and I felt a climb to 2,500 feet for an intercept of final at about 3 [and] 1/2 miles was more appropriate. About that time we got a 'too low gear' I again told him to climb which he did although slowly. I selected gear down to silence the warning in an attempt to unload my FO and get him to again listen to me, as my FO had by this point 'checked out' (his words from our debrief of the situation). He was now intermittently holding the side stick priority button and I did not want to initiate a struggle for control of the aircraft and essentially a single pilot go around so using captain's emergency authority I guided him through the approach and landing.I do want to state that on the previous trip this month and on the other occasions I have flown with this First Officer he has always displayed fine airmanship and professionalism and this situation is completely out of character for him.In retrospect my reluctance to call for a go-around or take control at a point where I felt it was still possible aggravated the situation. I made assumptions about why the FO had done what he had done, i.e., an error, which turned out to not be correct. He had become disoriented, overwhelmed. I would have not considered before this incident telling someone to 'go around' while on downwind.  In our position miles from joining final on a wide base I felt the same. In our debriefing I stated that one of my mistakes in the situation was to not have taken control early on. He indicated that he felt a 'go around' call may have brought him back into the situation. I actually tend to agree. I would not have hesitated in IMC to call 'go around 'if we were similarly out of sorts, why the hesitation in VMC.I want to focus on my misgiving and failings in this report, but do believe it is worth note that in our debrief of this incident we did discuss the use of the ND (Navigation Display) in helping to improve situational awareness. The ILS was briefed as a backup to the visual with the glide path intercept altitude being of note. Communicating his lack of situational awareness and disorientation. And following SOP regarding changes to altitude settings. These things in addition to those already mentioned that I feel I specifically could/should have done better.		Starting with the transition from the en-route phase with Center to the approach phase with Approach Control. An estimated 20 nautical miles southwest of the airport with continued ATC descent and course changes. An attempt is made to visually acquire the airport. The Captain states the location and direction of the airport. Crosschecking the navigational display, I state 'I do not have the airport'. We are handed off to Tower at 4,000 feet on a right downwind to Runway XX slowing to 210 kts and call for flaps 1. It is the first time I visually acquire the airport.Tower Controller: 'Do you have the airport?'. I respond 'yes', the Captain states he has the airport and informs the Tower. We are cleared for a visual approach to Runway XX abeam the numbers. I descend to 3,000 feet and slow to 190 kts and call for flaps 2. Using the ILS to Runway XX as a back up reference. My intention is to turn base about 1 mile inside of ZZZZZ (FAF) and descend to 2,500 feet, complete the landing configuration and turn a 4 mile final stabilized at 2,500 feet. I begin the right turn and start the FCU selection from 3,000 feet to 2,000 feet while cross checking my visual reference. I have again lost the airport, owing to the sunlight reflection angle combined with the flatness of terrain. While focusing outside I stopped my FCU selection and did not complete the setting to 2,500 feet. Turning my attention back inside the cockpit to crosscheck my reference. I am alerted to both warning sounds and lights of 'too low gear'. While attempting to ascertain what was happening I observe the Captain's hand selecting gear down, instructing me to climb and configure the aircraft for landing without my input. Disconnected the autopilot and using the course line on the navigational display and started a turn toward final. With a landing clearance, an uneventful landing and flight completion.First consideration: 20 miles from an unfamiliar airport, difficulty locating I should have requested vectors to final. Second consideration: loss of visual on base should have gone around. Third consideration: should have overridden the Captain on landing and gone around rather than continue to land. Fourth consideration: Task focused, not speaking in declarative CRM statements. Fifth consideration: 'Pride' allowing my years and hours of experience rather than the humility to admit needing some assistance. Personal observation: We as a crew tore this incident apart in 4 day debriefing for the remainder of our trip. Conclusion, I have learned and discovered and will go forward in a different manner.		A320 flight crew reported First Officer lost sight of the airport and descended prematurely while on visual approach.	
1699802	201911		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000		VMC	Turbulence					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1699802												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Human Factors; Company Policy; Procedure; Weather	Weather	Flight encountered moderate turbulence at FL380 near RADOK intersection. I was on my rest break in the flight crew rest bunks. The FO was the PIC (Pilot in Command) and the PF (Pilot Flying) and the [Relief Pilot] as PM (Pilot Monitoring). Some minutes after the turbulence I called the flight deck from the flight crew rest bunk. I spoke with the [Relief Pilot] and was informed there were no injuries reported; however, meals that were being prepared in the aft galley were tossed to the floor and some galley carts had tipped over.We discussed the intensity of the turbulence and it was agreed to be consistent with the moderate turbulence intensity as described in the FOM. A message was sent to Dispatch and acknowledged. The cabin crew made a lengthy PA announcement that the meal service would not be available due to the turbulence encounter. After I excited the flight crew rest facility I found the Purser and discussed the situation with her in the forward galley. I inquired if anything was broken and if special cleaning would need to be arraigned for broken service items or spilled liquids. I was rather surprised to now learn that six of the cabin crewmembers were reporting various minor injuries. The Purser requested paramedics and flight service supervisors to be available upon arrival.After gate arrival, I was summoned by the Purser to help with an issue at the 2L entry door. The paramedics were preventing the passengers from deplaning. The paramedics were insisting any cabin crew requesting medical assistance to deplane ahead of the passengers. After being told the FAs could not leave with passengers still on the aircraft, the paramedics insisted they be allowed to proceed to the aft of the aircraft and treat the cabin crew. The paramedics were asking to have the passengers re-take their seats so they could come aboard. The entire crew was at their stations for deplaning and passengers were standing up and blocking all the isles. I had to insist that they allow the passengers to deplane and per the purser's request, wait to treat any crew requesting care. The paramedics said they were not willing to wait and if anyone needed treatment they would have to make a new request when ready.The paramedics left the gate area and the passengers began deplaning. After the last passengers had deplaned the cabin crew all gathered their own luggage and deplaned. Those that were requesting medical attention waited on the jet bridge with the Purser, me, the flight service supervisors, and the Gate Agent. One of the flight attendants that mentioned she had some back muscle spasm chose to leave to catch a commuter flight home. After confirming that the ZZZ [Company] medical facility was open, it was suggested as an option rather than waiting for the EMTs to return to the gate. There was no interest in that option from the cabin crew and choice was made to just wait for the EMTs. I had the Purser circle the names on the list of the crewmembers that were reporting injuries. From the flight deck I placed a call to Dispatch to see if there was any information they needed.				B777 Captain reported experiencing moderate turbulence in cruise, resulting in injuries to flight attendants.	
1699806	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US				4000		VMC			Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 19000		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting; Workload			1699806	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure; Workload; Troubleshooting			1699821	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	Took off from Runway XXR at ZZZ. During climbout, passing approximately 4,000 feet, the Number 2 engine power reduced to approximately 35% N1. Tried closing and advancing the Number 2 power lever with no effect. Advised ATC and requested priority handling. Turning right to the west, realized that we were in a good position for an almost straight in approach to Runway XYR. Wind was light and almost a direct crosswind to the parallel runways at ZZZ, so I requested landing on XYR. No unusual engine indications were noted other than the lack of thrust. Left the engine running to maintain electrical and hydraulic systems. Landed normally with 15 degrees of flaps.CRFF (Crash Rescue Fire Fighting) inspection showed no leaks or other problems so we taxied to the gate. The landing was made at less than the max landing weight. On the ground, the engine idle remained at 30% N1. Probably could have slowed down and more thoroughly run the checklists but position and conditions were conducive to an immediate landing. Other than the lack of thrust the aircraft operated normally.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737 flight crew reported the engine rollbacked to approximately 35% N1 during the initial climb, resulting in an immediate return to the departure airport.	
1699831	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			IMC	Fog				5000	TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure; Human-Machine Interface; Distraction; Confusion; Workload; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1699831												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning		Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Human Factors	Human Factors	First Officer was PF (Pilot Flying). Captain was PM (Pilot Monitoring). The departure clearance was changed via CPDLC (Controller Pilot Data Link Communications) during taxi-out. [The first] SID was changed to [the second] SID after all briefings were complete for [the first SID]. I loaded the new route into the FMC from the CPDLC prompt. I failed to load the [second] departure into the FMC from the App/Dep Page. We briefed the [second SID] and I put a 4 NM ring around the ZZZ VOR in the FIX page to comply with the crossing altitude restrictions. Heading 296 was selected on the MCP (Mode Control Panel) and 2,000 in the altitude window. This was the completely wrong way to program this SID into FMC for the 'climb via' clearance. There would have been no deviation if I had deselected LNAV for the takeoff during the MCP setup. The FO (First Officer) hand flew the departure. At 500 feet the FO entered a right turn following the Flight Director guidance to waypoint ZZZZZ. This occurred during gear retraction and frequency change to [Departure]. I announced 'Runway heading! Runway heading!' FO turned back left to 296 degrees. FO had turned right about 30 degrees before correcting back to the left. We got a GPWS 'Bank Angle' alert during the left heading correction back to 296 degrees. I did not determine the maximum bank angle. There were no other GPWS alerts. Frequency change and climb with [Departure] were uneventful. There was no query by Departure.I don't even know where to start. There were multiple distractions during taxi out. The visibility began to decrease about 5 minutes before pushback. We were number 8 for takeoff and [Departure] held all departures for 15 minutes. I had initially started both engines before tower announced the ground hold. I shut down the right engine because I didn't know how long we would be delayed. In the end, I waited too late to start the right engine the second time. The CPDLC departure clearance change came late about the time I needed to start the engine. The new routing was confusing. I rushed through the FMC programming and failed to load the SID from the App/Dep Page. I questioned the 'climb via' clearance and the crossing restriction at the [second SID] DME. That became my focus. We had no shared mental model even though we discussed in detail the initial climb heading and altitude constraints. I built the initial climb for the [second] SID in the FIX page instead of loading from the FMC. The FO did not realize that I had not loaded the [second] SID in the FMC. I failed to cross check the FMA's before takeoff and during the checklist to ensure that the aircraft would track 296 degrees.I will slow down and make sure there is a shared understanding of the departure clearance and SID especially after a route change. I will load the FMC after a route change with the same steps as the initial setup. I will make sure the new route is understood thoroughly, programmed correctly, and briefed. I will slow down and cross check FMA's several times during a high distraction event. I have gone over and over the distractions that led up to the event. The significant end result was exceeding bank angle and causing a GPWS alert.				B777 Captain reported incorrectly programming the departure, which resulted in a wrong direction turn and a GPWS alert during initial climbout.	
1699904	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				6000		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 7000		Time Pressure; Workload; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1699904												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	On approach to ZZZ we were told to expect ILS 12. Abeam the field at 5,000-6,000 ft. we were told to expect the RNAV XXR approach. I was the pilot flying and my First Officer was flying. I remarked that we had no one in front of us and could be 'slam dunked'. Abeam the Final Approach Fix, we were turned base and descended to 3,000 ft. We received no calls after that and we were high and on base just barely outside the FAF. The First Officer said he had the field visual. I realized we were about to cross final without a vector or a descent. I requested lower and told ATC that we were visual with the field. Approach cleared us to 1,500 ft., cleared us for the visual approach and sent us over to Tower frequency. The First Officer did an excellent job realizing we were going through final so he switched off the autopilot and turned toward final as we were shooting across final for both Runway XXL and XXR. He corrected back to final but due to the short final, the descent, and the overshooting vectors we were extremely busy and couldn't engage LNAV or VNAV fast enough to catch the course or glide slope. I asked the First Officer is he was okay with flying a 'true visual approach with no guidance'. The weather was good, he had the field visually, and he said 'yes'. The First Officer hurried to configure and I rushed to finish the checklist. As I was 'heads-down' the First Officer was correcting to final course. Due to the fact that we were not in any approach mode (ILS or LNAV/VNAV) the auto-throttles were not working. We got low and slow on final. I stated to the First Officer that we were low and slow and that the auto throttles 'weren't working', meaning that they were appropriately not doing anything to aid the First Officer. I don't know if I made this statement clearly to the First Officer and should have been very specific that they were completely inoperative in this situation. By the time he realized they were not helping him and that he had to manually operate the throttles, we were low and slow. We were stabilized around 700 ft. after leveling off and correcting but we had been approximately 10 kts. below bug speed (5 below threshold) and all red lights on the PAPI's (Precision Approach Path Indicator). He corrected back to speed and glide path and made an excellent landing.The event occurred due to several reasons. ATC 'slam dunked' us into ZZZ. We were very high, very tight to final, and were turned base abeam the FAF after having our approach and runway changed at the last minute. Approach also failed to turn us to a dogleg or to final and shot us across final. We were rushed and didn't have time to set up the LNAV/VNAV backup to the glide path which would have also given us autothrottles (there is no ILS to this runway for backup). Both the First Officer and myself are new to the airplane and our seats and we were slow to set up the LNAV/VNAV so that it could back us up. The ultimate reason is that when we were given poor guidance by ATC, I did not ask for longer vectors, nor did I direct a go around on final. It is the Captain's fault (mine).Although we received very poor handling from ATC, I could have easily avoided this situation by requesting longer vectors to set up for the RNAV approach to Runway XXL. We had been cleared for the ILS Runway XY and were changed to Runway XXL at the last minute and then slam dunked. If we had requested extended vectors it would have given us time to set up for the approach better. I know better than to let ATC fly my plane. Once we were on final and got destabilized, I should have called a go-around. Instead I opted to call out corrections to the First Officer. He had done an excellent job on the trip and I felt he could fix the situation. He did correct and did an excellent landing but there is no doubt in my mind that we should have gone around and started from scratch. Definitely several lessons learned from this.				B737 Captain reported that a last minute runway change from ATC resulted in an unstabilized approach.	
1699916	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Climb; Takeoff / Launch		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1699916	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1700198	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Passenger; Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On departure, the first officer and I noticed a very strong odor in the aircraft. I proceeded to call the cabin to inquire if they could smell the odor that we were experiencing. The number one flight attendant informed me that she could smell it and passengers were complaining about the odor as well. It was a very strong odor that I have never experienced before. It had a chemical smell to it and we could not determine the origin. I proceeded to identify as a priority handling with departure and we returned for landing. The total time in flight was 10 minutes and the landing was uneventful. After being checked by the ARFF crew we proceeded to the gate. An entry was made in the logbook and maintenance was notified. It is also important to note that two days prior, this aircraft had to return to the gate due to an odor on the aircraft and according to ZZZ maintenance the aircraft had another logbook indicating an odor in the cabin. There were no health issued with the passengers, and the flight attendants declined to be checked in ZZZ.		Unknown fume event. Maintenance informed us they washed the engines the night before and did a high power run after, but this aircraft seemed to have a fume event history after they did some more digging into its history.		B737-800 flight crew reported strong fumes on flight deck during takeoff, consequently, they returned to the departure airport.	
1699943	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																										Pitot-Static System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 383		Troubleshooting; Distraction			1699943	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 292		Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1700001	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During my preflight walk around, I was looking at the left side landing light. It looked inoperative but with the side light it was hard to tell. So I looked at the right side to compare what it should look like and confirmed that the left side was out. So at that point I continued on with my walk around. Once I got back up to the flight deck I informed the Captain of the inoperative landing light where he then completed the MEL with Maintenance Control. After pushback and engine start, it was noticed that the Captain's pitot heat light was illuminated. I continued with my After Start Flow and then we did the Before Taxi Checklist. Once that was completed we ran the QRH for the Captain Pitot light and determined it needed to be MELed. Once the paperwork was completed we continued to the runway.During the takeoff roll, where I was the Pilot Flying, the Captain called for the reject around 70 kts. We cleared the runway and ran brake cooling and determined it was safe to proceed back to the gate. Once at the gate the Captain asked me to do a quick walk around of the aircraft. As soon as I got down to the aircraft I noticed that the Captain-side pitot tube was missing from the aircraft. I was not part of the conversation that determined when the pitot tube may have departed the aircraft, but was informed that I must have missed that it was missing during my walk around. Shortly after that I recalled that after looking at the lights I must have never went back to the Captain side of the nose to start the preflight from my normal starting point. This event was a great reminder that when doing [an] important task, once you get distracted you should always start from the beginning and start the task over again so important items aren't missed.		FO (First Officer) returned from exterior preflight and informed me that the left fixed landing light was inoperative. We confirmed this and MELed it. After pushback, we accomplished After Start Flow and the 'Captain Pitot' light stayed on. Circuit breaker was in, and we contacted Dispatch and Maintenance via phone to MEL this. We were able to continue per the MEL and taxied for takeoff. On the takeoff roll, I noticed, at about 70 kts on the FO's side, that I had no airspeed indication. I rejected the takeoff at that point, we cleared the runway, ran the brake cooling numbers, and requested taxi back to the gate. A three minute cooling at the gate was returned.At the gate, after writing up the discrepancy, I asked the FO to do a walk around, as we were going to terminate the aircraft and likely would not take this aircraft out. He returned immediately and informed me that the entire pitot tube was missing. I was met on the jet way by Company Maintenance Personnel, working on another aircraft. After discussing various possible ways the pitot tube could have been torn off, we suspected that it had happened in or out of the gate the previous night, or on the remote pad where the aircraft overnighted. Shortly thereafter, the actual pitot tube was found on the Ramp at the remote pad. We simply missed the pitot tube damage on the originating exterior preflight. We clearly missed the damaged/missing pitot tube on preflight. The FO could have been distracted by the actual runway turnoff light in his face, or by attempting to tell if, in fact, a left fixed landing light was inoperative. Regardless, we missed the damage. We MELed the cockpit indication correctly and applied all MEL procedures properly, to my knowledge.		B737-700 flight crew reported a rejected takeoff due to a lack of airspeed indication on the Captain's side.	
1700007	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				3000		Mixed	8		Night	1000		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Autoflight System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200		Troubleshooting; Other / Unknown; Workload			1700007												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	After a normal takeoff and complying with the 'noise abatement procedures,' the aircraft would not allow for the autopilot or autothrottles to be engaged. In addition, after a normal flap and gear retraction, all three green lights illuminated. The flaps in the up position, we could not reduce the thrust lever setting lower than approximately 60% without the 'Configuration Warning Horn' sounding. So, initially we were obligated to keep the power setting higher than required for maintaining the assigned altitude of 5,000 feet to silence the horn, but in doing so, we exceeded the speed on the SID and the 250 kts restriction below 10,000 feet.We also deviated from the SID and had difficulties complying with ATC multiple heading and altitude changes as we were dealing with the associated problems and figuring out our best course of action to stabilize the aircraft. We did not have any master caution or warning lights illuminate to help assist in identifying the problem until we would try to engage the autopilot and then we would receive an 'Autopilot and Autothrottle' Disconnect Warning horn and lights. We recycled the landing gear but it had no effect on the Warning horn. We were only able to control the aircraft speed, without the horn sounding, when the landing gear was in the 'down and locked' position.Even then, we were not able to engage the autopilot, autothrottles, LNAV/VNAV and we were resigned to fly the aircraft by hand with the landing gear in the down position. We initially elected to return to ZZZ but after discussing the deteriorating weather decided to go to ZZZ1, where the weather was better, longer runways and more ground support. Unfortunately, with the highly congested airspace, multiple ATC course, altitude and speed changes, TA, lights, and horns in the cockpit, we had difficulty in complying with ATC instructions and ATC provided priority handling on our behalf.If ATC would engage the aircraft 'less' during a difficult situation, allow pilots time to correct, trouble-shoot or deal with an aircraft exhibiting unusually characteristics, I think the crew would be in a better position to not only handle the problem, but also be able to comply more accurately with heading, altitude and speed changes and only if there were 'fewer' of them.				B737 Captain reported Autoflight system malfunctioned erroneously, activating the warning horn and along with the high workload departure procedure, prevented the use of LNAV/VNAV, autopilot, and autothrottles.	
1700035	201911							10000		VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach																										Trailing Edge Flap		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 139; Flight Crew Total 2389; Flight Crew Type 2389		Troubleshooting; Distraction; Situational Awareness			1700035												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Airport	Aircraft	While joining the ILS at ZZZ we experienced a leading edge flap transit warning. As Pilot Monitoring, I immediately pointed the warning to the Pilot Flying and reached for QRH. While I began the appropriate QRH procedure the Pilot Flying continued the approach. At this point I believed this was safest course of action as we were already in the valley between the mountains. I told the Pilot Monitoring there was no way to complete the procedure during the approach and we agreed to go missed. Because of our flap issue we could not perform the full missed approach procedure, flaps were locked less than 1. Once established outbound to the hold I continued the QRH. The Leading Edge transit lead me to several other procedures. We were able to extend the Leading Edge devices, but trailing edge flaps were locked and asymmetrical. Left flap at 1/4 and right flap about 1. We both agreed in the asymmetry. We ran the final QRH checklist (trailing edge flap up) because the left and right flaps indicated less than 1, test the flight attendants, [advised ATC] and performed approach to landing with the Leading Edge devices extended and the trailing edge devices indicating less than 1 degree. The landing was in the touchdown zone and on centerline. We exited at Taxiway Delta and requested airport fire rescue examine the brakes and landing gear. The scene commander determined due to our brake temperature we could not park at the gate. We were escorted to a remote stand. Fans were placed around the main gear and once the brakes reached a safe temp passengers were deplaned via air stairs and bussed to the main terminal. We completed our post flight duties, contacted Maintenance, Dispatch and the Chief Pilot. A transmitter breaker popped was determined to have been the cause of the malfunction.Lastly our decision to land in ZZZ was influenced by several factors. Limited options in the area, ZZZ1, ZZZ2 or ZZZ3 and weather. While ZZZ has its challenges due to terrain and optical illusions I believe it was the best runway given our situation. My first choice would have been ZZZ2, but we were down to 7,000 lbs. of fuel and with the leading edge devices deployed, I feel we would have burned too much fuel en-route and would have painted ourselves into a corner in ZZZ2. ZZZ1 runway is to rough for the high Vref and ZZZ3 had weather in the area.				A Boeing 737 pilot reported a trailing edge flap malfunction which resulted in the flaps being locked out in a slightly asymmetrical position.	
1700049	201911									VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb																										Landing Gear Indicating System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 149; Flight Crew Total 615; Flight Crew Type 615					1700049												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon takeoff from ZZZ. After completing after takeoff flow I noticed red/green left gear indicators on. I informed the Captain and completed after takeoff checklist. Captain remained at aircraft controls with autopilot on. I referenced QRH and landing gear disagree procedure while Captain communicated with Maintenance and Dispatch I informed ATC of speed requirements during climb. We completed the steps in the 'gear disagree' procedure and opted to continue to ZZZ1 and [request priority handling] with ATC. We checked hydraulic levels and they were normal. We discussed our options regarding any subsequent problems we might experience and Captain briefed me on his configuration plan for the aircraft on arrival and landing into ZZZ1. Maintenance and Dispatch responded to our choice of options and we pressed on to ZZZ1. Captain flew an uneventful arrival and approach into ZZZ1 and the landing gear lowered normally with all green indications. Maintenance Technicians met us at gate.				B737 First Officer reported left 'red/green landing gear disagree light' illuminated on departure, but opted to continue the flight.	
1700059	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class C ZZZ																								Brake System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 108; Flight Crew Total 3553; Flight Crew Type 1902					1700059												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury		Y	Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	After lowering the gear on final to Runway XXR in ZZZ, we received an ECAM stating that the auto brake system was inoperative. We accomplished the ECAM procedure and consulted the QRH, which stated that the normal brakes were inoperative also. I chose to go around because of the short runway and immediately asked for a diversion to ZZZ, which was our designated alternate. Passing through 2,000 feet, a Flight Attendant notified us that a passenger was having a seizure. We notified Dispatch and coordinated with ATC and ZZZ1 Operations, requesting paramedics to meet us at the gate. Things were very hectic during the 10 minute flight, but the First Officer and I worked well together.				A320 Captain reported that a brake system failure and a passenger medical emergency resulted in a diversion.	
1700214	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Training / Qualification			1700214												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	Other Landing	Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	I was flying an ILS (Instrument Landing System) approach to Runway XXR during VMC (Visual Meteorological Conditions) conditions. At 1,000 and 500 ft. AGL, the aircraft was stable and on glide slope with target landing speed. At approximately 400 ft. AGL I noticed a yaw to the left along with a master caution light. I corrected the yaw condition and asked the Captain to look at the caution. The aircraft remained stable and I landed without incident. Upon landing roll out the left engine was observed to be at 0 RPM.The Captain informed me that he was guarding the throttles and adjusted his position in his seat for better visual lookout. While adjusting his position his hand must have brushed against the left engine start lever and once out of the stop it fell approximately 1' and began to shut down the engine.Specifically provide guidance for hand placement for throttle guarding during an approach for the pilot monitoring.				B737 First Officer reported that the Captain bumped an engine start lever during approach and caused the engine to shut down.	
1700216	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Engine Control		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface			1700216												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	Below 500 ft. AGL, while guarding the throttles, I adjusted my position in the seat to gain better visual. Inadvertently knocked #1 engine start lever approximately 1 inch out of the gate. Immediately returned the lever into the gate, engines indicated normal. At about 200 ft., the #1 engine started to roll back and shut down. First Officer continued landing without incident.I did not realize a movement that slight of the start lever would shut down the engine. Will make sure my hands are clear of the levers while guarding throttles.				B737 Captain reported that he accidentally bumped an engine start lever during approach, causing the engine to shut down.	
1700717	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				34000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class B ZZZ																								FMS/FMC		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1700717												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Descending into ZZZ the aircraft locked onto a glideslope signal at around FL340 and started to climb. Took a couple of seconds to figure out what it was doing, but once I saw the G/S on the FMA was green (meaning it was locked on) it was clear the aircraft thought it should follow the glide slope so it started a climb. At that point the autopilot was disconnected and ATC was notified that we had a problem. All the automation was turned off and once the aircraft was stable in the descent the automation LNAV/VNAV etc., was re-established. Aircraft landed safely in ZZZ and was turned over to Maintenance. They discovered that the FCC computer on the A side had tripped a fault code and placarded the autopilot A inoperative. Flew the Aircraft back to ZZZ on autopilot B with no issues.				B737-800 Captain reported that an FMS malfunction resulted in an uncommanded climb during approach.	
1700731	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0										Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Physiological - Other			1700731	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1700747	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Cabin crew reported odor, fumes of a chemical nature very strong in back of plane. Pilots smelled 'dirty socks' and donned oxygen masks. We determined it strong enough to return to ZZZ. We were vectored back to an arrival and landed without incident, and did not declare an emergency.		On the climbout the flight attendants called up front and told us they could smell an odor. I was pilot flying so the Captain did all communicating with the flight attendants. We had a brief conversation and did not notice anything too bad at that time. Flight attendants called back again a couple min later and reported having headaches due to the dirty sock smell which escalated to more of a chemical smell in their opinion. We donned our oxygen masks and Captain accomplished QRH procedures as I was flying and working the radios. The Captain decided to divert back to ZZZ. We proceeded back to ZZZ while our masks were on and flew the ILS to XXL...I kept my mask on to the gate and the flight ended without any more issues.		A320 flight crew reported chemical fumes in cabin noticed by flight attendants. Subsequently, odor was detected in the flight deck. Flight crew executed a precautionary return to departure airport.	
1701021	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US			0			VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 17000		Situational Awareness			1701021												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	During approach I thought I smelled an 'oily' scent. No one else in the crew complained of any unusual smells so I thought it might just be my imagination. Being curious, I asked the F/O (First Officer) if he noticed anything unusual. He said he caught a whiff of something. We were on final approach and landed the aircraft. At the gate I asked our #1 F/A (Flight Attendant) if she noticed any unusual scent and she said no. I then asked the F/A working in the back of the aircraft and he said he smelled a chemical odor during descent.				A320 Captain reported smelling an 'oily' odor in the cockpit which was also reported by flight attendants in the aft galley.	
1701288	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	FO				39000		VMC							Air Carrier	A330		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1701288												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Manuals; Human Factors; Equipment / Tooling	Ambiguous	Approximately 39,000 ft., I was called up to flight deck from my break for a fuel problem. The FO (First Officer) and Relief Pilot had the ECAM message 'FUEL LO TEMP'. They ran the checklist and after they turned on the OUTR TK XFR, the fuel in the left tip trim tank would not transfer and the 'OUTR TO INR FAULT' message appeared. This is when I was called up, I instructed them to call maintenance and dispatch. When I arrived, maintenance did not have any solutions and dispatch had informed us that another aircraft had the same problem and descended to 34,000 ft. and was suspecting contaminated fuel. We increased speed and descended to 34,000 ft. This allowed us to transfer the fuel to the mains. We climbed back up to 37,000 ft. and continued on to ZZZ with no further issues.				A330 flight crew reported a fuel issue during cruise that was solved by descending and speeding up.	
1701294	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC							Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Visual Approach; Vectors																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Fatigue; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1701294												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	We were vectored onto a visual approach for XXC at ZZZ. ATC asked us to maintain 210 kts. as long as practical. In order to maintain 210 kts. I asked for flaps 5. My plan was to maintain 210 kts. until two miles outside the final approach fix ZZZZZ. At that point I planned to start to fully configure the aircraft for final approach and landing. Upon reaching two miles outside of the final approach fix ZZZZZ I asked the Captain, who was Pilot Monitoring, for gear down and flaps 15. As the flaps were moving to 15, I looked at the airspeed indicator and realized that we were not below the Flap 15 airspeed limitation of 200 kts. I was about to ask for the flaps to be retracted to flaps 10 so as not to have a flaps 15 over speed but by that point the aircraft quickly started to decelerate to below 200 kts. and I decided against asking for flaps to be retracted to flaps 10. After that we configured the aircraft as we normally would for final approach and landing. Other than the flaps 15 over speed the approach and landing were normal.I believe this event was caused by three factors. Expectation bias on my part and the Pilot monitoring part, failure to monitor airspeed by the Pilot Monitoring, and fatigue. Normally ZZZ Approach asks us to maintain 170 or 180 kts. till the final approach fix. I would have normally configured the aircraft with flaps 10 in order to maintain 170 or 180 kts. till two miles outside the final approach then I would automatically ask for gear down and flaps 15 to start the final configuration of the aircraft for landing. 170 to 180 kts. is below the flaps 15 airspeed limitation. In this case Approach Control asked for 210 kts. as long as practical. As I said previously my plan was to start the slowing and final configuration of the aircraft for landing two miles outside the final approach fix ZZZZZ. Having numerous/countless times being at 170 to 180 kts. approaching two miles outside the final approach fix I 'automatically' started to configure the aircraft for final approach and landing by asking for gear down and flaps 15. If I was at 170 to 180 kts. asking for flaps 15 that would not have caused a flaps 15 over speed but in this case since I was at 210 kts. when I asked 'automatically' for gear down and flaps 15. The Pilot Monitoring apparently did not verify that we were below 200 kts. when I asked for flaps 15 and went ahead and selected flaps 15. It could very well be the Pilot Monitoring had expectation bias as I did and expected us to be at 170 to 180 kts. when he selected flaps 15 after I asked for it. I also believe fatigue played a minor role in this event. We were finishing a three day trip. This was the third day and we had an early morning wake up, van and report time. We left the hotel at XA:10 local time for a XB:26 local report time. This was our third leg of the final day of a trip. We were both tired when we started this approach which affected thought processes and monitoring skills.As far as expectation bias is concerned. I should treat every approach as a unique set of circumstances and not fall back on doing things 'automatically' as I have on countless other approaches and which I did on this particular approach to my regret and consternation. As far as monitoring goes one must strive to constantly maintain vigilance and not act 'automatically' when verification of a limit, airspeed in this case, is involved. The fatigue portion is difficult to deal with. Both of us had adequate sleep the night before this flight but the operational demands of the airline require us to fly and rest at hours different from the normal hours we would keep while home.				B737 First Officer reported exceeding maximum speed for flap setting during initial approach.	
1701562	201911	1801-2400	ZOA.ARTCC	CA				34000					Night			Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	STAR SERFR3	Class A ZOA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 315		Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701562	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 55		Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701566	Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Procedure; Chart Or Publication	Chart Or Publication	Checked on with Oakland Center and were cleared direct to NRRLI and to descend via the SERFR 3. I mistakenly went direct to NARWL, a similar sounding fix. I put it in the FMC and executed. The Captain was on the PA at the time. When he came back I told him of our clearance and shortly after was asked by ATC if we were going to NRRLI intersection. I said that we were and the Controller then corrected the mistake. Continued the flight with no further error. Cleared to a way point/ went to wrong fix.		Enroute to MAKRS, Oakland Center cleared us to what we thought was NARWL way point and to descend via the SERFR 3 Arrival to SFO. A few minutes later Center questioned us on our course, when we reported that we were going to NARWL, the Center Controller then re-cleared us to NRRLI, which was where he had originally intended for us to go, and to descend via the SERFR 3, the remainder of the flight was uneventful. The fact that two similar sounding way points on the arrival was confusing. Two similar sounding way points names can be confusing. Maybe change one of the names on the SERFR 3 Arrival.		B737 Flight Crew reported navigating to wrong, similar sounding waypoint.	
1701567	201911	1201-1800	ZOA.ARTCC	CA				36000					Dusk			Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise	STAR SERFR3	Class A ZOA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 477		Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701567												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Chart Or Publication; Procedure; Human Factors	Chart Or Publication	While in cruise flight on the SERFR 3 Arrival, Oakland Center cleared us direct to the NRRLI way point.  We mistakenly selected the NARWL way point and began navigating to that fix. After a minute, ATC queried our track and asked if we were going to NRRLI. Recognizing our error, we reprogrammed the FMC to change our course for NRRLI. ATC indicated that this was a common error and that they were in the process of making a change to the arrival.  In the future, I will be more aware of this deviation while on the SERFR Arrival and be careful before making any changes to fixes in the FMC.				B737 First Officer reported navigating to wrong, similar sounding, waypoint on STAR.	
1701624	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US				7500		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 203; Flight Crew Total 14600		Situational Awareness			1701624	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Total 3600; Flight Crew Type 625		Situational Awareness			1701621	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On takeoff as we were setting power, there was a slight noticeable odor as we pushed the thrust levers up. While I did notice it, it was not particularly concerning to me as I was aware the engine had work done recently and the packs had been off as the aircraft was in MX all night prior to us accepting it. Climbing out of ZZZ prior to flap retraction we received an inter phone call from the rear FA XX_11 XX_16 stating that there was a fire and the cabin had a burning smell that was worsening and there was a little smoke or haziness. My FO (First Officer) confirmed the FA (Flight Attendant) said there was a fire and I immediately let ATC know that we would need to return to ZZZ ASAP. I did not specifically declare an emergency, but I did exercise my emergency authority. We were immediately given vectors for a downwind for 26. We continued to clean up the aircraft and I transferred control and ATC to the FO and I called the cabin again. I questioned the FA further who then indicated that there was no fire, nothing was hot, but there was an extremely strong odor with a burning like smell. With the urgency slightly reduced, I elected to call MX (Maintenance) and ask if the work they did may have created the odor and if it should clear up. MX indicated the work accomplished should not have created the odor, but that if it cleared up, we would probably be okay as long as we confirmed there was no fire. I again called the cabin and was told the smell was dissipating slightly but was still quite strong. I elected to return to ZZZ. We were vectored for a visual/ILS to Rwy XY and landed uneventfully underweight and taxied to the gate. AARF (Airport Rescue Firefighting) followed us to the gate and met the aircraft. After all pax (passengers) deplaned, AARF went to the aft cabin and confirmed no fire or heat but they did smell the odor. No passengers showed any ill medical effects and all deplaned normally. Nice job by the entire crew!		While climbing after departing from Runway XXR at ZZZ, at about 1000 feet, the aft Flight Attendant called the flight deck and informed us that she thought something was on fire in the aft galley/lav area, that there was an odor and some smoke, and it was '...getting worse.' The Captain and I had noticed a slight odor as we set takeoff power, but we had already discussed that the #2 engine had been bore-scoped recently and had been in Maintenance all night before we accepted the aircraft, and the little odor we noticed didn't seem concerning. However, upon hearing that there seemed to be smoke and it was getting worse in the back we immediately began discussing the need to return to ZZZ and informed ATC. We were given vectors for a left downwind to land on Runway XY. The Captain transferred control of the aircraft and the radios to me as he called the aft Flight Attendant to get more information. It seemed from that conversation that there was doubt as to whether there was a fire after all, as nothing seemed hot, but there was still a strong burning odor. I informed ATC that we wanted a longer downwind vector as we troubleshot and I loaded the ILS approach to XY. The Captain then called Maintenance and asked if the work they had done on engine #2 would cause the odor, and they didn't think it would. After further discussion with the Flight Attendants the Captain elected to return to ZZZ. We received vectors to a visual approach to XY and landed uneventfully. Fire trucks met us at the runway and followed us to gate XX. Passengers deplaned and none seemed to show any illness. After passengers had left the aircraft, fire personnel and maintenance personnel came aboard to investigate. Post flight discussions indicated there did not seem to be any fire or heat damage but there was still a burning odor in the cabin.		B737 flight crew reported Flight Attendant notification of burning odor, smoke and possible fire in the passenger cabin shortly after takeoff. Flight crew executed an air return.	
1701692	201911	1201-1800	TNCM.Airport	FO			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower TNCM	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																										Turbine Engine		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 20000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1701692												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	We have planned for a 27K MAX BUMP bleeds off take-off on Runway 28. My copilot and I have done this several times in the last 30 days while not together but both very experienced with the procedures and the airport. We followed the procedures to the letter and fully briefed the take-off. After being cleared for take-off and lining up with the runway I advanced the throttles to 40%. After they were established at 40%, I hit TOGA. Just as the aircraft started rolling it suddenly surged to the right and at the same time the co-pilot called out the #2 engine not accelerating. By then the #1 was at full 27K 103.1 N1. I was able to retard both engines and was able to keep the aircraft on the runway. We taxied back to the gate and called for Maintenance to come out and inspect the tires and write up the #2 engine slow to accelerate after 40% power setting. The inspection showed no damage or wear on the tires and no faults on EEC or any other systems.We talked with TECH in ZZZ and they said that this has occurred several times on the 737 and was usually the cause of having one engine that is new and the other engine having a lot of time on it. The older engine is 'looser' and may not accelerate like a new engine but when 2 older engines are matched up they accelerate at the same rate so you don't notice the acceleration rate. He said that was the case with our aircraft. Our #1 engine was just installed 2 months ago. Our #2 engine was the original engine installed NEW recently. The #1 engine has 253 cycles, the #2 engine has 12,588 cycles and is 10 years old compared to a brand new aircraft. I was told by the Technician that they just watched a video on this subject and in the video it recommends after the power is set to 40% to leave it there for 20 seconds prior to hitting TOGA. I told him that is not something we were told about or trained to do.I believe that as our aircraft are getting older and engines are getting changed with high cycles, we should be tracking this combination and have a procedure in effect such as the 20 seconds at 40% N1 prior to TOGA especially when high N1 is required such as 27K BUMP, to avoid what could have been a more serious outcome had this crew not acted so quickly and communicated so quickly regarding the #2 engine not accelerating after TOGA.				B737 Captain reported a rejected takeoff after #2 engine would not accelerate to match #1 engine.	
1701742	201911	1201-1800	SCT.TRACON	CA				8500								TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class E ZLA																												TRACON SCT		Cirrus Aircraft Undifferentiated		1					Cruise		Class E ZLA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701742	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701760	Conflict NMAC; Deviation - Speed All Types			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Procedure; Airspace Structure	Airspace Structure	Climbing out of PSP on the Cathedral 1, in the right turn to PSP VOR, SOCAL APP cleared us direct YUCCA intersection leaving 5000 ft. MSL which we did. We were cleared to 10000 ft. MSL with company airbus about MORON cleared to 11000 ft. MSL. Just after we were cleared to 15000 ft. MSL I noticed traffic at our 1130 position about 600 ft. above us right before SOCAL mentioned it was a Cirrus at about 9000 ft. and that it was no factor as it would pass in front of us. The FO (First Officer) was flying and I responded 'looking' to SOCAL so that we could agree on calling it in sight. The FO had just adjusted pitch while hand flying and was pulling the power back to remain under the cirrus when the TCAS issued an Resolution Advisory adjust pitch and the symbology in the HUD (Heads Up Display) was a perfect match to what the FO had already done. The traffic did pass in front of us about 400 ft. above us because of the actions taken by the FO. In the level off we exceeded 250 kts. below 10000 ft. MSL momentarily. Traffic call should have come much earlier from SOCAL APP.		We were climbing out of PSP. I was the PF (Pilot Flying). I was currently hand flying. ATC issued traffic at our 11 o'clock and 3 miles at 9000 ft., climb maintain 15000 ft. you will pass behind that traffic. We continued climb as normal and prior to even getting up to pitch attitude I noticed this wasn't looking good. I immediately adjusted to level off and fly lower than that traffic. I did verbalize all of this to the Captain. As I was nearing level off we received the RA (I believe it was 'monitor V/S?' (Vertical Speed)). I was already below the pitch the TCAS wanted. We passed behind and about 500 ft. lower. Notified ATC of the RA and continued on my climb. We did exceed 250 knots below 10000 ft. because of the abrupt pitch change and just all that was going on in a very short time. I did resolve the speed rather quickly.Yes I should've gone with my gut and override the thrust levers to pull the power back quicker so as not to exceed the speed or just turned off the AT's (Auto Throttles) for the time being. Also, ATC should've been more conservative about this crossing traffic.		B737 flight crew reported NMAC during climb out.	
1701746	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																										Nosewheel Steering		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1701746												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After engines stable and hitting TOGA (Takeoff Go-Around) both power levers came up evenly, then about 2 seconds later at 20 kts or less the tiller pulled hard to the left and I was not able to control it with force or right rudder steering I aborted the takeoff and regained control at low speed. Maintenance examined the tiller.				B737-800 Captain reported a nose wheel steering anomaly during takeoff.	
1701755	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1701755												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Passenger; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately 30 to 60 minutes after reaching cruise at 36,000ft, The A FA (Flight Attendant) contacted the flight deck to inform us that 3A PAX (passenger) reported a strange smell. The passenger described the smell as a transient burn odor. Passengers next to 3A reported not smelling anything abnormal. A FA also reported not being able to detect any abnormal odor anywhere in the cabin. I instructed the A FA to keep monitoring the situation and to report any change. About 30 to 45 minutes later, A FA reported that a passenger next to 3A now has also detected the strange odor and that 1A PAX is reporting not feeling well but did not smell anything abnormal. At that point I'm assuming 1A is probably unrelated to the odor at row 3. I instructed the FAs to turn the oven off and to keep monitoring. I also switch the pack flow control from low to norm to help clear lingering odor quicker. About 30 to 45 minutes after, we are getting reports that a couple more passengers at row 5 and 8, as well as the B FA and a non-rev FA have now noticed a strange odor. The A and C FA have not detected anything abnormal, but the A FA is reporting not being able to smell anything because she is experiencing burning sensation in her nose, eyes, and throat. The C FA reported the odor as resembling 'the smell when you open a gas oven', 'sewage', or 'dirty socks'. I asked if it smelled like sulfur and she replied 'yes!'. The odor is elusive, transient and of moderate intensity. The C FA is reporting having a light headache. We are now assuming the source might be AIR COND. I applied part of the SMOKE/FUMES/AVNCS SMOKE for AIR COND and turn Pack 1 OFF and ACARS Maintenance that we were experiencing a cabin air quality event. For the remainder of the flight no one detected any abnormal odor. PAX 1A reported feeling well approximately 30 minutes after turning pack 1 OFF. A FA filled a cabin air quality event form. I determined from the FOM Air Quality table that it was a severity level 2 event. Before the flight, Maintenance worked on engine #1 to replace an HPV (High Pressure Bleed) valve. During Post flight debrief with FA, everyone felt fine. Additionally, they reported thinking at one point that it 'smelled like a gas leak'. I made a logbook entry and contacted [Flight Ops Dispatcher] as per FOM. After sending an ACARS message to maintenance, we received no acknowledgment. I sent a message to dispatch half an hour later to asked if they had received my first message and they acknowledged they did. I think it would be nice to receive an acknowledgment.				A320 Captain reported Flight Attendant notification of passengers reporting abnormal odor at various times during flight. Flight Attendants also detected 'dirty socks/sulfur' odor during flight. Flight crew completed Air Conditioner fume QRH procedure which caused odor to dissipate.	
1701784	201911		ZZZ.Airport	US			800			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class C ZZZ																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer									Class C ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 164; Flight Crew Type 1702		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701784	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 195; Flight Crew Total 2755; Flight Crew Type 2755		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1701932	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airspace Structure; Human Factors; Procedure	Airspace Structure	Just after sunset, cleared visual approach to XXL in ZZZ. On the base turn, while getting set up on the approach and checking in with Tower, they mention that there will be VFR traffic in the pattern for Runway XY at a nearby airport, ZZZ1. We acknowledge but did not yet see the traffic, and were still turning in. The FO (First Officer) was flying, and set up on a good stabilized visual approach using the ILS as backup. I did see one TCAS hit to the left of course and a few miles away, initially assumed that was our traffic.Closer in, around the FAF, Tower again mentions traffic, but I respond that we didn't see it. I don't recall my exact words, probably negative contact. At this point we look in and see a TCAS hit directly ahead and slightly below. I continue to look outside for the traffic, but don't see it due to lots of lights against the dark ground. Initially I wasn't concerned as we didn't have an RA (Resolution Advisory), or even a yellow colored traffic icon. But at this point I see we are only 400 feet away. My FO was on the ball, and before I could even say anything he leveled off and offset to the right. We were near the fix ZZZZZ at this point, about 800 AGL, and were on course and glide path when we stopped the descent. The closest we saw on the TCAS was 300 feet separation, and the TCAS traffic icon was merged with ours, no separation. We never acquired the traffic visually.If we had not stopped the descent and offset, we would have been much closer than 300 feet. I can't say we would have collided, but it is definitely in the realm of possibilities. I can't emphasize enough how high a threat this situation was. When we landed we queried the Tower, and they just said it was traffic in the pattern, as if it happened all the time. At no point did they display any urgency as to the position of the traffic relative to us.Furthermore, we then realized that the reason we never got an RA was that we had just descended below 900 feet, and TCAS was therefore inhibited. In real time, that had not occurred to us, as we were too busy trying to find the traffic, fly the approach, then make corrections to avoid a collision.Our best guess is that the VFR traffic had just departed Runway XY, was somewhere around 500 AGL and either level or slightly climbing, but still flying towards ZZZ, i.e., had not yet turned crosswind. This meant it was flying directly in to the glide path for Runway XXL at ZZZ, which we happened to be on at the time. We could not see them due to ground light clutter and deck angle, they were probably flying in the same direction as us and therefore could not see us descending from behind, no RA was available due to altitude inhibit, and we were not getting any kind of avoidance instructions from Tower. We were exactly where we were supposed to be, in full communication with ATC, yet still were placed in a dangerous situation.		Cleared visual to XXL at ZZZ and went direct FAF to intercept final, AP (Autopilot) and ATR (Auto Throttles) off. On base turn to final, Tower called out VFR traffic off Runway XY from a local airport. Turned out to be ZZZ1. Captain acknowledged traffic call with negative contact. Since we were configuring at the time, we did a quick scan and didn't see traffic although TCAS showed traffic off to left of centerline with no altitude. Once approach stabilized at the GSI (Glide Slope Indicator), FAF = ZZZZZ1, Tower again calls out traffic departing Runway XY ZZZ1 and Captain replies, negative contact. TCAS shows traffic 1000 ft. or more below. Since it was dusk and deck angle was 2.5 - 3 degrees, aircraft was difficult to see amongst cultural city lighting.As we continued approach, altitude between aircraft decreased to 500 ft. on the TCAS. I then offset slightly to the right of course and started to level off, slightly inside of ZZZZZ, hoping the Captain could gain visual contact. When I leveled off, approximately 800 ft. MSL, TCAS showed traffic at 300 feet below and climbing. TCAS symbol merged slightly off to our left and 300 feet below. No RA (Resolution Advisory) or TA (Traffic Advisory) sounded. Once clear of symbol on TCAS, I continued approach from approximately 1 dot high on GS and quickly stabilized prior to 500 feet. We landed uneventfully. On taxi-in, I asked ground about traffic we encountered on final. They seemed to feel it was not out of normal operations. After Tower cleared us the visual, they really didn't point out traffic again. Even if we had been on an instrument approach, I don't think Tower would have provided separation.Difficult to say but if we had not leveled off and if the departing traffic continued to climb or fly extended centerline off ZZZ1, a collision could have occurred. We fell into the trap of relying on Tower for separation, thinking we would visually acquire the traffic and that the traffic would turn crosswind. Unfortunately, none of these occurred. Without reviewing the radar tapes, hard to say how close we were - departing aircraft was probably a high wing aircraft, on extended centerline, climbing and we are overtaking it, and we have a high deck angle making visual acquisition extremely difficult at dusk.Reviewing company FM (Flight Manual) and FOM and open source documents related to ZZZ1. FM - no RAs below 900 feet. No TAs below 400 feet, so I figured that would have triggered but it didn't. FOM - visual approach provides guidance when following an aircraft to the landing runway but does not provide traffic resolution for other scenarios. ZZZ1 - Remain less than 700 feet MSL due to traffic on XXL to ZZZ. Extended centerline off ZZZ1 at 700 feet puts you in direct conflict with traffic on ZZZ XXL.		A320 Captain reported experiencing a near mid-air collision during approach to landing.	
1701807	201911	0601-1200	EWR.Airport	NJ				6000								TRACON N90	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR		GPS; FMS Or FMC; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway 22L	Initial Approach		Class B EWR																												TRACON N90		Cirrus Aircraft Undifferentiated									Class B EWR																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1701807												Conflict Airborne Conflict	Horizontal 15000; Vertical 400	N	Automation Aircraft RA	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airspace Structure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	Received a Traffic Alert and subsequent, but very brief, Resolution Advisory with associated 'Monitor Vertical Speed' command for close proximity general aviation cirrus aircraft while being vectored by Newark Approach control for an ILS 22L approach. The incident occurred in the vicinity of PHLBO at approximately 6000' at approximately and in conjunction with a frequency change from initial approach frequency of 128.55 to 120.15. The TCAS 'Traffic, Traffic' was quickly replaced by a red target and 'Monitor Vertical Speed' command and red Avoidance Area before finally receiving 'Clear of Conflict' aural alert. The autopilot was disconnected per maneuver SOP and the Pilot Flying was able to maintain outside the red avoidance area without deviating from ATC cleared heading or altitude while I changed TCAS radar scale and visually acquired the general aviation aircraft. I initially communicated we were maneuvering in accordance with RA to the approach controller on 120.15, but after the event, reported the event as a TA only. This was inadvertently erroneous as, while brief in time, we did in fact have an RA with the closest point of approach with the cirrus aircraft being +400' and approximately 2.5 miles. Contributing factors to the event were: A) Simultaneous approach operations for ILS11 and ILS 22L, B) Three approach control frequencies being utilized versus the normal two(128.55, 120.15, 120.55) and C) congested airspace.				A320 pilot reported erroneously notifying ATC of a TA instead of an RA encountered during an ILS approach into EWR.	
1702004	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Physiological - Other			1702004	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Physiological - Other			1701848	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was [Captain] PM on Aircraft X from ZZZ to ZZZ1. Uneventful pushback, engine start, taxi and takeoff from XXR at ZZZ. On initial climb-out, I smelled a very faint odor in the flight deck. It smelled sweet, and I didn't think much of it at the time because I've smelled plenty of strange odors before and just assumed it was something galley or lav-related. As we continued the climb, the smell became a little bit stronger. It smelled almost exactly like Play-Doh. I asked my (First Officer) if he could smell something and he said no. To see if I was just imagining things, I put on my oxygen mask with 100% oxygen flow selected, and breathed with it for approximately 5 minutes. I no longer smelled the odor. Upon removing my mask, I immediately smelled the odor again. A few minutes later, we reached our cruise altitude of FL350. We completed our cruise duties. I could still smell the odor, and within a few more minutes, my eyes began to feel slightly irritated. The (First Officer/PF) reported that he could smell a faint odor, but reported no physiological effects from its exposure. To get a third opinion, we called the cabin and asked if the Flight Attendants could set up a barrier and if our #1 could come up front to see if he smelled something strange. Upon entering the flight deck, our #1 immediately reported a strong 'sweet' smell and a feeling of 'heaviness' in the air that was not present anywhere in the cabin. After leaving the flight deck, he walked to the aft galley and back to the forward galley and confirmed that the smell was only present in the flight deck. By this time, the 'Play-Doh' odor was a bit stronger, the (First Officer) began coughing, and we agreed that we were experiencing a fume event. We both donned our oxygen masks with 100% flow selected. We [notified ATC] with Center and told them we would be diverting to ZZZ2, which was almost immediately due south of us and within 60 miles. They cleared us to ZZZ2 via direct and we began our descent. The (First Officer) flew the aircraft and took the radios while I ran the QRC for fumes in the flight deck, which directed me to the QRH (chapter) to continue the procedure. I was extremely task-saturated nearly the entire remainder of the flight because the (First Officer) did not have any free hands to do anything but fly the aircraft (he was descending with full speed brakes and coordinating with ATC), so I also programmed ZZZ2 into the box as our new destination, got the ATIS, set landing field elevation, and then built the approach for Runway X. I set the inbound course and frequency for both me (Captain PM) and the (First Officer PF). He handed me his iPad so I could pull up the approach plate for him. I did so, and also gave him a quick briefing on the pertinent items for the approach. I ran the Descent and Before Landing Checklists. While completing these tasks, I was also running QRH for fumes in the flight deck. Step [XX] of the QRH procedure (as well as common sense) directed us to not delay landing in an attempt to complete all of the checklist items, so we prioritized landing at ZZZ2 ASAP over completing the entire checklist. Our masks began to fog up, so we switched to Forced Flow 100% (they cleared up quickly). I called the Flight Attendants as instructed by the QRH procedure to notify them that cabin lighting would be extinguished, but that passengers would have reading lights available. Communication was challenging, as the masks allowed us to talk to only one person at a time (i.e., if I wanted to talk to the FAs, I couldn't communicate with the (First Officer, PF) while doing so because the mask muffled my voice), which made proper CRM absolutely crucial so that nobody was left out of the loop. The irritation in my eyes was no longer an issue (it only happened when I had my mask off) but my First Officer reported chest pains and painful breathing, even with the mask on. I asked if he felt okay to land the aircraft, and he said he was, but I stayed alert and ready to assume control should he become incapacitated. As we would be landing 6,000 pounds overweight, I also ran the QRH procedure for an overweight landing. ZZZ2 approach vectored us for a visual approach and landing on Runway X, which was conducted without incident. The timespan from initiating the diversion at FL350 to touchdown at ZZZ2 was 20 minutes. My (First Officer) touched down smoothly in the touchdown zone and on centerline. We exited right and stopped the aircraft to ensure the situation was under control and that we were safe to taxi to the gate. After agreeing that we could taxi in without further incident, we notified ATC that it was safe to release the emergency vehicles, and taxied to the gate & deplaned the passengers. None of the passengers or Flight Attendants reported any adverse physiological effects from the fume event, although our #1 said that after landing, they could also smell the odor in the cabin as the aircraft depressurized and the fumes were sucked aft to the outflow valve. After contacting the company, Maintenance and Dispatch, we were sent to a hotel, and from there the (First Officer) and I immediately went to the ER, where we were treated for our respective symptoms we reported during the fume event. After 4 hours of observation, we were deemed to be medically OK and released from the ER.		While leveling off at 35000 from ZZZ to ZZZ1 the (Captain, PM) and I (First Officer) began smelling a very strong odor in the cockpit. After checking the air-conditioning and trying to figure out where the smell was coming from the (Captain) started getting irritated red eyes. Once this occurred we started the checklist for Smoke, Fire or Fumes. As the flying pilot I asked the (Captain) to start the checklist while I spoke with the #1 Flight Attendant to see if the smell was also in the cabin. The smell was contained only to the cockpit. Once I determined this I notified Company Dispatch of our problem. Dispatch responded with a diversion to either ZZZ2 or ZZZ. I elected to go to ZZZ being the closest airport at our 1:00 position. I then [notified] ATC and we both donned our oxygen masks for the duration of the flight. I then joined the (Captain) on the QRC, QRH procedures. With the QRH checklist complete we then started prepping for the Visual approach to runway X into ZZZ2. We printed the weather, discussed and set up the approach, and programmed the FMS for the approach. Being vectored for the descent and slowing for the approach I then called for the Overweight Landing checklist since we were going to land at 152,000 pounds. We set up for this by referencing the QRH, landing with flaps 30 selecting runway 08 as the longest runway for landing. The approach and landing was done with a slow descent rate and as flat as I could and within the touchdown zone of the runway. We then exited the runway at [taxiway] and entered [taxiway] pointing East. We stopped the aircraft to assess the situation and decided to call off the ARFF [Aircraft Rescue & Fire Fighting] equipment. We opened the cockpit windows and took our O2 masks off to continue taxiing to spot L for gate XX where we shut down. After shutting down we realized that during the QRH procedure for the Smoke, Fire or Fumes we isolated and shut down all lighting in the cabin, resulting in the flight attendants using flashlights to assist in deplaning the passengers which went very smooth. I then completed the aircraft logbook with an ODOR entry and an OVER Weight Landing entry. I called Dispatch and my Chief Pilot on call to inform them of the situation. My final call was to MedAir to see what they wanted myself and the (Captain) to do. We arranged a hotel for the crew and headed to the hospital to be assessed. I received a breathing treatment, steroid, and chest Xray, while the First Officer received eye drops. After being monitored for 4 hours in the ER we were released to return to our hotel for rest. We have no idea where the smell came from, while doing the checklist the smell continued after all the isolating we did.		B737-800 flight crew reported a fume event in the cockpit which resulted in a diversion.	
1702247	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		Mixed	Rain; Turbulence		Daylight				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 240; Flight Crew Total 16000; Flight Crew Type 10000		Physiological - Other			1702247												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	We were at cruise FL350. There was a line of weather to our east and south and we were IMC and started getting turbulence. It was enough that we had some power fluctuations. Within a minute I smelled the smell; hot, sour, musty odor. My eyes and throat felt dry and burned. Within a minute of that, we got a call from the cabin that they were smelling the fumes also. I told them I would call them back as soon as we addressed the issue. Because we were still in radar contact, I told First Officer (FO) to get the checklist up and I sent a message to Dispatch to let them know before we got to class II. My thought was they would turn us around or send us to ZZZ or ZZZ1, but they wanted us to continue. We ran the isolation checklist, but the smell was dissipating so we were never able to isolate it. All the while were in turbulence and deviating. Over the next 2 hours we had 10 or more fumes events where it was a burst of smell then dissipation. 4 of the events led to FO and I putting on our oxygen masks. Right before reentering ZZZ2 airspace, we got a message from Dispatch that they were considering sending us to ZZZ1. We felt that was the best and safest course of action, but then they said they wanted us to press on. Because we didn't feel incapacitated and there was no guidance to divert. We felt pressured to continue on to ZZZZ knowing that wasn't a decision made with safety as a priority. We continued to ZZZZ, our conscience bothering us, and had a couple more fumes events. The last being when we transferred the bleeds from the engines to the APU. Upon reaching ZZZZ, we contacted Medlink and relayed our symptoms. Symptoms of exposure were: headaches, burning eyes and throats, nausea, light headedness, and numbness in extremities. I was told by Medlink that my symptoms warranted medical attention and that mine weren't even the worst symptoms. Every person on my crew should call them. I was not comfortable with going to an emergency room in ZZZZ so I agreed to do subsequent phone evals with medlink and get back to anywhere in the US for evaluation. In the end, medlink released me, but recommended seeing my doctor at my earliest convenience. I was alarmed at the onset and severity of the symptoms. They did seem to ease fairly quickly after getting fresh air and more water, but never completely went away. Another concern outside of all of this was inflight leadership response. The conditions that two of them were suffering were far more severe than mine. But they were actually afraid to call medlink or get checked out because they spoke to two inflight supervisors. One of those two supervisors told our inflight: 'under no circumstance are you to contact medlink.' and 'you are only allowed to contact medlink if you are incapacitated.' In the end they contacted medlink after receiving guidance outside [company] inflight leadership that they should follow FAM (Flight Attendant Manual) guidance and 'take personal health seriously.' My biggest concerns are the reoccurring fumes events and the downplay by the company. Being pressured to continue the mission despite safety. Finally, feel of repercussions for taking one's own health into consideration.				A320 Captain reported an intermittent fume event resulting in physiological symptoms by entire crew. Reporter stated he felt pressured to continue to the destination in spite of cabin crew symptoms being more severe than flight crew. Entire crew received medical evaluation after flight.	
1702279	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			Mixed	5		Night	1500		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise; Parked		Class A ZZZ																								Fuel Crossfeed		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 468; Flight Crew Type 3000		Distraction; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1702279												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft; Chart Or Publication; Human Factors	Human Factors	Heavy traffic on way to airport caused Van Driver to drop us off at airport approximately 20 minutes late. We rushed to get to the gate and push as close to on time as possible. I accomplished flight duties while hearing from Captain that we had an MEL 28-X-XX. Right Center Tank fuel boost pump inoperative. I read the dispatch report as did the Captain. After push, and before takeoff, failed to recognize crossfeed valve in closed position (supposed to be open). Took off and at climbout Captain recognized that we had over 1000 pound imbalance from main tanks. We leveled off, referenced the QRH and accomplished applicable checklist. We shut off left center tank boost pump. Balanced mains and turned left center tank boost pump back on. Fuel initially fed from center tank but stopped feeding and started to feed from right main tank causing another slight imbalance. We failed to open up the crossfeed per the MEL which caused this to happen. At the time I was coordinating and programming for a diversion. Landed in ZZZ as a divert. Captain recognized the error after talking to Maintenance via telephone call.				B737-700 First Officer reported that distraction and time pressure caused the crew to miss a checklist item that resulted in a fuel imbalance and a diversion.	
1702293	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			250				Turbulence; Windshear		Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 522; Flight Crew Type 12000		Communication Breakdown; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1702293												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Weather; Company Policy	Human Factors	Upon receiving clearance to line up and wait on ZZZ XXR, ATC began relaying wind shear alerts at the departure end of our runway. We were already evaluating the weather cells off that same end. They appeared to be nothing more than heavy rain, similar to what we had experienced on our earlier arrival. The cells were a few miles east of us moving slowly to the southeast. Tower asked us if we were able to depart under the existing conditions. As a crew, we discussed the Tower wind shear alert and what might be a fairly clear path for our turnout if we elected to go. Based on the current situation we agreed that delaying our takeoff was the best course of action. I notified Tower and the Controller directed us to exit the runway. However the Controller then relayed that the previously detected wind shear had moved off of the field and out of our path. He went on to let us know that if we could accept a turn on departure to the north, then we should stay clear of any heavy precipitation on his radar. That agreed with what I was seeing visually so, with the FO's (First Officer) concurrence, I let him know we were ready for takeoff as long as he was able to clear us for an immediate turn. Tower then cleared us for takeoff with an immediate turn to heading 360.The timing of this entire discussion, from lining up to wait until being cleared for takeoff, was about five minutes. Though I believe the FO and I took the time needed to discuss our options and develop a shared mental model before departing, I think I failed to recognize that the FO felt a little more pressure than necessary to make the initial turn quickly to avoid the weather. On takeoff we executed the precautionary takeoff profile and began our climbout. At approximately 250 feet. AAE (Above Aerodrome Elevation), the FO rolled crisply to 30 degrees of bank, making the turn to the north. Though I was a little surprised, it was clear that she was initiating the turn earlier than our procedural minimum altitude of 400 feet AAE. She clearly had control of the aircraft and we were climbing away quickly.Under the circumstances I elected to not intervene, believing that doing so could lead to confusion and distraction during a critical phase of flight. We continued our climbout uneventfully. On reaching our cruising altitude, I brought the departure turn up with the FO. She confided in me that she was so focused on the initial turn to avoid the weather that she mistakenly started it early. Like me, she realized it almost immediately but elected to continue the turn as the safest course of action under the circumstances. We both agreed that waiting the extra 150 feet in compliance with our standard operating procedures would have still kept us well clear of the weather. This event shows that even two crew members who are fairly familiar with each other and comfortable communicating can miss important cues. Looking back I could have done a better job at managing our pace. If I had just taken another 30 seconds to review our plan and talk the takeoff through, it may have either put the FO more at ease or clued me in that she still had some concerns about the decision to depart.				B737-700 Captain reported First Officer made an early turn, prior to altitude limit, on departure.	
1702302	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US		4		2000					Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Flap/Slat Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 308		Confusion; Troubleshooting			1702302												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Manuals	Aircraft	This [report] is submitted in recognition of a scenario which is not addressed in the QRH - No LE device indicating lights upon landing configuration. This is not a one-off event since it occurred to another crew and aircraft last week. Situations were identical but crew analysis and actions were very different. No-flap landing for one and normal flap 30 landing for the other. Both flights landed uneventfully. Seems some QRH guidance here would be useful in future occurrences. An [incident report] also submitted to Company Flight Operations.On [flight], an instance of no LE device indication on approach caused Crew confusion because it's not addressed in the QRH. On initial approach into ZZZ, aircraft was configured for landing (gear down, flaps 30). All things appeared normal mechanically; however, we noticed no green LE FLAPS EXT light. Also no LE device annunciation panel lights. They were blank. No roll tendency was evident, press to test indicated operable LE device lights, circuit breakers in, and TE flap gauge indicated normally. Upon configuring everything appeared and felt normal. The only thing not normal was the absence of LE device lights.Conducted normal go-around to investigate and realized that this situation is not addressed in QRH. There is no discussion or guidance. Conducted a phone patch to Maintenance for enlightenment and Technician indicated that it was more than likely due to a PSEU/ FSEU fault which cannot be resolved during flight. Since this scenario is not addressed in the QRH, we had some discussion of no-flap or flap 15 landing but only because we were trying to make our situation 'fit' one of the QRH scenarios. Fortunately we had a jumpseat pilot in the cabin who could verify LE device and flap position for us. So those non-normal landing configurations were eventually ruled out because our situation did not warrant the increased risk. Using [resource management] by taking into account what we could feel and see, verification of LE and flap position with jumpseat pilot, and discussion with Maintenance, our issue appeared to be an indication problem, not an actual flight control problem. A normal configuration and landing into ZZZ ensued. PSEU light illuminated on landing. (This was due to the lack of info from the FSEU). ZZZ Contract Maintenance investigated and found the FSEU was at fault.				B737-700 Captain reported that a slat malfunction occurred for which there was no QRH procedure that resulted in a precautionary landing.	
1702312	201911	0601-1200	DCA.Tower	DC		2	800						Daylight			Tower DCA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B DCA																								Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 353; Flight Crew Type 4000		Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Workload; Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1702312												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		Y	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	The Captain was the Pilot Flying, on departure we received the Low Fuel Pressure lights, and due to this distraction he tried to deal with the alert instead of fly the aircraft. I told him he was drifting right and watch his course, he said that he was but he was slow in responding. I said to watch his course again and to go left; he eventually did but it looked as though we were very close to penetrating the prohibited airspace. Nothing was said after that and we continued on. I do feel that from the previous day and throughout the flight, this Captain was not prone to good CRM. He also seemed irritable in his tone and tenor; leaving me guessing how well he receives correction. I realize this Captain is a very new Captain and perhaps he was overloaded.I should have spoken up louder and more forcefully so that I was certain he heard me the first time I called out the deviation. I also feel that he should have had a better cockpit are and friendly environment from day one. His attitude and irritability should have been more open to Crew communication.				B737-700 First Officer reported that workload and troubleshooting on departure resulted in a course deviation.	
1702354	201911		ZZZ.Airport	US			0										Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 190; Flight Crew Total 285; Flight Crew Type 285		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch; Party2 Ground Personnel		1702354												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	Our first indication of the presence of an electric wheelchair on board was when during descent the purser asked us to give the destination station a heads-up. Neither form nor verbal notification was provided by station personnel.				A320 Captain reported they were not given any documentation for an electric wheelchair loaded in cargo. Flight crew became aware of the during descent to destination.	
1702430	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Fuel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Troubleshooting; Confusion			1702430	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Confusion; Troubleshooting			1702436	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	MEL; Aircraft; Procedure	Ambiguous	Dispatched on Aircraft X with 3 MEL items; 25-XX, 28-XX, and 28-XXA. I reviewed the MELs in OPS. Per 28-XX knew to look for a fueling slip and a form XXXXX, though I was not familiar with that form. Once at the aircraft during preflight we did receive both forms from different fuelers. Release was 14,612, fuel slip showed a total of 14,800 lbs. At the gate the #1 tank showed 7.40, the center 0.00, the #2 tank was blank, and the totalizer was blank. Spending some time attempting to understand the form XXXXX, I discerned it was our aircraft and date, and the stuck tank measured to an equivalent volume of 7400 lbs. So it looked we had a balanced load of empty center and 7400 lbs in each wing tank. I filled in 14.8 in the fuel block on the INIT REF page, as that block was blank.During pushback and engine start the ground crew reported a fuel leak from the #2 engine. I had him stay connected during the five minute warm up period. By the end of the 5 minutes the leak stopped. Taxi, takeoff, climb, and cruise were normal. ATC gave us 2 shortcuts, one direct ZZZZZ after ZZZZZ1, then later direct ZZZZZ2. Fuel check at ZZZZZ was right on. I subsequently did the MEL recommended fuel check using fuel used. That showed 2.7 used by each engine (5.4 total). Deducted from the starting fuel of 14.8 gave us 9.4. Just after that we passed abeam ZZZZZ3, where I recorded 9.2, compared to the MISC 14 prediction of 9.5.Shortly thereafter the #2 tank FWD pump LOW PRESSURE light began to flash. I pointed this out to the FO (First Officer) and commented something like 'well that's not right!' then 'well now why would that be happening?' Subsequently that light came on steady, followed by the AFT LOW PRESSURE light beginning to flash. I told the FO we had a big problem and that we needed to consider that the only fuel we might have available was the #1 tank fuel, as I pointed to the left quantity. We agreed, and at that point the #2 AFT LP light came on steady, both MC lights came on, and the #2 engine began rolling back.At this point it appeared we had a right engine failure. We ensured the FO had the aircraft, he clicked off the automation and managed the yaw and loss of thrust. I reached up and opened the fuel crossfeed. Our estimate was that N1 decreased to about 20-30 %. After a very mild audible and tactile vibration, the right engine began to recover, increasing N1 and N2 and returning to normal thrust and indications. The FO and I agreed on the diversion to ZZZ which was ahead at 11:30 clock code and by then about 100 miles ahead. I [spoke with] ATC and told them we were diverting to ZZZ. I had been to ZZZ before and knew the station had [company] maintenance. I asked for a series of right 360s to descend. ATC said they had traffic over there, asked if we could take a left vector heading to absorb the altitude loss and I said yes. The FO continued to manage energy while flying the aircraft. From there it was the normal ACARS MISC [message], backing up the FO as he deftly flew the aircraft and got us down expeditiously, running regular checklists, completing non-routine landing consideration tasks, talking to the #1 FA (Flight Attendant), making PAs, etc. The FO flew a flawless visual approach and spectacular touchdown, backed up by the ILS, to RW XXL.After landing on RW 1XXL and clearing the Runway I asked the ARFF commander if he saw any fuel leaking from the right wing or engine and he said no. Proceeded to gate XX. On the gate the indicating fuel gauges showed 3.52 in the #1 tank, 0.00 in the center tank. No other fuel light had ever annunciated (e.g. 'LOW' or 'IMBAL'). Maintenance was on the jet bridge, boarded on our arrival and we began the conversation, relating the details of what we saw over time. We agreed with their plan to stick the right wing, look for fuel leaks, talk to [Maintenance Control], and eventually refuel to a known quantity and stick again. The fueler reported the #2 wing tank was 'bone dry'. We discussedpossible causes of our symptoms: a fuel leak, bad fuel, fuel sticking inconsistencies. They began their work, saying eventually they would be doing an engine start and run to look for leaks, all of which would take some time.In speaking with dispatch to determine a rough fuel load that maintenance could use in their work, [I was asked] to speak to a maintenance manager who was there. The manager asked for a recap, what lights we had, what paperwork we had received in ZZZ1, what RPM the #2 had spooled down to, and asked if we could send him copies of the fuel slip and form XXXXX. I later had the FO do that. ZZZ maintenance continued to work with us. They reported there was no placard out on the refueling panel for the 28-XXa.I made periodic PAs to the passengers. The #1 FA indicated there were some passengers that wanted to deplane. I went up to the gate to retrieve an agent. We discussed the options and mutually decided to deplane everyone. Back on the aircraft calls with dispatch resulted in us learning the aircraft was taken OTS. 3 AML entries were made reporting the fuel tank LOW PRESSURE lights and subsequent rollback with empty #2 tank upon arrival at gate, the engine rollback, and the pulled CVR CB.We began to get texts from ZZZ1 Chief Pilots supporting us and offering assistance.		Flight X from ZZZ1 to ZZZ2 departed the Gate [a few] minutes ahead of schedule after having spent the night at the airport. While familiarizing and setting up for the preflight I noticed two of the three MEL's on the release were for fueling which showed the right gauge blank from the flight deck and a discrepancy on the fueling panel quantity indicator (option 2). While walking around the plane there were three men consulting on the fueling as this required a non-normal fueling procedure. After walk around completion, fuelers came to the flight deck and handed two pieces of paper to the Captain: a form and a Jet Fuel Service Record showing 7,400 pounds of fuel on each side of the aircraft (14,800 total). Sufficient fuel complying with the release (14,612 lbs.) After pushing back, the ground crew noticed fuel dripping from the right engine. Starting both engines with a follow up wait period of five minutes the drip had disappeared and we continued on. It was a normal takeoff on a good VFR weather day and the light load allowed us to climb quickly to flight level 390. Approximately 40 minutes into the flight the forward Number Two Fuel Pump Low Pressure light illuminated which caught our attention perhaps a minute later the aft number two fuel pump came on also. There was a brief pause when the N1 on the number two engine began to roll back. The Captain (non-flying pilot) quickly selected the fuel cross feed to open and the engine continued to operate normally. There was a momentary slight yaw and audible change discernible to the passengers as well. I turned off the autopilot. After assessing we may now only have the fuel showing on the left side, getting on the ground as soon as possible became the priority. The Captain [requested priority handling] and chose ZZZ as our new destination. The Captain was quite busy running checklists, informing the Flight Attendants and making PA's, in addition to monitoring pilot tasks. With so much altitude to lose we got vectors from the air traffic controllers for the descent and then lined up visually for Runway XXL and used the ILS for guidance. After I made a normal landing, the ARFF standing by the runway then cleared the aircraft of showing any fuel visibly leaking at the Captain's request. We continued taxiing to the gate where maintenance met the aircraft and after completing our shutdown checklist, they joined us on the flight deck while the passengers deplaned. Their measurement of the number two fuel tank showed it completely empty and 3,520 pounds remaining in the number one. We then began relaying information to dispatch, chief pilots, and union aids.For whatever reason the right fuel tank was not correctly measured and the information we received was false. We thought we had sufficient fuel but because of the absent fuel indicator for the right side tank and the belief that the tank had been fueled and measured properly we were wrong.The easiest correction would be to have the MEL address and corrected earlier, after that I would recommend when there is a question of fuel load that both sides of the aircraft be measured using the stick method.		B737 flight crew was dispatched with several MELs regarding fuel system. During flight crew received several fuel warning lights and the right engine rolled back. Crew diverted, landed safely, and learned their right wing fuel tank was empty.	
1702837	201911	0001-0600	ZZZ.TRACON	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload			1702837												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Make this a memory item. Too much going on trying to talk to crew, ATC, run checklists etc., while dealing with the O2 mask distraction while Flight Attendants and passengers are inhaling toxic fumes. O2 on, PACKS OFF, RAM AIR on.				A320 Captain reported a fume event during a busy initial approach phase.	
1703477	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US		100		15000		Mixed	Rain; Thunderstorm; Turbulence		Dusk			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Weather Radar		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 366; Flight Crew Type 2900		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1703477												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	Aircraft In Service At Gate; In-flight; Pre-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure; Weather	Procedure	We arrived at the aircraft for our flight to find the ramp closed due to heavy rain and lightning at the field. After a few minutes the lightning moved off the field and the ramp re-opened, but it was still raining. I went to the aircraft and found the Main Cabin door and cockpit door were left open during the heavy rain, resulting in water in the cockpit. When the Captain arrived we discussed and decided to have Ops bring us some absorbent pads to clean it up. With the cockpit floor now dry again we performed a normal preflight and departed uneventfully. Upon passing about 15,000 feet MSL we turned on the radar because of storms up ahead. The radar then failed in all positions (Manual and Auto, both sides). We discussed between ourselves and decided that continuing onto the oceanic tracks at night without radar, with storms in the area to be a bad idea. We had decided we would probably return to ZZZ, but would discuss with Dispatch prior to commencing the return. The Captain then attempted to make a call to Dispatch on SATCOM 2, only to have it fail as well. At that point we theorized that the water we found in the flight deck upon arriving at the aircraft might have leaked down into the floor and to the [Company] bay, causing shorts. The Captain was eventually able to get through to Dispatch on SATCOM 1, and all this information was relayed. After about a 15 minute call with Dispatch and Maintenance Control, it was decided to return to ZZZ. We started the diversion and ATC was notified. When we started the descent, the aft FAs (flight attendants) called to tell us that they smelled 'something burning' in the aft galley, but could not locate it. At that point the Captain took the aircraft and requested priority handling. I took the QRH and ran the Smoke or Fumes in the Cabin QRH Checklist. We had enough time and were able to complete the entire QRH and all the required items before landing, pertinent to the diversion. About 10 minutes after the initial call the FAs called us to tell us that the smell had dissipated and they were never able to locate the source. We landed on Runway 8R and stopped on the runway to have airport rescue and firefighting in ZZZ confirm that they didn't see anything on the outside of the aircraft. After a successful inspection from airport rescue and firefighting we taxied to the gate and deplaned the Passengers.				B737-800 flight crew reported water in the flight deck due to L1 door and flight deck door being left open during a severe rain squall.	
1703492	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			0				Rain; Snow		Night				Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Air Carrier	Other / Unknown	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1703492	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 247; Flight Crew Total 4538; Flight Crew Type 758					1703494	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Passenger	Taxi	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Last night I boarded flight X, a B777, flying from ZZZ to ZZZ1. While waiting for the boarding process to begin I noted the weather. The conditions at the departure airport were wintery. There was light snow falling prior to boarding. Earlier in the evening there had been periods of light rain. I noted that this would likely increase the trip time due to de-icing. I boarded the flight and took my seat [in the cabin]. Of note, this seat is located just forward of the left wing. I opened my window shades to see how much snow had accumulated on the wings to get some idea of how long the de-icing operation was going to take. I took a photo on my personal cell phone (attached) and sent it to my wife with a note (attached) that my arrival would likely be later than expected due to the need to de-ice the aircraft.Once the door was closed we pushed back as normal. The engines were started, and we began our taxi from gate. I sat back in my seat and tried to unwind from a long day of line flying. As we exited the ramp area I anticipated that we would be headed to the De-ice pad based on our direction of travel. I directed my attention to my personal tablet trying to find a movie to watch for the flight. After a couple of minutes, I looked outside again noting that we were taxiing at a fast pace and then realized we had passed the de-ice pad and were headed for RWY XX. At this point I realized the crew was about to depart without de-icing the aircraft. Reaching RWY XX we immediately entered the runway and began the takeoff roll. I knew there was nothing I could do at this point other than to document what I was seeing.I watched the rotation through my window noting that some of the snow was being blown off the wing and engine nacelle but much of it remained after liftoff especially on the leading-edge devices. We had, by now, passed the most critical part of the takeoff but I was so shocked that this had occurred it took me several minutes to consider what steps I should now take. I had considered contacting the crew in flight using the cabin interphone but decided that there was nothing to gain by doing so and I didn't want to alarm the cabin crew or passengers. I decided that I would email some of the managers and staff supervisors, as well as the Chief Pilot office in ZZZ1. During the roll-out I turned off my airplane mode and began to text the Operations and Maintenance Control managers. I again received no reply. Once we parked at the gate I decided to go to the flight deck and talk with the crew. It took several minutes for a clear path to develop. Once I had a clear path to the flight deck I went forward to talk with the crew. Upon reaching the flight deck only the First Officer was on board. The Captain had already left the aircraft. I introduced myself, handed the FO (First Officer) my business card and began to describe what I witnessed. I showed the FO the photos on my phone and advised him to file [a report]. I also told him that it was my responsibility to say something and that I would be reporting the event to the Chief Pilot. During our conversation the FO told me that during the walk-around one of the ground personnel had told him they had already 'squirted' the airplane. I found this difficult to believe since that is not the procedure in ZZZ.After my conversation with the First Officer I was able to talk with the the Operations Control manager. He advised me to talk with the crew, which I had already done, and to let them know that the Chief Pilot office would be contacting them, again something I had already done.I believe I have fully captured the events in this report from my point of view, but if you would like to discuss this further feel free to contact me.		An hour and fifteen minutes before takeoff, I showed at gate in ZZZ for my flight to ZZZ1. I began the normal First Officer cockpit flows and loading procedures. At the time, a mix of snow and rain was falling. The Captain showed shortly after me, and we discussed the weather. After the FMC had been loaded and ACARS initialized, I began to print the pertinent information from ACARS and the printer ran out of paper. Both the Captain and I tried to load new paper to no avail. I called maintenance to ask for help loading the paper and added that the flight attendant needed help with a cart which had locked wheels. A few minutes later, the Captain got the paper properly loaded. I left the aircraft to do the walk-around inspection.I did a thorough pre-flight in accordance with the FM (Flight Manual) and FOM (Flight Operations Manual) noting the wings and surfaces were clear of any adhering precipitation, which had turned to all rain by then. As I was leaving the area around engine #1, two ground crew personnel arrived. I introduced myself, and one of the men said he was with de-ice and that they had 'shot the wings' before we showed up at the aircraft and that everything looked, 'good to go.' The other man nodded his head in agreement, and I shook their hands and went back to the cockpit. I reported to the Captain my observations, and that I had discussed the aircraft condition with the de-ice personnel. The Captain added that he had been observing the ground equipment parked in close proximity and that they were clear of any precipitation. We pulled up the ATIS and the temperature was 34 degrees Fahrenheit. I then pulled up takeoff data that included 'wet' and 'anti-ice ON' numbers. After I briefed the planned takeoff data to the Captain, he went out to the jetway and observed the wing. When he returned he said things, 'looked good.'About that time, a maintenance technician introduced himself and entered the cockpit. He had fixed the cart and wanted to make sure we were able to load the printer paper. We spoke for several minutes and towards the end of the conversation, the Captain asked the maintenance technician how the jet looked to him on the outside. He said the jet was clear, and then left. While the Captain and maintenance technician were talking, I was monitoring the de-ice frequency to see what kind of activity was going on in the pad. I listened for several minutes and didn't hear any calls. After that, a customer service representative arrived at the cockpit and said we were waiting on one passenger, but that we wouldn't delay the departure for him. While we waited, the Captain and I continued to discuss the weather and our departure plan. The plan was for a reduced power, flaps 5 takeoff, wet runway numbers and anti-ice on because the ramp was wet. We updated the ATIS and the temperature was still 34. Since the temperature was above freezing and the precipitation had stopped, the Captain made the decision to taxi out normally. When I set the gate metering frequency in the radio, I could hear other aircraft calling and metering asking if they would require de-icing. About half said yes and half said negative. (This is an approximation on my part). I called metering and then ramp control and we taxied out and took off with anti-ice on. Taxi, takeoff, en route and landing were uneventful. After landing, we completed our parking checklist and sent the 'in' report. The Captain gathered his belongings and left. I stayed slightly after getting my stuff together and waiting for ground power to shut off the APU. After some time, still with no ground power, I decided to leave the aircraft. As I got to the door, a gentleman was in the hall between door 1L and the bathroom. I said, 'Hi,' and he turned and asked if I had been flying this aircraft. I said that I had been, and he said that he was sitting in the back on the flight and was scared on takeoff because we hadn't de-iced. I extended my hand and introduced myself because I couldn't see his company badge, but I could see his lanyard. He introduced himself and said he was a standards captain and worked at the training center and that he was very surprised that we hadn't de-iced before takeoff. I said the wings were clear on the walk around and he continued talking and said he had a picture of the wing; he tilted his phone towards me, but I couldn't see the picture. At this point I was concerned with the tone of the conversation and so I asked if he had spoken with the Captain. He said he had not. I was surprised at this because the Captain had just left the aircraft. He told me that I needed to call the Captain immediately and tell him that we needed to file a report and call the union. I told him that I had never filed a report before. (I was extremely flustered at this point and had forgotten that I have filed one before, but I'm not sure what the date was). He then said that I needed to talk to the Captain and to file one, but he did not elaborate as to why I should call the union. He said he was sorry and as I was feeling extremely uncomfortable at this point, I gathered my belongings and left the aircraft.		B777 First Officer and a Captain passenger reported being confronted by a fellow Captain flying as passenger regarding deicing.	
1703528	201911	1201-1800	IAH.Tower	TX				3000		IMC						Tower IAH	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B IAH																												Tower IAH	Air Carrier	Heavy Transport			Part 121				Climb		Class B IAH																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 197; Flight Crew Type 267					1703528												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	General Maintenance Action	Procedure	Procedure	Climbing out, Flight Attendant called at approximately 3,000 feet and reported a metallic sound hit the aircraft on takeoff and climbout. The takeoff itself was uneventful from the flight deck and no threat posed by the attitude at rotation, etc. However, we departed behind a heavy aircraft and once airborne experienced a lateral shifting of the aircraft in wake vortices, which may have shifted cargo. Flight control and flight response were normal throughout the flight as was systems integrity. I mentioned the Flight Attendant report to Departure Control, so that they might warn the Tower for a possible FOD inspection of the runway. Once at destination, a walk around was performed and there were no issues with the aircraft, no damage whatsoever.				B777 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence behind a Heavy aircraft during departure from IAH airport.	
1703627	201911	1201-1800	ZAB.ARTCC	NM												Center ZAB; TRACON P50	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B PHX																												TRACON P50	Air Carrier	A321		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B PHX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1703627												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	On arrival into PHX, 6 miles in trail of an A321. Started getting violent rapid side loading, banking, and minor airspeed fluctuations. Normally, we go in and then out of wake turbulence before we have a chance to react, but this was sustained. I describe it as violent but not obscene, for example the bank changes, side loading and airspeed fluctuations were uncontrollable, but limited to +/-7 degrees of bank, and +/- 4 knots on the airspeed. No way to tell side loading. As we reentered it for the third time, we told ATC we needed a half mile offset. ATC complied, and then asked us if we could rejoin the arrival. When they asked, we hit another bit of wake turbulence and said no. They gave us vectors until final, where we were 4 and a half miles in trail again. We flew one dot high and then shifted to a normal glideslope and landed uneventfully. Captain remarked that he had not felt wake turbulence like that in years and felt like we were following a 757.Close trail vectoring, and zero crosswind component. Winds were calm at altitude.It seems that spacing is going to a mileage restriction only, instead of a timing and distance based one. However, [with] airliners flying at 210-250 KIAS on the arrival, 6 miles can be as little as 1 minute, depending on tailwinds. Recommend going back to a combination of time and distance based restrictions for in trail spacing.	Reporter recommended returning to time and distance based in-trail techniques instead of distance only.			B737-800 First Officer reported encountering wake turbulence in trail of an A321 on arrival into PHX.	
1703644	201911	1801-2400	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO						VMC						Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Climb																										Hydraulic Syst Pressure/Temp Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1703644												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climbout received a HYD QTY LOW R EICAS alert approximately FL140. Subsequently received a HYD PRESS PRI R and then a HYD PRESS SYS R alert and Caution. Complied with all checklist items. After discussion with Captain, [Operations] and [Maintenance Control] elected to continue to destination. Did not declare an Emergency however requested to have emergency equipment standing by to inspect landing gear for potential fire from remaining fluid dripping on brakes. Aircraft made a normal flaps 30 landing using speed derived from the QRH. Captain assumed PF (Pilot Flying) duties prior to landing and I became PM (Pilot Monitoring). Aircraft cleared the runway and stopped to complete the inspection by airport personnel. After completion taxied unassisted to assigned gate.				B777 First Officer reported experiencing an in-flight hydraulic system failure.	
1704266	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Aeroplane Flight Control		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 464		Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness; Distraction; Confusion			1704266	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 489; Flight Crew Type 1029		Confusion; Distraction; Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness			1704282	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were on our first flight of the trip and in cruise at 38,000 feet. I was the Pilot Flying and the First Officer was the Pilot Monitoring. Approximately 1:30 minutes into the flight, I detected a faint odor that initially I thought might be from the air system. I mentioned this to the First Officer and then noticed smoke coming from the trip counter on my control wheel. The First Officer saw it too and we immediately put on our oxygen masks and smoke goggles and followed the QRC procedure of establishing communications. We followed the QRC which led us to the QRH. We followed the checklist for smoke, fire, fumes, and I also turned the MAIN PANEL brightness rheostat to off. At that point, the smoke had dissipated and no more smoke was emanating from the trip ID counter.We were able to determine the source, and since the smoke was no longer present, we elected to remove our oxygen masks and smoke goggles to more easily communicate and coordinate our divert. Given our location, we determined which airport was our best place to divert to. We advised with Center and were cleared direct to the airport. We were approximately 150 miles from the airport at that point. We notified Dispatch of the situation, and the First Officer informed the flight attendants of the situation. We told them to secure the cabin and also informed the passengers of the situation and our diverting to ZZZ. The landing was uneventful and no additional smoke was ever noticed. We taxied to the gate and the passengers deplaned normally. One thing a lot of us are guilty of is tapping the trip counter if the backlight is not working. This could unknowingly damage the wiring inside and cause a short which is possibly the cause of the smoke we experienced.		While at cruise altitude, the Captain's flight control wheel started to emit smoke and heat from the flight trip counter mounted in the upper right portion of the control wheel. We both donned our oxygen masks and goggles. The Captain then turned down the backlighting on his side via the rheostat knob just below his glare shield. The smoke appeared to dissipate. After completing the smoke fire or fumes QRC, we then completed the smoke fires or fumes QRH. As a measure of precaution in the event the smoke returned or a fire would start, we diverted to ZZZ. It was approximately 160 miles almost directly off of our nose. Given our altitude, the good weather, and knowledge of emergency services available, it was the best course of action. We discussed the situation with the flight attendants and had them clean up and take their seats. The Captain made an announcement to the passengers once we were safely on our descent. The smoke did not return and we landed uneventfully.		B737-700 flight crew reported advising ATC and diverting due to smoke in the cockpit during cruise.	
1704290	201911	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			Mixed			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Parked; Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Communication Systems		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 383; Flight Crew Type 20000					1704290												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	Incorrect van schedule caused pilots to show at aircraft about ten minutes late. Rain showers were present at the field. Aircraft was in position at gate with passenger ramp in-place. Crew Entry Door open. Significant standing water was present in forward galley and on cockpit floor. Water was thoroughly removed. Passing about 10,300 ft, the FO (First Officer), PF (Pilot Flying), attempted to turn on the Weather RADAR for thunderstorms en route. WXR FAIL appeared on both NDs. Attempted AUTO, MAN and MAP modes with System Control in NORM and TEST on both sides with no change. Circuit breakers were normal. Requested and received weather avoidance vectors from HCF Departure and Center. We experienced a simultaneous failure of SATCOM2. Call light 'C' was illuminated and could not be extinguished.An attempt to call Dispatch on SATCOM 2 failed. Contacted Dispatch through SATCOM 1 for external resources. Coordinated with Dispatch, Maintenance Control and Center to return to ZZZ. Referenced Diversion Information. Planned an overweight landing. On initial descent, 'B' FA called with 2 rings to report an unknown, burning odor in the aft cabin. Agreed to further access and report. 'B' and 'D' FAs called back approximately TWO minutes later, confirmed the odor, but no signs of abnormal heat, smoke or flame. We transferred aircraft control: Captain-PF; FO-PM (Pilot Monitoring), requested priority handling and ran QRC/QRH, 'Smoke, Fire or Fumes.' Conducted a visual approach to Runway XR. Stopped on the runway and got an external assessment from Airport Rescue and Firefighting. FAs reported odor had dissipated.				B737-800 Captain reported uneventful air return due to multiple avionics component failures accompanied by 'burning odor.'	
1704889	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				18000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1704889	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying							1704900	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were operating as (flight) from ZZZ1-ZZZ. While on descent into ZZZ through approximately 18,000 feet we noticed an odor once we entered visible moisture. The odor persisted while on descent but once we were out of the visible moisture, the odor lessened.   Upon arrival to the gate and after engine shutdown, one of the aft Flight Attendants called to report that the odor was very strong in the aft of the aircraft.   A logbook entry was made and Maintenance was called. I also spoke with the [Duty Manager] and made a report to him regarding the cabin odor.   After the aircraft was deplaned, the flight attendants and I felt some symptoms and we spoke with Medlink. After conferring with Medlink, we decided to go to the hospital for evaluation and treatment. During cruise, we had a Lavatory Smoke warning which only lasted a few seconds. The flight attendants performed an inspection of all three lavatories and found no evidence of smoke or fumes in any of the lavatories.		[Report narrative added no additional information.]		A320 flight crew reported a fume event during arrival descent. Entire flight crew received medical evaluation and treatment at local hospital.	
1705121	201911		LLBG.Airport	FO				5000								Center LLLL	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Climb	Direct																									GPS & Other Satellite Navigation		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 195; Flight Crew Total 24000; Flight Crew Type 8694					1705121												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Environment - Non Weather Related	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	Departing TLV, starting at 5,000 feet, our GPS was jammed until well into the Nicosia FIR. We complied with all SOP.				B777 Captain reported GPS jamming during climbout from LLBG airport at 5,000 feet.	
1705207	201911	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			0			VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise			Y	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing																		Cockpit Door		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1705207												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Security			Person Flight Crew	Pre-flight	General None Reported / Taken	MEL; Aircraft; Procedure	MEL	MEL 52-XX was in effect. After reviewing the MEL my First Officer and I noted that due to the cockpit door electronic lock being deferred that we had to use the deadbolt in the key inoperable position. The problem with this MEL is that the flight deck door cannot in an emergency be opened from the cabin. This left me with concerns that though are remote, the possibility exists that in an accident if both pilots are incapacitated they could be trapped for a period of time in the flightdeck. FAs would not be able to access the flightdeck in any way to help incapacitated pilots, nor would fire rescue with the deadbolt engaged. The only way would be through the flightdeck windows by fire rescue. The flightdeck door is a good design, however, with the electronic keypad being INOP, and with the flightdeck keyhole being purposely blocked by a metal plate on all B737 aircraft, the deadbolt in the key operable position is no longer a possibility. If the deadbolt in the key operable position were possible then FAs would be able to use a flightdeck key to override the deadbolt and open the flightdeck door in the event of pilot incapacitation. This MEL 52-XX with the deadbolt engaged does not allow for a timely response from FAs nor fire rescue to the flightdeck in an emergency.MEL 52-XX should be a non-deferrable MEL with the deadbolt in the key inoperable position. Or the key operable solution must be restored.				Captain reported disagreement with MEL52-XX being accurate.	
1705214	201911	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO												Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other; Other / Unknown			1705214	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1705210	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Human Factors	Human Factors	After my break I returned to the cockpit and advised the [Relief Pilot] who was relieving the First Pilot that I felt like I was running a fever, which I most certainly was. I felt it was either a UTI, or bladder infection. Onset was rapid, and decline was rapid. I realized the safest course of action was to have the FO's (First Officer) make the landing. The FO's contacted ATC and Dispatch and [requested priority handling]. Sudden onset of illness, resulting in high fever. Hydrate more. Three 3-day trips in a row with one day off between each. Most certainly I have been running dehydrated.		Captain was Pilot Flying. After his middle break, he said he had shivers and wasn't feeling great. I went on last break. When I came back after break, Captain was still not feeling well, looked red in the face, and the [Relief Pilot] said they had talked and decided [that] since [the] Captain did not seem 100% coherent, [it would be] best if he sat in [the] jumpseat during approach. As a crew, we decided I would fly the approach from the FO (First Officer) seat, [and the Relief Pilot] would sit in [the] Captain's seat. Requested priority handling with Approach. Briefed the Purser. Landed without a problem. Taxied to gate. Met by paramedics.		B777 flight crew reported the Captain's sudden onset of illness during flight resulted in the First Officer and Relief Pilot assuming flying duties that led to an uneventful landing.	
1705308	201911	1801-2400	SCT.TRACON	CA				5800		VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1705308												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Flying westbound from JWARD intersection, east of mountains just to the west of SNA, descending into SNA on the DSNEE4 arrival, cleared for the visual Runway 20R at SNA, FO (Flight Officer) flying pilot. Got terrain terrain, pull up, pull up at 5800 ft. MSL. Complied with warning, then normal approach and landing into SNA.Cause: Neither pilot thought we were low. Had mountains in sight. FO's first time into SNA, first time on DSNEE4 arrival contributed to lack of awareness. Captain briefed prior that SOCAL approach will keep airplane high on profile making it difficult to descend into SNA on a normal profile. This brief probably contributed to FO wanting to descend early. Captain had mountains in sight and thought clearance was going to be OK. Suggestions: Don't clear airplanes for visual into SNA when airplane is still east of 5600 ft. mountains east of SNA. Also make chart say keep 7600 ft. until west of mountains.				A320-200 Captain reported GPWS terrain warning activation on descent into destination in mountainous environment.	
1705320	201911	1801-2400	NCT.TRACON	CA				4500		VMC						TRACON NCT	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	SID TEKKY5	Class C SJC																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Confusion			1705320												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Airspace Structure; Chart Or Publication; Manuals	Chart Or Publication	We were filed on the BMRNG SID by Dispatch. However, the active runway changed to Runway 30R, and ATC cleared us via the TEKKY 5 to TEKKY, direct SJC, then BMRNG, flight plan route, maintain 5000 ft. When we were cleared for takeoff, ATC advised us to climb and maintain 4500 ft. Thought this was odd. The TEKKY Sid shows a maximum speed of 230 kts. at the first turn point. However this may be under the SFO Class B airspace. According to the ATC liaison, we cannot exceed 200 kts. We may not fly at our minimum maneuvering speed below the Class B. This needs clarification. The SID is apparently constructed for airspace compliance and track, not for flight below Class B airspace. We need to publish guidance for our pilots on what speed they need to fly and until what point.				An Air Carrier pilot reported a SID speed which is published at 230 kts even though the route is below Class B Airspace causes confusion.	
1705487	201911	0601-1200	EWR.Airport	NJ				6000			Turbulence					TRACON N90	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb	SID NEWARK FOUR	Class B EWR																								Autoflight System		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1705487	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying							1705506	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated	Procedure; Aircraft	Aircraft	We were given a climb to 14,000 feet after level off at 6,000 feet where we were in constant light/moderate turbulence. Controller asked us to expedite climb to 14,000 for traffic. I open climb that gave Thrust Climb/Open Climb in the FMA. When aircraft had approximately 1,000 FPM climb, I put thrust levers in FLX/MCT (Maximum Continuous Thrust) to give the aircraft more thrust to expedite. The aircraft with the autopilot on lagged in pitching up to the flight director and airspeed jumped from 250 to 270. I opted for selected speed and put 230 knots to initiate a faster climb rate. Speed was exceeded for approximately 30 seconds.		After departing EWR 22R on the EWR4 SID, we leveled off at 6,000. We were then given an additional climb with the exception to expedite the climb. At this point the, Pilot Flying (Captain) advanced the thrust levers to MCT. The airspeed started to rise above 250 knots, however, the aircraft was very slow to become fully involved in the climb. Momentarily, the airspeed indicated approximately, 270 knots indicated. The Captain allowed the automation to make the proper corrections.		A320 crew reported exceeding 250 knots leaving 6,000 feet during turbulence departing EWR citing inadequate autoflight system response to pilot inputs.	
1705551	201911	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				11900		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A330		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B ZZZ																								Air Conditioning Compressor		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 27000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance; Party2 Other		1705551	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1705796	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On descent through FL190, a distinct dirty sock odor was detected by both pilots. Oxygen masks donned. 7700 squawked and [advised] with ATC. Reason given for [advising]. Emergency equipment followed to gate, no external issues found. Normal shutdown checklist completed. Report filed by pilots. Confirmation of submission. Mechanic briefed. Mechanic informed me flight attendants reported to him the dirty sock odor was evident after gear retraction in ZZZ. I asked the mechanic for the AML back to update my write up. He refused stating he would advise the Maintenance Manager. He did not as evidenced by the narrative on [reporting website]. I followed up with the [point of contact] in Maintenance Control. He said he would accurately update my briefing in the comments section. He requested I refile my report and I did. He confirmed receipt. The On Duty pilot called to check on crew. ZZZ1 Director called me to check on crew also. ZZZ line mechanic inaccuracies in report narrative is an injustice to all parties involved and a negative aspect to safety in so far as correcting issues.		After a shallow descent was started, pulled for open descent to comply with STAR constraints, engines came to idle, and a strong 'dirty sock' smell was observed in the cockpit. The Captain and I agreed it was a fume event associated with atomized engine oil from our training lessons. We donned O2 masks, I was the Pilot Flying and took control of the Aircraft. Directed ECAM ACTION and the Captain performed the appropriate checklists while I flew the Aircraft and talked to ATC, and landed in ZZZ without any mishaps. Emergency vehicles followed us to the gate and boarded the aircraft and investigated the event. Suspected engine oil seal leaking allowing atomized oil to enter the aircraft pack system.		A330 flight crew reported a fume event during descent, advised ATC, and emergency vehicles followed the aircraft to the gate. Captain also experienced a communication issue with the mechanic regarding the logbook write up.	
1706031	201911	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				700					Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 246; Flight Crew Type 5000		Communication Breakdown; Fatigue; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1706031												Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Beautiful night, last leg into ZZZ, slightly high but configured early with flaps 5 and gear down. Announced and selected V/S to gently get down glidepath for smoothness. RNAV XXL was set up and we were getting vectored for the visual. Airport in sight since ZZZ1. Autopilot on. Good rapport with FO, felt like an easy-peezy, no brainer, in-the-Green arrival. We were cleared to descend to 1,600 feet.1. A few miles from the final approach we were cleared the visual. I announced 'LNAV to XXXXX,' executed direct to the FAF and let the autopilot get us going that way. Kept in V/S. 2. HUD was up and in use.3. We were slightly high so I left 950 fpm descent rate in V/S knowing it would intersect the 3 degree slope near 1,000 feet. We were fully configured a bit early, this was just an attempt to be smooth and require the fewest power changes.4. Disengaged the autopilot at probably 1,400 feet and started following HUD guidance. Little circle in the big circle. It could have been a simulator with turbulence set to 0 for how little control action was required. 5. '1,000 feet, Speed 135, sinking 9.' Little circle in the big circle.6. At some point before the 500 foot call, it dawned on me, ALL FOUR PAPI's had gone red!!! I immediately added power and got back on PAPI and the purple path (VNAV). I never pressed VNAV. The magenta indicator was there but I was on that guidance cue, blindly following V/S into the black below.Obviously I screwed up. We screwed up. I will be changing my own procedure to never use V/S for this purpose on final. It was an eye opener. Would the aircraft have sounded any Warning had we continued? I realize it can't warn for everything, but why not pick the low hanging fruit. It 'knows' where the runway is, it could easily report the unsafe condition. I'll fix my own procedure but let's collectively, as an industry, go to the next step. The was the end of a LONG day, so fatigue was a factor, but this was a super low-stress easy approach. Why didn't the FO say anything? Don't know. He asked the same question when we talked about it afterwards. I've NEVER DONE THIS BEFORE. But the more I thought about it the more it made me think. What if the VASI wasn't there or was inop? We could have easily still done the approach. How much lower would we have gone in a black-hole approach? It was probably the ease of the approach, the mundaneness of it, combined with fatigue, that allowed it to happen. I'd like to propose EGPWS get more E. If this can happen to me, it can happen to someone else. The airplane 'knows' its position relative to the runway in nearly all cases now. If the airplane is in close, within 5 miles, have it report 1) too Low, vertical path, 2) too fast, 3) check runway, and so on.   WE CAN FIX THIS.				Captain reported selecting the V/S mode rather than VNAV which resulted in the aircraft descending well below glidepath on final approach at night. Upon recognizing the runway PAPI indications, the crew climbed back up to glidepath.	
1706508	201912		ZZZ.Airport	US			0										Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 178.08; Flight Crew Total 1986.77; Flight Crew Type 1986.77		Physiological - Other			1706508	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Physiological - Other			1706498	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight; Taxi	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We started both engines and turned off the APU. Upon taxi, I noticed an acrid/dirty socks/musty smell when I added power. As I backed off the power on taxi, the smell seemed to dissipate. On power up taking the Runway (XXL), the smell returned. I made the decision to continue the takeoff and said to the First Officer that if this doesn't dissipate and go away we should return and he agreed. Once airborne, it got even worse and became a significant fume event. We donned O2 masks and goggles; I [advised ATC] due to fumes in the cockpit and we returned to land on [Runway] XXL. The First Officer flew and landed. I talked to ATC and ran the Fumes/Odor/Smoke Checklist. We landed uneventfully 2,000 lbs overweight.  Holding short of the runway, I called back to the Purser and asked him if he smelled anything. He said he didn't but would check in the back. I told him what we had experienced and that we would be returning if it continued. According to the flight attendants after landing, the smell was significant in the back of the plane and they said their eyes were burning as well. Upon arriving back at the gate, we were met by ARFF personnel and [Company] Maintenance. Firefighters boarded the plane and noticed the fumes as well, which were still present. The mechanic said they had done a 'compressor wash' the previous night and that it was his opinion that oil may have been freed from the compressors and entered the cabin. I was handed a Smoke/Fumes/Odor Form to fill out, which I did and returned it to Station Operations. Although some of the flight attendants complained to me about headaches and eyes burning, they declined to be checked out and told Inflight Operations they were fine and good to continue. I called the [Duty Manager] and we completed the human factors checklist. My Chief Pilot called to check on us and we said that while we may change our fit-for-duty status, for now, we were OK to continue. After talking about this more with the First Officer, we decided we were no longer fit for duty and advised the [Duty Manager].		During pushback from Gate XX at ZZZ, #2 engine was started normally, then First Officer began start of the #1 engine. After #1 engine light off, ground personnel told the Captain that an access panel had opened on the #2 engine. Upon the completion of the #1 engine start cycle, the number #2 engine was shut down, and ground personnel re-closed the panel. The number #2 engine was re-started without incident. Upon initial taxi, the Captain and First Officer smelled a strong oily, dirty socks smell. We checked with the cabin crew and nothing was noticed outside the cockpit. Fumes seemed to subside some during taxi to Runway XXL. Upon taxiing into position near the runway, with 40-45% power settings, the fumes seemed to reappear. Captain discussed the fumes with me and we decided to takeoff, and if the fumes persisted, we would [advise ATC] and return to ZZZ.Almost immediately after takeoff, the fumes became very strong. Crew immediately donned oxygen per the immediate action items and [advised ATC]. ATC provided amended clearance, and vectors to return to RWY XXL. First Officer, who was Pilot Flying, continued flight duties while Captain ran appropriate checklists, and coordinated our return with the requisite entities. First Officer executed an uneventful landing. Captain taxied back to Gate XY. Maintenance personnel boarded the aircraft along with fire crews, and said they had conducted a compressor wash prior to our event and suspect it was residual oil. After deplaning, Captain and First Officer discussed events with [Duty Manager] and Chief Pilot. Despite passing the company 'Fume Event Checklist', Captain and First Officer declared themselves no longer fit for duty.		B737 flight crew reported a significant fume event shortly after takeoff resulting in an air return.	
1706511	201912	0601-1200	ZDC.ARTCC	NY				32000		VMC						Center ZDC	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZDC																												Center ZDC	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZDC																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 222; Flight Crew Type 1953					1706511												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	We are just leveled off 32,000 feet. I noticed the aircraft start a small buffet and roll to the right. Shortly thereafter the aircraft rolled quickly to the left. I initiated the upset recovery technique to regain control of the aircraft. We lost our lateral navigation and deviated slightly off course. We also had a very minor altitude deviation of 40 feet. We asked ATC what type of aircraft was in front of us, their response was an A320. We quickly understood that we had encountered his wake turbulence. We were contacted by the flight attendants and we were told that two of them had fallen to the ground during the turbulence. One of the flight attendants said she twisted her ankle and it was minor. I asked if she was OK to continue and she replied yes and she did not need assistance at our destination. She continued her service and prior to the descent I asked again if she needed assistance in regards to her ankle. She responded no and was going to continue on with her trip. We landed and I ask the Flight Attendant one last time if she was OK and her response was yes.				B737NG Captain reported a Flight Attendant suffered a minor injury during a wake turbulence encounter at FL320 in trail of an A320.	
1706609	201912	0001-0600	ZZZ.TRACON	US				12500								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1706609	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1706982	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft; Human Factors	Ambiguous	I was the Captain operating flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1. This was day two of a three day for me and due to reassignment my original FO (First Officer) was not on the trip on this day. This was mine and the FO's first leg together. Everything was normal with preflight. The aircraft was towed up to the gate by Ground Personnel about an hour before departure and preparation was normal. I was expecting RWY XXL for takeoff, but when we called for taxi we were assigned RWY XY. FO accomplished the Before Taxi checklist and as we were taxiing out the Before Takeoff checklist was accomplished. We believe we checked everything and did the checklist in accordance with company procedures. Takeoff and climbout were normal. I was the PF (Pilot Flying) and was hand flying the aircraft until the [situation]. Passing 10,000 feet. I chimed the flight attendants as we continued climbing. Passing through approximately 12,500 feet. the Cabin Altitude warning illuminated and the aural horn came on. We donned our oxygen masks and told Center controller we were leveling at 13,000 feet. I then instructed FO to tell Center we needed a descent to 10,000 feet. The only light either of us ever saw was the red Cab Alt light. The FO got the QRH out and started the checklist. We were cleared to descend to 8,000 feet and the light extinguished at some point passing below 10,000 feet. The cabin altitude showed approximately 10,800 feet at the highest. The passenger oxygen masks did not deploy. We returned to ZZZ and landed on RWY XY. Taxi in and shutdown was normal. After we landed we discussed the event and I asked the FO if the switches on the Engine Bleeds, Packs and Crossfeeds we're out of position. He stated he couldn't remember (nor could I), but he thought they were. We both felt certain they were correctly positioned, but the more we questioned ourselves we both admitted we were not 100% certain.My thoughts are that we were not complacent in our preparation nor in accomplishing the checklists, nor were we rushed or distracted, but it is possible that the switches were in the incorrect position on takeoff and got positioned correctly during the [situation] and we simply don't recall either one of us moving them. Neither of us noticed any pressure surges before, during or after. It's possible they occurred and due to the warning horn and mask usage we didn't notice. I should have done a better job of instructing the FO not to touch anything prior to following the steps in the QRH. Also, it took us a while to get the warning horn off, which combined with using the oxygen masks and communicating with ATC and the flight attendants was distracting. Also, since I was hand flying the aircraft when the [situation] occurred, I wish I had immediately gotten the autopilot on. I would have been able to observe and manage the situation in a better way. After landing I asked him if he checked the pressurization per the After Takeoff checklist and he said he's not sure. We certainly have checklists that, if followed correctly, would/should trap these errors. I felt totally comfortable right up to the instant the warning horn and light came on. We weren't rushed, I was well rested and alert. I always make sure that when challenged with 'AC and Bleeds' during the Before Takeoff checklist that I physically point to the Pack AND Engine Bleed switches, making sure they are correctly positioned before I respond 'Auto and Set'. It's possible that the switches were in the incorrect position and that I looked, but didn't notice. I thought I was always diligent in doing this, but as I write this, I wonder if I saw what I wanted to see rather than where the switches actually were. I honestly am not 100% sure.Maintenance checked the aircraft thoroughly, including a pressure check, and found no discrepancies. The aircraft logbook was signed off and we were scheduled to continue our sequence to ZZZ1. I discussed with the FO that we needed to have our minds totally on the current flight and offered to take him off the trip and assured him there would be no repercussions if he felt he would be distracted while continuing the flight. He assured me he was fine, as was I. I briefed the FO that we would NOT discuss this event at all during our flight to ZZZ1 and I asked him if he felt good and able to fly. He responded that he felt good. We both elected to continue on to ZZZ1. I have told you exactly what happened to the absolute best of my ability. I instructed the FO to put in his own words what we experienced and what he thinks happened.Going forward, my intent is to get the autopilot on sooner. Also, I felt like it could have been better run if I had transferred control to him and I had run the QRH, notified ATC, Dispatch, the Flight Attendants and such. It all got completed, just could have been smoother. I hadn't thought about silencing the Cabin Altitude Warning horn in a long time. Today was a reminder that on any given day. Going forward, I'm doubling my effort to verify checklist items, expect the unexpected and follow all checklists precisely, brush up on all systems and to more fluidly and smoothly transition into the situation. I can assure that this will not happen again.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737-800 flight Crew reported a cabin pressurization malfunction during climb that resulted in a return to the departure airport and uneventful landing.	
1706751	201908		ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch; Parked			N	N	N	N	Unscheduled Maintenance	Testing; Inspection																		Engine Indications		X	Improperly Operated																																				Repair Facility		Air Carrier	Technician				Workload			1706751												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Maintenance		General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Aircraft; Procedure	Human Factors	I received a turnover that Aircraft XXX in ZZZ had a Right Engine exceedance. The previous Controller had talked with them and explained to them what needed to be done. When I took over, I was busy with other aircraft and was waiting to get an update from ZZZ. After I got caught up, I checked and the aircraft had departed. The item was signed off as EGT red box reset and inspection of the inlet and the exhaust. Knowing that this items are a chronic issue with the fleet and that the Inspection was not properly conducted per the AMM. I created a log entry to have the EEC interrogation accomplished to see if the faults still existed. I made the decision to stop the aircraft in ZZZ1 to be repaired. The EGT system on the 737 is a chronic known issue with the fleet. In retrospect, I should have locked the log item. This would have prevented the mechanic from clearing the item without getting Maintenance Management concurrence. Any item that needs Maintenance Management follow up should be locked by the Controller in charge and not unlocked until there has been verification that all the necessary steps have been accomplished.				Maintenance Technician reported an aircraft with an engine exceedance departed before the appropriate inspection could be accomplished.	
1706830	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Main Gear Tire		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 450; Flight Crew Type 10000					1706830	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 528					1707187	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Rejected takeoff at approximately 110 kts after aircraft started shaking badly, [making a] loud noise and pulling to one side. Captain was Pilot Flying. First Officer called out blown tire as I was in the process of rejecting the takeoff. We remained on the runway with about 4,500 feet left. Rejected takeoff procedure followed, brake cooling on [weight and balance] completed, after aircraft was inspected and unable to be towed off runway. Passengers were deplaned by stairs into buses with assistance of fire department. Aircraft logbook was filled out and handed over to Maintenance. Cabin Crew and Passengers were given updates throughout the process and time it took to evaluate the aircraft and deplane them.		During takeoff at ZZZ, at 110 kts, the right main inboard tire failed, causing a loud bang and strong vibration. I called 'tire failure' and the Captain simultaneously decided to reject the takeoff. He executed the rejected takeoff procedure and worked to maintain centerline. I verified the correct procedure (it was correct) and notified Tower. We stopped on centerline with 4,000 feet remaining. Maintenance and Airfield Operations advised us that hydraulics were leaking and we couldn't taxi and an attempt to tow the aircraft failed due to a locked brake, the passengers were unloaded onto busses.		B737 flight crew reported a tire failure that resulted in a high speed rejected takeoff.	
1706899	201912	1201-1800	ZAU.ARTCC	IL				27200		VMC						Center ZAU	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZAU																												Center ZAU	Air Carrier	B747 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Descent		Class A ZAU																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 197; Flight Crew Total 21964; Flight Crew Type 4223					1706899												Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	I was the Captain and the Pilot Flying to ORD. We were in contact with Chicago Center and given a descent to FL270 and we were given a speed restriction prior to upset event of 270 knots. The aircraft ahead of us was a Boeing 747. The B747 was doing 300 knots or better to increase the separation. At the time of the upset, we were separated by 11 NM.Approaching the level off at FL270, approximately 200 feet above that altitude descending at a rate of 500 feet per minute we encountered some moderate buffeting characteristic of aircraft wake turbulence. Within about 1 second of the onset of the turbulence, the aircraft started to roll to the left and the autopilot was unsuccessfully struggling to counteract the roll. I disconnected the autopilot and the First Officer called out 'Upset'. We had entered the B747's right wingtip vortex.Heading changed by a few degrees to the left and we were out of the rolling moment just as quickly as we entered. The seatbelt sign was quickly turned during that event by me. After the event, we were about 50 feet above our level off altitude and I hand flew the level off. I returned the aircraft to course and reengaged the autopilot. The First Officer commented after the event that we reached about 35 degrees of left bank. I had the First Officer make a PA explaining the event to the passengers to help calm their fears they may have had. I made a report to Chicago Center that they flew us into the B747's wake turbulence. They apologized and offered me an offset if I wanted. I accepted and we setup 1 mile right of course. The B747 was given direct FYTTE shortly after the upset while leaving us on the FYTTE FIVE ARRIVAL to BHAWK to further increase lateral separation and reduce the possibility that we would fly back into his wake turbulence.				B737NG Captain reported an inflight upset occurred at FL270 descending into ORD 11 miles in trail of a B747.	
1707343	201912	1801-2400															Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1707343	Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Air Carrier	Flight Attendant In Charge			Safety Related Duties; Service	Physiological - Other			1708048	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	ZZZ-ZZZ1 Fumes Odor in Cockpit [while] Airborne. Flight crew had previously flown leg (flight) ZZZ2-ZZZ on time in same aircraft. XA40 flight pushed from ZZZ on time.XB00 Airborne at cruise, Captain and First Officer smell light increasing volume of old sock odor and fumes smell. First Officer said it was giving him a headache. Captain said he could, '...taste it in the air.' Crew completed 'Elimination of Foul Odors in Flight Deck' procedure. O2 masks were on.XB05 Captain contacted Flight Attendant A (name) to ask if she smelled anything in cabin. She said 'no' so we, in step with QRH procedure, determined that fumes were coming from Pack 1, and shut off pack 1. Cabin pressurization remained stable. Per QRH procedure, after a measured 5 minutes of time, we assessed that fumes had dissipated and per QRH, 'No further action required.' and continued to destination ZZZ1.XB10p Notified AS Dispatch and Maintenance via ACARS. Dispatch replied acknowledgment and stated had notified Duty Manager.XB25p Captain contacted Flight Attendant A again via intercom to confirm no fumes in cabin. She replied that she could smell a light odor fumes only near cockpit door.No additional fumes noticed by cockpit crew until XC40. Note: Flight attendant communication and coordination during and after flight was very excellent.XC40 At top of descent, in managed descent mode upon thrust reduction, fumes odor returned to cockpit. Captain and First Officer re-donned masks did not [advise ATC], were given increased speed in descent down arrival. Pack 1 was off entire remainder of flight.XD01 First Officer uneventfully landed aircraft on Runway XXR in ZZZ1. After landing, started APU; however, as APU air use increased odor/fumes, APU air was taken off.XD07 Flight entered gate (number) and deplaned. Contacted Maintenance via radio who requested flight crew stay with aircraft as to describe event to maintenance tech. Captain experienced slight burning eyes and First Officer slight nausea.XD20 Captain called [Duty Manager] who instructed us to remain in jetway for ZZZ1 CFR EMT to arrive for initial medical evaluation.XD25 AS Maintenance Tech arrived (name), who stated upon entering cabin that he could smell oil fumes.XD52 ZZZ1 CFR EMT arrive and do quick health assessment. Gives crew reference to hospital for blood chem/CO2 test.XF08 Captain recontacted [Duty Manager] and notified of we were enroute to hospital for blood tests s were flight attendants.XF35a Captain contacted [Crew Scheduling], spoke to (name) who we notified that we were on way to hospital. He asked for us to contact [Crew Scheduling] after getting back to assigned hotel to adjust end of duty time.XF45 Hospital Emergency Room: paperwork, blood work and waited for results. Hospital ER Doctor said that only my First Officer's blood showed elevated, above standard levels of CO2, but under an amount which they consider dangerous. She provided paperwork that stated all crew were clear to return to work.XH15 Left hospital for assigned crew hotel.XH30 Arrived into hotel. Captain contacted [Crew Scheduling], as per previous instructions, notified of flight crews arrival to hotel. [Crew Scheduling] rep (name) stated that her co-worker (name) was incorrect and our duty day had in fact ended after our landing in ZZZ1 earlier and time spent in hospital emergency room due to fume event was irrelevant. Furthermore, we were expected to show on time at XA50, getting less than the required 10 hours of rest. And we hadn't even made it to our rooms yet. We replied that as we were both exhausted from the event, EMT, blood draw, and long hospital process, that even if were able to immediately fall asleep upon getting to room, we weren't probably going to be in any condition on reduced rest to do tomorrow's (now today's) next flight. (Name) said that we could call in sick. Captain and I were shocked and disappointed in this altered [Crew Scheduling] interpretation. We replied that we would try to get enough rest.XI15 Approximately, I got to sleep.XO05 I awoke to use restroom, immediately recognized exhaustion and inability to get enough sleep in reduced time. I called Crew Scheduling and called in 'Fatigue.'Cockpit crew experienced various volumes of fumes/sock odor causing physiological impact and required crew to be screened by Airport Fire Department EMT and then go to local emergency hospital for chemical blood analysis, which showed minimal but measurable levels of CO2 crew blood.[Crew Scheduling]'s questionable alteration and re-definition of flight crew duty day not taking into account the long and arduous safety and health procedures post flight including EMT analysis and late night into morning hospital emergency visits with blood draw AND further insistence that crew continue onto next flight of pairing with real reduced rest is dubious, unsafe and detrimental to the on time and safe flight operations at company. A veteran of six previous airlines, I have never seen a flight operation which would have a flight crew continue flying who had recently experienced an physiological impact event such as fumes and on top of that, a reduced real rest period. I highly recommend that (name), in the interest of safety and operational integrity, changes their procedure so that any crew who has experienced any impact to their health in such an event, be automatically removed from flight status for at least a day.		Cabin Air Quality event. Dirty socks odor in cabin mostly around Row 30, also much stronger in flight deck and forward galley area. All crew members reported illness due to fumes. We all were examined by paramedics, and went to emergency room at local hospital.		A320 flight crew reported a fume event of varying intensity during entire flight. Entire flight crew received medical evaluation at local hospital for physiological symptoms.	
1707874	201912	0601-1200	EWR.Airport	NJ				6000		VMC						TRACON N90	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS	Initial Approach	STAR PHLBO 3 RNAV	Class B EWR																												TRACON N90		Small Aircraft		1					Cruise		Class B EWR																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1707874	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1707904	Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types	Vertical 800		Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control		Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Airspace Structure; Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	The event at question occurred during descent to EWR on the PHLBO 3 RNAV Arrival. During descent ATC instructed to descend and maintain 6,000 feet from 8,000 feet. Since we were relatively far out (roughly over 'DYLIN'), I engaged Vertical Speed 1,000 FPM down. Crossing around 7,000 feet ATC advised us of traffic, a small aircraft leveled at 6,000 around one o'clock westbound, roughly 10 miles out. We started looking for the traffic, which I obtained around 6,300 feet around 12-11 o'clock position. The traffic at question also replied he had us insight before I had him. Upon getting ALT* roughly prior to 'METRO' we got the TCAS RA DESCEND command. Upon execution of the procedure per SOP we descended around 300 feet (5,700 feet), and advised ATC. Around 1-2 miles past our course we turned back to join the arrival and ATC gave us a heading to final.		ATC gave decent to 6,000 feet on arrival. Called out a small aircraft at 6,500 about 10 miles at 12 o'clock. Saw on TCAS but not visually until closer. Baron acknowledged seeing us and would avoid. Pilot Flying acquired visual just before getting the TCAS RA and following a descend RA. Went 5,700 feet then called ATC about the event and climbed back to 6,000 feet and requested a heading.		A320 flight crew reported complying with a conflict RA during descent to EWR on the PHLBO 3 RNAV Arrival.	
1708218	201912	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				25000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121		Passenger		Descent		Class A ZZZ																								Compressor		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 230; Flight Crew Type 1468		Distraction			1708218	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 452		Distraction			1708235	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	On descent into ZZZ near the ZZZZZ Intersection, we were about to start down from FL250 when we felt what seemed like a compressor stall as well as airframe vibrations. Checking the engine indications showed nothing abnormal nor out of limits. The Number 2 engine had slightly higher vibrations at 2.1. The Captain disconnected the autopilot and auto-throttles, and began reducing thrust. With the power back the vibrations did subside. During the descent the Captain added power on the #1 engine and we soon heard and felt what seemed similar to a compressor stall. The Captain returned the engine to idle and used #2 engine for power as necessary, however we still did not have any abnormal engine indications. At that time we notified ATC and requested direct to the field as well as Runway XXR. On the way into landing we reviewed the QRH for engine high vibration, engine severe damage, and engine limit or surge or stall. We also planned for a possible single engine flaps 15 landing just in case the Number 1 engine failed on final. All engine indications were still normal up until about a three to four mile final when the Number 1 engine vibrations began to increase with the throttle at or near idle. The highest vibration that I saw was about 3.4.Once we landed, Tower told us that they saw possible flames from the Number 1 engine and fire and rescue told us of smoke from the engine. We ran the Engine fire QRH to shut down and secure the engine, we did not fire the bottle however since we did not have any internal fire indications and the fire department was in the process of spraying the engine. We coordinated with the Fire Department and Maintenance on the field and when they agreed everything looked safe we were towed to the gate for deplaning.		During arrival on ZZZZZ X RNAV Arrival we had an unusual engine malfunction on the Number 1 engine requiring non-standard Crew Member coordination and communication between Pilots and Flight Attendants, significantly increasing the task loading. Since this was a rather unusual engine malfunction it is possible that incorrect checklist procedure(s) was (were) followed.In our attempts to identify the engine issue we transitioned from ENG Limit, Surge or Stall Checklist to ENG Vibration Checklist to ENG FIRE or Severe Damage or Separation Checklist. We notified ATC and we received clearance from ATC to proceed directly to ZZZ. During the descent several step-down altitudes clearances were received, however we are not 100% sure that all clearances were properly interpreted, read back and/or complied with. We were never informed by ATC about any violations.		B737-800 flight crew reported possible Number 1 engine compressor stall.	
1708637	201912	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC		Poor Lighting	Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 82; Flight Crew Total 8410; Flight Crew Type 233		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1708637	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 82; Flight Crew Total 2210; Flight Crew Type 121		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1709130	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance	Airport	Airport	During a rolling takeoff, the lights on Runway XXR failed. As we were transferring aircraft control from Captain to First Officer, a radio call from the previous takeoff told Tower the runway lights went out. Tower subsequently gave us a 'cancel takeoff if we'd like' instruction.Based upon how clearly we could see, various radio chatter, aircraft position/power (takeoff power set), and inter cockpit communication, I felt the safest course of action was to continue. The fact that the lights had failed on the runway was basically the last thing we registered during our transitional duties and various radio chatter from Airport Operations, Tower, and other aircraft. Albeit nighttime, our ability to discern centerline and runway edge remained clear. Visibility [was] 10 NM plus.		Tower cleared us for takeoff and as we were beginning our takeoff roll the previous aircraft reported that the runway lights had gone out. The tower then said 'Aircraft X, Cancel your takeoff clearance'. We had already begun the roll and both of us could clearly see the runway, so we decided to continue the takeoff and told tower we were doing so, at which time they re-cleared us for takeoff.		B777-200 Captain reported that the runway lights failed during a night VMC takeoff roll. Flight crew decided continuing the takeoff was the safest course of action.	
1708957	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US						IMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Check Pilot; Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1708957												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	Fume event on Flight ABC.				A320 Captain reported a fume event.	
1709495	201912		ZZZ.Airport	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																												X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 174; Flight Crew Type 1877					1709495												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	During climbout from ZZZ, we had an electrical irregularity resulting in loss of VHF1, Yaw Damper, four of our six CDS display screens, as well as other less important observed failures. During troubleshooting we discovered that a safety pin attached to a piece of luggage had somehow hooked around the Standby Power CB (Circuit Breaker) and pulled it out. We consulted with [Maintenance Control], but they could not advise us to reset CB as this conflicted with Flight Manual Guidance, and that resetting the CB could only be done using Captains Emergency Authority. After assessing our situation and available options, I decided the safest course of action was to reset the CB.Had we not reset the breaker:We would lose all the electrical system functionality and redundancies that the Standby Bus provides.We would be down to 2 of 6 cockpit displays (1 was deferred inop prior to flight).We would be down to one VHF radio + ACARS.I only made this decision because I was confident there was no underlying electrical problem, this CB is not on the list of reset-prohibited CBs, and resetting it was a much safer course of action than simply diverting with multiple degraded systems. To be clear, I would not have reset it if there was any doubt of the reason why it was out. After resetting the CB, we observed all affected systems return to normal operation.				B737 pilot reported a loss of VHF1, Yaw Damper, four of our six CDS display screens and found it was a CB that was pulled out due to luggage moving it.  Pilot reset CB and everything went back on line.	
1709667	201912	0001-0600	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						VMC						Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1709667												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	Fume event. The cause of the fumes must be addressed.				A320 First Officer reported a fume event during initial approach.	
1710232	201912	1801-2400	EWR.Airport	NJ			900			IMC	Rain		Night			Tower EWR	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B EWR																								Autopilot			Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 105; Flight Crew Type 1196		Confusion; Distraction; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1710232												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Near the METRO fix at 3,000 feet [and] 250 kts with autopilot engaged in LNAV ALT HOLD, while transitioning from the PHLBO3 STAR to the ILS Runway 4R approach into EWR, the Approach Controller initially gave us direct to the GRITY IF and cleared us for the approach, maintain 3000 feet until established. We used LNAV to go direct to GRITY, selected the APP mode, began to slow down and selected flaps 5.A few miles from GRITY, the Controller gave us a left heading to vector us just outside the FAF, DOINS, and told us to maintain 2,000 feet until established and 190 kts or more until DOINS. Since [we were] in a 737 with lower maximum flaps speeds, at a light weight, and with engine anti-ice on, we knew we would have a difficult time slowing and descending. PF (Pilot Flying) selected HDG SEL for the new vector. PM (Pilot Monitoring) selected 2,000 feet on the MCP and the PF selected LVL CHG and 190 kts. After some discussion on how it would be difficult to slow, PF requested flaps 10, gear down and extended the speed brakes. VOR LOC captured just outside of the FAF. PF selected target speed. As PF, I was fixated on the airspeed that was not decreasing and waiting for the next flap setting speeds. F15, 25 and 30 were selected. Both PF and PM believed that GS had captured, but it had not and we were still in LVL CHG. PM incorrectly set TDZE. PF was fixated on the air speeds. We changed to Tower frequency and checked in. [The] aircraft was still LVL CHG and TDZE in the MCP (Mode Control Panel) altitude [and] the aircraft descended below glideslope. After the 1,000 feet callout both the PF and PM began to realize that we were below GS. At the same time the Tower said that we were low altitude and should be at 1,700 feet. Our altitude was going through 900 feet. PF disconnected the AP (Autopilot) and added power and climbed immediately back to the glide path. Though unstable at 1,000 feet we were stable at 500 feet and continued to land.				B737 First Officer reported descending below glideslope due to inappropriate automation settings while on the ILS Runway 4R approach into EWR.	
1710256	201912									VMC							Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 139; Flight Crew Total 11079; Flight Crew Type 1796		Workload			1710256												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	We experienced a medical emergency approximately 1 hour into the flight. The other Relief Pilot and myself were on break for roughly an hour when we were asked to return to the cockpit due to a divert and medical emergency. Once we returned to the flight deck we coordinated with ATC, Dispatch and Maintenance regarding the divert and sick passenger. It was determined by all involved that continuing to ZZZ was not an option and ZZZ1 was the best choice in order to protect the operation and take care of the ill passenger. In addition, during the divert phase of the flight (last 1:30), we were in contact with Dispatch and Maintenance to determine whether or not we should dump fuel or land overweight. After a thorough discussion, we decided to dump 100,000 lbs. of fuel. During the dump process, one of the fuel nozzles failed. It caused the dump rate to be cut in half. This added another layer of complexity to the divert. We called Maintenance again to see if they could offer any help, which they could not. At this point, the entire cockpit crew came together to brainstorm and make sure all SOP was complied with. The Medical Guide and Diversion Planning Guide were pulled out in order to ensure we had not forgotten anything. The flight attendants and passengers were briefed, and, most of all, we thoroughly briefed the approach, landing, and ground phase in order to ensure the medical personnel ability to reach the passenger in the rear of the aircraft at the gate in a timely fashion.Once we arrived in ZZZ1 and parked at the gate we were all quite exhausted due to the medical divert and high workload. At that point a representative from Flight Operations entered the flight deck and asked if we would continue on to ZZZ. We all discussed our current state and came to the conclusion we did not feel safe continuing. The Flight Operations Rep. then said they needed one First Officer as they had found a Captain and one First Officer. At that point we had been on duty for approximately 8 hours and with a proposed scheduled departure of XA:30 for ZZZ would have added an additional 9 hours of duty, resulting in a roughly 18 hour duty day, if the flight would in fact depart at XA:30. At that point I did not feel fit and comfortable continuing to ZZZ and believe it would not have been safe, especially taking into account our current state of mind and additional duty time.Ultimately, it was my desire to continue and not delay the passengers any further; however, with safety in mind it simply was not prudent to continue.				B777 First Officer reported that a passenger illness resulted in a diversion.	
1710272	201912	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			0			VMC						Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent; Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 160; Flight Crew Total 1896; Flight Crew Type 1896					1710272												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During powerup for takeoff we noticed a strong odor in the cockpit. Because we hadn't noticed it earlier, it seemed that it could be coming from another aircraft or externally. After takeoff it was apparent it was coming from our aircraft, but dissipated during climbout so we decided to continue. During the descent below 23,000 feet, the odor was apparent again but not as strong. The engines were at idle. We were close to ZZZ at this point so we reviewed the fumes in the cockpit checklist and discussed donning the O2 mask and advising ATC should the situation become worse. Below 17,000 feet we started the APU and used the bleed.  It helped for a few minutes but the odor returned. The flight attendants also smelled the odor in the cabin. The Gate Agent immediately mentioned the odor as she opened the door. The engine oil was serviced in ZZZZ and the engines were washed.				B737 Captain reported a strong odor on takeoff which initially dissipated during climbout and returned during descent at destination.	
1710286	201912	0601-1200	SMF.Tower	CA				2000		IMC	Rain						Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 171; Flight Crew Total 2505; Flight Crew Type 2505		Human-Machine Interface			1710286												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Undershoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors; Aircraft	Human Factors	On descent and approach into SMF we were descending VIA the SLMMR3, I was the Pilot Monitoring. At approximately POZUR we were cleared for the ILS Runway 16L once reaching TENCO. We set 1,900 ft. in the MCP window for LEVEY and we were in LNAV/VNAV. Approximately at DIRBE we were fully configured target speed was set and we were slowing. We were on the VNAV descent path and shortly after passing DIRBE VOR/LOC and glideslope captured, TDZE was set in the MCP window. I was changing to the tower frequency and when I looked up the airspeed was about Vref and the trend was still slowing rapidly. I called airspeed and reached up to advance the throttles about the same time the Captain did. I looked to the attitude indicator and the aircraft was pitching about 15-20 degrees up. I called to push the nose down and at the same time pushed forward on the control column. Again VOR/LOC and GS were both captured and the auto throttles were on. The Captain recovered and we were only between 1/2 dot and 1 dot above glideslope and still outside the FAF, but at this point continuing the approach did not seem like a good idea so I called for a go-around. We were still outside LEVEY and above 2,000 ft. and Tower assigned us runway heading and continue descent and maintain 2,000 ft. We were then vectored around for another approach and landed. This could have been a false glideslope capture but we were on the VNAV descent path and on or slightly below the glideslope indicator.				B738 First Officer reported that a false glideslope capture resulted in an unstabilized approach, followed by a go-around.	
1710654	201912		ZZZ.Airport	US				33000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 43; Flight Crew Type 43					1710654	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 233; Flight Crew Total 16315; Flight Crew Type 3288					1710658	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were cruising east of ZZZ at FL330 when our right engine began to stall and roll back. We followed the QRH procedures and the stalls cleared below 50% N1. We then decided to divert to ZZZ and land.		We were at cruise at FL330 and the Number 2 engine pulsated and ran rough. We reduced thrust per the QRH procedures to where the engine ran smoothly. The thrust requirement for that was at about 50% N1. We then requested priority handling with ATC, completed the remaining QRH procedures, made all required notifications and briefed the FA's with the TEST acronym. We then landed underweight and uneventfully at ZZZ.		B737 flight crew reported a loss of engine power during climb, resulting in a diversion.	
1710735	201912	1801-2400	SFO.Airport	CA						VMC			Night			TRACON NCT	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B SFO																												TRACON NCT	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B SFO																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 18250					1710735												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Procedure	While being vectored for a night visual approach to Runway 28L at SFO, we encountered wake turbulence from a preceding B777. I would classify it as a moderate to moderate + wake turbulence encounter, lasting only 2-3 seconds. I was PM (Pilot Monitoring), FO (First Officer) was PF (Pilot Flying). We were vectored to 310 heading to intercept 28L final, I noted [the] B777 was 5.5 nm ahead, and NORCAL Approach had assigned that aircraft 180 KIAS to the marker/bridge DUYET. We were assigned 180 KIAS also. As we were capturing the final approach course for 28L, we encountered the moderate wake turbulence from the B777, which placed our aircraft in a momentary 15 degree left bank, followed by a 35-40 degree left bank, which disengaged the autopilot. The FO immediately took the controls and returned the aircraft to straight and level flight, stabilized. The left bank was only momentary, and the wake turbulence subsided. I noted that the B777 was only 4.5 nm ahead now, and surmised that they had slowed below 180 KIAS prior to DUYET, ignoring ATC instructions. We configured the gear & flaps and landed normally. I then called NORCAL Approach, discussed the incident with the sector supervisor, and debriefed the event with the FO. No further action taken. The event occurred in night VMC conditions on visual approaches to RWY 28L because the B777 had slowed below the 180 KIAS speed ATC assigned them to the marker (DUYET). Instead of 5.5 nm separation, we only had 4.5nm. I assume that ATC automatically assigns no less than 5 nm for smaller aircraft following a wide-body (heavy) aircraft. I believe it may be necessary to add at least 1 nm for added safety margin, if and when feasible, like on night visual approaches.				B737-800 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on a night visual approach to SFO in trail of a B777.	
1711447	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								FMS/FMC		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1711447												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Enroute to ZZZ1 at FL340, both Captain and First Officer FMC issues developed into a situation where the navigation to points became unreliable. Autopilot frequently disengaged, safe operation into ZZZZ1 with IFR operations could not be accomplished. Referenced QRH for FMC fail, non-routine landing, and overweight landing checklists. Priority handling was requested, decision was made to divert to ZZZ2 with VFR conditions, and long runway to perform overweight landing. Landing ZZZ2 uneventful, aircraft taken out of service. Photos of FMC values and indications which may have affected navigation attached.				B737 Captain reported a dual FMS failure resulted in a diversion.	
1711699	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000		VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Direct						Unscheduled Maintenance																			Oxygen System/Pax		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 113; Flight Crew Total 1193; Flight Crew Type 1193		Troubleshooting			1711699												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		Y	Person Passenger	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Procedure	Procedure	Aircraft was ferried from ZZZ to ZZZ1 and a flight test of a mechanical issue was accomplished on that flight, by I am assuming company test pilots.Aircraft preflight was normal and departure was normal until two ECAMs for an emergency exit door being open. QRH was run and aircraft pressurization was normal so flight was continued. Probably 10 minutes later an ECAM for an inoperative TCAS was experienced and run. ATC was notified that the TCAS was now inoperative. About 1 hour into the flight, we received a call from the flight attendants that a passenger noticed a 'placard' on the oxygen mask cover above row 25DEF that said 'Oxygen Canister Needed'. With no way to verify that the canister was in place or not, and with three open seats, we elected to move the passengers from that row to ensure they had oxygen available. During the process of moving them, it was discovered that row 23DEF had the same placard which turned out to be tape with the same wording on it.Now, we were in the position of potentially not having enough oxygen for the passengers in the event of a depressurization. I gave the aircraft and the radios to the FO (First Officer) and had him begin a controlled descent to 10,000 feet. I got a phone patch to dispatch and Maintenance Control. We discussed the situation and agreed that the proper and safe course of action was to stay at 10,000 feet and have the aircraft go to destination that the canisters could be verified to be, or not be in place. ZZZ2 was the agreed to destination since we doubted the ability of the aircraft to be able to get to the destination or back to ZZZ1 which was slightly closer at the time, at 10,000 feet.After the phone patch was over, and we had turned the aircraft towards ZZZ2 the Dispatcher sent a message saying we could arrive at ZZZ3 with 4.7 on the fuel. We were already heading back north, and 4.7 with an airplane that had already had a couple of spurious ECAMs and now had us doubting the safety of the aircraft maintenance inspections, led us to reject that option. We continued towards ZZZ2.As we were heading towards ZZZ2 it occurred to me that ZZZ1 was slightly closer and might give us the option of arriving about a comfort level of 5.0 on the fuel, and would give company maintenance the opportunity to inspect the aircraft and to give our passengers better routing options in case the canisters were not in place. The Dispatcher's initial run on system gave us 5.2 at ZZZ1, so we changed the destination to ZZZ1.After putting ZZZ1 in as the destination and with a direct ZZZ1 routing and a two fix approach for Runway XXC in the box, the fuel at ZZZ1 was 3.8 varying to as much as 4.1. This was not what the flight plan Dispatch sent us had. We did the basic math of time remaining times fuel flow and that agreed with what the box was telling us. Neither of us on the flight deck were comfortable with putting ourselves into a potential minimum fuel situation when we had just passed a perfectly good airport less than 40 miles away. I made a command decision to yet again go to ZZZ2. Dispatch was notified and we landed uneventfully in ZZZ2 with 7.0 approximately in the tanks.Upon arrival in ZZZ2, the passengers were deplaned and the suspect mask covers were dropped and found to have canisters installed despite the masking tape placards saying otherwise.The rest of the maintenance items were taken care of in ZZZ2 and the passengers were re-boarded and we departed ZZZ2 and completed the rest of the flight uneventfully.The Dispatcher and I spoke on the ground in ZZZ2 about the fuel discrepancy. The flight plans he sent us all had us going above 10,000 on the way to ZZZ1 and that clearly led to more fuel remaining than was really going to be there. He also said that he was struggling to get system to give a direct routing, but after the fact he was able to get a flight plan that confirmed the 4.2 or so in ZZZ1 that we and the box had come up with.				A320 Captain reported that a cabin placard indicated several passenger oxygen masks may have been inoperative, resulting in an expedited descent and diversion.	
1711760	201912	1801-2400	ZZZ.Tower	US						IMC	Icing		Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS XXR	Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying							1711760	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying							1711928	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft TA	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure	Procedure	We were operating Aircraft X, on approach to the ILS XXR to ZZZ. We were approximately 2-3 miles outside ZZZZZ, established on the ILS XXR, cleared for the approach and fully configured for landing. IMC conditions existed with moderate mixed icing in the clouds. Everything was normal until we received the following TA in exactly this cadence: 'traffic, traffic! climb, climb! increase climb, increase climb!' I commanded Go-Around, and the FO (First Officer) immediately disconnected the autopilot, selected TOGA and initiated a go-around maneuver, while I assisted with gear and flap clean up. While we were engaged with this we entered VMC conditions and I observed out of my left peripheral view aircraft position lights to our left and slightly below us, closing on us with no relative movement. I recall pulling back on the yoke with the FO and shouting 'climb, climb, climb!' As soon as we had done this we received a 'clear of conflict' message. This all took place in just a few seconds. I announced our go-around to ZZZ Tower, and we were told to track the 16R localizer course and climb to 9,000. When asked the reason for the go-around we told them we were responding to an RA. We were then handed off to ZZZ Approach, who advised us that there was another aircraft also on a go-around climbing to 11,000 feet. I'm not sure if this was the aircraft that caused our loss of separation, but I suspect so. We were then vectored for a subsequent approach to the ILS XXL which was accomplished uneventfully.Without hearing radio transmissions from the other Tower frequency I can't say for sure why the other aircraft was suddenly encroaching on our flight path, but I can say that the TCAS went from nothing to a red target to our immediate left with RA commands almost instantly. I don't know how close we came to the other aircraft, but in the brief time that we were VMC it seemed to me that we were very close.This is only conjecture, but I believe that an aircraft on the parallel approach to XXL executed a go-around, but failed to fly the localizer course and began turning into us, thus creating the loss of separation and RA event. For our part, we followed SOPs, as well as ATC directions. FO did an exceptional job of flying the aircraft first and getting us out of the path of the other aircraft. He also stated that when we received the RA, his VSI was completely red, with no safe 'green band' to even fly into.		I was recently asked to submit a report regarding a TCAS RA during the approach into ZZZ. This event occurred during the approach (ILS XXR) after we were cleared for the approach and fully configured. We were in IMC and icing conditions during night when the event occurred. Everything was proceeding normally and we were fully configured on a stabilized approach. I was the Pilot Flying and monitoring the instruments. There was no traffic displayed on TCAS and seemingly out of nowhere we received a TA and I saw the target appear on the MFD just to our left. Someone had been issued a go around just prior but I can't recall which runway that was from (XXL makes sense). Almost as soon as the target appeared with the TA, we received a climb RA. Despite the startle, I promptly complied with the RA and began the climb. Very quickly after the climb was initiated, we received the 'increase climb' command at which time I also complied. There was no RA vertical guidance given other than the aural climb instruction. Very shortly after this, we received the 'clear of conflict' aural message and continued cleaning up the aircraft and completed the go-around. The PM notified ATC that we were complying with an RA and that we were going around. We were vectored back around for the ILS XXR which was uneventful the second time. During the second approach, Tower inquired us about the other aircraft. Seemed like they (ATC) weren't entirely sure themselves who had transgressed into our final approach course and caused the RA. At the same time as our RA I seem to recall there was another aircraft executing a go around from a different runway.I believe another aircraft was executing a missed approach and transgressed into our final approach course. There was some confusion on the part of ZZZ Tower regarding who had gone around and where they were going.Seems to me that ATC should be slightly more vigilant in traffic separation and approach monitoring on final to ensure there are no transgressions. Overall seemed to be some confusion in the Tower with what had happened when we informed them of our RA and their subsequent query during our second approach about the traffic that had conflicted with us.		B737 flight crew reported that an unknown aircraft created a traffic conflict on final, and resulted in an RA and a go-around.	
1712960	201912	0001-0600	ORD.Airport	IL			1500			IMC						Tower ORD	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS 28R	Landing		Class B ORD																												Tower ORD	Air Carrier	Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer				IFR		FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ORD																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 192; Flight Crew Type 2888					1712960												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	Final approach to 28R ORD, CAT III approach was planned and briefed. There was another aircraft on the runway cleared for takeoff but not yet ready. Tower asked them several times if they were ready. They responded '20 seconds, 10 seconds, 5 seconds, ready' and were cleared for takeoff when we were on a 2 mile final. We were then cleared to land with a caution for wake turbulence. We were at 130 kts with flaps 30. Stable.Approaching alert height the runway approach lights were in sight. Around alert height the runway was in sight. Just after the 30 foot call the aircraft began veering to the left of center and 'burbling' was felt, due to the other aircraft's wake we believe. Around the 10 foot call I decided the best course of action was to disconnect the autopilot, announced disconnecting autopilot, then disconnected to prevent further left drift. The runway was in sight with a safe landing possible. I was concerned with our low airspeed and altitude that the tail would contact the runway if a go around was executed. Landed 28R and taxied to gate with no further incident. We fully debriefed as a crew at the gate.				B777 Captain reported encountering jet blast just before landing from an aircraft departing on the same runway.	
1713005	201912	1801-2400	EWR.Airport	NJ						Marginal						Tower EWR	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B EWR																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1713005												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Procedure; Human Factors	Procedure	The First Officer was flying the approach following a lengthy ground delay for weather earlier in the day and 45 minutes of holding during the flight. A B777 was ahead of us on the approach and apparently had slowed to final approach speed 8 miles out on final after the Controller had directed them to maintain 170 Kts. until the Final Approach Fix. We complied with the Controller's directions and slowed to final approach speed when directed. Due to insufficient separation with the B777, ATC directed us to go around. The First Officer complied and the subsequent approach and landing were uneventful. The B777 did not comply with ATC instructions on airspeed. All aircraft should comply promptly to ATC instructions and advise if unable.				B737 Captain reported that Tower issued them go around instructions due to insufficient wake turbulence spacing on the final approach course.	
1713094	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		3		1200		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 241; Flight Crew Type 241					1713094												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Ambiguous	On descent the Crew was given a 350 heading and a descent to 2,000 feet. The Pilot Flying put 1,200 feet in the altitude window but was corrected by the Pilot Monitoring. ATC asked if the field was in sight and cleared the Crew visual to Runway XX. The Pilot Flying dialed in 1,200 feet in the altitude window and began a descent on approximately a 90 degree left base for Runway XX. The Crew was directed to contact Tower and the Pilot Monitoring went heads down to put in the frequency. When the Pilot Monitoring came back heads up, the GPWS caution sounded with a yellow blob below point ZZZZZ. ATC also said they received an altitude alert at that time.The Pilot Flying initiated a climb until the caution disappeared and re-initiated the visual approach, but was too high to make a safe descent to land with the glare from the sun behind the airport, due to the time of day washing out the VASIs. The Crew initiated the go-around at approximately 1,000 feet and was given instructions by Tower to fly runway heading and climb to 2,000 feet. The Pilot Flying called for flaps 15 and landing gear up followed by set speed. The Pilot Monitoring saw the window was closed and checked climbout speed, and the Pilot Flying called set speed again.The Pilot Monitoring asked if he wanted flaps 1 since the speed was good and the window was closed. The Pilot Flying called for flaps 1 and set speed again with the window closed, and the Pilot Monitoring asked if he would like flaps up once the airspeed passed the 1 bug. The Pilot Monitoring was trying to call ATC at the same time, but put in the incorrect frequency issued by Tower for the go and had to query Tower for the correct frequency. During that time the Pilot Flying began a turn to follow the published missed approach and the Pilot Monitoring intervened with a direction to fly runway heading. Once stable at 2,000 feet and runway heading, ATC issued new vectors for the Crew to bring the aircraft back around to land.Continued vigilance of obstacles in the approach path such as the tower below the outer marker on this approach. It was not a controlling obstacle and it is difficult to see since it's depicted underneath the final approach course on the chart.				B737 First Officer reported a GPWS warning on final, resulting in a go-around.	
1713159	201912									VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 156; Flight Crew Total 659; Flight Crew Type 659		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1713159												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Airspace Structure; Procedure	Procedure	This was the last day of a four day trip. Preflight was going as planned until we were informed about a GDP for our destination. Our Destination airport had to switch to runways that are rarely used and it was creating some issues. The Captain and I took the extra time to discuss how this would affect our flight and kept a good line of communication with the Dispatcher. One of the threats that we were trying to mitigate early was the filed STAR was not used for the runways in use at our Destination. The Captain and I located two STARS that utilize the in use runway and planned on having our current STAR changed enroute.During the flight, I asked the Dispatcher if she noticed any flights having their STARS changed but she said that we were the first aircraft approaching our destination from the north. Shortly thereafter ATC changed our STAR and I attempted to load the new arrival into the box but was unable to since the computer did not associate the STAR with the assigned runway. After several failed attempts at programming the box, I requested ATC to assigned us a different STAR that was associated with the runway to mitigate the obvious threat at hand but they informed me that we would descend via the assigned STAR before receiving vectors for the assigned runway. While the Captain and I discussed the situation along with how to mitigate the threat, we heard two other aircraft having the same issue and requesting different STARs (one was definitely an Airbus).I set up the box as best I could with the assigned STAR and asked for a heading to allow me time to enter the approach when switched to the final controller. At one point during the flight, I asked the dispatcher about the STAR issue in the hopes that he could talk to the ATC desk or other dispatchers to proactively help other flights. The Dispatcher replied that there is not much they can do and we always have the ability to say 'unable'. The Captain and I discussed what the dispatcher said and could not find a good reason to deny ATC's assignment. Safety of flight was never compromised and we were able to discuss every way to mitigate the threat we perceived. The only thing the Captain and I want to know is how to get FIF to accept a Runway/STAR combination like this without having to manually insert each fix and altitude manually.				First Officer reported difficulty with instructions from ATC for STAR changes on approach.	
1713167	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			Mixed			Dawn			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																																Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 68; Flight Crew Total 276; Flight Crew Type 276		Time Pressure			1713167		Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 178; Flight Crew Total 3714; Flight Crew Type 1326		Time Pressure; Situational Awareness			1713168	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Procedure; Weather	Procedure	Initial takeoff out of ZZZ. Reviewed weather and noted that ZZZ1 was designated as takeoff alternate. Briefed both FO and Lead of the takeoff alternate. ATIS stated that RVR was 500 but continuously being updated. Tower was calling RVR of 1200 due to ground fog. FO and I briefed both his minimums for takeoff as well as reviewed the back of the Airport Diagram page. Airport Diagram page stated with adequate visual reference we needed 1600 or 1/4 mile. Taxiing out watched other aircraft depart as the ground fog was quickly burning off. As we approached number one Tower stated 'RVR is 1400 v 3500'. They did not provide a base RVR and I did not ask. We informed Tower we were ready when they were. Tower cleared us for takeoff and I stated to the FO that visibility was good and that I could see the departure end of the 9000 ft. [Runway]. My estimate was visibility was greater than 2 miles. We took off uneventfully and shortly thereafter atis stated visibility was 3 miles. On climb out, FO and I discussed weather and reports. Upon inquiry into the FOM, it seems that we should have either queried Tower for specific RVR or delayed takeoff until all numbers were above 1600. Also, I was lulled into a VFR mindset because in hindsight what I was physically seeing did not match the min RVR Tower stated.		During preparation for takeoff, morning fog was burning off at a rapid pace. Airport Diagram page showed take off minimums for runways in ZZZ to be 1600 RVR. ATIS was updating to RVR 500 however we clearly could see the runway all the way to the end which is over 9,000 ft. long. Airport conditions were over 2 mile visibility without restrictions. Tower confirmed [a] new report of 1400 RVR variable to 3500 RVR. We told Tower that we were ready for takeoff whenever they could clear us. We were cleared for take off shortly after. The doubt we had was lack of clarity on [the] actual RVR report even though we were VMC at this point and had no problem seeing all of the runway environment. Shortly after rotation ATIS updated again to 3 SM visibility. 3 flights prior to us departed which added to the confusion of required RVR. Conditions did not match RVR reports.		Flight Crew reported departing with RVR under minimums.	
1713233	201912	1201-1800	DCA.Airport	DC			0									Tower DCA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1713233												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Ground Conflict, Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Other / Unknown			Person Air Traffic Control	Other takeoff roll	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff	Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	We had an ATC directed rejected takeoff. DCA was arriving/departing aircraft on Runway 19. First Officer takeoff. We were close to our Max Structural Takeoff Weight so during our takeoff briefing, we discussed, because of the short runway and heavy weight aircraft, that a static run-up to at least 40% N1 before brake release was a prudent takeoff procedure for the conditions. We were cleared by DCA Tower to line up and wait on Runway 19 after an aircraft had landed. Almost immediately we were advised that we were cleared for takeoff with an aircraft on [a] 4 mile final. Being so heavy it took a little bit [of] extra time to taxi and maneuver into takeoff position on the runway. During taxi onto the runway, Tower kept updating us about the aircraft on final. I don't remember if they told us to expedite, but the tone of the Controller expressed urgency. Tower canceled takeoff clearance and told the aircraft on final approach to go around. We did the Rejected Takeoff procedure at about 10 knots and cleared the runway. We proceeded back to the Runway 19 holding pad.Tower felt we were too slow on taxi and take off roll. Tower should understand that a heavy aircraft moving slowly with a departure to a [distant] city should need more time for taxi and take off.				B737-800 F/O reported they rejected the takeoff at ATC request related to landing traffic separation issues.	
1713242	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 2					1713242												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Aircraft	Aircraft	At cruise, aft flight attendants complained of fumes near aft galley that smelled like burning plastic or electrical fire. No smoke was observed. No passengers complained. The two forward flight attendants went back and confirmed the smell, but were not exposed very long. The two aft flight attendants were exposed longer and complained of throat and chest pain. We elected to turn around and divert to ZZZ. The two aft flight attendants were taken for medical care. No one else was affected. On ground in ZZZ, passengers disembarked. ARFF personnel scanned the aft galley area with thermal imaging equipment and noticed nothing overheating. A mechanic searched the aft galley area and smelled nothing with the packs off, but did notice smell when right pack was turned on. Apparently something wrong in right pack causing fumes.				B737-800 Captain reported medical diversion after being notified by flight attendants of 'burning plastic/electrical fire' odor causing physiological issues. Two flight attendants received medical treatment.	
1713424	201912	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				31000		VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 64		Physiological - Other			1713424												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Human Factors	Human Factors	On the way to the airport, the FO stated he just started to have a little stomach issue but nothing serious. When we arrived at the airport he used the restroom and met me at the airplane. We performed our duties. Once airborne and 30 minutes enroute, he needed to use the restroom. He did throw up in a bag while in the cockpit. But he said he felt a little better afterwards. Halfway to [destination] he started to get the chills and put on his jacket. We discussed if we should divert or continue on. We elected to continue to destination. Restroom was used two more times. Each time feeling slightly better for the moment. We discussed having him sit in the [cabin] versus sitting in [cockpit] and having a FA take his role. We elected to keep everyone in their assigned duties. We landed without incident. Once we were taxiing to [the] gate, we notified Operations to send EMS over to the gate. All passengers disembarked the airplane as normal. Once everyone was off the airplane, the medical personnel showed up to give him assistance. Ultimately he was taken to the ER under the EMS care.				B737NG Captain reported that the F/O experienced stomach illness enroute to destination.	
1713427	201912						0			VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch	Direct																									RPM/N1/N2/Etc Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 174; Flight Crew Total 2513; Flight Crew Type 2513		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Other / Unknown; Workload			1713427	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 146; Flight Crew Total 2032; Flight Crew Type 1234		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Distraction			1714080	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	The Captain that brought the airplane into ZZZ briefed my Captain on why they wrote the autothrottles up. Supposedly, the autothrottle system had kicked off during their takeoff roll as well as in their climb because of a somewhat large split between the N1s. Or at least that was the presumption. Maintenance in ZZZ deferred the autothrottle system.During my Captain's brief, he mentioned that if he could not manually marry up the N1s to an acceptable level on the takeoff roll, he would reject because he felt that could possibly be an unsafe situation.During the takeoff roll, I manually set the thrust levers to achieve 40% N1 and waited for engine stabilization. I then advanced the thrust levers to about 80% N1 and hit the TOGA button. I continued advancing the thrust levers to somewhere around 85-90%N1 and called 'check thrust' while removing my hands from the thrust levers. The Captain then put his hands on the thrust lever and began fine tuning the N1s to meet target N1 of 95.5%.As we advanced down Runway XXL, I did not get the timely response of 'thrust set 95.5%' like I was expecting. I could tell the Captain was still fooling with the thrust levers out of the corner of my eye. I glanced at the N1s and saw about 91% on the left engine and 100% on the right. I took a look at the airspeed indicator and noticed about 105-110 kts. I made the call of '100 kts.' because Captain had missed it. I heard Captain say something to the affect of 'I can't get it to go past 91%'. It was not long after that when Captain called the reject. The last airspeed I recall seeing was 135-140 kts. I called the Tower and told them that we were rejecting the takeoff. The Tower copied and asked if we could exit left at [taxiway] after issuing a go around to aircraft behind us on final approach to Runway XXL. We complied and exited [taxiway]. I gave the 'Flight attendants remain seated' call. Captain called for the rejected takeoff QRC upon exit of runway, which we completed as well as the back page of the QRH.I then called for CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) and requested they take a look at our brakes as well as tires. CFR took a look and told us they read 100 degrees on whatever tool they use for inspection. We did not suspect tire damage after hearing back from CFR and did an after landing flow. We taxied back to the gate and Captain wrote up our rejected takeoff with speed of occurrence as well as why the reject occurred. We then handed aircraft over to Maintenance.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737 pilot crew reported that inability to match N1 power settings resulted in a rejected takeoff.	
1713758	201912	0001-0600	ZZZ.TRACON	US						IMC	Thunderstorm					TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Final Approach	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								ILS/VOR		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Distraction; Situational Awareness; Workload			1713758												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climb out, we had a left elevator probe light illuminate and it was determined that it was safe to continue the flight. On the ZZZ ILS XXL approach in IMC conditions with the glide slope and localizer captured and after passing the final approach fix the localizer rapidly went full scale deflection. I disconnected the autopilot and we immediately went missed approach. On the missed trying to regain control of heading and altitude, the caution bank angle sounded and I immediately corrected. Once stabilized on the missed and being vectored we double checked everything. We couldn't find anything abnormal and decided to try another approach to Runway XXL. On the second approach I coupled the second autopilot as the ceilings were lowering. Basically the same thing happened again. We entered a hold this time and again nothing was found to be wrong. We attempted to talk to Maintenance and Dispatch but the conversation was broken and couldn't be completed. Meanwhile our fuel was getting to the reserve quantity and the weather had deteriorated tremendously with thunderstorms over the field and low ceilings. It was then decided for safety to go to our alternate. The rest of the flight was uneventful ending with a normal ILS in ZZZ1. The return flight later in the day back to ZZZ was also uneventful with a normal ILS Runway XXL approach.We weren't able to determine what caused the localizer malfunction. The First Officer said he did see the elevator feel light flickering. In hindsight we probably should have gone to the alternate for better weather after the first go around.				B737-800 Captain reported a navigation error that resulted in a missed approach.	
1713772	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				7000		IMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Confusion; Distraction			1713772												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	While descending through about 7000 ft. MSL in IMC, PM (Pilot Monitoring) noticed his airspeed immediately dropped to 100 kias. PF (Pilot Flying) Airspeed was normal. PM was also PIC. PM immediately pushed power up and closed [the] throttles. At this point I saw the IAS disagree light and determined my IAS was correct. I disconnected [the] autopilot, said my aircraft and attempted to rejoin the STAR. During the event airspeed accelerated to almost 300 kias. We received a frequency change and slowed back to 250 kias. ATC did not notice the airspeed deviation. The CA (Captain) airspeed indicator recovered after about 40 seconds and worked for the remainder of the flight.Something caused the CA airspeed indicator to malfunction. The startle factor caused the Captain to assume [the] worst case and push power up before we were able to determine that his indicator was malfunctioning. By the time we knew all the information we had accelerated above 250 knots.				B737-800 First Officer reported the Captains airspeed indicator malfunctioned on the descent.	
1713984	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				20000		IMC	Cloudy; 7		Daylight	2500		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 440; Flight Crew Type 2419		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1713984												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Weather; Procedure	Weather	Descending on the STAR into ZZZ about 70 nm. out, we retrieved the updated ATIS as well as the [METAR]. Both indicated a tailwind exceeding limits into ZZZ on Runway XX. Winds were indicating about a 13 kt. tailwind. I asked [TRACON] if ZZZ was going to change Runways to XY since the winds were out of limits for Runway XX. They said they would ask and to continue on the 30 transition. We were switched over to another frequency and they asked what our limit was and we told them 10 kts. They said that ZZZ Tower said the winds were 120 at 9 kts. and to continue to Runway XX. Eventually we were cleared the ILS Runway XX and broke out into visual conditions about 2500 ft. MSL. We had Progress Page 2 up and were monitoring the tailwind as well as asking for multiple wind checks from Tower. Progress Page 2 showed a tailwind from 18-20 kts. all the way down final to include 150 ft. AGL where we initiated the go-around. During this time ZZZ Tower kept telling us surface winds were 120 at 9 kts. Tower saw we were going around and told us to fly runway heading and maintain 2,000 ft. The Pilot Flying hit TOGA and applied thrust to climb away and called for flaps 15. I moved the flaps to 15 and responded back to Tower.At this point the aircraft was accelerating and the Pilot Flying called for flaps 5 and I repeated and moved the flaps. The same with flaps 1. About 1,000 ft., we realized we had not placed the gear up, so we did so at this point. During this time I had to make multiple radio calls to ZZZ to confirm they wanted us runway heading instead of the published missed approach which was different. Once we got cleaned up, we were cleared to 5,000 ft. and placed on a heading back to Runway XX. Once everything had calmed down, the Pilot Flying and I realized we performed the incorrect procedure for the go-around and had cleaned up the aircraft fully prior to 1,000 ft. We debriefed the procedure and what we needed to do to mitigate the risks. We then started setting up for the next approach. At this point, the aircraft behind us on approach to Runway XX went around as well, I'm assuming due to winds. I called TRACON and asked them again if the Runway was going to be switched to XY. They were not sure and said it's Tower's decision and we might need to hold. Finally after 15 minutes of vectors, we were cleared the ILS Runway XY into ZZZ with no further incident. Pilot Flying and Pilot Monitoring further debriefed on the ground.The Pilot Flying and Pilot Monitoring both knew there was a strong chance of a go-around due to tailwind limitation. From that point, we both should have reviewed the procedures for a proper go-around and planned better in the case we needed to use it. Both pilots had close to minimum ground times on their overnights and better planning and talking through the procedure would have helped to mitigate the fatigue risk. As the Pilot Monitoring, I should have had better situational awareness as to our aircraft position and confirmed the appropriate callouts instead of trying to confirm communication with Tower. I should have been backing up the Pilot Flying in aviating first and communicating second. Talking through the procedure ahead of time and helping the Pilot Flying aviate first would have helped to mitigate the risk of cleaning up early.				B737 flight crew reported that a strong tailwind on final approach resulted in a go around.	
1714030	201912	1801-2400	RJJJ.ARTCC	FO				33000		VMC						Center RJJJ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																														Center RJJJ		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer							Cruise																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 119.42; Flight Crew Total 20487.75; Flight Crew Type 10487.75					1714030	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 72.07; Flight Crew Type 276.43					1714085	Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	Just prior to top of descent at FL 330 we experienced wake turbulence for 3-4 seconds. We went from smooth to light-mod turbulence according the iPad app. Seat belt sign was on. Flight attendants check-in PA was made and no injuries were reported. An aircraft was observed on TCAS 1,000 ft. above us perpendicular to our course. ATC was advised.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737NG flight crew reported encountering wake turbulence at FL330 in RJJJ airspace.	
1714144	201911	1801-2400	RFD.TRACON	IL				2100					Night			TRACON RFD	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Direct	Class D RFD																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Confusion; Human-Machine Interface			1714144												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Procedure; Chart Or Publication	Human Factors	We were departing. After takeoff at 400 ft. we executed direct to a fix and selected LNAV. At 1,000 ft., we set flaps up maneuver speed and climb thrust. At this time the First Officer stated Autopilot B on. We were given 3,000 ft. as our clearance altitude. I was busy swapping over to Departure and not looking up. As we were climbing through 2,100 ft., I heard bank angle. I looked up and noticed a 40-degree bank and Autopilot B not engaged. I quickly took the airplane and noticed we were on a roughly 220 heading instead of roughly 150 heading to the fix. Departure gave us a climb and asked if we were direct to the fix. We quickly recovered and loaded direct without further incident. This event was caused by over saturation and lack of system knowledge. We can learn to have better FMA awareness. As Pilot Monitoring, I should keep my head up more, and monitor what the Pilot Flying is doing.				B737 Captain reported that an autopilot failed to properly engage and resulted in a course deviation.	
1714226	201912	1801-2400	SEA.Airport	WA												TRACON S46	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B SEA																												TRACON S46	Air Carrier	B757 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B SEA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1714226												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	SEA was utilizing a south flow configuration even though surface winds were northerly 5-10 kt. We were turned in tight approximately 3-4 miles behind a B757 and instructed to maintain 190 KIAS to the marker for Runway 16R. I told the Controller unable due risk of unstable approach with tailwinds not to mention wake turbulence hazard behind 757. As our final speed in the B737-900 this day would easily be 25 kt more than a B757 there would be a significant chance of a wake turbulence encounter as our spacing tightened up. Approach Control countered with an instruction of 180 KIAS and we elected to continue. We soon encountered several sharp jolts of wake turbulence and my First Officer did a fine job of maneuvering above glide slope to get out of the wake. Our spacing with the B757 was quickly eroding as we were given instructions to contact the Tower. The Tower Controller quickly approved our request to sidestep to Runway 16L and we landed without further incident.The PIC is the final authority as to how the aircraft will be flown. The Approach Controller seemed incredulous that I did not wish to comply with her first request, but the reality of the situation was this was an unworkable plan. We elected to continue this approach with the assumption that we would either get to sidestep to 16L or go around. Lesson learned: be the PIC and speak up when you don't like what is happening. Recommendations: we had 13 kt of tailwind to about 500 feet AGL, why does SEATAC insist on the south flow configuration in these conditions? Also, 3 miles behind a B757, especially with a tailwind is not safe.				B737-900 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on approach to SEA in trail of a B757.	
1714450	201912	1201-1800	IAD.Airport	DC				8500		VMC						TRACON PCT	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B IAD																												TRACON PCT		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer									Class B IAD																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 138.92; Flight Crew Total 17500; Flight Crew Type 1897.82		Situational Awareness			1714450												Conflict Airborne Conflict			Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Human Factors	Ambiguous	During climbout from IAD on the JDUBB2 departure we were given a climb from 3,000 to 10,000 feet. The aircraft was very light. Rate of climb was higher than usual given the light weight, in excess of 4,000 feet per minute. TCAS alerted us to traffic passing above at 10,000 [feet] with a 'Traffic, Traffic' call followed immediately by a Resolution Advisory to 'Level Off.' I complied by lowering the pitch attitude to just below the Resolution Pitch Advisory indication on the ADI. The Clear of Conflict annunciation quickly followed and the climb to 10,000 feet was completed. Our aircraft was never closer than 3,000 feet to the target aircraft, we never completely leveled off or deviated from an assigned altitude.				B737NG Captain reported responding to an RA departing IAD.	
1714454	201912	1201-1800	ZJX.ARTCC	FL				36000		VMC						Center ZJX	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZJX																												Center ZJX	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZJX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 138; Flight Crew Type 1897					1714454	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 217.18; Flight Crew Type 1961.03					1714445	Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	During cruise climb to 38,000 feet and passing 36,000 feet flight encountered unexpected wake turbulence. Climbing at .77 Mach with a pitch attitude of approximately 7 degrees nose up and autopilot engaged, aircraft suddenly shuddered and went into a right bank of approximately 25 degrees. I immediately disconnected the autopilot and rolled wings level with a pitch attitude on the horizon. As the aircraft was climbing we did not lose altitude but rate of climb was reduced to near zero. The climb was then continued to 38,000 feet. Bank limits were not exceeded and the aircraft was not over stressed. I called the Purser and was informed that everything was fine in the cabin. Subsequently I learned that a Flight Attendant in the rear of the cabin had bumped her shin on the service cart during the wake turbulence encounter. She informed me that she thought she was fine and had not been injured. I have included potential injury as part of the event report as a precaution in case something manifests itself in the future. Dispatch was notified via ACARS and a phone patch was set up with the [Chief Pilot] for the purpose of verbally debriefing the event and a potential crew member injury. The nearest traffic to our position at the time of the wake encounter was an Airbus (A320) approximately 8 miles ahead and 2,000 feet above. The flight continued uneventfully. I continued to monitor the Flight Attendant's condition throughout the flight, and on the return flight. She continued to feel that she had not sustained any injury.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737NG flight crew reported a Flight Attendant was slightly injured during a wake turbulence encounter at FL360.	
1714458	201912	1801-2400	ZHU.ARTCC	TX				22000		IMC	Rain		Dawn			Center ZHU	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb; Initial Climb		Class A ZHU																								Indicating and Warning - Lighting Systems		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 192; Flight Crew Type 1249					1714458												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	Aircraft	Aircraft	Passing roughly 2,000 feet, autopilot off, FO (First Officer) hand flying, get direct CSHEL. I'm heads down in the box, when my peripheral sees an ECAM and System page on lower ECAM pop up. By the time I execute the direct and look over, [the] screens go back to normal [and there is] nothing on [the] Status page. FO has no clue, but I know some thing's up.Passing roughly 4,000 feet, No Smoking and LDG light green indications on upper ECAM screen cycle on and off a few times, then return to normal. Passing roughly 12,000 feet, 'Ding,' ECAM: C/B tripped rear panel W-Z. No breakers are tripped, but ECAM stays displayed for about 3 minutes. Then goes out. Passing roughly 18,000 feet, 'Ding,' ECAM: HYD B RSVR LO AIR PR. On status page, HYD B quantity good, system output press good - 3,000 - but air pressure indicating low.[I] messaged Technical Support Maintenance Control, [and] they ask about quantity and fluid pressure. I reply, 'Both normal.' ECAM lasts roughly 3 minutes and then clears itself. Passing roughly 22,000, LDG LT indication on upper ECAM screen cycles on and off. Finally extinguishes after two minutes.I messaged Technical Support Maintenance Control again [and] ask what's going on. They ignore me [and] never reply. In order to document [them], I wrote up all 4, tripped C/B, HYD low air pressure, smoking light, landing light. While this is all going on, I'm thinking the jet might be on fire. Always my first thought when random, inexplicable things start happening. Two thorough cycles through the system pages reveal all normal, which puts me at ease. Once established at cruise, all indications [were] normal and we continued [the] flight with no further incident.				A320 Captain reported multiple ECAM messages, advisory lights, and ECAM displays switching, then dissipating during flight with no observable systems failing.	
1714532	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000		VMC			Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ	N	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing; Work Cards																		Pneumatic Valve/Bleed Valve		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1714532	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1714535	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Aircraft	Aircraft	The aircraft was indicated to have arrived in ZZZ from ZZZ1. The inbound crew recorded an aircraft discrepancy in the AML for a 'BLEED TRIP OFF' on final approach into ZZZ, which the prior crew noted was successfully reset using QRH procedures.At the direction of Company Maintenance, an engine run was performed in ZZZ in accordance with Company Maintenance procedures to evaluate the status of previously noted BLEED TRIP OFF write up prior to logbook sign off. A Contract Maintenance Technician overseen by Company Maintenance provided test card procedure, accompanied the two pilots for an off-gate medium/high power engine run. The results of the engine run procedure indicated normal pneumatic system operation with no defects noted.After the engine run, the aircraft returned to the gate so that bags could be loaded and passengers boarded. At XA33, the aircraft departed ZZZ enroute for ZZZ1. Normal systems operations were noted for taxi, takeoff, climb - with intermittent level-offs - and approximately 35-minutes of cruise flight at FL380. A very slight pressure bump was felt which coincided with the R BLEED TRIP OFF light illuminating on the Pneumatics System overhead panel. After identifying the Pilot Flying, the QRH was referenced for the appropriate checklist procedure. The BLEED TRIP OFF light failed to reset, which resulted in the R A/C Pack being placed in the 'OFF' position in compliance with QRH procedure.Shortly after the completion of the QRH procedure, the crew discussed and agreed that a descent from FL380 to a lower altitude and lower PSID would probably be a wise course of action. A lower altitude was requested and ATC began to coordinate and approve the aircraft descent. Upon passing approximately FL360, the cabin altitude was noted to be rising and the red CABIN ALTITUDE Warning light illuminated, accompanied by the warning horn. The QRC and QRH were referenced for the appropriate procedures. With the gradual loss of cabin pressurization, not immediately arrested by procedure, the crew complied with emergency descent procedures and [requested priority handling] with ATC. The cabin altitude continued to climb, which was noted to top out at nearly 12,000 feet on the pressurization dial, while the emergency descent procedure was maintained.The aircraft was leveled at 10,000 MSL, and the cabin was still partially pressurized. A check with the cabin crew confirmed that the passenger oxygen masks had not deployed. The flight diverted to ZZZ2 where an uneventful approach and landing were executed. All passengers and crew deplaned normally and without any noted issues.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737 flight crew reported a recurrence of a BLEED TRIP OFF light inflight, causing a loss of cabin pressure, and resulting in a diversion and precautionary emergency landing.	
1714575	201912		ORD.Airport	IL				1800								Tower ORD	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B ORD																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer				Workload			1714575												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Aircraft; Airport; Human Factors	Human Factors	I (FO (First Officer)) was PM (Pilot Monitoring). Arrival to airport began with a late brief, somewhat late checklist calls and cabin PAs. ILS capture issues due to distance. Autopilot was actually steering the wrong way. Quickly realized the issue, but had trouble getting back into LNAV for better course tracking since it had already 'captured'. Still high and quite a ways out; had safe time and distance and altitude to solve the issue. Vertical deviations throughout this approach were minor, lateral deviations more pronounced. Deviations were called and were responded to by PF (Pilot Flying), always in the appropriate magnitude and direction. Despite this, there were many overshoots. They were not sustained overshoots. I strongly suspect ground aircraft intrusion into the ILS safety zone on taxiway could be the cause of needle fluctuations and subsequent chasing by PF in order to keep it 'corralled'. ATIS advertised 6SM BR OVC250. I found the conditions to be much closer to MVFR, with low clouds beginning to form near the airport. Airport was in sight around 2000 agl. Lost sight of airport around 1800 agl down to 1200 agl. During this time, lateral instability increased. Deviations called and responded to appropriately, but with overshoots. Energy state was always safe and we were vertically stable. Airport was in sight, aircraft fully configured, and lateral corrections in progress by 1000 agl. Stable all axes by 500 agl, and landed in the touchdown zone on speed.I replayed this approach in my mind many times and referenced the FOM and FH. I came to the following evaluation: scan policy was followed, stabilized approach criteria and configuration were met by the appropriate gates, and the next gate was continuously achievable, bracketing corrections due to conditions were correct and overshoots were not sustained. 500 agl to touchdown was stable and landed on speed in the touchdown zone. Approach callouts were not made except for 'landing' as I (PM (Pilot Monitoring)) was in tunnel vision on monitoring path.All this being said, as I believe we followed all policy and met all gates, for me this approach felt like the least successful of all my time at (airline), as it was near max tolerance several times, which is just so out of the norm for me. Hence, I decided to fill out this [report].As PM, I should have been more assertive in prompting briefs and checklists at the standardized times. Considering the 'feel' of the approach, I should have called for a go around when sight of the airport was lost. And I should not have missed the approach callouts.				B737-900 First Officer reported a safe landing followed a challenging approach that involved a number of lateral and vertical deviations that were within allowable limits.	
1714577	201912	0001-0600	ZAN.ARTCC	AK				36000			Windshear						Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Oceanic	Class A ZAN																								Communication Systems		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1714577												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather; Procedure; Aircraft	Ambiguous	We were flying from ZZZ to ANC and we were CPDLC. We had been step climbing as our optimum altitude increased and had been at FL380 for probably about 20 or 30 minutes. We had been mostly smooth at that altitude with just a few occasional bumps. Just about 60 to 80 miles south of PADQ we received an ATC message to contact Center.At almost the same exact time we entered into an atmospheric wave and our airspeed began fluctuating. I was the PF (Pilot Flying) and the CA (Captain) was the PM (Pilot Monitoring). The CA switched to the given frequency and attempted to make contact with Center. At this time we received another message from CPDLC saying that communication with them had ended. After multiple tries and unable to reach Center the CA tried using guard. There was still no reply.At this point our airspeed fluctuations had increased to going between almost barber pole and almost low speed yellow band. I had disconnected the auto throttles in an attempt to not over speed. Our route of flight at this point was not on a published route and we saw no one on TCAS so the CA said (and I agreed) that we should descend. We descended down to FL360. This altitude was much smoother. During the descent we continued to try and contact Center and were trying to look up a different frequency. The CA was about to send Dispatch a message when a Company flight answered us on guard and gave us the frequency that they were on, but that still didn't work. Finally Company asked Center what frequency we should be on and passed it on to us. It was close to but not the same frequency as what CPDLC had given us, leading us to conclude they had given us the wrong frequency.Once in contact with Center we told them what had happened. They told us we could maintain FL360. We gave them a pilot report of moderate to severe atmospheric wave with +/- 15 to 20 kts. The flight proceeded without further incident. After landing the CA called Dispatch and informed them of what had happened.I believe we acted appropriately for the given situation. The CA used his [PIC] authority the aircraft flying safely and to keep from exceeding any airspeed limits.The lapse in communication seems to be due to being given the wrong frequency as well as being unable to reach Center on guard while simultaneously being disconnected from CPDLC.				Air carrier flight crew encountered an atmospheric wave which caused large fluctuations in airspeed and necessitated a descent.	
1714727	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ										4														Powerplant Fuel Distribution		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1714727	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1714728	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight departed ZZZ1 under normal winter conditions after deicing. Our fuel amount was above planned minimum fuel at takeoff. The FO (First Officer) was the PF (Pilot Flying). The CA (Captain) was the PM (Pilot Monitoring). The flight was proceeding without incident and all fuel indications were normal until the Captain was preparing to shut off the center fuel pumps. He noticed a fuel imbalance of 300-400 lbs. The CA corrected the fuel imbalance. It then became imbalanced again in a relatively short amount of time. It was at this time that we both observed a drastic drop in fuel on the left main tank. It showed a loss of approximately 500 lbs. in about 3 minutes. I know that fuel fluctuations can be normal when the center fuel pumps are shut off but this seemed excessive. The rate of fuel loss from the left tank seemed to slow after that initial drop but we were closely monitoring the amounts and observed an imbalance of approx. 900 lbs. Once the fuel amount on the left tank stabilized we started discussing the possible cause of the fluctuations. And it was at that time that we started watching the total fuel burns more intently. Prior to the all of this happening, the recorded fuel burn at ZZZ2 on the flight plan was 400 lbs. over scheduled burn which seemed slightly unusual for the conditions but as we watched the FMC planned burn at ZZZ3 we realized that we were going to be over the scheduled burn by almost 2,000 lbs., which was excessive. According to the scheduled flight plan fuel burn, the fuel amount at ZZZ3 should have been 15.4. We crossed ZZZ3 with 13.5 indicated. We believed that we could have a fuel leak due to the amount of fuel loss in such a short amount of time and the fact that the total amount of fuel onboard was much lower than planned.The CA went into the QRH and went to the Fuel Leak - Engine checklist. We agreed that we met the criteria for the checklist and continued. The CA proceeded with the checklist, talked to the FAs (Flight Attendants), and contacted Dispatch and Maintenance. We asked the FAs to check for evidence of a fuel leak. The FA said they saw moisture on the left wing, but not the right wing, and the FA did not see mist coming from the wing. The CA's wife was also onboard the plane. She is a professional pilot so we asked her to check for evidence of a fuel leak. She reiterated what the FA said about moisture on the wing, but no mist spotted. While the CA was doing that, I was pulling the weather for ZZZ4 and ZZZ. Both airports looked good but the nearest airport was ZZZ. I selected the frequency and course for the runway listed on ATIS in case we decided to go there. We proceeded with the checklist. The CA requested a descent to our drift down altitude of 22,000 ft., briefed the FAs, advised the passengers, requested priority handling, pulled landing data for ZZZ, and shut down the left engine per the checklist. I was flying the aircraft and talking to ATC when needed.While all this was going on we were trying to communicate with Dispatch. They were asking a lot of questions via ACARS, we were very busy, and it seemed quicker to ask them to contact us via the ZZZ Operations frequency since the aircraft didn't have SATCOM. This proved difficult because we couldn't get a hold of ZZZ Operations and Dispatch couldn't hear us reply on that frequency. We finally talked to Operations once we were much closer but we never had a chance to talk to Dispatch in the air.I landed the aircraft single engine and slightly overweight at approximately 146,000 lbs. for a Flaps 15 landing, normally max of 144,200. The landing was uneventful and at a normal rate of descent. We taxied to the gate under our own power.		Flight XXX departed ZZZ under normal winter conditions after deicing. Our fuel amount was above planned minimum fuel at takeoff. The FO was the PF. The CA was the PM. The flight was proceeding without incident and all fuel indications were normal until the captain was preparing to shut off the center fuel pumps. He noticed a fuel imbalance of 300-400 lbs. The CA corrected the fuel imbalance. It then became imbalanced again in a relatively short amount of time. It was at this time that we both observed a drastic drop in fuel on the left main tank. It showed a loss of approximately 500lbs in about 3 minutes. I know that fuel fluctuations can be normal when the center fuel pumps are shut off but this seemed excessive. The rate of fuel loss from the left tank seemed to slow after that initial drop but we were closely monitoring the amounts and observed an imbalance of approx. 900lbs. Once the fuel amount on the left tank stabilized we started discussing the possible cause of the fluctuations. And it was at that time that we started watching the total fuel burns more intently. Prior to the all of this happening, the recorded fuel burn at ZZZ2 on the flight plan was 400 over scheduled burn which seemed slightly unusual for the conditions but as we watched the FMC planned burn at ZZZ3 we realized that we were going to be over the scheduled burn by almost 2,000 lbs, which was excessive. According to the scheduled flight plan fuel burn, the fuel amount at ZZZ3 should have been 15.4. We crossed ZZZ3 with 13.5 indicated. We believed that we could have a fuel leak due to the amount of fuel loss in such a short amount of time and the fact that the total amount of fuel onboard was much lower than planned.The CA went into the QRH and went to the Fuel Leak - Engine checklist. We agreed that we met the criteria for the checklist and continued. The CA  proceeded with the checklist, talked to the FAs, and contacted Dispatch and Maintenance. We asked the FAs to check for evidence of a fuel leak. The FA said they saw moisture on the left wing, but not the right wing, and the FA did not see mist coming from the wing. While the CA was doing that, I was pulling the weather for ZZZ2 and ZZZ1. Both airports looked good but the nearest airport was ZZZ1. I selected the frequency and course for the runway listed on ATIS in case we decided to go there. We proceeded with the checklist. The CA requested a descent to our drift down altitude of 22,000, briefed the FAs, advised the passengers, requested priority handling, pulled landing data, and shutdown the left engine per the checklist. I was flying the aircraft and talking to ATC when needed.While all this was going on we were trying to communicate with Dispatch. They were asking a lot of questions via ACARS, we were very busy, and it seemed quicker to ask them to contact us via the Ops frequency since the aircraft didn't have SATCOM. This proved difficult because we couldn't get a hold of Ops and Dispatch couldn't hear us reply on that frequency. We finally talked to Ops once we were much closer but we never had a chance to talk to Dispatch in the air.I landed the aircraft single engine and slightly overweight at approx 146,000 lbs for a Flaps 15 landing, normally max of 144,200. The landing was uneventful and at a normal rate of descent. We taxied to the gate under our own power.		Flight Crew reported increasing fuel loss in flight, which resulted in an engine shutdown and diversion.	
1714863	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		3		6000		IMC	10		Night	2000		TRACON ZZZ; Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ; Class C ZZZ																								Electrical Wiring & Connectors		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 365; Flight Crew Type 16000					1714863	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 366; Flight Crew Type 3000		Physiological - Other			1714884	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight; Taxi	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	The First Officer noticed smoke coming from the flight counter on his yoke. We [advised ATC] and returned.		Just prior to takeoff, both the Captain and I noticed a smell that we associated with the air conditioning system. The smell was faint prior to takeoff. After takeoff and selecting the flaps up, the smell became increasingly strong and changed to a burning plastic smell. I placed my hand on the upper half of the control wheel to initiate a turn towards a heading of 280. The flight counter (upper left) felt very hot to the touch, enough so that I couldn't touch that portion of the control wheel. I turned on my reading light and engaged the autopilot and saw smoke emanating from the flight counter. I informed the Captain and we immediately dawned masks and established Crew comm. We informed Departure of our [situation] and intention to return. Control was transferred to the Captain and I began running the smoke and fumes checklist. ATC initially vectored us to one runway then changed to a different runway which led to a 360 turn to better establish ourselves to land and configure. Captain gave me control back of the airplane, landed, and taxied to the gate uneventful after Ground EMS cleared us.		Flight crew reported burning smell and smoke emanating from the yoke on departure and returned to land.	
1715041	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				12000									Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1715041	Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Air Carrier	Flight Attendant (On Duty)			Service; Safety Related Duties	Physiological - Other			1715772	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We pushed back in the alley and started both engines. I noticed a smell of jet exhaust, but it wasn't too bad in my mind. The smell dissipated by the time we began our taxi, maybe 2 minutes. We taxied to 10R following 2 airplanes, I got one whiff of exhaust as we moved forward. But, again, not bad or even worth mentioning to the FO [First Officer]. The FO was PF Pilot Flying] and I was the PM [Pilot Monitoring]. We climbed to FL200, cruised for a few minutes and then descended via ZZZZZ arrival. Descending through about 12,000 feet we received a call from the FA [Flight Attendant]. I answered the call. I can't remember which FA called. She said 'I believe we had an air quality event'. I asked if everyone was OK and to describe her symptoms and what the odor was. She said the FA's in the back seemed to feel worse with scratchy eyes, scratchy throat, and a slight headache. We were now passing through approximately 9000 feet. I asked if we needed to have anyone meet them at the gate (medical wise). She said no, as the smell was gone and they weren't getting worse. I told them we'd be on the ground and at the gate in less than 15 minutes. They weren't sure what the odor was exactly, but when I asked if it [could] have been exhaust, she said that was what it most resembled, but was not sure. We landed and the FO made sure to have Maintenance meet us at the gate. As the customers were getting off the plane, I spoke with the mechanic and told him what happened. After everyone was off the plane the whole crew met up in the forward galley and we talked about the event. I asked about when they first smelled the odor. It was at cruise when they first smelled it and felt the symptoms. I asked the FA's if they were OK and if they needed medical attention. They said no. We left the aircraft with the mechanics. I asked if he wanted me write up the odor, because I had not put anything in the logbook. He told me that he didn't need it in the book. The FO and I debriefed the event and talked about it from start to finish. He also noticed the smell of exhaust, but didn't say anything because it happens from time to time. And the smell dissipated rather quickly. I wasn't sure where to file the report, I picked crew illness because I later found out that the 3 FA's were taken off of their trip due to the Air Quality Event. We were rushing to get to our next flight. We were at gate XX and were going to XY and had about 55 minutes to do so. I should have called a timeout and read the FOM about Air Quality Events. I should have written it up in the logbook and called the [Duty Officer]. Lesson learned - don't allow the tight schedules and plane swaps influence the task at hand.		I was the 'B' FA, After Door closure and demo, and compliance checks we sat in jump[seat] and noticed a strong smell, it smelled like exhaust fumes, the plane backed out and taxied to takeoff, smell continued to worsen. We were covering our nose and mouth and hoping it would dispute. Our eyes and throat were burning, and headaches began, at 10,000 ft we got up to do service. As we reached the main cabin it seemed to dissipate....but then the 'C' flight attendant and I noticed we started to feel fuzzy headed and lack of clarity...we completed our short pub service, trash and compliance and took our jump seats. Upon arrival and deplaning our symptoms seem to worsen...the gate agent also noticed the same smell upon our arrival. As we began to debrief with CA and FO it became clear none of us were fit to fly. We called Supervisor and went to ER in ZZZ. Suggestion: call forward to flight deck immediately upon noticing abnormal exhaust fume levels! Request back to gate!		A320 Captain reported Flight Attendant notification during initial descent of 'air quality event' in the cabin with Flight Attendants experiencing physiological symptoms.	
1715058	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																														Commercial Fixed Wing							Taxi																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Other		1715058	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Other		1715073	Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff	Human Factors	Human Factors	On takeoff roll at approximately 110 kts, Tower directed an abort due to a potential runway incursion by another aircraft. A rejected take off was accomplished normally. We returned to the gate for additional fuel and required inspections.		We were cleared for takeoff on Runway XXL in ZZZ. I was the PM (Pilot Monitoring)and the FO (First Officer) was the PF (Pilot Flying). We were accelerating to 110 kts when Tower instructed us to abort the takeoff due to an aircraft that had made a wrong turn onto Taxiway November and was about to cross our runway in front of us. We immediately initiated our aborted takeoff procedures and this combined with RTO braking allowed us to stop safely and short of Taxiway November.After clearing the runway, we taxied back to the gate. We coordinated with Operations, Dispatch, the [Assistant Chief] and Maintenance control to have the brakes inspected, determine cooling time required, refuel and plan for the continuation of the flight. We also checked with our FAs (Flight Attendants) and passengers to be sure everyone was OK to continue. We then pushed back and proceeded without further incident.		Air carrier flight crew reported a high-speed rejected takeoff as directed by the tower controller to avoid an aircraft that was about to taxi across the runway.	
1715070	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class B ZZZ	Y	Y	Y	Y																				Hydraulic System Pump		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1715070												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Company Policy; MEL; Manuals; Procedure	Company Policy	Our aircraft had 5 MELs and numerous other NEFs in ZZZ that we briefed thoroughly before departure. One of these was a deferred Pack 1. During cruise flight we spent a good amount of time briefing the arrival giving consideration to the icing reports, FICONs (Field Conditions), and gusty crosswinds we could expect. 45 mins prior to landing we received an ECAM for HYD G RSVR LO LVL and HYD G ENG 1 PUMP LO PR. We ran the ECAM and began to consider the ramifications. As speed breaks were partially affected, we knew the MEL for 1 pack inoperative now had an altitude restriction of FL310. We requested that altitude and advised ATC we were working a hydraulic issue and would get back to them with our intentions. With the ECAM complete we began to work through and brief the accompanying procedures. We ran the QRH landing distance numbers with G HYD LO PRESS and asked Dispatch to do the same via ACARS. Dispatch gave numbers quickly considering the FICONS and suggested 22L as the longest runway. We reviewed the now required landing gear gravity extension procedure and other inoperative items used for landing such as the number 1 thrust reverser, alternate brakes with accumulator, lack of auto brake, and slats flaps slow. Next the Flight Attendants were briefed and I told them I would soon talk with the passengers. We [requested priority handling] with ATC. Once down the landing gear could not be raised again and we wanted to decrease the chances of a go around in icing conditions with the gear down. We asked for trucks to inspect the aircraft after landing before taxiing into the gate. Passengers were then informed of the issue. I told the passengers this is not an emergency and that they would not notice anything different other than emergency vehicles meeting us at the runway to give us a quick inspection. Their pilots are frequently trained for these things and other than the pilots having a slightly increased workload it would be a normal landing. On approach to 22L we extended the gear via gravity extension and flaps sooner than normal. The landing gear doors remained down and we discussed the need to ensure they would clear anything we might taxi over later. The landing was normal and soft and the FO (First Officer) verified the correct brake pressures. We stopped the aircraft and set the parking brake. As the FO coordinated with ATC for our inspection which ATC preferred on the runway, I said a quick word to the passengers. Fire crews found no issues upon inspection and we taxied to the gate. Upon shutdown passengers were invited to ask the pilots any questions they had, all were happy. Flight attendants were happy and thanked for keeping passengers so content. Maintenance control and the [Operations] were called. A Mechanic upon arrival showed us hydraulic fluid draining from the engine one pylon. No additional commentsThe workload on the pilots was significantly higher throughout this flight due to the large number of deferrals even before our loss of a hydraulic system.				Captain reported an ECAM message in flight, HYD G RSVR LO LVL and HYD G ENG 1 PUMP LOPR, causing an inflight shut down and a precautionary landing.	
1715078	201912	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				37000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Fluid		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload			1715078												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight XXX was delayed prior to departure 1 hr as Flight Crew and Maintenance worked through 5 MELs and numerous NEFs (Nonessential Equipment and Furnishings). During cruise, FL370, (1 hr from scheduled touchdown) we received a green hydraulic low ECAM followed 2 min later by a low pressure ECAM of the green pump. ECAM was initiated by the Captain. PF (Pilot Flying) duties were transferred to the FO (First Officer) and Dispatch was contacted via ACARS. After discussing options with crew and Dispatch ZZZ was determined as the landing location. Priority handling was requested from ATC and inflight was briefed with what to expect. After all members of the team were up to speed the Captain informed the passengers what to expect. FO continued the PF duties through the descent with a thorough landing briefing. Runway XXL was requested as it was the longest available runway. Once we were released to approach control duties were reversed, Captain PF and FO was PM (Pilot Monitoring). At roughly 15 miles from touchdown Captain called for gravity gear extension from the QRH as per the the briefing. Once on the ground the aircraft was stopped on the runway while fire crash and rescue did a thorough inspection of the aircraft. It was determined that the aircraft was safe to taxi to the gate under its own power. After reaching the gate the Captain made a PA allowing for any questions from the passengers. Passengers were unloaded with no delay. After all checklist items were completed, Maintenance Control, Dispatch, and Assistant Chief on duty was contacted. Timely debrief took place with inflight team, Maintenance personnel on the ground, and Customer Service. Ground Maintenance confirmed hydraulic quantity was low on the Green system and hydraulic fluid was leaking from the Number 1 Pylon. No additional comments added.Work load was significantly increased due to the amount of deferrals on this aircraft. I have a growing concern of the sheer amount of deferrals I've observed on Airbus aircraft in recent months.				A320 First Officer reported receiving two hydraulic system malfunction messages during cruise, which resulted in a diversion and successful landing.	
1715281	202001	1201-1800	TPA.Airport	FL		3	0						Daylight			Ground TPA; TRACON TPA; Tower TPA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS 1L	Initial Approach; Landing; Final Approach	Direct	Class B TPA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 346		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1715281												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Landing Without Clearance			Person Flight Crew	Taxi	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	We flew a long pattern to Runway 1L arriving from the north and sequenced behind all other traffic. We were cleared for a visual approach well south of MacDill, already slowed to 210 kts. We intercepted the glide slope and flew an extremely smooth and uneventful approach. I remember thinking that it was awfully quiet, but not unusual since we were the last on approach. Mostly, I was glad to be done with a turbulent day and appreciative of the nice weather in Tampa. Only after clearing the runway did we recognize the error, as Ground Control had not been preset on Radio 1. We were cleared to taxi to our gate after a slight hesitation from Ground. Not as an excuse, but as a factor, Approach did not send us to Tower prior to landing.				B737 Captain reported landing without a clearance.	
1715335	202001	0001-0600	ATL.Airport	GA			50			IMC						Tower ATL	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B ATL																												Tower ATL	Air Carrier	Airbus Industrie Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Landing		Class B ATL																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 182; Flight Crew Total 6628; Flight Crew Type 2628		Situational Awareness			1715335	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 233; Flight Crew Type 655					1715344	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	Ceilings were sufficient for CAT I approach, but very close to minimums. On final, Approach and Tower issued wake turbulence advisory for heavy Airbus in front of us (6-7 miles) per TCAS. As First Officer Pilot Flying approach was stable (autopilot engaged). Approach light system was acquired just above minimums, and then runway/markings/lighting was acquired prior to 100 feet per FOM. I stated 'Continue' instead of 'landing' due to lights vs runway contrary to the FOM, creating a challenge from PM (CA) to confirm my intentions. Past the threshold (below 50 feet), the aircraft began to roll and was pushed slightly left of centerline due to wake turbulence. I rolled the aircraft back and attempted to correct back to centerline. I accepted a landing slightly left of centerline and the landing rollout was uneventful. After landing, ACARS data reflected a 5.0 degree bank on landing (maximum 7.5). In hindsight, a go-around would have been a better choice.		We were cautioned following heavy on ILS 8L. We saw we were 5 miles in trail. Per FOM since it was First Officer's leg we briefed the ILS CAT1 approach and back up CAT3. When we left [departure airport], forecast was well above CAT 1. RVR was 2,000, so we briefed that just prior to FAF get final RVR and if need be switch to CAT 3 change mins from CAT 1 barometric to CAT 3 radio alt mins and Captain take control. Second auto pilot was turned on when approach clearance given.At about 50 feet, I observed the nose come up slightly and I thought First Officer was starting flare early although very small up nose. Then at about 30 feet, we got a roll input which the First Officer countered and we rolled in opposite direction and drifted well left of center line. As I was about to call go around, the First Officer stabilized the situation and we landed left of centerline. It was by no means a hard landing in vertical drop and roll was stable. However much to my surprise when we checked post report on roll it showed 5 degrees with wingtip contact at 7.5. I would never thought it was that close to wing contact.Post shut down debrief reveal the first error on my part was engaging second autopilot when a First Officer non autoland was planned. Looking up in FM second autopilot can be turned on NLT 800 feet. This should have been delayed until decision to change to CAT if needed prior to FAF. This caused aircraft to trim nose up and it was that nose up trim the First Officer was fighting when he turned autopilot off. Second now that I have a better 'picture' of what excessive roll looks like a go-around should have been called.I can say though had RVR dropped and it was an autoland the first rolling movement would definitely command a go-around as an auto and would not have been successful.		B737NG flight crew reported continuing to landing after an uncommanded nose up and roll was observed on short final.	
1715441	202001	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 19800					1715441	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1715434	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	During takeoff noticed [a] strong 'dirty sock' smell. Donned oxygen masks. Confirmed with flight attendants that smell was present throughout cabin. Advised ATC and returned to ZZZ. After blocking in, ZZZ Maintenance told us, that they had done an engine wash the previous night. We were the first departure afterwards. Re-examine the engine wash procedure. This was my second odor and fumes event after an engine wash. Both ended in priority returns to the departure airport. The pack burn after an engine wash is apparently done at 70% power. Takeoff power is much higher!		Departing ZZZ, Captain noticed an acrid odor and verified with First Officer and cabin crew. Advised with ATC and returned to ZZZ. Maintenance confirmed an engine wash was completed overnight prior to this first launch of the day. Odor in cabin. Review maintenance procedures for pack burn after engine wash.		B737-800 flight crew and Flight Attendant reported a 'dirty socks, musty' odor during takeoff and executed an air return.	
1715444	201912	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			IMC							Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Ice/Rain Protection System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface			1715444												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Diverted	Procedure; Human Factors; Aircraft	Ambiguous	Arriving at the aircraft during boarding, the FO advised me that he had found 'a lot of things messed up' in the cockpit and that the aircraft (which we ourselves had flown in the night before) had obviously been seen by maintenance overnight. On pushback, I noted a large number of aircraft awaiting takeoff clearance, and so I told the FO to plan a single-engine taxi-out until the lineup for takeoff was shorter, which we did. We started the #1 engine roughly 5 minutes before takeoff, and I believe we accomplished all taxi and before takeoff checks in an unhurried manner and pace, with nothing abnormal to note. I specifically remember seeing the ELEC legend on my '6-pack' of annunciators remain illuminated before we commenced single-engine taxi, and noted that this was normal for single-engine operation, but I do not recall seeing any lights on the FO's 'six-pack.'Climbing through roughly FL250 on departure from ZZZ, I saw we'd have to climb through a thin layer of cloud and the SAT was below 10, so I reached up to activate the engine anti-ice. As I did so, I noted three annunciators illuminated for the FO's pitot, AOA vane, and elevator feel anti-ice, for all of which the switch was already in the ON position, and I believe I heard an audible 'click' at the same time. I called the FO's attention to the annunciators, and he replied that he had not seen them illuminated before I did. I asked him to do a RECALL check of the annunciators and, doing so, the ANTI-ICE legend remained illuminated on the 'six-pack' of caution annunciators on his side. Both of us were tempted to recycle the affected pitot-heat switch to AUTO and back to ON, but I directed him to consult the QRH and complete the applicable procedure, which he did while I continued PF duties. The procedure does not call or allow for any switch or CB resets, and only informs crew that the affected components are no longer anti-iced, and to avoid icing conditions. Since we were now above at least two layers of cloud, and since our intended destination, ZZZ1, was also at or near freezing and beneath at least one layer of an overcast, I informed dispatch of the need to avoid icing and possibly divert from ZZZ1. After a considerable length of time I did not measure but which felt like roughly half an hour, Dispatch 'seemed' to direct us to divert to ZZZ2, which had few clouds, but since the distance/time involved appeared it would leave us close to critical on fuel, so I asked them to provide a fuel projection, which did not come within several minutes. At this point, both the FO and I were concerned that further hesitation could create a critical fuel situation when none might have existed absent our hesitation, so we informed ATC that we needed to divert to ZZZ2, based upon Dispatch's advice and my own projection of being able to land with just-legal reserves. At that point, dispatch sent another message which 'seemed' to change their direction for us to divert to ZZZ3, and since this was by now (being just west of ZZZ4) far closer, we informed ATC of our need to change destination again, to ZZZ3, and asked dispatch to clarify if they were officially directing us to divert or not, as their communications had seemed ambiguous to this point and seemed to leave the debate/question unresolved. We did receive a  clarification soon thereafter, however, and diverted to ZZZ3 uneventfully, avoiding not only all icing, but all visible moisture.Once at ZZZ3, contract maintenance came aboard and, upon direction from [Maintenance Control], checked the CB's, finding a total of 4 (four) popped behind the captain's seat. 3 corresponded to the 3 annunciator lights which had illuminated on the overhead anti-ice panel, and the fourth was for HF radio #2, which we had not attempted to use. Seeing those breakers popped, hearing from the tech on the phone that the fact that a fourth anti-ice equipment control breaker was NOT popped while the other 3 were was extremely unlikely, and knowing from my review of the AML that the crew oxygen had been serviced the night before, I began to question whether they had in fact popped in the climb after takeoff, as I had perceived, or if they had been popped the night before, with the annunciators just not getting our attention before that, as adherence to pre-taxi/taxi and before takeoff checklists should have provided, via the RECALL function. Contract maintenance reset all the breakers, directed us to run the #2 engine with the anti-ice first OFF, then ON, then OFF again, noted normal operation of everything, and sent us on to ZZZ1.This event occurred because EITHER: 1) both one or more mechanics AND two pilots did not adhere to good practices and/or written procedures, causing a) circuit breakers pulled as part of a maintenance function not to get reset, b) the breakers pulled to escape both pilots' attentions during preflight, and c) the flight crew to remain ignorant of the inoperative status of the equipment affected by the breakers because of their failure to adhere to procedures developed to direct their attention to such a problem before takeoff (RECALL check's completion AFTER the second engine start), OR 2) the circuit breakers for the three pitot/AOA vane/elevator feel pitots found popped after shutdown all popped in flight by some cause that could not be duplicated, and the failure of those heaters did not, either by design or because of some other failure, cause a MASTER CAUTION alert to the crew.Mechanics should, if they don't already, follow a checklist for the restoration of any/all equipment and controls they deactivate/operate in the course of their duties, particularly those deactivated by pulling circuit breakers. Perhaps a 'maintenance in progress' sign on the pedestal or similarly prominent location, which is not to be removed until they ascertain restoration of all such equipment, would be a helpful memory aid. For pilots, single-engine taxi is practiced so rarely that, any time it is practiced, pilots' attention, reference, and faithfulness to the written checklist must be redoubled/maximized to ensure that the monitors and checks normally done before taxi are not omitted or 'completed' prematurely, rendering them invalid.				B737-800 Captain reported multiple popped circuit breakers were discovered during flight in addition to anomalies with the anti/de-icing system that required a diversion.	
1715592	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing																										Main Gear Wheel		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying		Flight Crew Total 21000					1715592												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We landed Runway XXR at ZZZ.  After clearing the runway a taxiing aircraft behind us notified us that we had a flat tire.  The aircraft controls and indications were normal so we continued to the gate.  After arriving we discovered the tire was severely damaged and some parts of under side of wing were damaged.  Maintenance was informed and an AML entry was made in regard to the Number 2 main tire problem.Possibly a faulty tire was installed on the aircraft the night before.  I am not an expert about what causes tires to fail.Suggestions: Maybe a better brand of retread tire should be used on the fleet.				B737-800 pilot reported a main gear tire failed during landing roll causing damage to under side of the wing.	
1716103	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1716103												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Human Factors	Human Factors	While operating flight I was informed by my FO (First Officer) that he was not feeling well after our conversation about an extensive dental procedure I had a few months back. FO looked a bit pale and certainly uncomfortable and said he needed to use the restroom. After summoning the A FA (Flight Attendant) to the cockpit, I heard a commotion in the front galley as the FO was exiting the flight deck. After looking back, I saw the legs of the FO on the ground. I immediately told the A FA to close the door and have a seat. At this point my position was around 300 miles north east of ZZZ at FL360. I referenced my manual on the procedures for incapacitated crew members and determined our next course of action. After about 5 minutes of communicating with the FAs on the condition of the FO they informed me he was up, doing well, and had his color back to normal. I spoke with the FO over the inter-phone system and he explained he was feeling much better and believed he was ok to continue. I discussed with the A flight attendant about staying upfront in the cockpit while the FO got situated so she could help if anymore issues came up. After talking with the FO and FA as well as a PAX (Passenger) that was a nurse who treated him, we determined he was back to normal and able to continue the flight as planned. I informed the FO I would be the pilot flying for the remainder of the flight and we discussed how to proceed. I determined a diversion to another airport was not in the best interest of safety. I considered policies/weather/distance/safety of my PAX's and crew and determined the best course of action was to not try and make a complex mountainous terrain diversion. ZZZ was clear with light winds and all our pre approach duties were accomplished for ZZZ. The flight then continued without further incidence. Once on the ground I told the FO to call the on-call chief pilot to determine the next course of action. We then talked with our medical team to get an assessment of the medical event at cruise. The Doctor told us he viewed it as an isolated incident and the FO was ok to continue. After talking with the chief pilots, the FO was removed from the flight and the rest of the crew was reassigned.After running through the incapacitated crew member section of the volume 2 . I would like to add a suggestion for a crew member that was incapacitated but returns to normal after treatment. A note to call the medical team if two pilots are present to discuss returning the crew member to duty or to divert.				A320 Captain reported First Officer became incapacitated during cruise flight.	
1716108	202001	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			0										Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch	Direct																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Check Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Other / Unknown; Training / Qualification; Workload			1716108												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Manuals; Procedure	Ambiguous	When the PF (Pilot Flying) called 'Set Flaps Up Maneuvering Speed' the PM (Pilot Monitoring) reached for the flap handle initially then went to the MCP. The 'Set Flaps Up' part of the call seems to cause this improper action during high workload. 'Set Flaps Up' should be removed from the call. The call could be changed to 'Set Clean Maneuvering Speed' or something similar.				B737 Check Airman reported the aircraft checklist verbiage needs to be changed to avoid improper action.	
1716462	202001	1801-2400	SJC.Airport	CA				5100		VMC			Night			TRACON NCT	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class E NCT																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1716462	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1716835	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	On our arrival into SJC at approximately XA:40PM with FO flying, NorCal approach cleared us down to 5,100 feet flying over low, unlit mountainous terrain just east of coast. With VMC conditions prevailing, NorCal asked if we would like a visual approach. With the airport in sight, the FO indicated yes he would. The aircraft (Nav Radios and FMC) was set up for the ILS 30L approach for back up guidance and had been appropriately briefed. We set 2,700 feet in the altitude alerter window for the crossing restriction at HIVAK (GS Intercept) intersection as back up. The FO asked for a Leg Intercept to HIVAK. While I was head down executing Leg Intercept and then looking at IPad to confirm Tower Frequency and tune radios, the FO had smoothly initiated a Vertical Speed descent that I did not notice or feel in the seat of the pants. A Terrain GPWS Alert got my full attention. With a delayed response in the right seat, I took control of the aircraft and immediately executed an escape maneuver to ascend free of terrain. When well clear of terrain, configured aircraft, reestablished stable approach, and made uneventful landing.Especially in critical phases of flight (below 10,000 and sterile), great example of the need for communication Between Pilot Monitoring and Pilot Flying.I've done this arrival countless times and am very familiar. In the Debrief I learned the FO was not familiar. Although I had brought up the terrain and how dark it was to the FO as we approached, I could have been briefed up better. Additionally, I do believe NorCal kind of baited Pilot Flying with a Visual Approach Clearance at night while still over unlit terrain.In the future, especially at night, the RNAV Z to 30L would have been a much better option and prudent.		During our initial descent into SJC, we received a GPWS alert followed by a warning. The recovery was initiated immediately by the Captain (PM). He followed the correct procedure and complied with this warning. We then exchanged controls and the rest of the approach was uneventful. I was the pilot flying. During the descent into SJC, we were cleared to descend to 5,100 feet. It was very dark and it was hard to see the surrounding mountains. I checked my VSD as well as the highest peak in the area and clearance was showing at 4,100 feet. Due to the long day and night flying, I was more comfortable doing the ILS 30L into SJC with vectors to final. However, we agreed on accepting a visual approach. We began the downwind leg as well as a gradual descent from 5,100 feet to be at the final approach fix at 2,700 feet. Upon beginning the descent in vertical speed, we received a GPWS alert for terrain ahead. I immediately disconnected the autopilot and leveled off the aircraft. Within a few seconds, we received a GPWS warning to pull up. The Captain took over the controls, recovered promptly and after climbing back up to roughly 5,000 feet, he initiated the descent back into SJC. We exchanged controls and I completed the approach and landed uneventfully.Whenever flying at night surrounded by terrain, I will insist in requesting an ILS or RNP approach to provide an additional margin of safety and protection from the surrounding terrain. I will also do a better job at communicating with my Captain that we have initiated a descent and to verify that what I'm doing looks correct, but most importantly, safe. The next time I hear a GWPS alert, I will respond more proactively and will initiate a climb more promptly without hesitation.		B737-800 flight crew reported a CFTT event during a night visual approach to SJC airport.	
1716606	202001							40000									Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization Control System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 356; Flight Crew Type 12000					1716606												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	At 40,000 ft. MSL we experienced the Cabin Altitude Warning horn and light. We accomplished the immediate action items, the QRC and the QRH for Cabin Altitude Warning, and Emergency Descent. We started an emergency descent to 10,000 ft. MSL. The cabin altitude was at 11,000 ft. The oxygen masks did not deploy. The cabin altitude was controllable in manual and soon was below 8000 ft. and descending. We continued to [destination] at 10,000 ft. We briefed the flight attendants and made an announcement to the passengers. The rest of the flight was uneventful.				B737NG Captain reported a safe landing following loss of cabin pressure at FL400.	
1716614	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		60		38000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning; Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 437		Workload			1716614												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately 60 miles northeast of ZZZ at FL 380, the #1 engine rolled back to 68% N1. The engine would not respond to forward thrust lever movement, only to a lower thrust lever setting. Since this partial engine power was not addressed specifically in the QRH I thought that turning off the associated EEC (Electronic Engine Control) might allow us to regain control. The EEC was turned off, without effect and was turned back on. The decision to divert to ZZZ was an easy one. The Flight Attendants and Dispatch were notified. ATC was advised, vectors were provided to a Runway XX arrival.  The engine failed completely on downwind.The Engine Failure and One Engine Inop Landing QRH checklists were accomplished. The First Officer was the Flying Pilot, he continued to fly and did most of the communicating with ATC. Performance calculation flaps 15 engine out data was used and briefings completed. Aircraft control was transferred and I became the Pilot Flying.  A normal engine out flaps 15 landing was made. After clearing the runway, ZZZ Fire Rescue looked over the engine and detecting no threats, we taxied to our assigned gate.Two passenger PA's were made while airborne and one after landing. There were no Passenger issues that I was made aware of, our Flight Attendants must have done a wonderful job. My First Officer did a perfectly professional job of flying and communicating with ATC. KUDOS to all the SIM Instructors I have had over the years.				B737-700 Captain reported loss of power in the #1 engine while at cruise altitude which caused a diversion.	
1716661	202001	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				27500		Mixed	Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; INS; FMS Or FMC	Descent	Direct	Class A ZZZ																								FMS/FMC		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 369		Workload; Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1716661												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors	Human Factors	On the ground in ZZZ we had a planned cruise altitude of FL 240, on the ZZZZZ [arrival] into ZZZ1. When we received our Weight and Balance calculation it was incomplete due to our cruise altitude of 240, and a crossing restriction on the [arrival] at ZZZZZ1 at or above FL 290. I, [the] FO (First Officer) went back into the flight plan to correct ZZZZZ1 to match our cruise altitude of 240 so we could complete the weight and balance/performance. Once we reached our cruise altitude of 240, we encountered turbulence, so we requested a higher altitude of FL 300. I FO forgot about what I changed in the box when I was on the ground in ZZZ. We received a clearance to descend via the [arrival]. We as a Crew had not yet briefed or reviewed the arrival. What didn't help was encountering a pocket of moderate turbulence, and before I knew it, the PF was trying to capture 240. We realized our mistake and asked ZZZ1 Center for lower. ZZZ1 Center never gave us a lower altitude, except he argued with me about my turbulence report.				B737 First Officer reported an altitude deviation due to a waypoint altitude setting in the FMS that was never changed.	
1716685	202001	0601-1200	RSW.TRACON	FL		6		2200		Mixed	10		Daylight	800		TRACON RSW	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS 24; VOR / VORTAC rsw	Initial Approach	Direct	Class C RSW																								GPWS		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 235; Flight Crew Type 16042		Workload; Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1716685	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 606; Flight Crew Type 1400		Workload; Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1716704	ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Aircraft; Human Factors; Weather	Aircraft	We had to find a route to get through the line of weather, on our second ATC assigned route, we made it through the weather somewhat easily. Handed off to Ft. Myers Approach. The current ATIS, Q, showed winds 180-7, 10 VIS, 800 Few, Temp/ Dew Point 23/23, 30.08. RSW VOR was out of service. Runway 24 was in use. I told the First Officer to 'Let Approach know we could NOT do the RNAV Runway 24 GPS Approach.' He notified Approach of this. Approach told us to expect the VOR Runway 24 Approach. We reminded Approach the VOR was out of service. Approach forgot. He said he would vector us for the Visual Approach since it was 1500 Few at that time. As we got closer, I told the First Officer, 'That sure is a pretty THICK Few clouds.' It was broken clouds at best, almost overcast. We asked what aircraft were breaking out on the approach. Approach said they would ask (other carrier). They never got back with us on that. We were vectored on a tight left base for Runway 24 at 150 ft.'. You could see many layers of broken clouds, easily 300 to 400 ft. below us at 1500 ft., no ground contact. We told Approach. Approach had us climb back up to be vectored for left downwind for ILS Runway 24. Approach asked us, 'Winds were around 200 at 7 knots. Can you accept that?' We said, 'YES.' Later, we were cleared to intercept the LOC Runway 24. We did. Coming tight on LOC Runway 24 at TROPC, no clearance. We asked for a clearance three times coming up to and ON top of TROPC, and finally got a clearance from Approach Control. We were descending on the ILS Ruway 6 Approach. I noticed the winds on the EFIS was a 30-knot tailwind! I told this to the First Officer. Then I told the First Officer, the winds must die out close to the airport. Soon after, Approach told us the winds are now '220 at 18 gusting 24!' I told the First Officer, we have to miss approach and go to our alternate. First Officer told Approach and they vectored us for missed approach, and handed us over to Miami Center (if I remember right.) Told them we 'Want to go to our ZZZ, which is our alternate.' Sent a message to Dispatch. Dispatch responded and said we needed to go to ZZZ1 for an alternate, since of the TFR coming into effect at ZZZ.We went uneventfully to ZZZ1, to a long taxi in at ZZZ1. Found out our Maintenance at ZZZ1 had the replacement part for the MEL there, and they replaced the part. Maintenance signed off the logbook and off we went to RSW, with another very long taxi out. I was told by Dispatch to talk to the Operations Center Chief Pilot. He was upset why we diverted, with weather 1500 Few. He wanted to know why we did not circle to land. I told him what the weather really was. He seemed more understanding. He said, do an [Report]. I said, we did not do anything wrong. He said, so maybe this MEL 34-XX-XX will be studied out more for future flights, or something like that.		Report requested from Chief Pilot, Two missed approaches followed by a diversion to ZZZ1. En route from ZZZ2 to RSW we had to pass through a front that was crossing the state old FL. The aircraft had the GPWS on MEL which prevented us from flying a RNAV Approach. Weather in RSW at departure time did not indicate that we would need to fly an approach to get in. Runway in use at RSW was Runway 24. The two approaches they had were RNAV and VOR. VOR was [Out of Service]. Weather via ACARS was 1500 ft. scattered with ten mile visibility or somewhere close to that. On contact with RSW Approach we told them we were unable the RNAV. They told us that we should be able to get in with the visual. Descended down to 1500 ft. and turn base. The cloud cover prevented us from getting into the airport visually. Went missed and were told to expect the ILS Runway 6. Winds were reported 220/7. Came in for the ILS and upon contacting Tower we were told the winds were 220/17G29. This exceeded the tailwind for the Aircraft. Went missed and diverted to ZZZ1. Landed without incident.		Pilot crew reported a divert necessary due to an MEL preventing an RNAV approach and the VOR becoming inoperative at the destination airport.	
1717025	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 437		Distraction; Human-Machine Interface			1717025												ATC Issue All Types			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	I had just turned and lined up on the runway. Tower cleared us for takeoff. As I powered up, the red in runway lights illuminated. I brought the power back and stepped on the brakes. I told Tower the red in runway lights were on, and they said to 'Stand by.' They said they had a false target, and the red runway center lights went out. They then cleared us for takeoff. We departed.				B737 Captain reported a rejected takeoff after red runway lights illuminated as they advanced the power.	
1717058	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				37000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 232; Flight Crew Total 28793; Flight Crew Type 2870		Workload; Time Pressure; Confusion; Distraction; Physiological - Other; Situational Awareness			1717058												Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Company Policy; Human Factors; Procedure	Company Policy	As I returned from [my] last break the [Relief Pilot] in the right seat started his brief on aircraft status. As I settled back into my duty station, the [Relief Pilot] then asked ATC if we were supposed to cross [fix] at 31000 ft. ATC confirmed that we were cleared to cross [fix] at 31000 ft. We were currently at 37000 ft. approximately 4 miles northwest of the fix. The Mode Control Panel altitude selector had never been set to the crossing altitude of 31000 ft. The legs page was correctly set for the crossing at 31000 ft. The Other [Relief Pilot] who was present during the ATC crossing clearance was out of the cockpit taking a final bathroom break as is typical during the crew's final return before top of descent.ATC subsequently cleared us to descend to 31000 ft., and made no mention of any traffic conflict. We continued on to [our] destination with no further issues. This was an oversight of not properly following SOP during a very busy phase of flight, and forgetting to reset the altitude selector around the same time they were undoubtedly calling back to the cabin to wake the First Officer and myself at the end of our rest period. When I returned to the cockpit neither [Relief Pilot] briefed the descent clearance, I was unaware until the [Relief Pilot] in the right seat asked ATC about the clearance. One major factor in this oversight was undoubtedly due to a passenger in the cabin being in the forward lavatory for an extensive period of time.Our return to the cockpit was delayed much longer than normal, as the passenger was in the lavatory for at least fifteen minutes, and blocked our return to the cockpit after our final rest period. I was resting in a seat and the First Officer was in the lower bunk. We had set the wakeup reminder for one hour prior to landing, which the [Relief Pilots] followed correctly when they called us back, we just couldn't enter the cockpit and had to wait it out. Just as we were getting ready to knock on the lavatory door, the passenger finally came out, and we returned to the cockpit approximately 10 to 15 minutes later than is in my comfort zone, right near [the] top of descent.				An Air Carrier Captain reported during a flight crew switch and cabin distractions they did not comply with a crossing restriction.	
1717068	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		VMC							Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 232; Flight Crew Total 28793; Flight Crew Type 2870					1717068												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	While in cruise at FL350 we received [a] Sigmet at XA:32 from Dispatch for volcanic ash. Estimated ash top FL200. At XA:48 we received Sigmet 2 from ZZZ Center, for same volcanic ash, also from surface to FL200. Approximately 100 [miles] east of waypoint at XB:15 some of the Flight Attendants started to complain of an acrid odor in the aft galley. We ran the Volcanic Ash Non-Normal procedure. It appeared that we did not fly into actual volcanic ash after referencing the checklist. It is possible we did enter a sulfur dioxide gas cloud, or less likely, in my opinion, into an ozone layer. Out of an abundance of caution, I turned off both recirculation fans and requested a climb to FL370 which ATC granted. I notified ATC, and then contacted Dispatch on the Sat phone. I also directed the Purser to wake one of the Relief Pilots, and had her inspect the cabin for odor and fumes. It was decided that continuing on was prudent, the acrid smell was not increasing in intensity, and lasted for approximately 45 minutes. None of the passengers complained of any discomfort, and apparently were unaware of any odor or fumes. After landing I briefed a Technician and a Flight Manager that my best guess was it was probably a sulfur dioxide gas cloud associated with the volcano eruption.				B777 Captain reported acrid cabin odor during cruise most likely due to sulfur dioxide gas from a volcano eruption in the vicinity.	
1717383	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US		50		37000					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 401; Flight Crew Type 21750		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure			1717383												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors	Human Factors	Earlier in the trip, the First Officer (FO) was rushing and executed FMC navigation changes before I had a chance to verify them. I asked him to slow down and give me a chance to verify before he executed changes and he accommodated my request. I also noted that the First Officer was doing what almost all [company] pilots do when an altitude change came in. He dialed in the new altitude on the MCP (Mode Control Panel), pointed at it, stated it and began the climb or descent with no input from the other pilot. I did not counsel him about this because that is so commonly the way altitude changes are handled, despite guidance to the contrary in the FOM.On the flight in question, we were on an assigned heading when the Controller cleared us direct and a climb to FL390. As I read back the clearance, I observed the FO dial 39000 in the MCP, point at it and state 'three nine oh' and press the altitude intervention button. I verified that FL390 came up as the target altitude on the MCDU (Multi-Function Control and Display Unit). While I was verifying that the aircraft was beginning to climb, the First Officer brought up [clearance point] on the FMC Legs Page 1. I verified the programming was correct and stated, 'That looks good' and watched him execute the change. I took my attention back to the altitude and could see by then that the aircraft was beginning a climb and the thrust was increasing. I never confirmed that the First Officer selected LNAV or that the aircraft turned [to comply with clearance]. A few minutes later the Controller called and pointed out our error. We then took the proper procedural steps to get the airplane on course.Slow down. I think guys feel like when they get two items in a clearance, they have to rush or they'll forget one of them. I think if we simply slow down and work methodically, we'll make fewer errors. Obviously, I failed to properly monitor the situation. I think if [company] focused on and insisted upon proper altitude change procedures, it would force the cockpit crew to slow down and not move on to the lateral part of the clearance until the vertical part had been worked through as a team.				B737NG Captain reported a track deviation occurred when the crew let themselves get rushed in executing a clearance change.	
1717390	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				1300					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 140; Flight Crew Type 2312		Confusion			1717390												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were doing the RNAV W to XXR from ZZZZZ. Aircraft was doing fine as we configured. Approaching 1,300 feet AGL, just as the flaps were set to 30, the aircraft pitched over and started a dive. I said, 'what's it doing?' a few times quickly then clicked off the autopilot and recovered the aircraft below 1,000 feet. Tower gave us a low altitude alert. I had the ILS in as backup, and I climbed back to a normal glidepath and landed uneventfully. The Captain and I discussed the situation and we agreed that the aircraft (for some reason) went to 'Speed' and was chasing the airspeed. We all have the aircraft go into speed during arrivals with no indication, but this was a first for me on approach. Had this been night/IMC, it could have resulted in something very bad.				B737-700 First Officer reported that while on final approach the aircraft pitched over and started a dive. After disengaging the autopilot, the flight crew recovered and made an uneventful landing.	
1717400	202001	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 439					1717400												Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	I was PM (Pilot Monitoring) on leg ZZZ to ZZZ1. We received takeoff clearance and subsequently saw red hold lights. The PF (Pilot Flying) immediately pulled back the power at a very low airspeed and we queried ATC. They cancelled our takeoff clearance and replied they were getting a false target at the departure end.Good recognition by the Pilot Flying. I don't think ATC has any control over that system but perhaps they could provide recommendations as well to eliminate false targets.				B737 First Officer reported a rejected takeoff after the red runway lights illuminated when the power was advanced.	
1717401	202001	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 160; Flight Crew Total 7285; Flight Crew Type 2812		Training / Qualification			1717401												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure	Procedure	Fairly high gross weight out of ZZZZ. Computer suggested flaps 5 reduced thrust takeoff.  V2 was 188.  Since the latest training event, or possibly [previous] training showed an increase in delayed rotation rates and tire speed exceedances (202kts) I decided to brief crew on that particular issue as a possible threat. Pilot Flying rotated precisely at Vr at a near perfect rotation rate and then stopped rotating at around 8 degrees nose up.  I used body english and a 'lets go' as I feigned pulling back on the control wheel but we still did not leave the ground until an airspeed of 207 to 208 as seen by the IRO. The winds were down the runway at 15 to 19 kts so its possible our GS was considerably less but that wasn't the real problem. It wasn't what the Pilot Flying was expecting! Once we were thru FL180 we debriefed. In conclusion, it turned out that none of the First Officers knew what the target pitch attitude for a normal takeoff was. (15 degrees.) Hence, even a beautiful rotation at the prescribed Vr would not avoid a late unstick speed if the proper pitch angle wasn't used, not to mention adversely affecting our single engine climb segments.				Captain reported First Officers did not know procedures for rotation angle during a non standard takeoff.	
1717740	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				24000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 147; Flight Crew Type 14792					1717740												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Auto Fail light illuminated on the cabin pressurization controller. The Alternate system for cabin pressurization Controller did not engage. Landing Alt and Flight Alt windows displayed 5 dashes. Cabin Altitude was increasing. Selected manual to regain control of cabin. Advised ATC and requested immediate descent to 10,000 feet. Was able to regain control of the cabin with manual control and proceeded to and landed normally in ZZZZ.About 30 miles or so from ZZZ all smoke alarms sounded. We had no indication of any smoke or fire. Emergency vehicles met us on landing and one followed us to gate XX. Passengers deplaned normally. All faults entered into the ELB (Electronic Logbook). This was not a rapid depressurization and no masks were deployed.				B737NG Captain reported a failure of the cabin pressurization Controller resulting in an immediate descent to 10,000 feet.	
1717750	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				25000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Cockpit Window		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 140; Flight Crew Total 10736		Troubleshooting; Workload			1717750												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Manuals; Human Factors; Aircraft	Aircraft	On climbout from ZZZ at about 25,000 feet the FO (First Officer) front window completely shattered. Our actions as a crew was an immediate level off and communicated with ATC. Reading the checklist came to a point of putting on shoulder harnesses, at this point I requested a turn for ZZZ. We worked together to get the airplane turned around with a new routing. The FO worked with ATC and I went back to the checklist for its completion. The last item on the checklist asked if the glass was inner or outer. In our case it was outer. Final line on checklist reads continue as normal and take off shoulder harnesses. So this is where I recognize the error, as dire as ' put on shoulder harnesses,' this was not the time to turn around. We once again discussed our situation with one another with a completed checklist and cautiously went with the checklist, that this is ok to continue to ZZZ1. Our fuel situation was fine as we proceeded to ZZZ1 fairly quickly. We were left separated in situations and busy unnecessarily. This is my disappointment.				B737 Captain reported a lack of clarity when following the checklist for a shattered cockpit window.	
1718063	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Direct																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 313; Flight Crew Type 7000		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure			1718063												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight		Procedure; Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related; Aircraft	Procedure	Aircraft was fueled to 11.0, which was 400 pounds above minimum planned. We were advised five minutes prior to push of a wheels up time. Pushed on time and taxied single engine. Ground advised departure time extended. Started remaining engine and departed. First indication that I missed the fuel check was at level off when FMC indicated 'Using Reserve Fuel'. We landed with 5.9 of fuel. I normally recheck the fuel prior to departure. Since we were already 400 pounds over planned and taxied single engine, my expectation bias was that we were good on fuel. I will be more deliberate in my fuel check habit pattern.				An Air Carrier pilot reported departing with less than minimum takeoff fuel due to ATC delays while taxiing.	
1718111	202001	0601-1200	EWR.Airport	NJ				6000		VMC						TRACON N90	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B EWR																														Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 1 Eng, Fixed Gear		1	Part 91	None					Class B EWR																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 174; Flight Crew Total 18796; Flight Crew Type 3333		Situational Awareness			1718111												Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Shortly after being handed off to NYC Approach near the SWEET Intersection going into EWR, ATC cleared us from 7,000 feet to 6,000 feet on a heading of 110. Once the descent began, ATC issued a traffic alert for an aircraft climbing through 5,000 feet on a southwest heading towards us that he was not talking to. I acknowledged the traffic, picked them up visually and stated we may get an alert on this traffic.My FO (First Officer) put his hands on the controls in preparation for flying manually. I then noticed the TCAS target climbing through about 5,300 feet, and within seconds we received a Climb RA. The FO manually took control of the airplane and complied with the RA.After a few blocked transmissions, we were able to advise ATC. Approach then issued a turn to 190 as we leveled around 6,300-6,400 feet. Once cleared of the conflict, we returned to 6,000 feet. The remainder of the approach was uneventful and the were no issues with our cabin crew who were performing their final cabin checks. Our altitudes were about 500-600 feet apart and the lateral distance appeared to be a similar distance.Regarding the conflicting traffic, it was a low wing, single engine general aviation aircraft, possibly a Cirrus aircraft. The conflict occurred precisely at the 20 mile DME ring of the Class B Airspace, and the aircraft was climbing as well as turning southbound at the western edge of the Class B.				B737 Captain reported taking evasive action after receiving an RA from a light aircraft on descent into EWR.	
1718455	202001	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				30000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	STAR ZZZZZ 1	Class A ZZZ																								Speedbrake/Spoiler		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 180; Flight Crew Total 20100; Flight Crew Type 9085					1718455												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were descending on the ZZZZ 1 arrival into ZZZ. As we were descending the First Officer extended the speed brakes to help us get down.  Upon retraction to about 50 percent stowed, the aircraft rolled right about 15 degrees. Then the autopilot rolled wings level.  Once the speed brake was stowed to 25 percent it rolled again to the right 15 degrees and then it went wings level again.  After the speed brakes were fully stowed, the aircraft rolled left 15 degrees and then back to wings level.  After that we knew we had a spoiler problem and we decided we would not use the speed brakes for the remainder of the flight.  After referencing the QRH and the manuals, we requested priority handling, we notified the flight attendants and we let the passengers know with a PA.  We descended uneventfully with priority handling and landed with vehicles standing by. We taxied to the gate.  We debriefed with maintenance and the flight operations.				B737 NG Captain reported experiencing several 15 degree rolls to the right due to a possible speedbrake/spoiler malfunction during arrival flight phase. The crew landed uneventfully without the use of speedbrakes.	
1718740	202001	1201-1800	BDL.Airport	CT												Tower BDL	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121				Initial Approach		Class C BDL																														UAV - Unpiloted Aerial Vehicle									Class C BDL																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1718740												Conflict Airborne Conflict			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Airspace Structure; Procedure	Ambiguous	Immediately after we broke out of the clouds on approach, we observed what looked like a drone to right of the Runway 24 at BDL. It had purple and red led lights. Pointed it out to Tower and to aircraft that followed us on approach.				A320 Captain reported sighting a drone while on approach to BDL.	
1718742	202001	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0										Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction; Workload			1718742												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Crew Chief delivered a Dangerous Goods Form to crew of dry ice in the aft cargo compartment.  Load closeout received prior to takeoff did NOT reflect the restricted article of the dry ice, but we did not notice it was missing until after departure. Upon reaching initial cruise altitude I noticed the missing item on the load closeout and called operations to confirm whether the dry ice was indeed on board. They checked with the Crew Chief that it was on board and then coordinated with loads to send us an updated load closeout. All of the weights and other numbers were unchanged. The only difference was the YES in the restricted articles aft column. Flight proceeded to ZZZ uneventfully after that.It was late [at] night and it was raining. We were max structural weight and the ATIS takeoff runway had a 10 knot tailwind which made us too heavy to use. We had to coordinate a takeoff runway change.  Because we were so heavy we went over the takeoff data for weight, runway condition, atmospheric conditions carefully and multiple times. Due to all those factors we overlooked that the restricted articles section was missing the Dangerous Goods Form for dry ice on the load closeout.Need to remember to check not only the weights and performance numbers before takeoff but also other information like the restricted articles section. Perhaps we could have placed the Dangerous Goods Form on the pedestal until after the load closeout was received and reviewed. Or, the closeout could be formatted differently to more emphasize there are restricted articles so that it would stand out as different looking and be something we looked for when reviewing.				B737-800 Captain reported crew discovered, while enroute, the final load closeout form did not include required Hazmat data.	
1718921	202001	0601-1200	ZDV.ARTCC	CO				28000		VMC						Center ZDV	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZDV																												Center ZDV	Air Carrier	B757 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Climb		Class A ZDV																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 227; Flight Crew Type 3018					1718921												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	Initial climb out of DEN. Seat belt sign on, flight attendants up. Climbing through approximately FL280 suspect we encountered wake turbulence from aircraft 10-15 nm ahead and 4,400 feet. above us (757). Auto flight was engaged. Abrupt 25 degree roll left and increasing. Disconnected autopilot and stabilized the aircraft. Offset 2 nm right of track and reported encounter to ATC. Checked with Purser, who reported that everyone was ok. Found out an hour later just prior to top of descent that one flight attendant had fallen during the turbulence encounter and had some side and back pain.				A320 Captain reported that a wake turbulence encounter resulted in an injury to a flight attendant.	
1718955	202001		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 195; Flight Crew Total 5265; Flight Crew Type 5265					1718955												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Aircraft	Aircraft	At FL350, for no reason, aircraft went into control wheel steering and started descent. Disengaged autopilot, corrected situation and notified ATC. No TCAS. Aircraft stable for duration of flight. Made logbook entry.				B737NG Captain reported that an uncommanded autopilot mode switch resulted in an altitude deviation.	
1718969	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				2000		VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach; Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Hydraulic Lines, Connectors, Fittings		X	Failed; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 138; Flight Crew Total 6158; Flight Crew Type 995					1718969												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	Taxi; In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon gear extensions, center hydraulic system lost all fluid. Landed with 30 flaps. Nose wheel steering was greatly reduced, but was able to clear Runway XXL and park on Taxiway B for tow-in to gate.				B777 Captain reported loss of center hydraulic fluid during final approach after gear extension resulting in steering issues.	
1719272	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			1500			VMC			Dawn			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway XXR	Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Landing Gear		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 177; Flight Crew Total 2219; Flight Crew Type 1295					1719272	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 183.03; Flight Crew Total 1678.55; Flight Crew Type 1678.55		Distraction			1719275	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	While acting as Pilot Monitoring, [on] ILS XXR, all systems were normal, and approach was stable and in the landing configuration (1,500 AGL), we received 'TOO LOW GEAR' at approximately 150 feet AGL. Prior to the GPWS alert, all indications were normal with the before landing checklist completed before 1,000 AGL; both landing gear lights and wheel page on lower ECAM indicated that landing gear was down and locked and GPWS did not annunciate descending through 750 AGL.We got the 'TOO LOW GEAR' warning and executed a go-around at that point. I looked at the lower ECAM and both LGCIU diamonds indicated the landing gear was down, but the normally 'green' landing gear lights were not illuminated. During the go-around, the landing gear retracted normally and all doors closed. During the climbout, we received an 'LGCIU 1' ECAM. The FO flew the airplane and assumed the radios once the go-around was complete and we were in clean configuration 10,000 MSL, 210 KIAS, heading 245 degrees. I ran the ECAM which directed me to turn the GPWS SYS switch the the 'OFF' POSITION.We decided to lower the gear (With LGCIU #2) at 12 miles to give us additional time just in case we had additional issues. When I placed the landing gear down, nothing happened; the landing gear did not extend. Due to our low fuel (approximately 4,000 LBS) status we elected to manually extend the landing gear. The landing gear extended without any lights on the landing gear panel; the lower ECAM indicated 3 green diamonds for LGCIU#2, amber for LGCIU#1, landing gear doors remained extended and 'Steering' was displayed in amber. The landing was uneventful and we slowly taxied to the gate area using differential braking/thrust we elected to be towed into the gate for the last 100 feet.		On final approach to Runway XXL at ZZZ we were configured and normal checklists complete according to normal Airbus visual approach profiles. At FAF as Pilot Flying I disengaged the Autopilot and Auto-throttles and hand flew the visual approach. At approximately 200' AGL we received the 'To Low, Gear' Aural Warning. We both noticed that there was no indication, red or green for all three, on the landing gear panel. Even though the gear had initially indicated down and we could hear the gear down we felt a go around was best course of action. CA XX_11 called 'Go Around'.  I initiated the Go around and gear was raised and seemed to work normally. We then received the LGCIU 1 Fault ECAM. At approximately 1000' AGL I reengaged the Autopilot and Auto throttles. CA XX_11 announced I would remain Pilot Flying, also work ATC communications, and he would work the ECAM and checklists. We advised ATC the reason for the go around was a maintenance item. We would need to work a checklist but could go ahead and be vectored for another approach. Checklist for the LGCIU 1 was short and we anticipated the gear would come down normally and we would use the indications on the Lower ECAM Wheel page. We also discussed the loss of Engine 1 thrust reverser and that I would use reverser 2 but only at idle thrust. Also discussed to lower the gear early in case of any further problems. Finals for runway XXL were then 25 to 30 miles in length. Considering fuel levels we planned to lower the gear on a 10 to 15 mile final. I remained Pilot Flying and CA XX_11 resumed ATC communications. When I called for Gear Down and Gear handle was lowered by CA XX_11 nothing happened. There was no change in the indications on the Lower ECAM Wheel page and no normal sound of the gear doors opening and gear lowering. I inquired if there was any mention of a gravity gear extension in the checklist. CA XX_11 said there was not. As Pilot flying I continued to prioritize my attention to flying the approach as CA XX_11 looked at the QRH checklist again, checked system pages and made sure the gear handle was in the proper position. CA XX_11 began the gravity extension and we could hear the gear doors open and verified the gear was down on the Lower ECAM Wheel Page. Touchdown was normal and smooth. Aircraft control was good even with the loss of nose wheel steering and reverser 2 was used at idle thrust. CA XX_11 took command of the aircraft prior to leaving runway center line and was able to steer the aircraft off using differential braking. CA XX_11 continued to taxi the aircraft south on taxiway F and asked if I was okay with him continuing to the ramp area. I responded I felt safe. Taxiway conditions were dry, there was not a strong crosswind, and he seemed to have no trouble controlling the aircraft using differential braking. Ramp crew was notified that we would be able to taxi to the gate area but did want to be towed into the gate.		A320 Crew reported that while on final approach with landing gear extended, a gear warning prompted a go-around. The crew manually extended the gear and landed.	
1719584	202001		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				18000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 135; Flight Crew Total 2980; Flight Crew Type 2980		Physiological - Other			1719584												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During descent into ZZZ at approximately FL180, the cockpit and cabin quickly became saturated with the smell of dirty socks. It was very strong and overpowering for several minutes. The smell diminished somewhat at the lower altitudes but continued to be present at a lower concentration all the way to the gate. This problem had been written up the previous day. It had an oily smell to it and burned eyes and throat. It felt like it was more difficult to concentrate during this fume event. After arriving home I experienced headache. All 3 pilots and purser felt the smell was abnormally strong. I did not have the opportunity to question the rest of the FA crew. It was written up for further inspection by maintenance.				B777 Captain reported that fumes entered the cabin during descent and resulted in physiological reactions in crewmembers.	
1719615	202001		ZZZ.Airport	CA			0										Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121				Takeoff / Launch																										Rudder Pedal		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 124.27; Flight Crew Total 6003.83; Flight Crew Type 4835.15					1719615												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	During the takeoff roll, a loose can of unopened Coca-Cola Zero rolled from behind the Captain's rudder pedals and stopped between the Captain's left foot and the left rudder pedal.  Since I was the Pilot Monitoring, I was able to remove the object and the takeoff was continued.  Had I been the Pilot Flying, this event would have resulted in a rejected takeoff. This object was not from our crew and was lost/loose at some time prior to us beginning our pre-flight duties for this flight. This event would have been a much greater threat to safe operations had anything else irregular happened during the takeoff roll.				A320 Captain reported that during the takeoff roll an unopened beverage can rolled from behind the rudder pedals and between his left foot and the rudder pedal. The Captain, who was the pilot monitoring, removed the can and the takeoff continued safely.	
1719849	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Autothrottle/Speed Control		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Training / Qualification			1719849												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	ZZZ Tower told us to go-around due to the aircraft landing in front of us not clearing the runway. On go-around I inadvertently pressed the autothrottle disconnect switch instead of the go-around switch. As I rotated and began the go-around litany, my First Officer stated 'Airspeed.' I had not added enough power during clean-up and our airspeed got below Vref.I am new to the 737, just reaching the 100-hour consolidation period last week. It has taken me over 3 1/2 months to accumulate the 100 hours. I am very junior on the aircraft so I am on reserve and have not flown very often. I transitioned from the MD80 which I have flown for over 11 years straight. On the go-around, I was having trouble locating the go-around switch on the throttles because my old habit pattern kicked in and I was searching for the switch where it used to be on the MD80. I then mistakenly pressed the auto throttle disconnect switch and initially did not added enough power for the go-around.I have to mentally practice the go-around procedure, including all of the physical movements I would be making during the go-around so that my hand can reflexively find the go-around switch instead of reverting to old 'muscle memory'.				B737 Captain reported that unfamiliarity with a new aircraft caused them to inadvertently disconnect the autothrottle during a go-around.	
1720154	202001	0601-1200				7		3000					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class B ZZZ																								Flap Control (Trailing & Leading Edge)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 475		Troubleshooting			1720154	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1720165	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	While being vectored for Visual Approach to Runway XX at ZZZ, the First Officer called for Flaps 5. I set the Flap Handle to 5 and about the same time ATC called our base turn. It was shortly afterward that I realized the flaps had barely moved, even though the handle was in the 5 detent. The indicators were split, 1 at 0, and the other at about 1/2 degree (I don't recall L/R). I pointed this out to the First Officer and we agreed to abandon the approach and work the problem through the QRH. While being vectored by ATC, we referenced the Trailing Edge Flap Asymmetry Checklist. This checklist directed us to bring the flap handle back to the Up position, which in turn led us to the Trailing Edge Flaps Up Landing Checklist. At that point I made sure the First Officer had the flight controls and ATC, and I contacted Dispatch/Maintenance through ZZZ OPS frequency. I informed them of our condition and told them we would probably need to divert to ZZZ1 in order to have a longer runway. We began to run weight and balance numbers and brake cooling data for Runway XY at ZZZ1. Dispatch was doing the same, but also ran numbers for Runway XX at ZZZ. They informed us that we would have a stopping margin of about 1,500 ft. with Autobrake Max. We went ahead and confirmed this by running the numbers ourselves, but neither one of us was comfortable landing on the shorter runway because we also had the Number 2 thrust reverser inoperative. This was something that was already on an MEL. Another big concern was brake cooling and potential for blowing tire fuse plugs.We both felt like the safest and most conservative choice was the longer runway at ZZZ1. I was fully aware that it would be inconvenient for everyone involved but had to remind myself of our operational priorities and their order. Dispatch, ATC, Flight Attendants, and Passengers were all fully informed of our situation. We [requested priority handling] with ATC and requested vectors for a 25 mile final to give us time to finish the QRH procedures which required us to deploy the leading edge flaps via the alternate method. I assumed control of the aircraft about 35 miles from the airport. We finished the QRH and made the high speed approach and landing on Runway XY without incident. CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) was waiting and we had them inspect the tires and brakes. They noted nothing of concern and based on my brake application at about 130 kts., we were able to proceed to a gate that had been coordinated for us. On shutdown we noted low B-system quantity of 74 and an RF displayed. Defects were entered in the logbook and per coordination from the Operations Center, the passengers and crew eventually deplaned, cleared Immigration and Customs, and boarded ground transportation for ZZZ.This was just an unfortunate flap malfunction. I don't think it was preventable.		While on downwind, descending through 3,500 ft. to 2,000 ft. for Visual Approach to Runway XX into ZZZ, we attempted to configured to flaps 5. One flap did not move and the other flap extended to about a half of a degree. We coordinated with ATC to level off at 3,000 ft. and got vectors while we worked the problem. We started with the Trailing Edge Flap Asymmetry Checklist. This checklist drove us to move the flap handle back to 'Up'. The flaps remained in an asymmetric condition so, per the QRH, we transitioned to the Trailing Edge Flaps Up Landing Checklist. At that point, the Captain began coordinating with Dispatch, while the First Officer continued to fly. After looking at the performance and brake cooling data at both ZZZ and ZZZ1, we decided to divert to ZZZ1 in order to land on a longer runway. Although we had the performance data to land at ZZZ, given the combination of the no flap landing and #2 thrust reverser MEL (inherited from a previous flight) doing so would have required a max braking effort, longer brake cooling period (over one hour) and ultimately a smaller margin of safety. We coordinated with ATC to fly a 25 mile visual straight in to Runway XY at ZZZ1. Additionally, we [requested priority handling with ATC], coordinated with Dispatch, and informed the passengers and flight attendants of the situation. We finished the Trailing Edge Flaps Up Landing Checklist, deploying the leading edge devices through the alternate flaps extension procedure, and the Captain landed on Runway XY, applying brakes at about 130 kts. We taxied off the runway, had the emergency vehicles check the brake temperatures (which they assessed as normal), and uneventfully taxied to the gate. At the gate, we noticed that the number 2 hydraulic quantity was low (the quantity indicated about 75 with an 'RF' annotated). Neither this condition nor any other hydraulic abnormality alerted throughout the flight. After coordinating for Customs and offline support, we ended the flight without further incident.		B737 flight crew reported a flap extension failure that resulted in a diversion.	
1720182	202001	1201-1800						7000		VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Main Gear Door		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 196; Flight Crew Total 20294; Flight Crew Type 11614		Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Maintenance		1720182												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight was uneventful until approaching ZZZZZ, the final approach fix for Runway XXR in ZZZ. When we placed the gear handle down, we received an ECAM for the landing gear doors not closed. The ECAM showed the gear down and locked but the gear doors in the fully extended position. I have seen the main gear doors open on the ground and know ground clearance is very close. Not knowing if they would clear on touchdown, I elected to go-around but kept the gear extended, planning to contact Maintenance for guidance. The First Officer flew the go-around and I asked her to stay below 220 kts. at 10,000 ft. MSL. I gave her the radios and asked her to tell ATC we would need delay vectors to troubleshoot the problem, while starting the ECAM procedure. I also made a PA announcement to the passengers quickly explaining the problem and telling them we were trouble shooting the problem.I referenced the QRH for L/G Doors not Closed, but the procedure is for when the doors don't retract on takeoff. The procedure directs to recycle the gear if the gear handle is up but does not address if the gear handle is down. I elected to cycle the gear up and down but the gear doors remained in the extended position. While I was doing this, I sent a message to Dispatch to 'Call Me' and received no response. After several more minutes, I sent 'Call ME NOW.' I have a screen shot of the AIRINC frequencies on my IPAD and was starting to retrieve it when I received the frequency on ACARS. I called ZZZ1 AIRINC but was not able to get immediate service because another aircraft was on the frequency. Eventually, I was patched to [another sector] and requested Maintenance Airframe. Waiting for Maintenance, I continued running the ECAM when I received another ECAM F/CTL Slat SYS 2 Fault, and then a CB tripped ECAM for CB tripped on the AFT panel followed by another ECAM for CB tripped on the overhead panel. The time delays were costing us valuable fuel which was now below 5,000 pounds. When we initiated the go-around, we had 7,700 pounds of fuel remaining but with the gear doors open and gear down our fuel was rapidly being depleted. I discovered after searching for tripped CBs they were associated with LGCIU 1 and in lieu of the rapidly depleting fuel and the time delays, I elected to reset tripped CBs, hoping they might be the cause and the solution to the problem. The overhead CB reset but the AFT one did not. I left the AFT out.During all of this I noticed ATC had moved us to the south of the airport by almost 30 miles and I asked the First Officer to have ATC keep us within 20 miles of the airport and a lot closer. Approach eventually worked us of to just southwest of the airport.By this time Maintenance came on, we were down to almost 4,300 pounds of fuel, and I explained the situation with the CBs and the gear doors. Maintenance asked if the gear showed down and locked which it did and I asked if there was a possibility of the gear doors impacting the runway on landing. The Maintenance Controller said there was a possibility [of] the gear doors sparking on landing. I am not sure how I ended the conversation with the Controller other than to say we will have to land with the doors open. At this point I called the Flight Attendants and told them to do a Cabin Prep for Evacuation. I asked them how much time they needed and they replied 10 minutes. I said unable, you have five minutes. I made a PA announcement to the passengers and explained the situation and told them it is possible the gear doors might be a problem and we would be landing soon.The First Officer had set up the FMGC for XXR and [requested priority handling from] ATC. I quickly briefed the approach and took the aircraft and asked her to tell ATC we needed to get the aircraft on the ground. I reminded her if the gear doors cause a problem and it leads to an evacuation, to grab the QRC and read the checklist. Just prior to landing, we received an ACARS message that said 'Upon furtherreview, the gear doors should not be a problem.' Great news but a little late. By now we were within the FAF for XXR. The approach was normal and at 500 feet AGL, I gave a 'BRACE BRACE BRACE' PA and the FAs started their commands. I made a very smooth touchdown and the ARFF crew said there were no sparks on landing. We rolled to a stop on the high-speed taxiway with NWS and LGCIU1 inop, and I made a PA to 'Remain Seated, Remain Seated.' Fuel was less than 3,400 pounds. We waited for a ground crew to come out and tow the airplane and they needed the gear pins that are stowed in the cockpit. We threw them out the FO's side window to the ground crew. The ground crew's interphone radio initially was very readable but became very scratchy and unreadable. It might have been because there was a different cord with the tug. We used hand signals to verify brakes released. We made the slow trip to the terminal where I briefed the mechanics, and Flight Ops representatives. A couple of lessons.The QRH needs to address Landing Gear Doors Extended on Landing. Would it have been better to retract the gear rather than leave them down? This would have reduced the fuel consumption rate and given us more time to trouble shoot the situation once Maintenance was on the line.The fuel consumption rates with the gear doors open and gear down is incredibly high. The QRH says 15% for the doors only. We were fortunate we were tankering fuel to ZZZ and had extra, but if we had been at normal landing fuel amounts, the situation would have been worse. Also, the noise level with the gear doors open is very loud.I had multiple ECAMS popping up on top of each other, with the CBs tripping. I never did see an ECAM for LGCIU1 fault but it was listed in the INOP systems. I was saturated.The lack of response from Dispatch was frustrating and wasted valuable time and fuel exacerbating our situation. When I did get through to the Maintenance Controller, I was left with very little time to be able to make any evaluative decisions and had to go with what I knew at the time.Having to go through a middle man (AIRINC) wastes time and when you get there you are on a party line.When I originally elected to go-around, I had no idea it would take us almost 35 minutes to get the aircraft back on the ground. The sense of time was very compressed. Had I known the gear doors would not be a hazard with them in the extended position, I probably would have elected to come back around immediately, evaluating the existing ECAM instead of wasting valuable time trying to contact Maintenance, but I was trying to use all our available resources. They weren't readably available.Training kicks in very fast. The Flight Attendants did a wonderful job preparing the cabin in a very short amount of time. It was the Purser's second trip since training. The First Officer, still on probation, did an outstanding job communicating to ATC, flying the aircraft, and setting us up for the follow-on approach. We were together as a crew, divided our tasks, and then came back together for the approach.I checked with Maintenance the following day and the mechanics had not been able to fix the problems.Writing this type of report has caused me to second guess and question all of my decisions. I should be satisfied the result was successful with no damage and no body injured. But I have to ask what could I, should I, have done better?				A320 Captain reported that troubleshooting a landing gear door malfunction caused the aircraft to land with critically low fuel.	
1720355	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US													Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1720355	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1720368	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Attendant	Taxi; In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	After level-off at cruise, the flight attendant called the flight deck to report fumes she noticed on taxi-out. She and [another] flight attendant said they felt mild symptoms of confusion & headache, but did not elect to call the flight deck till at cruise. We were de-iced after push and could have been a bit short of the 1 minute wait time in selecting ON on the engine bleeds on the second engine start, or there could have been other issues with the de-ice to cause such fumes. Further forward in the aircraft the fumes were not noted. No further fumes were noted on the flight.Always maintain caution with chemicals around the aircraft. Encourage crew members to come forward with questions/ issues in a more timely way.		After level off we received a call from aft flight attendants reporting that they smelled fumes in the cabin and were feeling sick after engine start in ZZZ. We had been de-iced at the gate with type one fluid on push back. After engine start I may have been a little to quick to turn on the #1 bleed system not giving at least one minute after engine start. We initially gave each Flight Attendant oxygen which did make them feel better. One of the flight attendants appeared to be ok however one we felt would benefit from a call to medlink. After counseling with medlink both flight attendants felt better. We had the flight met at destination by paramedics just to follow up with the flight attendants.Wait at least one minute after engine start to turn on bleeds: follow SOP.		B738 flight crew reported being notified upon level off by flight attendants of fumes and physiological symptoms on taxi out.	
1720755	202001	1201-1800						39550		VMC	10		Daylight	50000			Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Personal	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Commercial	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 12; Flight Crew Total 400; Flight Crew Type 0					1720755	Aircraft X	General Seating Area	Personal	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Commercial						1720760	Conflict NMAC	Horizontal 50; Vertical 200		Person Passenger	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airspace Structure	Airspace Structure	Aircraft X, flying at 39,550 ft. straight and level on a track of 93 degree had a near miss midair collision with an airplane flying opposite direction. RVSM not respected, the airplane was approximately 200 ft. and 50 ft. to the north of Aircraft X. Both airplanes were flying straight and evasive action were taken.I am a passenger of the airplane and I witnessed it with other passenger.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		Passenger reported a near mid air collision with an opposite direction commercial airliner during cruise, requiring evasive action.	
1720800	202001	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		40		36000		Mixed	Turbulence		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 519; Flight Crew Type 12000					1720800	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1720801	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Weather	Weather	Cruising at FL360 over northwest FL, we hit severe turbulence. Airspeed was uncontrollable, altitude uncontrollable, and autopilot kicked off. Gained over 300 ft. and lost over 600 ft. before autopilot kicked off. Airspeed all the way up to redline then all the way down close to stall. We gained control of the aircraft and started a descent. We asked ATC for a block altitude and they came back and said descend to FL 340. We were out of the turbulence at FL 350. No one was hurt and we continued on to ZZZ. We did notice that the wind changed direction and speed. Up to 30 degrees and speed change was over 40 kts.		Cruising at FL360 in light to occasional moderate turbulence, we encountered an unreported area of severe turbulence. We were unable to maintain altitude and began a descent. I immediately requested a lower clearance, which was given. Made a PIREP and continued. No injuries among Crew or Passengers. Flight Attendants had been seated prior due to the expected moderate turbulence.		Flight Crew reported severe turbulence and loss of aircraft control requiring evasive action.	
1720804	202001	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight					B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Door Area	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Type 16500		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1720804												Conflict NMAC; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Passenger Misconduct			Person Passenger	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Aircraft	Aircraft	During deplaning, a passenger approached me and asked about an aircraft that we got very close to. I responded that I was unaware of any abnormally close aircraft during our flight. He responded that we had gotten about 200 ft. away from what he thought was [another] aircraft. I asked him where he thought this occurred and he said, 'somewhere over ZZZ I think'. I assured him that our minimum separation enroute was 1,000 ft. and that we had no close encounters with any other aircraft. He claimed he knew exactly what 1,000 ft. looked like and that the aircraft he saw was definitely 200 ft. away. I asked him if he knew what kind of aircraft it was, hoping to explain that large aircraft appear closer but he said he had no idea what type of aircraft it was, just that he thought it was a (other carrier) aircraft. I again mentioned that we had not come closer to any other aircraft than the standard separation of 1,000 ft. We had no traffic alerts nor any other TCAS has events for the entire flight. The passenger left the jetway after this short conversation.				B737 Captain reported that a deplaning passenger expressed concern that during cruise another aircraft had passed closeby, with less than 1,000 ft of separation, although the separation standard had been met during flight and that no TCAS events had occurred.	
1721385	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				10000		VMC							Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb																										Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 10000		Troubleshooting; Workload			1721385	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Troubleshooting			1721395	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	During climb around 10,000 ft. to 15,000 ft. the PIC first noticed the aircraft required an unusually high amount of left rudder trim. We then noticed the left wing tank had an amber caution imbalance and was about 1,500 lbs. lighter than the right wing tank. I asked the PIC if we were in balance before departure, and he reminded me that we both had verified the quantity and balance before taking the runway. We then passed our first fix on the fuel log and noticed we were 1,000 lbs. down. The PIC then continued to trim the aircraft to engage the autopilot, while asking me to retrieve the Fuel Imbalance Checklist from the QRH, which lead us to the Fuel Leak Engine checklist. Once I retrieved the checklist the PIC transferred control and radios of the aircraft to me for the duration of the flight. We then weighed our options. Because we were by now 2,000 lbs. light in our left tank and knowing there are not many airports between ZZZ and ZZZ1, we decided to return to ZZZ. As the Captain proceeded on with the checklist, he asked me to [advise ATC] and to start heading back to ZZZ. I asked Center for direct for the field, center then handed us off to approach. On initial check in I asked approach for straight in Runway XX, knowing that it was the longest runway with a headwind. Approach asked if we wanted the visual, I then asked for the RNAV Z Runway XX. Approach cleared us direct to TACOH to cross TACOH at 10,000 ft. and cleared for the approach. Somewhere in the process approach asked if we wanted ARFF standing by and I replied, 'affirmative'. Approach then asked for number of souls on board and fuel remaining. Meanwhile the Captain busy completing checklists, notifying flight attendants, and notifying company, informed me that the flight attendants had smelled fuel on departure. The flight attendants smelling of fuel helped us confirm that our decision to return to ZZZ was correct . Then on about a 5-10 mile left base for Runway XX, the Captain came to the part of the Fuel Leak Engine checklist that required us to shut down the left engine. We weighed our options. There was 5,000 lbs. of fuel in the left tank, the fuel leak had subsided at the low thrust setting, engine indications were normal, there was a lot of traffic in the terminal area (a missed approach wasn't out of the question), we were chose to landing, and we didn't want to do a single engine missed at a high altitude/high terrain airport. We decided to to leave the engine running and complete the decent and before landing checklist. We landed without event and I stopped the aircraft and transferred control of the aircraft to the Captain. The Captain told the passengers to remain seated as we retracted the spoilers and set flaps to 40. ARFF then advised us that there were no leaks or fire and followed us off the runway to the gate, where they boarded and checked on the status of the passengers. All of the passengers were fine. The entire flight from takeoff until touchdown lasted approximately 15 minutes. The Captain did an amazing job of delegating responsibilities among the crew while still keeping everyone in the loop. Although there was a lot to do in a short amount of time, I never once felt rushed or in the 'red'. I attribute this to the Captain setting a great tone on day one of our trip that promoted great team work and communication.		On departure, passing 10,000 ft. we noticed a rapidly growing fuel imbalance between wing tanks. Left was 2,000 pounds less than right tank. Sight right roll tendency was noticed. I passed control and comms to the First Officer. I contacted Tower to query about fuel venting from left wing. None noted. [Advised ATC] and initially leveled off. First Officer flew a return to ZZZ. I ran the fuel leak checklist, emergency landing checklist, briefed the Flight Attendants and made PA. notified Dispatch via ACARS. I had #1 (Flight Attendant) confirm no visible fuel leak on left engine/pylon and left wing areas. First Officer flew a flawless approach and landing to Runway XX. We stopped straight ahead for ARFF inspection, then taxied to gate with ARFF chase.I elected to not shut down the left engine and land based on the following- No visible indications of a fuel leak. All engine indications were normal throughout. Landing was to be conducted in mountainous terrain with high traffic volume. We agreed that with the above indications, the best choice was to land ASAP and not expose ourselves to higher risk of a single engine approach and landing.		B737 flight crew reported that a fuel imbalance resulted in a return to the departure airport.	
1721778	202001		ZZZ.Airport	US				1400							1600	Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway XXL	Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																								ILS/VOR		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 458		Distraction			1721778												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Procedure	Procedure	While conducting the CAT II SA XXL into ZZZ we experienced momentary Flight Path symbol and Flight Guidance Cue (AIII mode) movement due to an aircraft departing on our runway as we were inside a five-mile final. This was obviously distracting while conducting an approach to a runway with a reported RVR of 1, 600 feet (I believe). My question is, why was the ILS Critical area not protected? It is my understanding that if the weather is less than 800 and/or two miles visibility, this area should be protected while aircraft are inside the OM or the fix used in lieu of. We have another question regarding protection of the ILS Critical area in ZZZ1. We were operating Flight XXXX, ZZZ1-ZZZ2. ZZZ1 weather was reported as 200 broken 1/4 mile visibility. We called Ground Control for taxi. 'Taxi Runway XX via U and W, report turning on to W.' There was no mention of 'hold short of the ILS Critical area.' Again, why not? If ATC does not specifically state a 'hold short' clearance then we are cleared to the runway hold short markings. My reason for submitting this [report] is to help me and possibly others understand the purpose of the ILS Critical Area. In advance, thank you for providing more clarity on this issue.				B737-700 Captain reported a momentary lost of flight path guidance while conducting an ILS approach at the same time a flight was departing from the approach runway.	
1721966	202001	1801-2400					500			VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1721966	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1721968	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were cleared to take-off Runway XXR via the RNAV Departure. At roughly 500 ft. AGL the First Officer called traffic ahead. The traffic was a helicopter hovering roughly 1,000 ft. AGL directly in the Runway XXR flight path for the SID. I observed the traffic visually and confirmed the traffic on my ND with an 'AMBER' TCAS advisory image at +600 ft. TCAS did not issue a RA, which it should have because the helicopter was directly in the lateral flight path of my aircraft. At this time I adjusted the vertical climb of the aircraft to pass directly beneath the helicopter. The First Officer advised Tower that there was traffic, at which time the controller issued the helicopter traffic to start a turn South. We passed beneath the helicopter roughly 500 ft. then I resumed the vertical profile of the SID. No lateral course deviations were taken at any time during the event. The rest of the departure ensued without incident. I asked the First Officer to inform ATC that the helicopter was really close to the flight path at this time, and that we would be filing an ASAP report. Suggestions: This incident could have been prevented if the VFR helicopter traffic had not been cleared to maneuver in the departing traffic flight path. Or, if we had not been issued a take-off clearance until the VFR traffic had cleared the flight path and had been referenced by ATC tower. The controller seemed to be task saturated since he was the only controller working in the tower that time of night. Perhaps this condition contributed to the lack of traffic coordination we observed.		After calling for push back clearance on ground frequency, the controller asked me to call him on tower for taxi. It sounded like the same controller was working both frequencies. After being issued a takeoff clearance, a normal take off and initial climb were initiated. After I raised the gear and disarmed the spoilers, I noticed a red anticollision beacon and notified the Pilot Flying there was traffic at our 12 o'clock. I transmitted to Tower we had traffic at 12 o'clock and the Tower immediately transmitted to our traffic to start their southbound turn. Simultaneously the Pilot Flying lowered the nose to stop our climb and I noticed the TCAS was now displaying an amber target 800 ft. above us. There was no RA. The helicopter commenced its turn south and once we were clear the Pilot Flying re-established the climb and continued on the SID.I'm not sure I have a suggestion. I felt we responded appropriately (Seeing and avoiding). I would have thought TCAS would have generated an RA based on our closure rate to 'maintain vertical speed' but I guess we did not meet the criteria?		A320 flight crew reported an NMAC on departure with a VFR aircraft.	
1722327	202001	0601-1200						5000		VMC							Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb																										Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1722327												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On takeoff, both the First Officer and I noticed a smell similar to burning plastic in the cockpit. There was no smoke in the cockpit. As we climbed out of 5,000 feet, the flight attendants called and reported a strong smell of something burning and that the passengers were reporting the same smell to them.I transferred control of the aircraft to the First Officer as I spoke with the flight attendants. We determined that the best course of action was to return to the airport. We requested to level off at 9,000 feet and advised ATC. I began the smoke and fumes checklist as we entered a downwind vector to return to the field. We descended and executed the Runway XX ILS approach to an overweight landing of approximately 163,000 pounds. Upon exiting the runway, we requested an inspection by crash fire rescue personnel who met the aircraft. They reported a normal heat signature on the aircraft. We returned to the terminal and parked at gate. We were met by station Maintenance Personnel to discuss the incident, and then wrote up the details for the smoke/fumes incident and the overweight landing in the AML.We loaded our passengers onto a new aircraft and departed approximately 2 hours late.I suspect that this event may have been the result of a recent engine change. Maintenance told us upon our return that there had been an engine change. Possibly some residual fluid that entered the bleed air system upon takeoff and climb at high power.I do not have any suggestions for avoiding a recurrence of this event I believe the situation was handled appropriately by both the cabin and flight deck crew when one considers the very real possibility of a potential fire.				B737 Captain reported that smoke and fumes in the cabin resulted in an air return.	
1722468	202001	1801-2400	SCT.TRACON	CA				4300					Night				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 608					1722468												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors	Human Factors	On arrival into BUR, we were at 8,000 ft. Controller gave us a 'Descend and maintain 4,000 ft.' Pilot Flying set 4,000 ft. and we began descent. At about 4,500 ft., we said to each other this doesn't look right. We were about to query ATC when we got a terrain alert. We began to comply with the alert starting a climb. ATC also at that time issued a terrain alert and to maintain 5,000 ft. We had a visual on terrain in front of us and it was no factor, as we were already climbing. We were given a phone number. ATC on the phone call (on the ground) apologized and admitted fault (after listening to the tapes) of giving us an incorrect (too low) altitude.I've been into Burbank VERY MANY times. I know that 4,000 ft. was too low for that segment. We were on the last leg of a long day; however, I felt alert and didn't feel fatigue was an issue for me personally. We were distracted by a few other items and we were also in moderate turbulence. It was the Controller's fault, but I should have noticed the problem sooner.				B737 First Officer reported ATC assigned too low altitude resulting in a EGPWS Terrain Alert.	
1722565	202001	1801-2400						36000		VMC							Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																										Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 205					1722565	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1722580	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Aircraft was at FL 360 and .78 mach when the auto throttles clicked off, we got two master warning lights, (No. 1 Engine) N1 rolled back to 45% and fluctuated. The aircraft started noticeably shaking. The First Officer was flying and pulled the number one throttle to idle, the shaking lessened but did not stop. I immediately started the APU. The flight attendants were ringing the cockpit, I had cabin now on my headset and could hear they were concerned the aircraft was shaking really bad in the back and that passengers were saying sparks and flames were coming from under the wing. I had already [advised ATC] and told them we had engine trouble. Ask them to stand by for further. I grabbed the QRC and started the ENGINE FIRE or ENGINE SERVER DAMAGE or SEPARATION check list. We did not have fire indications in the cockpit but with the shaking and reports of sparks and flames from under the wing I thought that the appropriate plan of action. I performed the checklist and First Officer flew the aircraft. We completed the first (few) steps on the QRC with in 2 minutes of the realization we had a major engine problem. After we had engine shut down I told ATC we needed lower that we had lost an engine and would get back with flight vitals. ATC told us we had ZZZ behind us we had a military base to our left and ZZZ1 straight ahead and asked out intentions. I quickly talk over with First Officer ZZZ was behind and about a thirty-minute spiral down to an unfamiliar military base, so decision was made for take straight in to ZZZ1. I completed the check list in QRH while First Officer flew and do normal radio. I coordinated with the flight attendants we had shut down the morning #1 engine we had [advised ATC] and that we were going to make a single engine landing in ZZZ1. I told them I would get back with the as far as the time to ETA and that we were not expecting an evacuation, I also told them I would make an announcement to the passengers shortly. I told ATC that we wanted the longest runway and to have safety vehicles to meet the aircraft at the runway about mid field. First Officer and I briefed the approach for Runway XX ZZZ1. We used a 1,000 fpm descent to allow descent rate with minimal power to maintain safe airspeed get the aircraft to 12,000 ft. about 20 miles from ZZZ1. I told First Officer I hope he didn't mind but I felt more comfortable landing the aircraft (this had no bearing on his ability his performance was stellar). At 12,000 ft. I took the aircraft and asked First Officer to verify all items on the check list were complete. We held off on the Grd Prox switch for the flaps 15 landing so we could keep out speed above 250 kts. Having First Officer verify items was to make sure this item and all others were complete, First Officer accomplished a non standard landing. I gave the cabin a 10 minute prep and a quick announcement for passengers before descending below 10,000 ft. Flew down wind slowing and descending to 3,000 ft. turned base at 12 miles. Completed a successful stable single engine landing on Runway XX ZZZ1. Stopped the aircraft at mid field. Told passengers remain seated three times. Safety equipment came to aircraft inspected entire aircraft were given the ok to taxi to gate. Safety equipment followed us to the gate without incident. In closing Cabin crew maintained professional calm attitude. More than effectively conveyed situation in the cabin to the flight deck so the best information was available. First Officer was stellar in his performance was level headed. He was task orientated focused and relayed information promptly and succinctly. He made sure I had all the information I needed to make decisions. This entire crew should receive recognitions for their duties preformed during this in flight emergency.		At FL 360 cruising to ZZZ the airplane started to shake noticeably and at the same time the auto throttle disconnected. Looking for clues the Flight Attendant called us reporting the shaking problem. The Captain and I realized the (No. 1) N1 dropped to approx. 45%, while the N2 was still around 85-90%, and a quick scan of the instruments nothing else out of the normal. The Flight Attendant called us the second time reporting passengers looking out the window saw sparking and fire below the left wing. We quickly realized we were losing the left engine. We [advised ATC] and we headed to ZZZ. We complied with the appropriate check list and descended to a lower altitude. We requested the longest runway in ZZZ (Runway XX) and the assistance from [crash fire rescue] on the ground. I flew the airplane while the Captain finished the check list and prepared the cabin crew for the emergency landing and he did the PA to the passengers. After that, the Captain flew the airplane and accomplished the landing at ZZZ on Runway XX runway. We stopped the aircraft on the runway and verified with [crash fire rescue] that the aircraft was safe to taxi to the gate.		B737 flight crew reported an engine failure in cruise that resulted in a single engine landing at the destination airport.	
1722643	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1722643												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Try to be more proactive with Maintenance especially when the aircraft has a history of fume events and not fly that particular aircraft until the problem has been found.				A320 Captain reported a fume event on aircraft with a history of fume issues.	
1722648	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						IMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Other / Unknown			1722648												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Aircraft	Ambiguous	Odors are subjective in nature. Communication between the cabin and flight deck needs to be less subjective. A better description of an odor, the location, duration and intensity would be more helpful.				A320 First Officer reported the need for more definitive information regarding the identification of odors on aircraft.	
1722721	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																										Pneumatic System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Other / Unknown; Troubleshooting			1722721												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	This was the 3rd fume event in this aircraft in 4 days. Maintenance at the airplane stated they were deferring pack 1 as a result. This course of action should have been taken after the first or second event, making the 3rd an avoidable incident.				A320 First Officer reported multiple avoidable fume events on same aircraft due to incorrect maintenance trouble shooting procedure.	
1722733	202001	0601-1200	SCT.TRACON	CA						Mixed						TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B LAX																														UAV - Unpiloted Aerial Vehicle									Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Situational Awareness			1722733												Conflict NMAC			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	EMP missiles installed on all [airline] aircraft. Just kidding, there is no corrective action that can be taken other than hopefully spotting a drone before striking it.				Air Carrier pilot reported an NMAC with a drone while inbound to LAX.	
1723437	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 16000					1723437												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	First leg of trip. Captain's leg. AML (Maintenance Log) Review showed aircraft had just had an engine change. On takeoff, noticed odor/ fumes. As climbing out it became stronger. Cabin crew called to say they had odor/ fumes in the cabin. I assumed Pilot Flying duties while Captain coordinated with Cabin, OPS, and ATC. [ATC was notified of the situation], CFR (crash, fire, rescue) notified. As flight progressed, odor/ fumes became less pronounced. Landed on XX, CFR said no heat signatures, followed us to gate. Normal deplaning. Cause: Engine change. Mechanic that met us said Maintenance was supposed to have done a high power run up- not sure if it was completed. Suggestions: Ensure aircraft is airworthy after major maintenance.				B737-800 First Officer reported an air return due to a fume event during initial climb.	
1723510	202001	1201-1800	N90.TRACON	NY				3000								TRACON N90	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B EWR																												TRACON N90		Commercial Fixed Wing				IFR			Landing		Class B EWR																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 168; Flight Crew Type 8275					1723510	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 143; Flight Crew Type 4606					1723525	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Environment - Non Weather Related	On dogleg to an ILS Runway 22L EWR, we experienced an uncommanded roll to the right that increased in rate and approached 30 degrees. We were following another narrow body aircraft and the winds were relatively light at altitude. Spacing was normal 4 to 4.5 miles. I was the flying pilot. We had just received a turn to the right from ATC. While the First Officer was responding to the vector on the radio, then aircraft started to bank due to the wake. I disconnected the autopilot immediately, counter rolled to the left, [I] added power and climbed to get out of the wake. We gained about 300 to 400 ft. and rolled farther right than ATC requested. The stall warning activated for about 1 second and the airspeed dropped slightly below our flaps 5 speed with the flaps at 5. Once out of the effect of the wake we rolled back to the proper heading and got back to the proper airspeed and altitude. The First Officer immediately informed ATC of the deviation due to the wake turbulence. We intercepted ILS final and had a normal stabilized landing.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737 flight crew reported that a wake turbulence encounter resulted in altitude, track, and speed deviations.	
1723709	202001	1801-2400	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO						Mixed						Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1723709												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	An air quality monitoring system [needs to] be installed on pressurized turbine powered aircraft.				A320 First Officer recommended that an air quality monitor be installed on pressurized turbine aircraft.	
1725078	202002	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			IMC			Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 207; Flight Crew Type 905		Workload; Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface			1725078	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1725095	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors	Human Factors	Night IMC through WX. Dark cockpit, procedures followed. Simply missed flap setting. I erroneously executed Landing Checklist. Instead of Flaps 30, Green Light, Flaps were actually Flaps 25: with a Green Light. Runway was in sight, FO (First Officer) flying, AP, AT off. GPWS call came approaching 500 ft. AGL, TOO LOW, TERRAIN, followed by TOO LOW, FLAPS. I called for and we immediately executed a go-around without incident. Was very busy go-around 1200-1400 ft. ceilings, IMC above airport and busy. Initial altitude 1700 ft. MSL, multiple vectors to downwind and low altitude. Followed vectors around to set up for [another approach]. And just to make sure we had practice, Tower cancelled approach clearance at 1500 ft. AGL due traffic compression. Went around and did it again.		Uneventful flight, excellent crew communication/coordination; checklists completed in timely manner; on approach (gear down, flaps 15), I requested flaps 30 - landing checklist. Captain moved flap handle and used checklist to read landing check list. On short final, 'too low flaps' was heard, Captain acknowledged and stated 'go around'; a go-around was executed, following standard go-around procedures and by all accounts event was a successful go-around and after checklist was completed Captain coordinated another approach to airport, and while on approach, ATC advised us of slower aircraft ahead and assigned speed; we complied and shortly thereafter, ATC apologized and issued 'go around' due to slower traffic ahead. We initiated another go-around and all procedures followed and it was a successful go-around. Once checklists complete, with my knowledge, Captain called and briefly spoke to flight attendants to inform of events.ATC vectored us for approach, and approach was successful. Upon landing, Captain made an appropriate and informative announcement to passengers regarding events. After arrival to gate and passenger deplaned, Captain and I debriefed events.		B737-900 flight crew reported executing a go-around after receiving a GPWS warning 'Too low, flaps' on approach.	
1725079	202002		BOS.Airport	MA			1000			VMC			Night				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																																Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Total 2180; Flight Crew Type 1396					1725079												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure	Procedure	On approach into Boston, at night with visibility unlimited. We were initially cleared direct to RIPIT, the outer marker, and to descend to 2000 ft, on approach over the water to Runway 27. The altitude at RIPIT is charted at 1700 ft.With no traffic between us and the airport, we were then cleared direct RIPIT and cleared for the visual approach. My plan was to keep the speed up as long as I was comfortable and then slow a few miles outside of the outer marker but I did not want to be high and fast, thus causing an unstabilized approach. I turned off the auto pilot and auto throttles and was hand flying the airplane in a gradual descent of maybe 200-300 ft./min with approach mode armed and still in open descent however, almost all of my visual reference was outside and not referencing instruments or flight director. I had the runway in sight during the entirety of the approach however at some point, I completely lost situation awareness and ended up down between 900 to 1000 ft. AGL with the gear still up and 210 kts. My relationship to the outer marker when I 'captured this error' is unclear, as I stated my having lost situational awareness. I immediately leveled the aircraft at 1000 ft., lowered the gear and slowed to flap speed and had an otherwise stabilized approach and landing on Runway 27 at Boston.Contributing factors to this incident would be flying into a 'black hole' and the visual references associated with that as well as fatigue. I did not feel fatigued at the time however, in recounting this incident and thinking about other small errors, such as missed radio calls, I do not think I was 'on the top of my game'. I did not realize this at the time otherwise I would have flown more conservatively. I had normal rest having been awake 12 hours and a duty start time of XA:00 with an event at XG:20.				A320 Captain reported descending out of assigned altitude during final approach, requiring evasive action.	
1725168	202002	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				28000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1725168												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff from ZZZ we noticed an unusual odor like wet socks as we continued the climb the odor never dissipated. We leveled off at FL280, I gave controls of aircraft to [the] First Officer and called Dispatch/Maintenance. I was told there was a previous write up, but were not able to duplicate. After consulting with both it was determined to return to ZZZ. An overweight landing was performed approximately 3,400 lbs over and touchdown was less than 200 FPM. Upon reaching gate Maintenance came aboard and at that point I was informed for the first time that apparently some blades were changed not sure if maybe some residual oil/grease might have been left and got into the system? I was surprised that Maintenance Control Center didn't know that maintenance had been done on that day.				A320 Captain reported an air return due to a fume event during initial climb.	
1725507	202001	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				10000		IMC							Air Carrier	A330		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 28000		Workload			1725507	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload			1725154	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Fume event on initial climb from ZZZ to ZZZZ reported by #1FA (Flight Attendant). Stopped climb at 10,000 feet and stayed by the ZZZ airport as we investigated the situation. After approximately 25 minutes we decided that a return to field was necessary and ATC was notified. During final approach due to high task loading, there was possible momentarily flap limit over speed. Landed without further incidence and priority handling was canceled. Airbus warning system did not indicate this and there was no paper limit report produced. The potential flap limit over speed was put in the AML (maintenance log).		[Report narrative contained no additional information].		A330 flight crew reported executing an air return due to a fume event during initial climb.	
1726067	202002		DEN.Airport	CO		5		10000					Night			TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B DEN																												TRACON D01		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer							Climb		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 642		Workload; Distraction; Situational Awareness			1726067												Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	I take full responsibility for this error. When we left the gate I felt alert and ready to accomplish the last flight of the night. We had already been delayed due to ATC constraints and weather in DEN. In these weather conditions everything takes longer. Taxiways were slippery so we had to taxi slowly. Deice took some time and the frequencies were congested, so afterward Ramp took our flight number and promptly forgot about us leading to us being three aircraft behind where we should have taxied out. This isn't a factor but was frustrating. We taxied to 16L and took off after almost an hour from push. We took off in LNAV and VNAV putting FL230 in the window instead of 10,000', which we would have put in previously for the hold down. I mentally noted the 10,000' level off. Shortly after takeoff in snow, low visibility and high traffic conditions, we hit wake turbulence. It wasn't super significant but it was enough that I was thinking about it beyond its finish and I shouldn't have. I opted to hand fly (biggest mistake). As we reached 10,000', I was hand flying and following the command bars, we got a TCAS TA and I slowed the climb still remaining on or just below the command bars with LNAV/VNAV and autothrottles on, but hand flying. I looked at my altitude and saw 10,300' and finally remembered that we had a hold-down. I returned to 10,000' while simultaneously hearing ATC ask about our altitude and then giving us a number to call. I have not had an altitude deviation in my career previously. The main factor that could have prevented this was using the appropriate level of automation. After the wake turbulence encounter I should have turned on the autopilot and managed the automation freeing my attention to reference the SID and remember the 10000 ft hold down. Distractions and alertness were contributing factors but could have been better managed.				B737NG First Officer reported overshooting an altitude restriction while hand flying climbing out of DEN after being distracted by a wake turbulence encounter.	
1726117	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		120		41000					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	STAR ZZZZZ																									Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 500; Flight Crew Type 11000		Workload			1726117												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	VNAV/Autopilot uses aggressive pitch changes to chase the VNAV profile and disregards airspeed target at top of descent.  Aircraft would overspeed if not for pilot intervention.  This occurs at (nearly) EVERY top of descent on seemingly EVERY flight.  In this case, we had a strong jet-stream tailwind component.  Descent winds were programmed and accurate.This is not a one-time event.  Every pilot experiences this and everyone complains about it.  We need to use speed brakes for drag at nearly every top of descent.  We often need to kick off the autopilot and/or switch to 'CWS' for a smoother transition - while pulling back to induce drag, break the rate of descent and reduce indicated airspeed/Mach.  This poor-quality system causes distraction(s) and increases workload.  It creates a poor quality 'ride' hence is bad for customer experience and provides substandard hospitality.  This is a hazard to flight attendant safety.  This all occurs nearly simultaneously with the increased workload associated with changing altitudes, ATC radio handoffs and traffic callouts/TCAS events.  More often than not, the auto-throttles simultaneously kick in and advance during steep descent, necessitating further pilot intervention to override this obvious unnecessary and distracting action. Sometimes, the FMC scratch-pad message 'drag required' will present itself - even as the autothrottles are advancing and the autopilot is dropping the nose!This cycle repeats itself as the aircraft transitions altitudes and experiences changing atmospherics, most obviously changing wind speed and/or direction.  The bewilderment at the automation is a regular passenger in the cockpit.In summary; 'what (the xxxx) is it doing?' is not just a statement of startled bewilderment, it is evidence of a gross failure of the VNAV/autopilot/auto-throttle system.  Preventive Measures: ['Use ALT INTV or descend now and begin an early, hopefully smooth interception of the descent profile.  Reprogram the VNAV.  Hire better engineers.  Update the FMC database.  Perform a systems analysis to discover the fault(s).  Redesign the Speed-Pitch-Autothrottle (VNAV/Auto-Pilot/Auto-throttle) System for better functionality.  Can we increase the size of the descent wind database? Teach the auto-throttles to be more aggressive and retard in anticipation of a nose down with increasing airspeed.  Introduce anticipation circuitry into the control system.  Can we get a little A.I. and put some learning-software on the jets? Analyze speed brake utilization data to get factual data of wasted fuel (cost impact).  Compare to other/better automation.  Improve descent atmospheric database.  Make the automation work better.				B737-700 Captain reported that the VNAV/Autopilot system used aggressive pitch changes to follow the VNAV profile, while disregarding the target airspeed at top of descent. The Captain stated multiple issues result from this and offered recommendations for addressing them.	
1726155	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				10000		VMC			Dusk				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Failed																																					Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1726155	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1726159	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; MEL; Procedure	Aircraft	Aircraft dispatched with MEL Right Pack Inoperative. I was Pilot Flying climbing to 8000 ft. on departure when the left BLEED TRIP OFF light illuminated around 7000 ft. FO was Pilot Monitoring and ran the checklist in the QRH (Quick Reference Handbook). The trip reset worked. All indications were normal at this point and we received further climb clearance. I initiated the climb as I was considering my options. The one good pack had just hiccupped and I was contemplating continuing versus a return to ZZZ when the same light came on again passing about 11,000 ft. It would not reset a second time and right after the reset attempt the Cabin Altitude warning light and horn came on. My decision was made for me. I disengaged the autopilot and autothrottles and initiated a fairly smooth pushover while directing the FO to tell Center we needed to descend back to 10,000 ft. Center was very accommodating as I suspect they heard the horn in the background. We were now passing about 12000 ft. going to 13,000 ft. before I was able to stop the climb momentum and descend. We barely had time to comply with the QRC (Quick Reference Checklist) before we were back at 10,000 ft. Complied with the rest of the QRC and QRH and asked for and received clearance for lower altitudes as we descended gradually back into ZZZ for an uneventful landing about 2000 lbs. below max landing weight. Cabin altitude did not exceed approximately 13,000 ft. so the passenger masks did not deploy.The only working pack was tripped off at the bleed. Don't dispatch aircraft with one pack.		We were operating a Single Pack aircraft as Right Pack was previously MEL'd by maintenance. On our departure climb out someplace around 8000 ft. we had a LEFT BLEED TRIP OFF Caution indicating a LEFT BLEED Overheat/Overpressure condition.We ran the BLEED TRIP OFF Caution QRH checklist and the BLEED TRIP OFF light extinguished which was considered reset and the end of the procedure. After some time as we continued our climb the LEFT BLEED TRIP OFF Caution light appeared again for the 2nd time.This time as we were re-running the QRH checklist for the 2nd time the BLEED TRIP OFF indication would not reset anymore and stayed illuminated confirming a BLEED Overheat/Overpressure condition. Shortly after we also received a CABIN ALTITUDE Aural Warning indication. We advised ATC, initiated a descent to 10,000 ft. and ran the QRC/QRH CABIN ALTITUDE Warning checklist.After completing the QRH checklist we assessed the situation and due to the multiple BLEED TRIP OFF Overheat/Overpressure indications coupled with the CABIN ALTITUDE Warning indication we decided to divert back to ZZZ for a normal landing. We safely made a normal landing, parked at the gate and handed [the] plane to the ZZZ maintenance team.		B737 Flight Crew reported that the aircraft was dispatched with one air-conditioning pack MEL'd, and the operating pack tripped off after takeoff, causing an air turnback.	
1726224	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				36000		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																										Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 137; Flight Crew Total 16000; Flight Crew Type 3300					1726224	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 177; Flight Crew Total 18670					1726467	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing through 10,000 ft., we received a Master Caution with a 'LOW PRESSURE' light illuminated over the Engine Driven Hydraulic Pump for System A. I used the QRH (Quick Reference Handbook) and completed the checklist for the malfunction, isolating the pump. Shortly after that, we noticed that the quantity indication was decreasing, and stabilized at 20%. We recognized that as a leak in the pump itself, or possibly the lines to the pump, and considered that the primary cause for the failure. As such, and given that the electric pump was operational and no other system degradation was noticed, we continued to ZZZ. We monitored the hydraulic page, and noticed that the quantity was further decreasing, at a rate of 1% every ten to fifteen minutes. We calculated that we'd land with 8% to 10%.During this time (from occurrence to top of descent), we coordinated with Dispatch and Maintenance Control. The decision to continue was supported. At the top of descent (almost immediately after starting down), the Master Caution illuminated again, this time with a 'LOW PRESSURE' light above the B system Engine Driven Hydraulic Pump. I delegated flying and radios to the FO, who was the Pilot Flying for the leg, and ran the same checklist as before. With the B system pump isolated, we noticed the quantity decreasing (initially 106%, now 80%.) It stabilized at 80% and didn't further decrease. After running the checklist, I told the FO that with two system failures that weren't at all typical, I would like to proceed direct to ZZZ. He agreed, and I did so with Approach.We were approximately 75 miles from the airport. I sent a message to Dispatch and Maintenance Control explaining the new problem. I called the Purser and told her what was happening, and discussed the [briefing] items. I told her that no cabin preparation was necessary and that we'd land in about 25 minutes. We concluded, and I made a PA to the passengers. I told them that we had a hydraulic problem, that the airplane was flying normally, but that as a cautionary measure we would be followed in by fire trucks after landing all the way to the gate, and that it was not something with which to be concerned.We got vectored for a right base and landed with no incident. On rollout, the A system quantity went to zero, and then restabilized at 12%. Nosewheel steering was normal, and braking action was normal. I elected to taxi clear of the runway and proceed to the gate with no further assistance. We ran the Parking Checklist and debriefed the flight, then per Gate Message, ran the Terminating Checklist. By that time, maintenance technicians were already inspecting the airplane. We could only see the left engine, and there was a large puddle of fluid underneath it.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737NG Flight Crew reported continuing to destination with a low pressure caution on the A system hydraulics, and as they approached destination, a low pressure caution on the B system hydraulics.	
1726705	202002	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1726705												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Find the source and stop the leaks. On descent into ZZZ, base to final, we had an extremely strong odor of engine oil in the cockpit. It smelled like sour dirty socks. It was so intense, almost 'thick' if you will. We donned our masks, turned off the packs, opened the ram air valve, and landed as we were approaching the final approach fix. No passenger reported any smell. The aft Flight Attendant reported a short faint smell.				A320 First Officer reported a strong odor of engine oil in the cockpit during initial approach and continued to landing at destination.	
1727117	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				15000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 157; Flight Crew Type 4961		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1727117												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing through approximately 15,000 ft. we had a left pack trip. We leveled off at FL190 and accomplished the PACK non-normal checklist. The left pack light extinguished, but re-illuminated after about a minute. We re-ran the PACK checklist again and the left pack light extinguished again. I suggested to the FO (First Officer) to check the three supply duct temperatures after the second left pack trip. We observed the aft supply duct temperature to be excessively high, approximately 70 degrees C, and rising. I then suggested that we turn the Aft Cab Temp Selector to off. After we did that the aft supply duct temperature seemed to stabilize, but was still high. I noticed the left ram door full open light was illuminated as well. There was also a slight burning smell in the cockpit. I attempted to have the Dispatcher call me in the hopes of getting a phone patch with [Maintenance] via the Call Me prompt. We never heard the Dispatcher over the frequency, and only briefly heard San Francisco Arinc. Arinc attempted several SELACAL's , but we could hear no one on the designated frequency. I suggested a new frequency to Dispatch via acars, but never heard back. At this point the left pack was stabilized, and we had burned some fuel by staying low. Both the FO and myself felt that it was much safer to return to [departure airport] rather than climb to FL390 with an overheating, compromised pack. We diverted safely and contacted both [Operations Manager] and Dispatcher. We also thoroughly briefed the mechanics at the return gate.				B737NG Captain reported returning to departure airport after twice experiencing left pack trip.	
1727545	202002	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						IMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																										Pneumatic System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 13000		Physiological - Other			1727545	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other			1727552	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	The aircraft arrived the night before and overnighted in ZZZ1. We were to depart at XB:16 the next morning. The ambient temp at XA:15 when we arrived at the jet was -10 with a wind chill of -30.  During engine start the oil clog ECAM messages displayed momentary for both engines due to the extreme cold. Both FO (First Officer) and myself discussed the warm up of the engines. FO was the pilot flying the leg and the flight was non-eventful during cruise. There was a front approaching ZZZ and Dispatch was keeping us informed of the weather. I spoke to Dispatch inflight and we agreed to add ZZZ2 as an alternate due to the line of weather that was to the east of the field.  When we checked in with ATC we were told to expect holding on the [name] arrival at ZZZZZ at FL200. FO entered the hold and at this point the weather was between us and the field below our altitude. Since the descent would be around weather I called the flight attendants and had them prepare the cabin for landing and to remain seated for the remainder of the flight. After one turn in holding, at FL200, ATC told us we were going to be vectored to an arrival to the east of the field due to weather. As we started our descent, on a vectored heading, I started to smell a 'musty sock' odor.  I looked at FO and asked if he was smelling the same thing. He said yes and we both put on our O2 masks. I made a quick call to the back to check on the flight attendants. The number one said he didn't smell anything and the FAs (flight attendants) in the back said that she did smell 'some sort of odor'. I remember thinking to myself that I was starting to feel the same hypoxic symptoms I felt during Air Force altitude training. FO and myself assessed each other and we were both feeling much better with O2. At this point we were in a descent, on vectors, going to the east of the field for weather. I decided to notify ATC due to the fumes and not knowing if the cockpit would remain clear of haze because the onset of the fumes was so rapid. ATC quickly gave us vectors to runway XX in ZZZ. We stayed on O2 for the reminder of the flight. After landing and clearing the runway, I turned the aircraft bleeds and packs off. EMT and fire department personal checked passengers as they exited the aircraft. Cause: As I stated above the engines were cold soaked on startup. This odor has been linked to oil and oil seals in the engine and packs. Maybe the first real thrust reduction exposed a gap in one of the oil seals. Suggestions: This type of odor is associated with tricresylphosphate (TCP). When we landed Maintenance told us that the only way to test the aircraft was to start the engine on the ground and then smell for the TCP 'musty sock' odor. I find it unbelievable in a fume event like this that there isn't a procedure to take a swab of the cockpit or cabin to detect the presence of TCP, compound that would easily be found. There has to be residue left behind in the cockpit. When I went home I shower and scrubbed myself clean.		On descent into ZZZ in IMC, Captain and I detected an unusual odor. We donned our masks. I was pilot flying so I maintained control of the aircraft while the Captain spoke with the FAs (flight attendants) and ran the QRH. [We notified ATC] and we proceeded direct to ZZZ while deviating around some strong radar returns. Landed without event and taxied with priority to the gate. Aircraft was removed from service. My only suggestion would be immediate removal from duty. The Chief Pilot laid out our options, but left it up to us to decide if we were fit to continue duty. It took some time to self evaluate after the adrenaline wore off, and I'm glad we went to get evaluated by a medical professional.		A320 flight crew reported a fume event during initial approach resulted in the crew donning oxygen masks and continuing to destination.	
1727748	202002	1201-1800	LAX.Airport	CA				7500								TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B LAX																												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	Widebody Transport		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Distraction			1727748												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	Downwind to base leg we encountered wake turbulence from an Aircraft Y, their position previously called by ATC. ATC traffic issued a traffic advisory, separate from the Aircraft Y that we were following. Due to our heading into the rising sun, we could not make eye contact with the second aircraft. ATC issued a clearance to descend to 3000 feet while we were presently leaving (approx) 7500 feet. ATC then queried if we had the airport in sight. F/O (First Officer) confirmed that it was. I began slowing the aircraft in anticipation of an approach clearance from ATC. We were issued a clearance to turn southbound (base leg). The landing gear was lowered, and flaps began to be positioned on flap speed schedule. I did not recall if we had in fact been given a clearance to proceed via a visual approach to Runway 24R. At this point, we queried ATC for that approach clearance. We were then issued a clearance for a visual approach to Runway 24R. I did not realize that I had overshot the final approach course, and corrected by turning to a more northerly heading to intercept the Runway 24R course. Proceeding visually, I had (what I thought) was 24R in sight. LAX Tower informed us that we were lined up for Runway 25R. We were re-cleared for a visual approach to Runway 24R, and I was able to proceed over to that runway in a stable landing configuration and land. My biggest takeaways from this event is to return to the most effective mode(s) of automation after an upset. I should have reengaged the autopilot. I also should have continued on the downwind leg until I had the aircraft configured in a less hurried manner.				B737-900 Captain reported lining up with the wrong runway after encountering wake turbulence from a preceding aircraft on a visual approach to LAX.	
1727839	202002	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class C ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1727839												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Monitored closely aircrafts with recurrent fume events to investigate the root cause of the problem until a permanent solution that yields the desired results is achieved. Including but not limited to replacement of currently used engine life-limited parts, and the check intervals of such parts that could be potentially leading to these fume events. During the takeoff and initial climbout phase at about 500 feet AGL the Captain and I smelled a strong dirty-socks odor. The Captain checked with the flight attendants to confirm there was no odor in the cabin. Shortly after, during the climb phase we ran the supplementary procedure for elimination of odor/fumes in the cockpit/cabin. After turning Pack 1 off, the smell started to dissipate and a few minutes later there was no odor. We notified Dispatch, applied MEL operational procedures associated with one pack off. The rest of the flight was uneventful.				A320 First Officer reported smelling a strong dirty socks odor during initial climb.	
1729239	202002	1201-1800	LAX.Airport	CA												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B LAX																												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	Widebody Transport		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction			1729239	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1729238	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure; Weather	Human Factors	IRNMN 2 RNAV Arrival [to LAX]. Downwind to base leg we encountered wake turbulence from an Aircraft Y, their position previously called by ATC.  ATC traffic issued a traffic advisory, separate from the Aircraft Y that we were following. Due to our heading into the rising sun, we could not make eye contact with the second aircraft. ATC issued a clearance to descend to 3000-feet while we were presently leaving (approx) 7500-feet. ATC then queried if we had the airport in sight. F/O (First Officer) confirmed that it was. I began slowing the aircraft in anticipation of an approach clearance from ATC. We were issued a clearance to turn southbound (base leg). The landing gear was lowered, and flaps began to be positioned on flap speed schedule. I did not recall if we had in fact been given a clearance to proceed via a Visual Approach to Runway 24R. At this point, we queried ATC for that approach clearance. We were then issued a clearance for a Visual Approach to Runway 24R. I did not realize that I had overshot the final approach course, and corrected by turning to a more northerly heading to intercept the Runway 24R course. Proceeding visually, I had what I thought was 24R in sight. LAX Tower informed us that we were lined up for runway 25R. We were re-cleared for a visual approach to Runway 24R, and I was able to proceed over to that runway in a stable landing configuration and land. My biggest take-away from this event is to return to the most effective mode(s) of automation after an upset. I should have reengaged the autopilot. I also should have continued on the downwind leg until I had the aircraft configured in a less hurried manner.		Coming into LAX with SoCal Approach. We hit some wake turbulence that disengaged the autopilot. Captain hand flew the rest of the way in. SoCal asked us to call preceding traffic [in sight]. Unable to find the traffic but called the field in sight when they asked. SoCal gave us a significant vector towards the runway and a large speed reduction. Along with a confusing ATC instructions with a lack of visual clearance that needed to be clarified we ended up being high and fast. We were trying to configure and get back on glide path as we turned on the final approach course. We overshot the final for 24R due to a slow turn onto approach. The airfield weather was hazy and because of that when I looked up to verify our position I saw two parallel runways but didn't recognize the terminals on the right instead of left because 25R was a lot more visible than 24R. I was heads down monitoring the pilot flying configuring and speed changes in order to be stabilized. As we were realizing our overshoot Tower alerted us and we corrected the track and landed on 24R without any separation or traffic issues per our conversation with SoCal afterwards. It turned into a high workload environment partly crew induced by not reengaging the autopilot to reduce the work load. We had the field in sight but because of getting turned in early, the aircraft was high and the configuration and speed needed to be fixed. This caused ourselves to fixate [on] the vertical path and speed resulting in a slowing than normal turn onto final causing an overshoot. The hazy conditions at the field didn't help cue me of the overshoot to the south side of the field because of very similar looking runways in front of us and lack of visual of the terminal buildings. As we intercepted the final approach course I should have recognized the slow turn and localizer being displaced to the right. Along with having my map on the 5-10 scale it didn't allow me to recognize the overshoot due to it being on too high of scale for how close the final approaches are. I had all the required and recommended instrument aids up to help but due to high workload and weird visual picture that lured me into thinking we were correctly lined up. I did a poor job of monitoring the entire big picture and to help maintain our full situational awareness. My lessons learned are to understand the threat of parallel runway environments with less than normal or ideal visual conditions. Use the automation and/or strongly recommend it to the flying pilot better. And lastly to not accept a clearance if it puts us behind the airplane.		B737-900 flight crew reported overshooting final on a visual approach into LAX, citing a wake encounter and a confusing clearance as contributing.	
1729427	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			1600			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																												TRACON L30	Air Carrier	Helicopter					Passenger		Cruise		Class B ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 218; Flight Crew Type 2292		Situational Awareness			1729427												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC			Automation Aircraft RA	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Human Factors	Procedure	Established on ILS Runway XXL at approximately 1600 ft. AGL a TCAS RA was received resulting in close proximity to several orange canyon tour helicopters. After clear of conflict a missed approach was executed. There were no traffic calls from ATC, we were on an instrument approach at the FAF and got three traffic contacts below our nose not in sight and slightly to the right, flying about 150 degrees opposite our flight path in approximately a one mile trail column formation. If not for TCAS we may have had a midair, on final approach, on an instrument approach! It took at least three calls to Tower to establish that we were missed approach [and] responding to RA. Our perception was that he was busy directing [another] aircraft to clear the runway. Then Tower stated that the RA was ok because the helicopters were on a published route. I TOLD HIM I WAS WAS ON A PUBLISHED INSTRUMENT APPROACH! And if it was ok why did I get an RA? He responded switch approach frequency. Unbelievably unsafe situation.				B737 Captain reported an NMAC with a flight of 3 helicopters on approach.	
1730024	202002	1801-2400	BNA.Airport	TN									Night			TRACON BNA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class C BNA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 520					1730024												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	We were on the RYYMN 2 RNAV Arrival into BNA. As we were crossing JNKNS waypoint we encountered wake turbulence from the preceding aircraft. The aircraft rolled and the autopilot disconnected as we were starting our turn at JNKNS. Because of the wake turbulence and autopilot disengagement we did not turn immediately crossing JNKNS but we did very shortly thereafter. ATC never questioned or queried us about our track and the remainder of flight was normal and uneventful.As long as there are aircraft flying on the same arrival, there is always the risk of a wake turbulence encounter. It is and will always be important for the crew to be aware of the potential risk of such an encounter no matter how small.				B737-700 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on arrival into BNA.	
1730068	202002							4400		VMC							Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	Visual Approach																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 202; Flight Crew Total 1428; Flight Crew Type 1428		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1730068												Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airspace Structure; Human Factors; Procedure	Airspace Structure	We were on RADAR vectors with Approach given a descent down to 4,400 ft. on the eastern side of the airport. Nearing the bottom of the descent hearing the '2500 ft.' automatic call got my attention and shortly after that while leveling at our assigned altitude I heard the 'TERRAIN, TERRAIN' aural warning so I immediately disconnected the autopilot and pitched up until the warning had stopped only a few hundred feet to my knowledge. Shortly after that we got a new descent to 3,000 ft. with Approach and followed up with an uneventful Visual Approach. I inquired the Captain why he didn't mention the event to ATC after landing as he was Pilot Monitoring, but didn't really get a good answer.				An A320 pilot reported receiving a GPWS alert while on ATC vectors and descending to an ATC assigned altitude.	
1730088	202002	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			100			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Landing Gear		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 82; Flight Crew Total 26000; Flight Crew Type 302		Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting			1730088												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff during landing gear retraction we got an EICAS Caution GEAR DISAGREE and GEAR DOOR. The Main Gear showed UP indication but the Nose Gear indicated in transit (white crosshatch indication). There was loud buffeting noise that also indicated probably Nose Gear in transit. We advised ATC of the malfunction and requested holding at 10000 ft. They advised that traffic was light and that they would provide vectors instead of holding. We followed FOM CRM technique for emergency situations. The First Officer was Pilot Flying and handled routine ATC communications. I as Captain handled checklists and all other communication with Flight Attendants, passengers, Dispatch and Maintenance. I made a PA announcement to explain the noise and contacted the Purser via interphone to advise her we were working checklists and would most likely be returning. I contacted Dispatch and Maintenance using SATCOM. I described the malfunction and requested if Maintenance had any other information about this type of gear malfunction. I advised them per GEAR DISAGREE checklist the landing gear would be extended using the alternate gear extension. The GEAR DISAGREE checklist was completed. The First Officer and I discussed landing performance and discussed fuel dumping versus overweight landing. We determined that our landing weight would be 447,000 lbs. which would be 2000 lbs. overweight. Landing performance data was evaluated and was well within safe parameters.Dispatch was advised of return and overweight landing. I advised ATC since this was a non-normal situation and our workload was increased. All checklists were completed and passengers, Flight Attendants were notified of return to [departure airport]. I advised the Purser that a normal landing was planned with taxi to gate. We landed  and exited at a taxiway so that ARFF could inspect [the] nose gear area. They advised [that the] area looked normal. I advised them no further assistance was required. We taxied to [the] gate and parked.				B777 Captain reported they had an unsafe gear indication, returned to the airport and performed an overweight landing.	
1730097	202002	0001-0600	SAN.Airport	CA				24000		VMC						Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	STAR LUCKI ONE	Class A ZLA																												Center ZLA		Military				IFR	Training			Direct	Class A ZLA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 214; Flight Crew Total 12244; Flight Crew Type 9256					1730097												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Company Policy; Airspace Structure; Chart Or Publication; Procedure	Procedure	I the Captain was the Pilot Flying using the autopilot while flying on the RNAV Arrival. Center gave us a vector south off of the lateral path and cleared us to descend to an altitude of 24000 feet. After 1 to 2 minutes on this vector, ATC re-cleared us direct to a fix and to descend to an altitude of 13000 feet. We turned direct and initiated the descent on the autopilot. Almost as soon as we began the descent, we received a TA 'Traffic, traffic' followed immediately by an RA 'Climb, climb now, climb, climb now'. As soon as I heard the TA I started looking for the traffic which appeared to be a flight of 2 aircraft on TCAS and then had to immediately respond to the RA by disconnecting the autopilot and auto-throttles and then following the RA Climb guidance. Neither the First Officer nor I saw the traffic. After receiving a 'Clear of conflict' I re-engaged the autopilot and auto-throttles and resumed the previous ATC clearance. We reported the RA to ATC. ATC said that they'd had several RAs recently due to a change in Departure Procedures. The remainder of the flight continued safely and we landed without further incident.				An Air Carrier pilot reported responding to a TCAS RA for a flight of two military aircraft.	
1730100	202002						0			VMC						Ground ZZZ; Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Speedbrake/Spoiler		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 114; Flight Crew Total 869; Flight Crew Type 869		Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface; Confusion			1730100		Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 194; Flight Crew Total 456; Flight Crew Type 456					1730070	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	Other Takeoff	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	As we taxied out I saw the Speed Brake Do Not Arm light flicker once as we taxied out of the ramp area and once about halfway to the runway. I pressed the speed brake forward to make sure again that it was [in the] down detent. It was in the correct position. The light did not come on again. We ran all of the checklists and there was nothing unusual. We ran the final items on the before take off checklist. Everything was operation normal. We were cleared for take off. The First Officer was flying. He said check thrust and I took the throttles for take off. As we rolled down the runway around 60 To 70 kts., I saw the speed brake do not arm light flicker then turn on steady. I also heard the configuration horn go off. I called reject, I have the aircraft around 75 to 80 kts. I pressed the manual brakes around 60 kts. to prevent the violent stop.The First Officer made the calls to Tower and remain seated calls. I slowly taxied off the runway. We called to the back to make sure everyone was seated and ok. The Purser said everything was fine. We did not ask for assistance since we were in the low speed regime. We ran the rejected Take Off checklist from the QRC. The First Officer ran his after landing flows. We asked Ground for a spot to work the problem. I called Maintenance with Operations.  I talked with [someone] who said this was a known anomaly (which I did not know about). He had me set the flaps to one then he had me activate the number two stick shaker. He said that we were good to go. The light never came on again. I made a call to the Flight Attendants and the passengers. We ran all of our checklists again and taxied back to the runway for take off. We took off without incident and landed [at our destination].		Shortly after applying power for takeoff, the SPEED BRAKE DO NOT ARM light came on twice. It came on briefly a first time, went away and turned back on and the Captain initiated the Rejected Takeoff. The decision to reject was performed around 80 kts. and we followed company procedures.		Flight Crew reported a rejected takeoff due to a Speed Brake Do Not Arm light coming on.	
1730237	202002							32000									Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1730237												Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	Close to level off at FL320, we experienced a fairly moderate jolt. ATC had previously descended another aircraft ahead of us from our right to left, and it's possible we flew through its wake. No injuries reported by cabin crew. Flight proceeded to destination safely. Maintenance notified.				B737-800 pilot reported encountering possible wake turbulence at FL320.	
1731756	202002	1801-2400						36000									Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																										Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1731756												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	In cruise at FL360 the lead Flight Attendant informed us of a foul odor in the forward cabin. She said the smelled was like that of dirty socks. At this point, we did not smell it. We ran the QRH procedure, elimination of foul odors in flight deck and cabin. We decided to turn off Pack 1. At that point the smell came forward and it was the dirty sock smell. Within 5 minutes the smell dissipated, in both the cabin and flight deck. We left Pack 1 off and the Crossbleed shut. After communicating with the cabin crew and determining the smell was gone we decided to continue to ZZZ. We sent a message to Dispatch about our situation but did not get a reply until I queried them about our first message. Then the reply was an affirmative that they got the first message. No guidance was offered. We landed uneventfully, but had to wait 19 minutes for a gate. During this period we requested paramedics and Maintenance to meet the aircraft. I wanted my crew and myself to be checked for any adverse effects from the odor. We were checked and cleared. When Maintenance boarded the aircraft they stated that they could smell the dirty sock smell.				A320 Captain reported they were notified by the Lead Flight Attendant of a 'dirty socks' smell during cruise.  The flight crew turned off Pack 1 per QRH and the smell dissipated allowing the flight to continue to its destination.	
1731803	202002	1201-1800	CLT.TRACON	NC				13000		VMC						TRACON CLT	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	STAR CHESLY4	Class B CLT																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 2800		Time Pressure; Distraction; Human-Machine Interface			1731803	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction; Time Pressure; Human-Machine Interface			1731804	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance	Airspace Structure; Chart Or Publication; Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	Aircraft was flying on the arrival to Runway 36R. ATC instructed 'cross KRISS at 13000 ft., then descend via KRISS arrival'. Crew set aircraft to comply with instruction. Approximately 2 miles prior to KRISS, Aircraft switched controllers and immediately instructed 'Aircraft, plan Runway 36L.' Pilot Flying changed landing runway in FMS. Upon changing landing runway, FMS deleted '13000 ft.' restriction and inserted published arrival restriction at KRISS of 'at or above 11000 ft.', requiring altitude restriction to be reprogrammed. By the time Pilot Flying put the 13000 ft. restriction at KRISS, aircraft was nearing 13000 ft. and sequenced to the next fix on the arrival (early turn) and began descent. Prior to going below restriction, crew noticed impending deviation and selected 'Level Off'. Crew immediately and pro-actively corrected and crossed KRISS at 13000 ft. as assigned but dipped momentarily below. Aircraft deviated by altitude not more than a few hundred ft., and subsequently met all assigned restrictions.When inserted new arrival (36L vs 36R), FMS re-strung the approach and automatically set back to the database crossing restriction, eliminating programmed and assigned ATC crossing restriction. Was a 'Swiss cheese' moment where change occurred all within a short period of time. Had the change been entered 20 seconds earlier or later, it wouldn't have been an issue. Wait until crossing restrictions aren't imminent, and be more proactive with confirming the FMS honors entered crossing restrictions.		Aircraft was flying to CLT on the arrival up to land 36R. ATC instructed 'cross KRISS at one-three thousand, then descend via arrival'. Crew set aircraft to comply with instruction. Approximately 2 miles prior to KRISS, Aircraft switched controllers and immediately instructed 'Aircraft, plan Runway 36L.' Upon changing landing runway, FMS deleted '13000 ft.' restriction and went with normal arrival window, thus requiring altitude restriction to be reprogrammed. By the time restriction was put in, aircraft was at 13200 ft. and sequenced to the next fix on the arrival, and began descent. Crew immediately noticed deviation and selected 'Level Off' when apparent the FMS would not honor crossing restriction. Crew immediately corrected and crossed KRISS at 13000 ft. as assigned. Aircraft deviated by altitude not more than a few hundred ft., and subsequently met all assigned restrictions. Be alert for frequency changes and landing runway assignments while under heavy workloads.		A Flight Crew reported after reprogramming the FMS to a different runway than planned they descended below a crossing restriction on the RNAV Arrival.	
1732066	202002	0601-1200	STL.Airport	MO			0						Daylight			Ground STL	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Taxi		Class B STL																												Ground STL	Air Carrier			2	Part 121	IFR			Takeoff / Launch; Taxi		Class B STL																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 535; Flight Crew Type 8000		Confusion; Distraction; Time Pressure			1732066												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Incursion Taxiway			Person Flight Crew	Taxi	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Separated Traffic; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Company Policy; Human Factors	Human Factors	We pushed from [the] gate, started both engines and turned the aircraft around and approached the ramp. The First Officer called Ground Metering. They responded to monitor Ground Control. Ground Control stated to turn right onto Taxiway Charlie and taxi to the runway. Approaching the taxiway hold short line, Ground Control stated hurriedly '(our call sign) hold your position.' Our aircraft was quickly approaching the hold short line and I knew our four Flight Attendants were up in the cabin working hard and quickly to prepare the cabin for departure on this very short taxi. I did NOT slam on the brakes, yet did apply moderate braking to comply with Ground Control's directive. The aircraft was approximately nine feet over the striped line defining the border of the ramp and the taxiway.I keyed the microphone myself, or asked the First Officer to tell Ground Control (I do not remember which) that we were well over the taxiway line. There was a very rapidly approaching Company aircraft who was taxiing southeast on [the] taxiway for departure. Ground Control asked that Company if he could pass in front of us at the intersection. Company aircraft stated 'no problem.' Ground then advised us to hold our position and give way to Company. This was a big problem for us. The late directive from Ground control put us in a very precarious position. We were exposed and I felt we were in a position that could lead to a ground collision. The Company aircraft was traveling at a very high rate of speed and I was very uncomfortable. I knew that nothing was going to stop our Company. It was clear he was intent on somehow maneuvering around us. There was not time to advise ATC of our disagreement and work out 'Plan C.'  Instead of complying with Ground Control's clearance, I turned the aircraft full tiller to the right and initiated a 360 degree turn back onto the ramp, away from the speeding Company aircraft aimed directly at us. As I did so I keyed the microphone and stated 'Ground, we're not going to do that. We're turning to the right and will get in line with Company after our turn.'I stopped the aircraft after our turn and noticed that Company had maintained it's high speed. He was now approaching a left turn at the next taxiway. During the turn, Ground told us to taxi behind Company. I was very concerned and a little upset that ATC would both put us in that position and attempt to clear an aircraft around us when it should have been clear from our statement over Ground that we were well over the taxiway  ramp line. Also at fault was the speeding Company aircraft. I wonder quite often when the pilots of this airline will come to their senses and slow down while taxiing. The action is unsafe, unprofessional and I do not appreciate it. The actions I took were the best I could do in the limited time I had to make a decision. I stand by them.				B737 Captain reported they were unable to stop in a timely manner at a taxiway intersection and then disregarded ATC's subsequent taxi instructions to avoid another aircraft.	
1732072	202002	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		120		38000					Dusk			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 517		Workload; Time Pressure			1732072												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Human Factors	Human Factors	At cruise altitude of 38,000 ft., roughly 120 miles south of ZZZ, we were notified by the FAs (Flight Attendant) of an ill Passenger. 'Vomiting, and in-and-out of consciousness, and nauseous'. Male, 60 years-old was also suffering from COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disorder). There were three Doctors onboard tending to the ill Passenger. As the Pilot Flying, I handed control over to the FO (First Officer), and contacted STAT-MD. The FAs headset was not working very well, compounding the issue, so I became the go between. STAT-MD suggested we 'lay the Passenger down, head resting, feet elevated 10 inches'. STAT-MD made the recommendation to leave the ill Passenger on the floor, in this condition, for 30 to 45 minutes. If there is no change to his condition, to call STAT-MD back. I relayed this to the FAs and disconnected with STAT-MD.The three Doctors on board disagreed with STAT-MDs recommendation, and said we need to land, 'NOW'. I was 100 percent going to take the advise of Doctors ONBOARD over STAT-MD. I reconvened as the Pilot Flying, and we came to the decision to divert to ZZZ. Since the EMK (Emergency Medical Kit) would be used, and the landing would be overweight (145,300 pounds), Maintenance would need to be involved. A diversion plan was made by Dispatch, and we [advised ATC]. Center cleared us directly to ZZZZZ on the Arrival, landing west. Things got busy, real busy. I began an, expeditious, descent to get on the VNAV PATH. Only about 80 miles from ZZZ when we began our turn for the arrival. My FO picked up ATIS and ran the numbers for XXL and XXR, including a look at the Brake Cooling module. We then briefed the arrival and approach. As we got lower we kept getting turned in on the arrival, which I was okay with. I admit, I was a bit in the Yellow for awhile. We were below 10,000 ft. when we finally got around to running the Descent, Approach, and Diversion Checklists. I extended the gear early, to help lighten our weight by as much as I could. The approach was made to Runway XXR. The, soft, landing was uneventful. We taxied off the runway, and right into the ramp. The FO contacted Ramp, Ops, and Maintenance. Once in the gate, I left the seat belt sign on and made a PA to the Passengers to remain seated. The EMTs quickly deplaned the ill Passenger and left. As soon as the boarding door was open, the Parking Checklist complete, and I turned on my phone, I was barraged, to say the least. I called Dispatch and spoke with him, the Chief Pilot on Call, Ground Ops, and Maintenance. I didn't even have time to debrief with my Crew. Once I was able to talk to the FAs it was made aware to me that an IV had been administered to the ill Passenger. It ran out as we touched down. The FAs said the ill Passenger's condition IMMEDIATELY deteriorated again. Further justifying our decision to divert. I feel I made the best decision I could, considering the circumstance and utilized all necessary resources to reach the desired outcome. A job well done by my Cabin Crew, and my FO. I also think a stellar job was made by Ops and Maintenance to get the aircraft on its way again.				B738 Captain reported a medical diversion.	
1732183	202003	0601-1200	SFO.Airport	CA			890			VMC						Tower SFO	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach	Visual Approach	Class B SFO																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 123; Flight Crew Type 1735		Situational Awareness			1732183	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 159; Flight Crew Type 544		Situational Awareness			1732528	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were established on the SFO FMS Bridge Visual Rwy 28R crossing the F101D fix (4.4 nm SFO) when the SFO Tower Controller on 120.5 issued a low altitude alert and told us that the altimeter setting was 29.94. I crossed checked the altitude and it showed us at 890' msl which was 310' below the published altitude at F101D. The First Officer was the Pilot Flying with the autopilot engaged with VNAV/LNAV. Immediately upon hearing the low altitude alert from the SFO Tower Controller the First Officer disconnected the autopilot, added power and began a climb to 1200 ft msl the at or above published altitude at F101D. VNAV Path was annunciated on the FMA but was not captured at F101D. The Tower Controller queried me about the low altitude alert and said that he was going to contact engineering since we were the second aircraft in a row to set off the low altitude alert at the same point. I told the Tower Controller that we were flying the visual approach with the autopilot engaged and didn't catch the low altitude crossing. Since the First Officer recovered/repaired the error we continued to a safe landing in compliance with the stabilized approach criteria.		[Report narrative contained no additional information]		B737NG flight crew reported receiving a low altitude alert from Tower on approach to SFO.	
1732250	202003	1201-1800						35000		VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1732250	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1732636	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	While at FL 350 the flight attendants notified us of a rubber burning smell in the forward cabin . They stated the smell lasted for about one minute. The First Officer went back to the forward cabin area to assess. He did not notice the smell, queried some of the passengers for any input and concerns. There were no ECAM indications showing a problem. We continued on to ZZZ and notified Maintenance.		At FL350, the lead Flight Attendant called the Captain and said she smelled a burning rubber odor. The Captain instructed me to go back to the first class cabin and investigate. After one of the flight attendants came in the flight deck, I went back to the first class cabin and forward main cabin and did not smell anything. I spoke to the first class cabin passengers and they said they did smell a burning rubber odor but it lasted only a minute. I went back to the flight deck and reported back to the Captain. We continued to ZZZ and landed without incident. The Captain spoke to Maintenance and the outbound crew about what happened.		A320 flight crew reported they were notified by the flight attendants of a brief "rubber burning" smell during cruise.	
1732266	202002	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						VMC						Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction; Situational Awareness			1732266												Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Airport; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	On approach to ZZZZ Runway X received a GPWS 'Too Low Flaps' alert. Executed successful go around. A combination of factors all taking place in a relatively short time span created a distraction that resulted in omission of necessary procedures allowing proper configuration of aircraft for landing. ATC interruptions for requested position reports along the approach, heavy accent from foreign language requiring repetitive communications increasing workload, steep descent due to terrain separation requiring intercepting the glide path from above and extending the landing gear earlier than normal to increase drag resulting in a disruption of normal flows. Airbus logic for increasing airspeed along the approach to maintain a desired ground speed creating a distraction as a result of strong and gusty winds. It is important to create additional barriers when changes from expectations have taken place, such as extending the landing gear earlier than anticipated so as to prevent potential omission of required tasks.				A320 First Officer reported executing a go-around at ZZZZ airport after receiving a GPWS terrain warning.	
1732512	202003	0001-0600	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Climb; Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 126; Flight Crew Total 107; Flight Crew Type 107					1732512	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1732871	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On takeoff we noticed an unknown odor in the flight deck. The #1 engine had previously been changed. Flight Attendant A called and they also smelled the odor in the cabin. As we climbed out the odor seemed to be dissipate. We looked at the smoke/fire/fumes checklist and determined it was not applicable to our situation. The flight attendants said the odor seemed to have somewhat cleared up so we continued on to ZZZ. They said the odor came back on the descent into ZZZ so we wrote it up when we landed. We also did a logbook entry and maintenance had me fill out the paperwork for odor in the cabin.		After takeoff, there was a strong odor in the flight deck. After reaching a safe altitude, I called the Flight Attendants and asked them if they smelled anything. I also asked if there was anything burning in the ovens. Both forward and aft Flight Attendants smelled the odor and said there was nothing burning in the oven. The odor dissipated fairly quickly in the flight deck on climb out and the forward flight attendants also said they didn't smell the odor anymore. Aft flight attendants reported that the odor was still notable but felt like it was dissipating. The odor also returned on descent.We talked to Maintenance upon landing and filled out appropriate forms, also entered appropriate write up in the maintenance log.The aircraft had just come out of maintenance with a replaced number 1 engine. We were aware of this and assumed the odors were attributed to maintenance performed on new engine.		B737 flight crew reported a strong odor was noticed during initial climb which dissipated as the flight continued, but returned during descent for landing.	
1733019	202003	1801-2400	ZDV.ARTCC	CO				32000			Turbulence					Center ZDV	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZDV																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness			1733019												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Airspace Structure; Weather	Weather	At 32000 ft. just north of PUB the aircraft experienced Moderate to Severe turbulence. About 10 minutes before the incident the aircraft was in continuous light chop due to cloud tops. Seatbelt sign was on and PA was made by Pilot Monitoring and Pilot Flying reduced thrust to Mach .76. Pilot Monitoring contacted ATC for rides above, at about that time the airspeed began to show a decreasing trend and the Pilot Flying added power to adjust, airspeed continued to decrease and the Pilot Flying added even more power. At about that time the turbulence started and the right wing dropped and aircraft bank angles showed in excess of 50 degrees kicking off autopilot. Airspeed loss was an excess of 30-40 kts. whereby Pilot Flying applied max power. Barber poles came together however no overspeed or stall warning occurred. The aircraft lost at least 500 ft. during upset recovery with no excessive force to the aircraft. After recovery Pilot Monitoring requested an immediate climb due to Moderate to Severe turbulence up to 36000 ft. and was given 33000 ft. ATC gave us a frequency change and got an immediate climb to 36000 ft., with no further incident.  During upset recovery aircraft lost at least 500 ft. This is an approximate guess by the pilots since the loss of airspeed and power at firewall along with excessive bank angles were in the primary scan. ATC gave an immediate climb clearance and crew complied. No further action was taken. Keep seat belt sign on when crossing know turbulent areas like over the Rockies, even when weather radar, and ATC aren't reporting anything. Keep the Flight Attendant's informed and always give those important PA's to passengers 'when seated to keep those seatbelts securely fastened in case we hit any unexpected turbulence'. Because it really does happen!				B737 First Officer reported unexpected moderate to severe turbulence caused a temporary loss of control.	
1733234	202003	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				12000		VMC							Air Carrier	A330		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch; Initial Climb		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 24000					1733234	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Check Pilot	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1733230	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff there was a strong smell in the cockpit and cabin which smelled like wire insulation or electrical equipment burning. Continued with climb out to 12,000 ft., then leveled off. Communicated with cabin crew and verified the extent of the problem. Decided to [request priority handling] with departure control and requested a return back to ZZZ for an overweight landing. Requested they roll the emergency equipment with approach control and ask approach to notify Dispatch of our [situation]. Returned to the field. Performed a visual approach to a soft landing. Remained on the runway till crash & rescue had a chance to inspect the aircraft. Then taxied to the gate and removed passengers.		Right after takeoff Flight Attendant called complained about strong burning smell in the cabin which we could also smell in the cockpit. Communicated with cabin crew to verify the extent of the problem in the cabin. Burning smell also got stronger in the cockpit. We decided to [advise ATC] and return to ZZZ. We also requested approach control to roll the emergency equipment and contact Dispatch. We Landed overweight, remained on the runway till crash and rescue had a chance to inspect the aircraft. Then taxied to the gate and removed the passengers.		A330 flight crew reported a strong "burning" smell was noticed throughout the aircraft during departure resulting in a return to the departure airport	
1733310	202002	1201-1800								VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other			1733310												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	It is come to my attention that Aircraft X has had numerous fume events. While I know the problem is being worked by Airbus, Pratt & Whitney, and the company, any aircraft that has had numerous events should be either fixed or removed from service. Poisonous or toxic levels of any of these fumes could have long term and short term effects on the crew members as well as the passengers who have no idea why they may even have symptoms. And additionally, operationally [Company] is ill equipped to deal with affective communication between the crew and Chief Pilots in any abnormal situation internationally. Satellite phones and or a device that the crew could have that would be issued would help.Descending into ZZZ for an ILS circle to land. We encountered a fume event. The event was short-lived and turning off pack number one seemed to stop the issue. I had smelled the fumes before in my career. But this time the fumes had an effect of causing a headache and lightheadedness. Upon arriving at the gate, I asked my First Officer how he felt. He said he had a headache and was also lightheaded I felt the same. I opened the cockpit door and ask the flight attendants if they smelled anything. They both said immediately that they had headaches and the fumes were extremely bad. They also indicated several passengers were covering their faces. Due to the fact we were in ZZZZ we did not go to the hospital. We went to the hotel and dead headed home I still had headaches on [date] and [date] I called to remove myself from [date] and [date] flying. I have some headache yet today.				A320 Captain reported a fumes event during approach to landing.	
1733501	202003		LTAA.ARTCC	FO												Center LTAA	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Check Pilot; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 135; Flight Crew Total 15938; Flight Crew Type 8869		Situational Awareness; Workload; Distraction			1733501												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Airspace Structure; Human Factors; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Aircraft	Ambiguous	Upon crossing into the Ankara FIR after crossing TOMBI intersection, our GPS was jammed intermittently for about 5 minutes. All SOP complied with.				B777 Line check Captain reported suspected GPS jamming during cruise.	
1733606	202001	0001-0600															Air Carrier	A330		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise; Descent; Initial Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other			1733606	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Check Pilot	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Physiological - Other			1733909	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Aircraft	Aircraft	After gate arrival at ZZZ, the #1 Flight Attendant asked to come to the cockpit to discuss a problem in the cabin. He explained that during the initial climb out from ZZZ1, all the flight attendants smelled a musty, moldy, stinky sock smell. He never informed us of this issue until we were talking at the gate in ZZZ. Flight Attendant went on to explain that soon after I signaled the flight attendants that we were passing through 10,000 ft. and I made my 'Flight attendants, prepare for landing' PA, the musty, moldy, stinky sock smell returned but, stronger this time. He said passengers were closing their air vents, and covering their noses with articles of their clothing etc. He said he didn't call me due to the sterile cockpit rule. All the flight attendants agreed that the smell lasted until reaching the gate. After confirming the same story with every flight attendant, I called Maintenance, entered it into the AML, and filled out the fumes report for Maintenance. The airport sent [paramedics] to the gate area and every crew member was checked. The main common factor seemed to be high blood pressure. The top number was over 200 for four of the flight attendants and the [paramedics] transported that four to a local hospital for additional tests and observation. I made sure each flight attendant had a buddy or someone that would stay with them. My symptoms were headaches and my blood pressure slightly higher than normal, extremely tired following the event.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		A330 flight crew reported they were notified post flight by a Flight Attendant of a "stinky sock" smell during climbout.  Several flight attendants and the Captain experienced health issues.	
1733678	202003	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				9000		VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach	Direct	Class B ZZZ																												Tower ZZZ		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer							Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 47; Flight Crew Total 14244; Flight Crew Type 10578		Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1733678	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 126; Flight Crew Total 10019		Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1733698	Conflict NMAC; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach	Horizontal 0; Vertical 400		Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Aircraft; Company Policy; Procedure	Ambiguous	On final approach to ZZZ XXL ILS we received a TCAS RA. Normally we get that when an aircraft is on final to XXR. We could not find the other aircraft. We then received an RA to climb and did so. I looked straight below us out the right window and saw the intruder. He was directly below us appearing to also flying to XXL. We got to about 400 feet directly above him before we got the RA. We notified ATC and they responded that the aircraft was on the ILS to XXR. He obviously wasn't. We discontinued the approach and eventually landed on XYR. Fortunately we had not taken the advice on the 10-7 page and selected TA only. If we had, we would have had a midair.		Cleared ILS XXL approach, was not informed that there was an aircraft on XXR, did not hear the aircraft on the XXR approach or the airplane  was not on same frequency. Received a TCAS RA, 1st Officer looked out window and told me that there was an airplane below [us].  Followed TCAS RA, flew the missed approach. Didn't turn the TCAS to TA only because we were on an ILS approach and we were not informed of traffic on XXR.		A320 pilot crew reported receiving an RA on final approach and executing a missed approach. Crew reported RA was late, ATC did not issue traffic advisory and transponder was not in TA only mode as per company policy.	
1733822	202003		LAX.Airport	CA			0			VMC						Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B LAX																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 175; Flight Crew Type 2187					1733822												Conflict NMAC			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	On ILS 24R approach in VMC to LAX passed small object at our altitude 2 nm outside the FAF JETSA. The object passed down the left side (south) of the aircraft at close range, but we did not hit the object. Due to its size and our speed we were unable to tell if it was a balloon or a drone. Reported event on VHF to ATC (LAX North Tower) and phone to [Chief Pilot].				B737 Captain reported a NMAC with a UAV on approach to LAX.	
1733887	202003									VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Physiological - Other			1733887												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Divert to ZZZ. Fumes and very slight haze around Captains side window, a smell of engine coking (burning oil or electronics smell). 15-20 minutes after departure I turned to my First Officer and asked if he smelled anything. He did not. We continued, the smell persisted and about 1.5 hrs into the flight I began to become very bothered by the smell. I felt like I needed fresh air and decided to begin breathing out of my O2 mask. I began to feel physical issues, like shortness of breath and a little dizzy and thinking about needing to throw up. After a few minutes I got out of my seat for a restroom break and to determine if the smell was in the cabin. It was not. While I was in the restroom the A Flight Attendant came up and sat in my seat. She complained of the smell to the First Officer. He went to check it out and realized it was on my side but not his. I came back from the restroom and at this point it became clear to us that there was definitely a smell on just my side. We ran the QRH and then talked to Dispatch and Maintenance. We used the masks and [advised ATC], then headed for ZZZ.				A320 Captain reported a "burning oil or electronics" smell during cruise that resulted in a diversion.	
1734310	202003	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				31000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1734310	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1734324	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather	Weather	After reviewing [weather briefing program] and discussing with our Flight Dispatcher, we decided to file for FL 210 from ZZZ until east of ZZZ1, at which time we had agreed to climb to FL 390. [Program] showed moderate turbulence or worse for all altitudes from FL 210 to FL 430 along the route until east of ZZZ1.During climb out with TRACON we were informed that Center reported there was no reason to stay at FL 210 because there was only LT CHP with OCNL MDT CHP, and all flights were flying 'normal' altitudes. Based on that report we requested FL 310. We queried each controller about rides and we were told same as before. At [time] we got a report from Dispatch about MDT Turbulence in an area along our route. After plotting it in RTE 2, we determined that the area was about 25 minutes ahead of us, and that we would be thru it in about 5-10 minutes.We queried Center about ride reports and were given nothing different than before, LT CHP, OCNL MDT. I then called the Purser and inquired about the service and advised that I was turning the seat belt sign ON, and to hurry up and expect continuous Light Turbulence with possible occasional Moderate Turbulence in about 20 minutes for up to 10 minutes in duration.At approximately [25 minutes later], we encountered severe turbulence at FL310. We immediately requested lower altitude. Altitude, attitude, or airspeed was uncontrollable, and the turbulence was so intense it was impossible to even press any buttons on the MCP. I disconnected the A/P and initiated the descent to FL 210 manually as authorized by ATC. Turbulence decreased when passing FL 290 in the descent, and shortly after I was able to reconnect the A/P.There were no injuries on board, but a lot of spilled liquids and food in the aft galley. I am not sure why the Flight Attendants in the aft part of the A/C had not been aware of the possible turbulence.We had the proper plan to fly at FL 210 until east of ZZZ1. In retrospect, I wish we had stayed with that plan. However, since both TRACON and Center insisted it was Lt CHP only, with occasional MDT CHP we felt conditions may have improved. I don't understand why Center did not have the same SIGMET we had from Dispatch. Even though we asked every new controller about the rides ahead, we actually were never sure how far ahead that controller was looking, or if he/she had any information about sectors ahead. Coordinate via SAT phone with Dispatch when these changes to the plan come up. We did inform Dispatch about our new altitude and received new flight plans from her, but a discussion on the phone instead of text messaging may have been more productive.		Filed for FL 210 after conversation with Dispatcher, and [weather briefing] analysis. On climb out with Center, controller informed us that there was no reason to level at FL210, as there were no turbulence reports, and only smooth rides ahead. We requested climb to FL310. In retrospect, asking the controller how far ahead she was looking regarding turbulence would have been appropriate. With Center, and subsequent sectors, we queried them as to rides at our altitude. We received a copy of a SIGMET from Dispatch with a report of MDT turbulence ahead (with coordinates that we plotted) between FL 250-320. We informed the FA's (Flight Attendants) 20 mins prior regarding possible turbulence, and again queried controllers regarding ride reports. The answer that at most we could expect light to mod occasional chop. [29 minutes later], we encountered severe turbulence. We requested lower from ATC. Initially, the aircraft would not descend. CA (Captain) disconnected autopilot. Aircraft almost oversped twice, and AP could not be reconnected. The controller had issued us a clearance to FL 210, but we could not reach the knob on the MCP, the turbulence was so severe. As we descended through FL 290, turbulence abated. Perhaps more coordination between SIGMETS, and ATC, because clearly the SIGMET was correct.		Flight Crew reported encountering severe turbulence during flight.	
1737016	202003	0001-0600	ZZZ.TRACON	US						IMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1737016												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On descent experienced a fume event. After landing the fume event was noted in the aircraft log.				A320 First Officer reported a fumes event during descent.	
1737886	202003	0601-1200	ONT.Airport	CA				5000		IMC						TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class C SCT																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1737886												Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Equipment / Tooling; Procedure	Equipment / Tooling	Vectors to TAKOE the IAF fix for 26R at ONT. Assigned 4500 ft. to cross TAKOE at or above 4300 ft. We were in IMC approaching TAKOE from the SE approximately 5 miles from fix. Got EGPWS alert with Terrain Pull Up. Started EGPWS maneuver and alert went away immediately. Climbed to approximately 5000ft and continued approach.Cause: We were assigned to descend to 4500 ft from SOCAL after deviating around weather. There is a tower on a hill in that area which is around 3300 ft. I am speculating that tower may have caused the alert. The event was too quick and did not last long at all to analyze all factors. Pass on to SOCAL to use a higher altitude in that particular area until over the IAF. But the chart dictates that 4500ft should have been adequate.				A320 Captain reported an EGPWS alert during approach to ONT airport.	
1738057	202003	0001-0600						21000									Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent																										Pneumatic System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1738057												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I started the trip in ZZZ the crew that brought Aircraft X in had a fume event on descent at 3,000 ft., it was determined by Maintenance that the problem was with Pack 1 so, Pack 1 was deactivated, during boarding we smelled it again with Pack 2 running and APU, Maintenance was called and the APU was deactivated.During descent through FL 210 we smelled it again with both engines running, both bleeds open, Pack 1 off, APU off, Pack 2 on.				A320 Captain reported a fumes event during boarding and also during descent at destination.	
1738266	202003	0601-1200	D01.TRACON	CO		10		9000					Daylight			TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B DEN																												TRACON D01	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing			Part 121	IFR			Landing		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 513		Situational Awareness; Distraction			1738266	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 414; Flight Crew Type 1500					1738272	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	Vector put us inside DRUMM intersection which was 9,000 ft, MSL to be on path. We were given 'Maintain 9,000 ft. until established, cleared ILS Runway 35R.' At that time, we talked about Aircraft Y landing in front of us and the need to maintain one dot high for wake turbulence. We also had a decreasing headwind which necessitated a lower power setting that we also talked about. We configured early to regain the glideslope from maintaining 9,000 ft. until established. The Pilot Flying asked for flaps 40 to help be in the slot. Concentrating on Aircraft Y that landed in front of us, I failed to crosscheck the altitude when he asked for flaps back to 30 until I moved the flaps. At that time, we were at 402 ft. AGL. I should have directed a go-around at that time. Lesson learned.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737-700 flight crew reported landing from an unstabilized approach to DEN.	
1738891	202003	1801-2400	MIA.TRACON	FL				11000		VMC						TRACON MIA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Initial Climb; Climb	Direct	Class B MIA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 16600		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1738891	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Private; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Total 11160		Distraction; Other / Unknown; Situational Awareness			1738893	Conflict NMAC			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Airspace Structure	Ambiguous	During climb out from MIA, passing 11,000 ft., 280 kts., a refrigerator sized object passed below right side of fuselage, maybe 10-20 ft. below. No significant velocity vector perpendicular to our flight path. Object had no wings or foils and was blue and silver. No observed propellers or thrust devices. Could have been some sort of shaped balloon but did not appear to be, as it seemed to have angular geometry. Very high to be a drone and had no likeness to any drone I have ever seen.		I was pilot monitoring as we climbed out of the MIA terminal area. I don't recall the exact altitude, but believe we were approaching sterile cockpit (10,000 ft.) on a climb to 16,000 ft. My eyes were momentarily drawn inside the flight deck, to a message on my CDU; I recall it was the flight log info being printed. Suddenly the Captain, who was flying, exclaimed loudly as if greatly surprised, although no abrupt control inputs were made and the flight path remained unchanged. He asked, 'did you see that,' to which I replied I had not. He proceeded to mention seeing an object that we just barely missed, passing underneath us. He subsequently contacted Miami center and described the event, with the contact being the size of a small car, possibly blue and black in color. ATC queried the contact's direction of flight; I believe the Captain said it was either stationary or moving across our path. The Captain and ATC briefly discussed the possibility of the object being a drone, or some sort of balloon. The event happened very quickly and during a short, relatively task-busy flight. Requisite flight tasks were completed and the flight continued to destination without further incident.		B737 pilot crew reported a NMAC with a large UAV.	
1738959	202004	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				41000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization Control System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 367; Flight Crew Type 10000					1738959	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120					1739292	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	F/O (First Officer) and I noticed a series of 'thumps' while in cruise at FL410. We suspected pressurization and noticed cabin climbing slowly at 500 fpm. Cabin did not stabilize and was just over 9000 feet. We decided to start a normal but rapid descent to a lower cruise altitude. We got the Cabin Altitude aural alert and warning light during the descent. Accomplished QRC/QRH, which required an emergency descent and deployment of cabin O2 masks. Notified ATC and got clearance to 14,000 feet. Passing about FL230 we noticed cabin was below 9000 feet and stable. We stopped descent at FL210 and diverted to ZZZ. Checked with F/As (Flight Attendants) to make sure there were no injuries and to advise that they and passengers could remove O2 masks. The rest of flight was uneventful. Preventative Measures: F/O suggested that we start down as soon as we saw the cabin climbing after the thumps. I wanted to see if cabin would stabilize since the rate was slow. If we started down earlier, we may have avoided the emergency descent/masks since the cabin stabilized at FL230.		We noticed a thumping/ vibrating sound. It sounded like something that had to do with pressure, as in an air pressure change in the aircraft. Associated with that noise was a cabin climb of about 500 fpm on the cabin rate of climb indicator. We were at 41,000 feet and had been discussing starting a descent as soon as we could, since the cabin was definitely climbing. We called Center and started our descent when the cabin reached about 9000 feet we were already on our way down. As we were descending through about FL390 the cabin reached 10,000 feet we donned our oxygen masks, went through the QRH, complied with the required items and continued our descent to 14,000 feet. ATC was great, they asked questions, but not in a rushed 'need an answer right this second' kind of way and were very helpful in our divert plan. Our cabin stabilized passing through 23,000 feet and was trending down to a lower cabin altitude. When things settled down, we talked to the flight attendants to make sure they were ok and get a status check on the passengers. The flight attendants were fantastic, definitely handled this event professionally and took care of passengers above and beyond. The passengers that we interacted with hardly seemed rattled or concerned at all; I feel this was likely due to how the cabin crew handled the situation. We landed in [diversion airport], taxied to the gate, shut the aircraft down and went on about our day.		B737-700 flight crew reported an uneventful emergency descent & diversion due to a loss of pressurization during cruise.	
1739000	202003	0601-1200	BWI.Airport	MD			0									Tower BWI	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch	Direct																									Position Computing System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Confusion; Workload; Troubleshooting			1739000	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Confusion; Other / Unknown			1739003	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	Taxi	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action	Airport; Aircraft	Ambiguous	We were cleared for takeoff on Runway 15R at BWI. I was the Pilot Flying. When we lined up on the runway the data in my [display] did not look accurate. I elected to vacate the runway. We assessed the information and were re-cleared for take off with no further issue.		Prior to pushing up the throttles up for takeoff, the Captain stated that their [display] was not correct. I told Tower we were going to taxi clear as there was an aircraft on final. We looked down and the Flight Director was not displaying guidance even though the switches were on and the FMS correctly loaded.At the gate we had some kind of electrical MEL (I can't recall what). Also the IRUs were not aligning like normal. We called Maintenance who sent contract BWI Maintenance who told us that the gates at BWI had a problem with acquiring the GPS signal (this was strange as the terminal was north of the plane so that the plane had a clear path towards the equator). We discussed and the Captain decided we would push into the alley to try and get a signal. Again that didn't work and we discussed with Maintenance on the ops frequency who told us the closer we got to the runway it would work. We decided to taxi towards the runway. On the taxi out GPS 2 acquired a signal, but was intermittent. We pulled into the de-ice area adjacent to Taxiway P by Runway 15R. Finally we got both GPS signals to acquire which was required for our RNAV route and Required RNP FMS message.If the BWI gates historically have problems with acquiring the GPS signal, then a note about that (and if associated the Flight Directors not providing guidance) should be added to the company pages in the Jeppesen FD Pro App. If the failure was associated with the MEL, then a note about it and the flight directors not providing guidance should be added to the associated MEL in the B737 NG Minimum Equipment List / Configuration Deviation List.		B737 pilot crew reported the IRU's would not align, resulting in a rejected takeoff.	
1740051	202003	1801-2400	ZAB.ARTCC	NM				38000		VMC			Night			Center ZAB	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class B ZAB																								Powerplant Lubrication System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1740051												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	In cruise at FL380. Noticed right engine oil quantity indicated 4 qts. Went to QRH and followed procedure. Oil pressure and temperature indications were normal and pretty much matched the left engine. Continued to monitor as directed. Called dispatch on crew phone ( which worked great BTW) and gave him a heads up. Agreed that we would continue with diverts ahead of ZZZ, ZZZ1 and ZZZ2. Quantity indication continued to decrease slowly over the next hour. Pressure and temperature were normal. Approximately abeam ZZZ1 we asked to descend to FL 300 due to turbulence. At idle power, oil pressure and temperature remained normal and basically matched the left engine. However, at level off when power was increased, we noticed that the right engine oil pressure began to noticeably fluctuate and decrease. We immediately [advised] with ATC and began a diversion to ZZZ2. With right oil pressure at or near the red line limit, we elected to shut down the engine. Complied with QRH procedures and executed an ILS/visual single engine approach to RWY 26. ARRF was standing by. We cleared the runway and stopped on the parallel taxiway. ARRF crew checked out the right engine and advised that there were no issues. They followed us to gate A-9. Maintenance met us at the gate and were debriefed.				B737 Captain reported decreasing oil quantity on right engine at cruise with subsequent fluctuating pressure and quantity resulting in an inflight engine shutdown and diversion to make an priority landing.	
1740054	202004	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				12000		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Cabin Lighting		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Total 20000					1740054												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	Nearing top of descent, the ISFD quit and displayed OUT OF ORDER. Descent began and passing approx 12,000 feet; received call from cabin that they smelled smoke. The smell was of a burning smell, similar to burned popcorn. It was prevalent in rows 10-14. I confirmed that no smoke was actually present just the smell.  We adjusted the packs cooler and the smell dissipated. We had [Runway] XXL in the FMS and received a change to [Runway] XXC. While trying to load [Runway] XXC into the box we found that both FMSs would only show [Runway] XXL and no other approaches would populate on the arrival page. We then received another call from the cabin reporting that the burning smell was back but no smoke present. With the ISFD having quit, the FMS anomaly suddenly present, and more burning smell present, began to think we had a melt down of some sort going on in the E & E compartment. [Advised ATC] stating we wanted [Runway] XXL since we couldn't as yet make out the field through the scattered clouds below us and couldn't select any other approach. Fire trucks gave us a once over and followed us to the gate. Kept the flaps at 40 just in case an evac was necessary if smoke began to fill the cabin. Burning smell persisted on the ground to the gate but no smoke was present. Had pulled the Smoke, Fire, Fumes Checklist out but I decided the most prudent and efficient act was to land ASAP and elected not to execute this checklist while on an accelerated final approach with no smoke present in the cabin. No FA or passenger needed medical attention. We never smelled smoke or a burning smell in the cockpit.Post flight maintenance found a bad light ballast in the vicinity of rows 10-13.  The ISFD failure and FMS anomaly were apparently unrelated but had occurred rather simultaneously.With more time, say if we'd been at altitude, the Smoke, Fire, Fumes checklist could have isolated the problem and may not have led to the interpretation of a more serious situation possibly developing.				B737 Captain reported a cockpit display failure with an unrelated simultaneous cabin lighting ballast failure resulting in a precautionary landing due to suspected electrical fire.	
1740564	202004	0001-0600	ZZZ.Tower	US				2500								Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Final Approach; Initial Approach	Direct; Visual Approach	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Workload; Other / Unknown			1740564												Deviation - Speed All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Exited Penetrated Airspace; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	The weather was VFR with rain directly over the field and and the winds were favoring RW 25. I elected to practice the VNAV RW 25 and requested radar vector for this approach. I planed for this and briefed the RW 25 RNAV approach before the decent started. I was cleared to 2500' and leveled at 2500'. I was configured with flaps 5 and speed was set. I was given a heading of 270 degrees to intercept the final approach course about 6 miles from the (FAF) ZZZZZ and cleared for the RNAV 25. As the autopilot was intercepting the inbound heading of 249 degrees we experienced a 15 knot loss of indicated airspeed and moderate turbulence. I immediately disconnected the autopilot and the auto throttles and simultaneously rolled wings level and increased the throttles. I pitched up when I was confident that the airspeed was recovered to a safe speed. I asked the FO to inform the approach controller that we aborted the approach. We maintained the 260 degree heading and climbed up to 4000'. We flew out for approximately 10 miles. After 10 minutes we received radar vectors for a right base and a visual approach without incident.The wind sheer was much worse than expected and I have never experienced anything like that.I will elect to hold until the strong down drafts of the isolated rain showers dissipate of move out of the area.				B737-800 Captain reported wind shear and loss of air speed on approach.	
1740617	202004	1201-1800						1000		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 97; Flight Crew Type 12000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1740617												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Aircraft	Procedure	Departed ZZZ Runway XX, Flaps 5. Reaching 1,000 ft., I called for flaps 1 and subsequently attempted activation of the Electric Trim switch. Left yoke stab trim did not function. Asked First Officer to try his yoke trim switch. Neither trim switches functioned. Ran QRH procedure for Stabilizer Trim Inoperative. ATC was notified of flight control problem and [priority handling requested]. A large amount of questions were asked during the initial run of the QRH. The QRH gives a condition: Both of these occur:Loss of electric trim through autopilot.Loss of electric trim through the control switches.As we got vectored off the departure procedure and distracted by ATC questions pertaining to the nature of our [situation], I didn't fully hear the initial reading of the condition calling for both of the above. As I had a heavy yoke from the takeoff configuration, I choose not to negative G unload the aircraft and turn on the autopilot. By not turning on the autopilot, I wasn't able to fully comply with the QRH conditions. QRH was continued and Stab Trim Cutout switches were selected to cutout. With the Cutout switches in cutout, the autopilot was not available.During our debrief and review of the QRH procedures it was discovered that the QRH called for loss of both items, loss of autopilot and flight control switches.				B737 Captain reported failure to fully comply with QRH procedure following a pitch trim failure.	
1740733	202004	1801-2400	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						VMC						Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing	Direct						Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection																																																									Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Other / Unknown			1740733	Hangar / Base		Air Carrier	Other / Unknown	Maintenance Powerplant; Maintenance Airframe			Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Maintenance; Party2 Maintenance; Party2 Flight Crew		1740788	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	Other landing	General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Human Factors	Ambiguous	I was the pilot flying during a visual approach to XL in ZZZZ. The approach was stable but below 50' I flared slightly late causing a firmer touchdown. Rollout and taxi-in was uneventful. After entering rest at the hotel the Captain called to inform me that Maintenance was conducting a hard landing inspection. I was copied in on an email shortly thereafter from Maintenance that the discrepancy had been cleared with no damage found. Although legally 90 day landing current due to a recent simulator event, I had not landed the actual aircraft in some time. I feel the landing, while firm, was not unsafe and with more currency in the aircraft this will be an isolated event.		see next narrative		B777 First Officer reported a firm landing. Two maintenance personal reported their procedure for inspecting the B777.	
1740851	202004	1201-1800					0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch																										Pitot-Static System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 24000					1740851	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1740852	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	Captain rejected the takeoff due to differences in airspeed indicators on Captain and First Officer PFD. At '80 kts.' callout (made by First Officer, since Captain was Pilot Flying), Captain PFD indicated 60 kts. Captain cross checked First Officer PFD and saw approaching 90 kts., while Captain saw 65-70 kts. on his PFD. Captain rejected takeoff, flight returned to gate, and Maintenance was advised.Close inspection of Captain's pitot probe (standing on a ladder and shining a flashlight INTO the tube) revealed an insect/bug embedded in the probe.During periods of aircraft non-use of 6-8 [hours] on the ground during the day, and overnight periods, the pitot probes should be covered to prevent bugs and insects from crawling into the warm probes and causing erroneous speed indications.		Takeoff roll was normal, until (as Pilot Monitoring) I called '80 kts.' Captain replied he had 60 kts. After a quick cross check by both of us, we determined we did have an actual airspeed miscompare of 20-25 kts. Captain aborted takeoff. I called Tower and informed them of abort and they directed us to an appropriate taxiway. Upon exit, I did my after landing flow, noted brake temps, and we discussed the situation. Returned to gate and Captain made the appropriate logbook entry and contacted Maintenance.After gate return, a mechanic showed me he had identified the cause of our problem. With the aid of a step ladder and flashlight, he showed me where a dark bug had become lodged in the upper Captain side pitot tube. The bug was removed successfully and after a couple of diagnostic tests and a reset, we departed for an uneventful takeoff and flight. Mechanic mentioned aircraft had been in ZZZ for around 45 hours. It appeared to have sat that length of time with no sensor covers installed. It would be wise to develop a protocol to protect our sensors when a plane is stored for any kind of long time period.		A320 flight crew reported that erroneous airspeed readout resulted in a rejected takeoff.	
1741323	202004	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			12000			VMC			Dawn			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								System Monitor: Indicating and Warning		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 15000					1741323												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related	Aircraft	We noticed several status messages during the flight: Flap/Slat control 1, Flap/Slat position sensors and a few others. We contacted Tech [OPS] and discussed. Decision was made to continue and possible options. As we approached ZZZ we were cleared the Arrival, Visual XXR and cleared to descend to FL 240. As we approached T/D (top of descent) VNAV path engaged and descent began. ATC then cleared us direct ZZZZZ 12000 and comply with altitude restrictions on arrival. I selected direct ZZZZZ with abeam points, executed/LNAV. FMC showed direct ZZZZZ with no abeam points. I then advised my FO (First Officer) that I would activate route 2 rebuild arrival and try again. The result was the same. We decided to use the Fix page and monitor our altitudes. Suddenly, we see red and hear aural warnings of overspeed. The VNAV indicator on the PFD was yellow with a line thru it. I immediately pulled the speed brakes and throttles were at idle. The aircraft speed was reduced. We figured the VNAV failure caused the loss of speed protection. VNAV failed to re-engage until on final approach when it engaged without being selected. After gate arrival our mechanics met the plane. After we explained what occurred the mechanics advised the aircraft has a history of items related to our events. Knowing the history of the aircraft could assist crews. However, multiple failures and their relationship with each other is a challenge for all.				Captain reported multiple aircraft status messages in flight and a failure of the autopilot caused an overspeed condition.	
1741450	202004	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 19385					1741450												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I had to reject the takeoff, below 80 knots, due to the number 2 engine not reaching the target N1 and not producing the requested/required thrust for takeoff. We were planned for a MAX-SPCL, 26K, dry runway, bleeds ON, flaps 25 takeoff on Runway XXR at ZZZ airport.I was the pilot flying this leg. When we were cleared for takeoff we were told cleared for immediate takeoff/no delay, traffic on a 3 or 5 mile final. I took the runway, advanced the throttles to 40% N1. When the engines had stabilized I pressed the TOGA switch and the throttles advanced. I quickly noticed that the number 2 engine N1 had not reached the target N1 but the number 1 engine was already at its requested target. I gave it about another second or two, and realizing it was not producing the required thrust I rejected the takeoff. We were definitely below 80 knots, as the First Officer had not called '80' yet, but I don't recall the exact speed.We cleared the runway and I advised the passengers and flight attendants that we had a minor engine problem that we had to check out and that I would get back to them when I had more information. We reviewed the rejected takeoff procedure, RTO considerations, and the brake cooling schedule. We concluded that no special procedures were needed as we had rejected the takeoff below 80 knots and when we used the brake cooling chart we fell safely in the 'no special procedures required' zone. The First Officer and I then discussed what had just happened. All engine indications at this time were now normal. I felt that maybe because we were rushed due to the landing traffic on short final and that this is a much shorter than normal runway that maybe I rejected too soon and didn't give the engine enough time to 'spool up.'  Maybe we could attempt another takeoff, perform a full static run-up of the engines this time to give the engines the best chance of achieving normal/required thrust and this time we would not be rushed and would be ready/expecting what to look for. The First Officer agreed and he coordinated with the Tower while I made a PA to the passengers and flight attendants explaining briefly what had happened, that all appears normal now, and that we were going to attempt another takeoff but that we would not continue if it wasn't safe.When we were cleared for takeoff the second time the number 2 engine did the same thing and only achieved approximately 85.4 N1 while the target was 99.2 N1. We rejected the takeoff again below 80 knots.We cleared the runway and while the First Officer coordinated for taxiing and parking, I made another PA explaining to the passengers and flight attendants that one of our engines wasn't working properly and that in the interest of safety that we could not continue the takeoff and flight to ZZZ1. An aircraft maintenance logbook (AML) entry was made while also reporting the malfunction to the dispatcher and  maintenance. The number 2 engine was not working properly and not producing the required thrust for takeoff.				Captain reported two rejected take offs caused by the number two engine not reaching target N1 speed.	
1741479	202005	0001-0600	IAH.Airport	TX			0			VMC						Tower IAH	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B IAH																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 8352; Flight Crew Type 6862					1741479	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1741473	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On rotation noticed odor smell from eyeball outlet on Captain side. Dissipated quickly. Check systems and noticed a Right 'HP' valve open on right engine and pack on 'Low'. Could not change pack output. Dispatch and Maintenance notified.		[Report narrative contained no additional information].		A320 flight crew reported fumes emanating from the Captain's side vents that dissipated shortly after takeoff.	
1742098	202005	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0				Rain						Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC	Cruise					N	N																																																											Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1742098	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer				Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1742099	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Dispatch	In-flight		Human Factors	Human Factors	At door closure, ramp agents informed us a fastener was not secure. I notified Mx (Maintenance) and they stated they'd send line Mx out. Line secured the fastener. We then pushed. We received a message airborne that we had left with the aircraft Out of service. I took full responsibility and apologized to dispatch - we simply had not been informed that we had been taken OTS (Out of Service), but I should have assumed so and contacted dispatch. We already had our performance solution so we had no communication or indication that we were OTS.Communication from Mx or dispatch that the aircraft was OTS would have avoided this, but I take full responsibility for not coordinating through Dispatch.		On pushback the ground crew noticed a fastener loose. We called Mx (Maintenance) and they walked out and tightened the fastener. We asked if they wanted it in the book and they said no and that we where good to go. We headed out and after takeoff dispatch sent us a message asking why we took off while out of service. We had no idea we were taken out of service and no message was sent to us saying we were being taken out of service. I think if someone is taking a plane out of service that a message needs to be sent. We had already gotten performance numbers so that fail safe was unusable.		Aircraft departed after it was removed from service due to maintenance work.	
1742337	202005	1201-1800	D01.TRACON	CO		10		6600		Mixed	10		Dusk	5000		TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 1200		Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1742337	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 115; Flight Crew Type 13800		Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1742433	ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Airspace Structure; Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	While descending on the RNAV Arrival, Approach gave us a clearance to descend to 11,000 ft. and airspeed was our discretion. At this point on the approach, it was VFR. We were on a right downwind with the airport and preceding aircraft on a base leg in sight. Approach Control instructed us to slow to 190 kts. and then descend to 7,000 ft. I pulled the speedbrake lever and dialed in 7,000 ft. There was a cloud layer just north of the field, but at this point, both the Captain and I had the other traffic in sight. As we were coming through about 7,500 ft., Approach instructed us to slow to 150 kts., turn to a heading of 040, and descend to 6,000 ft. This all happened very quickly and the expectation bias was that we were going to keep the approach tight with the other aircraft and follow them in. As the other aircraft turned base to final, they lost sight of the runway because of the cloud layer just north of the field. Approach asked us if we still had the traffic aircraft in sight. At that point. the other aircraft was well inside the cloud layer so we did not. Approach gave us instructions to turn to a heading of 140 to intercept the ILS course. As we were coming through about 6,600 ft. Approach told us to maintain 7,000 ft. and said that the MVA(Minimum Vectoring Altitude) for that sector was 7,000 ft. We leveled off and climbed back to 7,000 ft., and intercepted the ILS course on the 140 heading.		Descending on right base and cleared to 7,000 ft. ATC asked if we had the preceding B737 from north in sight. There were clouds in his vicinity, but I did not anticipate he would need to descend into clouds. Our flight path for base, dogleg, and final was clear of clouds with runway in sight. When asked, I told ATC we had preceding aircraft and runway in sight. We were cleared to follow the preceding traffic for a Visual Approach. We both understood clearance to 6,000 ft. However I was unable to query because the preceding traffic then began communication with approach that he was temporarily IMC and there were several communications regarding the tops and bottoms of the clouds.During this, the Pilot Flying was continuing his descent to 6,000 ft. ATC asked if we had traffic in sight. We had lost it behind clouds but the runway was still in sight for us. We were at 6,600 ft. descending when ATC then asked us to climb back to 7,000 ft. to his MVA (Minimum Vectoring Altitude) and went ahead and gave us a vector to intercept the ILS. We had runway in sight, but I suspect he was preemptively providing ILS in case we also became IMC. We may very well have missed the correct altitude clearance thinking we were cleared to 6,000 ft. vice 7,000 ft. or ATC may have just preemptively wanted us back up to MVA. I'm not sure at this point. Also, there was never any traffic or spacing issues during his event as the closest TCAS ever showed us to leading traffic was approximately 5.5 miles.		B737 flight crew reported after descending to the altitude assigned by ATC, they were told to climb back to their previous altitude because they were below the Minimum Vectoring Altitude.	
1742865	202005	1201-1800	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			50			VMC							Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 25; Flight Crew Type 2182					1742865												Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather	Weather	On takeoff roll above 100 kts, I noticed what I thought was a flock of small white birds on the departure end of the runway. After VR and the 'Rotate' call, I realized in the rotation that it was not birds, but a dust devil, tornado type rotation of trash and papers. It was quite large, just left of centerline, but covering most of the runway width, and spanning well above our altitude. The winds seemed to be quite intense, judging by the speed of the trash whirling around. As the airplane began to lift off, I noticed a vibration on the airplane, possibly a buffet, and the airplane did not seem to be climbing as expected. I began to reach for the thrust levers but was forced back to the yoke when we hit the dust devil and the airplane aggressively rolled to the left. The captain and I both applied aggressive roll input in the opposite direction. The aircraft was slow to respond, but eventually did and returned to level flight. I did not have time to look at the instruments and notate the roll angle or airspeed loss, but there were no aural or tactile alerts from the aircraft. Once through the dust devil, the aircraft climbed normally, and the rest of the flight was uneventful.				B737NG First Officer reported encountering a dust devil shortly after takeoff that resulted in an uncommanded aggressive roll to the left.	
1742917	202005	1801-2400	ZAN.ARTCC	AK				12000		VMC		Poor Lighting	Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								GPWS		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1742917												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	Aircraft	Aircraft	ZZZ - ZZZ1, Aircraft X coming at us from ZZZ1. Aircraft X held at 12,000 ft., good weather, winds favored Runway X. Decided to circle Runway X. GPWS got excited about terrain and announcing PULL UP. We were clear of terrain.				Captain reported erroneous GPWS alerts on approach.	
1743468	202005	1801-2400	AUS.Tower	TX				2500		Marginal	Turbulence					Tower AUS	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class C AUS																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1743468	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Situational Awareness			1743470	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Cleared direct to BALLD fix to intercept the localizer at or above 3000 ft. Once at BALLD we were cleared for the approach. I put in 2100 and started down not realizing that SSHOE was a missing fix and was at 2500 ft. We received a low altitude from Tower and the Captain took [the] controls and climbed to 2500 ft. Double check all fixes are in the box when cleared to outermost fixes on approaches.		We were cleared direct to BALLD to intercept the localizer for [Runway] 35R and maintain 3000 ft. until BALLD. Pilot Flying (PF) set in 2100 ft. in the FCU instead of 2500 ft. for the next fix (SSHOE). Pilot Flying continued down to approximately 2100 ft. prior to SSHOE before being alerted by Tower of the altitude deviation. PF did not correct the altitude deviation immediately. Captain took control of the aircraft in order to climb back up to 2500 ft. as published. Soon thereafter, Tower reported wind shear was being reported on the landing runway. Captain initiated a go-around. During the go-around procedure after the flaps were selected to 'zero' we received a momentary flap over-speed warning. After diverting to ZZZ the flaps were inspected by maintenance indicating no mechanical issues. In addition, the post flight report provided no indication of a flap over-speed ECAM.I should have been more cognitive as to what the PF was selecting in the FCU in comparison to our present trajectory on the approach path along with delegating my duties as the Pilot Monitoring. My attention had been going back and forth between configuring the aircraft for landing, monitoring the appropriate approach path, trying to confirm a safe distance from threatening weather, followed by reports from the Tower of possible wind shear on the landing runway. All of these factors made a go-around almost imminent which resulted in an unnecessary self-induced high workload and chain of events that could have been avoided all together with better planning and communication.		A320 flight crew reported receiving a low altitude alert from AUS Tower on approach to Runway 35R.	
1743773	202005	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000									TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Oxygen System/Crew		X	Design																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Type 5000		Troubleshooting			1743773												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	After starting the #2 engine, the pushback crew noticed a large amount smoke behind the engine which quickly went away.  Because there was very little wind. Some of this smoke entered the aircraft before we finished starting the #1 engine. Other than a slightly odd smell, it seemed to disperse quickly.  I verbalized this to the pushback crew and the First Officer. Additionally, I mentioned to the FO (First Officer) that I would call the flight attendants and make an announcement over the PA. Before taxiing, the F/A's (Flight Attendants) called to report the smell and that there was some possibility of smoke or haze in the cabin. I communicated through the F/O that we would check back with them after we taxied a ways to see if it was improving or getting worse. I also made a PA to the passengers.  At this time I believed the problem was that we had ingested exhaust fumes from the engine start. After taxiing for about four minutes, I checked back with the F/A's and the front two could neither see nor smell anything. The back two said they could still smell something, but not see anything.  They also stated that it was not getting better, but neither was it getting worse. I discussed the situation with the F/O and he felt there was no reason not to go, but thought one more check with the F/A's might help.  Since we just talked to them and they were alright with going, I elected to continue. We executed a normal takeoff and upon approaching the cleanup altitude, the odd smell returned and I stated that this was something that required us to return to ZZZ. Simultaneously, the F/A's also reported the smoke and fumes had returned. I am not sure of the precise order of events, but we donned our oxygen masks, notified ATC, and requested a return to the airport.With our masks on, the F/O and I struggled to communicate with each other and ATC. Basically the design shoots so much oxygen into the mask that it overpowers the microphone inside it.  We both operated the mask correctly and spent some effort and time verifying the settings, but nothing worked. For this reason, I elected to make a relatively quick return to the airfield. In all my experience (43 years), I have never had a problem like this with a mask.  I asked Maintenance Control if I should write both masks up and said no because they frequently get complaints like this. We flew a box pattern and made an uneventful landing with CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) truck giving us a good check out.  After coordinating with the company and flight attendants and briefing the passengers, we taxied to the gate. During the taxi we did not experience anymore smoke and fumes. Maintenance technicians checked the aircraft out, informed us that the 'B' system hydraulic system was over serviced and had entered in the ventilation system where it produced the smoke and fumes. Continued flying would not have improved our situation, so I was glad we made a prompt return. I think the decision to take the aircraft flying was not good in retrospect, but all of us thought there was no reason, at the time, to not go flying.  I have had similar situations just like this and the problem went away once there was sufficient air moving through the cabin. Looking back, even though there was contradictory inputs from the F/A's, there was still enough to warrant a return to gate. The problems with O2 masks is unacceptable.  Maybe they would have worked better at higher altitudes (we only climbed to 6000 ft), but it made the flight deck workload very high and challenging.				Flight Crew flying 737-800 aircraft encountered smoke and fumes in cabin after takeoff.	
1743967	202005	1201-1800	F11.TRACON	FL				11000		Marginal	Turbulence					TRACON F11	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class B MCO																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure			1743967												Conflict NMAC			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	On transition from Center to MCO approach, we were given a clearance to cross BAIRN int at FL110 on the BAIRN4 arrival. We had just switched to MCO approach while we were leveling at the assigned fix at the assigned altitude. As soon as we checked in, MCO approach alerts us to VFR traffic at twelve o'clock and four miles at FL105 and to climb and maintain FL120. In the seconds between the notification and execution of the order, I simultaneously scanned the ND and peered out the windscreen to visualize traffic while reaching to dial in the newly assigned altitude. As I pulled FL120 to command Open Climb, we got the RA to climb. We performed the memory items and executed. The last I saw the red traffic indicated by TCAS, it was 400 ft. below us. There is no reason this should have occurred. MIA and MCO controllers should have coordinated and issued an altered clearance well ahead of time to prevent the close incursion. This aircraft didn't just appear at the altitude, he was mode C equipped and the whole situation could have been prevented had the controllers done their jobs. Instead, and aircraft carrying approximately 140 souls onboard was forced to perform an evasive maneuver to avoid collision. Suggestions: ATC needs to pay attention 100% of the time and anticipate where aircraft are and where aircraft will soon be. I'm actually at a loss as to prevention. ATC is usually very good, especially MIA and MCO. But one moment of distraction by a controller can be catastrophic.				A320 Captain reported an NMAC event under ATC control during arrival.	
1745786	202006	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1745786	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1745782	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	After being cleared to descend VIA the arrival into ZZZ from FL 330, the #1 engine suffered a flameout , We heard a pop and the aircraft yawed , I observed the ENGINE FAIL alert on the upper center display. About 15-20 seconds later the engine initiated a successful auto relight. The engine operated normally for the remainder of the flight. I requested priority handling with ATC (Air Traffic Control). I was concerned that the engine might eventually fail and elected to divert into ZZZ1. We decided to land with Flaps 15 for performance considerations should the engine fail on final approach. We also elected to hold for a couple of circuits to get setup for the approach into ZZZ1 and to get our before landing checklist completed. Passengers and flight attendants were briefed during the hold. We told ATC that we would be stopping on the runway so that ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting) could inspect our #1 engine before we continued to taxi to our gate. During the runway inspection aircraft maintenance conducted a visual inspection of our #1 engine while at idle and recommended we shut it down due to abnormal surging or pulsation. Taxi to gate 12 was uneventful.		During cruise/right at top of descent, as the thrust levers retarded, the #1 engine flamed out. As the checklist was being consulted, the engine relighted and continued to operate as if normal. The captain advised ATC and changed our destination to ZZZ. We held over the CAMRN intersection to complete checklist items, coordinate the diversion, and brief the approach. We landed in ZZZ without further incident.There was an entry in the aircraft logbook that the aircraft may have had a 'foam ingestion event' approximately eight or nine days earlier.Since the cause is not yet determined, I can only speculate that the engine failure may have been related to the described FOD (Foreign Object Damage) ingestion.		B737 Flight Crew encountered an un-commanded inflight engine shutdown that resulted in a diversion.	
1746018	202006	1801-2400	PIT.Tower	PA		3	500			Marginal	Windshear; Turbulence; Thunderstorm		Night			Tower PIT	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class B PIT																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Situational Awareness; Distraction; Time Pressure			1746018												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Weather; Company Policy; Human Factors	Weather	Dispatch contacted us while on the ground to move up our push time 15 minutes earlier than planned, due to thunderstorm activity moving toward our destination. We understood this change would enable us to land prior to the thunderstorm being a factor. While en route we asked Dispatch for an update on the weather, while monitoring real time weather from WSI, and discussing the weather with ATC. ATC reported that the front line of storms were 15 miles from the field moving at 15 miles an hour, with Tornado Warnings on the west side. We had 35 minutes left en route and discussed going to the west side and hold or east side. The thunderstorm was going to be very close, but indications were that we would be able to land ten minutes before the storm hit.Dispatch suggested we deviate west of the weather and hold to wait for the storm to push through. We discussed all options, agreed on the plan of action. Our real time assessment was that we were going to be able to arrive prior to the storm, so we coordinated with ATC to proceed direct to the FAF (Final Approach Fix) bypassing the planned RNAV arrival which was already being affected by the weather. Approach and Tower kept us apprised of the weather being very close to the field, so I elected to keep our speed up as much as possible and as short a final as possible. We coordinated with Tower to land on a Runway 32 to expedite our landing but tail winds were gusting at 15 kts., so we continued to a different runway. We called visual with the field and were subsequently cleared for the visual approach.We arrived at the FAF high and fast, and as we turned final Tower informed us that it had just started raining, winds were gusting above 50 knots, and that there was a Windshear Advisory for the departure end. We could see heavy thunderstorm returns on the weather radar at the departure end of our landing runway and I determined for safety reasons that a go-around into the thunderstorm no longer was an option. Due to our high energy state and short final, the aircraft was not able to stabilize the approach at 1000 ft. Also, due to our high airspeed we were not able to configure with planned 30 flaps, but landed with 25 flaps. At approximately 1,000 ft, due to the gusty winds, we momentarily exceeded the 25 flap limiting speed by 15 kts. (Phase 1 Maintenance Inspection within 100 hours), and around 500 ft., we received an audible 'Terrain Warning' due to our high sink rate. I arrested the sink rate and landed safely within the Touchdown Zone in heavy rain and gusty winds. We exited the runway at midfield and uneventfully taxied to park.				B737 Captain reported landing after an unstable approach due to thunderstorms and wind shear.	
1747322	202006	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		10		37000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Type 10000		Workload			1747322	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 84; Flight Crew Type 1751		Workload			1747332	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Physical Injury / Incapacitation	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon descent the aircraft pressurization system failed, leading to a loss of cabin pressure.  Until point of malfunction aircraft operated with no observed anomalies or deficiencies. Flight was smooth and routine until shortly after reaching Top of Descent and leaving FL380. Descending from FL380 to FL300 Auto Fail light illuminated on pressurization panel. Estimate the illumination of the Auto Fail light occurred around FL370. First Officer acting as Pilot Monitoring located appropriate checklist in QRH and we commenced working through the items contained therein. Completed QRH 2.4. The Auto Fail light extinguished and we continued normal operation with panel now set to ALTN mode. Shortly thereafter there was erratic behavior in the differential pressure and cabin altitude gauge. The changes were noticeable in ears as the pressure/cabin altitude changed quickly in either direction. Scanned overhead panel and noticed the ALTN light no longer illuminated. Verbalized to First Officer that we should put our masks on. At this point estimate the aircraft was between FL360 and FL300 which was the initial floor of our assigned descent from ATC.  We both donned masks and within moments the Cabin Altitude light illuminated with corresponding warning horn and a very noticeable drop in pressure. Called for and First Officer immediately secured and began with the Cabin Altitude Warning QRC. PASS OXY ON light illuminated as well indicating Cabin Oxygen masks had deployed. Accomplished Cabin Altitude Warning QRC. Cabin altitude spiked but can't recall to what altitude. At one point, cabin descended rapidly and diff pressure climbed to redline on gauge.Outflow valve seemed to be uncontrollable, which made the onboard pressurization issue uncontrollable.  Coordinated with ATC for lower altitude and maintained an expedited descent that was essentially unbroken until landing. At completion of QRC we were directed to accomplish QRH 2.1. Used 2.1 through entire descent and uneventful landing.  During descent while coordinating with cabin crew, flight attendants informed flight deck crew that one passenger briefly lost consciousness but was awake again and being attended to by cabin crew. Notified ATC of need for EMS to meet aircraft at gate upon arrival. ATC facilitated request and local EMS met aircraft and evaluated passenger.  Upon arrival coordinated with Dispatch, both local Company Maintenance and Maintenance Control and Chief Pilot, to ensure aircraft abnormalities were properly documented in logbook, crew was taken care of and all other pertinent issues had been addressed.  I think this was an anomaly and part of flying aircraft as a business. Things break. Just glad it turned out the way it did. As far as self evaluation goes, I did forget a few items that bug me. One, I should've let the First Officer handle communication with the cabin as I ended up unnecessarily task loading myself. Also, I forgot to send an ACARs to Dispatch or radio them letting them know about the situation. We were pretty busy but no excuse. I'm pretty sure ATC notified Company if only just the local ops folks. The checklist was handled correctly but as ALTN system failed and we were directed to go to manual. The cabin pressure spiked and dropped several times and then failed. That was certainly an enormous distraction and it took us a minute to get masks on and fully reengaged with the checklist. Feel like I could've done a better job leading in those moments. Will say that I was very happily surprised by how much our training kicked in, when the event got bad. Kudos to the training folks!		A routine flight changed as we began our initial descent. Aircraft pressurization began to fail which led to the loss of cabin pressurization. Between FL380 and FL360 the Auto Fail light illuminated, along with corresponding dashes in the FLT ALT window and LAND ALT window on the landing altitude selector panel. I was the PM for this leg, and as such I completed the QRH 2.4 Auto Fail Checklist. This drove the pressurization mode selector to be placed in the ALT mode. This did extinguish the Auto Fail light and temporarily allowed for control of the outflow valve. At this point the CPT and I felt pressure changes in our heads and noticed somewhat erratic oscillations in both the cabin differential and cabin altitude. The Captain made the decision to don the O2 masks at this point. I referred back to the QRH 2.4 Checklist as the cabin altitude was now not controllable. The checklist drove us to selecting the manual mode on the Pressurization Mode Selector. The Captain communicated with the cabin crew to, as to their status and the status of the passengers. We were approaching FL300 at this point and communicated with ATC that we needed lower, due to a cabin pressurization problem.Very shortly after donning our O2 masks the CAB ALT light illuminated along with the corresponding warning horn. Captain called for QRC Cabin Altitude Warning Checklist. We were granted lower by ATC as well as handoff to APP Control. Thanks to both ATC and Approach and Tower, we were able to basically keep our descent going all the way down from cruise to 6000', and subsequent visual approach. The passenger O2 masks deployed automatically as we worked through the QRC Checklist. The Captain, again, checked in with the Cabin Crew to verify O2 masks had been deployed as well as their wellbeing. We learned here that a female passenger had fallen unconscious temporarily. She regained consciousness as we descended. As I continued to monitor the cabin altitude and manipulate the outflow valve the CPT let APP know we needed EMS standing by for her. At the completion of the QRC we continued on with 2.1 of the QRH and stayed with this checklist for the duration of the flight. The cabin altitude depressurized, as necessary, as we descended below 10,000 feet. The approach and landing were uneventful. I contacted Operations taxiing in to the gate and verified with them our request for EMS to meet us. They had not received word about the Passenger needing assistance. I fully thought I had contacted them airborne. This must have been missed on my part in all the commotion. Nonetheless, EMS showed up within a few minutes of setting the parking brake. The Captain proceeded to contact the Chief Pilot, Maintenance, and Dispatch to proceed with the necessary paperwork and communications. The things that I see could have been improved upon are communications and understanding task loading. Verifying each of our required tasks were accomplished could have been better. As these events unfolded rapidly, it definitely emphasized the importance of keeping a good pace, usually a slower pace, as tasks have the potential to be compounded.		B737-800 flight crew reported a normal landing following the loss of pressurization at top of descent.	
1747324	202006	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Pneumatic System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 2286		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1747324												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Passenger; Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We pushed back off the gate and started the engines. I placed the right pack in high because it was a little warm in the cabin and planned to place it in the normal position, which I did before takeoff. Right as we were about to taxi, I noticed an odor. To me it smelled a bit like a baby diaper and I thought it may have been a smell from the lav, and didn't say anything about it. We taxied out and I don't really remember smelling it. On takeoff I noticed the smell getting a little stronger but I just figured it was due to the increased bleed air feeding the packs. As we started our climb all indications were normal. At no time did I think the smell was overpowering. Somewhere around 9000', the Captain said, 'Hey they're getting smoke in the back'. I didn't have my service interphone on, so I assumed he must have been listening in. Then the flight attendants called and the Captain asked me to answer it. He said he had the aircraft. They told me they were getting a smell of smoke pretty strong in the cabin, but no visible smoke. I relayed this information to the Captain and he decided to notify ATC and return to the field. ATC gave us a left turn and a descent, but I don't recall the altitude. We donned our O2 masks and started the QRC. At this point, the flight attendants called back again and had a commuting Captain on the line. He said that it smelled like a recirc fan possibly going bad, but not a musty smell like one of the flight attendants had suggested. After relaying this info to the Captain I noticed all the automation was now off and we were is the left turn back to ZZZ. I suggested turning back on the automation and gave him autothrottles, Level Change and LNAV. We continued running the QRH in between talking to the flight attendants. I asked what runway we were going to and the Captain said XXR. I pulled up the chart and set myself up. At this point we were being vectored and I was cognizant of the fact that no PA had been made. I quickly told the passengers that due to the odor in the cabin we were returning to ZZZ and would be on the ground shortly. We landed and CFR (Fire Department) was waiting. The Captain said something about hot brakes so I quickly ran the data to see if it called for the brake cooling program to be run in the ACARS. I made sure to use our current fuel load and not the default destination fuel. We were not overweight so it was not required. CFR checked the outside of the aircraft including brakes and said all appeared normal. We elected to take off our masks at this point since there was no visible smoke or odor I could smell when I pulled my mask away from my face. We taxied to the gate uneventfully and deplaned normally through the forward passenger door.Preventative Measures: In the future, any time I smell anything remotely out of the ordinary, I will make sure to verbalize it. The Captain said he had thought it smelled like a burnt bagel. If we had talked about this before taking off, maybe we would have decided to go back to the gate or at least see if the flight attendants were smelling it in the cabin. Once in the air, it becomes a much bigger problem, due to the time constraint of wanting to do everything right but also just get the aircraft back on the ground, in case the problem gets worse. The oxygen mask adds a serious level of difficulty to communication. The PTT switch on the yoke is probably the best to use, versus the intercom switch on the audio panel.				B737-700 First Officer reported a return to the departure airport due to a fumes event during initial climb.	
1747363	202006	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				36000					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic System Pump		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1747363	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1747501	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately ten minutes after reaching cruise altitude FL360 we saw the master caution flicker. No light on recall. 2 minutes after that we had a steady master caution with hyd (hydraulic) light on recall. At that point we determined that it was System B through illumination of the low pressure lights as well as falling system B pressure on Hydraulic page. We [requested priority handling] with Center and requested vectors back to ZZZ. Set up for the ILS XX direct [the initial fix] and the CA (Captain) went through the QRH as we headed back. Uneventful flaps 15 landing, greeted by Fire rescue and they followed us to [the gate].		The aircraft had a loss of system B hydraulic pressure at cruise altitude FL 360. System quantity and pressure showed 0. We were approximately 75 miles west of ZZZ1 on filed route. CA (Captain) was pilot monitoring and FO (First Officer) was pilot flying. We requested priority handling and returned to ZZZ while completing appropriate checklists. Aircraft landed and stopped on RWY XX. After a safety check from ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting) we taxied back to gate with a fire truck escort as a safety measure.		B737-800 flight crew reported loss of hydraulic system during cruise.	
1748317	202006		ZZZ.Airport	US			0			IMC	Rain; Thunderstorm					Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		3	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								FADEC / TCC		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 43; Flight Crew Total 7964; Flight Crew Type 5296		Workload; Troubleshooting			1748317	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Other / Unknown	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload			1748328	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Aircraft was actually [Tail Number X], not [Tail Number Y]. It was grounded for more than 72 hours prior to our flight. Experienced significant delay (about 3.5 hours) due to thunder storm in terminal area. On pushback we got the EEC ALTN lights. Prior to taxi, with parking brake set CA (Captain) called [Dispatch] for patch to [Maintenance Control] ENG. Spoke to [Maintenance] about problem. Noted CA reset EECs, with ALTN extinguished. Inquired if there was any issue with continuing. Maintenance inquired about CDS flags or other cautions. None observed, OK to continue per [Maintenance]. Normal taxi out and initial takeoff. A/T disengaged almost simultaneously with the airspeed on FO (First Officer) reaching 100 knots (about 60 knots on CA display who was PF [Pilot Flying]). After startle, then quick cross check FO called out 100 knots and that there was a problem with airspeed indications ... noting his were normal. Transfer of PF duties prior to V1 ... normal rotation and takeoff by FO. Flew out on departure and got the aircraft cleaned up and safely away from the ground. Continued climb out to get out of MOD TURB in area due to TS to allow for troubleshooting. Informed ATC of the problem and asked to stop at FL200. Overspeed clacker started and we elected to stop the climb and initiate descent to 10,000 feet to continue troubleshooting. CA as PNF (Pilot Not Flying) was distracted by the EEC issue and slow to realize the Airspeed Unreliable situation. FO and [the jumpseater] did great job in keeping ACFT in safe envelope. Turbulence and distractions from warnings and annunciations contributed to PNF not completing all QRH checklists. Hand flown ILS to VIS runway XXL after speaking with flight attendants, passengers, and ACARS message to [Dispatch] of return and EMERG. Uneventful, safe, under max weight landing on RWY XXL. Taxi to gate followed by crash and fire. CA spoke to [Operations Management] on cell at gate after completion of parking checklist. ELB entry made. Spoke to local [Maintenance] in cockpit to try to assist in troubleshooting prior to aircraft swap to next gate for continuation of flight.CA supported by a superb FO yesterday (and [the jumpseater] who we brought into help). Good CRM and communication between crew. CA needs to do a better job separating PF / PNF to allow for more focused approach to identification of the problem and completion of all appropriate checklists. Spoke to [Union] member who noted that it seemed to be a good opportunity for learning, and I agree.		I was sitting in the jumpseat of Aircraft X on this particular day. ZZZ was experiencing very bad thunderstorms that morning. It was a [morning] scheduled departure that finally pushed back about [afternoon] local time. As we pushed back and started both engines I observed the crew doing their after start check. Running through their flow they received a Master Caution and it was in reference to the EEC's, both were lit Amber (Fault). The Captain reached up and reset them and the lights went out. He then called dispatch and [Maintenance Control] since we were off of the gate. He explained to [Maintenance Control] what happened and what he did. I looked at the FO (First Officer) and I was not quite sure if he was ok with that or not. [Maintenance Control] seemed to agree with the fix and we started to taxi out. Everything went well on the taxi out. We lined up on runway XYL received the takeoff clearance and then off we went. It was the Capt's leg. The next set of events I was a little unsure for a few seconds of what was taking place because both pilots had on Bose headsets so I could not hear their conversation clearly as their voices were not loud enough for me. This is a guess, but somewhere around 100kts I witnessed a transfer of controls and I saw a Master Caution light up. I looked straight up and I saw the EEC's both amber again. I then looked back at the flight controls and I figured out why they exchanged flight controls, I saw the IAS Disagree on the FO PFD. Going back to the 100kts, I am guessing it was around that speed because we were on the runway for a while before the V1 and Rotate call. Once airborne both pilots performed the PF and PNF (Pilot Flying and Pilot Not Flying) duties according to procedures. When I saw there was a break I asked the Capt to please hand me his QRH so I can at least help them get started on the problem. I looked up IAS Disagree and that led me to the Airspeed Unreliable checklist in the QRH. I turned to the table of content pages for Airpeed Unreliable 737-800 and handed the book to the Capt. Honestly I did not realize that was not the beginning of that checklist, I saw 737-800 under Airspeed Unreliable and I just went to 737-800 Airspeed Unreliable. It led me to the climb charts for the non-normal. I handed the QRH to the Capt. and he looked through the checklist. He read the tables and then decided to run the EEC checklist. Again he reached up and reset the EEC's I then stated and the FO backed me up, the EEC's are not your problem right now it's your Airspeed. He kept insisting that the EEC's needed to be handled first.During this time the FO was hand flying and getting bumped around pretty bad by the weather. We were still climbing out and then the Capt decided to [advise ATC] and return back to ZZZ. I believe we were at 13,500 climbing to 16,000. I then noticed on the FO PFD Altitude Disagree amber message. I was then fixated on assisting the FO with any airspeed deviations and altitude issues. I did see the FO had a really good handle on cross checking his PFD with the STBY PFD. He was doing an excellent job with everything. As we started a descent back down to 10,000 ft the Capt. Airspeed was doing like 330kts plus giving us a clacker and I think stick shaker. It was because of the erroneous info coming from the Capt side. Between 12,000 and 10,000 ft the IAS Disagree went away and the Altitude Disagree went away. Once we leveled off at 10,000 ft the Capt set the airplane up for arrival back into ZZZ and did the whole briefing for the FO, contacted OPS. During this time I [advise] the FA's (Flight Attendants) and spoke to the passengers. At this time in my mind I was wondering why there was no memory item in the checklist. However I later realized during debrief with the FO when I went to 737-800 that was not at the beginning of the checklist. We then started our descent and headed back for ZZZ and the IAS Disagree came back. As the FO asked to be configuredthe Capt. Airspeed went to 45kts again giving him the clacker and the shaker then I remember the airplane said either 'Too Low' or 'Airspeed' I can't remember which one. Again I couldn't hear all communications between the pilots because they had the BOSE headsets but I did hear the FO say something about running the QRC. The Capt. hesitated for a second but then ran through the Airspeed Unreliable QRC. He read through it and I couldn't see it clearly to back him up. As the clacker was going off the Capt turned his attention back to the PF. Then finally I said, 'Flight Director's need to be off then on to flying pilot' at the same time the Capt was reaching up on the MCP to turn them off I assume. Once that happened and the Master was back on the FO side then things calmed down. The FO landed the aircraft on XXL, again doing a superb job, and the Capt took the aircraft about 100kts and barked orders to tell the Fire Trucks no assistance needed and to tell the tower what gate they were going to. Then he asked me to tell the Pax 'Remain Seated Remain Seated' as we pulled off of the runway. We got to the gate and we did a small debrief but then the Capt contacted the {Operations Management]. The FO thought he forgot something on his initial walk around so he went back out for a postflight walk around just to double check. Fire Dept came to the Aircraft to check on the crew and then I exited. Once we swapped airplanes and was on our way. Overhearing what I could of the crews conversation, they were continuing the debrief they had on the last airplane after I walked away. To me it sounded as if the FO was questioning the Capt of why they did not abort the takeoff as they were both referencing the FOM and what constitutes an Abort. The Capt stated he only had a split second to make a decision of a highspeed abort and felt that the situation was under control so it was better to take the aircraft airborne because highspeed aborts can be dangerous. The FO was not too happy with that answer and they discussed this more. The FO then reviewed the EEC checklist and in the checklist it makes mention of messages on the Control Display Panel. The FO says that alone should have stopped him from running that checklist. The Capt did not think there were any messages on the Control Display and then I backed the FO up and said well you did have IAS Disagree and Altitude Disagree messages. The Capt said that he did not see the Altitude Disagree message. In the end the Capt did accept responsibility and said that once the plane was under control and the FO was flying that he should have disconnected and managed the situation better and read the checklist more careful. Instead he admitted that he was more concern of the aircraft being flown. They seem to agree on that and the Capt. did say he learned a lesson and will reflect on it. They both discussed about writing the [report] and then we continued on our way uneventful. There are a lot of lessons to be learned here, I am just not sure if the Capt had trust in the FO. Not saying this particular FO but just FO's in general. This could have been handled a lot better had the PNF taken input better and took his time.		B-737 flight crew, including jumpseat pilot, reported an uneventful emergency air return due to a malfunctioning EEC on takeoff.	
1748351	202006	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Direct																									Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 85; Flight Crew Type 2407		Distraction; Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Workload			1748351												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	In cruise, at FL390 got a Fuel Flow EICAS message along with a Thrust Asymmetry Computer EICAS.  Checklist directed an engine shut down due to excessive fuel flow to right engine.  Coordinated with Dispatch and Maintenance Control and with First Officer concurrence, decided to divert ZZZ.  Started descent, performed engine shutdown and notified ATC. Performed a single engine approach to XXR in ZZZ without incident.  Fire and rescue inspected airplane when clear of the runway and found no issues.  Normal taxi in and shutdown.				B777 Captain reported excessive fuel flow in-flight requiring an engine shutdown.	
1748397	202006		ZZZ.Airport	US						Mixed			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors																									Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1748397												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure; Human Factors	Aircraft	Just before calling 100 KTS (around 98) I heard an A/T 'snapping' off followed by red flashing A/T (auto-throttle) light which I canceled. Looked up at my airspeed indicator saw 105 and accelerating (100 KTS called as 105). Looked at CA (Captain's) airspeed indicator and saw it around 68 and hovering. (Not sure if he cross checked his IAS but no acknowledgment was received). Glanced at STBY indicator and saw IAS around 113 and accelerating. Announced AIS Disagree flag to the CA and said I have 'good data on my side - I can take the airplane. Transfer of control was performed. Rotation was performed few knots fast. Airborne at safe altitude CA initiated the EEC (Electronic Engine Control) Alternate Mode of Checklist for some reason (EEC's had switched to alt - same had happened in ZZZ1 after the 2nd engine start and was cleared with Maintenance) although I stated we have an Unreliable Airspeed with IAS Disagree flags. -*50 % of the time Unreliable Altitude flags on the altimeter were displayed also - CA never acknowledged seeing the ALT Disagree flag*- CA wanted to climb to 16,000 feet, which I questioned. (I believe I said 'you are not really thinking about continuing all the way to ZZZ2 with this so why climb higher than needed' and he responded that he wants to get out of the bumps (moderate to severe turbulence was reported in the area due to thunderstorms - we had light turbulence for most of the climbout). We flew in MVFR to VFR mostly. I believe we leveled off at 12,000 feet and then CA notified ATC. EEC checklist was performed but I don't recall CDS (Common Display System) displaying DSPLY SOURCE. I reminded him to pull out the QRC Unreliable Airspeed checklist which was pulled out although FD (Flight Director) switches were omitted by the CA and never turned off (I should have verified this memory item) The CA never read the QRH for the Unreliable Airspeed checklist, but the person on the jumpseat did (not sure if he was delegated for the QRH reading by the CA), omitting the conditions for the checklist and some other items but catching the FD switches verifying the pitch and power settings from the QRH for '800 series. Normal arrival with delayed vectors and landing performed. The flight never exceeded any flight parameters and limitations and stayed in normal flight envelope at all times. During the flight CA airspeed varied from 330 plus activating the high speed clacker to as low as 46 activating the stall shaker making the communication at times difficult. The light turbulence associated with thunderstorms created another distraction.				B737-800 First Officer reported confusion between flight crew after auto-throttle fail on takeoff was followed by air data computer failure causing unreliable airspeed, resulting in an Air Turn Back and Landing	
1750429	202007		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	VFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1750429												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation		Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	At cruise, the altitude warning horn came on. We donned masks and began a descent. Ran the QRC for rapid depressurization and then went to the QRH for bleed trip off light. That resolved the pressurization issue. We attempted to contact Dispatch for fuel info at our lower altitude but ACARS would not send and ARINC had no success. Later I learned Dispatch had received our send, but we did not receive their response. We had 8000 lbs of fuel so diversion was not necessary.We had just come out of an overhang at 380 with anti-ice on. The master caution flickered on with no SAP (System Annunciator Panel) to give us an indication WHY it came on. We both were surprised at the lack of SAP but noted the center fuel pumps depleted of fuel was the likely cause. Shortly thereafter the cabin altitude horn came on. We donned the masks and established communication, which is easier said than done since the masks are terribly distracting with their loud air noises. I requested 10,000 from ATC and the FO (First Officer) descended us. As I ran the QRC the FO scanned for possible causes and discovered both bleed trip off lights were illuminated and we turned to the QRH bleed trip off procedure which resolved the issue and we began to pressurize. I have X hours in this airplane as Captain. I have never touched an AI switch in my entire career. The Captains took care of the [type aircraft] AI switches and [another type of aircraft] had automated anti-ice systems not requiring pilot action at all... I was actually proud of myself for remembering to turn it on when we entered icing conditions! I had not yet selected it off as we emerged from the weather. I am sure glad this did not happen WHILE we were still in the weather. If the SAP would have illuminated we may have had a good chance at resolving the issue before [we requested priority handling from ATC]. We did hit the recall light to see if any SAP would illuminate. None did, leaving us no reason to suspect anything except fuel pumps.The final comment in the QRH procedure for bleed trip off light gave insight to why the bleeds tripped off and pressurization was lost: Flights above approximately 350 with Wing AI on may cause bleeds to trip and pressurization loss. Wow. I am shocked I learn about this limitation of the system as the final comment in a dramatic emergency procedure. Perhaps if the aircraft is not capable of maintaining cabin altitude with Wing AI on at 'approximately 350,' then it should be limited to non-icing conditions above 'approximately 350.' Perhaps a memory limitation could be added to prohibit this.				B737-800 Captain reported encountering an aircraft pressurization problem and having to start a descent.	
1750764	202007	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				2500									Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1750764	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Attendant		1750770	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Bird / Animal			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	Upon initial climbout from ZZZ about 1 minute after takeoff at 2500 feet MSL, the flight deck vent airflow decreased significantly. Engine, pressurization and pack indications were normal. The FO (First Officer) and I noticed that I had adjusted the temperature selectors full cold due to the very humid 100 degree conditions at ZZZ. We both agreed that the air conditioning systems had likely iced up based on previous aircraft experience. The FO selected higher temperatures to melt any ice. About a minute later, airflow returned to normal. At the same time we received an interphone call, which the FO answered, from FA1 (Flight Attendant 1) saying hot air was flowing into the cabin and it smelled like dirty socks. The FO said we turned the temperature up but would now turn it down and to call back if the problem did not get better. Cool air returned to the flight deck and cabin and we continued to ZZZ1 uneventfully. During the post flight walk around at ZZZ1, the FO discovered a bird strike in the right engine with feathers on the bypass fan blades and core compressor blades. During the van ride to the hotel, FA2, FA3, & FA4 said they called FA1 during the hot airflow incident on initial climbout and reported they smelled burned chicken, cooked chicken, and a burned bird strike smell. When FA1 called the flight deck, she said it smelled like dirty socks. My decision to continue to ZZZ1 would have [been] different if I had heard any words like burned, cooked, chicken, or bird strike. The decision of FA1 to not relay the very accurate observations of FA2, FA3, & FA4 to the flight deck was not good and could have allowed serious consequences to develop including engine failure, engine destruction, human injuries or loss of life. As a Captain, I listen very carefully to the observations of flight attendants and consider everything seriously. I suggest flight attendants do the same for each other.		Upon initial climb out from ZZZ about a minute after takeoff about 2,500 ft, the flight deck vent airflow decreased significantly. Engine, pressurization and packs indications were normal. The CA (Captain) and I noticed that he had adjusted the temp. selectors to full cold due to very humid 100 degree conditions at ZZZ. We both agreed that the air conditioning systems had likely iced up based on previous aircraft experience. I turned the selectors at a higher temperatures to melt any ice. About 1 minute later, the airflow returned to normal. At the same time we received a call from the cabin. I answered it and FA1 (Flight Attendant 1) saying the hot air was flowing into the cabin and smelled like dirty socks. Which I repeated 'dirty socks' to make sure that was the smell. I told her we turned the temp up but would now turn it down. Also to call back it the smell or heat didn't get any better. I also called back about 5 min later to check on the smell and temp. The FA's did say the smell was going away or gone and temp was cooling down better. With the cool air returned to the flight deck and cabin, and we continued to ZZZ1 uneventfully. During the post flight at ZZZ1 I discovered a bird strike in the right engine. There was feathers on the bypass fan blades and the core compressor blades. During the van ride to the hotel, FA2, FA3, and FA4 said they called FA1 during the hot airflow incident on the initial climb out and reported they smelled burnt chicken, cooked chicken, and or a burnt bird strike smell. When FA1 called the flight deck she said it smelled like dirty socks. His decision to continue to ZZZ1 would have been different if he had heard any words like burnt, cooked, chicken, or bird strike. The decision of FA1 not relaying the very accurate observations of FA2, FA3 and FA4 to the flight deck was not good and could have allowed to something more serious.		B737-800 flight crew reported communication issues between the flight deck crew and flight attendant crew regarding odor in the cabin.	
1751211	202007	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Night	CLR		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Failed; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1751211												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was pilot flying during a clear night flight going into ZZZ. As we were in a 1000FPM descent on the arrival, we received direct ZZZ. The FMC determined we were high and throttles started moving towards the idle position. As the throttles were in transition to idle I felt a light abnormal vibration. Looking down at the engine indications, I saw N1 and N2 decreasing, followed by ENG FAIL displayed on our EGT for the #1 engine.The Captain obtained the Engine Failure or Shutdown QRH and gave me the radios, as I was already pilot flying. The CA (Captain) notified ATC that we had an engine failure but did not declare an emergency. At this time, our EGT indicator was reaching its max limitation and the CA made the decision to move the start lever to cutoff, prior to reaching this step in our Engine Failure checklist, to try and prevent exceeding our EGT max limitation. Continuing with the Engine Failure Checklist, we reached the engine restart portion, at which point the CA decided to do an In-Flight Restart. I stated that we exceeded the EGT limit and because of that, I questioned if we should restart the engine. The CA felt comfortable proceeding and explained that when restarting, or if at any point, we see the EGT rapidly increasing or approaching its limitation, we would shut the #1 engine down. I agreed with his plan, and ultimately felt we could still maintain a safe operation. The CA proceeded through the In-Flight Start Checklist. The start was successful and indications were normal. At this point, we were still descending and being vectored onto approach. I believe we were on about an 18 mile final. I began to configure early and when bringing throttles up, we felt a light vibration again. We decided to continue the approach in a partial single engine configuration with the left throttle at idle thrust; when the left throttle was at idle, there was no vibration and the engine indication were within parameters. We felt it was safer to keep the engine at idle rather than shutting it down during this phase of flight. When we felt the vibration after the second engine start, we notified ATC to contact ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting) to meet us as a precaution. Previously, the CA had pulled up performance numbers for single engine approach and flaps 15. Additionally, the runway was 12250ft, so we both felt comfortable landing with our configuration. The CA ran the single engine landing checklist and I landed the airplane. After landing, I did my after landing flow, the CA shutdown the #1 engine and we taxied to the gate.Looking back on this event, I would have changed a few things. First, I would have [requested priority handling] as soon as ATC was notified that we lost an engine. Second, rather than restarting the engine after it had exceeded its limitation, I feel we could have left the engine shutdown and proceeded single engine. Third, despite having been very busy, we should have notified the FA's (Flight Attendants) and Dispatch.				First Officer reported an uncommanded inflight engine shutdown, causing a partial single engine landing.	
1751273	202007	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			50						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200					1751273	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1751306	Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Bird / Animal			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon departure from ZZZ at approximately 50 ft. we encountered a flock of birds that damaged the nose and multiple other spots on the aircraft. All indications were normal. After discussion with Dispatch and MX, we elected to continue. After about five minutes the Flight Attendants called reporting a smoky odor in the cabin. We chose a gate return as the safest course of action. We landed uneventfully. Upon arrival, the USDA bird guy informed us that we hit 11-12 birds.		Immediately after takeoff and 'landing gear up' call by PF, the aircraft struck a flock of birds. All systems indications were normal while we assessed any other damage and continued on the SID. We elected to level at 3,000 ft. while further assessed the situation and gathered information from the Cabin Crew. No abnormalities were noted at that point and after conferring with Dispatch and Maintenance Control, we decided to continue. In a follow up with the Cabin Crew shortly thereafter, we decided to make an air return to ZZZ as the Flight Attendants noted odor in the cabin. We reversed course and landed in ZZZ without further incident.		B737-800 fight crew reported the flight attendants notified them of a smoky odor in the cabin following an encounter with a flock of birds during takeoff.  The flight crew elected to return to the departure airport.	
1751275	202007	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US		2	1000						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 115; Flight Crew Type 5700		Confusion; Situational Awareness			1751275												Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Weather; Human Factors	Weather	We passed through 1,000 ft. AGL with a stable approach. Not long below 1,000 ft. AGL, the Captain turned off the autopilot and autothrottles. Soon we began to experience what I thought to just be turbulence from the heat of the morning. Along with the turbulence, the Captain began to say he felt something unusual happening with how the aircraft was flying. He said he felt an unusual rolling tendency. He increased speed slightly to gain more control. I rechecked the configuration of the flaps and gear. I was beginning to feel uncomfortable and also noticed we were becoming high. I suggested a go-around. The Captain immediately called for and executed a go-around. After level off, I confirmed the aircraft configuration again and reviewed the ZFW/landing weight. Then I requested new landing data for flaps 30 and 40. All the data looked correct for a 115,000 pound aircraft. The Captain asked for flaps 40 speeds and selected MAX autobrakes. We extended the downwind some to set up for the approach and make sure we were on the same page. If the Captain felt the same again, we'd go around and probably divert to a longer runway. We were both a bit flustered, so I tried to be very intentional at this point.The second approach was stable at 1,000 ft. AGL, but we noticed a 7-kt. tailwind. Wind was reported calm on the ground. The aircraft flew normally. As we went through 200 ft. AGL, we were slightly high on the glideslope, but the VASI showed on glidepath. Nearing touchdown, I felt the aircraft begin to float. I believe we landed long, but I don't know for sure. After touchdown, with MAX braking from the autobrakes, the Captain forgot to deploy the thrust reversers and I missed the 'Deployed' call out. We cleared the runway on the high speed taxiway.After the go-around, we should have spent more time talking about what had just happened and to calm ourselves. Maybe even get someone on the ground in the loop. Since I wasn't flying, I didn't understand what the Captain felt in the aircraft. We could have been better prepared mentally for the second approach had we communicated a little more before starting it. At that point, I suspect it may have been some unusual turbulence that was felt during the first approach. The use of MAX brakes on the landing was violent and with the previously experienced approach, it was too easy to miss the reversers. Spending more time talking things through may have helped be more focused during landing.				B737 First Officer reported the Captain executed a go-around after experiencing an unusual rolling tendency on final approach.	
1751870	202007	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				41000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1751870												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft	Aircraft	Cruising at 410, FO (First Officer) PF (Pilot Flying), Center gave us a crossing restriction of 270. FO started down at 1000 fpm. We were then given a late-night 'Direct ZZZ' clearance and the thrust levers came back to idle. The #1 engine soon thereafter indicated ENG FAIL which was a bit disconcerting. The EGT for #1 was significantly higher than #2. I grabbed the QRH and said I was planning on running the ENG FAIL checklist while we evaluated the engine indications. The EGT at this point was above 800. I elected to do an immediate shutdown. We verified the engine and went to cutoff. The EGT was just in the red and began falling after cutoff and I continued the ENG FAIL checklist.I conveyed our engine failure condition to ATC and we were switched to Approach and checked in. Based on the normal indications on #1, we talked about a restart. The FO expressed his concern regarding restarting an engine after it had exceeded EGT redline. We ultimately agreed to restart with the understanding that an immediate shutdown may be required. Considering this, I got single-engine landing performance for the [long] Runway XXR in ZZZ. The XB restart went well and the engine appeared to be in a stable idle condition. During the Engine In-Flight Start, step #10 dictates, 'Engine GEN Switch, affected side... ON,' which I did. Considering the APU was already on the bus I realized later that it might've been wise to leave it on the APU versus loading up an already questionable engine.Later during the approach, the FO applied power and the #1 began to shudder. He brought #1 back to idle and continued the intercept and approach with the #2 - #1 remained idle. We worked through the One Engine Inop Landing Checklist. I had not officially [requested priority handling] at this point. I had neglected to do that on my initial engine-out report. The FO reminded me of this and I got it done. Considering the first indication of a problem was during descent, there was not much extra time. I didn't feel like contact with Dispatch would've been beneficial as we were fine on performance and proceeding to our destination.During descent at low power settings the engine failure and restart was transparent to the passengers, according to the FAs (Flight Attendant) later. I did not advise the FAs because of the time crunch and an expectation of a relatively normal landing. In retrospect I definitely should have given them a heads-up if for no other reason than to explain the presence of fire trucks after landing. After a flawless approach and nice landing by the FO - we had briefed no #1 reverse thrust - I taxied off the runway, shut #1 down, and taxied to Gate XX for the subsequent phone calls to Maintenance, FODO (Flight Operations Duty Officer), etc. We both spoke with the FAs about the situation. I have appreciated the follow-up regarding the possible causes of the failure and support from the union and company alike.				B737 Captain reported an inflight engine shutdown that resulted in a precautionary landing.	
1752076	202007	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				41000		IMC			Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 136; Flight Crew Type 15000		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1752076	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Type 75		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1752122	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	Our flight was being vectored to go around convective activity. We were at FL410 going around weather and trying to avoid all thunderstorms. We experienced #1 engine was not producing full power and had to descend to FL380. The static noise was so loud we could not hear ATC. We needed to descend to a lower altitude and went down to FL380. I instructed my First Officer to call ATC on #2 COMM, at that time I took control of the aircraft. We descended to FL380 and were able to talk to ATC. We came out of the weather and the #1 engine was operating normally. We continued with no problems.		While navigating through a weather front with convective activity, aircraft performance degraded due to static air temperature (SAT) change. Flight was planned and initially flown at FL410. While in the middle of frontal passage the SAT increased significantly (-27 at FL380) and FMC Max altitude indications decreased to FL380 with a .73 Max Mach indication. I informed the Captain of this change on several occasions and also asked if he wanted to descend. The aircraft stayed at FL410 until we could hear and see indications of both engines beginning to roll back. At that point I twisted the altitude bug on the MCP to FL380 and stated to the Captain 'descend now'. He initially twisted the altitude select knob back to FL397 and asked me to talk with ATC. I immediately said 'I will get to them when I can, we just need to descend ASAP.' Due to the convective activity, radio static made it impossible to talk with Center anyway. Both start switches were in FLT and both engines stabilized with normal indications fairly quickly after initiating the descent. Communications were re-established with ATC somewhere between FL390-FL400 on VHF.This was my leg to fly. My plan was to deviate significantly around the weather, which would have added approximately 10 minutes to our total fight time. Captain wanted to fly through the front, so at his request he became the Pilot Flying until we stabilized on the other side of this weather. It was clear I was uncomfortable with this decision to navigate through the storm. I should have been more adamant about navigating around this weather and not let him continue flying above our Max operating altitude instead of simply briefing him of our performance limitations.		B737 flight crew reported descending to a lower altitude after experiencing a power loss at FL410 while penetrating weather.	
1752094	202007	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US		45		16000		IMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class B ZZZ																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer									Class B ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 11000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1752094	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1752089	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Undershoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors	Human Factors	Between ZZZZZ and ZZZZZ1 on the arrival at approximately FL 200, we were vectored off the arrival to the left/north and given a descent to 17,000 ft. We were told this was for traffic. The frequency was very busy. After several other calls, we were given, 'Descent to 10,000 ft., no delay through 14,000 ft.' The Controller the made multiple calls to another aircraft. One of the calls was partially blocked with the end being 'direct ZZZZZ1' and another aircraft responding to the call. As we continued our rapid descent between 16,000 ft. and 15,000 ft., TCAS gave a TA (Traffic Advisory) for a converging aircraft below us. We immediately switched from LVL CHG to VERT SPD and slowed our descent to 500 fpm. Shortly thereafter ATC queried as to why we had slowed the descent and if we were direct ZZZZZ1. We responded with the traffic alert and 'negative.' He issued direct and we responded. Once clear we continued the descent 10,000 ft. At no point prior to that did we respond to a clearance to ZZZZZ1. TCAS did not issue an RA (Resolution Advisory) during the advisory process.[Some] preventative measures [could be] keeping us on profile, making at least one aircraft predictable. Multiple vectors, multiple COMMS, multiple aircraft in dynamic flight, and one partially blocked call made for a difficult situation.		[Report narrative contained no additional information].		B737 flight crew reported an uneventful resolution to an airborne conflict due to a communications breakdown between flight crew and ATC during approach.	
1752100	202007	1201-1800	D01.TRACON	CO		5		10000					Daylight			TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb		Class B DEN																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 10000		Situational Awareness			1752100												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing out of Denver, the aircraft failed to level off at KIDNG at or below the 10,000 ft. restriction. LNAV and VNAV were selected and MCP showed LNAV/VNAV SPD. The 10,000 ft. restriction was still in the FMC, it was never deleted. I watched it climb right through 10,000 ft. and continue to climb. I punched off the autopilot and pushed us back down to 10,000'. At that point the flight director was obviously holding 10,000 ft., so I put the autopilot back on and then it started climbing after the KIDNG restriction. We peaked at 10,700 ft. four miles prior to KIDNG. ATC never called us and didn't seem to notice. I didn't write the malfunction up because there were no other problems and that was our third leg in the aircraft. I told the next Captain about the glitch and told him to keep an eye on it.It seems to have been a glitch by the autopilot. I've seen lots of VNAV confusion in the past, but I've never seen it blow through an altitude.				B737 Captain reported the autopilot overshot an assigned altitude during climb.	
1752841	202007	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				6000		VMC	Thunderstorm		Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Autoflight System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 16; Flight Crew Total 588; Flight Crew Type 588					1752841	Company		Air Carrier	Dispatcher	Dispatch Dispatcher						1752855	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climb out from 6,000 ft to 13,000 ft with autopilot lnav vnav engaged. Vnav stopped being engaged. As 15,000 ft selected and confirmed the auto pilot disengaged. 15,000 ft in MCP replaced with 50,000 ft readout and aural warning sounded till touch down. Reset of auto pilot auto throttle and flight director did not work. We requested priority handling from ATC. East heading and 15,000 requested for troubleshoot. Unannounciated checklist and others assessed as well as coordination with company attempted. No response from company so air return to ZZZ vfr requested. Flight attendants briefed, pax briefed, descent checklist accomplished as well as vfr approach and landing into ZZZ accomplished. Taxi to gate and parking accomplished as well.		Aircraft X returned to ZZZ shortly after taking off, I received an automated diversion message from the crew and noticed they had turned back toward ZZZ, I then ask if we are diverting and did not receive anything back from the crew, once on the ground they said they tried to ACARS and call dispatch and I never received any ACARS or calls from the crew besides the automated message that comes through. they said they had to fly back VFR due to losing altitude and heading capabilities, I assumed the communication breakdown was related and never saw them higher than Fl110. once I realized I could not reach them and they were returning to ZZZ I called Dispatch, Ops manager, and ZZZ Ops to see if they had heard anything and no one had. I then saw in the flight feed portion of ops reported the Mode Control Fault.		Air carrier flight crew reported autopilot system failure resulting in an expedited landing.	
1753100	202007	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch	Direct																									Engine		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface; Other / Unknown			1753100												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	Other Takeoff	General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Aircraft; Weather	Human Factors	We were performing a 26k takeoff (no temp derate due to winds of 020 at 5 kts.) from ZZZ. The temp was already warm at 33 or 34 per the ATIS. During the takeoff EGT limits were exceeded on both engines. After shutdown in ZZZ1 the Captain made a logbook entry for the over temp (red boxes remaining on the engine display) and maintenance came to the aircraft.High temp and density altitude in ZZZ, high engine times on this aircraft.Cooler temps, a more favorable wind component may have helped. Runway XX was not an option for us due to its shorter length.				B737 Captain reported due to high temperatures and high density altitude at the origin airport, both engines exceeded EGT limits.	
1753231	202007	0601-1200	LAS.Tower	NV				7000								Tower LAS	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B LAS																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Type 20000		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1753231	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 1015		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1753303	Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	Departing LAS on BOACH 8 from 26R. Climbing out on SID, and maintaining visual separation from preceding aircraft, I was hand flying and deviating upwind of wake. I overshot the 4800 ft. - 7000 ft. restriction prior to ROPPR and was advised of the overshoot by the First Officer several miles prior to ROPPR. Altitude was corrected prior to ROPPR. We were still with Tower at that point. The altitude window was set for FL190 as we were still on a climb via departure. So no audible altitude warning.I could have used CWS (Control Wheel Steering) to avoid the preceding aircraft wake while retaining VNAV for autopilot altitude control.		We were climbing out on the BOACH 8 Departure with the preceding aircraft in sight. Throughout the rotation and climbout, we were picking up the wake turbulence of the aircraft in front of us. The Captain was hand flying and elected to climb faster than normal to stay above their wake. As we were approaching ROPPR I was looking outside at the aircraft in front of us, and missed that we were quickly approaching the altitude restriction at ROPPR. Passing 6800 ft. MSL with a more than 3500 fpm climb, I called the altitude restriction at ROPPR. The Captain leveled the aircraft at 7400 ft. MSL and descended back to the 7000 ft. MSL altitude restriction.The departure and altitude restriction was thoroughly briefed prior to the flight. Other than maintaining my situational awareness, I believe that if we are hand flying and in VNAV we should still set the first hold-down restriction. Once the autopilot is turned on then we can set the top altitude of the climb via. I have been conditioned to listen for the 1000 ft. to go chime. However, I did notice the flight director cueing a level off and the autothrottles reducing the thrust, but by the time my brain registered why this was happening it was not quick enough to arrest the climb rate.		B737 flight crew reported overshooting an altitude restriction climbing out of LAS. A wake vortex encounter reportedly caused some distraction.	
1753247	202007	0601-1200	DEN.Airport	CO						Mixed			Daylight	8000		TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR		GPS; FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach; Descent	Other TBARR ONE & NIIXX ONE; STAR SSKII ONE	Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250		Distraction; Workload; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1753247	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Fatigue; Distraction			1753292	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Human Factors; Airspace Structure	Procedure	Originally inbound on the NIIXX STAR from southeast, DEN changed us to the TBARR 1 arrival that came from the southwest. We already briefed the STAR and approach and were briefing another STAR. Now due to the extreme addition to our route mileage, I had to coordinate with Dispatch about new burn and new Alternates. Then ATC gave us a new STAR the SSKII 1 and gave us the starting way point, which we incorrectly heard or entered making the aircraft turn to the wrong direction, ATC gave us an initial heading until we got the box corrected. The FMC was locked up and it took us a while to get that fixed. The First Officer and I were both in the Orange at that point. As we got closer to DEN, ATC started giving us vectors and kept us high. We had a bad transition from vectors to the approach, we were high and ATC asked if we saw airport to get us to take the visual. Everything was super rush trying to get the aircraft back on profile, we got it all together in the end, but the parameters were outside my comfort zone. I wish we would have slowed things down and got vectored back around for another approach.DEN ATC needs to be aware of what all is involved with multiple changes in a time critical phase of flight.		Arrival that comes in from the southwest with a runway change. We plugged in the new STAR and approach and started setting it all up and briefing it. We also discussed the added fuel burn, and the effect it would have. ATC seemed like they were in complete disarray, and losing the handle on their airspace. Getting closer and closer to the airport, in and out of rain and IMC, we were pulled off the TBARR 1 STAR and changed again to the SSKII 1 STAR.We set it up and briefed it as thoroughly as we could under very intense conditions. We were then pulled off of that arrival, vectored around south of the airport, given yet another runway change and left very high, very close to the airport. We finally landed on 35L and taxied to the gate, feeling like we had been beat up. I did this same trip last week and had the same experience, with the exception that last week it was two STAR changes and three runway changes close-in during the highest workload portion of the flight. DEN Approach really needs to be aware of the threats THEY are creating, and the opportunities for errors that come with all of these last minute changes. I don't think they are aware of what all we have to accomplish every time these changes occur, and how quickly the workload ramps up when they take place so close to the airport. If the ATC facility could somehow be made aware of this and understand we don't just hit a button and keep on tracking, I think it would maybe prevent this from being such a regular occurrence. This would reduce the risks and opportunities for errors, and increase Safety, which is always a good thing.		Flight crew reported receiving three STAR changes and multiple vectors from ATC, consequently increasing the workload to the point that errors were made.	
1753337	202007	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000			Mixed	10		Dusk	3500		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1753337												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Departing Runway XXR during initial climb I realized the Electric Stabilizer trim was inoperative. I was the PF (Pilot Flying) and advised the Captain who was the PM (Pilot Monitoring). We continued the climb at a reduced speed 210 knots and leveled at 10,000 ft. to run the QRH Stabilizer Trim Inoperative Checklist. We advised the Departure Controller and requested vectors to return to ZZZ. Completed the QRH, computed the performance non-normal and brake energy information. The Controller [gave up priority handling] and the fire trucks were standing by during landing. We conducted an uneventful flaps 15 landing to XXR and cleared the runway and taxied to the gate. Maintenance met the aircraft and a discrepancy was entered into the logbook by the Captain.				B737 First Officer reported Electric Stabilizer Trim inoperative.	
1753339	202007	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				12000		Mixed	10		Daylight	12000		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 148; Flight Crew Type 2895		Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Workload; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1753339	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 135; Flight Crew Type 135		Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1753348	Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors; Airspace Structure; Procedure; Weather	Human Factors	We were vectored off the STAR to follow another aircraft that was deviating around weather. We were then given direct ZZZZZ, not on the STAR, to cross at 12,000 ft. then direct ZZZZZ1, on the STAR, and DESCEND VIA the arrival. I noticed the aircraft leveling off at 12,000 ft. We were then given direct to ZZZZZ1 to cross at 12,000 ft. and descend via the Arrival. During the confirmation procedure, I noticed 12,000 ft. on the PFD and 12,000 ft. on the FMS for ZZZZZ1, but I associated the bottom altitude of 11,000 ft. with descend via instruction instead of the current instruction of crossing the fix at 12,000 ft., then descend via.Therefore, I confirmed the FMS for execution. I proceeded to review my STAR Approach Plate to confirm the bottom altitude on the arrival. ATC amended the instruction and cleared us to 11,000 ft. I was under the impression that we were complying with ATC instruction until the Captain queried if we missed a crossing restriction.		We were cleared direct to ZZZZZ and to cross ZZZZZ at 12,000 ft. Then we were cleared to ZZZZZ1 for the remainder of the Arrival. We were cleared to descend via the Arrival. There was weather around the ZZZZZ waypoint and another carrier in front of us wanted to deviate. They gave us a radar vector as well since we were behind them. Additionally, we were cleared to descend to 10,000 ft. We were getting radar vectored back towards ZZZZZ, and then we were cleared direct to cross ZZZZZ1 at 12,000 ft. We put direct to ZZZZZ1 in the FMS and both agreed and executed it. However, we were right at 12,000 ft. when I did that and the VNAV started to take it below the 12,000 ft. that we were cleared at ZZZZZ1, because on the arrival ZZZZZ1 has between 11,000 ft. and 12,000 ft. I tried to stop it but we went 200 ft. low and the Controller saw what happened and then cleared us to just cross ZZZZZ1 at 11,000 ft. The bottom line was we had a lot going on clearance wise and in the FMS and I just didn't quite catch the altitude quickly enough.		B737 reported descending below a crossing restriction during arrival.	
1753340	202007	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch																										Engine Indications		X																																					Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 1500		Human-Machine Interface			1753340												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	Other Takeoff	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	During the takeoff roll at approximately 75 knots, the Master Caution light illuminated. No indication was made on the annunciator panel and it was unclear what caused the Master Caution light to illuminate. The Captain elected to abort the takeoff. After clearing the runway we saw that the number 2 Engine Control annunciator was illuminated. 'Overhead' was never illuminated on the annunciator panel. We taxied back to a gate, called maintenance and swapped aircraft.				B737 First Officer reported Captain aborted takeoff due to Master Caution light illumination.	
1753346	202007	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						Marginal	Windshear; Turbulence		Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Time Pressure			1753346												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Company Policy; Weather	Weather	While on final approach the Captain and I both observed a high cloud layer with a small area of thick Virga underneath it develop directly over the field. Winds were reported gusty, and we were VMC with the runway and airport clearly in sight. A few miles from touchdown we received a warning of 'Go around, windshear ahead.' At that point we executed the published missed approach. It was windy with turbulent air not exceeding moderate chop but we were still VMC with the field in sight. ATC reported they were observing gusty winds with no adverse weather warnings.We accepted close-in vectors for a second approach and stayed within about six miles of the airport. We accepted a Visual Approach on the base turn and joined a five-mile final. On the second approach our radar picked up a small, about two-mile wide light green return over the approach end of the runway. Then ATC reported a Microburst alert with a 40 knot wind change. We immediately performed a second go-around and took a south turn away from this small band of quickly developed weather. At that point our attention quickly turned to fuel. We were forecast to land originally with only 6.5 in gas, and due to a good weather forecast we had no alternate and little extra fuel. The Captain reached out to Dispatch via the Ops frequency while I continued to fly the aircraft and looked up alternate options. We first looked at ZZZ1, which after brief calculations proved too far and had us landing with emergency fuel. I then suggested ZZZ2 as I knew it was close and had operated there at a prior airline. We checked the weather at ZZZ2, which was only around 50 miles away and it was VMC with light winds. However, we were shocked to find that ZZZ2 was not in our FMC database nor was it in our Jepps Plates. We began working with ATC to get information about ZZZ2 and we informed Dispatch to get us performance data for the airport. At that point the small band of weather was observed to have moved off the field to the south. We queried with ATC, who informed us the winds died down a bit and the microburst and windshear warnings were no longer observed. After getting the weather, runway length and localizer frequency for ZZZ2, we determined that would be the safest course of action if a diversion was required. We calculated the burn and determined we had the fuel to do one more approach attempt in ZZZ since the weather improved and then divert to ZZZ2 and land with 4.0 on the gas. We landed in ZZZ without further incident with 5.0 on the fuel. We should have more alternate airport options in our Jepps and FMC. In this case, we didn't have information on the nearest commercially served airport to our destination. I am surprised we don't have the plates for ZZZ2 and that it is not in our FMC database. It also makes me wonder how many close suitable airports to destinations we serve also aren't included. In an unlikely event such as unforecast weather, airport closure, runway closure, etc. the more diversion fields we have information for that are close to the destination the better. It would help immensely if we had approved alternate airports that were the nearest suitable airports to the markets we serve, especially when the nearest airport is a commercial one. This would allow us to better prepare, gather information, save time, get the performance, and set up for an approach. It would be safer. Also, it would be nice if [we] had a list of diversion options along with the distance from the destination airport. This would help in situations like this when fuel is low and quick decisions need to be made.				B737 First Officer reported two missed approaches with lack of information about nearest possible alternate.	
1753351	202007		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 193; Flight Crew Total 16007; Flight Crew Type 225		Time Pressure			1753351												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification			[Oceanic] Flight to ZZZ. At approximately ZZZZZ intersection just past equal time point between [departure] and ZZZ I was informed that a passenger was ill. Flight attendants surveyed passengers and enlisted the help of a registered nurse passenger; I approved release of the primary medical kit and first response kit and an initial assessment was accomplished. Assessment was relayed to me and Medlink patch via SATCOM with dispatch was accomplished. Medlink MD and on scene RN were in agreement that the passenger with previous history of Type 1 diabetes was suffering from diabetic collapse due to lack of insulin beginning with initial flight from [the previous leg] and then on to ZZZ. Blood sugar was measured in excess of 360 with chest pains. Medlink advised that the passenger needed emergency room treatment as soon as possible. We advised Oceanic and they provided present position, now approaching the ADIZ, direct to ZZZ. EMT response was coordinated to meet the aircraft upon arrival. ARTCC and TRACON provided priority handling with radar vectors and runway assignment to the ILS Runway XXL to ZZZ. A normal approach and landing was accomplished, ZZZ tower allowed us to rollout to taxiway [1] for quicker access to arrival gate. Upon arrival at gate the passengers remained seated as the EMTs entered the aircraft and took custody of the ill passenger. Passenger required EMT assistance to leave aircraft. Passengers then normally deplaned. ATC priority handling was direct routings/vectors and preferential runway assignment. They did offer 'speed at our discretion' but no speed FARs were exceeded. Due to time of day and light traffic our impact on other aircraft was minimal. Conducted debrief with full crew to resolve any questions and to ascertain any possible improvements in our own process and CRM. Aircraft maintenance was also debriefed about write ups related to use of medical kit and oxygen bottle/mask.				An Air carrier pilot reported an ill passenger. All coordination and service was accomplished appropriately.	
1753632	202007	1201-1800	BOS.Airport	MA			1000									TRACON A90	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B BOS																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1753632	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1753622	Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Weather	Ambiguous	After configuring for a flaps 30 landing on my hand flown, visual approach to Runway 22L at BOS, we experienced what I believe was a strong gust. This occurred just inside the final approach fix. The indicated airspeed rapidly increased and exceeded the flaps 30 placard speed of 175 kts. (The approach report from ACARS, retrieved upon arrival at the gate, indicated we reached 176 kts). The flap load relief system activated and automatically retracted the flaps to 25. This is the first time I've experienced this aspect of the flap protection system and my first reaction was that I'd failed to properly configure the flaps. The First Officer was quick to analyze what had just happened and when we had slowed sufficiently, we were able to reset the flaps to 30. The final flap setting, however, occurred just below 1,000 ft. The First Officer stated that we were not stable and I initiated a go-around. The First Officer backed me up on ensuring all the steps for the go-around were conducted in the proper sequence. We notified Tower and we were vectored around for another visual approach and landed without incident. The First Officer and I discussed the experience when we got to the gate. Though we don't like to do go-arounds, we were both satisfied that we did the right thing. I wrote up the flap over-speed in the AML and spoke with maintenance. A contributing factor may have been a very light landing weight, approximately 121,000 lbs. I can't praise the First Officer's situational awareness and professionalism enough. He made sure that all procedures were followed correctly and that all checklists were completed. As for me, I'll need to be more vigilant about airspeed control. I'll also reinforce the necessity to be prepared for a go-around on every approach--not just talk about it.		Visual 22L BOS at approximately 1,300 ft. AFL, autopilot off, we encountered an abrupt pocket that altered the pitch of the aircraft; possibly a wake from the preceding A321. As PM I noticed an increase in speed and initially noted 175 kts. (flap 30 limit is 175 kts). My attention was drawn to the airspeed and ensuring the proper attitude and path were maintained. The PF called for flaps 30 which had been previously set and confirmed leading me to believe we had exceeded 176 kts causing the flap load relief. (The Before Landing Checklist had been completed and flaps 30 had been confirmed set.) The flap handle was again verified set to 30. Aircraft was slowed and flaps returned to 30 passing 1,000 ft. AFL. Due to the flap movement and airspeed the approach was not stable and a go-around was performed. The flap report indicated that an overspeed of 176 kts. occurred. Maintenance was informed verbally and with an appropriate AML entry.The decision to execute the Go-Around stems from the culture and training that stress the importance of stabilized approaches.		B737-800 flight crew reported executing a go-around from their approach to BOS after a wind gust or wake encounter caused a momentary overspeed.	
1753981	202008	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				38000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Oxygen System/Pax		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 105; Flight Crew Type 304					1753981												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Security; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft	Procedure	FA (Flight Attendant) identified in flight that the aft lavatories O2 mask doors had the security seal improperly fixed. The seals could have prevented deployment of the O2 masks. The forward lavatory was MEL'd out of service. With approximately 45 minutes flight time, the Captain elected to leave only one aft lavatory open. FO (First Officer) concurred. Maintenance contacted after landing.				First Officer reported that a Flight Attendant discovered the security seals were blocking the O2 panels from opening in the Lavatories.	
1754462	202008	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																										Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Physiological - Other			1754462	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Physiological - Other			1754858	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During boarding a musty dry odor was discovered with the APU Bleed on and the issue was written up in the maintenance logbook and the cabin odor sheet was completed. Parties were then notified (dispatch and the Captain spoke with the ZZZ Chief Pilot), maintenance troubleshooted the issue and ended up deferred PACK 1 with the appropriate MEL. After departure and on initial climb out, the same musty odor returned intermittently. The Captain made contact with the cabin crew to check on if any unusual odors were present in the cabin- which they did not detect but were also wearing masks for coronavirus. As the climb continued, the Captain began to experience physical symptoms consistent with a fume event. The Captain asked the First Officer if he was experiencing any symptoms and he replied he was. At that time both crewmembers donned their oxygen masks using the 100 percent setting and decided that a return to ZZZ was the safest course of action. ATC was advised of an issue with the air conditioning system and request to return to ZZZ. No emergency was declared (aircraft was controllable, requested altitudes were granted and a pretty much straight course was given on return). The supplementary procedures for elimination of odor/fumes was run however since PACK 1 was deferred PACK 2 could not be turned off. Descending through 10,000 PACK 2 was turned off and the aircraft began depressurizing. The AIR PACK 1 and 2 procedure was followed and the RAM Air Valve began to ventilate the aircraft. Because this event happened on initial climb out and the extra fuel (FLT Plan had an alternate of ORD due to WX in DTW) the overweight landing procedure was followed. The Captain landed the aircraft at roughly 153,000 lbs. During taxi in, cockpit windows were opened to help ventilate. After the parking checklist was completed, the fume event and overweight landing were written up in the logbook and the associated cabin odor sheet. After all passengers were deplaned, the flight crew and cabin crew discussed the events that had just happened. The Captain notified the chief pilot and then both the Captain and First Officer contacted Medical and followed their directives.		I have had multiple recent events and am worried about continued exposure to possible dangerous chemicals. It seems maintenance only investigates/correct these issues after they have occurred. Sometimes, maintenance discovers the problem quickly which means they know exactly where to look to find the issue. These common areas that cause fume events (APU, engines, packs) should be inspected at least once a week to prevent a dangerous fume event.Objective cabin and cockpit sensors should be installed fleet wide to test for any dangerous chemicals in the air. The sensors should be able to warn Cabin and Flight Crews of the condition so it can be rectified. It should be set to warn us at levels that are much lower than what is dangerous so any negative effects of an exposure are mitigated.What is the status of the newer/upgraded cabin air filter installation that is supposed to remove these chemicals from the air?Flight crews should be able to proactively turn PACK 1 to OFF on descents to prevent exposure. We can only first be exposed and then run a procedure to hopefully remove the fumes.During boarding with the APU BLEED ON, I noticed an abnormal musty dry odor in the cockpit normally associated with a fume event/air quality event.Flight Attendants (FAs) were asked if they smelled anything unusual and they replied that they didn't.PACK 1 was selected OFF because the odor was present in the cockpit.The odor dissipated.The issue was written up in the maintenance logbook and the cabin odor sheet was filled out.Dispatch and the Dispatch Shift Coordinator (DSC) were notified as well as the ZZZ Chief Pilot.Maintenance secured PACK 1 closed and the item was deferred with the appropriate MEL.The flight left delayed due to the maintenance action.After departure and during initial climb out, the same musty dry odor returned with intermittent frequency and what seemed like reduced intensity.I was trying to determine if this was another fume event related to PACK 2.I called back to the Lead FA to see if they could smell any abnormal odors. The Lead FA reported that she did not smell anything abnormal.I texted Dispatch and notified them that we were having a possible fume event and we could return to ZZZ.As the aircraft continued to climb, I began to experience physical symptoms consistent with a fume event to include: numbness/tingling in the arms, fingers and top of head, lightheadedness, headache, nausea and fogginess. I asked if the First Officer (FO) was experiencing any symptoms and he replied that he was.I decided it was a fume event and we both donned our oxygen masks and used the 100% setting. A quick return to ZZZ was decided as the safest course of action.I took control of the aircraft from the FO, stopped the climb and notified ATC that there was an issue with the air conditioning system and we desired to return to ZZZ.ATC began giving prompt lateral and vertical instructions for a return to ZZZ.No emergency was declared because in my judgement, the situation was under control, the pilots were not compromised while using oxygen masks, the airplane had no control deficiencies. and the crew was already receiving expeditious handling by ATC.I pulled up the 'Supplementary Procedure for Elimination of Odor/Fumes in the Cockpit/Cabin' and began running it with the FO.Cross-cockpit and ATC communication was very difficult while using the oxygen masks.The goal was to eliminate the source of the fumes. PACK 2 would eventually have to be set to OFF, but since PACK 1 was already deactivated and deferred, it would result in the depressurization of the airplane. An emergency high-rate descent was decided against due to the proximity of mountainous terrain, the fact that the Lead FA did not report smelling any abnormal fumes and because it would further lead to an increase in workload while having to run various abnormal procedures while communicating with ATC.After running the relevant itemsin the fume event procedure, it leads you to consider the AIR PACK 1 + 2 FAULT to have a structured way to depressurize and ventilate the aircraft.During all this, the Lead FA noticed that the aircraft was descending and called the cockpit. I notified her that we were having a fume event, we were turning around and descending to ZZZ and to prepare the cabin for landing.I made an announcement to the passengers and notified them of an air conditioning problem and that we were returning to land.The FO assisted with the procedures and got the ATIS and ran a Landing Distance Assessment.At approximately 10,000 feet MSL, PACK 2 was selected OFF and the aircraft began depressurizing.The AIR PACK 1 + 2 procedure was followed and the RAM AIR valve was opened to ventilate the aircraft.We removed our oxygen masks following the aircraft ventilation. The abnormal odor subsided and now communication was much less difficult.The Overweight Landing procedure was followed and the aircraft was around 153,000 lbs upon landing.During taxi-in, the cockpit windows were opened for further ventilation.The fume event and overweight landing were written up in the maintenance logbook. The cabin odor sheet was filled out.After all passengers deplaned, both myself and the FO notified all FAs as to what happened and what actions we took. We notified them that we did not feel well and we were going to call Medical and seek medical attention. I advised them that if they were experiencing any adverse physical symptoms to call Medical and seek treatment. All FAs reported that they were fine.I notified the Chief Pilot about the event.I answered a call from the DSC asking about the event.We were both still experiencing negative physical symptoms and could not continue to operate another flight. Medical was called and we were seen at the Emergency Room for an inhalation injury.		A320 flight crew reported a fumes event during initial climbout resulting in a return to the departure airport.  The flight crew reported health issues and decided not to take the following flight.	
1754607	202008	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		5		6500					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach																															Personal	UAV - Unpiloted Aerial Vehicle		1	Other 107	None	Personal		Cruise																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Type 5398					1754607												Conflict NMAC; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	While descending into ZZZZ, the First Officer saw a silver or white colored drone at approximately 6,500 ft. MSL and within less than 50 ft. from our aircraft. The drone passed off our right side as we were on a vector for arrival. ATC was informed and we relayed the pertinent information.				B737-800 Captain report an NMAC event during approach with a drone within 50 feet from aircraft.	
1754622	202008	0001-0600	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						IMC							Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 43; Flight Crew Total 5255; Flight Crew Type 1304		Fatigue			1754622												No Specific Anomaly Occurred All Types			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors; Company Policy	Company Policy	The issue is what happens [in the late afternoon between flights at an international terminal]. Required to stay on the plane while passengers disembark. About an hour with constant PA announcements. Then we are placed on an inter-terminal bus. Neither clean nor comfortable. This lasted just over 2 hours. We had to change buses once because the AC didn't work on the first bus. (NO CHANCE FOR ANY REST AT ALL!) Allowed back on the plane to perform pre-flight duties. Already on the back side of the clock and put in deplorable conditions without even a facility to use for personal hygiene/bodily functions. (NO REST ROOMS!) Fighting bad weather on the return [flight] made this a very frustrating and possibly dangerous situation. We both felt very fatigued during the return flight. There has to be a better way. Both of us passed out on the ride back to the hotel. (I almost never sleep on a bus.)				Air carrier Captain reported experiencing extreme fatigue on a two-leg international assignment.	
1755094	202007	0001-0600	ZZZ.Tower	US			600			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1755094												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	Normal Takeoff and departure. While enroute and prior to Top of Descent the Captain and I started arrival planning and configuring the aircraft. We were given an RNAV Arrival. We were then cleared to descend via. While in the descent we were instructed to level off at a specified altitude, and given an altimeter setting. We were then cleared to a point further down the arrival, and to descend to a given altitude, setting us up for the Visual Approach. Day VMC conditions with light winds prevailed. Once we had the airfield in sight we were cleared for the Visual Approach. The Captain and I both had the ILS tuned and identified and planned on backing up the Visual Approach with ILS information. After we were cleared we intercepted the Localizer, and eventually the glide slope.The aircraft was completely configured for landing prior to the final approach fix. Stabilized approach criteria were meet prior to 1,000 feet and all looked well. The aircraft was in a position to land and all landing checks completed. Approximately 600 feet AGL we received a GPWS warning. The Captain and I looked at each other and outside. As the aircraft was established on the ILS and there was no visible terrain. However, we elected to comply with the Warning and perform a Go Around. We informed the tower of our missed approach, and received a heading and altitude. While turning to the outbound heading tower switched us to approach control. We were able to clean the aircraft up, and rebuild the approach for another attempt at the ILS. Approach control switched us to another approach frequency, who then turned us base for the Visual Approach. While briefing the approach we were given a turn to intercept the final and cleared for the Visual [Approach]. This time however we were over the final approach fix over 1,000 feet about the charted altitude and unconfigured at the same time the controller switched us to Tower. The Captain made the turn to final and started a descent, and I switched to tower frequency. I then looked at where we were in relation the ILS, and both the Captain and I agreed there was no way we would be able to make and attempt at a stabilized approach from this point.We informed tower that we need to perform another missed approach as we had simply been vectored in too high, too fast, and too close to the airport. Tower again gave us an altitude and a vector to fly. This time however while trying to clean up the aircraft and turn to the heading the approach controller started reading instructions to us, along with a runway change to a parallel runway. The cockpit at this time was simply too busy, and I told the control to 'stand by' that we where trying to clean up and reconfigure the aircraft. Once the task saturation had receded, I called the Approach Controller and asked for his instructions, while telling him we wanted to remain on the previous runway. We also informed him that during a missed approach there is a lot going on inside the aircraft and we needed a moment to coordinate. He gave us vectors for a downwind, and an associated altitude. This time he gave us an altimeter setting that did not match what we had. The Captain and I both looked at each other confused, and questioned the controller. He replied with the same altimeter setting. When we set it, all three altimeters needed the new setting, and the change was about 400 feet. We flew the downwind, and eventually received vectors to intercept the localizer and proceeded on the Visual Approach again backed up by the ILS. This approach and landing were uneventful, and Tower welcomed us to the airport. We taxied to the gate with no further issues.Changes in descent clearances, task saturation on a missed approach, poor vectors and being brought in too high, too fast, and too close on final for a stabilized approach. [We need] clear and concise instructions from ATC, and ATC understanding that a large Airbus is not a Cessna and requires time to get configured.				A320 First Officer reported an unstabilized approach that resulted in erroneous GPWS warnings and a go around.	
1756006	202008	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				25000					Dusk			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Workload; Human-Machine Interface			1756006												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing thru 25,000 ft., engine 2 hydraulic low pressure illuminated. Did QRH [procedure] after checked hydraulic quantity and pressure. Quantity was 8% and pressure was normal 2980. A few minutes passed and then pressure dropped to 0 and elec 1 low pressure light illuminated and FLT (Flight) Control low pressure illuminated. Started the Loss of System B QRH and [requested priority handling] with Center for a return. Talked with dispatch and got rerouted. The QRH went smoothly with plenty of time for alt flap extension and flap 15 landing. Smooth overweight landing. We had smoke coming from the number 2 engine. We shut it down and taxied to our gate and parked. Maintenance inspected the engine and passengers deplaned. After clearing the runway I would of shut down the #2 engine and shut off the right pack to make sure no hydraulic smoke entered the cabin.				B737 Captain reported engine 2 low hydraulic pressure during climb.	
1756780	202008	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Flap/Slat Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100					1756780	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250; Flight Crew Type 2804		Troubleshooting			1756959	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight		Aircraft; Manuals	Manuals	On approach to ZZZ XXL, First Officer called for Landing Checklist. We noticed no illuminated green 'leading edge flaps extended'. We did go-around and got vectors in the area at 3,000 feet while working the problem. I could not find a QRH (Quick Reference Handbook) checklist for the omission of that light. The FO (First Officer) and I exchanged controls. He was also unable to find a QRH checklist for this problem. While being vectored we extended the flaps and could visually see by looking out our windows that the leading edge flaps were indeed extending normally, and that it was an indication problem. The following lights were not illuminating at all. Leading Edge Flap Transit, Leading Edge Flap Extend, and the overhead Leading Edge devices indication lights. After triple checking no checklists would cover our problem, and after we verified the leading edge devices were fully extended, we made a normal flaps 30 uneventful landing. After landing the PSEU (Proximity Switch Electronics Unit) light illuminated.		As the Pilot Flying (PF) I called for flaps 30 and the Before Landing Checklist on the visual approach to XXL into ZZZ. The Captain (Pilot Monitoring) ran the checklist and we discovered that the Leading Edge Flap Extend green light was not illuminated. We executed a go-around to give ourselves more time to assess the situation. At 3,000 feet and after ATC gave us vectors, the Captain proceeded to find the appropriate checklist in the QRH. He could not find any checklist for LE FLAPS EXT green light not illuminated. He then asked if I could look and I transferred aircraft control to the Captain. I looked and didn't find any checklist addressing our exact condition. At that time, I did a push to test on the LE FLAPS EXT (Green light), LE FLAPS TRANSIT (Amber light) and LE devices annunciator panel and all tested good. The Captain called for Flaps 1 and we verified visually by looking out the window that the LE Flaps did in fact deploy and the flap position indicator showed flaps 1, but LE FLAPS EXT (Green light), LE FLAPS TRANSIT (Amber light) and LE devices annunciator panel did not illuminate. I looked through the QRH again to see if we had missed anything and the closest checklist we could think was the LE FLAPS TRANSIT (Amber Light) but that light was not illuminated, so we elected to not use that checklist. Also searched the non-normal performance data to see if there was an option to select for our condition and there was not. After discussing together and visually confirming the LE flaps deployed, and no roll or yaw was felt in the controls and it correctly showed on the flaps position indicator, we elected to land normally with flaps 30. After landing during roll out, the PSEU illuminated. Maybe a QRH addressing this particular condition. In looking back, I believe that we could have used our commuting pilot in the cabin to visually check the inboard LE Flaps and Trailing Edge flaps were deployed. Also contacted Dispatch and got Maintenance to get another perspective on the indications we were seeing in the cockpit.		B737-700 flight crew could not find checklist for Leading Edge Devices Not Extended.	
1757193	202008	0601-1200								VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Time Pressure			1757193												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew FLC Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Aircraft	Procedure	Departing ZZZ, neither autopilot would engage during climb out. The first officer checked circuit breakers, reset the glareshield autopilot disconnect bar, and we tested each yoke disconnect button but neither autopilot would still engage. No QRH procedure could be found. We reported the autopilot issue to ATC and requested to stop our climb at an altitude below RVSM. Next we called ZZZ Maintenance and their suggestion to reset the stab trim autopilot cutout switches successfully restored autopilot function, and flight continued normally at normal cruise altitude.This was the first flight of the day for both pilots and the first flight in several days for the aircraft. Preflight distractions included a previously unseen route error that kept wanting to reload from the CPDLC auto load and update. During pushback, a ground crew wing walker wanted us to abort our number 2 engine start because she felt the tug driver had cleared us to start sooner than she wanted the engines started. After pushback ATC informed us we only had 3 minutes to taxi to make our departure slot time.Suggest adding 'Stab Trim Cutout Switches' to the Before Start Checklist, and/or including a QRH Procedure for 'Neither Autopilot Will Engage.'				B737 Captain reported auto pilot not engaging as required and non QRH procedures were used to get autopilot to function correctly.	
1758212	202008	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			400						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class C ZZZ																								Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 105; Flight Crew Type 5400					1758212	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1758213	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff at approximately 400 ft. AGL, aircraft began to yaw slightly then returned to normal. At approximately 1,000 ft. AGL the aircraft had a more pronounced left yaw again. The Pilot Monitoring identified the #1 Engine Reverser light had illuminated with a slight engine rollback. We leveled off at 2,000 ft. MSL to identify and confirm the malfunction. ATC was notified and we were issued vectors while we ran the QRH Reverser Checklist. Because of the intermittent uncontrolled reverser deployment the #1 engine was shut down using the QRH Engine Failure Shutdown Checklist. We advised ATC and had emergency vehicles meet and inspect the aircraft upon landing. Control of the aircraft was transferred to the Captain. With the engine shut down and secured, we continued to get intermittent reverser deployment indications. The single engine approach and landing were normal. We taxied clear of Runway XXL for inspection. No issues were found with the aircraft so we elected to continue to the gate.		At liftoff, aircraft yawed left at 400 ft. AGL. Flight path returned to normal. At 1,000 ft. AGL aircraft yawed left again and #1 REV alert illuminated on upper MFD. PM (Pilot Monitoring) identified the alert. Told Tower we were experiencing an engine malfunction and wished to remain in the area to work our problem. Eventually switched to a priority Tower frequency and flew a box pattern. Cleaned up aircraft on schedule. Called for QRH Reverser Unlock procedure. Reduced thrust REV light went out. Began to prepare for air return. Told Tower our intentions and time requirements. Briefed Flight Attendants and Passengers. Communicated with Dispatch and requested landing data. Thrust REV alert illuminated again with rollback and yaw. Consulted QRH and led to Engine Inflight Shutdown Checklist. Transferred control to Captain.Advised ATC and asked for emergency equipment. Completed Engine Inflight Shutdown Checklist and requested vectors to ILS XXL.Completed One Engine Inoperative Checklist. Landed without incident. Emergency equipment inspected the aircraft and it looked normal. Emergency equipment followed aircraft to the gate. Completed Parking Checklist. Called Dispatch, Maintenance Control. Spoke to Dispatch Supervisor and Chief Pilot. First Officer and Contract Maintenance inspected #1 engine. No evidence of damage.		B737 flight crew reported an engine failure and return to airport.	
1758246	202008	0601-1200	DEN.Tower	CO				6000		VMC						Tower DEN	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B DEN																												Tower DEN	Air Carrier			2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Climb		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 91; Flight Crew Type 14749					1758246	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 144.53; Flight Crew Total 20000; Flight Crew Type 4302.90					1758235	Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	After previous aircraft lifted off we were cleared for takeoff. He was on same routing (Epkee) with pretty good right turn after takeoff. As I rolled into 30 degrees of bank out of about 6,000 ft. his wake pushed us over to about 45 degrees of bank. I rolled back and continued on departure.		During initial climb out after takeoff on 34L, we were in a right turn on the Epkee departure. We encountered wake turbulence and the airplane rolled to approximately 45 degrees of bank. The Captain promptly rolled back to our standard rate turn and we continued on the departure.		B737NG flight crew reported a wake turbulence encounter departing DEN rolled them into a 45 degree bank.	
1758480	202008	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000					Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121		Ferry / Re-Positioning		Cruise											0	4														Engine Control			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Dispatch; Party2 Other		1758480	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC; Party2 Maintenance		1758473	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft; Company Policy; Human Factors; Procedure	Aircraft	The main reason for filing this report is the [priority handling request] due to multiple repeat aircraft malfunctions. Also pertinent from a safety standpoint are events involved in the preparation and execution of this flight. I will address these administrative factors at the end of my narrative.Approaching ZZZ1 airspace at 39,000 ft. we experienced the ALT DISAGREE alert, accomplished the QRH (Quick Reference Handbook) checklist and determined the FO (First Officer) and IFSD (Integrated Standby Flight Display) altimeters were correct. The FO was flying and continued to fly as I ran the checklist. We next noticed the IAS DISAGREE alert and accomplished the QRH checklist for Airspeed Unreliable. The FO continued to fly as I ran the checklist and we determined that the FO and ISFD airspeed indications were correct. At this point the FO reengaged the autopilot and manually controlled the throttles. I advised ATC (Air Traffic Control) that we were negative RVSM (Reduced Vertical Separation Minimum) and we continued at 39,000 ft. I remembered from my AML review during preflight that the previous crew had also written up that both engines had gone into EEC (Electronic Engine Control) alternate mode. When I checked the overhead panel both ALTN lights were illuminated and we were in soft alternate mode. With all the PFD (Primary Flight Display) alerts, the FO and I didn't notice if the ENG light illuminated on the six pack previous to this. With the FO continuing to fly I ran the EEC ALTERNATE MODE checklist and placed the EECs in hard alternate mode. Because we were approaching ZZZ Center airspace I elected to continue to ZZZ and subsequently [requested priority handling] with ZZZ Center. I conferred with the FO and we agreed that diverting into a foreign airport at night would have only unnecessarily complicated our situation. I kept the FAs (Flight Attendants) informed of our situation throughout.Upon checking in with ZZZ Center with a [priority handling request] call I heard a response of 'standby'. Since we were in no need of immediate assistance, I waited a few minutes to check in again. When I checked in the second time I queried the controller if he had heard my [priority] call. He hadn't and after a brief exchange we determined that the standby call was for another aircraft; he was controlling on multiple frequencies and hadn't heard my initial check in call. None of this was detrimental to the handling of our flight, but he was understandably concerned that he missed my first check in with the [priority] call. Meanwhile the CA's (Captain) altitude and then airspeed slowly returned to normal and both disagree alerts disappeared prior to our descent from 39,000 ft. IAW (In Accordance With) with the Airspeed Unreliable checklist we did not reengage the autothrottle for the remainder of the flight, because I did not want to complicate our situation in case the problems reoccurred. The autopilot continued to operate normally.We were given direct to ZZZZZ and subsequently cleared the ILS Runway XX at ZZZ. After an uneventful approach and landing we taxied to the gate. Now I would like to highlight some issues leading up to the flight and initial execution which need to be addressed.When I was assigned the trip by scheduling I was not informed that it was anything other than a normal revenue flight. The first I knew it was anything different was upon landing in ZZZ2 when I talked to the agent meeting our DHD (Dead Head) flight and determined we had to go through customs and then back through security and our flight would depart from a hardstand with no passengers although we had four flight attendants. We were scheduled to depart 55 minutes after our scheduled arrival which was woefully inadequate given how events unfolded.After we exited customs we had a local [company] (I think) employee for escort which was helpful but the hoops we had to jump through were unknown by him or us. At security we were toldto scan the QR code on our phones to fill out a health questionnaire concerning COVID-19, but even though we answered no to all the questions we still had to wait for a 'medic' to check us out and ask us the very same questions, and from what the local agent could determine, it was because we were leaving ZZZ2, regardless of how we answered the health questions. We could have skipped the time it took to fill out the questionnaire and then we had to wait another 10-15 minutes for the medic. He asked us all the same health questions and took our temperature and used a pulse oximeter before clearing us to go through security.When we reached the area where we would take a van to the aircraft, two of our FAs were missing and they had arrived three hours earlier. We were approaching the scheduled departure time. After our remaining FAs showed up, the agent had to get permission for us to go to the airplane which included taking pictures of our (CA and FO) licenses and medicals. This delayed us another 5-10 minutes. Station personnel apologized for all the delays, but they were caused by the airport policies/procedures and not within local [company] control.After arrival at the airplane I was briefed by local MX (Maintenance) that they had changed out the CA's TAT (Total Air Temperature) probe and everything checked out. The next issue is the flight release. There was confusion when we called for our clearance as 'Aircraft X'. Clearance Delivery called us 'Aircraft Y'. After much back and forth confusion we finally figured out we should be 'Aircraft Z'. My mistake for not noticing it in the lines of the release where it is different. At the top of the flight plan it says Aircraft X and the nose number matched the airplane and AML which I am very conscientious about checking. It also shows Aircraft X on the TPS, FIL, NS, etc. To fill out this form it is even listed as Aircraft X. I guess this flight falls under the category of Non-Schedule Flights, but maybe it doesn't because it wasn't assigned a flight number beginning with [number]. Even after reviewing the FOM I'm still not sure what this flight would be classified as but it seems to be the closest to a positioning flight.The final issue I think bears mentioning is that I was assigned this trip while on a XA:00 RAP (Reserve Availability Period). As originally scheduled, it would have finished close to the end of my 14 hour RAP. As it played out it went into that twilight zone between 14 and 16 hours, which although it is legal, it may not be a smart way to do business. From my own personal experience deadheading feels more tiring than actually operating the flight and yet it is not considered in the number of legs for legality purposes. Fortunately, through good CRM (Crew Resource Management), checklist discipline and adherence to SOPs (Standard Operating Procedures) the outcome was a safe conclusion to a long day. Although fatigue wasn't a factor for me on this occasion, on another day given the same set of circumstances it very well could have.Incomplete information from scheduling when assigned the trip contributed to increased stress and confusion starting with arriving at ZZZ2 through departure. Multiple [critical issues] presented an increased challenge, especially at night, overwater, in foreign airspace after a long day.Scheduling needs to make sure it is clear to the crew when any part of their schedule is other than a normal revenue flight. Dispatch should also include a statement in the remarks section of the release if the call sign is other than what appears on the HI3, NS, TPS, etc.		We were flying from ZZZ2 to ZZZ. During our preflight preparations and AML review, the CA (Captain) advised me that the previous crew had written up both engine EEC's (Electronic Engine Control) for going into alternate mode. I was the PF (Pilot Flying) and the CA (Captain) was the PM (Pilot Monitoring). Approaching ZZZ1 airspace at 39,000ft., night time, we experienced an ALT DISAGREE alert. The CA accomplished the QRH (Quick Reference Handbook) checklist and determined the FO and IFSD (Integrated Flight Standby Display) altimeters were correct. We next noticed the IAS DISAGREE alert and the CA accomplished the QRH checklist for Airspeed Unreliable. The first (Indicated Airspeed) two steps for this checklist are turn both the autopilot and autothrottle off which I did. The aircraft continued to handle safely and correctly when this was done. Subsequently, per the QRH procedures, I reengaged the autopilot and manually controlled the throttles. The CA advised ATC (Air Traffic Control) that we were negative RVSM (Reduced Vertical Separation Minimum) and we continued at 39,000 ft. to ZZZ. The CA then scanned the overhead panel and saw that the EEC ALTN lights were illuminated and in soft alternate mode. The CA then ran the EEC ALTERATE MODE checklist and placed the EECs in hard alternate mode. At this point, the CA and I discussed possible courses of action. We decided that continuing to ZZZ was the safest course of action because the aircraft was handling safely and correctly; the QRH procedures for these alerts did not require us to either land ASAP or at the nearest suitable airport; diverting into a foreign airport at night would have complicated our situation; and we were approaching ZZZ Center airspace so working with their ATC controllers would be easier to deal with. We had four FA's on board with us (no passengers), and the CA kept the FAs informed of our situation throughout the flight.Upon checking in with ZZZ Center the CA declared a [priority handling] call as a precaution in case any or all of the system alerts we had dealt with earlier reappeared. The controller told us to stand by, he obviously did not hear our [priority] call on our initial check in. Since we were not in need of immediate assistance, we decided to check in several minutes later - this time we established positive communication with the controller with our [priority] call. Meanwhile the CA's altitude, and then airspeed, slowly returned to normal and both disagree alerts disappeared prior to our descent from 39,000 ft. IAW with the Airspeed Unreliable checklist we did not reengage the autothrottle for the remainder of the flight because we did not want to complicate our situation in case the problems reoccurred.We were given direct to ZZZZZ and subsequently cleared the Runway XX at ZZZ. After an uneventful approach and landing we taxied to the gate.[I believe the cause was] maintenance procedures that did not correct the writeups from previous flight. Or an aircraft system that failed.[I suggest] review maintenance procedures for the alerts we had to deal with on this flight. Ensure any corrections or modifications to these maintenance procedures are disseminated to both domestic and foreign maintenance department, both in house and contract maintenance.		737-800 Flight Crew reported several human and indicator errors involving electronic engine control, altitude and airspeed.	
1758982	202008	1201-1800	ZHU.ARTCC	TX				28000			Rain; Icing; Thunderstorm					Center ZHU	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb		Class A ZHU																								Pitot-Static System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1758982	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1758987	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Weather; Aircraft	Weather	We departed MSY in VMC with an area of rain showers and thunderstorms to the South and South West along our route of flight.Through route study and crew briefings we knew this weather would impact the first 20-30 minutes of our flight. Center advised us of moderate to extreme precipitation at our twelve o'clock. We received clearance for left and right deviations of course and told to proceed direct when able. We chose a clear path initially but reached the point in the flight we would have to proceed carefully. The clouds and rain showers began to obscure the flight path and we relied further on our satellite based and onboard weather radar. Engine thermal anti ice was on. As we were passing through FL280 the rain picked up and my windshield and wiper began to freeze over. Simultaneously the Captain's airspeed indicator gave erroneous low air speed readings 100-140 KIAs, IAS DISAGREE was displayed, and 'AIRSPEED TOO LOW' was announced. My instruments appeared to be the most accurate and I assumed pilot flying duties. The autopilot disconnected due to my physical intervention maintaining pitch. I announced my intention to maintain 6 degrees nose high in the climb to FL370 and leave the throttles at the climb limit thrust. I then disconnected the auto throttles as they began to retard. Due to the severe icing, I scanned the engine instruments and found no indications of reduced thrust. Next, it was time to navigate. We had to make small turns to avoid the worst of the embedded towering cumulus cells being returned on the weather radar. These developed along our path as we were deviating. We decided to press ahead to be clear of the weather in a few minutes instead of the longer path of turning around. We also amended our top of climb lower to FL330 to avoid being closer to MMO (Maximum Operating Limit Speed) with suspect instrumentation. We were climbing at about 280 KIAS. Upon reaching FL330, I leveled the aircraft and in a few miles we exited the weather. The CA's (Captain's) airspeed indicator returned to normal and we ran the Airspeed Unreliable QRC and QRH. Since icing was the suspected cause of the fault and the airspeed indicator was no longer unreliable we reengaged the autopilot, observed the flight path, reengaged the auto throttles, observed the flight path, and continued normally to our destination.Not correctly considering vaulted moisture in weather radar settings and no indications of super-cooled liquid droplets in WSI (Weather Services International).[I recommend] better WSI capabilities to display simultaneous types of weather information, i.e. icing and radar.		Departed MSY in VMC with an area of rain showers and thunderstorms to the South and South West along our route of flight.Through route study and crew briefings we knew this weather would impact the first 20-30 minutes of our flight. Center advised us of moderate to extreme precipitation at our twelve o'clock. We received clearance for left and right deviations of course and told to proceed direct when able. We chose a clear path initially but reached the point in the flight we would have to proceed carefully. The clouds and rain showers began to obscure the flight path and we relied further on our satellite based and onboard weather radar. Engine thermal anti ice was on. As we were passing through FL280 the rain picked up and my First Officer's windshield and wiper began to freeze over. Simultaneously the Captain's airspeed indicator gave erroneous low air speed readings 100-140 KIAs, IAS DISAGREE was displayed, and 'AIRSPEED TOO LOW' was announced. My instruments appeared to be the most accurate and the first officer assumed pilot flying duties. The autopilot disconnected due to the physical intervention maintaining pitch. The FO (First Officer) announced his intention to maintain 6 degrees nose high in the climb to FL370 and leave the throttles at the climb limit thrust. He then disconnected the auto throttles as they began to retard. Due to the severe icing, I scanned the engine instruments and found no indications of reduced thrust. Next, it was time to navigate. We had to make small turns to avoid the worst of the embedded towering cumulus cells being returned on the weather radar. These developed along our path as we were deviating. We decided to press ahead to be clear of the weather in a few minutes instead of the longer path of turning around. We also amended our top of climb lower to FL330 to avoid being closer to MMO (Maximum Operating Limit Speed) with suspect instrumentation. We were climbing at about 280 KIAS. Upon reaching FL330, the FO leveled the aircraft and in a few miles we exited the weather. The CA's (Captain's) airspeed indicator returned to normal and we ran the Airspeed Unreliable QRC and QRH. Since icing was the suspected cause of the fault and the airspeed indicator was no longer unreliable we re-engaged the autopilot, observed the flight path, re-engaged the auto throttles, observed the flight path, and continued normally to our destination.Weak or inoperative pitot heat, however we had no indications that the heat was inoperative.I don't know that there was any way to avoid this particular incident. The only changes that I would of made is I would have [requested priority handling].		B737-8 flight crew reported unreliable airspeed indications during heavy precipitation.	
1759257	202009	1201-1800	ZFW.ARTCC	TX				39000					Daylight			Center ZFW	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise		Class A ZFW																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Fatigue; Human-Machine Interface; Workload			1759257	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Relief Pilot; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Fatigue; Workload; Human-Machine Interface			1759444	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were in a very busy communication with Dispatch about the ZZZ weather and possible divert plan. We were receiving dozens of ACARS pages of info from Dispatch. ATC Center, I think, gave us a decent from FL400 to FL390. I put it in the MCP Altitude window and the FO (First Officer) confirmed. I gave him the aircraft and proceeded to call dispatch. Somehow, FL380 was put in the window. The ATC controller called to confirm we were supposed to level at FL380. We bottomed out at about FL382 and promptly returned to FL390.The cause was we were extremely busy dealing with dispatch, weather and route changes and then transferring the aircraft to the FO, who had just minutes earlier returned from his rest break. All of us are rusty with the reduced flying. Wake up call is [early] in ZZZZ. Fatigue at the end of a [long] flight has everyone a bit off their game. Add in not flying much.Returning at the end of a long flight we failed to catch an otherwise normally caught error. [We should] refocus on Pilot Flying and confirming the altitude.		I had just come off break. When I sat down there were over 10 messages waiting on the com page and I was told Dispatch wanted us to divert. We were in VERY busy communication with Dispatch about the ZZZ weather and possible divert plan. We were receiving dozens of ACARS pages of info from Dispatch. ATC Center gave us a decent from FL400 to FL390. The Captain put it in the MCP Altitude window and I confirmed it. He gave me the aircraft and proceeded to talk to Dispatch. Somehow, FL380 was put in the window. The ATC controller called to confirm we were supposed to level at FL380. I immediately noticed the error, put FL390 in the window and use vertical speed to arrest the descent and start a climb back up to FL390. We bottomed out at about FL382 and promptly returned to FL390.The cause was we were extremely busy dealing with Dispatch, weather and route changes and then transferring the aircraft to me was probably not as clear as I remember, I had just minutes earlier returned from my rest break. The altitude change occurred immediately after I sat down to this hectic environment. All of us are rusty with the reduced flying. Fatigue at the end of a long flight has everyone a bit off their game. Add in not flying much.When returning at the end of a long flight we failed to catch an otherwise normally caught error. [We should] refocus on Pilot Flying and confirming the altitude. Have the FB more in on the process on the initial seat swap.		B777-200 flight crew reported an altitude deviation during cruise due to fatigue.	
1759260	202009	1201-1800	ZZZ.Tower	US												Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Gear Lever/Selector		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1759260												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Arriving to ZZZ with an visual approach to [Runway] XXC, the landing gear handle would not move to the down position. A go-around was performed, QRH executed with gear override trigger pulled, the gear handle still would not move down. One last try before a manual gear extension was accomplished, the gear handle was tried again with more force and the handle finally moved to the down position and a crosswind landing was made to [Runway] XXR due to traffic. Fuel was 6.7 on approach and 4.7 after landing.				B737-800 Captain reported executing a go-around after the landing gear lever would not move to the down position. On subsequent approach, the gear lever was positioned down with considerable force.	
1759446	202009	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1759446												Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Other Person; Person Passenger; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During cruise, FAs (Flight Attendants) and crewmembers/passengers reported strong odor in the aft cabin, with the strongest concentration in the aft galley. Smell was reported as both electrical burning and cloth burning. Advised ATC, and was cleared by ATC to start a descent and turn toward ZZZ and initiated the SMOKE, FIRE, OR FUMES QRC/QRH procedure. During the QRH procedure, turned off the R PACK and called FAs. All reported dissipation of the odors. Uneventful descent, approach, and landing at ZZZ uneventfully. ARFF inspected the aircraft on the runway and followed taxi in to gate. No reports of illness or injury by the passengers or crewmembers.Cause: Unknown source, but likely a problem in the Right Side Air Cond system.				B737-800 Captain reported an "electrical burning" fumes event that resulted in a diversion.	
1759661	202009	1201-1800	DFW.Airport	TX			0				Windshear					Tower DFW	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1759661												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action	Weather	Weather	Aborted on Runway 35L at 120 kts due to Windshear warning. Returned to gate for maintenance inspection. Weather was moving close to the north end of the airport. Delaying departures would help prevent the situation from occurring again landing.				B737 Captain reported high speed rejected takeoff.	
1761448	202009									VMC						TRACON ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1761448	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1761455	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Human Factors	Procedure	Began initial descent into ZZZZ. The controller called to change the STAR. I had to ask 3 times to repeat the prescribed STAR to be flown. His English was very broken. Traffic on the frequency was light. We determined a change of STARs. I input into the FMS and confirmed and executed with the Captain. By this time we were either over [the IAF] to or just past the point. The aircraft overflew [the fix]. The Captain went into heading select and turned to course. Two to three miles past [the fix], the aircraft was back on an intercept outbound [from the fix]. Then the controller said turn to a heading to intercept inbound course, which we had already initiated. Continuing descent the controller instructed us to fly the circuit which was part of the STAR and we did as planned. During the descent the controller gave us 5 altitude step downs to comply with, 4 of the 5 were then subsequently modified back to a higher altitude. After the compliance with circuit instructions, controller changed the runway. I then reprogrammed the FMS, and confirmed execute with the Captain. After so many changes by the controller and workload was lowered in the cockpit, I queried the controller one final time to ensure we were all on same page. I stated to controller confirm ILS XXR, controller said negative ILS XXL, are you ready to commence approach. I quickly for 3rd time reprogrammed the FMS for ILS XXL confirmed with the Captain and executed. I then told controller ready to commence, and then briefed the Captain on differences of approaches, and new Baro, and landing distances. We landed uneventfully.Poor English by controller almost inaudible. Very late approach instructions modifications. Could be solved if controller gave us changes to STAR and approach a few minutes prior to descent instead of at last moment, and past Anchor points on STAR.		We were filed to [the IAF] and we set up for the ILS Approach. As we approached [the fix] the controller issued a new arrival but we had difficulty understanding the new clearance [due to] language problems. Finally it was determined after 3 difficult queries what we were to fly. After installing the new clearance into the FMS the Aircraft did not make the turn towards [the] next point. As pilot flying I recognized the overshoot and made a correction back to course just prior to ATC issuing vectors. During the arrival numerous runway changes were issued and at least 5 or more altitude instructions given. Just prior to base turn the First Officer queried the controller one final time to confirm ILS XXR. The controller said negative ILS XXL. We quickly for 3rd time reprogrammed the FMS for the ILS XXL, briefed and executed an uneventful landing on XXL.Language problems which caused confusion and delayed FMS programming on our part. Late arrival insertion caused overshoot. Overshoot caused workload to increase. Do a better job anticipating what other options may be available to your crew and get an early confirmation on what arrival to expect. Better to reprogram the FMS after sequencing to the next point then forcing a FMS change close to a turn point.		Flight crew reported numerous late clearances due to language barrier from a foreign controller.	
1761594	202009	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121		Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1761594												Ground Event / Encounter Person / Animal / Bird			Person Flight Crew	Other Takeoff Roll	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate	Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	On takeoff at approximately 100 knots we hit at least two seagulls, one entered the left engine. We rejected the takeoff. After clearing the runway we requested fire trucks out of an abundance of caution for the high speed reject. We ran the RTO (Rejected Takeoff) in ACARS. Data said we could return to the gate with a brake cooling time of 16 minutes. We returned to the gate after Fire and Rescue cleared us. No Passenger injuries were reported.				B737-700 flight crew reported rejecting takeoff due to bird strike.	
1761776	202009	1201-1800	ORD.Airport	IL						VMC						Tower ORD	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B ORD																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 140; Flight Crew Total 9100; Flight Crew Type 6200		Troubleshooting			1761776												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon climb out from runway 28R in ORD the Captain was unable to trim the stabilizer nose up with the yoke electric trim switch. He advised me of this and I helped him with manual trim from the trim wheel. We leveled off at 5,000 ft. and 250 knots for controllability and received permission from ATC to fly a box pattern to troubleshoot. We subsequently slowed to 230 knots for the pattern.We accomplished the Electronic Stabilizer Trim Inoperative procedure and got a patch through to [Maintenance Control] with dispatch monitoring on radio. Maintenance agreed with the procedure and with our decision to return to ORD. A flaps 15 landing was safely performed per the checklist and the aircraft was returned to [the] Gate.				B737 flight crew reported stabilizer trim malfunction during climb out.	
1761844	202009	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			500									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Landing		Class C ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1761844												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On short final below 500 ft. the FO (First Officer) was flying and had an un-commanded stabilizer trim down. He stopped it with using the electric trim in the opposite direction correcting the out of trim condition and landed uneventfully. After landing we debriefed what happened and decided to write the malfunction up in the aircraft log. I was unaware that the FO was experiencing a problem with the trim until after landing.				B737NG flight crew reported stabilizer trim anomalies on final.	
1761923	202009	1201-1800	LGB.Airport	CA				6800			3		Daylight	3000		TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway 30	Initial Approach; Descent	STAR DSNEE FOUR	Class E SCT																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 160		Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1761923	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 40; Flight Crew Type 18000		Workload; Training / Qualification			1761921	Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Human Factors	Procedure	Descending into LGB on the DSNEE arrival, we were cleared to descend via the arrival. Captain was Pilot Flying. First Officer was Pilot Monitoring. ATC cleared us to descend via the arrival for Runway 30 but didn't specify which approach to expect. ATIS stated runway in use but not approach type. Captain loaded and briefed the RNAV (Z). While descending via, and inside the branch point for the arrival, Approach Control cleared us for the ILS via the arrival. At that time we were in VNAV/LNAV with the lowest altitude on the arrival set in the MCP. Our position was a few miles East of MCKYE and descending.Upon receiving the clearance the Captain quickly loaded the ILS and executed it without verifying there were no resultant discontinuities. Because we were inside the branch point of the arrival a discontinuity did appear, even though we were going to the same runway. Once the execute (EXEC) button was pushed the discontinuity appeared and VNAV disconnected. Because we were descending at the time, we continued to descend. The crossing restriction at MCKYE was at or above 7200'. The aircraft descended to roughly 6800' before the Captain corrected and returned us to the correct altitude. We were back up to 7200' prior to MCKYE. After correcting the flight path, the correct approach was loaded in the FMC and the remainder of the flight was uneventful.Proper verification prior to execution would help prevent this type of event from occurring again. Make sure both Crew Members have seen the changes and agree on them before pushing EXEC. Also, once the arrival has commenced do not execute any FMC changes unless you are on the LEGS page and have cleared any discontinuities resulting from the approach or runway change.		We were on the DSNEE 4 Arrival for LGB and had previously entered the RNAV Y MIDDS transition (I did not close the MIDDS discontinuity per SOP). Weather was IMC due to fires in Southern California. We had been cleared to descend via and 4000' MSL was entered in the MCP window with VNAV PATH. Approximately 1 NM prior to DSNEE waypoint, we were given clearance for the ILS to Runway 30. I re-entered the arrival with the MIDDS transition for the ILS and immediately got a VNAV discontinuity from the FMC due to the re-entry. We were working on closing the discontinuity when I noticed the aircraft had descended 400' below the MCKYE restriction of 7200' MSL, approximately 3 NM prior to MCKYE. I immediately initiated a climb back to 7200' MSL to MCKYE and continued the descend via arrival. We did not see any traffic conflicts from TCAS and ATC did not mention anything.Expect the unexpected. I should have entered the next restriction altitude in the MCP before making changes to the arrival. Even though I did not expect a discontinuity, it would have been prudent because we were on the arrival and close to a fix with a restriction. Surprise at the discontinuity delayed action. I should have concentrated more on the restrictions and less on the FMC.		Flight crew reported momentarily descend below required altitude on the DSNEE FOUR arrival while programing FMC for a change from RNAV to ILS approach into LGB.	
1761946	202009										Turbulence					Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1761946												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Procedure; Weather	Weather	Operating flight as Pilot Monitoring. We were deviating to avoid weather along the route. We were cleared direct to ZZZZZ waypoint and asked for a deviation around weather but was initially denied. We were only authorized a deviation right of course in which were able to avoid the first TCU (Towering Cumulus) but the wind direction and velocity forced us into the second TCU as we were turning back onto course. Prior to entering the TCU the wings were leveled and we experienced a climb of approximately 700-800 ft. During the altitude excursion ATC asked why we had deviated and I replied that we were currently in the TCU. The autopilot remained engaged and within approximately 10 seconds the aircraft recovering back to the assigned altitude. ATC asked again for the reason and I replied that we had entered the TCU momentarily and was back on course and assigned altitude. ATC inability to provide deviations in either direction due to weather. Better communication between ATC facilities and increased tolerances left or right of course for hazardous weather conditions.				B777 flight crew reported altitude deviation due to weather.	
1761964	202009	1201-1800								VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning		Cruise	Vectors	Class B ZZZ	Y	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance																			Cockpit Window		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1761964												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were to ferry aircraft from ZZZ to ZZZ1 after engine replacement due to engine fire along with the mechanic who replaced the engine. After leveling at cruise, I noticed a crack in my side window and brought it to the mechanics attention. He had me mark the beginning and end of the crack with his marker. The crack continued to grow and a second crack appeared. I called Dispatch on my iPad and informed him of our situation along with asking him to provide us with possible diversion airports if the situation got worse, which he did. Approximately 30 minutes later, 2 more small cracks began to appear and the mechanic suggested we might want to divert due to possible window failure which we all agreed. Also remnants of Hurricane Sally were moving through ZZZ. I called Dispatch again and we decided ZZZ2 would be a good option. We landed in ZZZ2 with a normal landing. after arriving at the gate we counted 6 small cracks and 2 possible cracks on the inner pane. It was helpful to have an experienced company mechanic onboard along with help from our Dispatcher.				A320 flight crew reported they detected the side window cracking in flight, causing a diversion to a precautionary landing.	
1762077	202009	0601-1200	DEN.Airport	CO				11000					Daylight			TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC; INS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway 35L	Descent; Initial Approach	STAR TBARR ONE	Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Type 12000		Human-Machine Interface; Workload; Time Pressure; Distraction; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1762077												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury		Y	Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	Approach briefing: Briefed the TBARR 1 Arrival. Briefed both the ILS 35L and the RNAV Z Runway 35L including the runway transitions. ATIS reported that both were in use. During Arrival descent: was informed by Flight Attendant that a Passenger had difficulty breathing and was being attended to. Told Approach Control (second from the Final Controller) and we were given priority handling and he told us to expect Runway 35L. We confirmed the RNAV-Z 35L in the FMC.  Sent Dispatch a message. Flight Attendant called us numerous times during the arrival transition. The Final Controller told us 'after LDORA, cleared for the ILS 35L. We mistakenly did not execute the ILS 35L in the FMC, which now ended up going from LDORA to CRUPP instead of via the ILS 35L transition fixes. After passing LDORA Approach gave us a 060 followed by a 020 heading to join ILS 35L and cleared for us the approach.Our planning and preparation for the arrival and landing was performed thoroughly. During this high intensity period of time with many non-normal distractions we need to be cognizant when changes take place such as changes to arrivals and approaches and make sure those changes are loaded and executed. We could've also queried ATC of the approach that was being planned for us.  It would've been a great help if ATC, the second from the Final Controller told us which approach to expect instead of having the Final Controller just clear us for the ILS 35L via the transition not too far from LDORA fix.Navigated via wrong transition.				Captain reported navigating on via the wrong transition by executing the RNAV instead of the cleared ILS approach, following an onboard passenger problem.	
1762475	202009	1201-1800	ORD.Airport	IL				7000		VMC						TRACON C90	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class B ORD																												TRACON C90	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2		IFR		FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B ORD																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 76; Flight Crew Type 2284					1762475	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 99; Flight Crew Total 5809; Flight Crew Type 4260					1762514	Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Ambiguous	In descent from 9000 to 7000 with autopilot engaged hit by moderate jolt of wake turbulence. Continued descent and aircraft began to roll past 30 degrees angle of bank. Autopilot disengaged and aircraft rolled upright. Autopilot reengaged. Checked on flight attendants, all were ok. No passengers were up at the time. Enquired ATC about who we were following. Appeared to be a B777 ahead going to a different runway.		While descending from 9,000 to 7,000 we were given a turn to 170 for vectors to intercept the ILS 9R. In the turn, we experienced wake turbulence - with a bank angle approximately 30 to the right. The Captain disengaged the autopilot and hand flew the airplane back to level flight. I checked on the flight attendants to make sure they were all ok because it was so abrupt and sudden. All 4 of the flight attendants reported to me they were fine. Within a couple of minutes at 7,000', we encountered wake turbulence again. This time it was a 40 - 45 degree bank angle to the right. My Captain was still hand flying and recovered the airplane. I asked ATC what type of aircraft we were following, the controller responded we weren't following anyone but there was a 777 ahead of us but 2,000' below us. The 777 was being vectored ahead of us for [RWY] 10C. Perhaps more spacing was required even though we were being vectored for different but parallel runways.		B737 flight crew reported encountering wake turbulence on arrival into ORD in trail of a B777.	
1762515	202009	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Ground ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors																									Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 114; Flight Crew Total 9316; Flight Crew Type 9316					1762515												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On takeoff roll Runway XX, Tower said another pilot reported flames coming out No1 engine or left side. Rejected takeoff, ran all checklists, and shut down engine. Never had any abnormal engine indications. Had them roll the fire trucks and upon inspection all appeared normal. No smoke or abnormals reported by flight attendants. After all checklists complete and contacting Company and Maintenance had fire trucks follow us to our gate. ELB entry followed up with verbal to Maintenance. First Officer and flight attendants did an excellent job!				Captain reported a rejected take off due to a reported engine fire.	
1762721	202009	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				31700								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Time Pressure; Workload; Troubleshooting			1762721	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Workload; Time Pressure			1762720	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Due to weather and a supposed power outage at the airport, we were delayed approximately 1 hour from our scheduled departure time. This was the start of leg 2 for the day as we already operated out of ZZZ. There was another issue I will discuss after the conclusion of our [priority handling] event. [The] FO (First Officer) was pilot flying at the time. We took off out of ZZZ1 in very light rain throughout our departure. My FO stated it was barely enough rain to notice on the windshield. Light chop may have been experienced temporarily at most, but it was mostly smooth on climb out. We were cleared to flight level 31,000 where we stopped our descent. Shortly thereafter, while autopilot B was engaged, the autopilot started a significant descent all by itself. Unbeknownst to me and after speaking with my FO on the ground, the FO noticed his altimeter jumped from 31,000 ft. to 31,800 approximately. As soon as the autopilot started its descent, I took controls from the FO, disconnected the autopilot and leveled the airplane at 31,000 using my altimeter. I don't think we lost much altitude because I almost immediately disconnected the autopilot to arrest the pitch down moment. ATC shortly queried our altitude and we read back 31,000. ATC stated he saw us at approximately 31,700. About that time I looked over at the FO side and saw his altitude was the same as ATC verified 31700 ft. and looked at my display, which showed 31,000ft. ALT disagree was showing. At the same time, I noticed the FO airspeed was significantly slower than my airspeed and cross-checked the standby indicator - FO airspeed approximately 80 kt. slow. Airspeed disagree showed.At some point, I issued a pan pan pan. Standby airspeed and CA airspeed was the same and based on our thrust setting and altitude there was no doubt my FO's airspeed indicator was incorrect, 70-80 kt. too slow for our thrust and pitch setting. At that point, I determined, maybe incorrectly as I analyzed things on the ground, that since ATC had his altitude readout, I was basing our actual altitude on my FO's display and airspeed on my display. My reasoning is that 700-800 ft. high was safer than 700-800 ft. low, close to the ground, during approach and landing. Since I was now flying airspeed on my side and altitude on my FO's side, my altitude fluctuated up and down due to a gigantic 4 ft. scan instead of a normal 10 in. scan. Once I determined I could not hold altitude as expected, I asked for a block of altitude for airspace protection and was granted that clearance. The QRH for airspeed disagree was started, which led us to unreliable airspeed. We completed the checklist for unreliable airspeed and turned back towards ZZZ1. At some point after this realization I changed our pan pan to [requesting priority handling]. ATC apparently missed our [priority handling request] as he [provided priority handling] for us several minutes after I made the call myself. Upon realizing our altitude and airspeed unreliable, and ATC confirming ZZZ2 was VFR, I decided to turn back around another time to divert to ZZZ2 as it was a safer airport. Eventually, several altitude step downs were given to us for our descent into ZZZ2. QRH was completed and calls to Dispatch were then attempted.FO tried numerous times to contact company to relay our status and was unsuccessful. We attempted several calls on ZZZ3 Radio with no response. We asked ATC to call company and to contact us via ZZZ3 Radio, with no response from Dispatch. We then called on Guard to ask them to contact our Dispatch and let them know to contact us on ZZZ3 Radio; communication with Dispatch was never successful. With everything going on, I did not feel comfortable having my FO heads down sending an ACARS message...we were too busy.Descent checklist was soon completed and approach checklist was completed below 18,000. Holds were also accomplished to allow us time for checklists and getting weather info and setting up for the approach into ZZZ2 which were indicating 1100 ft. ceilings and better than required visibility. At some point in the descent, below 10,000 ft., my FO realized the EEC were both in alternate mode and a discussion commenced. We discussed the thrust lever position and thrust changes that occurred in ALT mode but due to our [priority handling situation] and attempting to get the airplane on the ground safely, I forgot to have the FO run the QRH for the EEC alternate mode. Due to having no weather forecast and knowing weather was in the vicinity of ZZZ2 due to the hurricane remnants, my main concern was getting the aircraft on the ground safely in as close to VFR conditions as I could get.After landing, while discussing the events with my FO, we realized we probably should have made time to run this QRH procedure. I just didn't have the weather info in flight to make a call to continue holding to accomplish this checklist instead of landing. Landing VFR in my mind was a safer option than running this checklist and possibly losing VFR conditions due to hurricane remnants. The approach was set up and briefed and we commenced our approach. Checklists completed. CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) standing by for us. The approach and landing was uneventful.After landing we were given instructions to taxi down a taxiway, XX or XY, [which] I did not feel was safe for us to turn down [to] as it was too narrow for my comfort. I also had concern that the taxiway could support our weight and relayed to Ground Control that I would not be accepting that taxiway. We did a 180 on a closed runway as Ground gave us permission to do so and frankly, that was our only option due to where the aircraft was stopped on the taxiway.We then returned to [the FBO] and parked. [The] incident was written in [the] logbook with my initial impression on what happed, the caution messages we received and that I thought airspeed was good on my side only and altitude was good on the FO side only. Airplane was chocked and secured, no door seals were on the aircraft. We shut down, secured the airplane, and then did the supplementary procedure for powering the aircraft down. We then exited the aircraft and closed the door. We called for the FBO to remove the stairs from the airplane for security purposes. The stairs were then pulled back from the aircraft and we exited the Ramp into the FBO. [The] Chief Pilot, DOO, Dispatch and Maintenance Control [were] all notified via my personal phone. The earlier situation I referenced was a runway change at clearance for pushback. Since we had not started the engines or changed configuration, I had the FO change our assigned runway and then consulted the TLR (Thrust Lever Resolver) for new numbers. I showed the FO the TLR data and he verified correct data for runway change. I then read him the new data to input since we were pushing back. What I did not realize is that RTO1 had not been selected at that time, but all other number were entered. At some point during the takeoff, prior to 60 kt., thrust was set, I noticed we had TO (Takeoff) selected instead of RTO1 based on the N1 setting. When I referenced our airspeed at this realization, we had already exceeded 80 kt. I think I made the 80 kt. call at 82 kt. I determined the safest outcome was to continue the takeoff based on our rejected takeoff parameters. I had in the back of my mind a 5 kt. difference in V1 between RTO1 and TO as I had looked earlier due to the weather in ZZZ1 at the time. This situation has led me to realize it is most likely a better habit to have the FO read off the TLR data and I enter the numbers myself in the FMC. This was something I just didn't catch as I was starting to get tired from sitting there for an extended time.[The cause was] airspeed and altitude [being] unreliable due to an unknown problem. After reflecting on what occurred, I believe none of the altitude or airspeed information on my FO's outboard display unit was correct. I erroneously believed that ATC's readout of our altitude confirmed my FO's altimeter was correct without realizing his entire system had likely failed, providing erroneous information to ATC. The weather from the hurricane was certainly a contributing factor as my main concern was getting the airplane on the ground safely and landing in VFR conditions.		Unreliable airspeed was the first issue. While flying level at FL310 it was my leg, and autopilot B was engaged. Suddenly, my altimeter shot up to FL317 and the autopilot initiated a descent. The Captain immediately took controls and disconnected the autopilot. As we were cross-checking and trying to gain an understanding of the state of the airplane, ATC queried our altitude because they showed us at FL317 instead of 310. Cross-checking revealed that the FO (First Officer) side was indeed showing FL317 while the CA (Captain) side showed FL310. Simultaneously, the FO-side airspeed showed an enormous slowing trend. Lastly, IAS and ALT disagree messages popped up on both the FO- and CA-sides.The Captain declared a pan pan pan at first then a bit later, [priority handling was requested], but ATC apparently missed the call as he [requested priority handling] for us at a later time. The [priority handling] was reported [due to] unreliable airspeed and altimeter disagreement. I ran the checklist for IAS DISAGREE, which referred me to the UNRELIABLE AIRSPEED Checklist. In communicating with ATC, we determined that the standby and CA-side altitude did not agree with ATC, and the CA-side airspeed made the most sense when compared to the ATC reports. The difference was about 80 kt. between CA- and FO-side IAS. Also, the displayed GS was always within about 10 kt. of ATC's speed reports. At this point, the Captain was flying with reference to his airspeed and the FO's altitude.The Captain initially asked for a return to ZZZ1 and a block of altitude as he struggled fly with reference to both sides. Because there was still about a 700 ft. discrepancy between FO- and CA-side altimeters, it was decided to pick a field that had VFR landing conditions. The nearest such airfield was ZZZ2. As I continued with the checklist, we selected 'all on 1' on the ALT SOURCE switch. At that time, the 'disagree' messages went away, and the airspeed matched, but altimeters still did not agree. Upon reflection, I realize that the Captain had transitioned to the local altimeter setting below 18000, but I was still in the standard 29.92. Once I updated my altimeter setting, both altimeters matched. Now the only error message was 'source 2' because we had de-selected it. The checklist also directed to disable transponder altitude reporting. It occurred to us later that the transponder may have been providing ATC an erroneous altitude because it was coupled to the FO-side. At some point, unclear exactly when, we noticed the EEC switches were both showing ALTN with the corresponding ENG master caution. Once the UNRELIABLE AIRSPEED checklist was completed, we spent the rest of the flight trying to contact Dispatch about our [situation] and diversion, but at this point, we were unsuccessful as we were probably too low to communicate over ARINC frequencies. We were also busy setting up the approach into ZZZ2. We landed without incident.The second issue was departure with wrong takeoff thrust programmed. When we were finally ready to push and start engines, Ground Metering advised us on an updated ATIS and were told to expect Runway XXR departure. I reloaded the SID in the FMS with the updated runway and got a message that V speeds were erased. I went to the page to re-load the V speeds while the Captain referenced the TLR (Thrust Lever Resolver) for the new speeds for the new runway. As he referenced the TLR, he read aloud that we still had an R-TO-1 takeoff, same flaps, same weight, same N1 values, and same CG. He then read me the new V speeds which I loaded in the FMS. Since the engines hadn't started yet, we considered these items as part of the preflight brief and declined to do a runway change checklist. At some point, the Captain noticed that we were at full TO (Takeoff) thrust instead of R-TO-1 thrust. My low time on the aircraft prevented me from having the experience necessary to know that changing the runway not only deleted the V speeds, but also deleted the takeoff thrust settings. I should have verified all of the information was still correct as the Captain read it out.The cause of TO data error was task saturation. Weather conditions at ZZZ1: IFR / rain / 500 ft. CIG / winds gusting around 15 from the east. Outside factors [include] delay to cargo loading. Pushed an hour late. Updated ATIS 3 times while waiting to depart. All 3 said departing on Runway XY. Realizing the transponder was transmitting erroneous altitude information earlier would have greatly simplified matters. All performance data should have been reviewed in the FMC for the runway change.		B737-800 flight crew reported an uneventful diversion due to unreliable airspeed and altitude indications during cruise.	
1762841	202009	1801-2400	LGA.Airport	NY		2	700						Night			Tower LGA	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class B LGA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 91; Flight Crew Type 91					1762841												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight		ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	We were doing the RNAV Visual to Runway 31. Weather was not a factor. Autopilot was on, everything was set, stabilized, on path, pretty much a perfect approach, or so we thought. VASI was also red over white, so from our end there was nothing wrong. Tower gave us a low altitude alert asking us to check our altimeter setting immediately. We had no GPWS (Ground Proximity Warning System) warning, like I said we were right on profile. My concern is, why did the Tower give us that alert? If we got that alert, shouldn't every Company aircraft flying the RNAV Visual to 31 get the same alert? Is there something wrong with the procedure? Their equipment? Or did we make a mistake? If we did, we don't know what we could have done differently. Could you guys look into this? Is LGA handing out this warning to everyone? Because if we received this alert based on what we were doing, then everyone landing on 31 on the RNAV should be in the same place altitude and position as us plus or minus 10 feet?				Flight Crew flying 737NG aircraft received Alert from Air Traffic Control on approach.	
1763931	202009	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Training / Qualification			1763931												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Strike - Aircraft			Person Flight Crew	Other Landing	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	On touchdown at ZZZ, spoilers came out and caused the nose to rise. As a result, we had a tail strike. Reported this to MX (Maintenance) Control. Green band on tail strike cartridge was still showing but paint was scraped off. FAs (Flight Attendants) in rear of aircraft also heard the strike. Also spoke with [Company Safety] and [Flight Operations].I realize this is mentioned in the FH (Flight Handbook). I will add this to the brief for now on. I have personally only experienced it once. We may also want to add this to the initial or recurrent sim training.				B737-900 Captain reported tail strike during landing.	
1764020	202009	0601-1200	ZZZZ.Airport	FO						VMC			Dawn			Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZZ	Y	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing																		Turbine Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 223; Flight Crew Total 18000; Flight Crew Type 12036					1764020	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 142; Flight Crew Total 12391; Flight Crew Type 12391					1764021	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Captain and I [deadheaded] to ZZZ to pick up a 737-800 to ferry back to ZZZ1 due to a loss of system A hydraulic fluid. Preflight showed no leaks and everything ok with the aircraft. I was PM and upon takeoff the Master Caution light came on for engine driven hydraulic pump for system A. The light went out and continued. Abeam ZZZ2 the master caution came back on for engine driven hydraulic pump number 1 system A. I pulled out the QRH and ran the Hydraulic Pump Low Pressure checklist which had us turn the pump switch off. With quantity and pressure still good using the electric pump we decided to continue to ZZZ1. Maintenance techs on the aircraft agreed with the decision. Soon after passing ZZZZZ intersection the number 1 Engine Oil Filter Bypass indication on the upper engine MFD illuminated. I pulled out the QRH and we decided to request a lower altitude since we knew we could not maintain level flight with one engine at idle. We asked for a descent to FL 200 and told them we were working on a problem with our left engine. Since the OIL FILTER BYPASS indication remained illuminated the next step per the QRH was to shut the engine down. As a result we had to land at the nearest suitable airport. We decided that ZZZ3 was the best choice since it was close the weather was good and we were both familiar with the airport. I continued with the checklist as [the Captain] flew the aircraft, [requested priority handling], and requested the diversion. We were cleared direct to the airport and a descent to FL 70. Shutdown and descent proceeded normally with me accomplishing the shutdown and [the Captain] continuing to fly and maintain communication with ATC. We then moved to the one engine inoperative and landing checklist which included calculating single engine landing distance. Hydraulic system A at this point had gone down to 25 percent. We prepared for the possibility of losing total system A pressure and a manual gear extension but was not a factor as the gear operated normally, [the Captain] performed a visual approach and landing with a safe landing in ZZZ3. We then sent a message to dispatch via ACARS that we were safe on the ground.		First Officer and I deadheaded to ZZZ to pick up a 737-800, which had been down for maintenance due to failure of the number 1 engine driven hydraulic pump failure and loss of system A fluid. Upon arrival we did our normal preflight and found no problems with the aircraft. The 3 Tech OPS employees who fixed the aircraft were embarked and we prepared to fly.Start up, taxi, and takeoff were normal. I was flying pilot and [First Officer] was monitoring. Early in the climb master caution light for the hydraulics came on and [First Officer] noted that the low pressure light for the engine driven hydraulic pump on engine 1 was on then quickly went out. Thinking it was anomaly with sensor and hydraulic quantity and pressure normal we continued. We were abeam ZZZ2 and the master caution for Hydraulics once again illuminated and [First Officer] noted the Low Pressure light for engine driven hydraulic pump on the left (number 1) engine was illuminated and stayed on. [First Officer] pulled out the QRH and proceeded to run the Hydraulic Pump Low Pressure checklist which lead to the pump being turned off. With quantity and pressure still good using the electric pump we decided to continue. We did get one of the Maintenance Techs to come to the flight deck and we showed and conversed with him about the issue. Nothing changed our thought process so we continued.Soon after passing ZZZZZ intersection and headed directly to ZZZZZ1 intersection, the number 1 Engine Oil Filter Bypass indication on the upper engine MFD illuminated. [First Officer] once again pulled out the QRH and we decided I would continue to fly and talk and he would run the checklist. We were at FL300 and decided to request a lower altitude as we knew we could not maintain level flight for long with one engine at idle. We asked for and received a descent to FL200 and told him we were working on a problem with our left engine. [First Officer] worked our way through the checklist resulting with the OIL FILTER BYPASS indication remaining illuminated. Per the checklist the next step was to shut down the engine and as a result of that we would need to land. We chose ZZZ3.[First Officer] continued the checklist as I [requested priority handling] and requested to divert. We were cleared direct the airport and a descent to 7,000. Shutdown and descent proceeded normally with [First Officer] completing the shutdown checklist. With the engine secured we moved on to the one engine inoperative and landing checklist to include the non-normal single engine landing distance. As we got closer to ZZZ3 the hydraulic issue took another turn. The quantity began to change first climbing to 106 percent and then slowly starting to decrease to mid-70 percent and then working its way down slowly till as we were coming down final it was in mid-20's. [First Officer] brought up the possibility of losing the 'A' system, so both of us were both prepared should that happen. Fortunately we did not have to run that checklist. Visual approach and landing was uneventful and normal. Landed and taxied to parking spot.We sent a message to Dispatch using ACARS that we were safe on the ground. The call I got after we had been on ground for 15 min. or so from the FODM gave me the feeling that it didn't go through but it was sent. It was confirmed by maintenance that in fact the 'A' system was depleted to about 25 percent and still leaking.		B737 flight crew reported inflight shut down and diversion due to hydraulic system failure.	
1764056	202009	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				3000		IMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS	Initial Approach																										Traffic Collision Avoidance System (TCAS)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1764056	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1764057	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the PF (Pilot Flying) doing CA (Captain) upgrade line check. Coming on the left downwind for runway 19L in ZZZ at 3000 ft. IMC we got a TA/RA and the TCAS was showing a traffic about 3 miles 12 o'clock same altitude, I disconnected the A/P (Auto-Pilot) and A/T (Auto-Throttles) and initiated the TCAS maneuver following the guidance which directed us to climb, PM (Pilot Monitoring) advised ATC and ATC informed us that there were no traffic in the vicinity of the airport and advised us to get back to 3000 ft. and vectored us for the ILS approach 19L and while we're descending back to 3000 ft. the FO (First Officer) side inbound DU (Display Unit) went blank; few seconds later the same display unit on my side went blank as well at this time we were already turning final and I saw the runway and decided to continue to land meanwhile the PM called the ATC and advised them that we had multiple display failures, 2 minutes later we got the displays back and we continued the visual approach to landing.False TCAS TA/RA and multiple display failures.		[Report narrative contained no additional information.]		B737-800 Captain reported TCAS warning on approach.	
1764237	202009	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			500			VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 118; Flight Crew Total 5209; Flight Crew Type 4332					1764237	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 145; Flight Crew Total 98; Flight Crew Type 9816					1764238	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was flying as the FO (First Officer) off[Runway] XXR in ZZZ. After liftoff I attempted to trim the nose up and there was no response from the electric trim. I notified the Captain and we continued the departure and cleaned the aircraft up. I reverted to the manual trim wheel to adjust the trim. Due to the load on the airplane, the wheel was moderately difficult to turn. I was aware that I could unload the aircraft to make it easier but I did not feel the need to upset the passengers at that time. The Captain tried his electric trim which also would not trim the nose up. However, the electric trim would trim nose down. We then leveled off at 11,000 ft., [requested priority handling] and ran the trim inop checklist. As the flying pilot, I only flew the airplane and talked to ATC while the Captain managed the [situation]. The checklist directed us to disconnect the trim system and fly a flaps 15 landing. We chose [priority] return at ZZZ and landed about 7,000 pounds overweight. We also called a deadheading check airman up to the cockpit as an extra set of eyes. I configured early so that I wouldn't need to trim much on final. The overweight flaps 15 landing was uneventful. ATC was very accommodating. I know I checked the trim system on the ground and it tested good. The Captain had a habit of resting his knee against the trim wheel I and recall warning him to move it prior to the check. At the gate maintenance boarded and we showed them how it would trim nose down but not up. Upon further inspection we realized if we trimmed all the way nose down it would then trim nose up, but only for .5 seconds before stopping again.		During climb out with my FO (First Officer) at the controls, he proceeded to trim the aircraft up with no response. I tried it on my side same no response. The aircraft would trim down but not up. We continued with our departure and climbed to 11,000 ft. FO is flying the aircraft manually increasing the airspeed to 230 Knots. Heavy weight aircraft. Engaged the AP to see if we could get the aircraft to trim. Same non response with the Stabilizer out of trim light and the speed trim light coming on at the same time. Both went out when we disengaged the AP. [Requested priority handling]. Ran the QRH for the Electric Trim Stabilizer Trim Inoperative. Consulted with maintenance and contacted Maintenance Control via the ACARS. Aircraft was hard to trim manually but it did work. Proceeded back to ZZZ and landed without incident.		B737 flight crew reported electric trim malfunction during climb.	
1764468	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				7000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121				Descent																										Traffic Collision Avoidance System (TCAS)		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1764468		Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1764471	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Descending below 9,000, we received a TA (Traffic Alert). At approximately 7,000 feet, the indications turned into an RA (Resolution Advisory) conflict resolution. Aircraft indicated we were on a collision path with another aircraft and recommended that we climb. We looked for traffic while simultaneously disengaging the autopilot and auto throttle to comply with TA/RA. We descended to close to 7,000 followed by a climb to approximately 11,000. RA continued to indicate a climb. We queried ATC multiple times to see if they showed any traffic in our vicinity. They indicated that they showed an aircraft at 7,000 feet that was west and behind our position that should not have been a factor. Nonetheless, we climbed but the TA/RA still indicated a climb despite having climbed almost 4,000 feet. We deemed that this was an erroneous message. I took the controls to establish a descent and get us back to a path that would help us descend, but then we got a descent RA. We again complied with this RA just to be safe. After descending almost 3,000 [feet] and we still did not see any traffic, we determined that the aircraft was erroneously indicating a descent. We concluded that we were getting a malfunction. The aircraft had previously been written-up for a similar event just a few flights prior to our flights. The write-up had been cleared by Maintenance the morning of our flights. We flew the aircraft twice this day. The first flight was from ZZZ to ZZZ1 which we completed without any incident. However, the erroneous indications returned during the second flight from ZZZ1 to ZZZ2. After our conclusion, we returned the aircraft to normal flight, transferred the controls, and landed without further incident. Possible transponder malfunction. We wrote up the aircraft and discussed the event in great detail with Maintenance.		[Report narrative contains no additional information.]		B737-800 flight crew reported Traffic Collision Avoidance System Malfunction.	
1764554	202010	0001-0600								VMC			Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Main Gear Wheel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1764554												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure	Procedure	8+00 Hours into flight received a Level 2 Alert *Tire Press*. Applied ECL and continued 4+15 to destination ZZZ. Coordinated with Dispatch, Maintenance, Standards and Tech Pilot. 1 Main Landing Gear Tire #10 indicated low pressure and demonstrated slow leak over final 4 hours of flight. Coordinated through Dispatch and ZZZ Tower for landing on Runway XX to maximize runway length, ease of runway vacate and interaction with maintenance for tire inspection upon landing. NOTE: Priority Handling never requested nor required. This report submitted to address confusion with ATC request for Souls on board and fuel remaining request to Company Dispatch and Duty Officer.				Captain reported confusion with ATC over procedures followed during a precautionary landing due to a slow tire pressure leak reported inflight.	
1764593	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000					Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Cargo Compartment Fire/Overheat Warning		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1764593												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	At Flight Level 390 about 150 north of ZZZ we received a Warning Aft Cargo Fire, notified ATC that we had an emergency situation and wanted to head back to ZZZ. Worked the checklist for AFT Cargo Fire warning, extinguished the fire bottle, fire warning went out after a few minutes. Continued to ZZZ for normal approach and landing. Fire Crew detected no heat coming from cargo area, continued to the gate and was met by Maintenance. Last I heard no fire but failed sensing. Flight crew did excellent job.				Air Carrier Flight Crew flying 777-200 aircraft reported aft cargo fire warning in flight.	
1765176	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121		Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Cockpit Window		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 115; Flight Crew Type 8500		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1765176	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 105		Situational Awareness; Distraction; Confusion			1765175	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	Other Takeoff Roll	Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	We started our takeoff roll at ZZZ. It was the FO's (First Officer's) leg. Shortly after the 'set takeoff thrust call' my window on the Captain's side started to slide open. I'd estimate our speed at 40 kts. It was barely open so I said 'continue' and reached up to close it. However, the window would not close and actually felt like it was coming out of the track. I assumed the window would easily close and that we still had plenty of time to safely reject at low speed if it wouldn't. At that point I became distracted and was worried that the window was falling in. At that point the FO asked if I wanted to reject and I said 'yes'. He initiated the reject as he saw that I was handling the window. As I brought my scan away from the window the reject was started and I noticed the airspeed was about 100 kts. The aircraft quickly decelerated as it was very light. I expected the airspeed to be less than 80 kts based on the short amount of time we had been rolling. The FO continued to stop the aircraft. At that point I took controls. He called the Tower and I made a PA to the passengers to remain seated. We then taxied off the runway at Taxiway X and coordinated with Maintenance to have the brakes checked. They gave us a thumbs up and we taxied to the gate. At that point the window was stuck in a half open position. We shutdown at the gate and switched to another aircraft. Rejecting immediately when the window started to slide open would have prevented the distraction and the rejection would have started at a slower speed. I assumed two things: that the window would easily close and that we were still below 80 kts when the decision was made. It may have been, but at the light weight the aircraft accelerated quickly and I think by the time the actions began we were over 80 kts.		At approximately 40 kts during takeoff roll, Captain side window slid open. Captain called continue and attempted to close the window. A few seconds later I looked over and saw the Captain was struggling to get it closed. I stated that I recommend we reject and he agreed. His hands were still on the window to prevent it from slamming back so I initiated the reject procedure. I did not notice the top airspeed, but I would estimate visually about 100 kts, autobraking did engage. Aircraft came to a stop abeam Taxiway X. Notified the Tower. We were instructed to exit on Taxiway. We stopped on Taxiway and called Company. Maintenance met us on ramp and checked the brakes. We taxied in. Window was unable to be shut. When Maintenance came to the flight deck and were able to move the window forward the handle seemed loose and the squeeze grip seemed loose so we all conferred and removed the aircraft from service.I specifically remember observing the Captain check his window just prior to calling for the before takeoff checklist so I think it was secure. We should have rejected as soon as the window slid open to prevent from getting to the higher speed. The aircraft was light so acceleration was very rapid.		B737 Flight Crew reported cockpit window opening during takeoff roll.	
1765183	202010		ZZZ.Tower	US			50			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B EWR																								Autoflight Yaw Damper		X	Failed																																					Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 96; Flight Crew Total 8178; Flight Crew Type 8178					1765183												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff and about 50 ft high, I heard the Yaw damper switch snapped into the off position, with a master caution flight control light on. With time permitting I ran the QRH yaw damper off checklist. When I reset the yaw damper switch back on the aircraft did a abrupt yaw to the left and the yaw damper switch snapped off again. We asked ATC for radar vectors to stay within the Airport area to work out the yaw damper problem. After talking to maintenance and dispatch about the yaw damper failure, Flight planned cruise altitude FL400, thunderstorms and rain at the destination we decided to return back to ZZZ. ATC gave us a visual approach [Runway] XX, on base leg turn 1,300 ft Tower gave us a low altitude alert. We landed safely and wrote up the Yaw Damper.				B737 First Officer reported Yaw Damper failure caused an air turnback and precautionary landing.	
1765275	202010	1201-1800															Air Carrier	A320			Part 121		Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																										Cockpit Door		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1765275												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight		Aircraft	Aircraft	Enroute on Aircraft X from ZZZZ to ZZZ we were unable to open the flight deck door. The flight deck crew had requested a bathroom break. The Captain went out and returned normally. However, when the First Officer called to return to the flight deck, the Lead Flight Attendant was unable to open the door. The bottom latch was not unlatching, while the upper latch and mid latch were unlatching normally with the door handle. The flight attendant tried using a metal key to pry the latch open. The Captain tried the unlock switch on the center pedestal. Nothing worked. We communicated with the First Officer and told him the flight deck door was stuck locked. The only option left to us was to pull the pins on the emergency/blowout panel on the door. When this was accomplished the opening allowed the flight attendant to exit the flight deck and the First Officer to return to the flight deck. This was done discreetly but was likely witnessed by some passengers in first class. Once the First Officer was inside the flight deck, the panel was reinstalled and secured with the pins. The rest of the flight continued normally. However, both the Captain and First Officer had to exit the flight deck using the panel once the flight was completed (this was accomplished after all passengers had deplaned) as the flight deck door remain stuck closed and locked. A write up was made in the Maintenance Logbook and the aircraft was turned over to Maintenance. Nothing more to add.The flight crew handled the unusual situation professionally.				Pilot flying A320 aircraft reported Flight Deck door stuck in closed position inflight.	
1765382	202010	0601-1200	SAN.Airport	CA	090	30		20000					Daylight			Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent		Class A ZLA																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer									Class A ZLA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 7500		Situational Awareness			1765382	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60					1765632	Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	We were cleared to descend via the arrival except maintain 12,000 ft. We were cruising at 24,000 ft northeast of MOMAR. We began to experience turbulence that resembled wake. At that point in time I was under the impression we were cleared to 12,000 ft. I selected Altitude Intervention to begin a descent out of the turbulence. I selected Altitude Intervention a second time to begin the descent. The crossing attitudes were erased at MOMAR due to selecting ALT Intervene. During both presses of the ALT Intervene the Captain and I VVMI'd.  We descended below the published altitude at MOMAR.		We briefed the arrival/approach as scheduled and then almost 40 minutes later we began our descent, and inadvertently double pushed and deleted the crossing restrictions at MOMAR. I didn't catch it until we were already out of 24,000 ft.		B737 Flight Crew reported overshooting an altitude restriction in descent due to a programming error.	
1765392	202010	0001-0600	ZZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Dawn				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch			Y	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing																		Autoflight System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 172; Flight Crew Total 16938; Flight Crew Type 15354					1765392	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1765639	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	The aircraft had preexisting maintenance issues when we received it for dispatch. There was a Class II Flight/Control maintenance message that was defferred. The number 3 IRU was differed from an inbound write up that stated the 'Capt Mach/Airspeed Inop and Nav/Alt discrepancy repeat writeup went to Air Data Capt3 position.' The MEL required that IRU 3 be maintained in the off position. Finally, the First Officer noted a skid mark on the number 1 tire that was subsequently found to be within limits by maintenance. Our Flight was cleared for take off with the Captain flying and Tower indicating that there was a 5 knot tailwind. The 100 knots call out had not yet been made and the thrust levers had been set to FLX/MCT when we received a Level II ECAM. The Captain glanced at the ECAM and noted an Auto Flight Auto Thrust Off ECAM. The Captain elected to continue the take off and to set TOGO thrust. The Captain's thinking at this point was that an Auto Thrust Failure would not affect the ability of the aircraft to fly and that setting TOGO would help with the tailwind. The First Officer then stated that there was another ECAM which indicated an Auto Flight Rudder Travel Limit System Failure. At this point, the V1 call out had not yet been made so the Captain elected to abort the take off. The Captain's thinking at this point was that there was a question now as to the ability of the aircraft to fly since the additional ECAM was potentially a flight control issue and the fact that both ECAMs were received after thrust power had been set. The Captain performed a rejected take off procedure. The aircraft appeared to be under normal control with no other apparent issues so just prior to bringing the airplane to a stop, the Captain instructed the First Officer to make a PA announcement for the passengers to remain seated. The Captain wanted to clear the runway at Taxiway X which is approximately halfway down the 10,187 foot runway but noted to the First Officer that it appeared that the aircraft was not moving forward easily after coming to a stop. At this point the First Officer and Captain discussed the possibility of a tire failure as a potential reason for the difficulty in moving the aircraft but it had not felt like a tire failed. We decided to tell the tower we were going to maintain our position on the runway until we could figure out what the issue was. At this point, we received a call from the flight attendants concerning a faint rubbery/acrid odor that was perceptible in the cabin. The Captain noted that the brake temperatures were still between approximately 450 and 550 degrees Celsius, although increasing, with no other abnormal system indications. The Captain and First Officer discussed that the smell was most likely from the aircraft tires as a result of the rejected takeoff and informed the flight attendants and passengers. The Captain was subsequently able to move the aircraft forward surmising that the aircraft may have been in a low spot on the runway that may have caused the difficulty in moving forward. As the Captain moved the aircraft slowly forward he noted that the aircraft was now moving freely and that there did not appear to be any issues with the tires. The First Officer requested from tower to clear the runway at Taxiway D, we completed our checklists, briefed our passengers and coordinated clearance to hardstand parking Spot 20 so that maintenance could look at the aircraft's condition. Upon arriving at parking Spot 20, the Captain talked with maintenance over the intercom and asked them to inspect the tires and the rest of the aircraft as a result of the rejected take off. Maintenance indicated that there were no visible issues except for the fact that the original skid mark on the number one tire was now larger. After maintenance inspected the aircraft exterior they brought the air-stairs up to door 1L and boarded the aircraft to run maintenance system checks. The wheelswere chocked and the parking brake was released to help cool the brakes. The Captain and Station Manager discussed deplaning the passengers and returning them to the terminal to await the results of the maintenance procedures. The Station Manager did an outstanding job of taking care of our passengers during the deplaning process and also provided our passengers with a complementary meal voucher. The Captain subsequently communicated with Maintenance Control, dispatch and the Duty Manager concerning the rejected take off. The Flight and Cabin crew also debriefed each other concerning the rejected take off. The Captain and First Officer discussed with Maintenance Control what the maintenance plan would be for the aircraft since we were still scheduled to take the aircraft to ZZZ1 and there appeared to be multiple maintenance issues. Maintenance Control indicated that they would perform various checklists and resets. The First Officer advocated that if time permitted that we should attempt to bring in a 320 Systems specialist if possible to answer some of our concerns including why we received the ECAMS after what we thought should have been an inhibited time period after take-off power was set. ALPA Safety reached out to the Captain during this time period so the Captain asked if ALPA Safety could connect the flight crew with an A320 systems specialist to talk about these issues which they did. Maintenance Control subsequently cleared all the maintenance issues with the exceptions of deferring the Auto Thrust system and Class II maintenance messages for Flight/Control and Air Bleed. Maintenance also changed out the number 1 tire due to the skid mark being worse after the rejected take off. We were subsequently dispatched again from ZZZ to ZZZ1 on the same aircraft. Just after take-off roll and beginning the climb out, with the Captain flying, the Captain lost his airspeed and mach indications. The Captain transferred control of the aircraft to the First Officer and the First Officer continued the climb out. The Flight then received a Nav/Alt Discrepancy ECAM. We performed the checklist. In order for us to restore the Captain's Airspeed/Mach indication we had to select Air Data Capt3 for the Captain just like the previous write up. We also had to select the First Officers Auto Pilot 2 since the Captains Auto pilot 1 would level off 500 feet off altitude. We Contacted Dispatch to get a phone patch to Maintenance Control and told Maintenance Control we were still having Air Speed, Mach and altimeter issues for the Captains side and wrote up the issues. We then proceeded to ZZZ1 with no further issues.		Rejected takeoff due to multiple ECAMs which were triggered and addressed as we transitioned from low speed to high speed regime.  The reject actions were as per SOP and the flight attendants thanked us for a prompt Remain Seated, Remain Seated notification.  Taxi back to ramp and a multi-hour maintenance delay ensued.		Flight Crew reported recurring ECAM messages after a rejected takeoff and subsequent maintenance action on the next flight.	
1765688	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US													Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb																										Hydraulic System Pump		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1765688	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1765804	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately 3 minutes after takeoff just after clean up received a HYD (Hydraulic) B ELEC PUMP LO PR (Low Pressure) ECAM message. Performed ECAM actions, follow ups, [requested priority handling], and returned to ZZZ. Extended vectors to land below max landing weight. Cleared runway, checked over by ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Fire Fighting), taxied to gate. Additionally APU was inoperative. HYD pump failure.Cause is unknown it was a maintenance issue.		ECAM Hydraulic B Electric Pump Low Pressure. Ran fix strategy. Decided to return to ZZZ. Delay vectors to burn off fuel to avoid overweight landing. [Requested Priority Handling], safety vehicles inspected aircraft after landing and followed us to gate. Landed underweight runway XX, normal landing. Also APU was deferred.APU deferred. Safest course of action was to return to ZZZ.		B737 flight crew reported Hydraulic Pump Failure after takeoff.	
1766284	202010	1201-1800	DEN.Tower	CO			2000			VMC						Tower DEN	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach		Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 152; Flight Crew Total 4914; Flight Crew Type 4914		Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1766284												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Airport; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	On approach to 35R, we were direct FRONZ, cleared visual approach. We were on an angled intercept to final. Captain asked 7,000 ft. be set for FRONZ, I did and after leaving 8,000 ft., tower advised our callsign and low altitude alert. I replied to ATC that we were ok, meaning level and clear of terrain at 7,000 ft. We intercepted the glide path to a normal approach and landing. During the low altitude alert we had finished slowing from 190 to 170 knots assigned. Several runway changes and speed changes preceded this event. I believe the Captain was trying to have the airplane, on the assigned speed, desired configuration and omitted the class B floor as I did.				B737 flight crew reported low altitude alert from ATC on approach.	
1766493	202010							38000		VMC				CLR		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								TCAS Equipment		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Confusion; Distraction			1766493												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	While cruising at 38,000 feet in VFR conditions the TCAS RA alert sounded. The First Officer and I performed the memory items and initiated the commanded climb. I advised ATC of our situation and was told by the controller that no other aircraft were observed in our area. TCAS then commanded us to descend and we also complied while looking outside for any intruding aircraft. The alert then said we were clear and went away. We regained our original altitude with no further conversation with ATC. All appeared normal and we determined the alert was false. The TCAS gave us 2 more false alarms. The 2nd was level at 30,000 feet and the 3rd was at 3,000 feet. While being vectored for the ILS Approach. Once on the ground the faulty TCAS was written up. Maintenance and Dispatch were made aware of the problem. After talking to Maintenance I was informed the TCAS system had been a continuing problem with multiple write-ups. I believe we were the first to get an RA alert, but it would've helped if someone had given us a heads up.				A Flight Crew reported receiving false TCAS RA advisories and stated maintenance told them it was a recurring issue with their aircraft.	
1766598	202010	0001-0600	ZZZ.Tower	US			1000			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR		GPS; FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class B ZZZ																												Tower DEN		Helicopter			Part 91	VFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Distraction; Confusion			1766598												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Conflict Airborne Conflict			Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Aircraft; Procedure; Human Factors	Procedure	We departed on the RNAV Departure. The Captain was flying. I was Pilot Monitoring. Shortly after takeoff, while in a turn on the assigned lateral profile, Tower asked if we had helicopter traffic in sight at approximately our 2 o'clock position. I responded that we had the green helicopter in sight. The helicopter was slightly higher than our altitude. Shortly thereafter we received an Amber TCAS TA alert followed immediately thereafter by a Red TCAS RA alert instructing us to continue our climb rate. The helicopter had made a left turn directly toward us. We responded according to the RA guidance to climb even though the helicopter was slightly higher than us. Additionally we added a turn to increase visual separation with the helicopter traffic as we climbed above them and they passed below us.Because I don't know what clearance the helicopter traffic had, I don't know what could have been done to prevent this incident. It does strike me as odd that the helicopter was flying so close to the charted SID lateral profile that low to the ground and that close to the departure runway.				B747-800 First Officer reported TCAS warnings during climb.	
1767173	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				11400					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																Beechcraft King Air Undifferentiated or Other Model																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1767173												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Airborne Conflict			Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Procedure	Ambiguous	ZZZ Center issued a clearance to climb and maintain 26,000 ft. At approximately 11,400 ft., we received a TCAS descending RA. The Pilot Flying without delay complied with the descending RA. The autopilot and auto throttle were disengaged, and a smooth adjustment to a pitch down attitude was completed to satisfy the RA command. The Pilot Flying followed this planned lateral flight path until approximately 10,500 ft. when the Clear of Conflict was heard. ZZZ Center was advised of the TCAS deviation.Some important points that I (Pilot Monitoring) and the First Officer (Pilot Flying) took away from this event includes knowing company policies and procedures. The Pilot Flying did not hesitate to comply with the TCAS RA. The teamwork between the roles of Pilot Flying and Pilot Monitoring worked. I can contribute this to our TCAS training. This real life TCAS RA with passengers onboard was just like the scenarios we receive in the simulators.[We also had] a concern regarding ZZZ Center. Our flight was never given any traffic advisories to be on the lookout for this aircraft. The TCAS equipment on the aircraft was our first signal that there was a traffic conflict. Only after ZZZ Center was notified of the TCAS RA, did ZZZ Center start issuing traffic advisories to another company flight behind us. This is my first time in XX years of flying Part 121 operations that I have come this close to another aircraft. When ATC asked if I could identify the aircraft, I had no trouble identifying it as a King Air. During this descending TCAS RA, the closest I saw the two aircraft apart was 500 ft.				A B737 pilot reported a near miss after following an automated advisory to descend. Pilot reported ATC did not issue a traffic advisory prior to incident.	
1767178	202010		ZZZ.Tower	US		2											Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach																										Flight Dynamics Navigation and Safety		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60		Confusion; Troubleshooting			1767178												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Aircraft	Aircraft	On short final into ZZZ Runway XXL, we received a GPWS 'Too Low Terrain' warning. We were on glideslope and on PAPI (VMC conditions). We executed a go-around and as we leveled off we received several error messages in the scratch pad [including] 'GPS L incorrect' and a 'Runway/Approach CRS ERROR'. The Captain was flying and could not get NAV data to display on his PFD (Primary Flight Display). He also was unable to tune the ILS frequency for the subsequent approach. We consulted the checklist, then transferred the controls to the First Officer whose displays and NAV data appeared to be reading correctly and hand flew an approach in VFR conditions to a landing without incident. There was a lot of confusion with multiple errors occurring in the cockpit. The GPWS that sounded while we were on the glideslope during short final seemed to be erroneous, but then again with all the errors with the NAV data on the Pilot Flying side, maybe his glideslope was incorrect. In the end, going around and troubleshooting put time on our side and allowed us to determine the best course of action.				A B737 pilot reported several erroneous warnings and display errors on the Captain's side, who was Pilot Flying, during short final. A successful go-around and landing was performed by the First Officer.	
1767231	202010	1201-1800	DEN.Airport	CO				14000		VMC						TRACON D01	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC; GPS	Initial Approach; Descent	STAR TBARR ONE	Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 110; Flight Crew Total 4571; Flight Crew Type 4571		Human-Machine Interface; Confusion			1767231	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 163; Flight Crew Total 3974; Flight Crew Type 3430		Human-Machine Interface; Confusion			1767623	Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were just outside the MNARK waypoint descending via the TBARR1 into Denver when I requested an unrestricted descent in order to expedite down through some turbulence. ATC granted my request and cleared us to descend and maintain 14,000 FT. I took the aircraft out of the managed descent mode and used open descent to descend to 14,000 FT. ATC subsequently amended our clearance as follows; cross BDIVN at or above 14,000 FT then descend via the TBARR1. The next waypoint after BDIVN is KUSHH which has an altitude restriction of 14,000 FT. The Pilot Monitoring (PM) put 13,000 in the FCU as the bottom of the TBARR1 is 13,000 FT at LDORA which is the next waypoint after KUSHH. I acknowledged the change to the altitude in the FCU but failed to put the aircraft back into a managed descent. We were between BDIVN and KUSHH when Denver Center switched us over to approach. As the PM checked in with approach I was expecting the aircraft to level off at 14,000 FT in order to meet the restriction by KUSHH. Just as the aircraft approached 14,000 FT two things happened simultaneously. One, I realized the aircraft wasn't leveling off as I expected, and two, approach cleared us to descend to 10,000 FT and turn left to a heading of 040. I don't believe there was an altitude deviation but I was certainly very fortunate that approach gave us a lower altitude when they did.		On TBARR1 arrival asked for expedited decent to 14,000 feet to get out of moderate turbulence. I put 14,000 feet in altitude window, captain started open descent. Next ATC transmission was a decent via following BDIVN intersection. I put 13,000 in altitude window. Approaching KUSHH intersection captain realized plane was not going to capture altitude to cross KUSHH at 14,000 feet. Managed descent was not selected. At same time got ATC frequency change and new instructions heading 020 descent to 10,000. I don't think we went below 14,000 feet, but would have if not given new ATC instructions while in an open descent Mode.		A320 flight crew reported failing to engage the correct descent mode upon receiving arrival clearance.	
1767276	202010	0001-0600	ZMA.ARTCC	FL				38000								Center ZMA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZMA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1767276	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1767284	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors; Weather	Weather	In Cruise Flight at FL380 approaching ZZZZZ north boundary of ZZZZ airspace we were denied right deviations for active military airspace. Left deviations at that point were no longer an option and appeared worse. We were on the western edge of a line of weather moving East we were deviating just slightly left of our course to go through what appeared to be the thinnest area of weather on our radar. I had made a PA and had all passengers and Flight Attendants seated. We experienced a brief 10 to 15 seconds of Moderate Turbulence which cause our Altitude to drop 200 ft. to about 37,800 ft. and the a gain of about 300 ft. to about 38,100 ft. and then settled back to level at FL380. We also experienced some airspeed loss and then gain briefly as well as some left bank. It felt much like wake turbulence briefly. Just then MIA center told us to maintain FL380 and contact ZZZZ; before leaving the frequency I stated we had experienced some moderate turbulence which had caused some altitude loss, a couple hundred feet, and that we were now level at FL380 and we were told 'roger' contact Center which we did. The remainder of the flight was uneventful and smooth to destination. [The cause of the event was that] a deviation 100's of miles earlier to the left would have been a better choice and I believe a couple of aircraft behind us after hearing my report to MIA Center deviated into the Military Airspace. I do not know if the aircraft behind me declared an Emergency to do so or not, as we were changing frequency to next center during the radio discussions. That may have been a better choice but I did not expect the turbulence to be that bad, but was very glad I sat the Flight attendants down prior, as well as making a very clear PA to the passengers that we were going to experience some Turbulence since we had to fly in the area of some weather and insisted that they be seated with their seatbelts securely fastened. An earlier plan for weather deviations 100's of mile away if necessary would have helped knowing any right deviations would be denied.		Level at 380 approaching XXXXX, there was some weather close to the intersection. Requested right deviation and Center informed that Warning Areas were active so unable right deviation. We encountered some moderate turbulence in the clouds for a few seconds. The airplane descended 200 ft. before returning to level. Miami informed to maintain 380 and to contact next center over ZZZZ which we did. Flight continued uneventfully.The cause of deviations was not being able to deviate due to Center's instructions.I suggest deviating ahead of time if known that no deviations allowed.		B737-800 flight crew reported moderate turbulence due to weather deviations being unavailable.	
1767434	202010	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Type 13000		Other / Unknown			1767434												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Dispatch; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	While at the gate pre flighting, I was informed that we were carrying an aircraft battery to ZZZ1 and was given two copies of the Notice to Captain (NOTAC) to sign. At that point, I had already reviewed our Release and signed it. I gave the Operations Agent a copy of the NOTAC and kept one myself. While at cruise altitude, I received an ACARS message from Dispatch informing me that the HAZMAT was not annotated in the remarks section of the Release in accordance with Bulletin. The Dispatcher and I made a pen and ink change to the Release and the Dispatcher informed he would report the event.				Captain reported in flight notification of Release document missing required Hazmat information.	
1768392	202010	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	360	10		28000					Night				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 145; Flight Crew Type 7448		Troubleshooting; Time Pressure			1768392	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 45; Flight Crew Type 21800		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1768421	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing out, approximately XX00Z, the left engine had loss of thrust associated with compressor stall / surges. Flight attendants rang the call button and indicated they heard loud banging and wanted to verify we heard the same. We shallowed our climb then leveled off at FL 280 and [advised ATC]. Subsequent flight attendant chime indicated they saw flames had extended aft of the #1 engine. We had already begun to tackle the surge / stall QRC followed by QRH checklist when they called advised of flames. We recognized and agreed that ZZZ was our best option due to proximity (80 miles roughly ) off our nose with optimum field capabilities. We coordinated with flight attendants and talked with a non-rev commuting Company Captain who had been sitting on left side of cabin and had seen the flames. During this process, we coordinated with ATC for direct and began a descent to ZZZ [and] fired off a quick message to Dispatch indicating engine malfunction and plan to divert. We coordinated with crew of the precautionary landing and advised passengers of our intentions. In the descent we completed the appropriate checklists, acquired the [weight and balance] data for landing in addition to brake cooling then were vectored for a visual approach to [Runway] XXL. We landed uneventfully and taxied clear of the runway and we were met by Fire and Rescue. We shut down the #1 engine and awaited CFR to evaluate. CFR gave us the all clear and we taxied to gate and began to coordinate with all appropriate personnel.		Normal climbout passing FL 282.  Initially heard banging outside of aircraft followed by a reduction in climb, decreasing airspeed and aircraft vibration.  Immediately followed by a call from the A F/A (flight attendant) reporting flame emanating from #1 engine.  Engine problem was confirmed in the cockpit by high temp (800 EGT) and diminished N1.  My F/O (First Officer) immediately notified ATC that we have an engine issue and need to maintain FL 280. I told him to [request priority] at that time. I reduced the thrust lever to observe changes in engine indications. The vibrations ceased and temperature reduction. We both believed we had a compressor stall. We were holding FL 280 on one engine, albeit with excessive thrust on #2 engine.  F/O asked for lower and we were cleared to FL 240. We had two jumpseaters onboard, so I got an experienced set of eyes on the engine.  He confirmed all that we knew and that the engine no longer had flames coming from the tailpipe. Assessing the non-normal together, I called for the QRC (quick reference checklist) Engine Limit or Surge or Stall Checklist.  The QRH (quick reference handbook) confirmed our assessment. We completed down to Step X (QRH) at that point. An engine shutdown was not required, but thrust lever remained at idle for remainder of flight.  ZZZ was 80 miles from our present position and we agreed was the nearest suitable airport.  ATC cleared us direct. While I was PF (pilot flying), F/O communicated with F/A's and passengers.  I sent a message to Dispatch about our diversion.  We ran the Diversion Checklist.  F/O ran weight and balance data for a 'One Engine Inoperative' approach and landing data.  We planned for and flew a 'One Engine Inoperative Landing'.  An uneventful landing in ZZZ culminated with a check of the engine/damage by Emergency Personnel.  We taxied under our own power to the gate.		B737 flight crew reported engine compressor stall and surges resulted in divert to precautionary landing.	
1769046	202010	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				2300		IMC	Turbulence; Icing; Rain		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR		FMS Or FMC	Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Human-Machine Interface; Time Pressure; Workload			1769046	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Time Pressure			1769055	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Unanticipated GPWS warning on approach requiring crew action. We were flying the freighter (tail), second leg ZZZ-ZZZ1 (1.5 hr delayed at start of day), 5-6 hours into the flight duty period (~XA:00 pm local time). Night IMC w/moderate to heavy rain showers in vicinity. I was Pilot Flying PF (Captain).Clearance was ZZZ...ZZZ1. On the ground at ZZZ we planned and briefed the departure and RNAV (RNP) Rwy XX from the ZZZZZ1 transition. De-iced, taxied, departed, climbed to cruise at FL280.Enroute to ZZZZZ1 at cruise, Center warned of areas of moderate to extreme precipitation from ZZZZZ1 fix to the airport and advised of better weather to the Northeast. After discussing a change to the arrival, we both agreed to request radar vectors to the approach segment from ZZZZZ (IAF). Center agreed to vector us around and down to put us on the published portion of the arrival with the stated intention of joining us to the approach path at ZZZZZ2. FO (First Officer) made the changes in the FMS and we briefed and then ran the Decent Checklist Change Triangles.Radar vectors included a descent and vectors resulting in established on published segment near ZZZZZ2 at 5000 and given approach clearance from center (I had directed PM to reset FMS Cruise Alt to 8,000 feet when we were on vectors). Once established near ZZZZZ2 at 5,000 cleared us for the approach. I engaged LNAV/VNAV and set airport elevation, but was high on the vertical path. I continued to slow to final configuration (gear down, Flaps 40) by ZZZZZ1, stable, but still 1,400 feet above the path in VNAV SPD (vertical navigation speed). After the turn at ZZZZZ1 I set 1,600 feet in the MCP ALT window (FAP 1,600 MSL) and at approximately 2,300 MSL, engaged Vertical Speed down in order to regain the VNAV Path before the FAP. As I approached the path, I reset MCP altitude to airfield elevation and engaged VNAV while pitch mode was in ALT ACQ (altitude acquire), resulting in an unrecognized mode change to V/S (2,100 ft/min) and projected vertical path well before the airport. It was at this point, we received a GPWS 'Caution Terrain' followed shortly by GPWS Warning 'Terrain Fly UP.'  I immediately executed an escape maneuver/go-around, climbing and flying the go-around flight path to ZZZ2 and mistakenly climbed to 5,000 MSL (missed approach alt 3,000 MSL), entering the hold as published. I requested and was given a descent to 3,000 MSL in holding.We then built and briefed the RNAV (RNP) Rwy XX from ZZZ2, ran the Descent Change Triangles, received our approach clearance, and executed the approach to landing uneventfully. The FO and I thoroughly debriefed the events of the flight. I then called the Duty Pilot and DISPATCH to report the incident. I should have requested radar vectors ZZZZZ1 for the best opportunity to acquire the VNAV path. I allowed the Controller to compress and to keep me high on my approach, and reducing my situational awareness. I configured very early, but it still wasn't enough to regain the VNAV path. In the future, I will not allow the ATC Controller to dictate the terms of my energy management and will give myself the Flight Handbook recommended 3 miles per 1,000 descent planning as a minimum starting point.Because we compressed our arrival and ended up high above the VNAV path I attempted to use the Vertical Speed method on LNAV/VNAV Approach rules to regain VNAV path prior to the FAP. Had I cross-checked my FMA after selecting VNAV, I would have noticed the V/S mode and perhaps executed an uneventful go-around. In the future, I will make sure to always verify my FMA mode on the PFD after making a selection on the MCP.Lastly, after fully configuring and still not descending to the path, I might have been able to acquire VNAV path prior to the FAP using Speed Intervention. There was chance we were light enough (low Ref Speed) to accelerate the idle pitch descent to regain the path, ensuring the pitch FMA remained in VNAV and avoiding the unrecognized and inadvertent V/S mode descent that caused the GPWS Warning. Recommend expanding the understanding of VNAV speed during initial and continuing qualification training in the B737.		Escape Maneuver occurred at approximately (time) local time on (date). Flight conditions at the time were night, IMC, rain, light turbulence.Leg 2 of a planned 4 leg day. Leg 1 was delayed 1 hours due to cargo loading. The flight was uneventful. Deiced in ZZZ after aircraft pushback. Filed route was ZZZ...ZZZ1. We planned and briefed the RNAV Rwy XX from ZZZZZ. Cruise was at FL280. PF (pilot flying) MFD was in WX Radar mode due to significant weather in the area. PM (pilot monitoring) was in TERR (terrain) mode with VSD (vertical speed) displayed. Cruise was at dusk. Shortly after starting the descent, ATC notified us that he saw an area of Moderate to Extreme precipitation ahead of us. ATC said it looked clearer to the north and asked if we would like vectors to the north for an approach via ZZZZZ1. We responded yes and he started vectoring us north. PF flew while PM loaded the arrival from ZZZZZ1 (via IAF ZZZZZ1). PM briefed the new approach and called for change triangles, which we accomplished. ATC had us do intermediate level-offs at 8,000 feet, then 7,000 feet. PF asked PM to reset the cruise altitude to provide an accurate flight path. In anticipation of being high on flight path, PF configured early to flaps 40. PM was actively monitoring the VSD and flight path, and requested lower when able from ATC. ATC said he would give us lower in a few miles. He then gave us a short vector prior to ZZZZZ2 with a descent to 6,500 feet. At approx 0.5 miles before ZZZZZ2 at 6,500 feet, ATC cleared us 'to cross ZZZZZ2 at or above 5,000 feet, cleared RNAV Rwy XX at ZZZ1'. When cleared for the approach over ZZZZZ2, PF dialed in field elevation in the MCP and descended towards the flight path. Flight conditions were night, IMC, rain, light turbulence. Approximately 1 mile prior to ZZZZZ3, we were still high on flight path so PF verbalized and set Vertical Speed down and reset MCP to 1,600 feet. PM saw the downward vector on the VSD and monitored it for approaching the flight path. Almost immediately thereafter, we received a 'Caution, Terrain' and the depicted island terrain went yellow on PM's MFD. This was quickly followed by a 'Terrain, Pull up' and the depicted terrain on the MFD went red. PF called for go-around and executed the escape maneuver. After aircraft was climbing, PF asked PM to reset MCP for missed approach altitude of 3,000 feet. PF initially had difficulty verifying the missed approach altitude on the EFB due to turbulence, transition to nighttime, and prioritizing monitoring the aircraft state while performing aircraft cleanup. PF put and verbalized 5,000 feet in the MCP window, thinking 5,000 feet was better in case 3,000 feet was incorrect. PMs intent was to devote more brain cells to performing the aircraft cleanup items and monitoring the safe aircraft state as we climbed. Aircraft was cleaned up, leveled at 5,000 feet and continued to holding at ZZZ2. We then queried ATC for a descent to 3,000 feet, setup the RNAV Rwy XX from ZZZ2. Approach was briefed by Captain, flown, and landed uneventfully. Once the aircraft reached parking and shutdown at ZZZ1, Captain and FO debriefed the events. Captain called and briefed Duty Officer, who approved continuing to the remaining 2 destinations. Captain also called and spoke with Dispatch. The remaining flights to were flown with minimal changes. Post flight, the Captain called Quality Assurance to see what they could tell us, so that we could learn further from this experience.Stay on the magenta line, and if vectored off, get back on it as early as possible. Even though the controller had plans to vector us back onto the RNP approach, a better outcome likely would have resulted if we started at the IAP. This way we would have been able to descend on a normal flight path, with adequate time and pacing, vs. having to stay higher due to the controllers higher vectoring altitudes. Request the IAP next time.At nighttime in IMC, I need to have a faster response timecalling for the escape maneuver/go-around. I should clarify this maneuver more, particularly the triggers. I should have been the first to call for it since the Captain was the PF. Instead of trying to salvage a bad or difficult approach, make the decision to go-around earlier. Memorize the missed approach altitude prior to each approach, or ensure my EFB chart is not zoomed-in so far that I cannot see it without a quick glance.Clarify various 'what-if scenarios' on RNP approaches, Escape Maneuvers, Go-Arounds. If I haven't flown as much recently, spend extra time clarifying. Communicate with Center that 6,500 feet at ZZZZZ2 creates a very difficult approach to accomplish. Recommend to them that vectoring to the IAF is likely a better option.		B737-700 flight crew reported executing an escape maneuver due to a CFIT event resulting from a compressed approach clearance. Crew completed subsequent approach in an uneventful landing.	
1769614	202011	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121		Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Engine Control		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1769614												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	Other Takeof Roll	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	The number 2 engine did not spool up on takeoff. The takeoff was immediately rejected with maximum speed of 30 knots. Returned to gate and aircraft was taken out of service by maintenance.				B737-800 flight crew reported rejecting takeoff due to engine malfunction.	
1771134	202011	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				7300		VMC				CLR		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Confusion; Distraction; Situational Awareness; Workload			1771134	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1771144	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Airspace Structure; Human Factors	Procedure	The Captain and I discussed not doing a Visual Approach. Passing through about 9,000 feet, we were going direct to the FAF; conditions where ceiling and visibility unlimited. Approach cleared us for a Visual Approach. We accepted and started descending to 7,000 feet, the altitude for FAF. At about 7,300 or 7,200 feet ATC advised us the Minimum Vectoring Altitude was 7,500 feet. The Captain immediately stopped descending and went to 7,500 feet until just outside the FAF. We made a normal stable approach.We didn't want to do a Visual Approach when we briefed it. We were lured in by the visual conditions and the fact that that everything was stable and non rushed. It was late. Lesson is, do not do night visuals at airports that you are not to familiar with, try to do what you brief especially around terrain. I did get distracted when I lost my flight deck WiFi when I was trying to pull up a new ATIS on iPad. Don't do visual approaches at night, especially near terrain. Fly what you brief.		While descending on the arrival and approximately 10 miles south of ZZZ, ATC asked us to report the field in sight. It was clear and unlimited visibility. So we called it. When cleared visual [Runway] XXL, I began a descent out of 9,000 feet. I felt that we were within the airspace, but when descending to the FAF altitude of 7,000 feet and at approximately 7,200 feet, we got a call from ATC saying altitude alert. Climb to 7,500 [feet] min vectoring altitude. We immediately climbed to 7,500 [feet] and continued approach. ATC handed us to Tower and nothing else said.During cruise, my First Officer and myself went through all company pages and arrival and approach plates. We discussed terrain and even said that we shouldn't accept a visual approach since it was night time. However, we got direct to FAF and not a usual vector outside the FAF. ATC asked us to report the field. We both evaluated that we were comfortable accepting the visual. We should have stayed with the plan because after being cleared approach, we descended below the ATC min vectoring altitude. Bottom line, be conservative and when flying approaches at night, I will no longer accept a visual approach.		B737-800 Flight Crew reported they descended below the Minimum Vectoring Altitude while conducting a night time Visual Approach.	
1771925	202011	1201-1800	LAX.Airport	CA						VMC						Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B LAX																												Tower LAX	Air Carrier	Heavy Transport		2		IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Final Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 57					1771925												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Procedure	On approach to 25L at LAX, ATC vectored a heavy Airbus onto approach in front of us. When I expressed concern for wake turbulence ATC stated only 3.5 miles was required between us. Since there was a tailwind on the approach and calm wind on surface I advised ATC that I'd like to slow to final approach speed to get more spacing with the heavy. On handoff to Tower, Approach Controller again reiterated that 3.5 miles was sufficient with a heavy Airbus. I thanked him and switched to Tower. We ended up with 4-5 miles separation and still experienced wake turbulence on short final.				B737 Captain reported ATC was uncooperative when he requested more spacing to avoid wake turbulence on approach to LAX.	
1772029	202011	1801-2400	DEN.Airport	CO												TRACON D01	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS; Localizer/Glideslope/ILS Runway 16R	Final Approach	Visual Approach; STAR TBARR ONE	Class B DEN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Training / Qualification	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1772029												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	ATC cleared us for a visual approach to Runway 16R into Denver after turning us on to a right base approximately 5 miles from MERYN intersection on the ILS 16R. After being cleared for the visual approach the First Officer (FO) selected the approach mode and a selected a slower speed. The FO was slow to turn towards MERYN and over shot the final approach course. I instructed to FO to turn immediately toward the runway and then back to the final approach course. The approach controller asked if we need a vector and I declined. The FO had previously briefed the ILS to 35R. During the approach briefing we discussed threats that included high elevation, an MEL that restricted RNAV approaches, and that we were tired. The flight was cleared to descend via the TBARR 1 RNAV Arrival with the Runway 16R transition. The transition ends at CLFFF intersection at 11,000' with a speed of 210 knots. ATC was informed that we could not accept an RNAV approach and we were told to expect a visual approach, which we backed up with the ILS approach. The flight was turned to a right base heading at 210 knots. While turning the FO requested a slower speed which ATC approved. ATC asked if we had the airport insight. When I replied that we had the runway insight we were cleared for the visual approach. The FO selected the approach mode. As the FO was selecting a slower speed I mentioned that she should turn toward MERYN intersection. She slowly selected a heading toward MERYN and over shot the final approach course. I think this was due to the angle in which the course was intercepted. I would suggest adding a discussion about accepting visual approaches to the approach briefing, as a possible threat.				Captain reported overshooting final approach course on a visual approach into Denver.	
1772434	202011	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US									Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Cockpit Window		X	Failed																																					Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 190; Flight Crew Type 15000		Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Physiological - Other; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1772434												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged	Aircraft	Aircraft	Captain's window opened and detached from aircraft during climbout. All checklists were completed, perhaps they were not completed in normal sequence due to severe noise associated with window detachment. Aircraft configuration was fulfilled after much consternation due to nearly nil communication due to wind noise and headset loss.				B737 Captain reported that the Captain's side window opened and became detached during climbout.	
1772437	202011	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				5000		IMC	Turbulence; Windshear; 2		Night	3000			Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach																																Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer																																		Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 2500		Workload			1772437												Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted	Weather	Weather	There was a line of storms crossing ZZZ.  We were told to expect holding at ZZZZZ.  We ended up holding.  We did one turn and then we got sent back on the arrival.  We ultimately got vectors from ZZZZZ1 and were following one Company aircraft.  The plan was to fly northeast about 30 miles at 5,000 ft to go around the weather and then come back south to land.  We couldn't get in front of it or through it.  Company turned around to try to get around the weather from the other direction.  We did the same thing.  We got a 270 heading and a descent to 4,000 ft and an assigned speed of 170 knots.  On that vector, I noticed airspeed decreasing and added some power.  Then the airspeed dropped to approximately 140 knots and the autopilot and autothrottles were disconnected.  I announced to the PM (Pilot Monitoring) that we were in windshear and I added power and started a climbing turn to the left.  The aircraft started accelerating and climbing, and we retracted the flaps.  The highest speed I saw during the recovery was 220 knots.  During this period, airspeed, pitch attitude and altitude was very difficult to control and we encountered what we believed to be severe turbulence.  The PM told ATC that we had severe turbulence and windshear and that we were turning to the left and climbing.  We tried to level at 5,000 ft and flew southwest.  ATC told us to climb to 6,000 ft for traffic.  I added power and the aircraft responded poorly.  Eventually, we got a climb going, but we were unable to stop the climb at 6,000 ft.  The aircraft continued to about 6,450 ft and ATC asked us what altitude we were going to.  PM told them that we were trying to get to 6,000 ft but were having a difficult time maintaining altitude with the turbulence.  On our way back down to 6,000 ft we got a TCAS TA for traffic at our 1-2 o'clock at 700-800 ft above us.  I continued down to about 5,800 ft to make sure we avoided that traffic.  We continued southwest and diverted to ZZZ1. If we had asked to stay in holding until we saw that the line had passed even further northeast of the field, it would have prevented this.  We had about 13.0 of fuel when we entered holding, so we had the time. [We were] unable to maintain assigned altitude, heading, and airspeed due to windshear and severe turbulence.				Air carrier First Officer reported the flight encountered wind shear and severe turbulence associated with a line of storms resulting in altitude/airspeed excursions, a conflict and a divert.	
1774443	202011	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000			VMC	Turbulence; Windshear; Icing		Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1774443	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Flight Instructor; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1774446	Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Company Policy; Procedure	Company Policy	While departing ZZZ Runway X on the RVAV departure, we experienced a wind shear ahead alert shortly after rotation and after the gear was selected up. While climbing through approximately 500 feet AGL, we entered an area of moderate to severe turbulence followed by a gain and loss of 20+ knots of airspeed. Airspeed excisions were brief in nature, and climb rate decreased from near 3,000 FPM to 1,000 FPM and momentarily less while we transited the wind shear. During this time we also experienced multiple quick rolling and yawing moments that required nearly full control deflection to counter.  Both myself and my First Officer agreed that the aircraft was at times momentarily out of control. It is my recollection that the maximum intensity of the turbulence occurred from 500 feet AGL to approximately 3,500 feet AGL. Again these excursions were very quick left and right rolling and yawing motions. During the event, the aircraft energy state never decayed to a dangerous level, however, we were in a lightly loaded -700 with max thrust selected for wind shear avoidance.  Above 4,000 feet, we experienced continuous moderate turbulence and icing up to 14,000 feet, and only light turbulence above that.  There were no injuries reported, and we did not suspect any aircraft damage. The flight attendants and passengers were briefed before takeoff. Before departure, both myself and the First Officer consulted the Airport Wind System reports as well as reviewed the required wind limits for the departure. The ZZZ Tower and Dispatch were contacted to get a better assessment of what they were seeing with the winds as well.  At the time of the beginning of the takeoff roll, the Tower reported steady state wind from the south east 28 KT gusting to 38 KT. The Airport Wind System alert was not functioning, however,  [other weather station's] wind and the gust 2-6 Airport Wind System was reporting. All wind reports were within limits at the time of takeoff, and had been for most of the morning. The Dispatcher reported the 8 High Airport Wind System Alert was intermittent all morning. Both myself and my First Officer briefed the departure and took steps necessary to mitigate the perceived threats. Wind shear take off procedures and power settings were used during the take off.  After level off, myself and the First Officer consulted the FOM and we were in agreement that we experienced a situation where the aircraft was momentarily not in positive control. A pilot report of MOD to SEV turbulence was relayed to ARTCC as well as company. Dispatch was consulted at this time, and a plan was made to divert to ZZZ1 for maintenance inspection as Maintenance did not want the aircraft stuck in ZZZ2. The remainder of the flight was uneventful. Ensuring Airport Wind System has full functionality and wind profiles are working to the greatest extent possible.		Preparing for departure from ZZZ we looked at weather reports including the ZZZ Airport Wind System and PIREPS.  Nothing indicated that we would encounter anything greater than moderate turbulence as we took off from Runway X. Shortly after liftoff we encountered moderate turbulence and at about 1,000 ft AGL received a 'Windshear Ahead' alert.  We then experienced approximately 30 - 45 seconds of severe turbulence. We gave ATC a PIREP and overflew our original destination of ZZZ2 at the request of Dispatch and Maintenance control to get the plane to a maintenance base.		B37-700 flight crew reported after encountering severe turbulence and wind shear, it was decided to overfly the destination airport and divert to a maintenance base.	
1774635	202011	1801-2400	ZZZZ.Airport	FO													Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model			Part 121	IFR			Cruise		Class A ZZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Relief Pilot	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1774635	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1774398	Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Human Factors; Company Policy	Ambiguous	While preflighting a stub flight we received an amended flight plan from Dispatch. The Dispatcher said that there was a bend in the flight plan and that he wanted to remove that. Because this was a stub flight our application struggled to upload the correct flight plan. It kept wanting to upload our original flight plan. The PACOTS east routing (Pacific Organized Track System) for that day was our original flight plan which had us departing the 130W longitude for DACEM onward to pick up the IRNMN arrival into LAX. However the Dispatcher rerouted us to fly from 140W bypassing 130W to begin an arrival using the RYDER arrival. This confused not only us but departure Area Control who had our original flight plan in their system. We departed the gate with the correct uploaded ATC flight plan but possessed on our iPads the amended flight plan from Dispatch... in addition our FO (First Officer) refused to coordinate with Dispatch to revise our our flight plan to its original format which resulted in erroneous flight progress forms from ACARS. To be clear the flight plan only deviated towards the latter end of the flight with the confusion resulting from the Dispatcher uploading an erroneous flight plan to remove a bend in the flight however that was not loaded to the PACOTS east routing for the day. Our FO contacted SFO radio to confirm our routing was correct with our FMS but neglected to inform Dispatch who thought we were still flying the amended route he had given us. It would be helpful if the company would provide guidance regarding how procedurally they would prefer crews coordinate changes to flight plans with ATC. Do they prefer we coordinate with ATC directly or would they prefer that we contact Dispatch and ask that they upload the correct route and that we check it with ATC. It would seem there is a loophole in regards to keeping Dispatch up to speed on changes to flight plans. I believe there is language in the FOM regarding any changes to the flight plan to notify Dispatch of those changes. In this case the changes were minimal however the arrival time differed by about 6-8 minutes from the original flight plan and the amended one from Dispatch. Dispatch was unaware that this stub flight was not flying the amended flight plan they sent us. When I, as the relief pilot, arrived from my break on the flight deck I noticed the erroneous flight progress forms and notified Dispatch who then sent us the revised flight plan. Dispatch was unaware that we were back on our original PACOTS routing. This was accomplished 2+45 into the flight. However if I had not done this it would not have gotten done as the FO refused to contact Dispatch to clear up the confusion. please advise. The First Officer refused input from both the Captain and myself, the relief pilot, to contact Dispatch and get them in the loop regarding discrepancies from filed flights from amended ones. Stub flights caused more confusion than necessary. Clear guidance is needed from company on how they prefer the crew to remedy route discrepancies from Dispatch versus filed. Do they prefer the crew to contact ATC and fix the problem ourselves or do they prefer Dispatch to get involved and fix the discrepancy.		Just prior to pushback, we received a message from Dispatch with regards to a change in our flight planned route. We delayed pushback until receiving new flight plan. Only change was to end of route after waypoint at 140W. We then called Clearance Delivery and did a full route verification. They still only had the original, unchanged route. Since the only discrepancy between the flight plans was at the end of the flight, we opted to depart. After departure, we received the flight progress report that showed the modified route (after 140W). During flight, after checking in with San Francisco ARINC, we asked for a verification of our cleared routed. ARINC verified it was the original flight planned route. We then sent a message to Dispatch with our cleared route and received an update. Dispatch believed we requested a look at modifying our route to remove a 'dog leg' segment from our route which we hadn't.  We were a STUB flight and that might have been a factor as the original flight was near to the arrival time. The change to our flight plan wasn't requested and wasn't essential to safety. Unless critical to flight safety, last minute changes to flight plans (especially international) can cause miscommunications and errors in navigation and should be avoided.		B-777 flight crew reported difficulty resolving discrepancies between the flight plan provided by Dispatch and the received clearance for a trans-Pacific flight.	
1774646	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				37000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Autothrottle/Speed Control		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1774646												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Traveling in level cruise at FL370, smooth air, Mach .80 we experienced an aircraft Mach speed exceedence. With no noticeable wind change the aircraft accelerated, uncommanded, up to Mach .84. The A/T system made minimal, if any, changes to the cruise power setting. The Captain (PF) immediately spun the Mach speed way back and extended full speed brakes. This was the point at which the A/T finally brought the power back. We stabilized the aircraft, configured the automation and continued to the destination with no other abnormalities.We possibly could have reacted more aggressively and sooner, but we fully expected the A/T system to respond correctly to the speed increase and reduce power accordingly. Aside from that, I cannot see anything else that could have prevented the situation.I don't know what we could change to prevent this situation again.				Air carrier First Officer reported a Mach speed exceedance during cruise.  First Officer stated the increase in speed was uncommanded and there was no noticeable wind change either.	
1775595	202012	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				2500					Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Climb	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								Elevator Trim System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 134; Flight Crew Type 5691		Distraction; Time Pressure			1775595												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the Pilot Flying. On climb-out I noticed my electric trim was not working. The First Officer's electric trim was also inoperative. We leveled off to assess the situation and complete the QRH checklist. We elected to return to ZZZ.				B737-700 Captain reported the Electric Pitch Trim failed on both the Captain's and First Officer's control columns.	
1775839	202012	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			700				strong tailwinds						Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Approach; Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1775839												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	While on the RNAVM XX in ZZZ1 we encountered a GPWS event. At the beginning of the approach we had a tailwind and the descent angle of the approach caused us to be a little fast starting the approach. As the pilot monitoring I should have caught this issue sooner and brought it to the attention of the PF (Pilot Flying). Because of the speed the PF disconnected the autopilot/auto throttles and slowed the plane to the proper approach speed. During the turn onto final the PF was visual with the runway and let the nose drop which caused a momentary don't sink. He corrected and the warning went away. Still with the airport visual he tried to correct on the VASI and received another momentary don't sink call at around 700 ft. We continued the approach and the landing was normal. I don't have anything to add for [this report].I have learned many lessons from this very short flight. I haven't had much ZZZ experience and my FO (First Officer) who was PF has had very little. We tried to mitigate the possible issues by having a slow cruise speed of 210 kts. and trying to get ready for the approach early. We briefed the 3.4 GP and felt we were in a good position for the approach. With the unfamiliarity of the situation and the weather in ZZZ this day I missed the strong tailwind that was making our situation worse. This put us behind and we had to play catch up. It was turbulent the whole flight and when he disconnected the AP/AT my [situational awareness] went down because I was worried about his flight path at that time. As I look back at the event and if I could do this again I would have us discontinue the approach early and come around to try again. Once we continued and got out of the [weather] and had the runway visually climbing back into it in that terrain did not seem the safest option. But if we had bailed early it would have made the situation easier the second time.				B737 Captain reported an uneventful landing after two GPWS alerts during approach due primary weather conditions.	
1776272	202011	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US							Turbulence					TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Captain				Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1776272												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	Have more of a warning from ATC of the turbulence expected to encounter. We did not know of the severity of the turbulence until we were in it ourselves. Experienced continuous moderate turbulence between 8,000 feet to 3,000 feet on descent on the RNAV arrival. We landed without incident.				Air carrier Captain reported unexpected moderate turbulence during descent.	
1776276	202011	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US						IMC							Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																										Coalescer Bag			Malfunctioning																																					Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1776276												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Be fast to recognize an air quality event.I was flying an A320 from ZZZ to ZZZ1. During the arrival we had an air quality event. The estimated duration of the event was 5 minutes. During the event the power levels did not change. I have experienced this in the same type of aircraft before. There was no evidence of smoke or fire in the cockpit and it was localized. The odor smelled like dirty socks and were mild. It was only in the cockpit. During the time of event, our air supply was the engines. I was able to turn off Pack 1 and isolate the problem within about 15 seconds. I reserve the right to change my statement as more informant becomes available.				A320 Captain reported a fumes event during descent which was described as a 'dirty socks' odor. The flight continued to its destination after the number 1 Pack was turned off.	
1776883	202012	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				16000			Icing						Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb; Descent																										Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1776883	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1776887	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climb out to ZZZ both airspeed disagreement cautions came on with +/- 10 knot difference between the Captain and FO (First Officer) airspeed [indicators]. Once at cruise all cautions went away and we continued. Upon descent and on the arrival we again got the 'airspeed disagreement' caution for about 5 minutes then it again disappeared. Continuing on the descent at around 16,000 ft. and in icing conditions the Captain's airspeed went to +/- 50 knots causing the throttles to chase the airspeed. I turned off the autopilot, autothrottles and both flight directors as per the QRH and quickly realized the FO side was working fine. The FO continued the descent.		Climbing out, passing approximately FL280 in IMC conditions, with the CA (Captain) flying, we received an 'IAS DISAGREE' indication. There was a split of approximately 10 KIAS between the CA and FO (First Officer) instruments with a corresponding split in Mach. I, the FO, got out the unreliable airspeed checklist in the QRH to look up the possible indications. After a few moments the indication disappeared and the IAS returned to normal. After beginning a descent the same indications returned and then disappeared as they had before. Passing approximately 16,000 ft. there was a large fluctuation in the CA's airspeed causing a large fluctuation in the autothrottles. The CA disengaged the autopilot and autothrottles. We transferred aircraft control since my indications seemed normal. The CA ran the QRH for unreliable airspeed [and] I flew the approach using the autopilot with the autothrottles disengaged to an uneventful landing.		B737 flight crew reported the Captain's airspeed indicator became unreliable and control was transferred to the First Officer.	
1778263	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121		Passenger		Descent																																Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1778263												Conflict NMAC; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance	Vertical 500		Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft TA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	I was PF (Pilot Flying) and received an RA about 20 NM SW of ZZZ. Descending from 7,000-5,000 feet, I received a TA and immediately reduced my rate of descent. I received an RA while leveling off at 5,000 [feet] regarding traffic 500 feet below, I complied and cleared the conflict. The aircraft was landed safely without issue.				Air Carrier First Officer reports an NMAC during descent.	
1778308	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121		Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic System Lines, Connectors, Fittings		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1778308												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was CA (Captain) and PM (Pilot Monitoring).  Departed late due to ELAC (Elevator & Aileron Computer) 1 MEL (Minimum Equipment List).  South of ZZZ at FL390, we had ECAM for HYD (Hydraulic) G RSVR (Reservoir) LO AIR PR (Low Air Pressure).  I ran the ECAM and told ATC we had a HYD issue.  ECAM put us into ATL LAW (Alternate Law) and FO (First Officer) had to hand fly.  We asked for lower altitude and told ATC we wanted ZZZ1 and I sent ACARS to Dispatch.   I was running the COM and getting ATIS and found out ZZZ was showing 002OVC (200' Overcast) and 1 1/2 vis (Visibility).  I asked ATC if there was anything better for weather and he said the region was all the same.  We decided to go to ZZZ.  Once the COM was complete I called and briefed the FA's (Flight Attendants) and made PA to the passengers.  We decided that I would take over and fly the approach and landing. We briefed the ILS 11 Approach and the FO hand flew the Aircraft until about 15-20 mile final so I could take my time and take a few minutes to 'gather my thoughts.'  We started to configure early and we had to gravity extend the gear.  Now we were in direct law and started to fly the approach..  We saw the approach lights and continued and saw the runway at minimums.  We landed.  We taxied to the gate and briefed the passengers and deplaned.  I made the phone calls to Company, Dispatch and Maintenance.  Maintenance looked at the issue and found out there was HYD fluid all over and we had a bad O ring. They fixed the issue and we were about to land in ZZZ1 about 5 hours late.				A320 Captain reported a Hydraulic System Failure inflight resulting in a diversion.	
1778504	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ; Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Final Approach; Landing	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1778504												ATC Issue All Types; Ground Event / Encounter Fuel Issue; Ground Event / Encounter Person / Animal / Bird			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Procedure; Airport	Ambiguous	Great weather, normal fuel burn. Entering terminal area we were vectored around then low and slow which created more fuel burn in terminal area. Did go-around for XXL due to an animal on runway. Aircraft in front of us did same. Declared minimum fuel on go-around in order to expedite landing since fuel now was an issue with extra fuel burn and not much to play. Controller was going to extend us downwind behind an arriving aircraft which would have added more time and fuel so I elected to [request] an immediate turn to the FAF for XXC. I didn't want XXL because of animal issue and another possible go-around. Controller vectored preceding aircraft off their approach and cleared us for visual XXC. Landed uneventfully with 3.9 fuel.....2.10 left tank and 1.8 right tank. Reserve Fuel on Flight Plan was 3.78.Cause: Dispatched with normal arrival fuel with very good weather. Controller vectored us low and slow which increased fuel burn. Suggestions: Next time I will not accept so many vectors and low and slow increasing fuel burn when I do not have extra fuel to play.				B737-800 Captain reported a low fuel situation developed following a go-around to avoid an animal on the runway.	
1779045	202012	0601-1200						35000		IMC	Icing		Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Pitot/Static Ice System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 84.85; Flight Crew Total 24902; Flight Crew Type 9591					1779045												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Enroute ZZZ-ZZZ1 on the red-eye at FL 33,000 ft. into the flight we had an ANTI ICE STBY PITOT ECAM. QRH was used. later we were at FL 35,000 ft. and Autopilot 1 disconnected, I attempted to reengage #1 or #2 autopilots and neither would engage. The FMAs went blank on both the F/O and my PFDs. Autothrust disengaged and went into Thrust LK. Thrust levers were matched with power setting and IDP switches used. We had our route displayed in the ND on both sides and MCDUs were operating normally. ECAM displayed:AUTO FLT AP OFF (RED)AUTO FLT A/THR OFF (AMBER)L/G SHOCK ABSORBER FAULT (AMBER)Indications looked similar to an FMGC Dual Failure except we had both NDs displaying our route, our MCDUs did not go into a subsystem page and no FMGC (Timeout) was observed. The Shock Absorber Fault ECAM had me thinking this may be a different malfunction that I have never witnessed or a coincidence. We contacted Dispatch/Maintenance via phone patch using the 'CALL ME' function on ACARS. Maintenance was perplexed of what was causing our malfunction. They had us reset the Auto Flight C/B's B1 (MCDU 1) B2 (FMG 1) and hold. C/B's were reset with no change. We attempted the Long Reset in the QRH Dual FMGC Failure with no success. Mintenance had no other options available.ATC was advised of our equipment malfunction and our inability to remain in ZZZ airspace. We were cleared down to FL 27,000 ft.. Review of our fuel, weather and capabilities with my F/O. Dispatcher we agreed to continue to ZZZ1 with the caution that if we had additional malfunctions, we would consider ZZZ2 as a divert airport. ATC was given our new suffix with the inability to operate in RVSM airspace.Purser was informed of our status.Prior to touchdown at FL 27,000 ft. both FMAs began to populate. Autopilots and Autothrust became available. All malfunctions except for the STBY Pitot cleared. We reengaged the Auto flight functions and remained at FL 27,000 ft. We landed on Runway XX at ZZZ1. Malfunctions were recorded on in the ELB, including a information writeup of what happened. Technician met the plane and we explained what happened.				Captain reported failure of aircraft display systems, causing aircraft to descend out of RVSM airspace.	
1779456	202012	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121		Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Wheels/Tires/Brakes		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting; Confusion			1779456	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting			1779461	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	After we were clear for takeoff on the Runway XX, at ZZZ airport. The Captain applied power; he waited for the engine to stabilize, then he pressed TOGA. As the plane started moving, it suddenly started veering to the right. He tried to correct it by pressing the left rudder, but the aircraft did not respond right away. After a few seconds, the plane veer back to the left, but it only went about 45 degrees to the left. After that, the Captain pressed the right rudder and was able to bring it back to control. Furthermore, we stopped at the runway; then, I called the tower to abort the takeoff. Finally, we were able to taxi off the runway and went back to the gate.		Cleared for takeoff Runway XX. Engines spooled up 'taxiing' up to takeoff point on Runway XX. With engines at 40% N1 pushed TOGA button and throttles advanced. We observed the white arcs advancing but the plane didn't seem to be accelerating normally. As I glanced inside to ensure the auto throttles were engaged (they were) the aircraft started to drift right. I tried to correct back with the rudder as we always do but it had no effect. As the right drift continued I rejected the take off and had to use almost full nose wheel tiller to correct back to centerline. We smelled burning rubber, so we taxied clear of the runway and had ARFF (Airport Rescue Firefighting) come inspect the gear and wheels. No problems found by ARFF. Tower had the runway inspected. Nothing found. I had ARFF follow us to the gate where we made an AML entry and had a lengthy discussion with Maintenance. During the takeoff roll the throttles never made it to Takeoff Thrust and I was looking outside the whole time, but the speed achieved was well below 80 kts. Probably around 40-45 kts. In addition, on taxi out when brakes were applied to stop in line they often 'chattered.'We suspected a brake problem or asymmetric thrust.I'm not sure why it happened. I've seen asymmetric thrust before and it is easily corrected with rudder to steer back to centerline. This plane was very difficult to get turning back to the left.		Air carrier flight crew reported the takeoff was rejected after the aircraft drifted to the right and was not responding to rudder inputs.	
1779605	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				11000					Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 150					1779605	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 9000					1779597	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Dispatch	Routine Inspection; In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	Ten minutes into the flight, we received an ACARS message. It stated that oxygen cylinders were on-board. We did not receive a Dangerous Goods Form on the ground. Once we landed in ZZZ1, we reached out to Dispatch and received a copy of the Dangerous Goods form. I signed it, and it was faxed back to ZZZ.		Approximately ten minutes into our climb out from ZZZ, we received a message from Dispatch telling us we had four oxygen bottles in the cargo area. We did not receive a Dangerous Goods Form from the Ramp prior to our departure. We continued on to our destination. In ZZZ1 we were given the proper paperwork. Captain signed it, and paperwork was sent, via fax, to ZZZ.		B737-800 flight crew reported in flight notification of Hazmat cargo onboard without proper flight crew documentation. Required Hazmat documents completed upon arrival at destination.	
1779728	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US												Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch; Initial Climb																										Cockpit Window		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1779728												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon gear retraction, after departure from [Runway] XXL at ZZZ, we heard a loud bang in the nose wheel well with an associated yaw of the aircraft. A burnt rubber smell was also detected and dissipated after 5 minutes. My thoughts ranged from, 'something hit us' to 'we blew a nose tire.' All engine and hydraulic indications were normal and the plane was flying fine with no control problems. I decided we had no immediate safety of flight issues and we continued the climbout. At 25,000 feet I had the First Officer take the radios and I messaged dispatch that I wanted to talk to Flight Operations. The Dispatcher and I talked briefly on the SATCOM and he put me on hold to get Flight Operations online. While waiting I ran through the QRH to see if there was a procedure for this issue and found 'Tire Failure.' That seemed to most agree with the possible problem and I was pleased to see that it called for us to proceed normally to the destination since that's what we were doing. Discussing what we knew with Flight Operations we all agreed that the problem was probably the tire and proceeding normally was the action we would take. Flight Operations also said they would call the Tower and Approach control to let them know we might have an issue.After level off the 'A' Flight Attendant came to the cockpit and asked if we heard a bang on takeoff. She said some of the 1st class passengers asked her if we were alright and she said that she was able to reassure them that we were. I told her what we suspected and that I would brief the FA's (Flight Attendants) near top of descent.We had plenty of time and I didn't want to get caught unawares so the FO and I briefed various scenarios and reviewed checklists including 'manual gear extension' and ' partial or all gear up landing'. By the time we were ready to brief the arrival I felt prepared to handle any scenario.Unrelated to the wheel issue, about halfway to ZZZ2 the FO fwd window overheat-light illuminated. We ran the checklist and never got it back so, knowing we had clouds and probably ice to descend through I opted to do the landing. I had considered it anyway, but that clinched it. Sure enough the FO's window was opaque until about the outer marker.ATC was ready for us thanks to the Flight Operations phone call. They set us up for [Runway] YYL and slowed us to 170 around ZZZZZ [Intersection]. I put the gear down at that time to give us some breathing room if it didn't come down. The nose gear was the first to show down and locked and I relaxed a bit. We told tower we anticipated a normal landing but they still asked for souls and fuel, which we gave them. They had an operations vehicle shadow us to check for debris. Touchdown was normal and the nose wheel felt fine when it made contact so we told Tower we could taxi in unaided.We were met by the ops manger and several mechanics. Disappointingly, the nose wheel and the well area looked perfectly normal. After all that build up, I admit I wanted to see some torn metal or bits of tire. It will be interesting to see what maintenance discovers.What I can say I learned from this, is that we have a solid system of communication at (Company).  The call to ATC by Flight Operations and Dispatch saved us a lot of on air negotiation for 16L. My FO contributed many helpful thoughts that aided our preparation for just about any gear scenario. The FA's took the news of a possible irregular landing calmly and knew what roles they would play should things get sticky. Although it all turned out to be much ado about nothing, it was a good test of the system and the system works.				B737-800 flight crew aircraft reported aircraft yaw and a loud bang in the nose wheel well during gear retraction. Additionally, the FO's forward window had an overheat issue.	
1779731	202011	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121		Passenger		Climb																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface; Distraction			1779731												Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overrode Automation	Human Factors	Human Factors	Departing ZZZ1 for ZZZ2 I was the PM (Pilot Monitoring) and the CA (Captain) was the PF (Pilot Flying). We planned a low altitude flight due to the shortness of the leg. Some altitudes in the legs page were edited to allow the lower cruise altitude to be selected. The flaps 10 takeoff went as normal (LNAV/VNAV). The PF called for flaps 1 as near as I can recall. I believe this is the case as most if not all of our previous legs had been flaps 5 takeoffs. I was distracted with a radio call or frequency change and I moved the flaps to 1 and did not catch the error. Shortly after that, the FMC changed to the descent phase and the speed bug moved to the flaps 5 maneuver speed, and the A/T (Auto Throttle) mode went from N1 to FMC speed, which resulted in the flaps 5 maneuver speed being the A/T target. As, or just prior to this happening the PF called for flaps up. I moved the flap lever to the up position, and since we were no longer accelerating got a momentary stick shaker. The PF instinctively lowered the nose reducing the AOA (Angle Of Attack). I increased the speed in the MCP window to get the A/T to command acceleration. The remainder of the flight was normal.Suggest crews be alert for unusual mode reversions when using low altitudes for cruise. I would probably use non VNAV takeoff procedures in the future. The big lesson is not new, vigilance absolutely has to be maintained during the 'red' parts of the flight path. The PM job is challenging and can be intolerant of errors.				Air Carrier flight crew reported a procedure error during flap retraction.	
1779868	202012	1801-2400	LAX.Airport	CA			300			VMC						Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Visual Approach	Class B LAX																												Tower LAX	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2		IFR			Final Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 119; Flight Crew Type 7347					1779868	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 49; Flight Crew Total 14330; Flight Crew Type 6839						Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	We were cleared for a visual approach following a Boeing 777 to Runway 25L at LAX. On short final we encountered what was most likely wake turbulence from the proceeding aircraft on short final ~300 feet AGL. This presented as a roll and shear to a headwind. This apparent shear to a headwind along with an addition of some thrust put me high on the profile and ~15 knots above target speed. The severity of the event was below what I would describe as an 'upset'. When the turbulence subsided we appeared to be in a good position to land, so I continued the approach and landed. Being high on profile and bleeding off the excess speed in the flare resulted in a touchdown that was most likely beyond the touchdown zone. Despite this we were in position, with normal braking, to exit at taxiway H8. Tower instructed us to exit at H9.		Upon landing on Runway 25L at LAX we landed outside of the normal 3000 feet of the touchdown zone after being preceded by a 777 to landing during light tail wind conditions. The briefing and approach were completely routine. We had originally planned to land on 24R but we were prepared for a possible change to Runway 25L. We were told on downwind that we would be following a heavy 777 and could expect 25L. Conditions were clear and we had the aircraft in sight. As I reconfigured the aircraft for landing on the south runway, the Captain made the comment that he would be prepared to fly a dot high. The Captain flew as he briefed and we experienced mostly calm winds all the way down the glideslope. By 200 feet AGL, we were back on glide slope.  The aircraft sank somewhat and by 100 feet we were almost a half dot low when we started to feel some of the wake of the 777.  The winds were still light, about 300/7.  The plane was very controllable and the Captain pitched up and added power slightly to stay on top of the wake. In doing so, the speed increased almost 15 knots and I considered calling for a go around.  It still appeared that we were going to be touching down in the first 3000 feet so I allowed the approach to continue.  The Captain flared the aircraft so that it looked like we would touch down right at about 3000 feet.  Without trying to hold the aircraft off, the plane continued down the runway at what seemed like 1 foot. The aircraft finally settled a couple hundred feet later and we turned off the runway and proceeded to the gate without any further issues.This was a case where one limit almost being crossed led to another limit being crossed. It looked like it was all going to work out.  We have all encountered 'disturbed' air from a wake before on landing especially at busy airports like LAX.  The question is how much of an adjustment do you have to make and at what point do you go around.  This looked very doable.  We had plenty of runway and performance.  This looked familiar with a landing in the touchdown zone.  I only thought later and realized that the Captain had elected to do a flaps 40 landing in a 737-900ER.  I know I have not done many flaps 40 landings in a 900ER in the last couple years, so I think my sight picture was off a bit for the flare and the touchdown.  We may have also gotten a bit more of ground effect than what I would have imagined.  The speed did not bleed off as fast as I had anticipated.		B737 flight crew reported encountering wake turbulence from preceding B777 on approach to LAX that resulted in a long landing.	
1780093	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				34000		VMC	Thunderstorm		Daylight				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb; Cruise	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 27; Flight Crew Total 1762; Flight Crew Type 654					1780093	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 41; Flight Crew Total 2125; Flight Crew Type 1050					1780197	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the Captain on Flight XXXX, ZZZ to ZZZ1.During the climb, passing FL330 to FL 350, ECAM ADR1.  Complying with instructions from ECAM, additional ECAMs: AP 1& 2, A/THR, RUD TRV LIM and GPWS.  We also lost both FD (Flight Display).[Advised ATC] and asked for vector to divert to ZZZ2.  ZZZ2 weather was clear, ZZZ had thunderstorms.Phone patch with Dispatch and [Maintenance Control] and advised of situations and intentions.Descended into ZZZ2.  Asked for delaying vectors to get under landing weight.Raw data landing was normal.  Completed [Maintenance Manual].		I was the First Officer on Flight XXXX, ZZZ to ZZZ1.During the climb, passing FL330 to FL 350, ECAM ADR1.  Complying with instructions from ECAM, additional ECAMs: AP 1&2, A/THR, RUD TRV LIM and GPWS.  We also lost both FD (Flight Display).[Advised ATC] and asked for vector to divert to ZZZ2.  ZZZ2 weather was clear, ZZZ had thunderstorms.Phone patch with Dispatch and [Maintenance Control] and advised of situations and intentions.Descended into ZZZ2.  Asked for delaying vectors to get under landing weight.Raw data landing was normal.  Completed [Maintenance Manual].		Flight Crew reported diverting caused by loss of ADR1 and associated NAV and FLIGHT Displays.	
1780133	202012	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Powerplant Lubrication System			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1780133	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1780220	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were at cruise when we noted the right engine oil pressure at 6 (normally around 12). We see that the number was going down, showing that we are losing oil. I started to go through the iPad QRH for an appropriate checklist. While looking, the Captain said 'your aircraft' so I took over pilot duties (it was his leg). He was looking at the Low Oil checklist when the oil qty went to zero, and the Captain decided to just shutdown the engine before it got worse. We [advised ATC] and a turn back towards ZZZ. We ran the engine shutdown checklist. I was flying, he was doing the checklists, radio, and the procedures, as recommended by our training department. We were still over max landing weight, and needed time to run all the supplemental checklists and procedures, so we decided to land at ZZZ and burn fuel to get under max landing weight at the same time. The single engine ILS into ZZZ was textbook. We taxied off the runway, was inspected by CFR, and then taxied to the gate.The Low Oil Quantity checklist is not easily found, as it is not in the index as 'low oil quantity' or 'oil low quantity' and it only reference oil pressure in the index.There was no audio or visual alert, when the oil quantity was half what it was supposed to be. We just happened to catch the small indication on the lower screen, and watched it drop to zero. I don't think we could have caught it any faster than we did.		Oil quantity on the Number 2 engine went to 0.		B737-800 flight crew reported loss of engine oil, causing an in-flight shut down and a diversion to a precautionary landing.	
1780305	202012	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000		VMC			Dawn			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class B ZZZ																								Indicating and Warning - Hydraulics		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 68; Flight Crew Total 2785; Flight Crew Type 227					1780305	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 23; Flight Crew Total 469; Flight Crew Type 469					1779880	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Loss Blue Hyd system. Applied QRH procedures and advised ATC.		Approximately 200 nm south of ZZZ we received our first indication of any issue with the blue hydraulic system. In level cruise flight we received an ECAM for HYD B SYS LO PR. While running the ECAM we noticed that the hydraulic quantity was low. The procedure calls for the Blue Electric Pump to be shut off, and as we did so, we monitored the quantity indicator. As it dropped, we received an ECAM for Hyd B RSVR LO LVL. After running the ECAM, finishing the QRH and conferring with dispatch we advised ATC for the loss of redundant systems. We chose to divert to ZZZ for a multitude of reasons including the nice weather, long runways, availability of ARFF (Airport Rescue and Firefighting) and the fact that it's a maintenance hub. We immediately received vectors and a descent from ATC for an arrival into ZZZ. The Captain finalized his briefings to both the flight attendants and passengers. We then updated the FMC, applied the landing distance procedure and landing calculations and briefed the descent and approach. We completed the RNAV to [Runway XX], landed and had ARFF inspect the aircraft for any potential hazards or safety concerns. Once complete, we taxied to the gate and completed the diversion safely.		A320 flight crew reported a loss of hydraulic pressure that resulted in a diversion.	
1780917	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US	150	7		2300					Dusk			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Human-Machine Interface; Confusion			1780917	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain		Flight Crew Last 90 Days 83; Flight Crew Type 83		Human-Machine Interface			1781962	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were on a visual approach to Runway XXL at ZZZ at 3,000 feet. Approach cleared us for a visual approach about 7 miles out and we were giving a heading towards the FAF following an airbus. PF set 2,000 feet in the MCP and we began a slow descent heading inbound to the airport. ATC issued a 'low altitude alert' at about 2,300 feet. We leveled off and intercepted the G/S just past the FAF. In addition, we had to rapidly slow since the airbus in front slowed rapidly and ATC wanted us to slow to approximately 150 knots or less. They really did not leave us much lateral room. We slowed and configured while descending. We were never near an obstacle, nor were we a threat to another aircraft. We were still above the 1,000 feet stable approach criteria (1,300 to 1,500 feet) and met the criteria proof to reaching 1,000 feet. We were lower than what was normally flow for that area but still seamed safe. Again on a visual approach. PF stated that he read 2,000 feet at the FAF and misread the altitude. The FAF altitude is 2,800 feet. I did not reverify it since we were visual and I was working on setting up radios and configuration for landing. I assumed he saw it correctly.I can't find where we violated anything. We were a little low for the area but still in a safe position to land and had visual clearance from all obstacles. I know next time to verify the altitude in the MCP from the chart myself. That is that only thing that may have stopped the lower alt in the first place.		On receiving landing clearance, I set 2,000 feet in the altitude window and planned to descend to that altitude at ZZZZZ, the FAF.  I misread the approach plate. The correct altitude at ZZZZZ is 2,800 feet. The Tower gave us a Low Altitude Alert at 2,200 MSL in VMC and we maintained that altitude (we were nearly at glideslope intercept) until glideslope intercept.  We landed uneventfully.		Air carrier flight crew reported entering the wrong altitude in the MCP and descending below FAF altitude which resulted in Air Traffic Control issuing a low altitude alert.	
1781721	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				30000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		4	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise; Climb		Class A ZZZ						Inspection																																																									Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1781721	Company; Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Dispatcher	Dispatch Dispatcher						1781722	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	Noticed slight airframe vibration in the climb.   At FL300 it was much worse. Getting even more vibration as speed increased.  Called up the flight control page and saw the R FLPRN was almost all the way up with the L in a normal position.  The control column was 2 degrees right wing down.  Called Operations Control and discussed.  Had the Tech Pilot on the phone also.  All agreed it was not normal.  Decision was to return to ZZZ.  Had to dump fuel.  Did this in coordination with Center.  Arrival was normal until landing flaps were selected.  When flaps started past 20 the airplane shook violently and rolled to the R.  Re-selected flaps to 25 and added 15 knots.  At the gate the maintenance tech could see the Number 6 spoiler was up.  Wrote up the discrepancies and debriefed the mechanic.  Crew did a phenomenal job.  Great back up.Cause - Spoiler float not locked down.		Aircraft X did a block turn back for a spoiler issues when I first sat down.  After nearly 5 hours on the ground, we received a duty extension and the flight departed. (XA16z) At approximately XA55z crew sent ACARS message:...ALT AIRFRAME VIBL ENG 1.0  R ENG .9R FLPRN IS UPVIBWORSE AT HIGHER SPD.I spoke with the crew on the SATCOM for over an hour bringing in Operations, Tech Pilot, Maintenance Control and Maintenance Control talked to mechanics that did the spoiler work.  We determined it was odd behavior for B777 controls and this aircraft usually flies straight. Determination was made to bring the flight back to ZZZ as this may not be an easy fix.		B777 flight crew and the flight Dispatcher reported airframe vibrations during climbout which worsened as the speed increased.  The crew contacted the company and decided to return to their departure airport.  Reportedly, the cause of the issue was a spoiler float was not locked down.	
1781775	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US													Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1781775												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the Captain on Aircraft X from ZZZ to ZZZ1 on Date. Just about top of descent both the FAs (Flight Attendants) and both pilots heard a high pitched squealing sound and the FAs believed it was coming from Door R1 near the floor.The FAs also reported a vibration in the floor at the bottom of Door R1.The FAs never reported smelling anything.About a minute after the sound started the FO (First Officer) thought he might be smelling something coming from his gasper vent on the forward instrument panel. I couldn't initially smell anything but possibly a few minutes later might have briefly but if we did it was very brief and minor. The FO described it as a hot plastic type of smell but very faint and for just a couple of minutes.The squeal and vibration lasted about 10 minutes and the rest of the flight was uneventful.I filled out an odor form for MX (Maintenance), they said it would help them troubleshoot our issue and therefore I also am filing a report.				A320 Captain reported experiencing a 'brief' fumes event at the top of descent. Simultaneously, the flight Attendants reported a temporary 'high pitch squeal' accompanied by a floor vibration.	
1781782	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				30000		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B777-200		4	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ	Y	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing																		Speedbrake/Spoiler		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1781782	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Relief Pilot; First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1781783	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; MEL; Procedure	Aircraft	Flight crew noted a slight rumbling vibration on climb out.  The vibration increased with airspeed and as we reach cruise at FL300 and increased speed we noted the increased vibration and that there was a control yoke displacement of approximately 2 units right in straight and level flight with all other trim neutral.  After discussing the situation as a crew, we decided to contact [Operations and Maintenance] for further discussion and possible solutions.  Due to a known history and MEL addition affecting spoilers, we selected the flight control schematic page and noted that the right flaperon indicated a near full upward deflection in level flight. Through our discussion with [a] tech pilot, it was suggested that we try utilizing rudder trim to determine how much was needed to neutralize the control yoke, and we determined that 4 units of rudder trim was required.  Without being able to fully determine what was causing the vibration we decided that a 13 1/2 hour flight and oceanic crossing would not be prudent, and most likely would not be possible due to fuel over burn.  We discussed possible alternate airports and [Operations] ultimately requested that we return to ZZZ.  We were North West of ZZZ1 when we coordinated with ZZZ2 Center for our return to ZZZ.  We coordinated with ZZZ1 Center to jettison fuel in order to be below max landing weight.  The fuel jettison of 78,400 lbs. was accomplished just south east of ZZZ3 VOR at FL200.  Arriving back into the ZZZ area, we coordinated for runway 36C.  As we configured the aircraft for landing, we noted a drastic increase in vibration as the flaps were extended from 20 to the planned setting of 30.  We adjusted to flap 25 for landing and flap 25 speed.  Although the vibrations and roll control were abnormal on final, the landing was uneventful.  After landing we elected to leave the flaps at 25 until mechanics at the gate could verify that retraction would not cause further damage.  After block in it was discovered that the number 6 spoiler was up.  We could not see indications for the number 6 spoiler on the flight control page due to the MEL deactivation.  Initial indications are that the number 6 spoiler which was deactivated under MEL was 'floating' or extended when it should have been down.		On taxi out, during the control check a 'spoilers' EICAS message displayed. PDM (Programmed Depot Maintenance) procedures were attempted, but we had to return to the gate for maintenance, as it was a 'flagged' item.  Spoilers were MELed.  After takeoff, we felt airframe vibrations that got worse at higher altitudes and airspeeds. We found the right flaperon was indicating all the way up, the left flaperon was indicating slightly down.  Additionally, the control wheel was right wing down about 2 degrees.  After consulting Maintenance and [Operations], everyone decided that the best course of action was to RTB (Return to Base) ZZZ.  Fuel jettison checklist was executed in order to land on 36C.  Due to excessive airframe vibrations at flaps 30, a flaps 25 landing was made.Uncommanded asymmetric spoiler deployment.		B777 flight crew reported uncommanded asymmetric spoiler deployment in flight.	
1781991	202101	1201-1800	ZZZZ.Airport	FO			0			VMC						Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																														Tower ZZZZ		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer		2	Part 91				Takeoff / Launch																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Type 2119		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1781991												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Ground Event / Encounter Aircraft; Ground Incursion Runway			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	Other Takeoff Roll	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Human Factors	Human Factors	The incident took place at ZZZZ on runway XX, where aircraft must back taxi to use full length. The CA (Captain) and I had just deadheaded down, with our first flying leg of the trip being from ZZZZ to ZZZ. I was feeling refreshed as I had napped for about 2 hours on the [deadhead] down. We had 1.5 hours on the ground, so did not feel rushed, even though it was my first time to ZZZZ. Boarding, preflight, briefing, clearance, pushback, start and taxi to hold short of Rwy XX were uneventful. The CA briefed language as a threat, but the clearance and ground control went smoothly. Initially we held short at taxiway E. Following a departure by another AC we were cleared to back taxi down to Runway XX approach end, and lineup and wait. While in our turn around, an aircraft checked in on final for our runway, I visually identified that aircraft and said aloud that he looked to be on a 6 mile final. Tower told that aircraft to continue.Once aligned with the centerline, I confirmed with the Captain that he was ready to depart, then told Tower "Aircraft X in position, ready for takeoff'. Tower responded that we were cleared for takeoff runway XX, and to contact departure when airborne. I replied that we were cleared for takeoff, runway XX, and echoed the departure frequency we were just given.The Captain and I confirmed with each other we were both ready to go and he pushed up the throttles. As we both were watching the engines spool up, we began to roll forward down the runway. Tower then said something to the effect of "Aircraft X, please do not takeoff, there is an aircraft...' As I started to look up, my first thought was about the aircraft on final. The CA and I both looked at each other, confused about the nonstandard radio call. Tower sounded a bit flustered, and then I saw 2 strobe lights at the far end of the runway. I said 'Reject' aloud, then answered tower "Aircraft X rejecting takeoff.' The CA executed the rejected takeoff, but at only 40 knots or so, was really just bringing throttles to idle stopping the aircraft. I called 'Remain seated, remain seated' on the PA.At that point I started to see an aircraft that went with those strobe lights and a white twin engine civilian airplane lifted off our runway. Somehow this aircraft had gotten onto or been cleared onto RWY XX for an intersection takeoff. There had been Spanish being spoken on the frequency but I did not connect that discussion with an aircraft on our runway. The same frequency is used for clearance, pushback, ground, and tower control.As we stopped...perhaps 500-1000 ft. down the runway, tower then told us we were cleared for Takeoff again. We replied negative that we would need to taxi clear or do a 180 turn and go to the approach end. Tower then told the aircraft on final to go around while we taxied. The CA called for the Rejected takeoff checklist, which we ran. We used the card first then QRH back cover. The CA also used the PA to briefly explain what had just happened. We quickly discussed our options, and decided that we could do a 180, and rerun the before takeoff checklist. Subsequent lineup, and takeoff were uneventful.				B737 First Officer reported rejecting takeoff due to ground conflict with departing aircraft.	
1783249	202101	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ	N	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Testing; Inspection																		Trailing Edge Flap		X	Malfunctioning																																				Hangar / Base	Other Exterior	Air Carrier	Technician	Maintenance Airframe; Maintenance Powerplant			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Maintenance; Party2 Maintenance		1783249		Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1783123	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Company Policy; Human Factors; Logbook Entry; Procedure; Aircraft	Procedure	Aircraft X in ZZZ with pilot discrepancy 'F/CTL FLAPS FAULT. ECAM OCCURRED ON APPROACH WHEN MOVING FLAP LEVER FROM 1 TO 2. AFTER RECYCLING FLAP LEVER PER ECAM, FLAPS OPERATED NORMALLY.' Post flight report indicated flap jam in flight phase 6 cruise. 4th shift started troubleshooting per TSM and I was assigned to continue. TSM stated both flap actuators were required to be changed in the event of a jam with no faults due to known icing problem. Concurred with Maintenance Control that this was desired and safest step to continue. Gave all information to my crew chief, and supervisor and that the plane needs to be taken out of service until the parts are removed and replaced. Supervisor outlined that his boss argued that it's not the problem and proceeded to find any way to keep aircraft green. Both myself and work partner were pressured for well over the night to not ground Aircraft X and call it good. We raised the fact the TSM explicitly states with no faults both actuators must be changed. Due to ongoing peer pressure I reluctantly agreed to sign Aircraft X airworthy on the technicality that although the aircraft is reporting a flap jam, the phase of flight it occurred was in cruise and probably an indication error. However, prior to Date on Date-1 the exact fault and exact write up was entered. Corrective action from ZZZ2 base was a reset of LH #1 torque limiter. The TSM for this faults states that when resetting a flap torque limiter, the TSM refers to lubricate drive system, do a detail inspection and replace rotary actuator which have a tripped torque limiter. This was not done in ZZZ2 when an indicator was tripped, reset, and signed off. See [Logbook Entry]. I advised Maintenance Control and supervision of this oversight and advocated for replacing flap actuators.Event occurred because management doesn't take un airworthy as acceptable.Keep management where it belongs. Managing their supervisors and leave the technical aspect to the mechanics with the proper steps and actions from the manuals.		Plane arrived in ZZZ with Flight CTL flap slat fault, was written up previous day for same thing. Contract maintenance with Maintenance Control guidance troubleshot fault - no faults where found, we ran engines and cycled the flaps per Maintenance Control request, no faults occurred. Approaching ZZZ1 at 2,000 ft. at flaps 2 selected we had a ECAM FLT CTL FLAPS FAULT message. Did go around and followed vectors to 3,000 ft., advised ATC and followed QRH, We did all steps but were both misinterpreted the checklist. Should have put lever to flaps 3, instead of flaps full and calculated the wrong approach speed as we now believe we misinterpreted the max speed chart for approach speed. 190 kts. instead of 165 kts. Report said we were 185 kts. I believe. On the approach I knew something was not right, thought about going around but was worried about the fuel time. During approach we had full slats no flaps. We requested longer runway (12,000 ft.), Landing was normal I let plane slow to 100 kts. before applying breaks. Never heated up beyond 150 [degrees] more or less. After firefighters looked over the airplane we proceeded to gate. Should have referred to SOP in performance for added info but thought we were right and worried about fuel. I have done this procedure in training, I ran the checklist and made the mistake that I was warned about. I believe frustration with a reassignment, irritated with a broken airplane (multiple problems) that was delivered to us and worried about fuel during checklist all added to my distraction and lead me to misinterpreting the checklist.Slow down slow read checklist slower, have more fuel to run all the way through.		Multiple repeat flap failures caused a no flap landing and subsequent errors during maintenance trouble shooting and repair of the affected system.	
1783426	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch			N		N		Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection																		Compressor		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 144; Flight Crew Total 13533; Flight Crew Type 11537		Troubleshooting			1783426	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1783427	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Ops normal from pushback to takeoff roll.  I was flying pilot, [the] Captain non-flying pilot.   Power was set and autothrottles and FMAs all had normal indications. At approximately 60 knots there was a loud bang and the jet swerved to the right.   I steered the jet back to centerline with rudder and the Captain initiated the abort.  We stopped quickly on the runway and told ATC we'd aborted and instructed the passengers to remain seated.  After quickly assessing the risk, and talking with the Lead Flight Attendant, Captain determined it was safe to taxi clear of Runway XX and we stopped on the parallel taxiway. We requested assistance from [the] Fire Department to look at the landing gear to ensure we hadn't blown a tire or had smoke/fire coming off the brakes.   After getting the all-clear, we coordinated with Operations/Tech Ops to return to Gate X.  We closely monitored brake temp indications during the taxi back and saw that they were all decreasing in normal fashion as expected.  We were met by many company personnel at the gate and Maintenance was able to quickly determine we had an Engine Number 2 compressor stall during takeoff roll.  Passengers deplaned in normal fashion through Door 1L, and the flight crew was then escorted up to ZZZ Flight Ops to discuss our readiness and ability to continue flying the trip.  Captain and I then proceeded to fly as scheduled.		During takeoff roll, heard a loud bang and aircraft swerved to the right.  Executed rejected takeoff procedure.  Exited runway.  Personnel inspected aircraft externally.  No visible damage and no fires were detected.  Returned to gate.  Maintenance data showed engine stall on Number 2 Engine. My compliments to the First Officer and Flight Attendants for their professionalism during this unexpected event.		Air carrier flight crew reported that an engine compressor stall occurred during takeoff roll, causing the aircraft to swerve and the crew to reject the takeoff.	
1784451	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				11500		Mixed	Cloudy		Daylight	11000			Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class C ZZZ																								Exterior Pax/Crew Door		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 40					1784451	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1784433	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown		N	Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climb out, after pressing the ATTEND CALL button as part of my flow, the A Flight Attendant immediately called the flight deck and complained of a loud squeal coming from the main door (L1) and that they could 'see daylight' coming from the door seal. I could hear the squeal in the background on the interphone. I looked on the overhead panel and verified that there was NO door light illuminated, I then went to verify pressurization and noticed that the cabin altitude was approaching 9,500 ft. I immediately instructed the First Officer to level off and then called ATC and reported a 'pressurization issue' and requested 10,000 ft.. We did not get a CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING at any point. I then requested to return to ZZZ and they gave us a further descent to 5,000 ft. and direct to the field. I made a PA and let the Passengers know that we were returning to ZZZ because of a pressurization issue and that we would be on the ground in ten minutes. I never requested priority handling because I didn't feel like it was necessary because we had already received an immediate descent clearance, but ATC declared one for us and had the trucks standing by on arrival. We had just enough time to run all of the checklists and get our landing data. The arrival and landing was uneventful. I called the Flight Attendants and asked how everyone was. The A and B Flight Attendants said they felt a 'little dizzy' but they said all Passengers seemed fine and there didn't seem to be any issues. Before opening the main door on arrival I inspected the seal and on the aft part of the door from top to bottom I could indeed see daylight and could run my hand in between the seal and door frame from top to bottom.		Loss of pressurization in the climb, due to forward entry door not sealed properly due to malfunction. Climbing through 10,000 ft. and beginning the Climb Checklist, the Flight Crew received a chime from the Cabin Crew that the forward entry door was squealing loud and air rushing through the front area enough causing the Flight Attendant's hair to be circulating with the flow of air. No indication of the event on the flight deck. Cabin pressurization reached 9,500 ft. The aircraft reached a peak altitude just over 12,000 ft. before descent.  Returned to ZZZ uneventful.  ATC themselves gave us priority handling for our return to ZZZ. Upon landing and at the gate, all Passengers were removed and no indications of any medical distress.		Flight Crew reported a diversion caused by an L1 door leak that prevented the cabin from pressurizing.	
1784767	202101		ZZZ.TRACON	US				2200		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	SID ZZZZZ	Class C ZZZ																														Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 105; Flight Crew Total 4773; Flight Crew Type 4773		Situational Awareness			1784767	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 23; Flight Crew Total 3465; Flight Crew Type 3465		Situational Awareness			1784220	Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure	Procedure	I wish to report a near mid-air collision.  We were operating Aircraft X, ZZZ-ZZZ1, on a 737-900ER. The FO (First Officer) was the PF (Pilot Flying). I was the PM (Pilot Monitoring). Our IFR clearance was [SID]. Upon takeoff clearance from Runway XXR, we were given a heading of 290 with a initial climb limit of 3,000 [feet]. We received no notification of any traffic that we should be looking for upon takeoff clearance or during our initial climbout. As we were climbing out and cleaning up the aircraft, we received a TA at out 1 to 2 o'clock and high. I stopped my PM duties to search for source of the TA. As I searched, we got the altitude alert for the level off. I found the aircraft. He was not moving in relationship to our direction of travel. I told the PF to stop his climb. As I was saying that, we received the RA. The PF lowered the nose the slow the climb, as he was doing that the TCAS annunciated 'monitor vertical speed.' I glanced at the MFD to see the aircraft was only +200 feet above us. I was still on the 5 mile range and the symbol was inside the 2.5 mile tick mark at our 1 o'clock. The PF executed the RA escape maneuver to comply with the RA. I then told ZZZ Departure that I had the GA aircraft in sight, as we still hadn't been given any traffic alerts. The TCAS announced 'clear of conflict' and we resumed the climb uneventfully.		Aircraft X, ZZZ-ZZZ1 I was the PF (Pilot Flying). Our clearance was [SID]...off Runway XXR heading of 290 with a initial climb limit of 3,000 [feet]. We received a TA at about 2,200 [feet]. There was an aircraft approximately 2,500 feet. No notification from ATC about the traffic. TCAS told us monitor our vertical speed which I did. I performed an RA escape maneuver and TCAS said clear of conflict. We notified approach about the traffic and TCAS RA. It seemed as if there was something going on at ZZZ or surrounding airport with an emergency at the time...rest of the trip was without incident.		B737-900 flight crew reported taking evasive action after receiving a TA and RA.	
1784768	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US	225	5		1000		IMC	7		Daylight	1500		Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Final Approach; Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 130; Flight Crew Type 3140		Situational Awareness; Workload; Distraction; Fatigue; Time Pressure			1784768	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Type 11000		Distraction; Fatigue; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload			1784765	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Company Policy; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	For the ILS Runway 04R at ZZZ, performance numbers were run for a Flaps 30 landing with a good stopping margin at Autobrakes 3. ATIS winds were calling approximately 070/13 - no gusts. Upon switching to Tower at ZZZZZ on the localizer course and tracking the glideslope, Tower cleared us to land with winds now gusting to 20. As PF (Pilot Flying), I elected to add 4 knots to the Vtarget and as an added Safety measure at ZZZ, I selected Autobrakes MAX to compensate for a slightly increased approach speed. The PM (Pilot Monitoring) attempted to quickly re-run the performance data, but the gusts were not yet accounted for in the METAR. Rather than going heads down any longer inside 1,000 feet AGL, we agreed a 4-5 knot addition to VTarget and using Autobrakes MAX would be more than sufficient to account for the gust factor.At approximately 500 feet AGL, we received a GPWS Too Low Flaps Alert. The PM immediately called for the go-around and it was initiated. A quick scan of the flap handle and I realized that I had neglected to call for Flaps 30 from a Flaps 15 setting, and the Before Landing Checklist was not run. We were quickly vectored around for another uneventful ILS. The minor distraction of adding a gust factor and changing the Autobrakes setting sent me out of a normal approach configuration sequence and we ended up both missing the final flap setting and subsequent checklist.With the normal ZZZ procedure of not contacting (and often not switching to) Tower until reaching the fix at about a 5-mile final, it's possible for us to miss out on cues to changing weather on the ground. We were number 3 or 4 in a sequence of landings on 04R. Had we switched to Tower five or more miles sooner, we may have caught the gusting winds earlier and had more time to type in the adjusted winds and send away for the performance numbers prior to 1,000 feet AGL. This was another series of events combined with mild fatigue that led to poor task prioritization on our parts.		The short version is that we got 'Too Low, Flaps' at 500 feet because we were at Flaps 15. We went around. Planned winds were 070/7. Tower winds were 070/12G20. I re-ran the performance landing numbers at 1,500 feet, but did not override the METAR and got back the numbers for 070/7. The PF (Pilot Flying) chose to add 5 knots to VTarget and we agreed to go from Medium to Max braking and continue.There is no guidance I am aware of as to when we would be required to re-run our landing numbers. I should not have tried to re-run the landing numbers at that point. That would have eliminated the distraction that led to not selecting our planned final flaps or running the Before Landing Checklist.		B737-800 flight crew reported executing a go-around due to improper flap setting on short final.	
1784993	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				2000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb; Initial Climb		Class B ZZZ1																								GPS & Other Satellite Navigation		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1784993												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Aircraft	During climbout from ZZZ, above approximately 2,000 feet AFL, an amber GPS-L enunciated. I continued to hand fly the jet as we climbed into IMC conditions in the pre-dawn darkness. The GPS-L caution distracted me from calling 'flaps-up' at the appropriate speed. The Captain called my attention to this as we approached 240 knots IAS. I called for flaps up and the captain retracted them normally. I engaged the autopilot after flap retraction. At no time did we exceed the 250 knot flap placard limit. Maximum speed I remember seeing was 244 knots IAS with flaps not up. The rest of the flight to ZZZ1 was uneventful. I believe my recency of experience was a factor; this being my first unsupervised flight since re-qualification. Also, night, IMC in low marine layer clouds while hand flying the initial portion of the RNAV departure. The GPS-L caution was a nuisance warning that served as the distraction. I was focused on hand flying smoothly and lost situational awareness of aircraft configuration.Earlier use of automation may have helped with my situational awareness during the nuisance GPS distraction. My habit is to hand fly the aircraft until clean.				B737-800 First Officer reported a GPS-L annunciation on departure which distracted flight crew from calling flaps up at the appropriate speed.	
1786277	202102																Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1786277	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1786278	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors	Human Factors	In cruise received notification from Dispatch that due to dry ice loaded with live animal in Forward Cargo divert recommended. Almost immediately after digesting message from Dispatch received call from a Flight Attendant indicating medical [situation]. Diverted to ZZZ2 without incident. Required overweight landing, followed QRH and landed without incident. Made required logbook entries and notifications. Maintenance accomplished requisite checks and entries proceeded to ZZZ1 without incident.The cause of the Hazmat violation [Cargo Illegal Load] was that flight crew received ACARS message to anticipate NOTOC (Notification to Captain) but did not receive NOTOC until departure time through window. Reviewed NOTOC indicating dry ice but did not trigger my mind for live animal loading in same compartment. Same during review of closeout. Captain suggest a normal procedure of receipt of NOTOC with normal inquiry of live animals boarded. Live animal notice of some kind to trigger mind about carriage restrictions.		While enroute to ZZZ1 we received a message from Dispatch that we had dry ice loaded in the forward compartment bin with a live animal and recommended a divert to ZZZ3. The Captain and I discussed that we would be extremely overweight for ZZZ3 that was right under us so we were discussing ZZZ2 as a diversion airport. As we were discussing, we received a call from the #1 Flight Attendant that a passenger was having a medical [situation]. We diverted to ZZZ2, elected to land overweight, followed QRH, and landed without incident. Captain made the required logbook entries and notifications. Maintenance eventually accomplished the required inspections, dry ice was taken off airplane, and we then proceeded to ZZZ1 without incident. The cause was that we received a message to anticipate a NOTOC (Notification to Captain), but we did not receive the NOTOC until departure time through the Captain's window. Captain reviewed NOTOC and said we were good so I overlooked the fact that we had a live animal in the same bin. When I reviewed the load close out, I saw there was a live animal, but I did not know the restricted articles were dry ice so it did not trigger me that something was amiss. Flight crew suggests Crew Chief stating there is restricted articles, what it is, and if there are live animals. A more thorough review of the NOTOC on my part as First Officer.		B737-800 flight crew reported an uneventful Hazmat diversion which happened to coincide with a passenger medical event.	
1786585	202102		ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Total 5521; Flight Crew Type 5521					1786585												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	Other Take Off	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Procedure; Manuals; Aircraft	Aircraft	At approximately 100 kts, I noticed and called out a IAS Disagree warning indication on my PFD. I immediately looked at the First Officer and the STBY Airspeed indicators to compare speeds and saw the same warning on his. The difference in speed between both was about 5-7 knots (see FM, Airspeed Disagree Alert (amber) Indicates the Captain's and First Officer's airspeed indications disagree by more than 5 knots for 5 continuous seconds), and took about 5 to 10 seconds before the warning went away. My decision was to continue the takeoff since the IAS discrepancy was small and we were in the High-Speed regime. Following the disappearance of the warning indication, I closely monitored the speed differences which was reduced to 1-1.5 knots. Throughout the rest of the flight. An ELB write-up was made and local maintenance contacted to give a full description of the event after landing.Once we were at a safe climbing altitude, we discussed the situation further. I mentioned that if the difference in IAS had been larger I would have opted to reject. Interestingly; the First Officer expressed relief that we did not reject, and that we could have taken off even with the stick shaker going off, similarly as we experienced in a recent MAX training session (during climb), and that the company does not want us to reject other than for the exceptions stated in the FM. His honest answer left me to ponder and wonder if I made the right decision, and to take a closer look at our Normal and Non-Normal procedures, guidance, and its implications, and if any further clarification is needed or due. With that said, I would like to review on how would they apply in the following scenarios. For ease of page reference, I utilized FM dated XX-XX-XXX, however the manual date at the time of the event was [on] YY-YYY-YYYY. The information appears to be identical with the exception of a slight page numbering difference.  As a point of reference to determine a reliable airspeed indicator, the checklist states 'An airspeed indication that differs by more than 20 kts or 0.03 Mach from the airspeed shown in the table should be considered unreliable.' Also, there is NO guidance for pitch attitude on takeoff for this situation other than an initial pitch to 15 degrees followed by the immediate actions, which would call for a pitch and thrust reduction just off the ground following gear retraction. The immediate above action items seems contradictory to safe and sound practice of flying away from the ground and executing an emergency procedure at a safe altitude. Should safer guidance be included?It seems very clear, as always, that excellent flying skills and judicious Captain's Emergency Authority will be needed.	Reporter stated his main concern is he feels the procedure for dealing with IAS disagree scenarios is a vague and potentially misleading.  Reporter stated the issue seemed to resolve itself within just a few seconds.			B737 NG Captain reported an airspeed split on takeoff roll and elected to continue. Post flight and reviewing the incident, Captain described misgivings over published policy.	
1786794	202102	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US				4000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Other / Unknown; Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting			1786794												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight; Routine Inspection	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Captain was pilot monitoring. FO (First Officer) was pilot flying. Passing through 4,000 feet MSL on climbout from ZZZ we noticed a fuel imbalance developing - right wing main tank fuel indication already 400 pounds lower than left wing. Right wing main tank fuel appeared to be feeding at approximately twice the rate of the left and it appeared that the center tank fuel was not feeding at all. Engine fuel flow readings appeared normal and equal. By 10,000 feet MSL the imbalance had increased to the point that the IMBAL alert was activated. We coordinated with ATC to level off at 12,000 feet MSL in order to reduce fuel flow and begin taking appropriate action. Upon leveling at 12,000 feet MSL we observed the right fuel tank quantity feed rate appear to normalize and then observed a 50 pound increase in right main tank fuel quantity. Fuel imbalance at that point was 1,100 pounds. We initiated the IMBAL checklist in the QRH. Observing what then appeared to be normal tank quantity indications for a crossfeed operation, we coordinated with ATC to continue our climb and proceed on course to the destination knowing we would also coordinate with Dispatch and maintenance promptly to determine if proceeding to ZZZ1 was advisable. As soon as we established climb fuel flow settings and pitch attitude, the right main tank quantity began to decrease again at what appeared to be the same abnormal rate observed during the initial climb. We then requested priority handling initiated the return to ZZZ (nearest airport) and started the APU as a precaution. I advised Dispatch, the cabin crew, and the passengers of the situation using SOP. As soon as we stopped the climb to initiate the return to ZZZ the right main fuel tank indication again stopped decreasing at an abnormal rate. We concluded the IMBAL checklist and for the remainder of the flight we observed no further indications of abnormal rate of decrease in right main wing tank fuel. During the descent, I spoke with the flight attendants and they confirmed that there were no visible indications of fuel leaks on either wing or engine. We landed uneventfully at ZZZ with a total imbalance of 1,220 pounds with 8,370 pounds in the left wing, 7,150 pounds in the right wing and 13,470 pounds in the center tank - 29,000 points total. The aircraft was below max landing weight at touchdown. ARFF (Airport Rescue and Firefighting) personnel were standing by as requested. After landing and before further taxi, ARFF personnel inspected the aircraft closely and reported no leaks, no visible damage, and no abnormalities. We shut down the number 2 engine as an additional precaution and requested that the ARFF vehicles follow us to our gate. Maintenance personnel were debriefed in person at the gate upon arrival.Captain suspects suspect faulty fuel crossfeed valve.Reporter recommends company consider revisiting the IMBAL checklist in light of this particular malfunction.				B73-800 Captain reported an uneventful air return due to an airborne fuel system malfunction during initial climb.	
1787327	202101	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1787327												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	All crews should be informed and knowledgeable about emergency procedures in order to be prepared for such unexpected events in flight.After an uneventful preflight preparation and exterior inspection we departed ZZZ, myself as Pilot Flying and the Captain as Pilot Monitoring. We climbed to planned cruise altitude without issue. Shortly after reaching cruise and just south of ZZZ1, an ECAM message and associated chime presented on the lower ECAM screen indicating 'ENG 1 SYSTEM FAULT'. We worked through the indicated actions and referenced the COM procedures. As the engine was reduced to idle thrust only, we began a descent and began diversion planning as it became clear that we could not continue to ZZZ2 with this issue at the lower altitude required on a single engine at ZZZ3. Initially ZZZ was selected, but while I was flying and communicating with ATC the Captain in consultation with the dispatcher elected to divert instead to ZZZ4. With plenty of time to prepare we did all necessary tasks for the diversion and proceeded to an uneventful landing on ZZZ's longest runway, XXR, with fire/rescue services on standby. After landing with no issues we taxied to the assigned gate and shut down the aircraft without issue.				A320 First Officer reported an in-flight engine problem required a diversion and single-engine approach and landing.	
1788048	202102	1801-2400	D01.TRACON	CO	030	45		18000		Mixed			Night	10000		TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	STAR CLASH3	Class B DEN																												TRACON D01	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing			Part 121	IFR	Other unknown		Descent		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Type 75		Situational Awareness; Distraction			1788048												Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Aircraft	Human Factors	On a descent into DEN, we were cleared to descend via the CLASH 3 Arrival. I confirmed that the final altitude was 9,000 ft. and selected it in the altitude window. About 10 miles from EVRLY, we experienced wake turbulence for about five seconds, and then experienced it again. I selected Vertical Speed and leveled off the descent to get above the wake turbulence. After we were clear of the wake turbulence, I selected a 2,000 FPM descent to get back on my vertical path. At that time, we were told to switch to Denver Approach. Upon checking in with Denver Approach they changed our runway to 35L. At this point I was concerned about changing the approach in the FMC. I forgot to re-engage the VNAV. Because I forgot to re-engage the VNAV, the aircraft did not slow down to 250 kts for EVRLY. The aircraft also continued a descent below 12,000 feet., which was the lowest altitude at TWNSN. The aircraft continue to descend until I notice the deviation. By this time I was leveling at 10,000 feet. and was below the 11,000 feet. altitude required for PNBAL. ATC advised me to slow the 210 knots, and gave me a vector for the final approach to Runway 35L. They also gave me a descent to 9,000 feet. The rest of the arrival went as planned.				B737 Captain reported distractions from a wake turbulence encounter on arrival into DEN contributed to altitude and speed deviations.	
1788532	202102	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			800			VMC	Wind					Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach; Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Aileron		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1788532												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	At 800 feet AGL, clicked autopilot off for a visual landing. At 500 ft., FO called 'Stable, cleared to land Runway XX Center.' I replied 'Landing.' Between 500 and 400 feet AGL tried to make small lateral correction with aileron but aileron movement was limited. Used a touch of rudder for correction. Clicked autopilot button again and crossed checked then confirmed that 'Flight Director' was displayed in PFD. Landed uneventfully but elevator movement was very heavy in flare. After landing at taxi speed, I tried to move the yoke left and right and there was very limited movement. The First Officer tried as well with the same result. We checked the Flight Control and Hydraulic Displays and both were normal.I considered a go-around but quickly [determined] a go around would be extremely difficult with big power changes and limited flight control capability. The flight path was set up to touch down in the landing zone on speed so I elected to land.Unsure what caused the flight controls to be limited. After landing, I debriefed Maintenance in the cockpit and put a detailed write up in the logbook.				B777-200 Captain reported aileron flight control stiff during landing and limited movement after landing.	
1788606	202102	1201-1800	D01.TRACON	CO	030	10		10000		IMC			Dusk			TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B DEN																												TRACON D01	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR			Descent		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer				Distraction; Situational Awareness			1788606												Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control		Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	While descending on the CLASH Arrival just prior to EVRLY we encountered a wake turbulence event 2x in short succession with roll deviation. Autopilot disconnected, was immediately reconnected by the Captain (PF). He disconnected VNAV and initiated Vertical Speed mode to reset our descent path to be slightly above the previous aircraft path, to prevent a third event. About this time, ATC offered us a runway change from 35R to 35L, additionally they requested us to slow to 210 kts. The Captain went to reprogram the FMS for Runway 35L, I was confirming the Captain inputs at this time. The aircraft was still in Vert Speed rather than VNAV, neither of us noticed this and we descended below the STAR required altitude and did not slow to 210 kts. as directed. A query from ATC as to our speed brought us back to reviewing our flight path, at which point we leveled off and reduced speed. ATC made no further comments as to our flight path deviation and at that point broke us off from the STAR with a vector to intercept the LOC. No loss of ATC separation was reported to us.				B737 First Officer reported speed and altitude deviations on arrival into DEN, citing distraction from a wake turbulence encounter as contributing.	
1788808	202102		ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Landing Gear			Malfunctioning; Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 42; Flight Crew Total 3418; Flight Crew Type 3418		Troubleshooting; Workload			1788808												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	On approach to ZZZ gear was selected down but normal gear indications did not appear and there were no associated noises such as gear extending into slipstream, etc. PF (Pilot Flying) executed a missed approach and while being vectored we tried to lower the gear normally with the same indications and we got the L/G Gear Not Down locked ECAM. Accomplishing ECAM led us to the L/G Gravity Extension QRH and after accomplishing checklist the gear safely extended. We (advised ATC) and coordinated with Flight Attendants and Dispatch and ZZZ for subsequent runway closure and tow to the gate.				A320 Captain reported a landing gear malfunction.	
1788957	202102	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				6500		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Gear Down Lock		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 89; Flight Crew Total 4693; Flight Crew Type 2898		Troubleshooting			1788957												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Aircraft X was uneventful leading up to the approach at ZZZ. We had been cleared for the approach to Runway XX and were established on the localizer and glideslope. The autopilot and autothrust had been disengaged earlier in the approach and the aircraft had been configured to Flaps 3 when Gear Down was requested. When the gear lever was selected down the three indicators on the landing gear panel indicated red UNLK (UNLOCKED), no noise from the landing gear doors opening was heard. Captain [Name] and I communicated and confirmed on the Landing Gear Systems page that the landing gear had not extended. We decided to level out at 6,000 feet MSL while we confirmed the situation and attempted to cycle the gear. We communicated to the tower we would be going around for a landing gear failure approximately 3mi prior to the runway. Tower provided vectors and a climb to 9,000 feet, we initiated a go around over the threshold per ATC instructions and cleaned up the aircraft per SOP. The autoflight was re-engaged after the go around. During this time after the failure of subsequent attempts to cycle the gear and with the lever still down, we eventually got the L/G GEAR NOT DOWNLOCKED ECAM. The ECAM was run according to SOP by Captain [Name] while I flew the aircraft and handled radio calls. We [received priority], stated our intention to return to land on Runway XYR and requested CFR (Crash Fire Rescue Equipment). Gravity extension was successful and we were vectored to the approach. Approach and landing were normal with the exception of the gear doors remaining open. We landed with 8000 lbs. of fuel. We stopped on the runway and Operations was standing by to tow the aircraft back to the gate.				First Officer reported a Go Around and precautionary  landing after landing gear lock problems.	
1789423	202102	0001-0600								VMC			Dawn				Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 116; Flight Crew Total 396; Flight Crew Type 182					1789423												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was deadheading in the cabin when I was asked by the Captain to come to the flight deck due to an [urgent situation]. By time I reached the flight deck the number two engine was already shut down. I assisted the primary crew anyway possible to lessen the workload and stress. I assisted with cabin and airport operations communication, checklist completion, and any other tasks the crew needed help with. The primary crew did a fantastic job and needed very little assistance.				Deadheading Pilot reported being asked to come to the Flight Deck and act as an ACM (Additional Crew Member) during an inflight event.	
1789839	202102	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ	N	Y	Y	Y	Unscheduled Maintenance	Inspection; Testing																		Flap/Slat Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Type 3800		Workload; Troubleshooting			1789839	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Troubleshooting			1789841	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	Arriving at the aircraft and checking the logbook, I noticed that the last crew had written that the Takeoff Configuration light and horn activated on takeoff (speed of activation was not noted). I surmised that the crew rejected the takeoff and subsequently swapped out of the aircraft after returning to the gate and the aircraft was not flown afterwards. The corrective action was a check of the Takeoff Configuration Warning System with no faults noted. Since nothing was actually corrected, the Captain and I discussed the possibility of it activating again on our takeoff. We pushed late due to an unrelated finding of damage in the floor of the Forward Cargo Bin C. The bin was deferred and bags transferred to the aft bin. We were relatively full on this flight and our V1/VR speeds for Flaps 1 on Runway XXR were 125/135. The engine start and taxi out were uneventful with the throttle burst yielding no takeoff warning. On the takeoff roll at approximately 130 knot (5 knot after V1) the takeoff warning horn and light activated. We elected to continue the takeoff. The initial climb was normal and we discussed that we would be writing this up in ZZZ1. The Captain called for 'flaps up' at the appropriate speed and I moved the flap lever to UP. We had a few distractions from ATC due to hold-down altitude that was cancelled. Before I moved the landing gear handle to OFF, I checked the flap indicator and noticed that the flaps were still at 1 with the LE FLAP EXT green light illuminated. After confirming that the flap handle was up, I told the Captain the issue and that we need to keep our airspeed below 250 kts. and level off to handle the problem.After notifying ATC that we would need to level off, they gave us vectors for terrain while we ran the QRH for Trailing Edge Flap Disagree. Since the QRH doesn't really address the disagreement when attempting to RETRACT the flaps and only deals with setting the flaps for landing, we turned our attention to coordinating the air return. Dispatch was advised of the return as well as Station Ops in ZZZ. Our current gross weight was a little over 130,000 pounds so we extended the gear to add drag and burn off excess fuel for landing. The F/As (Flight Attendants) and passengers were notified of the return. Since this was a flight control issue, we [received priority handling] and notified ATC that we were requesting [Runway] XYL but would need time to reduce our landing weight to 128,000 pounds. We felt this was the safe option as time was not an issue. We then ran the QRH, ran the performance calculation for the Trailing Edge Flap Disagree QRH, and then the brake cooling module. The flaps extended normally with the Alternate Flap system to 15 degrees. The only slight issue was although I read the section directing the flap lever to be moved to the final flap position (15), we were sidetracked by discussing slowing during the extension and this was not done until after the QRH was completed. This allowed us to see our Flaps 15 maneuvering speed. After the delay vectors were complete and we were below the maximum landing weight, we made an uneventful landing on [Runway] XYL. Brake cooling allowed us to take right to the gate and the ARFF (Airport Rescue and Fire Fighting) trucks followed. No other issues occurred. We swapped into another aircraft and continued the pairing.		The aircraft was an originator. The previous day a takeoff had been rejected because of a spurious Takeoff Config Warning and the maintenance resolution was no faults noted. The F/O (First Officer) and I discussed possible reasons for a false warning lasting a few seconds, which refreshed our memories of the warning system and thankfully prepared us for a repeat occurrence. We pushed late due to an unrelated finding of damage in the floor of the forward cargo bin. The bin was deferred and bags transferred to the aft bin. On takeoff, approximately two seconds after the F/O's V1 call, the configuration light illuminated and the warning horn sounded. The F/O called VR, and we lifted off. The light went out and the horn stopped about the time the wheels left the ground. The F/O's performance and adhering to procedure during this event was flawless, which combined with our earlier discussion allowed the situation to be handled easily. We briefly mentioned writing the issue up again in ZZZ1. I called for Flaps Up, from their takeoff '1' setting. F/O moved the flap lever to Up, and then moments later told me the flaps didn't go up, and instructed me not to go over 250 [kts.]. We were still at about 200 knots, so no airspeed issues. I told F/O to tell ATC we needed to level off and maintain low airspeed. Initially we told ATC we would follow the SID and get back to them. ATC gave us a terrain clearance altitude. We confirmed the flaps were not up. I called for the QRH and we agreed that 'Flap Disagree' was the appropriate checklist. The checklist starts off by saying 'to prepare for landing' so we paused the checklist, agreed we were not going to ZZZ1 with flaps out, and I messaged Dispatch we were returning to ZZZ, told ATC, and received clearance to return to ZZZ. I notified the F/A (Flight Attendants) and told the passengers we were returning to ZZZ. We ran the QRH for Flaps Disagree, the procedure went mostly uneventfully. We were at 130,000 pounds, so we took delaying vectors to burn fuel to 128,000 [lbs.]. We checked the brake cooling for Autobrake 3, no issues. We did [receive priority handling]. We landed Flaps 15 and taxied to the gate. Positive takeaways. The CQT training from (year)?, (year)? that emphasized flap malfunctions prepared us well to handle the stuck flaps. Negative takeaways. The QRH for Flap Disagree was obviously written with a failure occurring during approach in mind. For our situation of failure on takeoff, the procedure kept us with the flap handle up until we had returned to ZZZ and were preparing to land, which meant we had flaps out, but no maneuvering speed. We know that Flaps 1 speed is 180 knots approximately, but it would have been one more item in our favor if we had moved the flap handle to match the flaps to give us the normal sight picture on the displays. While we were extending the flaps with the alternate switch, between watching the flap gauge and calls from ATC, we momentarily missed the instruction in the QRH to move the flap handle with the flap movement. We followed proper flap speeds as flaps were extending, but that that was because of our experience, not the display. The QRH should have the instruction to move flap handle with flaps before the actual instruction to move the flaps so that crews can discuss the handle in advance. We ran the brake cooling for Autobrake 3, and then tried to run again for Autobrake 2. The brake cooling module did not work the second time. We recalled a recent (Company communication piece) about Dispatch doing something to mess up performance calculation results, which could be rectified by requesting landing data again, but that did not work. Something in the performance calculations needs to be corrected so that brake cooling can be run more than once. Brake cooling was not an issue, even with Autobrake 3, so this was a small nuisance. Based on passenger comments, F/As did a great job. F/O's performance was excellent, our teamwork together allowed the situation to be handled easily.		Flight Crew reported a repeat failure of the Take Off Warning System and a failure of trailing edge flaps to retract caused an Air Tun Back and a precautionary emergency landing.	
1789931	202102	0601-1200	ZLA.ARTCC	CA				31000								Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZLA																												Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZLA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1789931												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	We were at FL320 on the HLYWD arrival into LAX, and we had been given a vector off course for spacing. Upon rejoining the arrival we were given slower speeds until we actually saw an aircraft 5 nm in front of us. We were then assigned a speed of 250 kts indicated, then descend to FL240, followed by a clearance to descend via the arrival. I was concerned as we started our descent as we were clearly descending at a higher rate than the aircraft ahead of us and would be flying through their flight path at a close proximity plus winds at altitude were approximately 110kts. Suddenly our airplane began a roll to the right, I would estimate 20-25 degrees of bank, then it began rolling to the left at least 30 degrees of bank if not more and the autopilot disconnected itself. We were having an upset due to wake from the aircraft ahead of us so the Captain quickly recovered and I advised ATC what was happening and that we needed something different. They approved an offset to our course, and then advised the aircraft ahead was a B777. 5nm is inadequate spacing between a heavy aircraft and others. Fortunately we recovered well and landed safely. 99 percent of the time our folks in ATC do an absolutely outstanding job, and we couldn't do this without them! This event is unusual, but highlights an opportunity we can improve.I would also like to see better communication from ATC and a better understanding of the safety ramifications of wake turbulence - I frequently get the impression that they don't understand this risk adequately. I want to know if I'm in trail of a heavy aircraft! Sooner than later! It matters, it changes my expectations and I'm not consistently getting this information. I recommend more spacing, more and consistent communication about heavy aircraft ahead, and if I need to be vectored for spacing then let's address speed at the same time so I'm not constantly changing my configuration when I need to focus on flying.				B737 First Officer reported encountering wake turbulence on descent into LAX in trail of a B777.	
1790145	202102	0601-1200	ZZZZ1.ARTCC	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZZ1	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZZ																								EICAS/EAD/ECAM		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1790145												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Prior to departure Maintenance was called due to FOs (First Officer's) MAP failure on navigation display. Maintenance did a reset and signed off logbook as reset ok for service. FOs MAP was restored. Approximately 2 hours into flight and approximately 30 mins from ZZZZ1 enroute to ZZZZ, FOs MAP failed, followed by Captain's CWS (control wheel steering) fail, autothrottle failure, and SINGLE FMC operation message. Very shortly later, approximately 10 seconds, LNAV/ VNAV failed, both FMCs failed. Opposite autopilot selected, autopilot B. Captain's CWS was restored and one FMC was restored allowing for the engagement of LNAV/VNAV, and autothrottle was restored. FOs MAP still indicated a failure. Within approximately 60 seconds we had another dual FMC failure, followed by another LNAV/ VNAV failure, autothrottle failure, Captain's CWS failure. At this time with over two hours left to ZZZZ and navigating by raw data we decided it was the best course of action to divert to ZZZZ1 where the weather was favorable for landing with these navigational failures. We notified ZZZZ1 Center of our issues and intent to land in ZZZZ1. Around this time the Captain's FMC was restored automatically and we were once again able to navigate with LNAV/VNAV and the autothrottle and Captain's CWS was restored and autopilot was operational. As we neared ZZZZ1 we informed ATC of the need to hold to burn off fuel due to being overweight for landing which took approximately 10 minutes. The remainder of the flight was uneventful, we landed on Runway X and commenced the ILS approach in VFR conditions. The FOs MAP warning flag remained in view for the rest of the flight. My FO, Fas (Flight Attendants) did outstanding jobs. Great communication. ZZZZ1 ATC also very professional and helpful. In the 21 years I've been on the 737 both as an CA (Captain) and FO I've never experienced a dual FMC failure. I've experienced several single FMC failures, but not dual failures leading to several other navigation failures.				Captain reported a diversion to a precautionary landing following the loss of both Flight Management Computers which caused the loss of Cockpit Display Units and most navigation systems.	
1790183	202102	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US						IMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Pressurization Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1790183												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Company Policy; Aircraft	Aircraft	Captain (CA)=Pilot Monitoring (PM), First Officer (FO)=Pilot Flying (PF). Discovered pressurization outflow valve in the full open position during the after takeoff checklist (no Auto Fail lights, both Packs, ISO valve and bleeds all in proper position). Discussed with the FO and advised ATC we needed to level off at 10,000 ft to investigate an issue and asked for straight ahead to avoid terrain (initially assigned 15,000 ft on the SID). FO was PF and had them take the radio. Pulled the QRH and referenced AUTO Fail or Unscheduled Pressurization Change 2.2. At this point we discussed and surmised we did NOT have an AUTO Fail issue nor and unscheduled pressurization change as the Cabin appeared to be modestly, although suspiciously pressurizing, as we were building some differential and the cabin altitude was relatively low.  Troubleshooting forbids switch manipulation unless inclusive of a QRH or other written procedure hence we chose not to manipulate cabin pressurization controller from AUTO to ATLN. Additionally, we recalled a bulletin from Standards Department dated 7/xx/2020 titled 'Cabin Pressure Controller Blinking/Blanking issue'. This bulletin states that on our 900ER fleet from (tails)(we were in tail) the digital controller can blank and or blink or become so dim its unreadable. Additionally, the bulletin states the controller should right itself shortly...We did not have any blinking/blanking or dimness. Our outflow indicator was bright and solid indicating full open and remained so for the duration of our flight. In an attempt to determine if the cabin would hold we elected to continue our climb to 12,000 ft at which point we got a significant bump felt by the entire crew.... At this point I attempted to contact Dispatch via ACARS to establish two way radio communications. We had plodded along south at a low altitude for a good duration in an attempt to solve the issue. I became concerned with our flight plan fuel. We were dispatched with no reserve for ZZZ1 (weather severe clear at arrival time). Was there enough to complete the mission or might we find ourselves having to divert for fuel 'if' we felt the cabin psi was solid enabling us to continue? We we're now down near (mountain) at 12,000 ft IMC picking up a fair amount of ice as weather was now an issue. After multiple attempts to get a hold of Dispatch to no avail, Flight Attendants complaining about ears, a QRH procedure that didn't quite fit, no ability to troubleshoot, a bulletin that didn't match enough to convince me and a possible fuel stop with an open maintenance issue stranding passengers....in consultation w/ my FO I made the command decision to return to ZZZ. In my mind, this was the safest option and provided our passengers an opportunity to reset. Additionally, it brought the aircraft back to a maintenance base for investigation and repair. We informed Dispatch via ACARS and setup to return and received an amended release via ACARS. We did not declare an emergency as it did not appear warranted/necessary. We ran performance including our LTP (landing threshold point), discussed our TPC and landed significantly under max landing weight as we had a light load. During block in as L1 was opened we felt another pressure bump despite the outflow valve remaining in the full open position. I greeted all deplaning passengers and answered questions, debriefed full crew and discussed scenario and concerns. Flight Attendants met with supervisor and were replaced. No crew members/ nor passengers asked for medical attention. Swapped aircraft, checked duty time and pressed on.Lesson learned:I should have used SATCOM to raise Dispatch in lieu of ACARS. We traveled a fair ways south and company frequency became weak. We switched to XXX.X and sent a message but never could get radio contact. We became distracted by the accumulation of ice buildup at that point in the journey. Bulletin concern: If it is related to the digital controller bulletin why would the company continue to allow problematic controllers be installed? NOTE: The original design Pressurization Control Panel may be installed in any 9ER aircraft from (tail) and subsequent as a repair for a Pressurization Control Panel issue. Therefore, even though (tail) and subs were delivered with the upgraded panel, this issue may occur on these aircraft if the upgraded panel has been replaced with an original panel.				Captain reported aircraft failed to pressurize correctly on takeoff and after trouble shooting, elected to do an Air Turn Back.	
1791268	202102	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			1100									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Check Pilot; Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1791268												Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	I was pilot monitoring on this flight. ATC was giving us vectors for a visual approach to RWY XX at ZZZ. We were at 2,000 feet on a right base between waypoints ZZZZZ and ZZZZZ1 (1,500 feet MSL) when we told Approach we had the airport in sight. We were cleared for the visual and told to contact Tower. I said 'glide slope alive' and the First Officer asked for gear down flaps 15. I looked down to select the Tower frequency (which I didn't have preselected) and told them we were on the visual for RWY XX. We were cleared to land. When I saw we were capturing the localizer I noticed that the glide slope was now full scale deflection (low). The First Officer had selected a lower altitude (1,100 feet) on the MCP without telling me and so we were below the final approach fix altitude of 1,500 feet at ZZZZZ1. I called for a go around and we were vectored back around for a successful landing. The cause was a long flight across the country with last minute vectors to final for a visual approach led to the First Officer thinking we were high nearing the FAF and incorrectly setting an altitude lower than glideslope intercept altitude without verbally advising the Captain.				B-737-800 Captain reported an uneventful 'go around' due to a CFIT event during final approach.	
1791837	202102	1201-1800	ZZZ.Tower	US				100		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 180; Flight Crew Type 3300					1791837	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1790897	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was Pilot Flying. Just after liftoff, the nose trim ran high speed nose up on its own. I waited a quick moment and told the Captain that I was not doing that trim command, and then pushed forward on the yoke to stop the runaway. After retracting the flaps, I relaxed forward pressure and the trim continued nose up runaway at slow speed. I pressed the electric time nose down on the yoke, and the up trim stopped its uncommanded nose up runaway. Then we ran the Stabilizer Runaway Checklist and returned back to ZZZ uneventfully.		During takeoff from ZZZ, at approximately 300 ft. AGL, the aircraft trim began running continuously, high-speed, in the 'Nose-Up' direction. The F/O (First Officer) stopped the trim by pushing forward on the yoke. This action stopped the trim movement. At 1,200 ft. AGL, after retracting the flaps, the trim began running again, continuously 'Nose-Up' in the slow-speed mode. This time the F/O used the Electrical Trim switch in the opposite direction and this action stopped the movement. We ran the Runaway Stabilizer Checklist and returned to ZZZ for an uneventful approach and landing.		Pilot reported a runaway stabilizer trim system and elected to air turn back and make a precautionary emergency landing.	
1792349	202103	0601-1200	RNO.Airport	NV	180	1	500						Daylight			Tower RNO	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class C RNO																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 180; Flight Crew Type 2000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1792349	Aircraft X	Flight Deck		Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 103; Flight Crew Type 103		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1792351	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	Moderate turbulence below 14,000 feet.  Heavy winds as well. 290/19 G29 on the ground.  Cleared Visual Approach 34L. Approach was normal, but Captain very busy dealing with speed fluctuations and turbulence.  I was double checking the winds with Tower. Watching terrain as well.  Adjusted REF speeds for gusts and prevailing winds. Captain called for flaps 30, I inadvertently moved flap handle to 25 and did not know it.  Everything was good until short final, we got "Too Low Flaps" aural alert.  We executed go-around immediately. Need to look down and see if correct flap detention is selected.		On flight to RNO we received a Too Low Flaps warning from the GPWS around 500 feet AGL on an approach to Runway 34L. We had continuous moderate turbulence below 13,000 feet MSL on our approach and that was distracting. We were on a high right base leg because of the terrain. We were cleared for the visual on the right base leg. Also, on base we were gear down flaps 15, and near flaps 15 approach speed. I called for flaps 30 and the Before Landing checklist well above 1,000 feet AGL, because I wanted the extra drag. I did not notice the flaps were at position 25 during the Before Landing checklist. Our REF speed was plus 9 and then increased to plus 15 with the new Tower winds.  At about 500 feet AGL we received the Too Low Flaps warning and I did a go-round. We were vectored for another approach to Runway 34L and did a normal landing.I felt task loaded on the approach. I was concerned about the turbulence, terrain, and changing surface winds. I let these additive condition distract me while doing the Before Landing Check List.  I should have bought myself more time by asking for a longer down wind on the approach.		B-737 flight crew reported executing a go-around upon receipt of a configuration GPWS warning.	
1792355	202103	1201-1800	SAN.Airport	CA			200				Cloudy		Daylight			Tower SAN	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B SAN																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 1900		Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1792355	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 80; Flight Crew Type 6000		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1792372	Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning		Flight Crew Landed As Precaution	Human Factors; Weather	Human Factors	Visual to 27 at SAN using RNAV Z. Ceiling was BKN015. Crew briefed visual procedures at altitude but was informed that ATIS had changed late in the descent, crew adjusted minimums to account for the updated ceiling. Earlier I had queried the Captain if he preferred flaps 40 for SAN and he said he wanted flaps 30. Vref was 141 knots, wind component added 6 knots for a VTARGET of 147 knots. Captain configured early and we were stable at 1,000 feet AGL with a slightly high VVI (Vertical Velocity Indicator) due to SAN Runway 27 3.5 degree approach and the high weight and subsequent high approach speed.  We were visual at that point, I think we broke out at 1,000-1,200 feet. Every time I crosschecked the speed and VVI, the Captain was stable, with the knowledge that the 3.5 degree approach and speed (as mentioned in the Safety Alert) would result in VVI rates over 1,000 feet. I was crosschecking radar altitudes and glideslope guidance more than I usually do, but this is my personal habit for SAN. As I did the normal 'in and out' visual crosscheck, nothing seemed amiss. Directly over or slightly after the parking deck on short final, the Captain slightly adjusted his visual aim point as is common technique at SAN. My eyes were outside at this point. We both heard a 'sink rate...'. I don't know what the second phrase was, as the Captain acknowledged the first sink rate forcefully and almost instantly. I quickly looked inside and I think I saw 'pull up' on the bottom of the PFD for what seemed like an instant. I can't be certain, however, and it can't be understated how fast it went away if indeed it was there. I looked back up and we were two red and two white on the PAPI, slightly fast, and Captain was in what looked like a good position to land. We made a safe landing and applied moderate braking and exited the runway. In the postflight, the Captain and I disagreed about what the second term was at the aural system annunciated. He thinks he heard 'sink rate, sink rate.' I actually don't know what the second term the aural system annunciated was, as I didn't hear it clearly due to the Captain's acknowledgment of it. I do think I saw a very fleeting 'pull up' on the PFD, but I can't be perfectly certain, and Captain is adamant that we didn't get the GPWS warning, only the sink rate caution. I want to be clear that I understand the limitation with regard to the warning annunciator. In hindsight, I can see now that a go-around could have been a prudent decision even though the aircraft appeared to be a safe and normal position for short final at SAN. I want to highlight this issue for a few reasons. It bears repeating that phase of flight, temporal distortion, and flight deck confusion can all conspire to create a situation where two trained and alert crew members can see different things. The other thing I want to highlight is how heavy -800's, going into SAN, can potentially be affected by a flaps 30 approach. I don't know why we don't have recommended flap settings for certain situations. We've all been flying light aircraft for almost a year now and perhaps it would help to remind crews about heavy aircraft with higher approach airspeeds and subsequently higher VVIs in higher altitude environments or steeper approaches like SAN.		On approach to SAN when transitioning outside, we briefly received a sink rate call. At the gate, the First Officer was unsure if it was just a sink rate call or sink rate pull up. I adjusted sink rate and landed normally.		B737-800 Flight Crew reported on an unstable approach receiving two cautionary messages the were missed by flight crew.	
1792383	202103	0601-1200	SMF.Airport	CA	045	30		37000		VMC	Turbulence		Daylight			Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb	SID SCTWN 3	Class A ZOA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1792383	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 154; Flight Crew Type 1823		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Other		1792361	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather; Environment - Non Weather Related	Weather	During climb out of SMF, everything was normal with some occasional light chop. We had asked the Flight Attendants to remain seated due to crossing the Sierra Mountains. We were climbing at approximately .76 Mach. Upon leveling at FL 370, we experienced moderate bordering on severe mountain wave. Our airspeed began decreasing rapidly and the F/O (First Officer) disconnected the autopilot, applied full thrust, and began a shallow descent to regain airspeed. The airspeed decayed to about .70 - .71. It was about a 30 knot decrease. I may be a little off on the Mach numbers. We descended about 500' and the airspeed began increasing.  I reported the encounter to ATC and we began climbing back to FL 370. The wave climbed us to FL 375, and at that time we requested a descent to FL 350. The Controller cleared us to descend to FL 350. We did not encounter any additional mountain wave and nothing more than a little light chop. We checked with the Flight Attendants to make sure everyone was alright, and the remainder of the flight was uneventful. I would like to add that the F/O did an excellent job of recognizing and reacting to the situation.		We had just leveled off at our cruise altitude of FL 370. (Filed for FL 390 but we were too heavy at that time.) During climb we had anticipated and encounter some light turbulence. Nothing more significant was encountered. WSI (Weather Service International) and Jeppesen weather showed areas of turbulence covering about the first 45 minutes of our flight. Our clearance was on the SCTWN 3 RNAV Departure. We were level at FL 370 when suddenly our airspeed had dropped from M.76 (250 KIAS) to 220 KIAS this was approximately 10-15 above the yellow maneuvering bar on the airspeed indicator and the green vector bar was indicating that the slowing was continuing and at a significant rate. I disconnected the autopilot and autothrottle and pitched down to keep airspeed. I also increased thrust to max and I was able to recover airspeed about 500' low at FL 365. In my attempt to climb back to FL 370 we started climbing at a significant rate and stopped the climb at approximately FL 376. The Captain reported to ATC that we were in MVA (mountain wave activity) and that we were unable to hold altitude. I continued to hand fly until I was sure we were passed the MVA and re-engaged the autopilot. The rest of the flight was uneventful.		B737-700 flight crew reported encountering moderate to severe mountain wave for which led to a rapid decay of their airspeed.	
1792387	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ1																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1792387												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	Departed ZZZ without Dispatch release remark of 'HAZMAT on flight - refer to [Dangerous Goods Form]'. When I discovered the error I notified Dispatch via ACARS.				B737-700 Captain reported Dispatch release missing required Hazmat information.	
1792922	202103	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US									Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Time Pressure			1792922												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	About one hour from landing, we started to smell a faint burning scent from the packs. It was very faint, but noticeable. It smelled like glycol or plastic burning. It remained constant until we initiated a descent from FL390-FL370. The smell became more pronounced. For lack of another checklist or procedure, we ran the smoke fire and fumes QRH. The smell was only in the cockpit and not the cabin. Since we could not determine the source. We deemed it appropriate to take the safest course of action to expedite getting the aircraft on the ground with no undue delay. We [advised ATC] and asked for CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) to meet us to check for anything out of the ordinary upon landing. We initiated a high speed descent and approach, and performed a safe landing. CFR met the aircraft and checked it over and we taxied to the gate.				B737-800 First Officer reported a flight deck fumes event during cruise.  The crew ran the appropriate QRH checklist and continued to destination.	
1793127	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US		7		1600					Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach; Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Landing Gear Indicating System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 140; Flight Crew Type 3031					1793127												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was Pilot Flying. On approach to Runway XXR at ZZZ, after calling for the landing gear down and flaps 15, we noticed that we had three green and three red landing gear indications. The overhead panel showed three green indications. We were able to complete the QRH and Landing Checklist and determined that the landing gear was down and locked. We continued the approach with the intention of landing. At approximately 500 feet AGL, we received the oral warning 'TOO LOW GEAR.'I was no longer sure that the landing gear was down and locked.  I called for the go-around and executed the go-around procedure.  We decided to leave the landing gear down and cleaned the flaps up to 5 degrees.  The Captain was working the radios and informed ATC of the go-around.  ATC gave us a heading and cleared us to 2,000 feet. They asked for the reason of the go-around. The Captain informed them that we were having landing gear indication problems. The Captain then decided to request priority handling.We completed the QRH and were able to get three green indications without red indications. The Captain informed ATC we had solved the problem and could continue for another approach and landing.  ATC assigned us Runway XXL.  We set up the FMC, re-briefed the approach, completed all of the appropriate checklists, flew the approach, and landed uneventfully on Runway XXL.  We taxied to the gate, shut down the engines and completed the Shut Down Checklist. While at the gate we had realized that due to workload, we had forgotten to inform the flight attendants of the priority handling situation.To prevent this from happening again, we need to slow down, and complete all briefings to the Flight Attendants and Passenger cabin prior to landing. Just because we were able to solve the landing gear problem and workload was heavy, does not alleviate the duty to inform the Cabin Crew.				B737-700 First Officer reported a landing gear indication issue during approach resulting in a go-around.  The flight crew ran the QRH procedures, resolved the issues and landed safely on their second attempt.	
1793455	202103	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000			Mountain Wave					Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121		Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1793455												Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Weather	Weather	We were cruising at FL390 and we called the back for a break. I was the second pilot to exit the flight deck and while in the main cabin I noticed the pitch of the aircraft.  I then made a brief break and called to return to the flight deck.  Once the flight deck door opened and the A came out I noticed the thrust levers advancing to max power and the airspeed indicator slowly approaching the amber band.  I then immediately called for a lower altitude as I took my seat and once I was in my seat I looked at my First Officer and said we need lower now.  My First Officer then dialed a lower altitude on the FMC and selected descent via V/S (Vertical Speed). I then gathered all info on what we were experiencing and thought either we had an unreliable airspeed or runaway stabilizer.  As I looked back at our altitude, I realized we weren't descending and our airspeed was slowly approaching amber pole.  At that time I disconnected A/T (Autothrottle), A/P (Autopilot) and started a slow descent and requested my First Officer to call in our descent to ATC.  ATC then notified us that we needed to stop descent at FL370 due to lower aircraft.  My training kicked in and I figured if this went back we would need around 10,000 feet to recover so I then requested a vector off course.  We then were given 10 degrees left and I leveled off at FL370 continued to hand fly and one by one acquired A/P, HDG, VNAV.  Our Airspeed gained approximately 30kts and we were stable at FL370 on a heading.  ATC asked why the need to descend and I replied we encountered mountain wave and could not hold altitude. ATC then replied that we were the first to complain of mountain wave. Seconds later an aircraft at FL470 notified of +/- 1000 gain/loss and another airliner requesting lower due to mountain wave. We were then given direct to a waypoint on our flight plan and landed at destination without further incident. Be more vigilant while single pilot operations.				Air carrier Captain reported altitude and airspeed deviations as a result of mountain wave activity in cruise.	
1794414	202103	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				18000		IMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1794414	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1794423	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Weather; Aircraft	Aircraft	This flight was a planned departure from ZZZ to ZZZ1.  Weather was overcast, rainy with relatively calm winds and temperatures decreased during preflight from around 10C to 4C prior to departure. Pushback was delayed due to ramp congestion but only delayed takeoff slightly.  The Captain was operating as Pilot Flying (PF) for this flight.  The First Officer was Pilot Monitoring (PM).  Takeoff was uneventful. The departure was flown autopilot off.  While heading toward ZZZZZ and climbing at 280 KIAS, sometime after passing FL180 and climbing to FL230 in Instrument Meteorological Conditions (IMC), Boeing 737-800 aircraft experienced extreme airspeed indicator swings on both sides of the cockpit.  Indications wildly fluctuated between 300 KIAS down to 180 KIAS.  The PF asked the PM if his indications were normal and the PM responded that his indications appeared erroneous as well and he had an airspeed disagree indication. The pitot heat was verified ON. The autothrottles started to chase the airspeed fluctuations and were subsequently turned off.  PF maintained the climb power and pitch angle and asked the PM to get a higher altitude from ATC.  ATC responded with a 'standby for higher' response.  The PM attempted to select Vertical Speed and it would not engage.  All vertical guidance was unavailable.  The PF asked the PM for a groundspeed and the PM responded that he did not see one.  There was no groundspeed on the Captain's (PF) side as well.  The Standby Flight Instruments were difficult to read as they had been dimmed for the takeoff and now the sun was coming up. Simultaneously, we were approaching a turn from ZZZZZ to ZZZZZ1.  PF elected to maintain wings level and continued straight ahead as to not compound the problem by turning in IMC conditions with obvious airspeed errors.  Since the sun was just rising we could see the top of the weather was very close. As we approached FL230 we continued to climb and notified ATC.  ATC cleared us to FL380.  After reaching an altitude, probably FL240/FL250 in Visual Meteorological Conditions (VMC) the aircraft returned to nominal limits.  The flight continued under VMC to ZZZ1.  The aircraft flew normally after the event and landed safely.  Discrepancies were noted in the Aircraft Maintenance Logbook (AML) and maintenance personnel were debriefed by the Captain. Recommend a third air data inertial reference unit (ADIRU) on the B-737 so the bad ADIRU can be removed from the equation during a failure and still have a comparator feature.		Early morning departure out of ZZZ to ZZZ1. During climbout on the SID just after passing through the transition through FL180 I noticed we had lost the flight director and a yellow 'speed disagree' flag on the PFD (Primary Flight Display). I then looked at the MCP and VNAV/LNAV was off. Tried to re-engage and it wouldn't due to the PFD flag. About that time I noticed the speed had dropped from 280 KIAS to about 260 KIAS and we were off course having missed a slight turn on the SID. The Captain then said his was not right and asked me what mine said. I had said mine didn't seem correct either. Then he asked me what our ground speed was and I looked and mine was blank, not showing and wind/GS/TAS. At that time I told Center that we were having a problem with our instruments since we were off course and altitude. He came back and gave us present heading and FL230. The Captain then asked me to see if we could continue our climb since we weren't far from being on top of the weather, I asked and was told to stand by. A few seconds later Center gave us FL380. At this time I notified ATC having noticed we were getting further off our heading and the situation was getting worse. As we started to climb everything came back, air speeds matched, flag went away, was able to re engage LNAV/VNAV and flight directors. Few seconds after that we were on top in VMC. Everything came back just as quickly as we lost it. We were cleared to our next fix and resumed normal operations. We discussed our options and mutually decided that since there was clear weather for the rest of the way to ZZZ1 and everything was working that it was best to continue rather than turn back into the weather and return to ZZZ. The rest of the flight was uneventful and sent an ACARS message to let Maintenance know we would be writing the aircraft up when we landed. We landed in ZZZ1 and the Captain made the entry in the logbook when we got to the gate.		B737-800 flight crew reported Air Data Computer malfunction inflight.	
1794663	202103	1201-1800	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO												Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach; Final Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Workload; Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1794663	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer				Workload; Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface; Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1794664	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Chart Or Publication; Human Factors; Procedure; Company Policy	Human Factors	Turning final ZZZZ fully configured at 150 knots I armed the Localizer first than the Approach mode. First Officer inquired and received the landing clearance. I noticed that the glide slope didn't capture and that we were going above the path. I tried to descend in Vertical Speed, but it didn't come active, so it wouldn't work. Approach mode light still on. I disconnected the Autopilot, lowered the nose and started a descend using the VGSI (Visual Glideslope) path indicator and the outside PAPI (Precision Approach Path Indicator). During the descent we tried to reset the Approach/Glideslope unsuccessfully. The approach was pretty bumpy with some good downdrafts. The aircraft went below the glide slope and I started to correct back when the pull up warning came on then stopped right away. Then the glide slope warning followed. I was just under the path recovering and had 3 red lights and 1 white on the PAPI. Speed around 10 knots above Vref and decreasing toward Vref. I was very focused on the approach and continued to an uneventful normal landing.We were configured early, ready for approach, but the Glideslope not capturing and the uncertainty concerning the landing clearance created a distraction that put me behind the aircraft and I developed some tunnel vision trying to remedy the approach. I became fixated and continued when I should have discontinued the approach and gone around. After landing the First Officer said to me that she had mentioned going around, but it hadn't registered with me during the approach. I tell First Officers that there is no mission, but today I didn't follow my own advice. It's easy to become over focused without realizing it. When in doubt, go-around.		The event began while beginning the ILS Approach. The company notes on the approach dictate not to arm the ILS until reaching a certain position because of the possibility of false localizer signals. The Captain was the Pilot Flying and I was the Pilot Monitoring.  I remember thinking that the Pilot Flying armed the approach later than I would have since we were well beyond the designated point. My best guess on what happened is that because of the delay the localizer armed but the glideslope never captured because we were already too high. Whatever the reason for the automation not doing what we had expected, the result was that we realized that the Glideslope never captured and were trying to get the aircraft to come down.Not only were we distracted with being above the intended glide path, but we were handed over to tower late and I was busy with getting clearance to land while the Pilot Flying was flying the plane trying to get back to a stabilized approach. As the aircraft was getting back to a normal glide path (3 red, 1 white on PAPI) around the 500 foot mark I gave my stabilized approach callout and realizing we were slightly above speed and correcting, but the vertical speed exceeded what is acceptable. At this point I said 'I think we should go around'. I can't remember if it was just before or after I said this that we got a 'PULL UP' alert. The Captain elected to continue the landing. Later after discussion, I learned he may have had tunnel vision to the point where my go around call didn't register with him.My unfamiliarity with ZZZZ did not help our situational awareness as well as being distracted with getting switched over to tower (late) and attempting to get landing clearance around the same time we realized the automation did not perform as expected. As a FO/PM not being assertive enough when a go-around was needed.Abiding by company SOP to the letter. No matter what, at 500 feet if stabilized approach criteria aren't met, it's an automatic and firm 'GO AROUND'.		B737 flight crew reported they descended below the glidepath on their approach resulting in an unstable approach and a  terrain warning but the flying pilot continued the approach and landed.	
1794827	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		50		17500					Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Type 2452		Troubleshooting			1794827	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60; Flight Crew Type 55					1794573	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	We were cleared to FL 230. Passing through 17,500 feet, #1 engine rolled back to approximately 30%N1.  The N1 and EGT had flashing red and yellow arcs and the lower section had a red flashing box around both engine indications. Our initial analysis was engine failure.  We requested to level at FL 180, communicated to ATC our situation and requested vectors to return to ZZZ.  Upon further analysis of the engine condition, the Captain (Pilot Flying) stated the engine was still running but noted the indications were not normal. We also saw the EEC in ALTN mode.  Captain continued to fly and directed me into the QRH for the EEC ALT MODE checklist since the engine was running and not surging, stalling, or any indications dictating complete failure or the need to shutdown.  We completed the QRH for the EEC ALT MODE according to our situation and indications.  We discussed and agreed to also reference and ran the QRH [for] One Engine Inoperative Landing.  I continued communications with ATC, while the Captain communicated with F/A's and made a PA to passengers. I computed [landing] data and set up the FMC. Captain flew the approach and landing, taxiing clear of the runway to a safe location.  Autobrakes were used and no manual braking activated until slowed (approximately 50 knots) and prior to the exit of the runway.  Based on our weight and configuration [calculations] prior to landing required the use of autobrakes and brake cooling calculations.  I calculated the [landing data] for Non-Normal One engine Inop with 15 Flaps landing.   Brake Cooling calculations returned a 58 minute cooling period, which we accomplished at the turnout abeam runway.  Emergency Rescue Crews communicated to us no visible smoke from brakes and heat signature negative. After the 58 minute cool down, we were given clearance to taxi and proceeded to our gate.		When we arrived at the gate, Maintenance was working on the #1 engine. The previous crew that had the aircraft had returned to the gate. When they started the number one engine they got the Engine Control light. Maintenance cleaned the cannon plugs did an engine run and returned the aircraft to service. Our engine start and initial climb were all normal. We were on radar vectors for our route and climbing through 17,500 feet or so when the #1 N1 Gauge flashed yellow twice. We both saw it, then the #1 engine rolled back to 31% on the N1 but the engine didn't quit. We leveled at FL 180 and requested vectors back to ZZZ. We ran the EEC ALT Mode Checklist and the Engine Failure or Shutdown Checklist from the QRH. Landed Flaps 15, autobrakes 3. We ran the brake cooling calculation prior to landing. We had a 58 minutes of break cooling.		B737 flight crew reported returning to departure airport after experiencing #1 engine anomalies that reduced thrust to idle.	
1795361	202103	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				20000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZZZ																								Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1795361												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was the First Officer and the pilot monitoring. We were cleared to descend via the ZZZZZX. Approaching FL200 on the descent, I noticed my airspeed start to decrease rapidly without any change in airplane thrust or pitch. I called out 'airspeed' while inspecting the standby and Captain's airspeed indicator. We called unreliable airspeed and started the QRC (Quick Reference Checklist). We followed the QRC followed by the QRH (Quick Reference Handbook). We were able to confirm that the Captain's airspeed indicator was accurate based on the pitch and power settings and correlating with the standby airspeed indicator. The QRH instructed us to reengage the autopilot and change the ALT SOURCE selector to the Captain's side. This allowed my airspeed indicator to correspond with the Captain's and standby airspeed indicator. After we dictated which airspeed was accurate, ATC had given a heading and altitude to continue into ZZZ. I completed the QRH checklist for Unreliable Airspeed but kept the unreliable airspeed tables open to make sure the pitch and power settings correlated with our speed. We continued to fly the ILS approach into ZZZ without any further incident. The Captain notified Maintenance and Dispatch once we were blocked in and the door open. We were unable to conclude the cause that created the unreliable airspeed situation. But, I will become more familiar with the airspeed unreliable checklist and table so that I may refer to it and utilize it more efficiently in the event of another similar occurrence.				B737-800 First Officer reported unreliable airspeed indications on Captain's side during arrival descent.	
1797411	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Landing		Class D ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1797411												Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	As we arrived in ZZZ, the First Officer and myself had planned on the ILS to RWY (Runway) XX as the winds had favored that runway. The winds had shifted and favored RWY XY as we were handed off to approach control. As I was the flying pilot, the FO (First Officer) hastily entered the RNP to Runway XY. This was also our first time into ZZZ. As the aircraft captured Final App... it looked as if we were on profile... and we were also visual. I soon realized that the altitude constraints had not been loaded due to the fact that the approach via had not been entered. I arrested the descent, however, we did get the 'pull up" warning ....a go around was performed followed by the published hold. We then performed the ILS to RWY XX, circle to [Runway] XY to a normal landing.				Air carrier Flight Crew reported GPWS Alert on approach.	
1797878	202103	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				3500								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Descent		Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Time Pressure			1797878	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Troubleshooting			1797876	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	During cruise STAB OUT OF TRIM light illuminated intermittently.  Complied with STAB OUT OF TRIM QRH procedure.  Maintenance notified via ACARS. Descending with autopilot and autothrottle on, aircraft did not level off at the preselected 4,000 feet.  I disconnected the autopilot and autothrottle and began to return to 4,000 feet.  I tried the electric trim and found it to be inoperative.  I notified the Captain of the inoperative electric trim.  The Captain tried his electric trim and also found it to be inoperative. Captain conferred with me about disconnecting the STAB TRIM CUT OUT switches.  I agreed and the STAB TRIM CUT OUT switches were disconnected.  Captain opened the QRH and we complied with the STAB OUT OF TRIM QRH procedure.  The STAB OUT OF TRIM QRH procedure directed us to the STABILIZER TRIM INOPERATIVE procedure, which we complied with.  I tried to operate the manual trim with my left hand could not move the trim wheel through a full rotation.  I notified the Captain of this and he tried the manual trim.  Captain seem to require both hands to operate the manual trim.  Captain notified ATC and we requested delay vectors. We requested the longer Runway of XXR.  We performed a flaps 15 landing on XXR.  Descended below assigned altitude of 4,000 feet due to malfunctioning elevator trim.		This report is being submitted to cover the altitude deviation on approach into ZZZ. The altitude deviation was a direct result of a malfunction of the stabilizer/stabilizer trim system. The other required category reports will be covered in a separate irregularity report.Approximately two hours after departure and level at FL350, the STAB OUT OF TRIM (amber) light illuminated. We ran the STABILIZER OUT OF TRIM QRH procedure. Autopilot B was engaged and remained engaged while the light was eliminated, the aircraft maintained altitude and airspeed in light mountain wave and light chop conditions. Since the auto pilot appeared to be trimming the aircraft normally and in accordance with QRH, we continued normal operation. We notified maintenance control and Dispatch via ACARS and made a maintenance logbook entry for the condition. Dispatch acknowledged receiving the message. The light remained illuminated for approximately 10 mins. During this time both the first officer and myself reviewed not only the QRH, but Stabilizer Trim Inoperative QRH procedure in the event that these stabilizers stopped trimming at some subsequent point. We also reviewed be available information in both the flight handbook as well as the systems handbook.The STAB OUT OF TRIM light continued to illuminate intermittently in durations that ranged from several minutes to just a few seconds. We also climbed to FL370 with no abnormal indications and with the B autopilot remaining engaged. I observed some movement of the trim wheel at various times.During the cruise portion of the flight, the light was extinguished most of the time, but we did have the intermittent illuminations periodically. We informed dispatch and maintenance control that the light was illuminating intermittently.On the arrival into ZZZ we were given several intermediate level offs as well as crossing altitude and airspeed restrictions all of which were met with autopilot B engaged.While being vectored around some weather, for a visual approach to Runway XXL, we were kept somewhat high on the profile and were in a fairly aggressive dissent to 4,000 feet.  Autopilot B remained engaged throughout this descent. At 4,000 feet the flight directors commanded a level off but the aircraft continued to descend below 4,000 feet and did not capture the altitude, the first officer (pilot flying) immediately disengaged the auto pilot and auto throttles and began to correct The flight path, but due to the out of trim condition of the stabilizer the aircraft descended to approximately 3,500 feet before being able to level off the aircraft with considerable back pressure on the yoke.We informed ZZZ Approach that we had a stabilizer issue and were attempting to correct back to 4000 feet. They directed us to maintain 3000 feet. At about this time, the first officer informed me that 'I don't have any trim' and I saw him activate the yoke stabilizer trim switches for nose up and nose down trim with no movement of the stabilizer trim wheels. I attempted to trim the stabilizer using the yoke switches on my side with no effect as well. The first officer was holding a great deal of back pressure on the yolk and stated 'I need nose up trim' at this point with his concurrence after having verified that neither yolk could trim the stabilizer electrically, I selected both stabilizer trim cut out switches to cut out.At this point I told the first officer that he was going to continue to be the pilot flying and also have the radios while I reviewed the QRH procedures. After I verified that we had accomplished all of the steps and had been directed to the next QRH section. Well I was reviewing the QRH procedures the first officer attempted to trim the aircraft manually and stated 'I can't move the wheel'. It was at this point I believe where I [requested priority] with approach and told them that we had a stabilizer trim failure and we were attempting to trim the aircraft manually we requested and receiveda vector to do this and we reported our souls on board as well as fuel and requested ARFF assistance at the airport. At this point I attempted to trim the stabilizer nose up manually from my side but was unable to turn the stabilizer trim wheel. I directed the first officer to attempt to slow the aircraft to clean maneuver speed in an attempt to unload the stabilizer so that we could trim the aircraft manually. At some point approaching clean maneuver speed I was able to begin moving the trim wheel nose up but it was still quite difficult and required a significant two-handed effort to move the wheel at all. I managed to get several turn nose up trim and the first officer said 'that's good'. At this point, we were in a more or less in trim condition at clean maneuver and clean. We continue to execute QRH and requested the longer Runway (XXR). It was at about this point I believe that I informed the flight attendants of our situation and that we would be landing in about 5 to 10 minutes, to expect a normal landing but that there would be fire trucks standing by.We completed QRH and had a normal flaps 15 landing on Runway XXRWe rolled out normally and cleared the runway and informed ground control and the ARFF captain that we did not require any further assistance.We taxi to the gate and shut down the aircraft, debriefed the flight attendants, and spoke with maintenance on the ground about our situation.		B737-900 flight crew reported Stab Trim failure resulted in an altitude overshoot and limited control ability.	
1798204	202104	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																												TRACON ZZZ		Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer							Initial Climb		Class C ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1798204	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness			1799787	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance			Received a climb RA while flying the [Runway] XXL RNAV X. We climbed at 1500-2000 FPM, a small civilian aircraft flew under our aircraft and missed us by 400 feet. We heard approach control give traffic advisory to the other aircraft for a '737'. We received no traffic advisories. I consider this event a near miss.No traffic advisories received from approach or vectoring to either aircraft to avoid the conflict.The Approach Controller needs to do their job. Not vector another aircraft into our aircraft. The pilot of the other aircraft should not take climbing vectors into a 737. (Day VFR Weather, 10 miles plus visibility)		Cleared RNAV (RNP) X [Runway] XXL at ZZZ and subsequently received a TCAS RA approaching ZZZZZ. A light civil aircraft called us in sight and approach told them to maintain visual separation. Light civil aircraft was being vectored by approach control when RA commanded a climb to which the Captain (Pilot Flying) executed. We never saw the aircraft but the TCAS display showed them 400 feet below us. When resolved we were cleared a visual approach and landing without further incident.		B737-800 flight crew reported an uneventful landing after an NMAC event requiring evasive maneuver during approach. No traffic information provided to flight crew.	
1798253	202104	0601-1200	ORD.Airport	IL				2500		VMC						Tower ORD	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B ORD																												Tower ORD	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach		Class B ORD																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 67; Flight Crew Type 3441		Situational Awareness			1798253	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 22; Flight Crew Type 3125		Situational Awareness			1798243	Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	We were flying the ILS 27R to ORD. When cleared for the approach level at 4,000 ft, we twice had to disconnect the autopilot from the aircraft wake ahead of us. Spacing was the usual 3 miles and winds were straight down the path at 40 kts. Other aircraft were radioing similar issues with wake.  I decided to hand fly the approach the rest of the way to better control the speed and avoid the wake. The Final Controller told us to slow to 170 kts and contact Tower. We were cleared to land by Tower and continued the approach at 170 kts. Spacing was holding at 3 miles, but as we approached the marker I noticed our spacing was closing faster than normal. At about 2.5 outside the marker I made the decision to start slowing to avoid going around. (Full disclosure, I have been in the exact situation before and had to go around twice on this approach.) What I misjudged was how fast the 737-700 slows down. We were at approach speed too early and the Tower asked our speed and instructions. We made the aircraft behind us go around from my error in speed control.		While on ILS 27R into ORD, we were experiencing wake turbulence from the aircraft ahead of us and appeared to be quickly closing the distance between us. We had been assigned 170 kts until the final approach fix 'SIBLY' and began slowing 2 miles prior to the fix to stop the closure. The Tower Controller queried our speed, and emphasized we were to hold 170 until the fix.		B737 flight crew reported slowing below assigned airspeed to avoid wake turbulence from aircraft ahead on approach to ORD, forcing traffic in trail of them to go around.	
1798540	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 103; Flight Crew Type 15560					1798540												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	Other Take Off Roll	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Cleared to line up and wait Runway XX. When cleared for takeoff, pushed power to approximate 40% then selected TO/GA. As the thrust levers advanced, there was a very loud bang and yaw to the left. We had just started rolling. The thrust levers were brought to idle and we stopped on the runway. Initially, with the bang and yaw I thought we hit something or ran over something. As I was looking out the window, the First Officer stated we had a #1 Engine failure. Tower asked if we wanted equipment. We asked if they saw smoke or damage, and they replied 'No', along with another aircraft on the taxiway. I made a PA for everyone to remain seated. I then called the F/A's (Flight Attendants) and asked them to look at the Captain side engine for smoke or flames. They reported it looked normal. We ran the Engine Failure Checklist, and discussed running the Rejected Takeoff Checklist. Since the event happened as the aircraft had just started to roll (probably less than 10 knots) we didn't run the Rejected Takeoff Checklist, knowing that brake energy and cooling wouldn't be an issue. After we taxied to the gate, I made calls to Dispatch, Maintenance, and the Chief Pilot on call, while the First Officer went to the ramp to inspect the aircraft. He discovered bent and broken turbine blades with metal pieces loose in the engine. He came back upstairs and called ZZZ Ground to do a runway check for engine debris. I made a PA to the passengers explaining that we had an engine failure at power up, and the aircraft would be taken out of service. They would receive more flight information in the terminal. After the passengers had deplaned, the crew got together and debriefed the event. I informed the F/As of what had happened and let them ask questions.				Captain reported engine failure on take off roll causing a rejected take off and a return to the gate.	
1798576	202104	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors																									Turbine Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 126; Flight Crew Type 2822		Distraction; Confusion			1798576												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	Other Take Off Roll	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After the addition of thrust for T/O (takeoff) and selection of TO/GA (takeoff go-around) and N1 attaining T/O power, the aircraft was already rolling and had achieved approximately 15 knots of forward motion.  At that point we heard a very loud bang followed by a hard left yawing motion. The PF (Pilot Flying) immediately retarded the throttles to idle, and applied smooth breaking, which brought us to a complete stop.  We were angled left by about 30-40 degrees in relation to the center line of Runway 4, just prior to taxiway. I immediately informed ATC that we had stopped on the runway, the PF informed the PAX (passengers) to stay seated.  At that point we began to assess the situation. Initially we thought it was a blown tire or nose wheel steering issue. In that moment of assessing I noticed the #1 Engine Fail Caution illuminate in the #1 EGT Gauge. I informed the PF of the indication. It was the only indication we saw. No fire, no red lines only the engine failure alert caution. We reviewed the Rejected Takeoff Procedure, completed the QRH for Engine Failure. We then assessed the rolling and breaking capability of the aircraft and we found it to be normal.  We got clearance to exit the runway and return to the gate. As we pulled in to the gate, we remembered that we had not run an After Landing Checklist and quickly completed that, followed by the Parking Checklist. Then, the jetbridge malfunctioned and we had to wait until Airport Ops came to fix it. Once the jetbridge was attached I exited the aircraft to inspect Engine #1. I found the N1 fan blades and the stators directly behind them to be in a normal appearance. When I looked into the tailpipe of the turbine section, I found metal debris from the engine and both the aft fan blades and stators in front of them to be damaged and missing pieces. I hustled back to the Flight Deck to inform Ground Control that they needed to do a FOD check of the runway as we had, had and engine failure and that they needed to check for small engine parts.				First Officer reported engine failure on take off roll, resulting in engine damage and a return to the gate.	
1798873	202104	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			500			VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Initial Climb; Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ					Unscheduled Maintenance																			Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Commercial; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 26.95; Flight Crew Total 1678.40; Flight Crew Type 1678.40		Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface			1798873	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 50; Flight Crew Total 17600; Flight Crew Type 3600		Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting			1798889	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Separated Traffic; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Equipment / Tooling	Aircraft	All preflight items complete INCLUDING Main Electric STAB TRIM CUTOUT switch with normal results. Student Captain was Pilot flying for this leg. Shortly after Takeoff the PF commented that his electric trim switch was not working and asked me to try mine, which also did not work. I asked him if the elevator was working and he replied that it was and felt normal. We asked ATC to level off at 12,000 ft to work on a flight control issue and was approved. We did not ask for priority handling at this time...we engaged the A Autopilot successfully but shortly after the 'STAB OUT OF TRIM' light came on. As we began to look up the appropriate Non-normal, the PF disengaged the autopilot and tried to manually trim the stabilizer. Although he was successful it looked like it was difficult to rotate so I asked to give it a shot to feel the force required to rotate. The force required to rotate was abnormally high but did provide results and we were flying a trimmed aircraft at 240 knots. By now I was considering that a Jammed Flight Control (the stabilizer in this case) was the source of our STAB OUT OF TRIM  problem. So the first checklist we addressed was the Jammed or Restricted Flight Controls Non-normal and also the Jammed or Restricted Flight Controls procedure (the script/expanded explanation). Around this time we requested priority handling with ATC because checklist called for landing at nearest suitable airport. We were hoping that we might get electric trim back when moving STAB TRIM switch on pedestal to OVERRIDE. Did not happen. Called Dispatch with Maintenance Control on the line hoping for some systems expertise. Ended call with where we started. We began to discuss possible value of running STAB OUT OF TRIM checklist. We did. But before doing so we noted the hazard of running multiple checklists and capped our STAB TRIM OVERRIDE switch to normal. The Jammed Flight Control non normal resulted in moving both STAB  TRIM switches to CUTOUT but also called for landing at nearest suitable with Flaps 15. About this time we got on radio again with Maintenance Control, Dispatch and also the Flight Operations. Our hope was that Flight Operations might be able to get a Hydraulics of Flight Controls Maintenance expert on with us. We discussed both checklists and Flight Operations talked about the potential effect of slower speeds. This call ended with general consensus we were on the best track and we discussed whether to land sooner or burn down to below Max landing weight. Because we felt the aircraft was stable and our condition was not likely to get worse we chose to burn fuel. I talked to PF and told him it would be prudent for me to do landing since that would be his first one from left seat in aircraft. We briefed our arrival plan which included our trim plan as we reduced airspeed and extended gear and flaps. As we descended and slowed we reminded ATC of need for priority handling equipment. Only significant thing to report was that by the time we had fully configured and slowed to final approach speed it was extremely difficult to trim manually. In fact, with one pilot exerting maximum effort, you could maybe get the manual strection about a quarter turn. All of this led us to believe that the Stabilizer was DEFINITELY jammed. Landed uneventfully, had equipment check us out then taxied to gate. Flight Attendants were given TEST brief and told there was no need to prepare for evacuation.		All systems checked ok at gate, but after rotation we discovered our electric stabilizer trim was inop. The aircraft was controllable, but required moderate yoke pressure.We got vectors over the water and stopped our climb at 12,000. We engaged the autopilot momentarily, but the Stab out of Trim light led us to complete that non-normal checklist.We used the Manual Trim Procedure, but felt the force required was excessive, and the trim wheel was not rotating freely.Dispatch contacted and priority handling requested. We completed the Jammed Flight Control and the Stab Trim Inop checklists, both which call for Flaps 15 landing.After again contacting Dispatch, Maintenance Control, and Flight Operations, we planned to divert back to ZZZ.We burned down to max landing weight and landed uneventfully.		B737 NG flight crew reported a jammed horizontal stabilizer trim system and elected to make an Air turn Back and precautionary landing.	
1799118	202104	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	090	18		6000					Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 156; Flight Crew Type 2370		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1799118	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1799510	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	While being radar vectored to the visual final for Runway XX at ZZZ, Approach Control issued a descent to 6,000 feet MSL. We read back the clearance and proceeded to descend to 6,000 feet MSL. The Captain did observe that we seemed closer to some radio Towers on a hill than what would appear normal, but we received no GPWS Alerts. We continued on vectors and flew an uneventful approach and landing. After a call from Tower a few hours later, it was explained that the descent clearance to 6,000 feet was issued to a (other carrier X) whose readback was on a different Approach Control frequency. Our readback to that clearance as Company A (very similar sounding call sign) must have occurred at the same moment as (other carrier's) reply. Since the (other carrier X) aircraft had been on a different approach frequency, we had yet to hear any other communications to or from that aircraft, and ATC had not issued a caution for the similar sounding call signs. Additionally, ATC did not issue a low altitude warning if we had gone below the Minimum Vector Altitude in the sector we were within.When managing multiple frequencies, ATC should ensure that aircraft with similar call signs are alerted to possible miss interpretations. Furthermore, ATC should minimize the use of simulcasting on multiple Approach frequencies and consolidate traffic to one radio frequency.		We were being vectored for the visual approach to Runway XX at ZZZ from the east. ZZZ Approach Control issued a clearance to 6,000 feet MSL. We were later contacted that the clearance was for (other carrier) X, on another frequency and not for Company A. The clearance apparently took us below the MVA for that sector. The Controller was controlling multiple frequencies (he was busy) and never advised us of similar sounding call signs. We did not receive any terrain cautions from the aircraft or Controller.  The subsequent approach and landing were uneventful.Listening during communications is always the most important.  Being advised of similar sounding call signs is an important step to help alleviate threats and for Crews to be more vigilant.		B737-700 flight crew reported a CFIT event during a visual approach due to an ATC communications breakdown between flight crew and Approach Control who was working multiple frequencies with similar callsigns.	
1799598	202104		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000		VMC			Dusk			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1799598												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flying Aircraft X on a ferry flight from ZZZ - ZZZ1. Climbing out of FL350 for FL390 the flight crew was alerted of a bleed trip off of the No.2 Pack. The Bleed Trip Off QRH was called for and completed with no resolution as we leveled at FL390. As directed by the QRH the No.2 pack was turned off. At this moment ATC gave us a clearance up to our cruising altitude of FL410. The crew declined the request and relayed the need for a lower altitude (initially FL310). The request was granted and the crew began descending the aircraft to assure the single pack could maintain adequate cabin pressure. Passing FL380 the crew received a cabin altitude warning light. The appropriate memory items where accomplished, requested priority handling and descent to 10,000 was initiated. During the descent, the high cabin altitude and decent QRH procedure were accomplished by the First Officer while the Captain negotiated the descent. Passing through 27,000 feet the cabin altitude reached a level which activated the automatic deployment of the oxygen masks. Reaching 10,000 feet, the Captain verified aircraft was stabilized, crew was checked for any injuries, and the FO attempted to reach company via AIRINC. With the low altitude prohibiting any contact with ARINC and the aircraft was not equipped with datalink, the discussion began on where to go next. Departure airport ZZZ was approximately 200 NM behind us. ZZZ2 was 190 NM in front of us. And the Destination of ZZZ1 was an additional 300 NM past ZZZ3. The crew discussed the options and in an abundance of safety elected to divert to ZZZ3. The crew notified ATC of the decision to change destination and asked the ATC facility to notify our company of the situation and destination change. The flight continued to ZZZ3 and landed without further incident. Dispatch and Chief Pilot were notified once the aircraft landed.Gaining the ability to contact dispatch Via ACARS when AIRINC is not contactable would have helped in the decision making of this flight.				B737-800 First Officer reported executing an uneventful landing after a mechanical diversion due to a pressurization pack failure during climb.	
1799686	202104	1801-2400	ZZZZ.Airport	FO				15000		IMC	Thunderstorm; Turbulence						Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																										Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 244; Flight Crew Total 4699; Flight Crew Type 3101		Workload			1799686												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight ZZZZ - ZZZ1 (all nighter). Runway XXL departure IMC conditions with TS (thunderstorms) in terminal area. Crew briefed to remain seated due to expected turbulence on departure. Passengers briefed in welcome aboard announcement that the Flight Attendants would remain seated due to rain showers and bumps on departure. Performance for A/C weight of 145k, Flaps [set], Runway XXL bleeds on reduced thrust T/O. Normal T/O executed with a climb to cleared altitude FL230 commenced. Passing approximately 11,000 [ft., high elevation airport]. Cabin Alt Red warning light illuminated and horn sounded. Horn silenced - pressurization panel checked - and confirmed correct positioning of switches as terrain noted and communication coordinated with ATC for 'safe' altitude and significant weather avoidance required. Aircraft climbing at normal rate and leveling at 11,000 [ft.] was not an option. O2 masks donned and immediate action items of QRC executed. Remainder of checklist executed as Flying Pilot coordinated deviation with ATC and Captain with regard to safe altitude and course for primarily terrain avoidance and significant weather avoidance as a secondary threat. 17,000 ft. deemed to be safest altitude based on position of aircraft. Requested and set. Executed checklist items concurrently with NO resolution to regaining aircraft pressurization. All indications were normal, but obvious that aircraft was not pressuring at all. Plan determined by flight crew to coordinate safe corridor passage through the 'valley' - again based on terrain primary and weather secondary. Coordination with FAs indicated that passenger O2 had deployed automatically (and of note was deployed manually too during procedure), and that crew and passengers were 'safe'. ACARS NO COMM was illuminated likely due to A/C altitude and mountains blocking signals. Crew coordinated for lower altitudes as safe MORA (Minimum Off-Route Altitude) conditions permitted to account for passenger O2 eventually being depleted. Initially 15,000 [ft.], then 12,000 [ft.] coordinated to attempt to get cabin altitude below 10,000 [ft.]. At 12,000 ft. with the pack switches selected to high - crew was able to get the cabin altitude to below 10,000 ft. On initial communication with the cabin crew - communication with the forward cabin was nearly impossible due to loud noise by door 1L (our apparent source of no pressurization). Crew communication conducted via aft galley inter phone to determine crew and passenger status. Cockpit crew determined return to ZZZZ not best course of action due to terrain, weather, heavy weight of aircraft and passenger O2 depletion. ACARS comm with Dispatch established as determination for diversion and potential diversionary airports weighed. Both ZZZZ1 and ZZZZ2 considered. ZZZZ1 determined to have more threats due to lack of familiarity, time and rising terrain by the airport. ZZZZ2 considered and subsequently determined (in coordination with Dispatch) due to familiarity, the fact that we have been routinely using it as an alternate for ZZZZ flights, safe passage from our current location to airport at 12,000 ft. and relatively easy weather avoidance to get to airport. Aircraft integrity was addressed and deemed not to be an issue to choose the more distant diversionary airport. Coordination with ATC-1 and ATC-2 Control completed to receive priority handling (even though very few aircraft were in the sector). Coordination with Dispatch continued throughout with updated runway data, weather and ZZZZ2 [company] ground operations taken care of. Clearance given to descend for the ILS [Runway] XXR approach to ZZZZ2. Crew understood and briefed overweight landing procedures for ZZZZ2 having previously agreed that the threat of landing at 133,000 lbs outweighed the threat of remaining airborne for another 45 min to burn down below max landing weight. Of note, on descent through 7,000 ft. a loud sound [was] heard in [the] cockpit - aircraft integrity deemed safewith no abnormal engine indications. Aircraft executed a normal approach and landing to Runway XYR in ZZZZ2. Upon arrival maintenance items entered for O2 deployed, aircraft pressurization, and overweight landing. Upon post flight it was determined the aircraft had at least two bird strikes which were also entered in maintenance log book. Maintenance quickly determined that door 1L had failed to seat properly but had given a closed condition indication (never any door indication illumination in the cockpit). Challenges existed in ZZZZ2 for both crew and passengers due to aircraft flight originating in [foreign country] with current COVID restrictions in place and requiring adherence.				B-737 Captain reported executing a challenging mechanical diversion due to pressurization failure during climb. The diversion was concluded with an uneventful overweight landing.	
1799719	202104	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				34000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Engine		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer				Troubleshooting; Time Pressure; Workload			1799719	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Workload; Troubleshooting			1799724	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing through 34,000 feet we encountered a noise and grinding sound with a significant vibration through the plane. I noticed an odor and told the Captain and at that exact time the flight attendants called up saying that there was smoke in the cabin. We looked at the instruments and noticed both engines were erratic in their readings and a high vibration occurring on the number 2 engine. All of these events were happened at the same time.  I flew the aircraft the Captain worked the checklist and requested priority handling.  We shutdown the number 2 engine. An airport was right in front of us and we decided to divert there. Captain took care of the passenger PAs and flight attendants while I controlled the aircraft and flew.  After completing all the checklists and on downwind Captain took the aircraft and landed the plane. Fire Department was on scene and looked over the plane after we landed. They didn't noticed any leaks or fires so we taxied to the gate with them following us.  We had paramedics meet us at the gate to treat any passengers or crew members in case they needed assistance.After we made it to the gate and the passengers deplaned we inspected the number 2 engine.  There was damage to the nose cone and fan blades and inner engine cowling.  Something happened in flight for the parts to become damaged.  We were at FL340 so I doubt there were any birds or anything we hit, not sure why this happened.		Climbing through 34,000 feet at climb power we encountered a cyclic, significant vibration through the airframe. The First Officer then noticed a toxic odor which I then noticed. This was immediately followed by a call from the flight attendants advising that the cabin was filling with smoke accompanied by a toxic odor.  This all happened over a period of about 15 seconds. We then looked at the engine instruments and noticed erratic engine indications along with reduced thrust and high engine vibration on the number 2 engine. The First Officer continued flying the aircraft and I communicated the situation to ATC with a call and a request for lower altitude. I completed the engine severe damage/separation checklist resulting in a precautionary shutdown of the number 2 engine. I then advised ATC we would be diverting for landing.After that I called the flight attendants to check on the status of the cabin. They told me the smoke and smell were dissipating rapidly. I advised them of the situation and that we would be making a precautionary landing  and would be landing in about 25 minutes. I then advised Dispatch through the ACARS message of our situation. After checking in with the First Officer on the status of the diversion I made a PA to the passengers advising them that we were having problems with one of our engines and that we were going to make a precautionary landing.  I also told them that in an abundance of caution they may see some safety equipment inspecting us after landing. Through the descent I made several more calls to the cabin to check on the status and made another PA to reassure the passengers. I also completed the one engine inoperative checklist along with the non routine landing checklist. I elected to assume control of the aircraft for landing and took the controls after we briefed the approach and after landing considerations. After landing without incident, we cleared the runway on a high speed taxi way and stopped so that the Fire Department could make an inspection of our aircraft. After Fire Department told us they saw no abnormalities I elected to taxi to the gate.The FO and I made a visual inspection of the number 2 engine and saw damage to two compressor blades along with damage to the spinner and the inner engine cowling. I was surprised at the small amount of damage considering the effects to engine performance and the smoke and fumes in the cabin.  I'd like to add that the First Officer did a great job in his duties which allowed me to focus on my duties. His calmness, superior airmanship and advice were instrumental in the smooth handling of our situation. I'd also like to commend the flight attendants especially the number 1, on their calmness, communication and professionalism in a very stressful situation.		B737-800 flight crew reported engine vibrations along with smoke and odor in the cabin. They shut down the number two engine, diverted, and landed safely.	
1799780	202104	0601-1200														Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		1	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								GPS & Other Satellite Navigation		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface; Distraction; Confusion; Workload			1799780												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	While enroute 1.5 hours into flight, both GPS units began showing intermittent faults. I began coordinating with Dispatch. Eventually, both GPS units failed completely. Dispatch and I agreed that diverting to ZZZ was the best option. It was accomplished uneventfully. No GPS jamming reported by other aircraft or ATC.				An Air Carrier pilot reported both GPS units failed.	
1800152	202104							34000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 108; Flight Crew Total 8359; Flight Crew Type 6478					1800152	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 57					1799345	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Just prior to leveling off at FL340, Flight Attendant called and stated there was an electrical burning smell in the forward galley. There was not any smoke nor fire, just an odor. We notified ATC, turned back toward ZZZ, accomplished all checklist items, and landed uneventfully. After clearing the runway, we had the fire department check the airplane for signs of a fire. They saw nothing so we taxied back to the gate. At the gate, the fire department came on board to check the galley. They found nothing. No deviations from FARs.		Flight Attendant reported faint smell of fumes in forward galley.  Verified by [company] deadheading pilot.  Ran smoke/fumes checklist.  Verified no fire or persistent fumes.  Checklist said to land.  Captain made the call and we returned to ZZZ.  Overweight landing also accomplished and written up in ZZZ.		A320 flight crew reported an 'electrical burning smell' during initial climb resulting in a return to the departure airport.	
1801208	202104	0601-1200	SEA.Airport	WA			0									Tower SEA	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B SEA																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1801208												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Company Policy; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	When checking in with SEA Tower, Tower told us 'Cleared to land on [Runway] 34L', and he then asked us which gate we were parking at. We then heard him ask every other aircraft the same thing. Somebody said that's not important right now (which I wholeheartedly agree with), but Tower insisted that it was. I know they want to make their runway exit and taxi plan but asking us which gate we're going to is not necessary, and it's a huge distraction at a critical part of the flight. We're trying to complete our landing check, make sure we're landing on the correct runway, and flying the jet. I don't want my First Officer looking up a gate, or being distracted away from his monitoring duties, and I don't want that for myself when I'm the monitoring pilot. Another thing that SEA Tower is famous for is giving taxi and/or runway hold short instructions while we're slowing through 100 kts. after landing. That also is a critical time, and we're often transferring controls right about then. Tower needs to WAIT until the aircraft is clear or almost clear of the runway and SLOWED down before they start issuing instructions. Nothing further. Educate the tower controllers on critical phases of flight and the their taxi plan is NOT critical. They need to understand that they are there to help the pilots, but instead they're a huge distraction.				Air Carrier Captain reported SEA Tower Controllers were requesting gate information during a critical phase of flight. The reporter stated SEA Tower routinely transmits instructions during critical phases of flight.	
1801309	202104															Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery		Cruise		Class B ZZZ																								Powerplant Lubrication System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1801309												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately 30 minutes after reaching cruising altitude, the lower display illuminated indicating low oil quantity in #2 engine. It indicated 3. Oil pressure and oil temp matched #1 engine. We opened QRH and learned there is no NNC (Non-Normal Checklist)  that covers low oil quantity. Upon a deeper review of our manuals we learned via the 737 FCTM (Flight Crew Training Manual) that the controlling engine indications that could lead to an inflight engine shutdown in regards to engine oil are only pressure and temp. We decided that as long as pressure and temp were good we would continue normal operations. Also based on aircraft logbook and Local Maintenance briefing regarding an earlier flight with a low oil quantity indication we felt it was an indication issue.We monitored pressure and temp and also made contingency plans for an engine shut down just in case. Within 15 minutes the quantity indication dropped to zero, but temp and pressure still matched the #1 engine.  After another 15 to 20 minutes we noticed oil pressure was slowly declining with  temp still good. Oil pressure continued to decrease until illuminated red. Ran QRH LOW Pressure NNC, which directed us to the inflight engine shutdown NNC.We informed ATC that we were performing a precautionary engine shutdown and will need to divert to ZZZ. ZZZ was chosen based on our cruise altitude and distance at providing the best drift down and descent airport. We completed the inflight engine shutdown NNCWe then completed engine out landing NNC and proceeded to ZZZ and landed without further incident. Upon landing we stopped on runway and were towed to ramp.Low oil quantity.				737-80 flight crew reported loss of engine oil inflight.	
1801761	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ	N	Y	Y	Y																				Powerplant Lubrication System			Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Troubleshooting			1801761												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	I was the Pilot Monitoring on this leg, approximately after 30 min of TOC, the lower display popped up showing Eng 2 low oil qty (3 qts), the Captain asked me to pull the QRH and look if there was a NNC (Non Normal Checklist), and we found out that there is no NNC for low oil quantity, upon searching our manuals we found information regarding this situation, explaining that there is not a min oil qty per se, but that oil temp and oil press are governing, and both indications we normal matching Eng 1. The Captain and I discussed the previous write up that the airplane had regarding low oil qty upon TOD, and how Maintenance personnel had previously drained the oil tank, filled it up again and checked for leaks. Since oil temperature and pressure indications were normal on Eng 2, we assumed it was an oil qty indication issue and decided to continue the flight closely monitoring Eng 2 oil temperature and pressure indications. In the following 10 to 15 mins the oil qty dropped to 0, but all other indications remained normal. Approximately 30 mins after the oil qty indication had shown 0 the Eng 2 oil pressure started to oscillate down and back up in a normal range, gradually getting lower every time, once it remained in the amber range the Captain and I started to anticipate a precautionary engine shutdown. Once the Eng 2 oil pressure indication entered the red range at approximately 14 PSI, the Captain called for the LOW OIL PRESS QRH NNC, we followed the NNC and proceeded to the ENG SHUTDOWN QRH NNC, we informed ATC and Dispatch of our mayday status and proceeded to ZZZ. After completing the ENG SHUTDOWN QRH, we proceeded to the ONE ENG INOPERATIVE NNC, dispatch sent us the landing data for ZZZ RWY XX We landed in ZZZ without further incident, stopping on the RWY and being towed to the ramp.The engine had developed an oil leak that went undetected.I understand the ZZZ1 Maintenance refilled the oil and perform an engine dry run, and then checked for leaks, perhaps the leak would have been discovered if the engine had been started and ran for several minutes.				B737 First Officer reported decreasing oil quantity followed by total loss of oil pressure caused an inflight shut down and a precautionary landing at destination airport.	
1802825	202104	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				30000		VMC	Icing		Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Pitot/Static Ice System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1802825	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1802839	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	After descending from cruise flight at 360, we leveled off at FL300. We entered the clouds on descent and activated the engine anti ice system. Once level for a few minutes at 300 I noticed a rapid drop of airspeed on my side. I pushed the throttles up and informed the Captain. At that point we realized we had unreliable airspeed on the First Officer side. The Captain assumed control of the aircraft. We had encountered a small amount of rime icing and decided to climb back up. At this point we also realized the Captains altimeter was off. We [advised] through ATC, ran the QRC and QRH checklists. We diverted to ZZZ. We advised Dispatch and the flight attendants of our situation. Passing 15,000 feet our instruments began to return to normal and were equal upon landing.  We also accomplished they overweight landing checklist. We landed at ZZZ without incident. I believe the icing conditions could have contributed to the event. Check the pitot static system. Or computers. We had no indication of any heat malfunction.		After descending from FL360 to FL300 the First Officer, who was the Pilot Flying, noticed his airspeed indication decaying. In the descent we had entered IMC, and the engine heat was selected on due to the OAT. After cross-checking the pilots, standby, and copilots instruments I assumed control of the aircraft and we accomplished all QRC and QRH checklists for unreliable airspeed. We also determined the pilots altimeter was unreliable. [We advised] ATC and we diverted to the nearest suitable airport, which was ZZZ. The flight attendants, passengers and Dispatch were informed of our decision to divert. As we descended and the air became warmer the copilots airspeed and the Captains altimeter started to agree with the correct indications. By approximately 15,000 MSL all instruments were in agreement. Due to our weight we also accomplished the overweight landing checklist. We landed uneventfully at ZZZ and taxied to the gate. An AML entry was made and we entered crew rest at ZZZ.The icing conditions encountered in the descent to FL300 seem to have overwhelmed the pitot-static heating system. There was no indication of anything more than light icing from the pilots perspective. The pitot-static heating system on the 737 needs to be evaluated to ensure this situation doesn't happen to other aircrews.		B737-800 flight crew reported icing conditions resulted in unreliable airspeed indications, followed by a descent and diversion to a precautionary landing.	
1803155	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				8500		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class C ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 30; Flight Crew Type 15000					1803155	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 97; Flight Crew Type 2380					1803164	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	While climbing through 9,000 ft. MSL out of ZZZ, we experienced two VERY loud bangs from the #2 engine. We immediately suspected a compressor stall. Scanning the engines, I noticed the #2 engine decreasing in RPM. I immediately pulled the #2 engine to idle and it stabilized there. We coordinated a level-off at 9000 ft. with ZZZ Departure and flew an extended downwind. We ran all appropriate checklists and determined that the engine appeared to be performing normally. Following the checklist, we pushed the power up on the effected engine to approximately 80% with no adverse indications, there was no need to push it up any further. No [priority handling was requested] and we elected to return to ZZZ and fly a flaps 15 approach and landing to Runway 28R. Flight Attendants and Passengers were advised of the situation. After all checklist items were complete, we accomplished an uneventful approach and landing. Weight or brake energy was not a factor.		Departed [runway] XXR, from ZZZ, radar vectors made a turn to south-southeast, passing through MSL 8,000 ft. deleted thrust reduction at Captain's request, and around MSL 8,500 ft. two loud bangs came from #2 engine followed by vibrations. Contacted ATC, leveled off at MSL 9,000 ft., and ran QRC 'Engine Surge or Stall' followed by the QRH as directed. Engine seemed to run normally after checklist. We returned to ZZZ.		B737 flight crew reported compressor stalls during climb and elected to return to the departure airport.	
1804131	202104		DEN.Airport	CO				7000		VMC						Tower DEN	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B DEN																												Tower DEN	Air Carrier	Commercial Fixed Wing		2	Part 121	IFR			Landing		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 107; Flight Crew Total 1992; Flight Crew Type 328		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1804131												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were told to expect the RNP approach to 34L from our initial check in. On downwind we were told to expect the visual instead. We had many speed changes. The final speed change was to expedite our slow down to 170. The First Officer called for gear and flaps as necessary. It didn't look like he was going to level at the final approach fix altitude so I was watching that and the plane in front of us. He never called for the landing checklist and I never thought to do it. I completely lost situational awareness. The First Officer ended up getting a sink rate that was quickly corrected. The plane in front of us gave us moderate wake turbulence. We debriefed and decided this was a failure on both of us to maintain situational awareness.				B737NG Captain reported they failed to complete the landing checklist because of distractions and loss of situational awareness.	
1804712	202104	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				33000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization Outflow Valve		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Troubleshooting; Workload; Distraction			1804712												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight was scheduled to depart ZZZ. We arrived in the flight deck and performed all the normal procedures we have for a normal push back. Captain did the preflight walk around and I loaded the FMC. We agreed the OFP (Operational Flight Plan) looked good and the rest of the flight plan looked sufficient for a potentially bumpy ride to ZZZ1. Captain advised to Flight Attendants to stay seated out of ZZZ until we knew the ride would allow for their safe duties. I was the flying pilot and after Captain did his first flight of the day and PM (Pilot Monitoring) duty brief, I briefed the SID. We planned for a normal pushback with 2 engine start for a quick taxi for departure. We performed the BEFORE TAKEOFF CHECKLIST on taxi out down the ramp for takeoff on runway XX.While we were taxiing down to runway XX, I noticed there was a split between the L and R duct pressure. I made note of it to Captain and we briefly discussed these splits we have seen before in this aircraft and recall it being a known normal occurrence. I cycled the L PACK (Left) switch to OFF and back to AUTO as well as the ISOLATION VALVE switch to OPEN and AUTO and saw the duct pressure needles sync together. We decided it would be a safe action to depart and noted we would keep an eye on the duct pressure and pressurization.We departed runway XX and CLIMBED VIA THE SID. I hand flew the departure to the mid 20s and then turned the autopilot on. I noted Captain mentioned 2 different times that the aircraft was pressurizing normally though the duct pressure still was split. The ride was mostly good and the flight attendants were up but the seat belt was still on. As we approached our level off altitude of FL340 the CABIN ALTITUDE horn and the MASTER WARNING lights illuminated. We verified the CABIN ALTITUDE was high on the pressurization panel and performed the Immediate Action items for CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING OR RAPID DEPRESSURIZATION/EMERGENCY DESCENT. The CABIN ALTITUDE was not controllable, and we continued with the descent. There was a definite issue with immediate communication over the inter-phone with the masks on and all the noise. Getting the mask on and the audio panel correct to be able to speak and hear over the speaker poses a threat in this airplane if not practiced. We were able to communicate with each other and ATC but it was challenging with all the noise and distractions.Captain identified as a priority flight with ZZZ Center and we began the initial descent over the mountains. I initially turned off the AUTOPILOT to pitch over and begin the procedure. Once we were established in a descent, I turned the AUTOPILOT back on and descended to FL190 with the high terrain in mind. We saw an aircraft below us on the TCAS and had to shallow the descent initially before we were cleared lower. Captain proceeded with completing the checklist and communicating with the Flight attendants, passengers, and dispatch. Descending through approximately 16,000 feet I noticed the CABIN ALTITUDE was in a normal range of around 7,000 feet and we took off the oxygen masks. We leveled off at 11000 in the vicinity of ZZZ2. Initially we were planning on diverting to ZZZ2 because we were so close, but Captain's experience and familiarity with ZZZ3. This led to the decision to divert and there was difficulty communicating with dispatch possibly due to our low altitude so we opted to landing in ZZZ3. The flight attendants and passengers were all briefed for the diversion to ZZZ3. I left the AUTOPILOT on and we flew the RNAV (GPS) Runway XY 29 approach and landing. All checklists were completed before ZZZZZ intersection and the landing was normal. CFR ( Aircraft Rescue & Firefighting) was there upon arrival.We were parked at the hard stand in ZZZ3 and the ramp personnel brought over the ramp to allow the passengers to vacate the aircraft. Operations were there to help while we figured out the plan of action for the passengers. Captain discussed the situation with the ChiefPilot, Dispatch, and Maintenance and I aided the flight attendants with passengers. I walked to the OUTFLOW VALVE and noted a mesh substance blocking the OUTFLOW VALVE from fully closing. Captain manually opened and closed the valve and I videoed the movement.				First Officer reported an emergency descent and diversion followed by a precautionary landing caused by Foreign Object Debris blocking the outflow valve from operation.	
1805789	202105	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				37000		VMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ; Class B ZZZ2																								Autoflight System		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Fatigue; Human-Machine Interface			1805789												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Separated Traffic; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Software and Automation; Aircraft; Procedure	Aircraft	At XA:55Z the master caution light, auto pilot disconnect aural  warning and IRS annunciation light activated. The FO was the PF and immediately began to hand fly the aircraft. As PM, I verified the aircraft was stable and performed QRH XX.X IRS fault. After completing the required items and discussing all of the impact items, I sent ATC a CPDLC message as to our loss of navigation equipment and inability to remain in RVSM airspace due to the loss of the auto pilot. ATC instructed to remain at FL370 due to traffic. We called dispatch via SATCOM and relayed our current situation and reiterated our need to use the ILS RWY XX into ZZZ due to the loss of RNAV capabilities. After a conference call with Operations and Maintenance, we continued to ZZZ and landed uneventfully via the ILS RWY XX.We split the flying duties to avoid fatigue (30 minute intervals). ATC was given 2 hours to plan our arrival to the opposite runway, and they did an exemplary job. ZZZ1 was built in route 2 as our primary divert airport.				Captain reported total loss of the autoflight systems required close coordination with ATC for traffic separation and special flight handling due to loss of RVSM and RNAV capabilities.	
1805954	202105	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				18000		IMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 12500					1805954												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing out of ZZZ in moderate rain and passing through about 18,000 feet, with the FO flying, we got an airspeed disagree and altitude disagree on our displays, a Master Caution for Engine, autopilot (B) kicked off, and VNAV disappeared all within a few seconds. We compared instruments and determined that the Standby and Captain's displays matched with airspeed at 280 and close to the same altitude still climbing. We transferred aircraft control and ran the airspeed unreliable checklist/altitude unreliable checklist. After reporting our difficulties with ATC, we leveled at FL250, to finish checklist and run checklist for EEC alternate mode. At that time the FO instruments showed an airspeed of 130 KIAS and 25,500 feet. It took a while to get through checklist, but we were able to get autopilot-A back.  We talked about the bad weather at ZZZ and decided to continue to ZZZ1 due to VMC conditions there. On descent passing through 15,500 feet, FO instrumentation appeared to recover. Approach and landing were uneventful.  Priority handling was requested to ensure ATC was aware of our indication problems.				B737-700 Captain reported a loss of reliable airspeed and altitude on the FO's side during climbout.  The Captain's instruments were functioning properly, so the crew decided to continue to destination due to it having better weather conditions than their departure airport.	
1805959	202105						0						Night				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise; Parked																										Hydraulic Syst Reservoir Tank		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 80; Flight Crew Type 80		Other / Unknown			1805959												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	Aircraft In Service At Gate; Routine Inspection	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	The hydraulic reservoirs have a big red caution label. It reads, 'CAUTION DO NOT OVERFILL HYDRAULIC RESERVOIR. FILL TO APPROX. 2/3 BETWEEN REFILL(RFL) AND FULL (COCKPIT PANEL SHOULD READ 92% FULL) REFER AMM 12-12-00.'Below the caution label there is an ATTENTION label. It reads, 'ATTENTION Do Not Overfill the RESERVOIR. Overfill can Cause Hydraulic Fumes To Enter the Flight Deck and Passenger Cabin'Some years ago I had an air return when the cabin filled with acrid mist causing eyes to sting and some to choke. The flight attendants thought we were on fire! The Captain and I used goggles and oxygen to safely return. This event was caused by both hydraulic reservoirs being filled to capacity. I believe the cockpit panel read 107%. If I'm recall correctly this is the most that will register.Although I have not since had visible or detectable odor, I believe crew and passengers are breathing atomized hydraulic fluid whenever the reservoirs are over serviced. The leg noted in this report from ZZZ-ZZZ1, started with both reservoirs reading 100%. At shutdown they were at 92%. Where did the difference go in a three hour operation? The day before, my aircraft had 107% in system A and 92% in system B. I alerted Maintenance of the over service. The Technician proceeded to move System A to B. The result was both reservoirs at 100%.I have dealt with this issue individually before. I find a small portion of our technicians understand the significance. Sometimes I meet with great resistance.[Recommend] a notice for Aircrew to remind them of the placards and similar or more specific training for all our Maintenance Technicians and Maintenance Control.				B737-800 Captain reported loss of Hydraulic Reservoir quantity when reservoir is overfilled. Reporter theorized that hydraulic fluid is being atomized resulting in harmful cabin fumes and physiological symptoms.	
1806219	202105	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US			500										Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	Localizer/Glideslope/ILS ZZZ	Final Approach; Initial Approach																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface; Troubleshooting			1806219												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Equipment Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Human Factors; Procedure; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	Human Factors	After intercepting the glideslope it was noted by both pilots the aircraft was not slowing down normally.  Despite lowering the gear and deploying speedbrakes we were having a difficult time getting 10 knots below the next flap speed to continue to configure a variable tailwind was noted between 5 & 10 knots which helped explain what we were seeing.  Continuing to investigate however both pilots thereafter realized we were abnormally low and the PF disconnected the AP and leveled off.  Tower called with a low altitude alert shortly after that and we acknowledged it, configured normally and were stable by 500 feet AGL.  Landing and roll out was normal.  There were multiple approach NOTAMs that day and we miscalculated the effective times on the one stating the ILS XXL was unusable.  We were an hour off and were arriving inside the last 30 minutes of the effective time.  It turns out NAVAIDs maintenance was manipulating the glide slope signal at the time, causing the glide slope to appear lower.The ILS to XXL was OTS and NAVAIDs maintenance was transmitting a lower than normal glidepath. It would have been helpful if ILS status had been repeated on the ATIS, as it normally is, but it was not.  During the low altitude call from tower they were even quick to add to the call that it 'was in the NOTAMs'.  Further investigating with my prior position with FAA Flight Inspection it is still in their guidance that NAVAIDs maintenance is not supposed to transmit a localizer signal if they are going to manipulate the glide slope signal to avoid this hazardous, misleading, information.  The localizer and glide slope both were transmitting, with a proper ID, at the time of the incident, we even crossed ZZZZZ at the appropriate altitude of 3,000 and showed on path.  It was after that when the aircraft would no longer slow, as it was chasing a depressed glide path.  In the end we missed the NOTAM but then multiple holes of the swiss cheese lined up; thankfully it was day VFR and we saw the solution with our own eyes in time.  The other layers of protection normally there (ATIS, maintenance procedures) would have helped overcome our oversight.				A320 First Officer reported CFIT event during approach that was due to maintenance before performed on the glide slope equipment but not being announced on ATIS.	
1806534	202105		ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO				18000								Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb																										Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 130; Flight Crew Type 754					1806534	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 139; Flight Crew Total 17060; Flight Crew Type 4033					1806528	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing through FL180 I engaged CMD (Command) A autopilot. When I pressed the button the airplane instantly had a loud banging noise, jolted very aggressively, nosed down, and started oscillating.  It was almost to the point I thought I was going to accomplish upset recovery procedures.  I was quickly able to level the airplane off and safely hand fly.  We contacted Maintenance and unanimously agreed that the airplane was safe enough to continue to hand fly the plane for the duration of the flight. Maintenance asked if we tried autopilot B. The jolt and aircraft response was significant enough that we did not want to try arming the autopilot again.  We safely hand flew the aircraft back to ZZZ1. In our debrief we discussed that for a split second we initially thought it was so severe that we were losing aircraft control. We were greatly startled and hands were shaking for a good 5-10 minutes after the event happened. We were scheduled to fly to ZZZ2 after the flight, arriving the next morning. We hit an emotional lull after the flight and we both determined we were not emotionally, nor physically, fit to continue our pairing.  We called in fatigued and were given hotel rooms immediately.		We were climbing through 180 out of ZZZZ.  The CA (Captain) went to engage CMD (Command) A and the aircraft made a loud bang and had a violent nose down control input. It was so severe that we thought the flight attendant could have been injured.  It was so bad for a moment I thought that we might have hit something mid-air.  I was completely shaken up and so was the Captain. We refused to attempt to re-engage any autopilot. We were just about to divert but we had Dispatch run the numbers for us at non-RVSM altitude. We calculated 6.0 remf on this 700 so we opted to hand fly it. We worked in 30 minute shifts. The arrival was busy with many changes enroute. We were both shaking at the initial event and were completely calmed down enroute but we both started to feel drained after the adrenaline started to wear off. We were both obviously not fit for our next leg which was scheduled to arrive after midnight into ZZZ1. We both got a hotel at ZZZ even though I could have commuted home that evening, I was toast at that point and just went to bed.		B737-700 air carrier flight crew reported autopilot malfunction inflight.	
1807534	202105	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US													Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Transponder		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1807534												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	The situation happened on leg 1 on the second day of a 4 day sequence.  We departed ZZZ for ZZZ1. As the First Officer I completed both the interior checklists and performed the exterior inspection.  I noticed during the walk around that one of the red navigation lights was inoperative.  When I got back to the flight deck I confirmed that it was previously written up and had been deferred in the AML.  No other issues were noted during the preflight. The Captain was the Pilot Flying and I was the Pilot Monitoring. We departed ZZZ on Runway XXL. The takeoff and departure were uneventful.  Weather was clear with unrestricted visibility. The climb was continued until we leveled off at FL340. At approximately XA:13Z, I started to smell something.  It smelled like burning plastic. At the same time I noticed the smell the Captain asked 'Do you smell that?' Before replying we both saw smoke coming from the 'FL' button on the transponder located on the center console.  At this time we both we executed the items for 'Smoke, Fire, or Fumes', donned our oxygen masks and established crew communication.  At that point the Captain gave me both aircraft control and the radios so he could focus on the abnormal. While I monitored the autopilot the Captain continued with the 'Smoke, Fire, or Fumes' checklist.  I confirmed with him that the proper switches were being turned off in accordance with the checklist. We noticed that the smoke and fumes were decreasing and did not need to run the 'Smoke or Fumes Removal' checklist.  While the Captain attempted to locate the transponder circuit breaker to isolate the problem he noticed that the 'ELEC PANEL LIGHTS AFT/CENTER' circuit breaker located at the base of the front of the center pedestal was popped. We were confident the problem had been isolated and the Captain informed me that he was about 90% sure we were going to divert but he wanted to check with company to see if we were missing anything.  We quickly realized that a step in the checklist had us turn off the internet and he would be unable to use the crew phone app to communicate with the Dispatcher. The Captain then took the radios back and attempted to get phone patch with the Center Controller.  The controller was unable to give us the phone patch but was able to relay information.  After communicating through the controller and using ACARS, it was decided that we would divert to ZZZ2.  The Captain notified ATC and informed Center we wanted to divert to ZZZ2. The controller asked if we want ARFF (aircraft rescue firefighting) and the Captain said yes. The Captain then gave me back the radios so that he could run more checklists.                                                                                                                                                                             The Captain continued with the checklists the controller asked what runway we wanted to land on. The Captain and I agreed that we wanted the longest runway due to the fact we would be landing overweight. I then requested Runway XXR. I got the airplane set up for an ILS approach to XXR. I started the descent into ZZZ2 while the Captain finished checklists and briefed the passengers and Flight Attendants. Then the Captain told me that he ran the 'Non Routine Landing Considerations' checklist and that the passengers and flight attendants had been briefed. He talked about the heavy weight landing and did a landing assessment. He asked if I had anything to add or could think of anything else we might need to do before landing. I told him that I think we had covered everything and I had nothing else to add. He then briefed the approach and took aircraft control.  We completed both the descent and before landing checklists. After landing we cleared the runway at taxiway. I relayed to ARFF that unless they see anything abnormal from the outside our plan was to continue taxing to gate. They informed us that they did not see any unusual readings on thethermal scan.  ARFF followed us to the gate.  The Captain had me make a PA to the passengers that we would be taxing to gate and they would be accommodated after deplaning. The taxi and engine shutdown at the gate was uneventful. At the gate we were met by a member of the company Flight Office and he asked if we needed to be checked out at the hospital. We both said we did not require medical attention and that no more than a few seconds went by that we breathed in fumes before getting on oxygen. He then told us that he was pulling us off the remainder of the trip and could positive space home.  The Captain completed writing up items in the AML. We then debriefed when it was just the two of us left on the airplane. I told him I thought maybe I could have done more to help.  He said I did what he needed by flying the airplane and communicating with ATC while he ran the appropriate checklists and backed him up in his decision making process. We both agreed that we handled the situation in the way we are trained to handle abnormal at the school house. The cause was smoke coming from the 'FL' button on the transponder located on the center console.				B737-800 First Officer reported a fumes event in the flight deck during cruise resulting in a diversion.	
1807908	202105	1201-1800	LAX.Airport	CA		35		10000					Daylight			TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B LAX																												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Approach		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 85		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1807908	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 55		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1807909	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	We had been cleared the ILS 25L Approach while on the ANJLL Arrival and assigned 250 knots. At about 12,000 feet, we got a call from the cabin that the passenger we had called STAT-MD for earlier in the flight, was feeling worse. I asked the First Officer to call OPS for medical assistance to meet us at the gate. When he returned back to the ATC frequency, we were approaching 10,000 feet and hit wake turbulence sufficiently strong to cause the airplane to go into control wheel pitch and roll. I redirected the aircraft to KRAIN, re-engaged LNAV and VNAV, and asked ATC what we were following.  I was told we were following nothing. This didn't make sense since we had just hit very strong wake turbulence. While established on final, ATC told us to slow to 170 knots. This surprised both of us, because the last speed assignment we recalled being given was 250 knots. 170 knots would be a huge speed reduction and we were still about 25 miles out. ATC then asked us if we were at 210 knots. We told the Controller no, and that we had been assigned 250 knots. He told us we had been instructed to slow to 210 knots back when we complained about the wake turbulence (approaching 10,000 feet). We were now overtaking a B777 ahead, so our approach clearance was canceled. We were given vectors for spacing and vectored back onto the localizer and then re-cleared the ILS 25L. Neither the First Officer no I recall hearing the 210 knot speed assignment. We had only heard 250 knots and read that back. The 210-knot speed assignment probably occurred about the time the Flight Attendant called us about the passenger feeling ill again and just as we hit the wake turbulence.		A bunch of things happened at once which led to us missing a slower speed assignment. On the arrival to LAX, cleared the approach to 25L and told to maintain 250 knots. The flight attendant called up and said that our previous medical issue in the back (we had earlier talked to STAT-MD and gotten clearance to continue) was not feeling well again, and asked for paramedics. As the Pilot Monitoring, I called OPS at LAX, to arrange for emergency personnel - meaning I was off frequency. At the same time, we encountered wake turbulence, strong enough to knock the aircraft out of both LNAV/VNAV. The Captain, Pilot Flying, reported the wake turbulence to Approach, after getting the aircraft established on profile; apparently ATC told us to slow to 210 knots. Neither of us heard or read back the speed change. A few miles later Approach canceled our approach clearance and said we were too fast. We responded that we were assigned 250. They said no, we were given 210.		B737-700 flight crew reported missing an ATC speed assignment on arrival into LAX when they were distracted by a wake turbulence encounter in trail of a heavy B777.	
1808518	202105		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				22000		IMC						Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121		Passenger		Descent	STAR ZZZZZ2	Class B ZZZ																								Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 92.82; Flight Crew Total 1087.45; Flight Crew Type 1087.45					1808518												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition	Aircraft	Aircraft	Proceeding directly to ZZZZZ intersection on the ZZZZZ 2 RNAV arrival. Was cleared to descend via ZZZZZ 2 RNAV arrival on [Runway] XXR. Prior to ZZZZZ, at approximately FL220 during descent, noticed that the cabin altitude was at 8,300 feet and increasing. I called attention to the First Officer that we might be losing cabin pressurization. The Pressurization screen on ECAM was displayed and noticed that both the inlet and extract valve were fully closed but the safety valve had opened.Since we had already been cleared to descend via ZZZZZ arrival and had an altitude of 12,000 set in the MCP I decided to expedite the descent to see if I could control the rate of ascent in the cabin. At approximately FL180 it appeared that we could not control the rate of ascent in the cabin and that at its present rate the cabin would exceed 10,000 feet shortly. I then [advised] with ATC and requested 10,000 feet which was granted. I advised the Lead Flight Attendant that we were experiencing cabin pressurization problems but to expect a normal landing. As we leveled at 10,000 feet I monitored the cabin and noticed that it had climbed to 12,000 feet but stabilized. It took several minutes before the cabin normalized at our cruise altitude of 10,000 feet. I asked the First Officer  to see if he could find an applicable checklist to run. During this time I maintained control of the aircraft and communicated with ATC. The First Officer advised that he was able to manually control the outflow valve and that the checklist recommended shallow descents thereafter. I continued to ask for delayed vectors from ATC just north of ZZZ as the First Officer and I reviewed the state of the aircraft and decided it was safe to proceed with the landing. I asked ATC for shallow descents on the arrival to [Runway] XXR into ZZZ. The landing and taxi in were uneventful.				Air carrier Captain reported malfunctioning pressurization system on descent.	
1810107	202105	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC; GPS	Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Main Gear Wheel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 173; Flight Crew Type 9800					1810107	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 225; Flight Crew Type 2286					1809535	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On takeoff from ZZZ we felt a rough bumping at 100 knots. I was distracted by the cause of the roughness and didn't notice the exact speed we initiated the rejected takeoff at but felt it was at least 100 knots, but not above 120 knots. I determined the aircraft was unsafe to fly, called for the aircraft and rejected the takeoff. The First Officer (FO) alerted Tower as we were slowing to a stop. Once we were stopped, I made a Public Address telling the Passengers to remain seated. I was surprised to find that I didn't have a non-normal checklist for the situation and it felt odd as we had so much time to wait for the Fire Trucks to arrive. During that time I communicated with the Flight Attendants FAs to check on them and asked them to look for fire or smoke. They verified no indication of either and also confirmed that there were no injuries. Crew Fire Rescue pulled up and reported the two left main tires were blown and no fire, though they did hose them down briefly. The FO suggested starting the APU, which we did, and I was able to shut down the engine as the Fire Department was tending to the aircraft. We coordinated with Operations by radio and Dispatch by ACARS, and made Public Addresses to keep the Passengers informed and calm. The Passengers were then deplaned by air stairs and bused to the terminal.		I had done the walk around and hadn't noticed anything unusual about the tires. The brake-wear pins looked fine. Taxi out was normal and even the initial takeoff roll was normal and didn't notice anything. After I heard the Pilot Monitoring call 80 knots, we felt a jolt and then the aircraft started shaking and getting progressively worse. We rejected the takeoff at about 100 knots with the appropriate flows and callouts. As we were slowing (I believe I saw 43 knots), I called Tower to let them know we were rejecting the takeoff. We got the plane stopped. The Captain called the Flight Attendants. The Tower let us know they actually heard the tires blow and that they were sending out the Fire Department and the Airport Operations. They then switched us over to another frequency to coordinate. We started the APU and shutdown the engines and then the Fire Department sprayed something on the aircraft left side. They used the word hot-spot. The people were then deplaned via air stairs and bused to the terminal, and we again confirmed that no one needed medical attention and were uninjured. After thinking about it, I know in training we had been told to wait until below 60 knots to call the Tower, but I'm not sure if that had changed to being at a complete stop or not before calling and could not find guidance in our manuals.		B737-700 flight crew reported a rejected take off due to aircraft shuddering.	
1810740	202105	1201-1800	MIA.Airport	FL				8000								Center ZMA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZMA																												Center ZMA	Air Carrier	Widebody Transport		2		IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZMA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1810740												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	Approximately 80 miles before top of descent MIA Center descended us to FL360 behind a widebody which was left at FL380. There was a quartering left tailwind of approximately 30-35 knots. We were given 280 knots assigned for spacing. The widebody entered the STAR from our left or the east. Our separation grew from less than 10 miles to just at or slightly over 10 miles with our descent and speed. At approximately 60-55 miles from the new top of descent from FL360 we experienced a rapid roll to the left of approximately 30 degrees. I turned off the autopilot and the plane then rolled right rapidly, about 20 seconds later. We [asked] for a speed of 250 [kts]. ATC gave the speed when we reported the wake turbulence. I continued to hand fly for a while until we could assure ourselves the wind dropped a bit and our separation had increased. I had the FO (First Officer) call the FAs (Flight Attendants) everything was secure and everyone was okay. We had them take their seats [and] the seatbelt sign was on at the time of the event. Upon leveling at 8,000 [feet] and turning westbound we had a second event even though the 777 was turning base ahead and we were just entering the downwind and crossing the bay. Approach had kept the 777 high and gave them relief on the restrictions as they descended. The next day, MIA Approach cleared us to 8,000 [feet] behind an aircraft ahead with tight spacing. We did get a slight roll left then right but I immediately slowed to 230 and had the FO tell MIA we were slowing. This time the aircraft ahead was not kept high and so I hand flew slightly above VNAV path to assure at FL230 we would not have wake turbulence. Later [that day] Tower cleared us to takeoff before [another air carrier aircraft] had rotated. After I lined the jet up on the centerline and after takeoff clearance, we waited 8-10 seconds for [the other air carrier aircraft] to rotate. We then took off. We were given heading 105 [on departure, and the other air carrier was given heading 120]. The aircraft behind us was given 090. We encountered wake turbulence behind [the other air carrier aircraft] due to crosswinds. MIA has minimized separation and rushed operations such that it is my experience safety is impacted.First, the next time MIA says keep it rolling and cleared to takeoff, I will ask to exit when an aircraft is still not near rotating. Second, MIA needs to review the operating practices in play. Lack of separation at FL380, being placed below and behind a heavy with a quartering tailwind, 10+ miles separation was not enough with 30+ knot tailwind. Change the practice of [descending] a non-heavy behind and below a heavy to descending the heavy below the following aircraft.				B737 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on descent into MIA.	
1811516	202105	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				20000		IMC	Icing		Night				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ; Class B ZZZ																								Pitot/Static Ice System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface			1811516												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Weather	Weather	First Officer flying through FL200 on way to FL270, in IFR conditions. First Officer and Captain's airspeed indicators [were] unreliable. Standby airspeed indicator correct. Captain's airspeed indicator came back, but First Officer's out for 5 minutes and EEC (Electronic Engine Control) Systems went to Standby. QRC and QRH procedures accomplished as well as followup procedures. Anti-icing systems working, Engine and wing heat on. Stayed as much out of weather, ZZZ closed, ZZZ1 still in VFR weather and location of heavy maintenance for the B-737. Briefed flight attendants and passengers. Captain flew to ZZZ1, safety vehicles checked out aircraft.Good crew coordination of verification of the unreliable airspeed indicators.Don't fly into clouds with icing conditions. May have been multiple problems that happened at the same time. EEC Systems went to standby while in heavy thick clouds, on climb out, through the freezing level.				Captain reported diverting after airspeed indications became unreliable in IMC conditions and the destination airport closed.	
1811697	202106		ZZZ.ARTCC	US				37000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise																										Air Cycle Machine		X	Failed; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 115; Flight Crew Type 2544		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1811697	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 14; Flight Crew Type 260		Troubleshooting; Time Pressure			1811700	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Diverted	MEL; Aircraft	Aircraft	Aircraft was dispatched with an inoperative Pack #2 due to a regulator fault and [the flight] was in full compliance with the MEL at departure. Flight was completely full in the cabin. Due to Pack #2 being inoperative it was difficult to cool the main cabin temp using only Pack #1. Using only Pack #1 at FL370 we were able to maintain 72 degrees in the cabin using max cold and high flow. Approximately 200 nm west of ZZZ at FL370 we received an ECAM AIR PACK 1+2 FAULT. ATC was notified and [priority handling was requested]. Upon checking the pressurization and bleed air page, it appeared that Pack #1 was overheating showing an output temperature in amber yet we never received a Pack 1 OVHT ECAM. The cabin altitude was still under control. A descent to 10,000 ft. was initiated without exceeding the cabin altitude before we complied with the ECAM to turn off Pack #1 leaving us unpressurized. Contact was made with Dispatch and Maintenance Control to discuss the situation resulting in a diversion to ZZZ.		Approximately 240 nm from ZZZ, at a cruise altitude of FL350 and while in communication with Center, an ECAM aural and Master Caution sounded for AIR PACK 1+2 FAULT. The CA (Captain) said he noted a momentary amber overheat temp in Pack 1, but it did not trigger the PACK 1 OVHT caution. I (Pilot Monitoring) did not see this indication that had subsequently returned to a green inlet temperature. Pack 2 was previously secured and turned off per the MEL deferral on our 2nd release. Pack 1 showed a fault light. The AIR PACK 1+2 FAULT ECAM directed us to secure Pack 1, descend to 10,000 ft. MSL or MEA and then turn on the RAM Air when the pressure differential was less than 1.0. Even though Pack 1 had a fault light in the overhead panel, it appeared to be partially functioning, the cabin pressure holding and the cabin altimeter only increasing at a very slight rate. A flight crew decision was made to begin an immediate normal descent to 10,000 ft. without the use of oxygen while closely monitoring the pressurization page to ascertain the true nature of the Pack 1 malfunction. The CA directed me to assume pilot flying duties and ATC responsibilities while he coordinated with Operations, Dispatch, flight attendants and the passengers. We began a coordinated 1500-2000 fpm rate of descent via two ATC-assigned step down altitudes of FL300 and FL200 before ultimately reaching 10,000 ft. The aircraft was lowered in a steady continual descent with no pauses which I believe helped our situation. Cabin pressure held steady around 8,000 ft. during the normal descent. Upon reaching 10,000 ft., Pack 1 was secured, differential pressure reached less than 1.0, RAM AIR was selected on and the cabin altimeter climbed from approximately 8,000 to 10,000 ft. as expected. We completed all remaining ECAM items until the screens were normal. The rate of fuel burn at 10,000 ft. MSL made reaching our initial destination of ZZZ1 impossible, and it was decided that ZZZ was a suitable diversion while 200nm away and with 11,600 pounds of fuel aboard along with the best Maintenance, aircraft and passenger accommodation options. We informed ATC, Dispatch, Operations, and proceeded accordingly. We switched to the next Center and the Captain [requested priority handling] and returned to the pilot flying role while we coordinated an uneventful arrival via the ZZZZZ to Visual XX approach at ZZZ and ultimately parking at gate XY. We were then met by the ZZZ Chief Pilot at the gate and proceeded to fill out a risk mitigation form following the event. We then accepted a new aircraft and release at gate XZ and ultimately brought the same passengers to ZZZ1 two hours later than originally planned.In totality, I felt the event was handled safely and professionally. The CRM skills taught and utilized allowed both the CA and First Officer to properly divide duties while ensuring sufficient cross checks and communication. At no time did I feel rushed or stressed. I believe the deliberate delay in securing the faulty Pack 1 that appeared to be functioning along with the continual scanning of the pressurization page allowed us to avoid an unnecessary priority descent, use of pilot oxygen, or the deployment of passenger oxygen that might have accompanied a rapid depressurization. All in all, I felt the CA was composed and handled the event in line with Company desires. While I regret the delay the passengers experienced, I believe we handled the event in a safe and caring manner.		Air Carrier Pilot Crew reported departing with one air cycle machine inoperative and during cruise flight the other air cycle machine indicated a fault condition necessitating a diversion.	
1811787	202105	0001-0600	ZZZ.TRACON	US				20000		IMC	Cloudy; Icing		Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Airspeed Indicator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1811787												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Weather; Aircraft	Weather	Clean configuration. Airspeed 280 knots. Autopilot on. IMC conditions. Being radar vectored around significant squall line. Anti-ice on. Climbing to cruise altitude and passing altitude about 20,000 feet when my airspeed indicator suddenly went erratic with plus or minus several (at 30 knots greater and less than commanded) indications. Airspeed indicator never reached stick shaker or over-speed warnings limits. Immediately took control of aircraft from autopilot and crosschecked the primary flight display with the Captain and standby indicator. Recommended Captain become pilot flying as his indications were consistent with standby indicator and airspeed indicator was not as erratic as mine (more consistent with turbulent air flying in the 737). After transfer of aircraft control to Captain, I completed the Quick Reference checklist (QRC) and QRH for unreliable airspeed indicator. When double checking the pitot heat was on I notice both Engines Electronic Control (EEC) were in soft alternate mode. During the entire time the aircraft was in IMC the airspeed indicator was erratic and EEC remained in soft alternate. A few minutes later, at level off at cruising altitude (low 20,000s) and out of IMC the airspeed indicator returned to normal along with EEC. Remained clear of clouds and landed uneventfully at ZZZ1. We did not return to ZZZ after [Notifying ATC] because we were more than half way and the squall line was over ZZZ.Unknown as to why airspeed indicator was unreliable and erratic in IMC turbulent air and then returned to normal when clear of clouds. Also did not know why EEC went to soft alternate and then returned to normal.				First Officer reported air data related system failures due to suspected inflight icing conditions which diminished after leaving IMC.	
1811848	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Data Transmission and Automatic Calling		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Human-Machine Interface; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1811848	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1811860	ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Software and Automation; Procedure; Aircraft; Human Factors	Software and Automation	We received a request to go direct to ZZZZZ. Dispatch had filed us on a southerly track to avoid significant weather on the H-track. The only options given were to accept or standby. We were not going to accept the re-route so we hit standby. The standby was accepted. We sent a message that we could climb instead of a re-route and ATC gave us a climb.Sometime later, the re-route request showed up again but this time the only option we were given was to accept. We ignored the request but that left the ATC icon up on the Upper DU [Display Unit] (so future ATC alerts may not be noticed).This annoyed me so after crossing ZZZZZ1 we noted that accepting the persistent direct routing request would only bypass our next waypoint by about 10 NM. I accepted the ATC routing but waited for a reply from ATC before proceeding. ATC responded with ROGER. So, we went direct ZZZZZ per the clearance. ATC then sent a message asking us to confirm request. We never sent any request so we followed up with a direct message stating we were accepting the previous direct routing from ATC. Shortly thereafter we asked to call ZZZ Center.I talked with the controller and explained the situation. He closed it out on his end with no action but quality control will still review.With CPDLC, ATC cannot ask a question but only provide one answer (accept). That leaves us with an open request.		There should be a CPDLC option to accept, reject, or standby. Not just accept or standby. If you don't accept, the message stays open, and the ATC alert never extinguishes. This could cause a future message to be missed.		B737-800 air carrier flight crew reported accepting a reroute clearance on CPDLC that they had initially put on standby.	
1812269	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US			300			VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Autothrottle/Speed Control		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 65; Flight Crew Type 65					1812269	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 13000					1812276	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	The Captain was flying and after takeoff the white FMC message dealing with the autothrottle appeared and the Pilot Flying noticed an amber EGT exceedance.  The autothrottles and VNAV were inoperative at that point after takeoff.  The TAT probe was indicating a higher than normal temperature which we determined to be the root cause of all the problems.  We consulted with the QRH and Dispatch and all agreed to air return to ZZZ without any further issues.		Just after takeoff the aircraft experienced a loss of data from the TAT probe (repeat gripe).  This initially manifested itself as an autothrottle limit error and a rapid increase in #1 EGT (yellow backfill on gage but no other indications) and loss of VNAV and related FMC functions.  We continued to climb to 16,000 feet MSL and informed ATC that we may need to return to ZZZ.  I passed aircraft control to the FO and initiated radio contact with Dispatch.  After initial contact, radio communications with Dispatch were lost, but Dispatch replied via ACARS that they agreed a return to ZZZ was prudent.  Release was amended.  ATC, cabin crew and passengers were informed; no emergency was declared.  The aircraft returned to ZZZ without incident.TAT failure is a high workload event similar to unreliable airspeed.  The number of items that rely on the TAT is huge, and once the event occurs there is (a) no real clue as to what the causal factor is, (b) no QRH guidance for the NN, and (c) a plethora of conflicting data and flight deck indications.  Thought should be given to educating our Pilot group to the risks and indications associated with TAT failure.		Flight Crew reported an air turn back after the TAT probe failed, causing the auto throttle to malfunction.	
1812431	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC							Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 17.83; Flight Crew Total 1891.37; Flight Crew Type 1891.37		Troubleshooting			1812431	Aircraft X			Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 163.22; Flight Crew Total 3683.40; Flight Crew Type 2844.17					1812433	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR		N	Person Flight Crew	Other Take Off	Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Procedure; Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	Previous flight had reported engine fluctuations on takeoff and climbout. Maintenance took a look at the plane and Ops checked it good. On our takeoff, once takeoff thrust was set we began a normal reduced thrust takeoff with an EPR (Engine Pressure Ratio) setting of about 1.41. Once above 80 knots the Captain called out that the EGT and N1 were fluctuating and took control of the aircraft and performed the Reject. The auto brakes engaged at Max. We made the required callouts, cleared the runway and performed the QRC. We did receive HOT Brakes due to the reject and allowed them to cool before returning to the gate.		Advancing power was normal with Flex FMA (Flight Mode Annunciator) displayed and EPR (Engine Pressure Ratio) thrust set prior to 80 kts. As the aircraft accelerated EPR indication on eng 1 began to fluctuate. As takoff roll continued EPR EGT and N1 began fluctuations with EPR going above yellow tick mark and almost as low as 1.3 EPR (TO EPR was 1.43). Reject was initiated at approximately 110 kts and we noticed ATC and cabin and cleared the runway to run the rejected to checklist. After allowing the brakes to cool we returned to the terminal.		A320 flight crew reported a rejected takeoff after engine fluctuations reoccurred during takeoff roll and returned to the gate.	
1813032	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch; Taxi																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Confusion			1813032												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew; Person Visual Observer (UAS)	Taxi	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Procedure; Weather	Procedure	We were operating our flight ZZZ to ZZZ1. This flight operates at close to if not at its max weight where maximum performance from the aircraft and crew are required due to the short runways. Headwind operations, or more importantly, absence of any tailwinds, should be the main concern of ATC and pilots when utilizing this airport when such performance is required.In the hours prior to this flight ZZZ was running a south operation, departing RWY XX, as one would expect with a reasonable headwind (180/7kts). Approximately 20 minutes before departure, they switched to a north operation, now departing RWY XY. This change seemed illogical as it now was using a runway that had a 7 knot tailwind for takeoff. With this change our flight was unable to depart as we were too heavy due to a tailwind weight penalty of 9,030 lbs from our runway limit.I asked ZZZ Ground/Tower Control why they had changed to a north operation. The answer we received was that arriving aircraft on the XXXXX Visual were encountering a tailwind at altitude and therefore the change. This puzzled me. Is this an official procedure that the FAA ATC has at ZZZ or shouldn't runway selection for takeoff and landing be based on surface winds?We had no choice but to wait for an opening for RWY XX, which caused a significant delay to our companies reliability operation, not to mention putting other aircraft in an awkward position of either compromising safety margins or accepting lengthy delays. Switching runways around based on winds at altitude rather than safety of landing and departing on short runways with tailwinds, puts the pilots in the position as the last line of defense for safety.  Just FYI, we also only had one reverser. Although RTO does not include the reversers in its stopping distance calculations, it was still a consideration to us.ATC changing runways from a headwind to a strong tailwind. Putting pilots in a less optimal position to operate their aircraft.The additional work load and max performance requirements when utilizing a short runway with a tailwind should be avoided as much as possible especially at ZZZ. Every foot of runway concrete is paramount to operating safely at this airport. Therefore every knot of tailwind compromises that safety margin.				B737-800 Captain reported ATC changed the departure runway creating a unacceptable tailwind takeoff condition. ATC explained the switch was to accommodate the aircraft on the STAR encountering a tailwind.	
1813633	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				4000		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger			SID ZZZZZ4	Class C ZZZ																												TRACON ZZZ	Other Unknown	Helicopter		1		VFR	Other Unknown		Cruise		Class C ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Distraction			1813633	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Distraction; Situational Awareness			1812557	Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft TA; Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Airspace Structure; Procedure	Procedure	TCAS RA. On departure from ZZZ on Runway XX, we were given left turn to 149, climb 6,000 feet. In the turn, ATC said maintain 4,000 feet for traffic at 10 o'clock at 4,500 feet. As I went wings level, I could see the helicopter at 10 o'clock within a half mile of our current position. About 3,500 feet, we got a TA. I slowed my vertical speed as I approached 4,000 feet but upon capture, we got a RA telling us to 'monitor vertical speed.' The red line for the RA was on the horizon so I pitched just below the horizon. The First Officer informed ATC of the RA and that we were in a descent. ATC responded saying we were below the MVA (Minimum Vectoring Altitude) and to get back to 4,000 feet when able. At 3,600 feet, we were clear of conflict and climbed back to 4,000 feet. We continued the departure as normal. There wasn't much room for vectoring because of the mountains surrounding the valley. I believe the helicopter was VFR because of their altitude. ATC could have possibly kept us on the ground longer or given a delayed turn until helicopter was no longer a conflict.		TCAS RA on departure from ZZZ. Initially received climb via the ZZZZZ4 SID except maintain 5,000 feet. Prior to takeoff received amended clearance of left turn 149 climb 4,000 feet. ATC called traffic in the turn and we were looking. Nearing completion of turn and climb received TA followed by RA target 700 feet to 500 feet above and got the 'monitor vertical speed' we descended and made the standard  callouts to ATC. At 3,700 feet ATC warned of low altitude and below MVA (Minimum Vectoring Altitude). We were VMC and received the 'clear conflict' and climbed back to 4000 feet and continued the flight uneventfully.I think ATC knew about this target prior to takeoff and that's why they amended the clearance. I believe their hands were tied with a vector because of rising terrain and altitude separation due to Minimum Vectoring Altitude. We followed our training and everything worked out, would have been worse in IMC. Captain (Pilot Flying) says he saw helicopter but I couldn't see from First Officer seat in left turn.		Air carrier flight crew reported they responded to a TCAS/RA for a VFR helicopter by descending then were advised by ATC they were flying below the Minimum Vectoring Altitude.	
1817229	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Other / Unknown			1817229	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Other / Unknown; Human-Machine Interface			1817244	Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were flying ZZZ1 to ZZZ, a XA00 report in ZZZ1. It was the First Officer's (FO) initial trip back from a 10-month furlough. We were on the ZZZZZ RNAV arrival from the northeast. I was the PF. ZZZ was landing north. ZZZ airport had an overcast layer obscuring the airport. At the appropriate time, I briefed the ILS XXR approach. We set up the FMS and tuned/identified the NAV radios for XXR, the normal approach for this arrival. When we checked in with ZZZ Approach, we were advised to expect the ILS XXL. I briefed the approach and we tuned/identified the NAV radios. We were being vectored to intercept the localizer. It looked like we overshot the localizer on the NAV display, so I turned to intercept. ATC advised us, 'It looks like you are lining up for the wrong runway,' and turned us off of the approach. There were no conflicts with any other aircraft. They then told us to expect the ILS XXR. We flew that approach successfully to a landing. While we were setting up for the XXR approach, we discovered we never set up the FMS for the ILS to XXL. [I believe the cause was] early sign-in. Overcast skies obscuring airport. [It was also the] FO's first trip after furlough. [I suggest] verification that both the NAV radios are tuned and identified and the FMS is set for the proper runway/approach.		Planned and briefed approach for ILS Runway XXR and was given ILS Runway XXL. The CA (Captain) set up and briefed new approach. I verified the frequencies and inbound course but neither of us set the new approach into the FMS. Was getting vectors to the ILS XXL [and] we had the correct ID for the ILS. When the CA looked at the ND and saw himself flying through the course for what was ILS XXR, the wrong approach, he corrected the heading to go back to intercept the course on the ND. The Controller said you are turning to line up for wrong approach and told us to go around.I have not flown since [last year]. I also do not have much experience in the aircraft. Was first flight since furlough. [I plan on] getting more flying in and becoming more comfortable in the aircraft, getting back into the rhythm of the flights.		B737-800 flight crew reported a clearance deviation due to automation mismanagement and CRM issues.	
1818701	202106	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			1000						Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 122; Flight Crew Type 122		Workload; Distraction			1818701	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 211; Flight Crew Type 211					1819076	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Passenger	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft	Aircraft	During the takeoff roll, the FA's (Flight Attendant's) dinged us twice. We continued the takeoff. Upon rotation they dinged us four times, twice before clean-up altitude. After reaching clean-up altitude I called the cabin and the FA's reported passengers seeing flames and sparks coming from the right engine, as well as hearing popping noises. The FA's reported the entire cabin lit up brightly, but that there was no smoke in the cabin. We informed ATC that we had a #2 engine fire, were returning for landing, and requested fire truck assistance. We had no cockpit indications of a fire or malfunction. Both engines were producing symmetric power and operating normally, according to our gauges. Upon landing and shutting down the engines on Taxiway XX, Fire Rescue took temperature readings and reported them in the normal range. We were towed to the gate.		Incident for [the] flight [from] ZZZ to ZZZ1. I was the pilot flying. During the takeoff roll with takeoff power set, the flight attendants called the flight deck with two dings. We elected to continue to takeoff. Right at rotation speed, the flight attendants called the flight deck again, but this time with four dings. We continued the takeoff and climbing through 1000 ft., the Captain transferred the radios over to me and she started coordinating with a Flight Attendant.Flight attendants were reporting that the passengers on the right side were saying that the engine was on fire and they heard a popping noise and at the same time the flight attendants said they noticed that the cabin lit up momentarily. However, there was no smoke in the cabin, all engine indications were normal, and none of the Fire Handle Warning lights were lit. The Captain [requested priority handling] and told Tower that our #2 engine was on fire and that we needed to return for an immediate landing and requested the fire trucks. Tower cleared us for Runway XXR, I continued to climb to 3000 ft. at 210 kt. and started my turn for a left downwind for Runway XXR. On downwind, Tower cleared us for the visual and cleared to land XXR. A flaps 30 landing was conducted and we taxied off the runway on Taxiway XX where we shut the engines down. The Fire Crew inspected the engine and stated that everything seemed normal and that their heat sensors also indicated normal readings. After a short time, we were towed back to the gate.		B737-700 flight crew reported an engine fire that was noticed by the passengers during takeoff and performed an air turnback.	
1818728	202106	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				35000					Daylight	5000		Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								APU		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 110; Flight Crew Type 16000					1818728	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 80; Flight Crew Type 3300					1818686	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; MEL	Aircraft	Enroute from ZZZ to ZZZ1. Aircraft had MEL for #2 engine generator. Dispatched with APU powering #2 transfer bus. At 35,000 ft. over ZZZ2, APU failed and did automatic shutdown. We diverted to ZZZ2 due to only one AC power source remaining. Uneventful approach and landing. Multiple, unforeseen failures caused the event.		Right generator was MEL'd after gate return at ZZZ. MEL required APU to be operated continuously on the ZZZ - ZZZ1 leg. During cruise FL350 the APU failed, leaving us with a single generator. Accomplished QRH items for 'APU low oil pressure' and decided to divert to ZZZ2 due to the requirement to land at [the] 'nearest suitable airport' from MEL and QRH. [Requested priority handling] in the descent due to [the] nature of [the] malfunction and overweight landing. Landed uneventfully in ZZZ2 6,000 lb. overweight. At ZZZ2 we deplaned passengers, and after a six-hour delay, the APU was repaired and overweight inspection [was] completed. Completed flight to ZZZ1 uneventfully.		B737-700 flight crew reported an APU failure inflight combined with an MEL'd engine generator resulted in single-generator operations, which led to a diversion to the nearest suitable airport.	
1818754	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			800			VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B ZZZ																								Toilet Furnishing		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 177; Flight Crew Total 13601; Flight Crew Type 1290		Troubleshooting			1818754												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During our flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1, Aircraft X, a flight attendant seated near the 4L lavatory reported that about 3 minutes after take off he began to notice a urine smell coming from the 4L lav.  The smell began to dissipate about 15 minutes later.  We suspected a contributing factor could be the high deck angle associated with the take off.  This has been a continuing write up for a few weeks.  Unfortunately maintenance personnel have been unable to identify and fix the exact cause.  Hopefully this report will help bring together the appropriate resources to fix this problem.  Our passengers and flight attendants will appreciate it.				B777 Captain reported urine odor emanating from the aft lavatory during initial climb which lasted for approximately 15 minutes.	
1819099	202106	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000					Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Troubleshooting; Time Pressure			1819099	Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 154; Flight Crew Type 1609		Situational Awareness; Workload; Distraction; Confusion			1819338	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Bird / Animal; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew; Person Passenger	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Eighty miles southeast of ZZZ, while we we're deviating around thunderstorms, we received a call from the B Flight Attendant that we had a strong electrical smell in the aft galley. A few seconds later another call from the flight attendant said the cabin now has the strong odor and a passenger said there is a haze. We notified ATC and were cleared direct to ZZZ. We had a thunderstorm between us and the field, so this required some deviations. We elected not to don the mask because we didn't have smoke or odor in the cockpit. We briefly talked about it and didn't want the added condition. We also decided that we would don the mask at first sign of smell or smoke. Things obviously were very busy because we made it from 36,000 [feet] to the Runway in 18 minutes. By the time we were pointed in the right direction we started to run the Smoke Fire Fumes Checklist but never finished it. We were very tired from the multiple reroutes, PM trip to an AM, back to a PM. I elected not to finish the checklist because I wanted all eyes up so we could get the aircraft on the ground safely. On approach we also had a bird strike, which was a non-event and we landed 10,500 pounds overweight, also a non-event. After we cleared the runway we had the fire trucks shoot the brakes, and after we ran the brake cooling numbers found that we could taxi to the gate.		At roughly XX:20 CT and at FL 360, while diverting around thunderstorms and turbulence approximately 80 miles southeast of ZZZ, we were alerted by the B-Flight Attendant of a strong electrical odor in the aft galley.  Moments later we were notified that it had spread through the cabin with haze reported by a Passenger.  Considering the seriousness of the issue and our #1 priority of Safety, we immediately [requested priority handling] with ATC and received direct ZZZ. The Captain turned off the galley power and advised me to alert Dispatch while he flew and communicated with ATC. We continued to get updates from the Flight Attendants/Dispatch/ATC and kept them in the loop of our emergency diversion into ZZZ. The Captain continued to deviate around thunderstorms and turbulence while I adjusted the route in the FMC, loaded the approach and got weather and landing information. We quickly discussed our arrival with overweight landing, minimum braking, and plan for fire trucks to meet us. We ran the Descent/Arrival Checklists and just started the Smoke/Fire/Fumes Checklist when, as we were now very near the airport, the Captain requested we stop the checklist as he needed me heads up with him and our focus to get the aircraft quickly on the ground. We got the airport in sight, configured, completed the Before Landing Checklist and had a bird strike on approach with a very smooth touchdown and roll out to the end of [runway] XXR. We cleared the runway, advised the Passengers to remain seated and the fire trucks evaluated us with an all clear. We ran a brake cooling with no limitations, so we taxied to the gate with the fire trucks following us. There were no damages to the aircraft or injuries to the Crew or Passengers.The short 15 minutes from first notification to landing in ZZZ was filled with the threats of task loading, additive conditions and Crew factors.  It was the last leg of a four-day trip and we were both fatigued from many trip changes/delays, and I was on an FDP extension leaving ZZZ1 and two prior trip  extensions.  We had additive conditions of thunderstorms and turbulence to deviate around, both en route and on descent while dealing with the emergency.  We had task loading with the very short period of time to assess/handle the situation/navigate/communicate/run checklists etc., and get the aircraft on the ground in minimum time.		B737-700 Captain reported a fume event during cruise resulted in a diversion.	
1820049	202106	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing																										Wheels/Tires/Brakes		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1820049												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance			Person Flight Crew	Other Landing	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Procedure; Aircraft; Human Factors; Manuals	Human Factors	Complete right side brake failure upon landing in ZZZ.  Brake line became detached. Failure of some sort after brakes had just been replaced on #3 and #4. Surprised that entire right side brakes failed.  One brake line detaching should not have resulted in complete right side failure.				Air Carrier Flight Crew reported brake failure on landing.	
1820122	202107	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US			6000			VMC			Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Oil Contents Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument						1820122												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On climb out from ZZZ, we noticed engine oil quantity on engine #2 had gone to 0. We notified ATC and returned to ZZZ. All other indications were normal. The landing was uneventful and we taxied to the gate.				First Officer reported loss of #2 engine oil quantity caused them to make an air turn back to a precautionary landing.	
1820446	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US				1000		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors																									Compressor		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1820446	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1820276	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	After a 5 hour maintenance delay to change a flat tire, we departed for ZZZ1.  We executed a normal, flex, CONF1 takeoff from [Runway] XXL in VFR weather with light winds and a light aircraft.  I was PF (Pilot Flying) and was hand-flying the departure.  At approximately 1,000 AGL I heard several loud bangs from the right side of the aircraft.  My first thought was bird strike but the CA (Captain) called out 'engine failure.' I called out 'my aircraft' and we determined there was a problem with the #2 engine.  I don't recall any ECAMs but I do recall seeing the #2 EGT was high.  I was continuing to hand-fly and CA suggested we reduce thrust to idle on the #2 engine.  I agreed and we reduced the thrust and the surging and vibration ceased and the engine parameters went into the green.  CA suggested an immediate return to the departure field and [advised] ATC and coordinated a downwind turn and level-off.  I engaged the autopilot and prepared for a return to runway XXL with an ILS approach. CA set the FMGC up for the approach and I flew the downwind and base.  CA advised the FAs (Flight Attendants) to prepare for a precautionary landing and gave a short PA as well.  ATC vectored us to the ILS and cleared us for a visual approach once I had the field in sight.  I executed a normal landing on XXL and we taxied clear on taxiway 1 and had the fire department inspect the aircraft.  CA made several PAs instructing the passengers to remain seated and that the situation was under control.  I noticed the #2 EGT was increasing and approaching the upper limit and we shut the engine down as the fire department was inspecting the engine.  CA coordinated with operations a return gate and once the fire department was done and deemed the aircraft safe to taxi we returned to gate.  The passengers deplaned normally.  CA then called Dispatch to advise them of our return.We both determined the safest course of action was to get the aircraft back on the ground as soon as possible.  This was made easier with good weather, no air traffic, and being below max. landing weight.  We were only in the air ten minutes and this didn't give us time to do much troubleshooting or communication with anyone except ATC and the flight attendants.  I was 100% busy flying the aircraft the entire time, but due to excellent ATC and constant communication with the Captain/PM, I felt in the yellow most of the time and in the green for the final approach and landing.  I only felt I was 'in the red' for the few seconds between when the engine began surging until the CA said  'engine failure.'  That statement immediately told me what was happening and I snapped into our trained procedures.   We continued to discuss the plan and agreed that a quick return to the field was best throughout the short flight.  Since the engine seemed stable at idle and we had no ECAMs we decided not to complicate things by troubleshooting but rather execute a quick precautionary landing.  I feel our combined experience in the Airbus as well as constant precise communication and exchange of ideas led to the successful outcome.  We backed each other up, trapped errors, and executed our plan efficiently and effectively. I don't see this event being preventable, but I think our training and emphasis on SOP and communication made for a good outcome.		On takeoff, at approximately 1,000 feet AGL #2 engine compressor stalling with high EGT.  We brought the thrust lever to idle and stalling stopped.  We [notified] ATC and returned to field.  Firefighters inspected the airplane and it appeared normal.   Taxied to the gate.		Flight Crew reported compressor stall required an air turn back and a precautionary landing.	
1821532	202107	1801-2400	EWR.Tower	NJ			10			VMC						Tower EWR	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B EWR																												Tower EWR	Air Carrier	A321		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B EWR																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 91.17; Flight Crew Total 9879.23; Flight Crew Type 9879.23					1821532												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Environment - Non Weather Related	On the RNAV Stadium Visual to runway 29 we were vectored behind an Airbus 321 on the ILS to 22R.  We joined the visual at GIMEE with the airport and the [A321] in sight.  We had been cleared to land prior to crossing GIMEE.  Tower reported winds calm, ATIS had winds 200/6.  Prior to SLIMR we were configured and slowing to approach speed.  At approximately 1,500 ft AGL I called for the landing check and called to the FO that [the A321] appeared to be crossing the numbers and spacing looked good.  Continued in LVAV/VNAV till the autopilot rolled us out on final at about 1,000 ft AGL.  At this point PAPI showed slightly high and corrected to visual glide path.  A rapid right roll was encountered just prior to touchdown causing contact of the right main. This slowed the roll but aircraft was still rolling right.  Immediate left aileron was applied along with deployment of the auto speed brakes quickly caused the left main to touch down.  Roll out was normal from this point.  Wing drop was so rapid that I thought the right gear leg had collapsed.  The event occurred so quickly that there was not even time to consider a go-around.  The application of power and lifting off again would likely only have exacerbated this situation placing us at a bank angle at low speed with an at the time unknown problem.  During debrief we determined that the event occurred just prior to the intersection with Runway 22R and was likely a wake turbulence event.  An ACARS generated report showed a max bank angle of 6.9 degrees with a predicted wing tip strike at a bank angle of 7.5 degrees.  The FO inspected the aircraft for possible damage, none was found.  The event was reported to Tower, but they did not seem concerned.  I contacted Clearance Delivery to get more information on the preceding aircraft.  CD took more interest and sounded like it would be reported.	Reporter stated intersecting runway wake encounter events were more unusual in his experience, but this one was quite severe.			B737NG Captain reported encountering wake turbulence shortly before touchdown on Runway 29 at EWR from an A321 on short final to Runway 22R.	
1821910	202107	1201-1800															Air Carrier	B777-200		4	Part 121	IFR	Cargo / Freight / Delivery	GPS	Cruise																										GPS & Other Satellite Navigation			Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Confusion; Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness; Distraction			1821910												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Procedure; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings; Software and Automation	ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	Suspected GPS jamming between LOGMU and ROKUB on T561 XA:17Z-XB:03Z. EICAS alert for NAV UNABLE RNP. Followed checklist and used radio/IRS updating during the event. Was able to resume normal GPS ops for remainder of flight. Most likely some crazy militants in the area. Perhaps pay the big bucks and fly over Siberia on this route. Most likely less GPS jamming and probably more efficient for flight planning. Although I appreciate the longer rest opportunities on these double crewed flights.				An air carrier Captain reported GPS jamming.	
1822494	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US	150	3		3000		IMC	5		Night	800		TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class C ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 230; Flight Crew Type 19000		Communication Breakdown; Time Pressure; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1822494	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Workload; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1822513	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Human Factors	Human Factors	On approach in to ZZZ, we were cleared direct ZZZZZ intersection and to descend to 3,000 feet There was a thunderstorm cell between ZZZZZ and ZZZZZ1 (Final Approach Fix) on the approach to Runway XXR to ZZZ. We asked ZZZ TRACON (Approach Control) for direct ZZZZZ1 to avoid the thunderstorm cell. We were anticipating a visual approach to Runway XXR because the most recent ATIS (weather report) was reporting only Few and Scattered clouds at the airport with adequate visibility for a visual approach. The TRACON Controller asked if we would be able to 'get the airport?'My First Officer and I were slightly confused by his phraseology and tried to discuss what the Controller meant. At that time, we were within a few miles from ZZZZZ and just about to level off at 3,000 feet. The Autopilot Mode Control panel was in Vertical Speed (Altitude Acquire) and LNAV to ZZZZZ. I was anticipating going to ZZZZZ1 and was ready to change the altitude to 2,800 feet in MCP for the autopilot. We were then cleared on a 160 degree heading to join the final approach course of 128, and cleared for the approach. I didn't hear the Controller say 'maintain 3,000 feet until established.' I selected VOR/LOC and dialed 2,800 feet into the autopilot Mode Control Panel. Because we were in a descent and in Altitude Acquire, the autopilot continued to descend towards 2,800 feet.My First Officer said we were supposed to maintain 3,000 feet. I had missed that part of the clearance so I dialed 3,000 feet into the Mode Control Panel, but by that point the aircraft had descended below 3,000 feet and was continuing to descend and would not ever capture 3,000 feet. I was also trying to slow the aircraft and get it to capture the localizer. Seeing that we weren't going to level off, I tried to use Vertical Speed to make the aircraft climb back to 3,000 feet, but it was continuing to descend. Our descent was exacerbated by the fact that I still had the speedbrakes deployed almost to the flight [descent]. ZZZ TRACON (Approach Control) said he he had a low altitude alert and his MVA [Min Vector Altitude] was 2,800 feet. We were now at about 2,300 feet. I disconnected the autopilot and autothrottles, started a climb and we decided to go missed approach. I finally stowed the speedbrakes, climbed to our clearance altitude and we then got re-vectored by ZZZ TRACON for another approach to Runway XXR and landed safely.		We were being vectored around some weather coming into ZZZ and were given a descent to 3,000 feet and direct ZZZZZ outside of ZZZZZ1 on the ILS XXX. There was some confusion as ATC asked if we would be able to get the airport. I wasn't sure if he meant visually or altitude wise. After a pause, as I pondered his meaning, I responded that we were IMC. At the time, I didn't think we were high, so I assumed he meant visually. Just a couple miles outside of ZZZZZ, Captain asked for a turn to ZZZZZ1. I paused because I was anticipating a vector shortly to intercept the final approach course. I then asked ATC for the turn inbound, and ATC responded that he was just about to give us a vector and then provided the clearance. We were given a 160 heading to join and maintain 3,000 feet until established and cleared for the ILS to [Runway] XXR. This extra communication ultimately caused a delay in getting turned in. Because it all got really busy, I didn't see that Captain dialed in 2,800 feet. So, I don't think it acquired 3,000 feet and was now descending below it. Then I think 3,000 feet was reset into the MCP and yet the aircraft was still descending. All of this was happening while I was replying to ATC and I didn't realize what happened until after we talked about it later. We triggered a low altitude alert, and ATC informed us that we were to maintain 3,000 feet until established. I responded that we were correcting. Captain was climbing back up to 3,000 feet and we were slightly south of the localizer. I figured by the time we got back to 3,000 feet, we would then be high for the approach and not being established on the localizer, I said we should go-around. Captain agreed, and we did. This whole thing took me out of the Green, made me feel rushed and got behind in my responsibilities. A go-around was the best decision.		B737-800 flight crew reported a CFIT event during approach resulting in a go-around. A communications breakdown between flight crew and ATC was cited as a contributing factor.	
1822540	202107	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				31000					Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																																																															Facility ZOA.ARTCC	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 195; Flight Crew Type 9845		Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1822540	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Distraction; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1822497	ATC Issue All Types; Airspace Violation All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Exited Penetrated Airspace; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Airspace Structure; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Environment - Non Weather Related	Primarily, en route ATC was task saturated the entire day working multiple frequencies while providing speed and vector control, versus sequencing traffic on the arrivals. On the leg from ZZZ to ZZZ1, we were handed off to ZZZ near the waypoint ZZZZZ.  After multiple failed attempts to reach ATC, we returned to the previous frequency to try an alternate frequency. This frequency was very busy as ATC was working multiple frequencies and also providing multiple aircraft with vectors and speed control. We were given 10 degrees left.  ATC told us to expect direct ZZZZZ1.  As we approached ZZZZZ2, we tried multiple times to reach ATC on the frequency assigned. Unable to reach them we tried the alternate frequency multiple times with no success. Shortly after those attempts, ATC transmitted on Guard frequency for us to turn south immediately. We replied that we are turning south and had attempted to reach them multiple times on two separate frequencies.  Their reply was, 'Yeah, right!'		On (date) at approximately (time), we lost communication with Center, while on a vector and ended up in Special Use Airspace. About 10 minutes prior, we were switched to a frequency from previous Controller. We were unable to get contact on this frequency, so we went back to our previous assigned frequency and were given a new frequency. We were able to contact Center on this frequency. While with Center on this frequency, we were given a vector for spacing with an expectation to rejoin our arrival. After a few minutes, without hearing anything we began to query the Controller. We had no contact. We tried a few times, when the Captain said we should try the other frequency we were given earlier. As we switched over, we hear Center simulcasting on Guard, to come up. As we contacted Center, the Controller asked us where we had been and we explain that we were up on the frequency where they were talking to us earlier. The Controller then proceeds to give us a vector and tells us that we are in Special Use Airspace. Once we cleared the Airspace we continued with the arrival uneventfully.		A flight crew reported they flew into military Restricted Airspace when were assigned a vector off course and issued a frequency change. They could not establish communications on the new frequency in a timely manner due to frequency congestion and saturation on the new frequency.	
1822607	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				11000					Dawn			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B ZZZ																								DC Battery		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine						1822607	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1822608	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	After takeoff, in our climb, smoke was detected in the cockpit. The smoke was coming from the First Officer's yoke. We also started to have BAT DISCHARGE caution light come on multiple times. Followed appropriate check list and QRH procedures. Decided to go back to the airport. We got clearance to go directly to the airport and got vectors for ILS. All the appropriate procedures and check list were completed before landing, with verification of speeds and weight. After an uneventful landing, we did not need any further assistance, and went to our parking spot.		Advised ATC due to smoke in cockpit. Returned to [departure airport]. QRH was read, approach was briefed and setup. Checklists were followed. Uneventful landing was made under maximum weight. Battery discharge light annunciated several times during the event. First Officer smelled electric burning, then noticed smoke. Caution light annunciation and battery discharge light noticed during normal scan. I should have waited and asked ATC to standby before announcing the aircraft condition. This would have given time for the crew to evaluate all resources and outcomes before making a decision.		B737-800 flight crew reported returning to departure airport after experiencing smoke from the First Officer yoke and a battery discharge annunciation.	
1823119	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US	270	8		6000		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	STAR ZZZZZ 1	Class C ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Improperly Operated; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 300; Flight Crew Type 15000		Situational Awareness			1823119	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 210; Flight Crew Type 3500		Situational Awareness			1823115	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach		N	Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Software and Automation	Environment - Non Weather Related	I was the Pilot Flying on the ZZZZZ 1 Arrival into ZZZ. ZZZ TRACON asked us what approach we wanted to [Runway] XXR into ZZZ. We requested the RNAV visual to [Runway] XXR. We were cleared to 7,000 feet and cleared to intercept the final approach course for [Runway] XXR after ZZZZZ. We thought we were cleared for the approach so we set the next altitude of 4,000 feet in past ZZZZZ.  After crossing ZZZZZ the aircraft started descending to 4,000 feet. ZZZ TRACON informed us of a Low Altitude Alert and had us climb to 7,000 feet, then he Immediately cleared us for the visual. Told us to take down the phone number for possible pilot deviation.		Both our first time to ZZZ.  We were cleared to intercept final segment. We said to each other, 'ZZZZZ is IAF,' and then we determined it was the final course we were supposed to intercept.  We should have asked ATC to repeat, but we put 4,000 feet in the autopilot, as in VNAV, to continue published approach, but 7,000 feet was still our clearance limit.  On the published segment it continued down, inside ZZZZZ, below 7,000 feet.  ATC queried us at 6,000 feet and told us to climb.  We had the field in sight and ATC then cleared us immediately for the visual.I was so focused on terrain and being in VNAV. As Pilot Monitoring, I should have questioned the clearance and any confusion we had. I am sure what we heard, thought we heard and what ATC said were all different.		B737-700 flight cew reported a low altitude alert and initiated a Go Around at an airport that both crew members were flying into for the first time.	
1823520	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US						VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent; Initial Approach	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1823520	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1823534	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	On final approach, we lost the green hydraulic system. We entered holding to complete the non-normal procedure and get fully configured. We also requested a longer runway and emergency equipment. The landing was made to a full stop because of loss of normal brakes and nose wheel steering. We were then towed to the gate.		We experienced a mechanical failure (HYD G SYS LO PRESS) upon our descent into ZZZ on the ZZZZZ1 Arrival beginning approximately outside ZZZ waypoint. I was Pilot Flying, the Captain followed appropriate ECAM actions and checklists following company procedures. We advised ATC based on this ECAM considering our location and situational aspects.  We elected to hold over the final approach fix for GPS approach to Runway XX while we completed all of our necessary actions in the interest of safety. Upon completion of these duties, we exited the hold, completed approach and landed successfully coming to full stop on the runway. Based on the mechanical condition of the aircraft as result of systems lost from hydraulic system, we coordinated with company operations to tow us in due to loss of nose wheel steering and deplaned normally. Mechanical malfunction (HYD G low pressure) ECAM.		A320 flight crew reported the loss of the green hydraulic system resulted in a precautionary landing and a tow to the gate.	
1823945	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				34000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 116; Flight Crew Total 6707; Flight Crew Type 5884		Situational Awareness; Training / Qualification; Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface; Distraction; Confusion; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1823945												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Human Factors; Procedure; Software and Automation; Aircraft	Human Factors	Inadvertent switching off of IR1 mode selector which deactivated that associated ADIRU. ADR and IR data not available. This occurred during flight one of day two of an initial Captain upgrade. The trainee came off the B777. He has been with our company for many years. The three legs of day one was extraordinarily busy. It was immediately clear to me during those three legs he had studied hard during training and had mastered much of the material. On the first two legs of day one I had a LCA (Line Check Airman) in-training watching me conduct the line check as part of that LCA's upgrade training. I commented to the new LCA not all trainees will be so well prepared as this one.This flight was leg four of the IOE and the first flight of day two. Our transportation to the airport was 15 minutes late and there was a detour so we arrived at the aircraft about 30 minutes prior to departure. Normally, I introduce myself to all the FAs (Flight Attendant). However, because we were on IOE, [we] were somewhat late to the aircraft and on Release 6. I only introduced myself to the FM. I felt I did not have time go to the back to introduce myself to the FAs assigned to the rear of the aircraft.We had been in the seat for approximately 3 hours. The upgrade Captain requested I ask the FM to prepare the meals and that we would then take a restroom break when the meals were ready. I did so. The FM called us about 25 minutes later to state the meals were ready. The upgrade Captain offered me the opportunity to use the Lav first. I declined and said he should go first and I would then go. The door chime rang and then I heard a knock. That knock is not standard. However, it occurs occasionally with legacy Company 2 flight attendants. We put on our masks. I saw the upgrade Captain move to get out of his seat, stand up, and let the FA in while he then left the cockpit. I noticed nothing odd about his movement out of the seat and to the rear of the cockpit. I saw out of the corner of my eye the FA was standing near the rear of the cockpit just behind and to the right of the Captain's seat, a normal position for a relief FA.My concentration then focused on something else. I think it was because we were getting ready to enter a large area of convective activity. I might have been looking at WSI and comparing that to what I was seeing on the weather radar. Why my gaze was generally focused forward and right I am not sure, but it was. I planned to brief the relief FA about this weather and its possible effect on their service in a few minutes. It was at that point, maybe 30-45 seconds after the upgrade Captain had left the cockpit, that for lack of a better description, 'all hell broke loose.' The cockpit went from a completely benign environment to one of confusion. The startle effect was in full force. The first warning was a continuous repetitive chime and the warning lights flashing. I immediately looked at the E/WD (Engine/Warning Display) and noted about 4-5 ECAMs listed, primary secondary and independent failures in both red and amber. I silenced the CRC (Continuous Repetitive Chime) by pushing [the] master warning light. That silenced the CRC. However, there still was an aural alert going off that I could not silence, which created a fair bit of consternation on my part. I look at my PFD and saw the FMAs were wrong. I then saw the AP (Autopilot) was off and that there was nothing where I expected to see the AT (Autothrottle) condition. I noted the aircraft climbing slightly and corrected that. I knew the AP disengaged for some reason but didn't know why. I thought about engaging AP 2 but then thought that may create new problems, so I elected to hand-fly until the ECAMs were sorted out. I saw we were no longer in NAV but again, wasn't sure why. I did see that the aircraft didn't diverge much from the 'green line' so lateral stick inputs weren't necessary. At this point ATC called to ask us something about recycling the transponder. We wereon speaker at that time; I responded we had a problem and to standby.I then noted the airspeed increasing and pulled the TLs (Thrust Lever) back; I didn't want to add an over-speed to our problems. This silenced another aural sound. I do not recall seeing a Thrust Locked ECAM or FMA which I thought would have been the source of the alert I couldn't silence. I glanced at the Captain PFD (Primary Flight Display) and ND (Navigation Display) and saw they didn't look right.The aircraft was reasonably stabilized now but, in my mind, because the ECAMs were displayed, the situation wasn't. I then told the FA to recall the trainee Captain to the cockpit. He opened the door, looked and then closed it, and said he was in the bathroom. I told the FA I knew that and I wanted him back in the cockpit now. He then had the FM knock on the Lav door. The trainee Captain returned to the cockpit and the relief FA left. The trainee Captain was incredulous when he looked at the E/WD. I told him I was hand-flying, had the aircraft and radios, and that he was to handle the ECAMs. It was at this point I noted we were about 20 knot slow, well within flying speed but slow for planned speed. I had overcompensated for my worry of overspending the aircraft. I pushed the power back up. Airspeed had fallen out of my cross-check. The trainee Captain methodically and correctly went through the ECAMs. As I recall the first several ECAMs had no action items, one ECAM had us switch the air data switch to three. At some point during this, ATC stated they now had data on us and asked if we needed assistance. We told him we were completing a procedure and to standby. Once the status messages were read and the ECAM procedure completed, I reached up to engage AP two. It did so. I armed the AT and then moved the TLs to the climb detent to engage it. Our ATC clearance was to proceed direct to a fix so we reinserted that. I don't recall having to pull or push to capture our assigned altitude but I can't be sure.It was now maybe 4-5 minutes after the initial event where I could take a breath. I recalled the ECAM and saw the ADR 1 fault. I then looked over to the ADIRS panel and saw IR mode selector one was in the OFF position. It, of course, was on for the flight up until some point in [the] last few minutes. I asked the trainee Captain if he recalls hitting the switch with his head or back as he left his seat. He said no. I then thought perhaps the switch was defective. This aircraft came to us as a through flight. We did not have to turn them on so we had no knowledge of how that switch felt by turning it on. We again discussed it and he stated he didn't recall even touching the overhead panel while leaving the seat.Because I didn't introduce myself to the FAs at the rear of the aircraft prior to the flight, I wasn't sure which FA came to the cockpit during our break. I asked the FM to ask the FA who came to the cockpit to call us on the interphone. That FA did and I asked him if he recalled touching anything on the overhead panel; he stated he did not. What I found unusual was that during the event, the FA seemed very nonplussed by the cacophony of sounds in the cockpit. I would have expected a FA to at least ask what was going on, but maybe he was somewhat shocked by what he saw and heard during his short time in the cockpit.The trainee Captain and I discussed that I would write up the ECAM and then conference Dispatch and Technical Support Maintenance Control to provide further details of what we thought may have occurred. I wanted the switch looked at but also didn't want Maintenance to search for a problem which might not exist. I ACARS'd Dispatch with the request and he set up the conference call. The communications with Technical Support Maintenance Control were reasonably good and he captured exactly what I wanted him to know and he appended it to our write-up. Since the Captain's displays were significantly degraded, we switched PF/PM roles. Fromthat point we continually deviated for weather, were put in holding, and changed our alternate three times. We over-burned by almost 5500 lb on this flight, more than on any flight I have been on in [an] Airbus. It was an understatement to say this was a busy flight. I don't know how this happened. The final flight of the day was a termination. As the trainee ran that checklist and turned off the IRUs, he stated he couldn't believe how he accidentally managed to switch that style of switch - pull and turn - off by getting out of the cockpit.I am disappointed in myself for not more thoroughly reviewing the flight manual for ADR 1 fault after things had settled down. We did not have a GPWS TERR FAULT light and no ECAM direction to switch it off so it remained on. We discussed that threat in that we were now the aircraft's GPWS system and to be cognizant of the terrain. We also discussed changing the transponder to position 2 but did not do so because there was no ECAM direction to [do] so and ATC noted no anomalies in our reporting. Also, once I saw the IR mode selector switch in the off position, I should have considered reviewing the NAV IR fault non-normal even though we didn't have that ECAM. I did not do a word search in the flight manual for ADR. Had I done so, I would have seen that there may be RVSM limitations associated with its failure and should have gone to the FOM for further information. I recall many years ago, our Line Manager at the time, wrote up in his weekly update a similar event. As I recall he wrote the trainee somehow managed to hook the window shade into that same switch and turned it off. That of course didn't happen here.				A320 Captain reported that a component was inadvertently switched off after the other pilot left the cockpit for a lavatory break and a multitude of warnings and ECAM messages went off as a result.	
1824040	202107	0001-0600	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Night				Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing; Taxi	Vectors																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 14; Flight Crew Total 161995; Flight Crew Type 616		Training / Qualification; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1824040												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		N	Person Flight Crew	Aircraft In Service At Gate	General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	We took off out of ZZZ to ZZZ1 for a night ETOPS flight. Everything was normal. Although the crew was current, all hadn't flown much in the past XX months due to COVID. We briefed this fact and were aware that we would all have to be on our toes due to the fact that our flying skills were rusty.The flight was normal. We operated the flight very close to the flight plan.After a normal approach and smooth landing, we taxied to the gate. After shutting down the engines and performing the Parking Checklist, we were surprised to see an overweight landing message being printed. We had landed approximately 700 pounds overweight.We sent the appropriate overweight landing code and I called local maintenance to report the issue.				Air carrier Captain reported an overweight landing at destination airport caused by failure to monitor fuel burn during flight.  Captain cited rusty pilot skills caused by COVID inactivity as a contributing factor.	
1824764	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				17000								TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class E ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Distraction			1824764	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument			Distraction; Workload			1824530	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Company Policy; Human Factors	Aircraft	I, the CA (Captain), was PM (Pilot Monitoring); the FO (First Officer) was PF (Pilot Flying). During the climb-out phase in level flight in mildly hazy VMC at 17,000 MSL at 290 KIAS with Autopilot B engaged the Master Caution, the 'FLT CONT' item on the CA-side SAP ('6-pack'), and the right half only of the 'STAB OUT OF TRIM' light illuminated. Press-to-test of the 'Stab out of trim' light showed both bulbs operable. After establishing that FO was actively remaining the PF, I performed 'Stabilizer Out of Trim' QRH procedure with the result that the autopilot electric trim and both pilot yokes trim switches were inoperative in both directions. I notified ATC that we had a mechanical problem that would eventually result in [requesting priority] and landing at a nearby airport, probably ZZZ, but for now we were content to remain at 17,000 slowly outbound from ZZZ while we worked the problem. Knowing we were also over landing weight, nicely under control, and still had a lot of checklists to run, I had no desire to rush into diving towards some final approach fix before we were ready to land. I performed the 'Stabilizer Trim Inoperative' QRH procedure with the result that manual hand-cranked trimming was possible in either direction, albeit against unusually high resistance and with a 'cogging' feel as if pushing against a powered but stalled electric motor. This was true even when the aircraft was in-trim. One pilot could move the trim using one arm, but two pilots using one arm each worked much better for anything more than a couple turns of the trim wheel. We found that one pilot could both hand-fly and crank the trim, but tended to make unwanted aileron inputs while doing so. So it was doable in a pinch but not smooth. The forces required to turn the trim wheel did not appreciably reduce as we later slowed & configured. In the course of reading & discussing or doing the 'deferred items' section of the 'Stabilizer Trim Inoperative' QRH I started to activate the GPWS Flap inhibit switch.  The FO stopped me saying we were above the 250 KIAS speed limit at that time.  That sounded familiar to me, but no such limitation is stated at that point in the QRH.  Nevertheless, we chose to delay activating the GPWS flap inhibit until we had slowed to first configuration speed around 220KIAS. Discussed with [the] FO whether ZZZ was the best divert option and which runway to use. ZZZ & longer Runway XXL was the unanimous choice. By that time we were a few miles south of [the] VOR on a south-southwesterly course for ZZZZZ, roughly 100nm from ZZZ.I then performed the 'Non-routine Landing' QRH procedure, alerting ATC for a return to ZZZ XXL with ARFF (Airport Rescue Firefighting) standing by. Coordinated with ATC for return via direct arrival for ILS XXL. Asked Company explicitly to alert ZZZ Approach & Tower of the intended non-standard XXL landing. Notified Dispatch via ACARS report. Briefed FAs via intercom, and passengers via PA on the situation, my expectations, and their roles.  We selected the non-standard landing Runway XXL based on that runway being much longer than the more typical landing Runway XXR.  That was a minor miscue, because I/we had not considered the effect of the large displaced threshold. Which I now consider insignificant.  For landings eastbound the difference is 2500 feet and would be significant. Lesson learned: the big runway length number on the 10-9 page can be significantly misleading; take the time to dig into the 10-9A details page. The fact that was our takeoff runway and within Jepp FDPro I'd used the green highlighter to extend the highlighting all the way to the takeoff threshold didn't help.  What had been useful to help direct my taxi to the correct threshold for takeoff became mildly misleading when evaluating which runway to land on. Which was not a side effect I noticed at the time or had considered previously. Still working as PM I performed normal divert preparations and top-of-descentprocedures: Reconfigured FMS & ACARS for ZZZ arrival, obtained ATIS, set up radios and performed approach brief, landing assessment, etc. Notified ZZZ Ops of our return by direct radio call to obtain a gate assignment.We discussed that we'd be a little above the normal max landing weight, but the already requested priority covers for that administrative requirement, and the runway available was nearly double what the QRH procedure's performance chart said was required. Performed descent checklist and before landing checklists and flows as normal.  Throughout the evolution the FO kept us neatly in control, tight on the FD despite sometimes having to hand-crank the trim himself, caught a few radio calls I missed, and demonstrated overall high situational awareness and excellent systems knowledge. This is especially impressive for someone fairly new to the airplane who is just back from 6 months of furlough. During descent below 10,000 ft approaching downwind we changed control to CA as PF w FO as PM. We sighted the field while on base. During the turn-on to final I was using visual lookout primary, backed up by the LNAV purple line and the ILS. There was a moment of confusion as the ILS and LNAV came together before I was aligned with what I had thought was the landing runway.  Having landed on XXR dozens (hundreds?) of times over the decades, that habit was unhelpful for a few seconds before we got fully established pointing at the correct runway. We then realized that Runway XXR is concrete and very easy to see in mildly hazy conditions while Runway XXL is asphalt and much harder to see. In fact XXL was invisible until we got closer in following the LNAV & LOC. My general habit on any landing involving parallel runways is to verbalize seeing all the relevant runways by name and explicitly saying which one I'm aligning / aligned with. E.g. 'I see XXL and XXR. I'm aligning with XXL.'  For whatever reason that verbalization didn't occur; with sorta predictable results.Intercepted final outside intermediate fix in level flight at 3,000 ft. Configured to flaps 15 early and slowed gradually to Vapp of approximately 160KIAS before glideslope intercept at final approach fix. FO kept the airplane nicely in trim on my requests.Made a flaps 15 slightly overweight (approx. 148K#) approach and landing to a smooth touchdown on ZZZ Runway XXL just shy of the 2000ft marker. Upon stopping made a PA to reassure the passengers & remind them the safety vehicles would be seen around the airplane. ATC gave us an alternate frequency to contact ARFF on. Which surprised both of us a bit. Once we were communicating with ARFF I requested they inspect us for hot brakes or damaged tires.  ARFF found normal brake temps and no tire damage. At ARFF's request I released them at that point and we taxied normally to [the] gate.Following shutdown the FO attempted to manually crank the trim to the usual post-shutdown 5 units from the 8-9 units we'd used at touchdown.  At zero airspeed the trim was still 'cogging' and almost as difficult to move as it had been in flight. So we left the trim at the landing setting for Maintenance's edification. Which point we discussed with the Chief Pilot who met the flight and again later with the maintenance folks who also met the flight. I made appropriate aircraft maintenance log book entries for the stab trim failure and for the overweight landing. Spoke with Dispatch by phone before leaving aircraft per FOM. The next morning I filed the required report. In all I think this event worked out pretty close to textbook, minus the small glitches identified above.The main electric trim failing was the causal factor. Everything else is just details.1.	 Subsequent research on the ground shows that activating the GPWS Flap Inhibit switch is called for in 11 different QRH procedures, and in the Flaps 15 landing procedure.  Only the 'One Engine Inoperative' QRH procedure and the 'Flaps 15 Landing' procedures mention that limit; the other 10 QRH procedures do not.I do not know whether those differences are deliberate or an oversight. Suggest someone on the 737 fleet team look into this. If the difference is deliberate there probably ought to some verbiage available in QRH or [procedures] to explain the rationale so others don't mistakenly apply a remembered restriction where it's not intended. 2.	 We were both surprised to see that only half of the 'Stab out of trim' annunciator illuminated.  Which left us wondering at first if we had a warning system malfunction or a trim malfunction.  I surmise now that the right bulb is connected to FCC (Flight Control Computer)  B and the left is connected to FCC A.  And that had the Captain been PF, we'd have seen the left half illuminate instead.However it works, the details of left & right are not documented in [manual] where the annunciator is described. At a minimum there should be a statement like 'Only half of the annunciator may illuminate. That is still a valid indication of an out-of-trim condition.'3.	Overall, from the 'Stab out of trim' annunciator illuminating to touchdown was about 30 minutes.  From reviewing the ACARS logs I know it was at least 28 minutes and less than 35.  As the CA / PM / QRH operator I was working quickly and diligently almost the entire time to accomplish all the non-routine and normal procedures in full without hurrying.  Had we turned back even a couple minutes earlier the airplane would have been approaching the airport before we were fully caught up and mentally transitioned from handling an [urgent inflight situation] to performing a normal low-stress, albeit flaps 15, landing.Given the reality that following all the procedures fully takes nearly 30 minutes, I believe we do a disservice to pilots in the sim, rushing around the traffic pattern with an engine out. That encourages hurrying, doing QRH steps without really thinking them and their implications through, etc. We fight how we train, and we're training to hurry. I question the wisdom of that.Further I question the wisdom of planned arrival fuels that only allow for approximately 30 minutes to flameout at traffic pattern altitudes.  The so-called FAR '45 minute reserve' is 45 minutes at cruise, not 45 minutes at traffic pattern altitude with e.g. flaps stuck partly extended. I now conclude that performing a major QRH evolution discovered in the arrival traffic pattern while down near Company planned arrival fuels would necessitate skipping a lot of steps or giving them at best a lick and a promise.I believe it is unwise to plan arrival fuels that tightly. Yes, we commonly have additional hold and / or Dispatch fuels.  But each of those may legitimately be burned in delays encountered before the [urgent situation] appears.  This saves Company a lot of money on the many flights that don't turn out worst-case. But it really sucks to be the pilot who draws that short straw, and is faced with either doing a rushed job of handling the [situation] or running out of fuel.That's planning, in extremis, to fail. An industry-leading FAR 121 carrier should not be planning to fail. In extremis or otherwise.		At a level off of 17,000 ft we noticed stab out of trim light. I was the PF so it was my aircraft. The Captain ran the stab out of trim QRH checklist which lead to the QRH stab trim inoperative checklist. We did not regain electric trim. Manual trim was what we were left with. Then the non-routine QRH checklist was performed. The manual trim wheel was difficult to work for both us even when the plane was near an in trim condition. Speed was slowed to about 270kts from about 290kts then. [ATC was advised] and we planned and set up for an arrival back to ZZZ.  I flew the aircraft and worked the manual trim while the CA performed checklists, set up for the arrival and approach, and the briefings. Manual trim did not become much easier until on approach when aircraft was slowed to approach speed, however even then it was difficult. The CA took the aircraft and I became the PM. The landing was nice and smooth. Once on the ground using the manual trim wheel to reset the stab trim to 5 units was still very difficult.		Air carrier flight crew reported experiencing a Stabilizer Out of Trim problem during climb out. They completed the appropriate QRH checklist and performed an air turn back.	
1825023	202107	1801-2400	ZJX.ARTCC	FL				37300		IMC			Daylight			Center ZJX	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise		Class A ZJX																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1825023	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 160; Flight Crew Type 19000					1825649	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Weather	Weather	While cruising at FL370, approximately 40 miles south of SAV, we experienced a severe updraft, causing the aircraft to rapidly climb to approximately 37,300 ft and then rapidly descend to approximately 36,500 ft. There was no weather displayed on our weather radar in our area of flight. The fluctuations lasted for about one minute. The rest of the flight continued as normal.		In cruise at FL370, encountered clear air turbulence. No radar echoes, must have been convective activity building below our altitude. Updraft produced rain from below, lifted aircraft 300 ft above our cruise altitude. First Officer applied upset recovery controls which required descent 600 ft below our cruise altitude. Reported the event to Jacksonville Center. No other traffic in the area, no evasive action required. Flight continued normally without further incident.		Flight crew reported encountering severe updrafts and downdrafts resulting in deviations from their assigned altitude. The flight crew recovered from the upset condition and continued the flight without issue.	
1825156	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US												Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B ZZZ																								Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1825156												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Aircraft	Aircraft	Dirty sock odor descending into ZZZ. [Suggest] aircraft filtered bleed air.				Air Carrier Captain reported a fume event of 'dirty socks odor' during descent to destination.	
1825783	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Dawn				Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 121; Flight Crew Total 3004; Flight Crew Type 3004					1825783	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 104; Flight Crew Total 2843; Flight Crew Type 2843					1825788	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	Other Take Off	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	We commenced our takeoff normally. At around 95 kts, we got an ECAM for Y RSVR Lo Lvl and Y RSVR Lo Air Pr. The Captain initiated the abort with a max speed of approximately 100 kts. All callouts and notifications were made and we were able to taxi clear of the runway and stop. ATC notified us that we had trailed smoke so we asked him to roll the ARFF (Aircraft Rescue and Firefighting) equipment. We ran the rejected takeoff checklist and then ran the ECAMs of which there were several all relating to the hydraulic issue. ARFF indicated that we had dumped a lot of hydraulic fluid. We were towed to the gate for repairs and offloaded the passengers and bags.		Received a Master Caution light and ECAM during takeoff roll on Runway XXR at ZZZ and accomplished a rejected takeoff. The Master Caution and ECAM occurred at approximately 95 kts and Reject initiated between 95-105 kts. During the reject, several ECAMS displayed indicating loss of the Yellow Hydraulic system reservoir and Yellow system air pressure. At slow speed and with normal brakes and nosewheel steering available, I opted to take high speed Taxiway XX to safely clear the runway and bring aircraft to a full stop while the First Officer coordinated with Tower controller to roll the AARF (Aircraft Rescue and Firefighting) equipment as Tower stated they saw what appeared to be smoke trailing from the number two engine. While awaiting AARF response, we ran the Rejected Takeoff QRC and handled the ECAMS, started the APU and communicated with the passengers [and] inflight crew. After AARF arrival and initial inspection, we shutdown the number 2 engine with AARF on scene, then chocked the gear and shutdown the number 1 engine which had been used to keep normal brakes available. We coordinated with Operations and Ground Control for uneventful tow back to the gate for repair.		A320 Flight Crew reported a Rejected Take Off when the Yellow Hydraulic system showed low quantity and had to be towed back to the gate.	
1826321	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US	075	10		3000		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Trailing Edge Flap		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Type 9000		Troubleshooting			1826321												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	During initial climbout from Runway XXR, the Trailing Edge (TE) flaps failed to retract from the takeoff position, flaps 1. I noticed this, as I prepared to place the Landing Gear lever to OFF and autobrakes to OFF. I directed to the FO (First Officer), who was PF (Pilot Flying), to slow to 210 KIAS while I coordinated with ATC to level off at 6,000 ft. MSL and Hold PPOS, approximately 15 miles southeast of the airport. I pulled out the QRH as we assessed our indications. The TE Flap gauge showed a slight split in the needles, with the R needle slightly retracted from flaps 1 and the L needle at flaps 1. I considered both Flap Asymmetry and Flap Disagree Checklists, and we decided the Asymmetry Checklist was more correct, despite the flap lever being UP. The PF did indicate very slight anomalous roll indications, prior to engaging the AP (Autopilot). I then informed the Passengers of our issue, briefed the FAs (Flight Attendants) that we would hold and work the problem and that we would be diverting to either ZZZ1 or ZZZ. Dispatch was very helpful in getting the Company Operations Center involved and suggesting ZZZ, editing the Release and giving us fuel and weight and balance data for worst case scenario. We began the QRH, holding at the deferred items for descent and landing. I then coordinated for the RNAV Z XXR Approach, briefed the Passengers of our decision to return to ZZZ, and briefed the FAs on the ETA and after landing plans. We briefed and commenced the approach, completed the QRH, ran brake cooling and reviewed the plan one last time. The PF flew a very nice approach at Vref 40 + 30 to a smooth flaps 1 landing with AB3 required and selected. On rollout I suggested he quickly disarm the AB and let the aircraft roll out at max reverse thrust to allow for more brake cooling. The data indicated 18 minutes of cooling at the gate so we taxied to the gate, shut the aircraft down and coordinated with Operations and [Maintenance Control]. Training and Company operational priorities had us well-prepared and allowed us to handle the issue safely and with ease.				B737 Captain reported trailing edge flaps did not retract after takeoff and was diagnosed as a flap asymmetry problem, resulting in an air turn back.	
1826339	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				10000		VMC							Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	GPS; FMS Or FMC	Climb	SID ZZZZZ																															Small Aircraft, Low Wing, 2 Eng, Retractable Gear																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 83; Flight Crew Total 17407; Flight Crew Type 48		Time Pressure; Communication Breakdown; Distraction; Workload	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1826339	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 53; Flight Crew Total 7297; Flight Crew Type 449		Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Workload; Distraction; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1826643	ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance	Vertical 490	N	Automation Air Traffic Control; Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Human Factors	The Captain was hand flying the aircraft on the ZZZZZ RNAV Departure off of Runway XXR from ZZZ, climbing to FL 190. ATC issued our flight an intermediate level off altitude of 10,000 ft. Approaching 10,000 ft. and about to level off, our flight received a TCAS RA to 'Maintain Vertical Speed,' due to an aircraft that appeared to be descending towards our aircraft and crossing our flight path. The Captain complied with and performed the TCAS RA maneuver. Then, the ATC Controller and what sounded like an ATC Supervisor intervening, issued instructions to the other aircraft to turn and climb immediately. At this point, our flight's TCAS RA changed from a 'Maintain Vertical Speed' to a 'Descend Now.' The Captain complied with the changing 'Descend Now' TCAS RA maneuver. Once the radio communications cleared, the First Officer was able to inform ATC of our TCAS RA. As a result of our TCAS RA maneuvering and the ATC issued turn and climb instructions to the other aircraft, the other aircraft appeared to pass our aircraft forward, above and to the right at less than 500 ft. Our flight then continued to ZZZ1 without further incidence.		Departing ZZZ on the ZZZZZ ZZZZZ1 transition, we were initially cleared to FL190. Passing 8,800 ft., we were told to stop our climb at 10000 ft. The Captain was hand flying. At 9,800 ft., we received a 'maintain vertical speed' in a climb RA. ATC gave the conflicting aircraft, a light twin, an immediate climb and turn to the east. The traffic was pointed out to us and I responded, 'Roger. Aircraft X TCAS climb.' I then spotted the aircraft (conditions were VMC) but was unable to tell ATC due to radio congestion. As the conflicting aircraft began to climb away from us, we received a course reversal to 'DESCEND. DESCEND NOW' RA. The Captain followed the guidance. ATC gave us a frequency change and I read back the clearance and added 'TCAS descent' and changed frequencies. I estimate we passed within 500 ft. as the aircraft passed above us and to the right, turning away from us. The flight continued without incident to ZZZ1.		B777 flight crew reported an NMAC during the departure climb	
1827225	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Nose Gear Wheel		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 182					1827225	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 60; Flight Crew Type 2435					1826293	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	[There was a] nose gear tire issue followed by a loss of System A Hydraulics. We were flying Flight XXXX from ZZZ to ZZZ1 on (date). As we were rolling down the runway, we heard a loud bang from the nose gear area. It went away as quickly as it arrived, engine parameters were normal as well as directional control. After the takeoff, we talked about what it could have been. During the climb to our cruising altitude, we decided to call ZZZ Tower back and ask them to inspect Runway XR for any clues to what had happened during our takeoff roll. It took a while before we got an update from ZZZ Tower. They had found a two feet by two piece of rubber. We immediately contacted Dispatch and Maintenance, and we discussed various options. We were now in cruise and still bound for ZZZ1, when all of sudden the Number 1 Eng Hyd A LOW PRESSURE light started to come on and off a few times. We got the QRH Checklist and ran the HYDRAULIC PUMP LOW PRESSURE.I looked down on the instrument panel and noticed the System A Hydraulics quantity was down in the low 20's percent. We could watch that number was dropping fast. We now went quickly over to another chapter in the QRH, to LOSS OF SYSTEM A. We also [requested priority landing] with Center, and they immediately gave us direct to ZZZ1 airport. We determined that it was likely that a possibly piece of rubber had hit some of the Hydraulic lines. We quickly updated Dispatch about our loss of Hydraulic A quantity, and that we had [requested priority landing]. Dispatch gave us the Landing Distance information data, and we compared it to our own. We were all in agreement to continue and land in ZZZ1. We informed our Flight Attendants about our situation and what they could expect from our landing. We flew overhead ZZZ1 airport, and manually extended our landing gear per QRH. After completing all the steps in the QRH, we lined up with Runway XXL and landed. I immediately noticed the tiller was «locked» and unusable upon landing, and had to use rudder and differential braking to get back on center line. Both NORM and ALT nose wheel steering where both unavailable. We elected to stop the aircraft on the runway, and asked the Fire Trucks to visually inspect the aircraft from the outside. After getting the OK from them, we had Company Maintenance meet us on the runway with a tug, and to tow us clear of the runway. We had just cleared [Runway] XXL when the tow bar snapped, and they had to get another one. Once we got the second one, we were towed over to Gate X where our passengers were able to deplane.		Departed ZZZ Runway XR. On takeoff roll, prior to rotation, a noise was heard similar to a hand slap on the front of the aircraft. Takeoff was continued with no adverse indications. As we continued to climb, the Captain and I assessed the probable cause of the noise. ZZZ Tower was advised of possible FOD on the runway. Before we were out of range of ZZZ Tower, we inquired for any findings of the FOD. Tower advised a piece of tire rubber was found on the runway. Still unsure whether the rubber was from our aircraft or a piece we hit on takeoff roll, we continued the assessment. Dispatch was brought into the loop and we all agreed to continue. As we continued to discuss the current situation, the #1 HYD LOW PRESSURE light illuminated and we ran the HYDRAULIC PUMP LOW PRESSURE QRH Checklist. SYSTEM A HYDRAULIC quantity was noted to be around 20 percent. SYSTEM A HYDRAULIC quantity continued to drop. LOSS OF SYSTEM A QRH Checklist was started. [After] reassessment of the loss of HYDRAULIC SYSTEM A and the noise heard at takeoff, [they] led us to believe that we had blown a tire which had damaged the hydraulics. A [priority landing was requested] with Center and direct ZZZ1 was issued. We continued discussions with Dispatch, as well as informed the Flight Attendants of our current conditions, plans and what to expect on arrival in ZZZ1. All went as planned with the completion of the LOSS OF SYSTEM A Checklist and landing on Runway XXL in ZZZ1. The only unexpected item on landing was no nose wheel steering on landing. Both NORM and ALT nose wheel steering was inoperative upon landing so we were unable to exit runway. CFR (Crash Fire Rescue) met the aircraft on the runway to assist. Aircraft was towed to the gate from the runway.		B737-700 flight crew reported hearing a loud bang from the nose gear area during the takeoff roll. In flight, the hydraulic system malfunctioned and the crew performed a manual gear extension prior to landing.	
1827593	202107	1801-2400	ZZZ.Tower	US			500			VMC			Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1827593	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness			1826700	Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Automation Air Traffic Control; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	On the final phase of the Visual Approach, backed up by the ILS Runway XY to ZZZ, with the intention to capture the glide path from above, the Auto Pilot (AP) and Auto Throttles were disengaged. Prevailing conditions were Good Visibility and varying Tail Winds of up to nine (9) Knots. During the final portion of the descent to touchdown, with a visual transition to the PAPI reference already established (PAPI were showing 3 RED-1 WHITE), the Aircraft descended below the referenced visual glide path, with the tendency to fly over the Runway XY overrun area at an altitude (AGL) of less than 50 ft. The moment the Captain, having the Pilot Monitoring (PM) duties, mentioned his estimation for being low on the glide path, the aircraft was overflying a point short of the RWY by about 500 feet. He consecutively called for a Go Around, which was immediately executed by the PF (Pilot Flying). The Flight landed after a RADAR pattern on the same runway uneventfully.[The cause was] Human Factors / Pilot Flying error on visually estimating the projected Glide Slope to Runway XY, with the current Tail Wind conditions. Root cause is the initially higher angle of descent / rate of descent to capture the glide slope from above, that ended up to an over correction - reverse situation of flying finally lower than the projected/correct slope. [I suggest] earlier Aircraft configuration for the Final Approach Phase and timely request from the ATC for lower altitudes / earlier descent to the recommended altitudes that lead to a better projected sequence of flying tasks.		FO was flying pilot. Performed visual approach and FO had a hard time trying to catch the glide slope from above because he was trying to slow down and put flaps down while on OPEN DESC. FO called for 'the bird', which caught me off guard because it is the first time anyone has asked for the flight path vector while trying to intercept the ILS and while disconnecting the AP. He not only disconnected AP but also eventually the auto thrust without previously briefing it. I told him to never disconnect the auto thrust again while trying to save a visual approach. Finally he was established on the ILS. Below 500 feet he said he was going to follow the PAPI. I glanced down at the VSI to make sure we were stable and when I looked backed up we had 4 red on the PAPIs and at the same time we received a GS GPWS warning. Became unstable. I loudly and sternly called 'unstable, go-around.' Performed go-around as RA called 50 feet. FO performed a soft go-around. I overrode his decision after he brought back TL (Thrust Lever) to CLB and I pushed TL to TOGA since we were in ZZZ (high airport elevation) with high OAT. I honestly could not tell you the altitude when the FO brought back the TL to CLB. I wanted to make sure we were safe since the go-around was performed at 50 feet from an unsafe position. At 50 feet RA, aircraft was not anywhere near the approach end of the runway. Came back for an uneventful landing.FO descended below glide slope and PAPI showed 4 red.Soft Go-around procedure should be changed or at least highlight the need to take into account airport elevation and OAT when deciding to make a soft go-around from a low altitude with an obvious performance deterioration.Soft Go-around procedure should be changed or at least highlight the need to take into account airport elevation and OAT when deciding to make a soft go-around from a low altitude with an obvious performance deterioration.		Flight crew reported that the First Officer made errors during final approach to landing and the Captain elected to call for a go around.	
1827640	202107	1801-2400	SEA.Airport	WA												Tower SEA	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B SEA																												Tower SEA	Air Carrier	B737 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach		Class B SEA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument						1827640	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1827637	Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Environment - Non Weather Related	Arriving SEA, ATC cleared us to follow aircraft ahead to visual approach 34L.  We were given 170kts to final fix due to ATC spacing us just over 3 miles behind a B737.  I was Pilot Monitoring, the First Officer the Pilot Flying had our plane on autopilot using heading select and vertical speed to navigate onto the localizer and glideslope.Just as the plane was joining the localizer, a mild buffeting was felt followed by a rapid roll in direction of the turn. Aural warning 'Bank Angle' annunciated.  The First Officer immediately disconnected the autopilot and reduced the bank angle to normal parameters.  The rest of the approach was uneventful.On visual approaches, especially with close spacing, fly above the path of the preceding aircraft.		While on dogleg to final for RWY 34L, descending to 2,400 feet MSL following preceding B737 by 3.5 NM encountered wake turbulence while on autopilot.  As autopilot initiated turn to join the localizer the aircraft encountered the wake turbulence causing a rapid roll to the right (in the direction of the turn).  The aircraft rolled past 30 degrees of bank and an aural warning 'bank angle' sounded.  The Pilot Flying recognized the excursion and disengaged the autopilot and reduced the bank angle to normal range.  Pilot Flying continued the approach and landed uneventfully.Consider staying above preceding aircraft's altitude/flight path to avoid wake turbulence or increase range between aircraft.		B737-900 flight crew reported encountering wake turbulence on final approach to SEA in trail of a B737.	
1827657	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US				7000		VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing	Vectors; Visual Approach	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 136.2; Flight Crew Total 4292.53; Flight Crew Type 2785.77		Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1827657												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Weather; Human Factors	Human Factors	Thunderstorms were quickly approaching XXL final approach course upon our arrival at ZZZ. ATC provided significant vectoring and cleared us for visual XXL during base leg of approach.  Base leg was squared off toward final approach fix due to approaching weather. During turn to final approach course, pilot flying went below GS (Glide Slope). Tower issued low altitude warning. Pilot initiated corrective action. Aircraft Landed uneventfully.				First Officer reported descending below altitude on approach and climbed back to correct altitude to continue the approach to landing.	
1827759	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US				1500		VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class C ZZZ																								Flap/Slat Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X		Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 137; Flight Crew Total 6835; Flight Crew Type 6835					1827759	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 150; Flight Crew Total 9500; Flight Crew Type 1010					1828234	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Upon configuring flaps from 25 to 30, Trailing Edge Flaps indicated a split and stopped tracking at 27 degrees. LE FLAPS TRANSIT light was also illuminated (amber light). A go-around was accomplished to enable time for non normal checklists to be run. LEADING EDGE FLAPS TRANSIT checklist was run which led us into TRAILING EDGE FLAPS DISAGREE checklist. Flaps were left at 27 degrees with flap handle at 25 setting. Vref15 +5 was the approach speed selected and an uneventful landing and taxi to the gate was accomplished.		On Final Approach to XXL in ZZZ and configuring from flaps 25 to flaps 30 at about 1,500 feet the trailing edge flap needle indicators displayed a split and the flaps stopped at approximately 27 degrees. The LE FLAP TRANSIT light was also illuminated. After taking a few seconds to realize what was happening and coming through approximately 1,000 ft the Captain called for a go around. I executed the go around, however upon attempting to retract the flaps we realized they were stuck at 27 degrees and would not retract at all. I continued to fly the plane and the Captain advised ATC and then ran the non-normal LEADING EDGE FLAP TRANSIT QRH followed by the TRAILING EDGE FLAP DISAGREE QRH. We received vectors from ATC and took our time setting up for a flaps 27 landing using the VREF15+5 knot wind additive. While being vectored back to the final approach course the Captain TEST and the FA's and made an announcement to the PAX. We landed and taxied to the gate uneventfully.		Flight Crew reported receiving a trailing edge flap asymmetry indication with a leading edge devices in transit light while on approach and elected to perform a go around in order to accomplish checklists and subsequently landed at destination airport.	
1827797	202107	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				21000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 191; Flight Crew Type 5741					1827797	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 108; Flight Crew Type 1305					1827800	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Flight planning, preflight, ground operations, and takeoff were accomplished per SOP and were uneventful. Captain was pilot flying. Passing 18000 STD, altimeter was selected and AP 1 was selected. At XA:17, we got a master caution light, with HYD G RSVR LO LVL, HYD G ENG 1 PUMP LO PR, and HYD G SYS LO PR displayed on the upper ECAM. After confirming no Green Hydraulic QTY, I asked the FO (First Officer) to request to stop climb and return to field. Captain continued to fly and handle radios. FO accomplished the ECAM procedures, turning off the PTU and Green Eng 1 Pump. QRH was referenced after ECAMs completed, screens normal.We requested priority handling with ATC. Advised Dispatch through ACARS that we had lost all Green Hydraulic QTY, were returning to ZZZ, and would require a tow off runway due to loss of nosewheel steering. Purser was advised of the malfunction, that we were returning to field in 20 minutes, and a cabin prep was not required. A visual approach to XXL was briefed after runway assignment and the descent checklist was accomplished. Aircraft was configured early due to slow flaps/slats and alternate gravity gear extension. Approach and landing were accomplished per SOP and were uneventful. Aircraft was stopped straight ahead on centerline. Engines shut down with timing after starting APU. Aircraft was inspected by Fire Response Team, confirming gear doors down and fluid [was] leaking from under plane behind gear. Super tug arrived and towed us clear of runway to Gate XXX.		On climbout passing approximately 21,000 feet, we got an ECAM indicating loss of the green system, LO G Press, and another green system ECAM but I cannot recall it now. We ran the ECAM action items which had us turn off the PTU and Eng 1 G pump. As soon as the ECAM action items were complete, we communicated with ATC, advising them we needed to stop our climb, and requested priority handling and requested a return to ZZZ. The CA (Captain) and I split up the flying and comm duties, he advised the FA's (Flight Attendant) of our situation, and sent a message to Dispatch. I talked to ATC and flew the aircraft for a brief time while he handled the message to Dispatch.We got the expanded checklist and discussed the effects of [a] loss of the green system in terms of manual cranking of the gear and needing to stop on the runway due to loss of steering, which the CA sent an ACARs to the station to start working on a tow for us to minimize us sitting on the runway. I also advised ATC to let ZZZ know we would be a full-stop on the runway and would need to be towed. We were given priority handling back to ZZZ and we briefed the approach and threats of a go-around since the gear could not be retracted once it was hand-cranked down. The CA flew the approach XXL and the landing and stopping at the end of the runway was uneventful. We dropped the landing gear pins to the tug team and got towed back to the gate. We debriefed once we were off the aircraft and the passengers disembarked. The CA was cool, calm, and collected and flew a textbook approach. We worked well together as a team; I can't think of much that we could have done to improve on how the situation was handled.		First Officer reported loss of all green hydraulic system fluid caused an Air Turn Back and precautionary landing.	
1828652	202107	0001-0600	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC			Night				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Air/Ground Communication		X	Failed; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1828652												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Aircraft	Immediately after takeoff from ZZZ all communication was disrupted by extreme static which inhibited the ability to transmit or receive any communication by either VHF 1 or VHF 2 radio through headsets, hand mics, or speakers. The static continued throughout the entire flight with brief periods of communication possible for 1-5 minutes. Crew elected to continue normal departure and climb and adhere to our filed route clearance while squawking 7600. Dispatch was notified via ACARS and was requested to coordinate down link ATC communications. During brief periods of open communication we informed ATC of our intentions to fly our filed route, arrival STAR and land on runway XXC at destination ZZZ1. Captain and FO discussed and agreed that the safest course of action was to fly our filed and comply with lost communication procedures and not complicate the current flight plan by returning to departure field or an unplanned alternate. FO flew the aircraft from takeoff to landing and captain handled the communication and ATC coordination issues. Arrival landing, taxi and parking at ZZZ1 were uneventful and flight was completed safely.				Captain reported loss of all VHF communications and elected to continue the flight to destination airport.	
1828895	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				37000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Air Data Computer		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1828895	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1828896	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Weather	Aircraft	During cruise at FL370 while deviating for thunderstorms in IMC conditions we encountered an airspeed unreliable condition. After performing memory items we ran the Airspeed Unreliable checklist. While going through the checklist we may have deviated from our assigned FL370 however we don't think we did. While running the checklist the event seemed to end. We continued through the entire checklist to make sure we were aware of all of the possible actions required if the event returned. This event occurred on the same aircraft about 3 weeks prior and maintenance addressed it at that time.I don't have any suggestions as we followed SOP and checklist usage. We don't think we had an altitude deviation but we aren't sure.		During Airspeed Unreliable event at FL370 and IMC, CA (Captain) was pilot flying. Both CA and FO (First Officer) airspeed and altitude were unreliable for at least a minute or more, Airspeed Low and IAS Disagree alerts. Accomplished procedures, and hand flew based on Standby Flight Display. As best we could tell, stayed within +- 200 ft of FL370. Exiting IMC, indications returned to normal. ATC made no comment about altitude.Factors include equipment malfunction and icing. Equipment malfunctions unavoidable		B737-800 flight crew reported unreliable airspeed and altimeter in IMC conditions. Systems returned to normal after exiting IMC.	
1829057	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US		10							Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1829057												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Diverted	Aircraft	Aircraft	While at en route cruise FL370 Flight Crew noticed a decrease in left engine oil quantity.  The white exceedance box illuminated around the secondary engine parameters and the left oil quantity soon went to reverse video.  At this time, oil pressure showed normal at 69%.  As we watched the oil quantity continue its slow drop, the decision was made to divert to [a suitable airport] out of precaution.  Knowing we were above MLGW (Maximum Landing Gear Weight), we requested to hold and burn down.  ATC directed us to hold as published.  I believe we were at 11,000 ft.  We lowered the gear, extended the speedbrake and dropped flaps to expedite the fuel burn.  Once the oil quantity dropped below 10% and anticipating a loss of oil pressure, [we requested special handling from] ATC.  Eventually with oil quantity reaching zero, oil pressure started to fluctuate between 37 and 53%.  At this point feeling the engine oil pump was beginning to cavitate, the Captain took controls and ordered the QRH ENG Low Oil Press checklist and subsequent Engine Shutdown Checklist based on the abnormal indications. We did not wait for the Low Oil Press light to illuminate because we knew it was soon to come. The left engine was secured.  Once MLGW was ensured, Captain landed [using] flaps 15.  Aircraft cleared the runway at the end where fire trucks were waiting.  Airport personnel inspected the left engine and gave the go-ahead for taxi to the gate. During this event, the passengers were well informed of the situation and the diversion.  Dispatch was notified and offered assistance as needed. Post flight inspection revealed the bolts holding the left engine Air Turbine Starter had sheared.  With the ATS (Air Turbine Starter) detached, engine oil which normally lubricates the seals was leaking from the breach.				B737-800 Captain reported diverting to an alternate airport after inflight shutdown following loss of left engine oil quantity.	
1829075	202108	1201-1800	EWR.Airport	NJ						VMC						Tower EWR	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B EWR																												Tower EWR	Air Carrier	B767 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Taxi																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 145; Flight Crew Total 10603; Flight Crew Type 10603					1829075	Aircraft X			Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 112; Flight Crew Total 2791; Flight Crew Type 1163					1829088	Ground Event / Encounter Jet Blast			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	We were flying RNAV Stadium Visual to Runway 29. We were on speed and fully configured by the approach gates. I was hand flying. As we were rolling out on final, [a] B777 was holding in position on Runway 22R at intersection Y. As our aircraft was approaching Runway 29, [the] B777 was cleared for takeoff on Runway 22R. Flight conditions were VFR, there was about a 9-knot tailwind on final. Up to the point of the incident, approach was normal. As I started to bring the airplane into a flare, we experienced a pretty significant upset. To the best of my recollection, we first experienced a significant yawing to the left, followed by a wing drop. I cannot say with any certainty whether the left or right wing dropped. I applied multiple aileron corrections to recover and the touchdown was normal. As we rolled out, it became very obvious that the upset was caused by the B777 applying takeoff thrust as we crossed 22R behind that aircraft. As we rolled to a taxi speed, the Captain advised the Tower of the incident. Also, one of the aircraft awaiting departure on 22R made a comment about what they observed, making us believe that the upset was quite significant to be noticed by another aircrew. Upon arrival at the gate, both Captain and I made a post flight aircraft walk around. We did not observe any type of damage or scratches on either wings, nor the engines nor the tail.		On short final to Runway 29 at EWR. At approximately 100 ft noticed a 777 was cleared to Takeoff from 22R at Y. I noticed we had a crosswind of about 8-9 kts from the left. While in our flare, approximately behind the 777 we experienced a large jet blast of 50 to 60 kts from left. Aircraft got displaced to right. Due to large wind addition, left wing briskly rose due to added lift. Once jet blast hit vertical stabilizer aircraft yawed strongly to the left as well. The aircraft also ballooned up about 50 ft due to all the added wind/lift. I considered a go around but reasoned that any asymmetric thrust application to go-around thrust from a slow spooling engine in an older -800 would prove disastrous in such a low energy state and may possibly have resulted in a cartwheel as we had minimal controllability. This was evidenced to me by the large control wheel applications by the First Officer resulting in only minor flight path modifications. To be frank, if I were to apply full go-around thrust and one engine spooled right up and the other lagged, I did not feel that I would have adequate aileron and/or rudder to control the airplane from it's present state. The First Officer did a masterful job (15 years in present seat) of adding just enough power and slowly bringing aircraft back to centerline. We had a firm but safe landing and stopped with about 1000 ft of runway left. Again, the First Officer showed his experience and did a masterful job recovering. I never even for a second doubted the outcome.		B737 NG FO reported his aircraft was 'significantly upset' just before touchdown at EWR by jet blast from a taxiing B777.	
1829527	202108	1801-2400	LAX.Airport	CA				10000		VMC						TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B LAX																												TRACON SCT	Air Carrier	B767 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Descent		Class B LAX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 250; Flight Crew Total 18000; Flight Crew Type 12000					1829527												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	During IRNMN arrival descent into LAX we encountered a small amount of turbulence I would classify as light above FL190 that felt like wake turbulence so we asked ATC who we were following.  Oakland Center responded that it was a B767.  We were surprised that we got the wake off them with 10 miles separation but it was minor.  Winds were light aloft.  After we got on to Approach Control frequency and were still descending via the arrival we received a little more that was stronger and I had the First Officer seat the flight attendants over the PA.Just as we were in the LNAV turn over BAYST to JUUSE at approximately 10,000 feet we encountered moderate wake turbulence from the B767 that was still 9 miles ahead of us and we encountered significant roll rates with the wake turbulence and banked about 25 degrees Left.  I was holding the controls to stabilize the aircraft and the autopilot kicked into Control Wheel Steering Pitch and Roll because I was countering the significant left roll.  The First Officer was still off seating the flight attendants so I told Approach Control we were encountering wake turbulence and we needed an immediate turn away from the B767.When countering the wake roll we ended up slightly south of the magenta line, maybe .1 or .2 Actual Navigation Performance due to arrival turn when the wake was encountered so I manually flew the aircraft back on the LNAV track.  ATC then gave us a 070 heading and to maintain 9,000 feet while in the process of flying the magenta line.  We were vectored north of the B767 and then we preformed the RNAV Y 24R and the B767 was sent to the south Runway 25L.  We encountered no further wake turbulence.  We checked on the status of the flight attendants and they were OK and they did their final checks from their jumpseats.  Upon arrival at the gate, I personally checked with all the flight attendants to explain what happened and to verify one more time that they were OK, which they were.	Reporter stated he was surprised at the strength of the rolling motion when they had 9 miles separation.			B737NG Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on descent into LAX in trail of a B767.	
1829597	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC	Wind					Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777-200		2	Part 121	IFR			Takeoff / Launch																										Nosewheel Steering		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1829597												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff	Aircraft	Aircraft	During initial takeoff roll, at takeoff thrust setting and less than 30 kts, aircraft began uncommanded hard left turn. Initiated slow speed reject, tried to counter with full right rudder and full right nose wheel steering. Tiller was binding, only 10 degrees of travel available as if fighting left tiller inputs. Continued manual braking to a full stop with the aircraft nose close to 45 degrees left of centerline with the aircraft fully blocking taxiway. We informed Tower of the rejected takeoff and that we required a tow back to the ramp. Suspected nose wheel steering malfunction				B777 Captain reported rejecting the takeoff and returning to the gate when the aircraft initiated a hard left turn early in the takeoff roll.	
1829988	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				39000					Dawn			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 180; Flight Crew Type 8000		Physiological - Other			1829988												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Human Factors	Human Factors	Halfway through our flight, our B-Flight Attendant asked to come into the flight deck and talk with us.  When she got to the flight deck she indicated that there was a very strong cleaning/bug spray smell in the aft galley.  And, our C-Flight Attendant was complaining of coughing and a hard time taking a deep breath because of the strong cleaning smell.  Our A-Flight Attendant didn't smell anything.  No passengers were indicating concern and, we didn't smell anything.  I informed the Flight Attendant (FA) if she was medically concerned we would patch through to medic so she could communicate her concern.  All the FAs indicated they were feeling OK and did not want to contact medic.  They just wanted someone to look at the aircraft in ZZZ2.  At that point I told her we would put the packs to high to help eliminate the strong cleaning odor.  We contacted Dispatch through ACARS to discuss the situation and see if there was an anti-microbial spray completed the night before.  Also, to inform Dispatch that they were not comfortable taking the aircraft on our next leg.  Dispatch wanted a patch through ARINC. We agreed on a patch to medic. During their discussion, Dispatch sent us an ACARS to divert to ZZZ1.  The First Officer (FO) notified ATC of our intention to divert. I notified the FAs and passengers of our intent, as well. As a precaution we accomplished the Smoke, Fire, or Fumes QRC and referenced QRH Smoke, Fire or Fumes Checklist in case the situation escalated.  Dispatch notified us the aircraft had been sprayed the night before. We communicated with our A-Flight Attendant on what to expect on landing, taxi, and at the gate.We kept in constant communication with the A-FA to constantly reassess our situation.  On our descent into ZZZ1, medic recommended the two Flight Attendants in question use the portable oxygen.  With that information I notified ATC.  We then accomplished all appropriate checklists and landed uneventfully.				B737 Captain reported a fumes event during cruise resulting in health issues for some flight attendants and an eventual diversion.	
1830049	202108	1801-2400	ZLA.ARTCC	CA				34000		VMC						Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZLA																												Center ZLA	Air Carrier	B787 Dreamliner Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZLA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 91; Flight Crew Type 393					1830049												Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	Upset/wake turbulence event. Approximately five minutes prior to this event First Officer and I had completed our arrival brief and I had given a PA to the passengers that the seat belt sign would be coming on in the next 10 minutes. Ahead of us was a B787 at [our altitude], FL360. [The B787] was directed by ATC to descend and maintain FL340. Within a few minutes we were assigned the same clearance. Shortly after leveling at FL340, our aircraft entered a rapid, shuddering turbulence, followed by a rapid roll to the right. We both immediately put our hands on the controls and recited the upset recovery call outs.  We disconnected the autopilot, though the autopilot may have disconnected by itself, and autothrottle. We both fly with our FPV (Flight Path Vector) up so we could see immediately, intuitively that we weren't stalled, but our push was slight, not even half a G, which added to our effective counter roll rate. Prior to our response to the upset, the autopilot had already put in aileron control forces counter to the roll, but the aircraft was still rolling to the right. As we took control, the aircraft reached approximately 40 degrees before the aircraft responded to our control inputs, then rolled left to approximately 20 degrees. Based on the fact we were at FL340 we left the thrust where it was positioned, possibly a slight reduction, as the energy state was good and a large thrust change was not necessary. Our altitude loss was minimal and the descent was very shallow. The recovery point at which we managed our flight path divergence started at approximately FL337, only a 300 FT loss.  Rudder may have been used - Indicated by both an overshoot, subsequent bank to the left and a report from the flight attendants that we not only rolled, but yawed. We advised ATC that we had encountered wake turbulence which had caused our descent. They offered us direct HLYWD moving us from the wake of the preceding aircraft. We then checked on the Flight Attendants and passengers to ensure there were no injuries. The seat belt sign had been off and there was a passenger in the lav, but no injuries were reported. The First Officer and I debriefed the event but, because ATC started our descent onto the Arrival, I failed to make a PA to the passengers.  After landing, I waited at the flight deck door to thank the passengers. I saw that the event was more impactful than I had realized and a PA would have gone a long way to reassure the passengers and Flight Attendants.	Reporter stated he was surprised at the strength of the wake when he was 8 to 9 miles in trail of the B787.			B737-800 Captain reported an upset at FL340 descending into LAX in trail of a B787.	
1830416	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Center ZZZ1	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise; Parked		Class A ZZZ1																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1830416												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	Just prior to pushing, I was handed a Notice to Crew (NOTAC) for aircraft batteries in compartment D. I signed one and kept it, signed the other and returned it. I didn't look at it closely and didn't realize until cruise that the release was supposed to note it as well.				B737-800 Captain reported aircraft departure with flight release missing required 'Hazmat cargo' amendment note.	
1830724	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				5000		VMC	Turbulence; Rain; Thunderstorm		Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 85; Flight Crew Total 3687; Flight Crew Type 3687		Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1830724	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 81; Flight Crew Total 3888; Flight Crew Type 140		Communication Breakdown	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1830733	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	Flight was deviating off the ZZZZZ arrival due to thunderstorm activity. Subsequently cleared direct ZZZZZ and told to descend to 5,000 feet. While it is possible both the Captain and FO (First Officer) heard the wrong altitude, the FO read back 5,000 feet. Even though the published altitude is 6,000 feet, this did not seem unusual to us as we were flying below storm overhangs and had barely flown any of the arrival up to this point.  After reaching 5,000, the controller asked why we were at 5,000. We informed him that was our cleared altitude. He informed us 6,000 was the minimum vectoring altitude.		On arriving into ZZZ, we were taken off the ZZZZZ4 arrival due to thunderstorms in the terminal area. Upon contact with Terminal Radar Control we were issued radar vectors. The Air Traffic Controller stated that we would rejoin the arrival at ZZZZZ. We were told to fly heading 090 and cleared to descend from 9,000 to 6,000. The clearance was read back and confirmed by both of us. Shortly thereafter the controller gave us a further descent down to 5,000. The clearance was read back and confirmed by both of us. After the clearance was read back, ATC never intervened on the altitude that we read back. ATC then told us to fly direct to ZZZZZ. Whilst we were level at ZZZZZ at 5,000, ATC stated that [the next fix] had an altitude of 6,000 ft. I stated that we were cleared by him down to 5,000. There were no traffic conflicts.		Flight crew reported descending 1000 feet below minimum altitude on approach as cleared by ATC.	
1831010	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.TRACON	US				8100		VMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	Localizer/Glideslope/ILS ZZZ	Final Approach		Class D ZZZ																								Autopilot		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 58.88; Flight Crew Total 288.58; Flight Crew Type 288.58		Confusion; Situational Awareness			1831010	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 45.27; Flight Crew Total 15000; Flight Crew Type 10000		Situational Awareness			1831036	ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Ground Event / Encounter Ground Equipment Issue; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Person Air Traffic Control	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Aircraft; ATC Equipment / Nav Facility / Buildings	Ambiguous	During approach into ZZZ while receiving vectors was cleared to 9,500 feet MSL and told cleared for the ILS XX and to maintain 9,500 feet until established. Selected APP and aircraft captured GS and LOC and was descending on the app with GS and LOC locked on. Got a call from approach at 8,100 feet letting us know we were low and to climb up to 8,600 feet I immediately disconnected the auto pilot and initiated a climb while maintaining LOC. After crossing ZZZZZ I then recommenced my descent and flew visually using the PAPI as my guidance. Landed the aircraft within the touch down range and stopped on the runway. CA taxied aircraft to gate. During taxi we were informed by approach that the GS had issues and they thought it had been fixed. We initially intercepted the GS and LOC approximately 20 miles out. At approximately 2 miles out the GS was back in line with the PAPI.		We were doing the ILS Z Runway XX into ZZZ. Night time, good visibility, no clouds. ATC had us at 9,500 feet, turned us to intercept final outside of ZZZZZ and cleared us for the approach. Autopilot was on. We were flaps 1 about 190 knots at the time and about 22 miles on final. We intercepted the localizer and the glide slope at about the same time. At which point we were busy setting touch down zone, putting flaps down and gear. A moment later ATC gives us an altitude low warning, climb immediately to 8,600 feet. The flying pilot ( first officer) immediately disconnected auto pilot and initiated climb. We were around 8,100 feet, on the glide slope, auto pilot on when this was initiated from what we could tell. We had just passed ZZZZZ (FAF, but 17 miles out). The controller mentioned they had some glide slope problems just a week or two prior, and asked us if we were ok to proceed visually. Still being over 10 miles out, runway and PAPI in site, and glide slope still showing slightly below us, we proceeded visually with reference to PAPI and GS. The problem may have been from the auto pilot intercepting the LOC and GS at the same time and possibly overshooting the GS as it sometimes does when far out and possibly exasperated by configuration changes. This all happened about 15 to 20 miles from touchdown. Or it could have been from a faulty GS signal that they have had problems with previously.		Flight crew reported descending below minimum altitude on approach and climbed back to correct altitude. ATC advised flight crew that there had previously been problems with the glide slope.	
1831256	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US									Dawn			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121		Ferry / Re-Positioning		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Type 14000		Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1831256												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport	Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	Air return due to pirep telling us we had a 6 foot flame coming out of engine 2 on takeoff.Mid-way down the runway for takeoff, a regional aircraft 'other carrier flagged', stated 'using the afterburners, huh?' Since we were empty (no Passengers, and not much fuel), we were accelerating quickly, and thus his comments made sense. Upon rotation, the Tower asked the regional aircraft, 'what was that you said'. To which he responded, 'looks like (Company) is using afterburners, a six-foot flame was coming out of the back of the #2 engine'. Upon reaching clean up altitude, we ran all the appropriate checklists, and returned back to ZZZ. The fire trucks were called by ATC, and they performed an inspection upon taxiing clear of the runway. We were cleared to taxi to the gate, and the event was entered in the logbook, and Maintenance and Dispatch, and the Chief Pilots were notified.The regional aircraft could have been more clear in his comments, and we could have aborted the takeoff at low speed.				Air carrier flight crew reported another carrier flight crew commented about flames exiting engine tailpipe during takeoff roll. Flight crew returned to departure airport.	
1831286	202108				090	40		39000					Dawn				Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 180; Flight Crew Type 4500		Workload			1831286												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Human Factors	Human Factors	Halfway through our flight our B-Flight Attendant asked to come into the flight deck and talk with us. When she got to the flight deck she indicated that there was a very strong cleaning/bug spray smell in the aft galley. Additionally, our C-Flight Attendant was complaining of coughing and having a hard time taking a deep breath because of the strong cleaning smell. Our A-Flight Attendant didn't smell anything. No Passengers were indicating concern and we didn't smell anything. The Captain asked the Flight Attendant (F/A) if she was medically concerned, and if so, we would patch through to medic so she could communicate her concern. All the F/As indicated they were feeling OK and did not want to contact medic. They just wanted someone to look at the aircraft in ZZZ. At that point the Captain told her we would put the packs to high to help eliminate the strong cleaning odor. We also contacted Dispatch through ACARS to discuss the situation and see if there was an anti-microbial spray completed the night before. Additionally, we informed Dispatch that the F/As were not comfortable taking the aircraft on our next leg. Dispatch wanted a patch through ARINC to get F/As, operations and medic on the line for a conference call.  During their discussion, Dispatch sent us an ACARS message to divert to ZZZ1. As a result, we notified ATC of our intention to divert. The Captain notified the F/As and Passengers of our intent, as well.As a precaution we accomplished the Smoke, Fire, or Fumes QRC and referenced QRH Smoke, Fire or Fumes Checklist in case the situation escalated. Dispatch notified us the aircraft had been sprayed the night before with antimicrobial spray. We communicated with our A-Flight Attendant on what to expect on landing, taxi, and at the gate. We kept in constant communication with the A F/A to constantly reassess our situation. On our descent into ZZZ1 medic recommended the two Flight Attendants having issues breathing use the portable oxygen. With that information, we [advised] ATC to have Medical Personnel standing by. We then accomplished all appropriate checklists and landed uneventfully.The anti-microbial spray the night before was a bit much. That needs to be more regulated. I think the call to divert was a good call, but happened very fast. Our Crew day also ended up being super long with this diversion on our first leg. We were OK to continue after the diversion, but that kind of day, with what we went through, is probably not completely safe.				B737-700 First Officer reported Flight Attendants were experiencing physiological symptoms from 'very strong ' cabin fumes. After consultation with Dispatch it was determined to divert for maintenance action.	
1831345	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0			VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class C ZZZ																								Pilot Seat		X	Improperly Operated; Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Human-Machine Interface			1831345												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew		Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	During takeoff roll, FO (First Officer) seat became unlatched horizontally, causing FO to slide aft. As pilot monitoring, I assumed control of the aircraft and steered us back to runway center line until FO was ready to assume control again. Seat not properly locked horizontally. Latching mechanism possibly in need of adjustment. Double-checking seat lock with a good 'shove' prior to departure. Also closer maintenance inspection of seat latches may be warranted.				Captain reported assuming control after First Officer's seat slid back and prevented him from reaching the flight controls.	
1831349	202107	1201-1800	ZZZ.Tower	US						VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb; Initial Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Horizontal Stabilizer Trim		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface; Workload			1831349	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface			1831348	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Aircraft; Procedure	Procedure	Immediately after takeoff, the horizontal trim failed. Neither pilot could activate the electric trim. The manual trim wheels were used to keep the aircraft in a trimmed condition, though with a significant amount of difficulty possibly due to the trim motor clutching against the manual inputs. The Stabilizer Trim Inoperative QRH procedures were followed.  [We advised ATC] and the flight returned to ZZZ for an uneventful flaps 15 landing.I don't know why the electric horizontal trim failed. I do know that the manual trim was significantly more difficult to operate during this event than it had been in previous instances where I've had to use the manual trim wheels. The aircraft was never too far out of trimmed condition, but a strong binding was felt as we rotated the trim wheels. It was suggested to me after the event that this binding sensation was in fact the trim motor 'clutching' against the manual inputs.  I'm not a strongman, but I worry that two smaller, weaker pilots might have found themselves in extremis.Figure out why the manual trim wheels felt as if they were binding, and correct that design condition.		Everything that occurred prior to the time we became aware of the Stab trim failure was normal and uneventful. Shortly after Takeoff, the Captain said his stab trim didn't seem to be working and asked me to check mine.  My stab trim was also inoperative. There were no other secondary issues. We continued with the departure while reviewing/going through the Stabilizer Trim Inoperative procedure. Once we had gone through the QRH procedure, we [advised ATC] and returned uneventfully to ZZZ where we wrote up the problem. We were given a new airplane about an hour later and continued our trip to ZZZ1. Cause, unknown maintenance issue.		Flight crew reported horizontal trim became inoperative with binding when attempting to use manual trim wheels. Flight crew elected to perform an air turn back.	
1831420	202108	0601-1200	D01.TRACON	CO				12250		VMC						TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B DEN																												TRACON D01	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Climb		Class B DEN																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 17; Flight Crew Type 3302		Situational Awareness			1831420	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 55; Flight Crew Type 204		Situational Awareness			1831416	Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	We were climbing out of DEN on the EEONS 8. Crossed HIDEF at 10,000 feet and started the climb again. We started to pick up wake turbulence from the 777 in front of us through about 11,000 feet. We received a call from ATC for traffic northeast of us as we were coming through 11,500 feet. We started looking for the traffic and when I looked back inside we were going through 12,000 feet about 1-2 miles prior to SHOBO. We began correcting back to 12,000 feet. Max altitude was about 12,300 feet. No call or mention of the deviation from ATC.		While climbing out on flight from DEN via EEONS8 departure from 34L, we were given a traffic call from ATC as we neared SHOBO. At the same time, we encountered wake turbulence from a heavy aircraft on the same departure. As Pilot Monitoring, I was looking north for traffic as the aircraft was responding to the turbulence and controlling the aircraft. Simultaneously, we neared SHOBO, which has a no higher than 12,000 feet restriction. PIC called altitude at approx 250 feet above the restriction, and smoothly leveled, then began descent to maintain 12,000 feet until passing SHOBO, then resumed climb. No ATC comm was received during this time.		B737NG flight crew reported a distraction from wake turbulence contributed to climbing through an altitude restriction departing DEN in trail of a B777.	
1831916	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Tower	US				3000		VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Trailing Edge Flap		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 122.22; Flight Crew Total 1640.65; Flight Crew Type 1640.65		Workload; Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1831916	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 52.1; Flight Crew Total 399.03; Flight Crew Type 101.95		Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Workload; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1831886	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Crew departed late due to brake accumulator issues on previous aircraft, and a potential one on this aircraft.Ground Ops were completely normal and the FO (First Officer) had no problems deploying the flaps to 3 for takeoff.Takeoff was normal and as we were turning to an assigned heading, we reached the cleanup altitude. I called for flaps 1 and noticed that the FO had some what I felt was minor difficulty bringing the flaps up to 1. This is not unusual, a hand slips, or the lever used to move the flap handle out of the detects isn't gripped correctly for easy retraction. I thought little of it.When I called for flaps UP, the raising was again delayed, and I noticed my FO reaching around with his right hand to put both hands on the flap lever to get it up. We talked about him having trouble getting them up, and I flew the aircraft only to ensure that I did not over speed the flaps. Eventually he was able to get them up which I saw on the speed tape on the PFD and I verified on the display, and flap handle position.We discussed what had happened a bit but decided to focus on safely exiting the terminal area and getting to altitude since the aircraft was now in the safe, and proper configuration. We made a plan to contact Maintenance Control at cruise and notify them of the problem and get advice about continuing the flight.After reaching altitude, the FO told me what he had encountered and explained how difficult it was to move the lever that released the flap handle from the detent to raise the flaps.A phone patch was initiated with dispatch and Maintenance Control and we discovered that our radio 2 wasn't able to receive over long distances. We ended up substituting radio 3 for radio 2 and temporarily disabling ACARS to complete the phone patch. Maintenance Control was very helpful on the phone patch and felt like he knew what the problem was. Maintenance Control was asked if he felt the aircraft should continue to ZZZZ, and he wanted it to go to a maintenance hub. As a Captain, I agreed with that thought process and asked the Dispatcher to bring in the Ops Manager so that we could all agree on a plan of action. We could have landed the aircraft at ZZZZ if there had been a flap malfunction, but the aircraft would have been stuck there quite a while.Eventually after consideration of landing lengths, the close to overweight landing weight of the aircraft, and the potential for wet runways in ZZZ1, that the best and safest place to divert the aircraft would be ZZZ1 in case the flaps would not deploy normally. Consideration was based on the worst case, flaps up scenario.Destination was changed with ATC once the plan was agreed upon by all parties and the phone patch was terminated and ACARS restored.Maintenance Control was asked via ACARS if we should leave the flaps down after landing and they asked them to retract them as much as we could.Fortunately, the landing in ZZZ1 was uneventful. The flaps deployed normally. Upon taxi-in. The flaps were retraced to position 1, where the handle binding began to be very difficult to overcome.Upon arrival at the gate, after shutdown, I tried to move the lever that releases the flap handle from the detent and found that it was impossible to move with just one hand.		We were doing a flaps 3 takeoff from [Runway] XXL. After the acceleration altitude, and above flap retraction speed, the pilot flying called for flaps to 1. I noticed, while moving the lever from 3 to 1, that the flap lever was nowhere near as smooth and free moving as it usually is, rather, it was quite difficult to move. As we accelerated through flap 1 retraction speed, it took considerable effort to get the lever out of the detent. I mentioned it to the captain who quickly made sure to raise the nose and not over speed the flaps as I used both hands to get the flap lever compressed and moved to the up position. The flaps moved as expected, with no issues, the flight control page showed normal, and the hydraulic page showed normal. We continued the climb out of ZZZ, and when reaching a safe altitude, I took the controls and radios with ATC, while the captain initiated an incredibly detailed conversation with dispatch and Maintenance Control. After exhausting every bit of information we could give, and every insight Maintenance Control had, the decision was made with dispatch and everyone in planning that they were talking to, to divert to ZZZ1 instead of potentially having a flight control issue on landing in ZZZZ, on a shorter runway, at an international station, and then leaving the plane there. We didn't [request priority] but asked approach to slow down early to get the flaps out and make sure they extended. We had planned every contingency for having to go around and then [request priority] if they didn't, and were ready to run whichever QRH abnormal procedure would have applied based on where the flaps ended up. On the approach, the flaps came down just fine and a normal landing was made. After landing, we planned, with maintenance approval, to attempt to retract the flaps but to stop if they locked again and not force anything. The lever was much less stiff from flaps full to flaps 1 than earlier, but again locked at the detent there. We left the flaps at 1 into the gate, let ramp know to tell anyone they could, and had maintenance meet us there. Sounded like they were going to replace the entire flap lever section.		Flight crew reported difficulty in raising the trailing edge flaps and elected to divert instead of risking a grounded aircraft at an outstation.	
1832035	202108	1201-1800	ZZZZ.Tower	FO						VMC			Daylight			Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Other Go Around	Vectors																													Tower ZZZZ	Air Carrier	Medium Transport, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach	Vectors																									Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1832035												ATC Issue All Types; Conflict NMAC; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Aircraft TA; Automation Aircraft RA; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Separated Traffic; Flight Crew Became Reoriented; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Airport; Procedure; Human Factors	Human Factors	This event was a near midair collision in ZZZZ. On Aircraft X ZZZ to ZZZZ. Weather at ZZZZ was not a factor even though there was a significant storm cell over the final approach fix to Runway XX. ZZZZ was reporting 10 miles visibility. While making approach into ZZZZ we were told to go around due to previous aircraft reporting a pothole with gravel on approach end to Runway XX. Tower immediately closed the runway. We then went around and did published missed procedure. We entered holding at 8,000 feet at ZZZZZ per instructions. After we exited holding (now at 6,000 feet) the controller gave us instructions to go direct to ZZZZZ1 fix and begin RNP approach into Runway YY. We loaded the approach to the opposite Runway YY since the pothole was on the approach end of the Runway XX and proceeded to ZZZZZ1 on about a 200 heading. Then unexpectedly, the controller gave us a vector of 090. In hind sight, we think this vector was meant for another aircraft. The new heading of 090 ended up sending us directly for another aircraft at same altitude. TCAS began with a TA then an RA. At same time controller frantically gave us a new vector. After properly executing the resolution advisory, we narrowly missed the conflict aircraft and proceeded back to ZZZZZ1 and did the RNP to Runway YY for normal landing. We never saw the conflict aircraft however they did have us in sight, and I don't recall who the conflict aircraft was. There were two jets in holding. We think the controller got our call sign confused with someone else for the 090 vector. English as a second language may have been a factor in this event. Both the F/O (First Officer) and myself remember hearing our call sign in the 090 vector. In the end it was TCAS that saved the day.				B737-800 Captain reported a near midair collision while executing a go-around and took evasive action at a foreign airport.	
1832490	202108		ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Squat Switch		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 34.42; Flight Crew Total 5732.72; Flight Crew Type 5732.72		Troubleshooting			1832490												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	As weight left the wheels, my primary and secondary nav, heading, DG, compass failed. Along with the failures I had a PWS (Predictive Wind Shear) fail message and a TCAS fail message. My flight director failed as well. As we continued to climb, FO flying the aircraft, I began to troubleshoot the problems. I ran 2 checklist as they did not correct the issues.  I opted to return to ZZZ. At that time I requested priority handling. I continued to communicate with company, FAs and passengers. The FO flew the ILS to [Runway] XXR. We landed and taxi to the terminal.				B737 NG Captain reported loss of navigation systems and instruments on lift off. Captain elected to return to the departure airport.	
1832706	202108	0001-0600	ZZZ.ARTCC	US									Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Dispatch		1832706												Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Hazardous Material Violation		N	Person Dispatch	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	While at cruise en route to ZZZ, we received a TO Data Available message via ACARS. Shortly after, Dispatch notified us that the HAZMAT we had onboard (we had proper paperwork for the HAZMAT) was not included in the Zero Fuel Weight (ZFW) calculations. We reviewed the new Takeoff Data for the correct ZFW and made the adjustment to the FMC, and calculated new landing data for that weight. The rest of the flight proceeded as normal.				B737-700 Captain reported Dispatch notification during cruise stating Hazmat cargo weight not included in the aircraft Weight & Balance data. Weight & Balance data corrected and flight continued.	
1833041	202108		ZZZ.Airport	US			500									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Landing Gear Indicating System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 138; Flight Crew Total 5300; Flight Crew Type 201		Distraction; Troubleshooting; Workload			1833041	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 59; Flight Crew Total 2205; Flight Crew Type 2205		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting; Workload; Distraction			1833050	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	I was First Officer (FO) on Aircraft X operating to ZZZ. We were conducting the ILS XXR circle to XY approach when we received an abnormal landing gear indication. When I lowered the landing gear, the annunciator panel showed 3 red lights along with 3 green lights. The overhead panel showed 3 green as well. We briefly discussed continuing the landing as there was some confidence the gear was down. The discussion continued until around 500 ft. and I felt strongly that we shouldn't be making such consequential decisions that close to the ground without running a checklist. We performed a go-around, ran the checklist, got the gear down without the red lights, and landed successfully.		ILS XXR circle XY at ZZZ. Lowered gear just prior to FAF (Final Approach Fix). 3 red, 3 green illuminated. After evaluating with First Officer (FO), executed go-around on XY due to gear disagree. Standard missed. Ran checklists. Vectored for longest runway... ILS XXR. Extended gear on 10-mile base. All indications normal. Executed normal landing. Taxi to gate to find puddle [of] hydraulic fluid at left main. Possibly unrelated.		B737 flight crew reported an issue with the landing gear indications.	
1833173	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Ground ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Cruise; Taxi																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 2; Flight Crew Total 519; Flight Crew Type 519		Distraction; Troubleshooting			1833173	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 120; Flight Crew Total 3102; Flight Crew Type 3102					1833190	Conflict Ground Conflict, Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Illness / Injury; Ground Event / Encounter Other / Unknown			Person Flight Crew	Taxi	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors; Procedure	Human Factors	After pushback from Gate XXX and given our taxi instructions 'XX-XY-XZ etc...' to XXL for departure, we commenced our single-engine taxi. While I was taxiing at a slow speed, taxi light on, I noticed a truck that appeared to be stopping on the roadway across Taxiway X between XZ and XY to give way to our aircraft. However, the truck did not stop and they rolled right through Taxiway X without giving way to our aircraft. I applied brake pressure to prevent the plane from hitting the truck. Due to the slow taxi speed, we did not think the braking action to prevent the collision was too firm to knock anyone down or warrant a drastic brace from anyone if they were up. We notified ATC about this taxiway incursion and they asked if there was an Operations car on frequency to go after the truck. We took off, were in cruise, and were past the CP (Critical Point) - [middle of the flight] - when we set up for the first bathroom break. I went out first and I asked [the] First Class Flight Attendant (FA), 'If everything is all right back here.' They responded, 'Yes.' I went to the bathroom, came back up to the cockpit, and was talking to the Purser who also said everything was 'fine' as well. When the First Officer (FO) came back he then informed me that [the] FA informed him that when I had to brake to not hit the truck, she hit her right side against the forward galley wall with some of the quarter turn edging. I then called back and asked if she needed medical help in ZZZ1. She replied that she was "icing it and took Tylenol and she would not need medical attention in ZZZ1." I asked the other FAs if they were all right; one stated that they didn't even feel the braking action and two others stated they felt it as they were standing but everyone else was fine. To be clear, the first time I was told something was wrong was past the CP. Before departure I briefed the FAs that if there is anything wrong or abnormal, even if we are in sterile cockpit, do not hesitate to call up and let us know. Also, during the flight, no calls to see if [there were] medical personnel on board to help with an injury were made by the FA crew.Prior to the top of descent when we set up for another bathroom break, the Purser came in and I asked him how [the] FA was doing. He said, 'She seems fine, she wants to go home, she's just a little sore.' When I went out, I asked how she was doing. She stated, 'She wanted to call in enroute injured, but then wanted to deadhead home.' I told her, 'I don't know that they will be able to deadhead her home, but if she is enroute injured please let me know and I will contact Dispatch.' She then said she was 'enroute injured.' I said, 'Do you want medical treatment in ZZZ1.' She said, 'No.'I then texted Dispatch that we had an FA who was enroute injured and would call them when we landed per the reporting requirements. I also requested priority handling and asked the paramedics to meet the aircraft to evaluate her, just to be safe. We landed without incident. When the paramedics came on board, [the] FA refused additional medical care from them and stated to the FO, 'Why did you call them out, you didn't need to do that.' Appropriate reports were made per the manual to the Dispatcher [and others].		During our flight from ZZZ to ZZZ1, most of which was uneventful, we had a minor injury on taxi out of our galley Flight Attendant (FA), but we were not notified until after the CP (Critical Point), [middle of the flight]. On taxi out we had to stop taxi for a truck that did not stop as we were taxiing X XY XZ from Gate XXX. As we came to a stop at the XX XY intersection, we at the time were more concerned about the truck that did not stop, and believed that we did not stop too abruptly for the truck but just more startled that it did not stop. Hindsight, we did not hear any clunking or hitting - and although we stopped slightly faster than normal, we did not realize that [the] FA may have been knocked off balance a little bit. We did make a call to Ground to let them know that the truck did not stop but we quickly continued our taxi not thinking too much of the incident. While the initial incident where [the] FA hit her arm may not have been that bad, it sounded like it got worse as we flew with some aching and building pain, so she let the flight crew, us, know about her sore arm and sore neck.As the flight continued, we reached radio/ACARS reception into ZZZ1 when [the] FA decided that the pain was too much and she was in flight injured and would not be able to work the return flight leg from ZZZ1 to ZZZ. As I continued to fly, the Captain (CA) coordinated with Dispatch and attempted to call ZZZ1 Operations to let them know the situation. After the CA was given all the information and was notified that she was incapacitated and unable to work the return flight, the decision was made to request priority handling [and] have paramedics meet us at the gate when we arrived to check on [the] FA.At top of descent the decision was made by [the] FA that her arm was hurt too much and after coordination with [the] FA, Dispatch, and calling Operations, we were approximately over ZZZ2 and the CA decided to request priority handling as a precaution due to an incapacitated crew member and in the best interest of the crew member's health. Our flight landed and taxied uneventfully and [the] FA [was] checked out by the airport paramedics. As we left the flight, we wished FA a speedy recovery; she seemed in good spirits.		B737 flight crew reported that a Flight Attendant sustained a minor injury due a braking event on the runway to avoid a truck not giving way to the taxiing aircraft.	
1833323	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US			0									Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																										Air Conditioning Distribution Ducting, Clamps, Connectors		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Other / Unknown			1833323	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1833744	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	Other Takoff roll	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Gate; Flight Crew Rejected Takeoff; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Human Factors	Human Factors	Captain (CA) & First Officer (FO) commented on a dirty sock smell on taxi out, no other abnormal indications. Upon adding takeoff power, left side of cockpit filled with a smokey/steamy mixture and acrid smell. Captain rejected. Maintenance (MX) advised there was an engine wash performed night prior and the aircraft engines were not run up properly after the procedure. We ended up having MX onboard for the runup who had us perform duties from a checklist that should have followed this engine wash including a run up of at least 5 min at 70% N1. The odors were still present during this time. If MX had been performed accurately the night prior, this could have been avoided. And, unfortunately, this routine wash is performed under a 'work card' making it invisible to pilots during a preflight in any MX accounting systems or via the aircraft logbook.		At approximately 90 KIAS, smoke came out of cockpit floor, under Captain's side, and rejected takeoff. Uneventful. Cleared off runway stopped and had emergency equipment check for fire. All clear and taxied to gate. Maintenance said engine wash were performed overnight, probable cause of smoke/steam on takeoff roll. Maintenance did a high power engine run, delayed flight and re dispatched after maintenance cleared aircraft. Maintenance crew did an engine run, at gate, versus high power one the night prior.  According to mechanic, a duct clamp was installed improperly in the E&E bay; residual water and duct clamp coming off on ducting might have contributed to what appeared to be smoke/fumes/vapor on takeoff roll after takeoff power was applied. [Reporter recommends Maintenance] perform high power engine run after every engine wash. If non-high power engine run, performed by maintenance overnight (info only entry to AML should be recorded so as to keep flight crews informed).		B737-800 flight crew reported smoke and fumes emanating from left side of the cockpit during takeoff roll. Flight crew executed a rejected takeoff and maintenance was requested.	
1834396	202108	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Climb	Direct	Class B ZZZ																								Navigational Equipment and Processing		X																																					Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 89; Flight Crew Type 3528		Distraction; Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Confusion; Workload			1834396	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 53; Flight Crew Type 1131		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Workload; Distraction; Confusion			1834427	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Overrode Automation; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing through approximately 500 ft. AGL in a right turn to 360, the departure heading selected on the MCP by the FO (First Officer) prior to takeoff, the FO's flight director, FD2, failed; the MCP (Mode Control Panel) altitude window indicated 50,000, not the pre-takeoff selected altitude of 3,000, and the FO's command bars biased out of view. The PF continued the climbing turn using PFD (Primary Flight Display) raw data in night-time VMC conditions. Cross-checking respective PFDs revealed no flags or indications as to the specific failure.In the busy initial climb segment, confusion as to the cause of current flight instrument condition gave way to confidence in the raw data. Directing the PF to continue to climb using raw data, I attempted to select 3000 ft. on the MCP; the altitude knob spun freely without any effect on the window. I then turned off the FD1 and FD2, reselected 3000 ft. in the MCP altitude window, confirmed the heading bug was at 360, selected heading, and turned the FD switches back on, FD2 first, in an attempt to restore FD2 guidance for the PF. Immediately upon switching on FD2, the MCP altitude returned to 50,000 and no command bars appeared on the FO's PFD.Switching to Departure Control, ATC gave us direct routing to the first fix, ZZZZZ, and continue climb to 11,000. I asked ATC for a heading, mentioning our concern that our navigation might be unreliable. They provided a 010 heading which when selected, had no effect on the FO's heading bug. Changing heading on the MCP failed to change the bugs on the FO's ND (Navigation Display) and PFD compass displays. I confirmed compass heading on the wet compass corresponded with both the Captain's and FO's compasses. They asked if we needed to declare an emergency; I declined as we had good raw data and were still diagnosing the malfunction.I attempted a second reset of FD2 with the same negative result. Leveling at 11,000, the PF reported the autothrottles were not holding commanded airspeed; we disengaged the autothrottles. Turning both FDs off again and selecting FD1 on provided accurate command bars on the Captain's PFD. We agreed that continuing using raw data was possible, and that full FD capability was available from FD1. I transferred aircraft control to myself and agreed with my FO's suggestion of going to the QRH for additional troubleshooting/procedural steps. A satisfactory QRH procedure to handle this malfunction was difficult to find and both FMS1 on the Captain's ND and FMS2 on the FO's confirmed FMC operation. We asked ATC for direct down range and confirmed FMS accuracy using FD1 guidance of MCDU1 entered direct routing. I engaged the autopilot and autothrottles and we continued on course using LNAV/VNAV.We discussed the status of the aircraft and the conditions at our destination, ZZZ. We elected to continue to ZZZ where we completed the trip without incident. I submitted an electronic logbook entry and called Maintenance Control after arrival. Approximately 35 minutes into our hour-long flight, I realized we had not requested [the] planned climb above our initial cruise altitude, FL310, and that contingency fuel had been burned during a slower climb than normal. Burn, alternate, ZZZ1, and 45-minute FAA fuel was still available, though a missed approach would have compelled diversion to ZZZ1. The FD2 diagnosis and distraction lead to the initial loss of SA regarding erosion to our fuel state. Beginning descent and approach planning brought for our new fuel and prepared us for contingencies. Communication throughout was key to understanding the status of the aircraft and mitigated the initial startle and confusion at such a low altitude early flight stage.		We were cleared for takeoff by ZZZ3 Tower with instructions to turn to heading 360 and maintain 3,000 ft. The pilot flying which was me, the First Officer, put 360 in the MCP and deselected LNAV. We took off and as we were climbing through about 500 ft., the command bars disappeared and altitude went to 50,000 on both the PFD and MCP. We leveled at 3,000 on heading 360. ATC gave us climb to 11,000 and direct to a fix. The Captain told him we needed a heading we were having trouble with the flight director. ATC gave us a head of 010 and climb to 11,000 ft.The heading bug on the First Officer's PFD and MFD was stuck on heading 360 but was 010 on the MCP and on the Captain's displays. The Captain checked the PFD heading with the compass and it matched. We had no flags on the PFD or MFD. The Captain turned off both flight directors and then turned them both back on with the First Officer's being the master. When he turned it back on, the altitude went back to 50,000 and we could not change it. The command bars never came back. We saw that the Captain had a flight director and that his heading bug would turn with the MCP. The Captain then turned the FDs off and put his FD as the master. When he did that, the altitude went back to normal. On the FO's (First Officer) displays there was still no FD and the heading bug was stuck on 360 but now the altitude was correct. We continued the flight to ZZZ with the Captain flying.		B737 flight crew reported that shortly after takeoff, the First Officer's flight director began malfunctioning, leading to the MCP and heading bug also being inoperative.	
1834405	202108	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US				1500		VMC						TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Final Approach																										Air Conditioning and Pressurization Pack		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 108; Flight Crew Total 13531; Flight Crew Type 224		Distraction; Other / Unknown; Time Pressure			1834405	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Total 4251		Workload; Time Pressure; Other / Unknown			1834693	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew; Person Flight Attendant	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Outside the marker [Runway] XXR Captain received call from purser about chemical type smell moving forward from aft cabin. Noticed it on extreme short final but took measures to alleviate, if possible, the stated smell and monitored for reduction or increase.  The landing in gusty conditions required focus there. On landing APU selected to start on final was on line and directed APU bleed air to packs and selected recirculate [fans] off immediately causing cessation of the smell and acknowledged alleviation in command.  Smell was barely noticeable but a tad acrid then removed on taxi in.  Recirculate [fans] back on blew it out completely. Never a need nor concern for use of cockpit oxygen. No persons affected nor did anyone use O2.  No injuries. Event terminated at gate with a write up and briefing of mechanics plus filling out of required maintenance 4 page review by Captain.		On approach in ZZZ the Captain received a call from the lead Flight Attendant about an acrid chemical smell moving forward from the aft cabin.  By that time we could smell it in the cockpit as well.  We turned off the recirculate fans to stop the spread of the smell, which helped, but then focused on the landing.  We started the APU on short final and turned off the engine bleeds after exiting the runway, using the APU to power the packs.  After turning off the engine bleeds the smell dissipated and we turned the recirculate fans back on. No oxygen was used or needed by the crew or passengers.  We parked safely at the gate and briefed me and made a maintenance write up.		B777 flight crew reported a fumes event on final approach during landing. After executing a safe landing flight crew took measures to alleviate the fumes followed by requesting maintenance.	
1834648	202108	1801-2400	MIA.Airport	FL				5000					Night			TRACON MIA	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B MIA																												Tower MIA	Air Carrier	B747 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach		Class B MIA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 200; Flight Crew Type 16000					1834648	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 270					1834643	Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	We were seven miles behind a 747 on approach to Runway 9 in Miami, flaps 5, 170 knots on glidepath, when the aircraft abruptly went into 30 degrees left bank. We commanded full right aileron and there was no response for an unknown amount of time (possibly three seconds). We regained wings level flight only to encounter another abrupt 30 degree roll to the left with lack of aircraft control. After much discussion, my First Officer and I were worried that after such a 'minor incident' (seven miles behind a 747) that the event should cause such a severe loss of aircraft control. We both agree that the incident should be investigated.		We were 15 miles out on the localizer to Runway 9 at 5000 feet. We were seven miles in trail of a B747. We encountered wake turbulence and the aircraft yawed to the left 30+ degrees for 3-5 seconds. We inputted right aileron with no results. The aircraft returned to level flight on its own and immediately went into a 30+ degree left bank turn for 3-5 seconds.		B737 NG flight crew reported momentary loss of control during a wake turbulence encounter on approach to MIA in trail of a B747.	
1835561	202108	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				36000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Pressurization Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																					Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 75; Flight Crew Total 8870; Flight Crew Type 7380					1835561	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 76; Flight Crew Total 1151; Flight Crew Type 76					1835792	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	At FL390, pressurization in cabin started to increase. Started a descent and went through the checklist. Shortly afterwards the cabin went to 15,000 feet and O2 masks were deployed. Had requested priority handling with Dispatch and ATC. Alerted FAs (Flight Attendant) of the problem, what to expect. Landed in ZZZ1, intended destination. Debriefed crew, Maintenance, Dispatch, [and] union. Everyone said it was done by textbook procedures.		AT FL390 we were cleared to descend and passing through FL360 we noticed the cabin altitude increasing. We told ATC and initiated an immediate descent. Complied with ECAMs and QRH procedures. Briefed FAs (Flight Attendant) and safely landed aircraft. Debriefed with crew, Maintenance, Dispatch, passengers, and emergency medical support staff.		A320 flight crew reported loss of cabin pressure control and requested priority handling and proceeded to land at destination airport.	
1837027	202109	0601-1200	ZOA.ARTCC	CA		60		36400					Daylight			Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZOA																												Center ZOA	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Cruise		Class A ZOA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 100; Flight Crew Type 15000					1837027												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Environment - Non Weather Related	Departed SJC for ZZZ, encountered aircraft upset at FL 364 due to close proximity and below Boeing 777 at FL 370. No injuries or aircraft damage were apparent.	Reporter stated he was surprised at the intensity of the wake turbulence.			B737 Captain reported an upset climbing through FL364 after encountering wake turbulence from a B777 at FL370.	
1837579	202109	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US			0						Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800			Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch																																																																	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain							1837579												Ground Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence; Ground Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Weather	Weather	On takeoff roll, Runway XX, with gusty left crosswinds, the aircraft suddenly and without warning, 'skidded' left. Corrected the ground track and continued the takeoff without further incident.  FAs (Flight Attendants) reported being aggressively shoved sideways in their seats.				B737-800 Captain reported the aircraft suddenly skidded to the left during takeoff roll due to gusty crosswinds requiring corrective input.	
1838701	202108	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US						VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Fuel Quantity-Pressure Indication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1838701												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance; Inflight Event / Encounter Fuel Issue		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Inflight Shutdown; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Enroute from ZZZ to ZZZ1, shortly after leveling off at cruise altitude, we received an IMBAL (imbalance) message and saw that our right fuel tank had 1,000 lbs less than the left tank. We had just turned off our center fuel pumps a few minutes prior. We performed the IMBAL checklist which then led us to the Fuel Leak Engine checklist. By this point it was decided that the safest course of action was to return to ZZZ. We requested priority handling and were cleared direct to ZZZ and were given a descent clearance. We shut down the #2 engine per the Fuel Leak Engine checklist. We also performed the One Engine Inoperative Landing checklist, the Non-Routine Landing Considerations checklist, and the Overweight Landing checklist. I was the Pilot Flying until downwind, at which time the Captain took the controls and landed the aircraft. We stopped on the runway and had the firefighters check out the aircraft. Everything looked ok to them and we were cleared to taxi to the gate. The Captain made an AML (Aircraft Maintenance Logbook) entry for the suspected fuel leak and overweight landing.The only causal factor was the mechanical irregularity of the right fuel tank showing 1,000 lbs less than the left, which gave us the IMBAL message.				B737-800 First Officer reported a fuel imbalance message in flight, causing an in-flight shut down of the #2 engine and a return to the departure airport.	
1839202	202109	1201-1800	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				28000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class A ZZZ																								Air/Ground Communication		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 177; Flight Crew Total 2351; Flight Crew Type 2351		Communication Breakdown; Confusion; Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1839202												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural FAR		N	Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft; Human Factors; Procedure	Aircraft	During climb out, FA (Flight Attendant) called on intercom to report a very loud banging noise behind the wing area, aircraft right side, row 31. FA said it sounded like someone pounding on the fuselage with a sledgehammer repeatedly. We leveled off at FL280 immediately and reduced speed from 280 KIAS to 250 KIAS. I asked the FO to look for a nearby field with good weather.  FA recorded the noise via video on her cell phone and brought it to the cockpit. After reviewing the video, we decided to divert to ZZZ. We notified dispatch and coordinated with the FA's and informed the passengers. During the descent and configuration of the aircraft for landing at ZZZ, with the flaps at 5deg, I called for Flaps 10 while the aircraft speed was at 209 KIAS. I immediately said 'delay that stay at flaps five.' I believe the FO moved the flap handle out of the 5 detent and returned it immediately. The flaps remained at 5 degrees and I continued to slow the aircraft. I called for gear down an Flaps 15, flaps 25, flaps 30 landing checklist. I noticed the stick shaker indicator on the airspeed tape was in an unusual position and cross checked the configuration to find the flaps were at 5. We were at approximately 1200 feet when I announced 'going around', but did not hit TOGA as I did not want GA thrust. At this point, the FO and I were convinced that the two problems were related; that there was some structural damage to the flaps.We climbed to 5,000 feet and referenced the Checklist for FLAPS - TRAILING EDGE FLAPS DISAGREE. We returned the flap lever to 5, then selected 2. The flaps retracted to 2. The checklist directed us to 'Proceed normally.' We had a discussion about what that meant and were not comfortable with extending the flaps again, with suspected mechanical damage to the flaps already existing. We got a phone patch with Maintenance Control and dispatch on HF and discussed the two issues and our checklist. They had no additional advice. We discussed the plan to land in the current flap configuration (flap 2), using the flap up speeds with Maintenance Control and dispatch. The FO had already assessed the landing distance and we referenced the FLAPS- TRAILING EDGE FLAPS UP LANDING checklist. We landed on RWY XXL at ZZZ, at VREF 40 + 40 + 5. I used auto brakes 3, and rolled to the end of the runway. We had the fire/rescue look us over for damage prior to proceeding to the gate. They reported a large cable hanging from the satellite antenna. The PSEU (Proximity Switch Electronics Unit) light came on after landing. The FO and I debriefed the events in detail post flight and during the next two flights. It was not until the following morning, that I realized that in all likelihood, I caused the flap malfunction by calling for flaps 10 at 209 KIAS. The SATCOM antenna seal failure noise was significantly concerning, and I assumed the flap issue was directly related.				First Officer reported a SATCOM antenna became dislodged on take off, repeatedly striking against the fuselage. Flight crew elected to divert for a precautionary landing.	
1839503	202109	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US												Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Visual Approach	Class C ZZZ																								Pitot-Static System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Troubleshooting; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Distraction	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1839503												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	It was the FO (First Officer)'s leg into ZZZ. He had briefed the ILS approach which was the landing runway on the ATIS. A flap 40 degree landing was planned. The weather was VFR at the field with a cloud base at roughly 4,000 ft. MSL. ATC had us on a left downwind and turned us on a 90 degree base to final for Runway XXR. The FO had a 2.0 mile ring around the FAF ZZZZZ in the box to be fully configured for landing by then. And to the best of my recollection we were fully configured by the FAF. Runway XXR was in sight and ATC cleared us for the visual approach which was backed up by the ILS XXR. As we turned on to final for the Runway XXR at ZZZ, an accelerated red airspeed zipper came up on the bottom of the speed tape. There was an 'airspeed low' aural warning. The FO pushed the power in immediately, a stick shaker was felt, and the red airspeed zipper came down for the top of the airspeed tape. The FO disengaged the autopilot. There was very narrow gap on the upper and lower red zippers on the airspeed tape and the FO flew between the narrow gap. The FO stated, 'I'm not sure what is going on'. The chain of events happened at a very accelerated pace. Then the aircraft accelerated and the red zippers opened up and I called out ' watch your speed'. I remember seeing the aircraft accelerate near 159-160 kts. and I saw the red zipper on the top of the airspeed tape at 162 kts. In recovery, the aircraft climbed approximately 200-300 ft. We had Runway XXR in sight and the FO was in good position to land. I asked the FO if he was good with landing or if he wanted to do a go-around. The FO elected to continue the approach. Considering what we had just experienced, I felt that was a good choice. The FO continued the visual approach flying on the ILS XXR glide slope and localizer. At 1,000 ft. the FO made the 'stable' call out. At 500 ft. I made the 'stable' call out. We experienced no more airspeed red zipper indications or aural warnings for the remainder of the approach. The FO had a good landing. Upon landing I saw that the speed brakes did not automatically deploy so I manually deployed them. I noted after clearing the runway that due to the distractions, I failed to finish the 'below the line' checklist which I later mentioned to the FO. After the flight the FO and I decided to call Maintenance Control to discuss what had happened to us. Maintenance Control notified me that our Aircraft X had a 'history' of unreliable airspeed and that the FO's pitot tube was changed out. The Maintenance Representative voiced concern that it may be that our aircraft had a much 'bigger' maintenance issue and he wanted to pressure test the pitot system before releasing the aircraft. I put the 'write-up' in the maintenance book. I notified the outbound crew of what had happened and that my understanding was that Maintenance was on the way. ZZZ Operations was notified as well.I agreed with the Maintenance Representative that Aircraft X possibly had a bigger Maintenance issue at hand. When I called out 'watch your speed' as the airspeed was approaching 160 kts., the First Officer had the same indication and matched mine. I have never seen anything like this event ever. So it seems possible that the airspeed was correct but the red zippers were not correct. Due to the distractions on final approach, I did not complete the Before Landing 'below the line' checklist and this did not register until touchdown when I had to manually deploy the speed brakes.I did not complete the Before Landing 'below the line' checklist. A go-around could have possibly helped in this situation but I do feel now as I did then, that the safest thing to do was land the aircraft straight ahead.				Captain reported auto pilot disconnected and the Flight Crew received erroneous airspeed indication which complicated the approach. After landing the Captain reported failing to run the after landing checklist due to distractions.	
1840323	202109	1801-2400	ZZZ.Tower	US			200			Mixed	25		Dusk	14000		Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B ZZZ																												Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	Any Unknown or Unlisted Aircraft Manufacturer		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B ZZZ																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 102; Flight Crew Type 9000		Workload; Time Pressure; Other / Unknown; Distraction			1840323	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 190; Flight Crew Type 4725		Workload; Time Pressure; Other / Unknown; Distraction			1840338	Conflict Airborne Conflict; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT; Inflight Event / Encounter Weather / Turbulence			Automation Aircraft Terrain Warning; Automation Aircraft RA; Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew FLC complied w / Automation / Advisory; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Diverted	Weather	Weather	Approaching ZZZ on the ZZZZZ3 RNAV Arrival Runway XXR transition and while joining an approximate 20 mile final, ATC advised us of traffic joining the [Runway] XXL approach course. Traffic was in sight and within a few moments we received an RA command of 'Descend'. The First Officer, who was the Pilot Flying, disengaged the automation and complied with the RA. I advised ATC of the conflict and we continued our approach for XXR with autopilot/autothrottle disengaged. While continuing the approach, we heard an aircraft executing a 'Go-Around' on XXL due to windshear. The F/O (First Officer) and I communicated with one another to 'Be ready' in case windshear was encountered. During the approach I observed unremarkable wind speeds 7-15 knots; however, winds varied from direction as descent continued. The aircraft was fully configured and on airspeed well before 1,000 ft., landing clearance was received and the Before Landing Checklist complete. At the 500 ft. call, the traffic that we were following to XXR, had landed and was just clearing the runway. Light unremarkable turbulence was encountered during the approach with little fluctuations in airspeed until approximately 200 ft. AGL. At that point I felt the aircraft sink and observed what seemed to be an instantaneous decrease in airspeed. 'Pull Up' appeared on the PFD (Primary Flight Display) and then I called 'Pull Up'.The F/O appeared to have observed the same warning and was initiating the escape maneuver. I called 'Windshear' and as pitch and power was applied, the aircraft performed as commanded. No change to configuration occurred until both the F/O and I agreed that the aircraft was no longer in jeopardy. A normal go-around procedure was then completed. ATC advised several aircraft (after our go-around) of 'Microburst Alert! Loss of 40 knots on short final'. ATC gave a frequency change to Departure Control, that neither the F/O nor I heard. After several minutes of distraction, ie. ACARS Dispatch for alternate, excessive radio traffic, communication with passengers, I contacted Tower and communication with Departure Control was attained. A divert plan was received from Dispatch; however, ATC advised that approaches and landings had resumed in ZZZ. Due to the lengthy delayed vectors, we determined that conditions had improved and enough fuel was on board, to attempt another approach and landing. A normal approach and landing was performed to Runway XXL in ZZZ.As a note, the F/O informed me of the two-tone aural alert and windshear warning. It's quite possible that when I said windshear it was due to what I saw on the PFD and or me repeating what was already said by the aircraft. The event happened so quickly I can't recall.		We began our descent on the ZZZZZ 3 Arrival [Runway] XXR transition. As we approached the ZZZ terminal area ATC vectored us off the arrival with an intercept heading and cleared us for the visual approach to XXR. A descending RA ensued during LOC interception to XXR with the autopilot on. The conflict aircraft was in sight and I disconnected the automation and complied with the RA command. This lasted a few seconds and only descended a few 100 feet, whilst maintaining localizer centerline. Once the RA was clear we opted to continue the approach. As we were configuring on the visual approach to XXR we heard an aircraft transmitting to ATC that they were going around on XXL due to windshear. At that time we received no predictive windshear/or reactive windshear warnings and all indications in the flight deck seemed normal. In fact the aircraft we were following to XXR had not transmitted any adverse conditions as well. We continued fully configured by 1,000 ft. AGL to XXR.At approximately 150 ft. the aircraft felt as if it started to drop/fall and the Captain called 'Windshear' and a 'WINDSHEAR' warning followed. I performed the windshear escape maneuver. The threat only lasted what seemed like a few seconds and we were through the microburst. ATC then issued microburst warnings after our encounter. Once we were in the clear, we were vectored back to ZZZ for an approach to XXL. At that time the microburst was no longer a threat and three or four aircraft already landed XXL. We agreed that returning was the most viable option instead of diverting to ZZZ1. We landed with no further incident. It wasn't until we were getting vectored back to ZZZ that the Captain informed me that a PULL-UP was also received. During the event I did not hear or see a PULL-UP warning. I was so focused on not hitting the ground and executing a WINDSHEAR escape maneuver. Before the event we discussed the possibility of a windshear encounter due to the other aircraft go-around. So when the Captain and aircraft called windshear, windshear was on the forefront of my mind and a WINDSHEAR escape maneuver was executed.		Air Carrier flight crew reported a CFIT event due to a TCAS RA followed immediately by a wind shear warning during landing. Flight crew executed an evasive maneuver and sequenced for another approach.	
1840551	202109	1801-2400	SEA.Tower	WA												Tower SEA	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb; Takeoff / Launch		Class B SEA																												Tower SEA	Air Carrier	A321		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb; Climb		Class B SEA																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1840551												ATC Issue All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	Encountered significant wake turbulence immediately after rotation behind A321.   Wake turbulence was constant so we informed Tower we would require offset from departure course (34R.Bangor9).  Reported A321 in sight and offset appx. 0.2mi left of course.   We were cleared for takeoff while the A321 was rotating and my first thought was to delay a bit.  I wish I had as I have never encountered wake turbulence so close to the ground nor do I wish to try it again.It is worth noting this is the second time recently I have encountered wake turbulence behind an Airbus 321 in Seattle and my First Officer was recently cleared for takeoff behind a Boeing 757 without standard separation in Seattle.  In the future, I will not accept a clearance to takeoff so close to another aircraft and I believe it would be wise to follow up with Seattle Tower to ensure standard departure separation is being used and they are not getting rushed with the high amount of departures they work these days.The Seattle Controllers do a remarkable job handling the heavy traffic load we have seen this summer but perhaps there is a trend here and they are getting a bit rushed.  As a pilot, I will ask for a bit of time before taking off behind another aircraft next time.				B737-800 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence departing SEA in trail of an A321, commenting that spacing appeared insufficient.	
1840593	202108	1801-2400	ZZZ1.ARTCC	US						VMC			Night			Center ZZZ1; TRACON ZZZ2	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class A ZZZ1; Class C ZZZ; Class E ZZZ1																								Powerplant Fuel System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Situational Awareness	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1840593												ATC Issue All Types; Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Human Factors; Procedure; Aircraft	Aircraft	In the vicinity of ZZZZZ intersection power was reduced to idle as aircraft intercepted descent path on the ZZZZZ 5 arrival.  Master Caution, Fuel, and Filter Bypass light on #1 Engine illuminated. Flying duties were transferred to First Officer and QRH procedure was accomplished.  Only the #1 Engine Filter Bypass was illuminated and the engine was operating normally. Decision was made to continue to ZZZ and request priority handing as a precaution and to avoid any unnecessary delays. ATC controllers were very helpful with one exception. When we checked in with Approach Control  (I believe the frequency was X) the controller immediately issued us a 40 knot reduction in airspeed to sequence another aircraft in front of us. This would have created an unstable approach and was the exact situation we were trying to avoid by asking for priority handling. We reminded him that we were a priority aircraft and were beginning to slow as we configured. Normal landing was uneventful with Tower and Ground controllers again very helpful.  Dispatch, FODO [Flight Officer Duty Officer] and line maintenance in ZZZ were contacted and briefed. Flight Attendants were debriefed.Brief Approach controller on importance of assisting Emergency Aircraft.				Captain reported an engine fuel filter bypass light illuminated. Captain elected to request priority handling to land at destination airport.	
1841627	202109	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US				8000		VMC						Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class B ZZZ																								Electrical Power		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting; Human-Machine Interface			1841627												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural MEL / CDL; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Maintenance			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Beginning descent into ZZZ, a cascading electrical failure occurred causing multiple light to illuminate and several system to fail, including iff, trim, left aft fuel pump, equipment cooling supply, pack controller left, multiple window heat over heats, etc...noted TR2 was zero amps.  Ran QRH checklists...until 8,000 feet MSL when landing became primary task. Requested priority handling and landed uneventfully. Airport Support personnel assisted, no external hot-spots found.  Taxied to gate. Coordinated with local Maintenance Control.A cascading electrical failure occurred on descent into ZZZ.  It is unknown how to prevent electrical failures.  Recommend development of QRH multiple electric failure check list...executing all individual light procedures is time consuming and diverts attention from large situation.				Air Carrier First Officer reported multiple EICAS messages displayed and several electronic system failures during descent. Flight crew completed part of the QRH checklist. Flight crew requested priority handling and executed a safe landing.	
1841989	202109	0601-1200	PHX.Tower	AZ	270	3	900						Daylight			Tower PHX	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Climb		Class B PHX																												Tower PHX	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb		Class B PHX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 87; Flight Crew Type 9600					1841989												Deviation - Speed All Types; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	I was the Pilot Monitoring on the flight. The F/O (First Officer) was the Pilot Flying during the event. It was day VMC with no adverse weather and light easterly winds. Autothrottle with LNAV/VNAV modes were engaged on the flight director, but the PF was hand flying throughout the event. Thrust was reduced to Level 2 for take off.We were departing PHX 7L on the FYRBD 1 RNAV Departure.  ATC cleared us for takeoff to visually follow an A320.  When we rotated off, the A320 was about 3-4 miles ahead of us climbing out on a different departure that departed to the southeast, but had a similar track initially. We experienced the first wake roll encounter just before selecting flaps up, about 900'-1,000' AGL, the aircraft rolled about 20-30 degrees and the PF was able to counter and stop the roll with full aileron deflection but it stayed in the bank about three seconds before returning to wings level. I could feel light air frame buffeting from roll spoilers being fully deployed. He called for flaps up. I selected flaps up and the aircraft encountered the wake again and rolled the opposite direction, again the PF corrected with full aileron deflection and I could feel the spoilers deploy, the aircraft had a more noticeable buffet, speed stagnated and began to decrease towards flaps up V min maneuver speed (yellow speed tape band). The flaps were retracting as this happened and the min maneuver speed (yellow band) was increasing. I immediately saw the pitch limit indicators appear and quickly descend to almost touch the flight path indicators, indicating we were approaching a high AOA (Angle Of Attack) and close to stall. I said to the PF to lower the nose and increase speed, which he was doing as I said it. I deleted the reduced climb setting in the FMC and the engines went to climb power, I am not sure why neither of us didn't just push the thrust levers up. (Distraction? surprise factor?) The speed stabilized just above V min and began to increase as the aircraft rolled to wings level. We gained about 10 knots in speed when we again encountered the wake, the aircraft rolled again to about 30 degrees despite the PF applying full aileron to counter it. Again with full roll spoiler out, the speed stagnated, we could feel buffet and the aircraft stayed in the banked condition for 2-5 seconds before responding to the control inputs. Again wings level the speed began to increase and we continued on the departure path. The preceding aircraft diverged from our course and we then continued with no further events. While ATC may have provided the minimum separation, we clearly needed more than what was provided. I have not experienced this type of encounter following an A320 ever.  Usually it is a 757-767 type or larger. In hindsight, we should have performed the nose high upset recover maneuver and manually applied full thrust. I think the fact we routinely experience some wake events with only minor attitude changes led us to not react as we should have. The reduced climb thrust setting placed us in the A320 wake longer and hampered our recovery. Higher initial climb thrust would have allowed us to out climb or at least experience less airspeed stagnation.	Reporter stated he was surprised at the intensity of the A320 wake.			B737-800 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence departing PHX in trail of an A320.	
1842347	202109		ZZZ1.ARTCC	US						VMC						Center ZZZ1	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ1																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 185					1842347												Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor; Inflight Event / Encounter Other / Unknown		Y	Person Flight Attendant; Person Passenger	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approximately 15 minutes prior to top of descent into ZZZ we were informed that a passenger heard a pop and smelled something electrical in the vicinity of row XX. Initially the flight attendants could not verify an odor. As we began our descent, a few more passengers smelled an odor and it was confirmed by one of the flight attendants. We ran the smoke, fire, and fumes checklist to a point the fumes had dissipated. At no point was any smoke ever noticed and the fumes were only confirmed by a few passengers. By the time we completed the checklist and the odor was gone and we were in the terminal area at ZZZ. We elected to land and taxi to the gate as the odor was gone and no evidence of smoke or fire existed.				B-737 Captain reported Flight Attendant notification of cabin fume event during cruise which dissipated after a short time. Captain continued to destination and executed a safe landing.	
1842939	202109	1801-2400	ZZZ.ARTCC	US				24000		VMC			Night			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class C ZZZ																								AC Generator/Alternator		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Workload; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1842939												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation - Altitude Excursion From Assigned Altitude; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Automation Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Leveling at FL240 during descent into ZZZ, ATC asked us to report our altitude because she was not receiving anything from us. When we looked down, the Captain noticed that IFF 2 had a fault and switched it to his side. At about that time we both noticed a power surge which made my clock blank momentarily and the Captain noticed lights on his panel flash. Next we had a series of lights come on spaced apart by a few minutes at first and then began to come on more quickly as time passed. First we had a cooling fan supply light. Then when cleared to descend via the arrival, I pushed recall and noticed the left pack light remained illuminated. Suspecting something wrong with the power distribution system, I cycled through electrical panel wafer switch positions and found no amps coming from TR 2 or 3 and negative 3 amps on the battery. I hacked my clock. About 5 minutes had already passed. I took the radios while the Captain ran the checklist for the pack light next and shortly after, we lost the left aft fuel boost pump. I told the Captain I thought we had a power distribution problem and we were draining our battery. I thought we might be slowly shedding components due to their power sources being associated with the battery. As we passed 10,000 ft. I told the Captain and he put away the book and ran the landing checklist. While on the descent, I recommended [requesting priority handling] so we could land ASAP. I configured early in prep for a slam dunk and the Captain and I agreed to starting the APU just in case. By this time we were on base and more lights had come on. We lost the B Electrical Hydraulic Pump, had a right side window over heat light, battery discharge light, TR fail light, and a few pitot lights had come on. Then while turning to final, I noticed the short vectors would overshoot me so I clicked off autopilot and turned. That's when I noticed I had no electric trim. The Captain manually ran the trim for me as we slowed and configured and we had an uneventful landing. After the fire trucks greeted us and found no hot spots around the E & E bay, we discussed whether we had enough power on the battery and I noted the time had been 17 minutes from when I noticed the negative amps on the battery and started the clock. We continued to taxi to the gate and after the fire crew came up I went with them down to the E & E bays to do my own inspection. I didn't see or smell anything. Aircraft X is an old airplane. The older planes get the more they will have gremlins like this. Maintenance discovered a faulty number 2 generator or regulator with some certainty that an Aircraft phase was not getting to the busses. How many more lights would have come on eventually? Just thank goodness we were so close to landing when it failed.				First Officer reported that system annunciator lights began to illuminate so Flight Crew requested priority handling and made a landing at the destination airport. Maintenance determined an integrated drive generator had failed and on phase was not working.	
1843108	202110	0601-1200	ATL.Tower	GA			200						Daylight			Tower ATL	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ATL																												Tower ATL	Air Carrier	B757 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Climb		Class B ATL																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 220; Flight Crew Type 4500		Distraction; Situational Awareness			1843108												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew		Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Human Factors; Environment - Non Weather Related	Human Factors	At 200 ft. AGL on climbout we entered wake turbulence from the 757 in front of us. The aircraft rolled to the right and we continued to climb just right of course. 0.18 was shown on the MFD. Winds were 150 at 07 and we were LNAV to RONII. Before we could get back on the track, ATC assigned us to heading 070 and stated we were south of our track. In our next radio call we stated what happened and the Controller cleared us to continue the departure. I feel increased separation from 757's would mitigate these occurrences from happening.				B737-700 First Officer reported a track deviation occurred on departure from ATL when the flight crew was distracted by a wake turbulence encounter.	
1843669	202110	1201-1800	ATL.Tower	GA			200						Daylight			Tower ATL	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ATL																												Tower ATL	Air Carrier	B757 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Initial Climb		Class B ATL																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine						1843669												Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued Advisory / Alert; Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Procedure	During takeoff/climbout (200') we entered wake turbulence from a 757 in front of us. The aircraft rolled to the right and we continued to climb just to the right of course. (0.18 was shown on the MFD). Winds were 150/07 and we were LNAV to RONII. Before getting back on LNAV track, ATC assigned us to a left turn to 070 and stated we were south of our track. In the next transmission, we stated what had happened (wake turbulence) and the Controller cleared us to continue the departure.	Reporter stated he has noticed ATC departing aircraft with reduced spacing to B757 aircraft, resulting in more wake encounters.			B737-700 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence departing ATL in trail of a B757.	
1844022	202110	0601-1200						36000		VMC			Daylight				Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Pressurization System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1844022												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	During cruise at FL360 the First Officer and I felt a pressurization change in our ears. We checked the overhead pressurization panel and saw that the cabin climb indicator was indicating a rapidly climbing cabin. The cabin altitude indicator was also increasing. We donned our oxygen masks and I elected to follow the Rapid Depressurization Checklist in the QRH. We [requested priority handling] and were cleared to descend. We descended to 10,000 ft. then completed the remaining normal procedures checklist before diverting to ZZZ. One major factor in the event was crew communication in the cockpit. With the speakers on and the breathing noises carrying over the interphone we had to repeat ourselves numerous times increased the workload of the event. Reading the checklist and verifying the actions combined with ATC instructions, the Flight Attendant briefing and announcements to the passengers proved to be challenging. Another factor was the decision to go with the checklist that I felt was the safest for the passengers and crew. I felt that taking any extra time to look at the other checklists that may apply would be time consuming and compromise our margin of safety. [I suggest] reviewing and using oxygen masks in the simulator more.				B737 Captain reported a cabin pressure malfunction which caused them to initiate an immediate descent and divert to a precautionary landing.	
1844197	202110		ZDV.ARTCC	CO				36000		VMC						Center ZDV	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent		Class A ZDV																												Center ZDV		Commercial Fixed Wing		2		IFR					Class A ZDV																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 149.38; Flight Crew Total 1442.53; Flight Crew Type 1442.53					1844197												Deviation - Altitude Crossing Restriction Not Met; Deviation - Altitude Undershoot; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related	Environment - Non Weather Related	At cruise and approaching Denver at FL360 in smooth air with the A autopilot in command mode and LNAV/VNAV engaged, DEN Center instructed us to descend and maintain FL320. Shortly after initiating a VNAV descent to comply with the clearance we experienced an upset event due to wake turbulence. While completing the upset recovery procedure the most bank I saw was 35 degrees and while we did deviate slightly from our clearance laterally we promptly returned to our route and were easily able to comply with our vertical clearance, leveling at FL320. While I ensured there were no injuries in the cabin the F/O reported our wake turbulence encounter to ATC. DEN Center took our report but then delayed our clearance to descend via the CLASH 3 RNAV arrival until we were well above our vertical path. We accepted the clearance and began a descent but after a further ATC speed restriction to 260 knots we realized about 5 miles prior to EVRLY we would be unable to comply with the altitude restriction of FL200. Due to ATC frequency congestion we were unable to inform ATC of how high we were and crossed EVRLY approximately 400 feet above the restriction of FL200. We then returned to our vertical path and landed at DEN without further incident.				B737 NG Captain reported encountering wake turbulence at FL360 in ZDV airspace.	
1844338	202109	1201-1800	A11.TRACON	AK												TRACON A11	Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ANC																												TRACON A11	Air Carrier	B747 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Final Approach		Class B ANC																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness			1844338												Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter; Inflight Event / Encounter Loss Of Aircraft Control			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Regained Aircraft Control	Procedure; Environment - Non Weather Related; Human Factors	Environment - Non Weather Related	Wake turbulence encounter following a Boeing 747 on final approach. On radar downwind in VFR conditions we were cleared a visual approach following a 747 that was already established on final.  On the dog leg to final I was descending to arrive at the FAF at what would be a normal intercept for glideslope to runway 07R. Autopilot and autothrottles were connected at this time.  I noted on TCAS that we were 6 miles behind the heavy as we were about be aligned on final. Suddenly we encountered the wake turbulence from the preceding aircraft and the aircraft rolled quickly to the left. I took over controls manually and disconnected the autopilot and autothrottles while simultaneously recovering the airplane from the sharp bank to the left. The event happen very quickly, so I am unsure as to what bank angle we reached but estimating more than 30 and less than 45deg. We received a roll authority alert as the auto pilot had been unable to maintain level flight with the quick onset from the wake turbulence.  The aircraft recovered and we re-established ourselves on final. I mentioned to the First Officer that I was going to climb and maintain above the preceding aircraft flight path and also stay above glide slope for the remainder of the approach, planning to touchdown at 2,000 feet. The rest of the flight was uneventful and we landed normally in Anchorage.Looking back on this event I could have easily avoided encountering the wake turbulence.  When cleared for the visual approach behind the 747 I should've maintained my altitude instead of starting a gradual descent. I had plenty of time to descend later and descending at that time only increased the chances of encountering the wake. At the time, I was considering the fact that our distance was adequate and that we wouldn't have any issues following the 747 using normal visual cues /angle approaching the runway. I was wrong.  Even 6 miles behind the heavy the turbulence was strong and the rolling tendency abrupt. Not worth the risk, not to mention the discomfort to the passengers and crew. In the future I will certainly keep this in mind for a visual behind a preceding heavy aircraft to avoid this type of encounter.				B737-900 Captain reported encountering wake turbulence on approach to ANC in trail of a B747.	
1845120	202110	1801-2400	ZZZ.Airport	US	090	7		1500		VMC			Night			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach	Vectors	Class B ZZZ																								Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 170; Flight Crew Type 4922		Time Pressure; Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting			1845120	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 86; Flight Crew Type 10515		Situational Awareness; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting; Workload			1845127	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft; Procedure; Human Factors	Aircraft	While on vectors off of the Arrival Runway XX slowing to assigned speed 170 knots, we felt a high vibration, 3.8 vibration indication, on the number 2 engine.  Turning on final to Runway XX and slowing to final landing configuration, flaps 30, number 2 engine high vibration increased to an unacceptable level, which lead us to retard the number 2 thrust lever to idle.  We decided to continue the approach using flaps 15 with the number 2 engine at idle.  Number 2 engine at idle had no abnormal vibrations and engine indications were normal.  We selected AB3, GND PROX Flap Inhibit switch to Flap Inhibit and completed a new landing checklist for flaps 15.  We discussed go-around would require the use of flaps 1.  PM (Pilot Monitoring) selected flaps 15 approach speed on the FMC.  PM flew an uneventful approach and landing using the number 1 engine for approach and thrust reverse after touchdown.  Exited taxiway 1X taxi via 2 hold short 2Y.  We reviewed the QRC Engine Limit or Surge or Stall.  After running clock for two minutes (perceived three plus minutes) I closed the number 2 engine start lever via normal post landing ground procedures.  I called the F/A's (Flight Attendants) and told them the condition of the number engine and asked them to look out the right side of the aircraft and let me know if they see anything abnormal.  FO (First Officer) notified ZZZ Maintenance and asked them to meet us at the aircraft.  Uneventful taxi to Gate XX.  Passenger noted while walking off the aircraft she saw two puffs of fire come out the back of the engine after the landing gear came down.Our previous training for engine failure on final approach led us to continue the approach and land using flaps 15 as the safest course of action. Postflight we debriefed the event.  We both agreed we should have declared an emergency.  This would have provided a warning to Tower had our situation escalated resulting in an engine fire on final or during taxi.  Crash/Fire/Rescue (CFR) shadowing our aircraft would have provided information such as fuel leak or a tailpipe fire.		While on vectors off of the Arrival Runway XX slowing to assigned speed 170 knots, we felt a high vibration, 3.8 vibration indication, on the number 2 engine.  Turning on final to Runway XX and slowing to final landing configuration, flaps 30, number 2 engine high vibration increased to an unacceptable level which led us to retard the number 2 thrust lever to idle.  We decided to continue the approach using flaps 15 with the number 2 engine at idle.  Number 2 engine at idle had no abnormal vibrations and engine indications were normal.  We selected AB3, Ground Proximity Flap Inhibit switch to Flap Inhibit and completed a new Landing Checklist for flaps 15.  We discussed go-around would require the use of flaps 1.  PM (Pilot Monitoring) selected flaps 15 approach speed on the FMC.  PF (Pilot Flying) flew an uneventful approach and landing using the number 1 engine for approach and thrust reverse after touchdown.  We reviewed the QRC Engine Limit or Surge or Stall.  After running clock for two minutes (perceived three plus minutes) I closed the number 2 engine start lever via normal post landing ground procedures.  I called the F/As (Flight Attendants) and told them the condition of the number 2 engine and asked them to look out the right side of the aircraft and report anything abnormal.  FO notified ZZZ Maintenance and asked them to meet us at the aircraft.  Uneventful taxi to gate. Female Passenger noted while walking off the aircraft she saw two puffs of fire come out the back of the engine after the landing gear came down.		Flight Crew reported excessive engine vibration and elected to continue the approach with the #2 engine thrust lever at idle.	
1845250	202110	1801-2400	PHX.Airport	AZ												Tower PHX	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Landing		Class B PHX																												Tower PHX		Airbus Industrie Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR			Landing		Class B PHX																								Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1845250												Inflight Event / Encounter Unstabilized Approach; Inflight Event / Encounter Wake Vortex Encounter			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Executed Go Around / Missed Approach	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Procedure	Landing behind an Airbus aircraft on runway 26 in Phoenix with a 6 knot wind from the north we encountered wing tip vortex turbulence and induced roll.  The copilot made an excellent choice and performed at aborted landing/go-around.  We re-entered the pattern on downwind and received vectors for an uneventful landing.  Approach Control apologized for vectoring us too close behind the Airbus and Tower had asked them to expedite off the runway.  We were at least 2 minutes behind the preceding aircraft. If the wind hadn't been from the north, I don't think we would have had any problem, but the copilot did a great job!  Many passengers thanked [us] for being safe.  Thought that the copilot made an excellent early decision to go around instead of trying to 'save' it.				B737-800 Captain reported executing a go-around after encountering wake turbulence in trail of an Airbus just before landing at PHX.	
1845257	202110	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US													Air Carrier	B737-900		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors																																																																Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Workload; Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1845257	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Communication Breakdown; Workload; Time Pressure	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 Flight Crew		1845252	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Deviation - Speed All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	On descent into ZZZ we smelled fumes in the cockpit of possible electrical nature. Followed QRC checklist. alerted flight attendants and shortly after we smelled it they did too in mid cabin. We had a similar smell that lasted about a minute soon after level-off from ZZZ1, but lasted only about a minute then dissipated. So when it occurred again on descent we requested priority handling and got expedited handling to Runway XXL in ZZZ. The aircraft itself went through MX before we got it in ZZZ1.  Anti-ice valve issue, changing wheel well light, and enroute some galley lights went out. Landed without incident after being cleared by airport [support personnel] we continued to taxi to the gate. Passengers and crew did not complain about any air quality issues affecting health.  Flight control parameters exceeded. Speed brakes were extended with flaps greater than 15 degrees in my haste to make an expeditious approach and landing in case smoke fumes returned.		After top of climb about 100 NM from ZZZ2, we started to smell a slight burning odor. The Captain and I started to discuss it. He thought it smelled like a match, I thought more like a forest fire or maybe something from the galley. We called back and the flight attendants said they were smelling something too, very light, about mid cabin. We asked them about the galley. They said no smell was in the galley or near the bathroom. The flight attendants suggested and we all decided that they go row to row to investigate and ask all passengers to unplug their devices. During that time the smell got stronger and the Captain and I donned our oxygen masks. The flight attendants said the smell was strongest mid-cabin when they walked back and started to dissipate when they came forward. The Captain took off his mask and said that the smell was nearly gone. After a few minutes I took my mask off too and also found that there was no odor. The entire crew discussed possible causes of the odor, possible diversions if it comes back, and formed a consensus that we continue the flight, allow passengers to reconnect their devices and monitor the situation. The Captain and I both kept our oxygen masks unstowed.During the decent, on the ZZZZZ arrival into ZZZ, we noticed the odor started to come back and slowly get stronger. We both donned our masks and established communication. The Captain instructed me to tell ATC about our situation and request priority handling and we needed to land as soon as possible. While I was coordinating with ATC, the Captain called and told the cabin crew that we were requesting priority handling and were going to land as soon as possible. I think that he told them to call us if they see smoke, and also overheard him do portions of the safety briefing. ATC vectored us off the arrival and gave us a different runway. I ran the 'smoke, fire, or fumes in the passenger cabin or flight deck' QRC. The Captain elected not to go into the QRH as we were on downwind for the runway and the priority was to land. While I was programming the FMC, he pulled the oxygen mask away from his face and discovered that the odor had dissipated as it had done before. The Captain decided to keep his mask off as there was no evidence of fumes and I was having a certain amount of difficulty with communication. I elected to keep my oxygen mask on, just in case. We were cleared for a visual approach to XXL. The Captain kept the speed up and then slowed to configure the aircraft. I was monitoring the aircraft's energy state to ensure we would not have to go around and was looking for the [support] vehicles. We landed, the support vehicles followed us off the runway. They did an external inspection and found no signs of smoke or fire. The Captain thought it was safe to taxi to the gate, so we did. The [support] personnel met us at the gate and we briefed with them.  The smell was transient in nature both times.Communication is very difficult with the oxygen mask on and the old communication panel where the hot mike switch does not stay on.		B737-900 flight crew reported a fumes event during descent to destination.	
1845873	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US				14000		IMC			Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Climb	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Cockpit Window		X	Failed																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 153; Flight Crew Total 17200; Flight Crew Type 5853					1845873												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Aircraft Damaged; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	Climbing out of 14,000 ft. we heard a loud bang. While investigating what it was there was a second bang and this time I saw glass shards coming off window 3L and saw the windows inner pane had completely shattered.  We leveled off at 16,000 ft. assessed the situation and decided to turn to ZZZ. Dispatch was notified and with no pressurization, controllability, or overweight landing issues no emergency was declared. Approach and landing were uneventful.				Captain reported cockpit window 3L shattered during climb and elected to perform an air turn back to a safe precautionary landing.	
1846671	202110						0										Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise																										Turbine Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 131; Flight Crew Total 13707; Flight Crew Type 212		Situational Awareness; Troubleshooting; Distraction			1846671												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Weight And Balance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Procedure; Aircraft; Human Factors	Aircraft	With shutting down an engine, dumping fuel and returning to the departure airport, we might have landed slightly overweight. With all that was going on, I forgot to record and keep track of our landing weight. I do not think we did land overweight as I believe we would have received an ACARS message stating such but just in case, I am submitting this report as no logbook entry was made to cover an overweight landing.				Captain reported confusion about actual landing weight during an event with an in flight shut down and an air turn back event.	
1846994	202110	1201-1800	ZZZ.Airport	US	090	5		5800					Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger	FMS Or FMC	Descent	STAR ZZZZZ	Class E ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 154; Flight Crew Type 7000		Training / Qualification; Situational Awareness; Human-Machine Interface			1846994	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine			Human-Machine Interface; Situational Awareness			1847002	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Issued New Clearance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Became Reoriented	Human Factors	Human Factors	While descending on the ZZZZZ Arrival into ZZZ Airport we were given a heading off of the arrival and a speed and altitude.  We were previously cleared to descend via the ZZZZZ [arrival].  Approach gave us a heading and a descent to 8,000 feet and slow to 220 knots.  The First Officer (FO) dialed in the heading, set the speed and engaged Level Change.  Shortly thereafter Approach re-cleared us direct to ZZZZZ [fix] and descend via.  The First Officer selected direct ZZZZZ set 4,000 feet (the bottom altitude on the arrival) and engaged LNAV.I did not notice that we were still in Level Change or if they selected VNAV.  I looked out the window and commented that the arrival really brought [us] close to the terrain on this arrival.  I crosschecked the VNAV PATH on my display and noticed we were well below the path. I told this to the Pilot Flying and they immediately went to Vertical Speed, set the min altitude for ZZZZZ1 and started a slow climb.  I called Approach to let them know and I believe at the same time they were calling us.A few seconds later we were all on the same page, so to speak, and we were given the clearance descend via and landed in ZZZ uneventfully. It wasn't immediately apparent to me that we were below the path as I have flown into ZZZ1 many times, and ATC step-descends us fairly close to the same hills.  I have only been into ZZZ once before over a year ago.  We never received any Terrain Alerts and there was no yellow or red on my ND.  The radar altimeter was not registering yet.  Approach did inform us that we had gone below their minimum vectoring altitude.  There was no unnecessary conversation going on that I can recall, I just missed the fact the we did not re-engage VNAV when re-cleared to descend via. More diligence in confirming MCP selections on the FMA.		On the ZZZZZ Arrival in to ZZZ we were descending via, then Approach changed the clearance to a heading and descent to 8,000 feet. I selected the heading, airspeed, and altitude assigned using Level Change. We were then cleared direct ZZZZZ [fix] and descend via again. I selected 4,000 feet as the bottom of the arrival and continued in Level Change instead of reselecting VNAV. Before reaching ZZZZZ1, we realized we were off the VNAV PATH and immediately stopped the descent and started a climb back to path. We told ATC what we were doing and they said we were below the minimum vectoring altitude and assigned 7,000 feet. Once we reached 7,000 feet, we were back on path and continued the arrival. There was a breakdown of [CRM]. I failed to recognize the improper descent when selecting a new altitude.		B737 Flight Crew reported they inadvertently descended below minimum vectoring altitude during arrival due to not recognizing that the MCP had been incorrectly set. The Flight Crew subsequently climbed back to the correct altitude.	
1847023	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.TRACON	US	090	48		12000					Daylight			TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class E ZZZ																								Engine		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 210; Flight Crew Type 13000		Workload			1847023												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical			Automation Aircraft Other Automation	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Approaching ZZZ, we experienced an EGT FAIL on the #1 engine with erratic engine indications. We notified ATC of Aircraft status, gave soul count of XXX, fuel [remaining], CFR (crash fire rescue equipment) to meet aircraft upon landing, and our desire to land on longer runway of XXL.  Flight Attendants were fully briefed on the situation. QRH pertaining to ENG FAIL complied with. First Officer (FO) maintained control of aircraft while Captain completed all checklists, briefings, and ATC coordination. At approximately 6,500 ft. MSL, all checklists were complete and appropriate data entered. Captain took control of the aircraft, as PF, to perform the single-engine approach and landing. Aircraft landed normally.  CFR circled aircraft to ensure all was well. Passengers briefed. Brake cooling module was performed. We waited the required XX minutes for cooling. Aircraft parked at the gate with normal Passenger deplanement. Captain met with ZZZ Maintenance. Captain performed a full Flight Attendant de-briefing.  Captain then called the ZZZ1 Chief Pilot on call, Maintenance, Supervisor, and Dispatch. Captain further met with the ZZZ Chief Pilot to complete debriefing. No further incident ensued.				Air carrier Captain reported an EGT fail on the #1 engine and other erratic engine indications during approach. The flight continued to a single-engine approach and landing, with normal passenger deplanement.	
1847186	202110	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Initial Approach		Class B ZZZ																								Flap/Slat Control System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1847186												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Aircraft	Aircraft	On approach to ZZZ flight #X. Cleared ILS approach Runway XXR. Captured ILS and started configuring the aircraft to flaps10, for an ATC assigned speed of 170 KIAS. The aircraft was not slowing as I anticipated. I asked the copilot to select flaps 10. He responded the flap lever was at 10. I checked the trailing edge flap gauge and cycled the flap handle to 5 then back to 10. No change! Flaps were remaining at flaps 5. I instructed my copilot to report a missed approach.I climbed the aircraft straight ahead and complied with ATC instructions. I placed the autopilot on and transferred Pilot Flying duties and communications with ATC to my copilot. I opened the QRH and accomplished the trailing edge flaps disagree checklist. I [requested priority handling], requested Runway XXL (the longest runway) for approach and landing. I requested and received non normal landing data via ACARS and notified ZZZ operations of our situation.I notified my flight attendants of the problem using the briefing format, advising a landing in 10 minutes. I addressed the passengers via the PA of our problem, and assured them of a successful outcome.At approximately 15 miles out on the ILS Runway XXL, I completed the checklist items of configuring the flaps to 15 via the alternate method and completed the dual response landing checklist. At 10 miles on the ILS I assumed the role of Pilot flying and landed.Landing and rollout was normal. We continued to the gate and advised maintenance. After all passengers had deplaned, I debriefed with the crew and thanked each of them for their excellent work.				B737-800 Captain reported a flap disagree during approach. The flight crew executed a missed approach, were able to set the flaps using an alternate method, and continued to a normal landing.	
1849372	202110	0601-1200	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO												Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B777 Undifferentiated or Other Model		2	Part 121	IFR	Ferry / Re-Positioning		Climb																										Toilet Smoke, Overheat, Fire Detectors & Warning		X	Improperly Operated																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1849372	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Troubleshooting			1848700	Aircraft Equipment Problem Less Severe; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Aircraft Equipment Problem Dissipated; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem	Human Factors	Human Factors	Smoke detector, crew rest, master caution EICAS activated on climb out. The Relief Pilot (RP) obtained a fire extinguisher and opened crew rest door. An acrid odor was present but no smoke was visible.  The EICAS message and crew rest smoke detector cautions went away soon after the door was opened.  The RP investigated LAV, Galley, upper cargo deck (no freight, ferry flight) and EE (electronics) compartment. No smoke was present. On further investigation RP found a small piece of black tape covering the smoke detector green light in the lower bunk. It was beginning to melt.  With the EICAS message and caution gone and with zero payload we elected to continue to our destination.  This event could easily be avoided if someone would have not covered the green light with the electrical tape.		Smoke detector, crew rest, master caution + EICAS activated on climb out. The Relief Pilot (RP) obtained a fire extinguisher and opened crew rest door. An acrid odor was present but no smoke was visible.  The EICAS message and crew rest smoke detector cautions went away soon after the door was opened.  The RP investigated LAV, Galley, upper cargo deck (no freight, ferry flight) and EE (electronics) compartment. No smoke was present. On further investigation RP found a small piece of black tape covering the smoke detector green light in the lower bunk. It was beginning to melt.  With the EICAS message and caution gone and with zero payload we elected to continue to our destination.  It's possible the heat from the covered smoke detector green light melted the tape causing smell and setting off the Master Caution and smoke detector.  I speculate that the tape was placed upon the light by a crew member, on a previous flight to prevent smoke detector light from shining in their eyes while sleeping?		Air carrier flight crew reported smoke detector activation during initial climb. The cause was attributed to melting tape placed over crew rest smoke detector light.	
1849996	202110							35000								Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise		Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Flying; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1849996												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed As Precaution; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	Diversion to ZZZ due to Loss of System B hydraulics. We were about 5 minutes past level off at FL350, First Officer was flying, VMC flight conditions, smooth ride.  First indication of a problem was Autopilot B disengaging. The First Officer took over flying manually.  About 30 seconds later, we got a Master Caution Light with a HYD annunciator.  We both looked at the overhead panel; at that point, the Flight Control B Low Pressure Light was the only light illuminated, followed 15-30 seconds later by both the System B ELEC and ENG Hydraulic Pump Low Pressure Lights. I started to run the LOSS OF SYSTEM B checklist in the QRH, and requested priority handling with ATC ZZZ1, and asked them to standby for our intentions.  We were about 80 miles northwest of ZZZ at that point; the First Officer checked the ZZZ weather, which was VFR with winds ~200/15 (aligned with Runway XX). I informed Center that we were going to divert to ZZZ. I completed the QRH in the descent, followed by the Non-Routine Landing Considerations checklist.  I notified Dispatch via ACARS 7700 page, and requested ARFF (airport rescue and fire fighting) response. Alternate Flap Extension, approach and landing were uneventful. I took the aircraft for approach and landing since First Officer had less than 50 hours in the 737.  We rolled out ~7,000 feet down the runway (between XX and XY taxiways).  ARFF did a visual inspection and checked the brake temperatures via their equipment--at that point we were cleared to continue taxiing to the gate.  The First Officer did a post flight inspection, and did not find any significant fluid leaks (the B system was about 72% full post landing).Not a causal factor, but the low-time First Officer performed extremely well during the event--both flying the aircraft and with PM duties after I took the aircraft for landing. Not a prevention, but we did a visual approach backed up by the RNAV GPS Y Rwy XX, which has a significant offset from runway heading, and I ended up flying the approach visually, with limited input from the flight director.  We would have been better loading the non-precision or RNAV approaches, which are aligned with the runway.				B737-800 Captain reported loss of #2 Hydraulic System during cruise flight. The crew performed a diversion and precautionary landing.	
1850307	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US												Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-700			Part 121		Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class E ZZZ; Class G ZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)			Confusion; Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1850307												ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Track / Heading All Types; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Clearance; Flight Crew Took Evasive Action; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification	Airport; Human Factors	Human Factors	Departing ZZZ we taxied to Runway XX. We had completed all checklists as well triangles for the runway change they requested and was about to takeoff. ZZZ radio issued us a clearance with a void time of 4 mins past the hour. ATC then said, your cleared to depart 'direct ZZZZZ'. We placed ZZZZZ in L1, confirmed it, executed and departed. As soon as we lifted off the flying pilot (my FO) was still VMC and said this doesn't look right and took immediate correction away from the mountains back to the left. I clarified with Center that what they wanted was the ZZZZZ1 departure via the ZZZZZ transition and not direct ZZZZZ.  Center agreed and we corrected back on course. We both had VSD up and had no terrain alerts or warnings on the VSD.Although it did not initially feel rushed looking back, we were at the end of the runway ready to go with many checklists and changes happening in a short period of time that seemed to be going quickly and smoothly.  I think this gave us a sense of being on a roll and feeling caught up. If we had not been so close to departing, I may have stopped to look at my chart which would have caught my attention and prompted me to speak up before executing this issuance in the box.				B737-700 Captain reported a track heading deviation during takeoff resulting in a CFTT.  The Captain stated the First Officer noticed the error and immediately corrected back on course away from the mountains.	
1850348	202110	1801-2400	ZZZ.TRACON	US						VMC			Night			Center ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Hydraulic Main System		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)						1850348												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	While climbing out of approximately 20,000 feet we received an ECAM G RSVR low level alert and noticed that the fluid quantity was zero.  I transferred control to the First Officer and completed the ECAM actions.  After referencing the QRH, there were many items now unavailable for use on landing.   Among these were gravity gear extension and no nose steering upon landing.  I contacted dispatch and maintenance to let them know our situation, and they advised us that ZZZ and tech ops would be ready for us upon landing.   I reiterated to dispatch that we would need a tow in from the runway upon landing and was assured they would be ready.  We briefly thought about diverting, but with a full load we were overweight.   We elected to continue to ZZZ.We discussed at length on the flight southbound what we needed to do upon arrival.   I chose not to inform inflight or the customers of the failure until closer to ZZZ.  I called the #1 up to the cockpit about an hour out and explained to her we had a code yellow.  I went into detail the nature of the problem and how it might affect our landing.   I explained that I didn't think there would be anything abnormal on landing other than the fire trucks and being towed to the gate.  Everyone was professional and did their jobs as trained.  I made a PA to the customers about 40 minutes out and explained the failure in layman's terms and what they could expect on landing.Out of an abundance of caution with a hydraulic failure, I instructed the First Officer to request priority handling with ZZZ approach.  After all pertinent information was exchanged we requested a 20 mile final to be sure we could get all checklist items completed before landing.  On downwind we slowed and lowered the gear successfully by the gravity extension.   We then configured the aircraft for landing.  ATC asked if we were ready for the approach and we flew a 15 mile final to runway 10L.  We reiterated to the tower that we would not be able to clear the runway upon landing.  We touched down about 1500 feet down the runway and braked to a stop abeam Taxiway XX.  I made a quick PA when the aircraft stopped to ask everyone to stay seated.  We completed the after landing checklist, started the APU and shut down the engines.  The tug then entered the runway, hooked up to us and towed us to Gate XX.  The required logbook entries were made and I discussed our failure with CFR and tech ops.  Tech ops said there was a major leak on top of engine number one in a hydraulic line.				A320 Captain reported diverting after the loss of the green hydraulic system and made a precautionary landing.	
1850487	202110	0601-1200	ZZZ.Airport	US				3000					Daylight			Tower ZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B ZZZ																								Electrical Distribution		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; Captain	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 146; Flight Crew Type 19000		Time Pressure; Troubleshooting			1850487	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Not Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 175; Flight Crew Type 3600					1849848	Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Flight Deck / Cabin / Aircraft Event Smoke / Fire / Fumes / Odor			Automation Aircraft Other Automation; Person Flight Attendant; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Returned To Departure Airport; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action	Aircraft	Aircraft	All operations normal, flaps 5 takeoff, departing Runway XX on the ZZZZZ1 out of ZZZ. Captain, Pilot Flying, First Officer, Pilot Monitoring. Upon flap retraction from flaps 1 to flaps up, we received a Master Caution light. Associated with the Master Caution light was the following, Overhead, HYD, Fuel, Electric and anti-ice lights. At just about the same time the First Officer lost all cockpit display system instrumentation, primary flight display and primary and navigation display, as well as his flight management computer. The flaps were stuck at position one even though the flap handle was in the up position and seated. Multiple failures in rapid succession. Keep in mind, we are still in the takeoff regime of our flight, while trying to communicate with the Tower Controller handing us off to the Departure Control, while communicating between ourselves as to what we saw, and the appropriate actions necessary. I told the First Officer to [request priority handling] and a return to the airfield. We told the Departure Controller souls on board and amount of fuel onboard while turning up wind, All the time accomplishing the cover of the QRH, over and over. I flew the aircraft and talked with ATC while the First Officer kept an open line of communication with the cabin crew, while preparing the aircraft for a flaps 1 overweight landing. On a right, downwind flaps1 to Runway XX, prior to turning base, the cabin crew advised the right side of the cabin was dark and we smelled fumes. I told the Tower we needed to land immediately and made the turn to join final Runway XX. I told the First Officer to advise the cabin crew and passengers we would be stopping on the runway, to remain seated and standby for further instructions. It was a smooth landing. Airport Rescue Firefighters (ARFF) met the aircraft and did an inspection and found no indication of smoke or fire. I made the decision not to evacuate the aircraft at that time. I had the fire department follow us to the gate and let us know if they saw any smoke or fire, that would prompt an immediate evacuation of the aircraft. Once we arrived at gate, fire department personnel boarded the aircraft as passengers were deplaning. We were in the air a total of 18 minutes, either in the takeoff phase or landing phase of flight. No injuries.		All operations normal departing on XXL on the ZZZZZ1 out of ZZZ.  Upon flap retraction from 1 to UP, we received a Master Caution light with the OVERHEAD, HYD, FUEL, ELECTRIC and ANTI-ICE annunciators lit.  FO (First Officer) lost all information on the two right side displays as well as the right side FMC.  Flaps stayed at 1 with the handle positioned to UP.  Captain continued to hand fly the aircraft and kept speed at 205 knots with speed window remaining open. ATC queried about loss of MODE C and requested altitude passing.  Flight Attendant called and mentioned loss of right side and fumes. FO incorrectly assumed loss of right side engine initially from FA assessment but later confirmed it was loss of right side cabin lighting. Captain immediately requested a return to ZZZ and FO coordinated a right turn for visual to Runway XY. Two good engines and generators.  While Captain hand flew the aircraft; the FO set up Captain side navigation radios, Mode Control Panel and loaded an approach into the FMC.  Visual to the longest runway, XY, was chosen for a flaps 1 landing. FO coordinated with F/As for the emergency return and informed them we would be full stopping on the runway and emergency crash response vehicles will be looking over the aircraft, and we will not be evacuating the aircraft immediately. There was time to run equipment cooling QRH and placed in alternate.  On downwind, Pilots noticed the slight acrid smell of an electrical fire which got stronger throughout base and final. Touchdown was soft in the first 1,000 feet and landing roll went just past the 2,000-foot remaining marker.  Fire response looked aircraft over and determined no visible smoke or fire.  Captain elected to taxi to gate and deplane using a jetway. FO turned off recirc fans, IFE, Galley switches.  APU was not started and ground power but no air was used at gate. After shutdown pilots noticed the strong acrid smell in the cabin.  Fire response inspected aircraft and found no evidence of fire or smoke.		Air carrier flight crew reported an electrical system malfunction accompanied by cabin fumes. Air crew completed the QRH procedure and executed a safe return to departure airport.	
1850795	202110	1801-2400	SFO.Airport	CA						VMC						Tower SFO	Air Carrier	B737 Next Generation Undifferentiated		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Takeoff / Launch		Class B SFO																												Tower SFO	Air Carrier	Widebody, Low Wing, 2 Turbojet Eng		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Taxi																										Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	First Officer; Pilot Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP)	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 129; Flight Crew Total 8648; Flight Crew Type 8648					1850795												Ground Event / Encounter Jet Blast			Person Flight Crew	In-flight	General None Reported / Taken	Environment - Non Weather Related; Procedure	Ambiguous	Winds and weather had SFO operating with both arrivals and departures using Runway 28L/R. We were cleared into position Runway 28R after a B777 landed on the same runway. The B777 cleared the runway but held short of Runway 28L. We were then cleared for takeoff. The takeoff was normal and I was Flying Pilot. I happened to notice the B777 to the left between the parallels as I began a normal rotation. As we passed the B777 we experienced a hard jolt from the left. Thankfully it was a sudden but short jolt as it required some maneuvering to settle the flight path. I am not sure if the B777 had been cleared across but it felt like a significant jet blast we got from the heavy. No anomalies or limitations were exceeded. The flight continued normally.				B737NG First Officer reported encountering jet blast on takeoff from a taxiing Widebody aircraft.	
1850833	202110	1201-1800	ZZZZ.ARTCC	FO				14000		VMC			Daylight			Center ZZZZ	Air Carrier	B737-800		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Descent	Vectors	Class B ZZZZ																																																															Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Pilot Flying; First Officer	Flight Crew Multiengine; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument			Situational Awareness; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface; Confusion	Party1 Flight Crew; Party2 ATC		1850833	Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Multiengine			Situational Awareness; Confusion; Communication Breakdown; Human-Machine Interface	Party1 ATC; Party2 Flight Crew		1851105	ATC Issue All Types; Deviation - Altitude Overshoot; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Clearance; Deviation / Discrepancy - Procedural Published Material / Policy; Inflight Event / Encounter CFTT / CFIT		N	Person Air Traffic Control; Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Flight Crew Took Evasive Action	Human Factors; Procedure	Procedure	ZZZZZ 1B arrival into ZZZZZ. Received clearance 'descent to FL250 at ZZZZZ.' Complied. After ZZZZZ, received clearance 'descend via ZZZZZ 1B, maintain 14 thousand,' We started our VNAV path descend. Passing ZZZZ1, we were given vectors, heading 070 while still descending to 14K. To the northwest of ZZZZ2, we were given clearance 'direct to ZZZZ3 and descent to 10 thousand (or descend and maintain 10 thousand, definitely not DESCEND VIA'). LNAV and VNAVing to ZZZZ3, noticed we are not descending below 14K due to the altitude restrictions in the FMS, and knowing we weren't given descend via for the 10K, I either V/S or LVL CHGed to start descending to 10K. Passing 11.5K, VFR, it seemed odd that they would want us that much lower than published. We asked approach to confirm descend to 10K. ATC responded with we're below the altitude on the arrival, I immediately dialed in something higher in the altitude window and started a climb. At the same time we received CAUTION TERRAIN, ATC came back and said we're given a descend via to 10K and now to climb and maintain 12 thousand. We climbed back up to 12K and completed the arrival and approach. Being a domestic pilot my entire time at [Company], pretty much all of it in ZZZ. This might be the second time I've been to ZZZZ with the previous occasion probably over 6 years ago. Only ever flew into [Country] a handful of times and no [Continent]. I made sure I read all the airport familiarization pages, all the Jepp pages, all the [Country] info in the FOM. And was extremely vigilant regarding 'direct to vs direct' and 'descend via and descend to' clearances. Those were the key words I was listening for. Me and the Captain both agree and the second clearance did not include descend via. I do believe my lack of outside of US experience played a role. I trusted foreign ATC and thought the that low descent was common local procedure. In training and all the distance learning modules, we get hammered on the difference between DIRECT TO and DIRECT. I don't recall as much of an emphasis on DESCEND VIA vs DESCEND UNRESTRICTED (which is official ICAO phraseology on an arrival) and DESCEND TO/DESCEND AND MAINTAIN, which is a FAA phraseology for US arrivals meaning to descend unrestricted. I believe these points should be emphasized in future training modules. In regards to ZZZZ ATC, in my opinion they should only use 2 descent terminologies on arrivals with altitude restrictions. Either DESCEND VIA or DESCEND UNRESTRICTED. Using a DESCEND AND MAINTAIN or DESCEND TO clearance on an arrival with altitude restrictions, from what I can tell is non-standard per our FOM and should not be used.		CLEARANCE......Descending Via ZZZZZ 1B except maintain 14,000. Just after ZZZZZ vectored off the STAR to the east for spacing and given a descent to 10,000 ft. with multiple speed changes. Approaching 10,000 ft. in VMC conditions I queried the Controller about our altitude he said we should be at 14,000 ft. descending via. We were on vectors. We immediately initiated a climb to 14,000 ft. and as per his request leveled at 12,000 ft. We were cleared direct to ZZZZ3 and were told to rejoin the STAR. We landed XXL I believe there was a freq change to a new controller who forgot about us. He made no mention of our altitude until I asked him to confirm. There was also no mention of rejoining the STAR.Remain on the STAR/SID to the max extent possible.		B737-800 flight crew reported communications issues resulted in a descent below assigned altitude during the approach. Flight crew climbed up to correct altitude and continued the approach to landing.	
1851036	202110		ZZZ.ARTCC	US												TRACON ZZZ	Air Carrier	A320		2	Part 121	IFR	Passenger		Cruise	Vectors	Class A ZZZ																								Indicating and Warning - Landing Gear		X	Malfunctioning																																				Aircraft X	Flight Deck	Air Carrier	Captain; Pilot Not Flying	Flight Crew Air Transport Pilot (ATP); Flight Crew Instrument; Flight Crew Multiengine	Flight Crew Last 90 Days 108.03; Flight Crew Total 961.32; Flight Crew Type 961.32		Troubleshooting			1851036												Aircraft Equipment Problem Critical		N	Person Flight Crew	In-flight	Air Traffic Control Provided Assistance; Flight Crew Overcame Equipment Problem; Flight Crew Landed in Emergency Condition; Flight Crew Diverted; Flight Crew Requested ATC Assistance / Clarification; General Maintenance Action; General Flight Cancelled / Delayed	Aircraft	Aircraft	At cruise altitude, just prior to T/D, we received ECAM 'WHEEL N/W STRG FAULT' followed shortly after by 'BRAKES SYS 2 FAULT', neither of which had an ECAM procedure.  I therefore asked the First Officer to reference the QRH, both of which had a QRH procedure.   There was no airborne procedure for the WHEEL N/W STRG FAULT.  We did apply the QRH procedure for BRAKES SYS 2 FAULT, but as soon as BRAKES SYS 2 FAULT cleared, we got a 'BRAKES SYS 1 FAULT' ECAM.I transferred aircraft control to the First Officer, and also delegated the radios to him.  I made voice contact with NAME via ACARS *call me request.  Dispatch got Maintenance Control and an Operations representative on the call.  I discussed the nature of the failure with all parties and after discussing continuing to ZZZ or diverting to either ZZZ1, ZZZ2, or ZZZ3, decide the safest choice considering runway length, weather, and emergency resources was a diversion to ZZZ3.Dispatch gave us fuel burn to and landing data at ZZZ3.  They also notified the station and arranged resources.I briefed the First Officer what transpired and decided to have him remain Pilot Flying.  I notified ATC of the diversion and advised ATC.The First Officer made a normal landing.  We landed uneventfully, came to a stop on center line, and the emergency vehicles approached the aircraft and have the all-clear for us to be towed off the runway to the gate.				A320 Captain reported Brake Sys 1 and 2, plus N/W steering fault ECAM messages in flight and elected to divert and make a precautionary landing.	
